age">1416</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "four-vedas-english-translation", "page": 1416, "quote": "XII, 4. The necessity of giving away sterile cows to the"}]</data></node><node id="av-bloomfield-11-1"><data key="title">AV 11.1 · The preparation of the brahmaudana, the porridge (Bloomfield)</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">A selection in the supplied Bloomfield anthology, not a claim of complete book coverage.</data><data key="doc">four-vedas-english-translation</data><data key="page">1419</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "four-vedas-english-translation", "page": 1419, "quote": "XI, 1. The preparation of the brahmaudana, the porridge"}]</data></node><node id="av-bloomfield-12-3"><data key="title">AV 12.3 · The preparation of the brahmaudana, the porridge (Bloomfield)</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">A selection in the supplied Bloomfield anthology, not a claim of complete book coverage.</data><data key="doc">four-vedas-english-translation</data><data key="page">1421</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "four-vedas-english-translation", "page": 1421, "quote": "XII, 3. The preparation of the brahmaudana, the porridge"}]</data></node><node id="av-bloomfield-9-3"><data key="title">AV 9.3 · Removal of a house that has been presented to a priest (Bloomfield)</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">A selection in the supplied Bloomfield anthology, not a claim of complete book coverage.</data><data key="doc">four-vedas-english-translation</data><data key="page">1426</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "four-vedas-english-translation", "page": 1426, "quote": "IX, 3. Removal of a house that has been presented to a priest"}]</data></node><node id="av-bloomfield-b20"><data key="title">Atharvaveda · Book 20 selections (Bloomfield)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Selected hymns only; the anthology does not supply the complete book.</data><data key="doc">four-vedas-english-translation</data><data key="page">1428</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "four-vedas-english-translation", "page": 1428, "quote": "XX, 127. A kuntâpa-hymn."}]</data></node><node id="av-bloomfield-20-127"><data key="title">AV 20.127 · A kuntâpa-hymn. (Bloomfield)</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">A selection in the supplied Bloomfield anthology, not a claim of complete book coverage.</data><data key="doc">four-vedas-english-translation</data><data key="page">1428</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "four-vedas-english-translation", "page": 1428, "quote": "XX, 127. A kuntâpa-hymn."}]</data></node><node id="av-bloomfield-12-1"><data key="title">AV 12.1 · Hymn to goddess Earth. (Bloomfield)</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">A selection in the supplied Bloomfield anthology, not a claim of complete book coverage.</data><data key="doc">four-vedas-english-translation</data><data key="page">1429</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "four-vedas-english-translation", "page": 1429, "quote": "XII, 1. Hymn to goddess Earth."}]</data></node><node id="av-bloomfield-b13"><data key="title">Atharvaveda · Book 13 selections (Bloomfield)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Selected hymns only; the anthology does not supply the complete book.</data><data key="doc">four-vedas-english-translation</data><data key="page">1433</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "four-vedas-english-translation", "page": 1433, "quote": "XIII, 1. Prayer for sovereign power addressed to the god"}]</data></node><node id="av-bloomfield-13-1"><data key="title">AV 13.1 · Prayer for sovereign power addressed to the god (Bloomfield)</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">A selection in the supplied Bloomfield anthology, not a claim of complete book coverage.</data><data key="doc">four-vedas-english-translation</data><data key="page">1433</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "four-vedas-english-translation", "page": 1433, "quote": "XIII, 1. Prayer for sovereign power addressed to the god"}]</data></node><node id="av-bloomfield-11-5"><data key="title">AV 11.5 · Glorification of the sun, or the primeval principle, as a (Bloomfield)</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">A selection in the supplied Bloomfield anthology, not a claim of complete book coverage.</data><data key="doc">four-vedas-english-translation</data><data key="page">1437</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "four-vedas-english-translation", "page": 1437, "quote": "XI, 5. Glorification of the sun, or the primeval principle, as a"}]</data></node><node id="av-bloomfield-11-4"><data key="title">AV 11.4 · Prâna, life or breath, personified as the supreme (Bloomfield)</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">A selection in the supplied Bloomfield anthology, not a claim of complete book coverage.</data><data key="doc">four-vedas-english-translation</data><data key="page">1439</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "four-vedas-english-translation", "page": 1439, "quote": "XI, 4. Prâna, life or breath, personified as the supreme"}]</data></node><node id="av-bloomfield-9-2"><data key="title">AV 9.2 · Prayer to Kâma (love), personified as a primordial (Bloomfield)</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">A selection in the supplied Bloomfield anthology, not a claim of complete book coverage.</data><data key="doc">four-vedas-english-translation</data><data key="page">1440</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "four-vedas-english-translation", "page": 1440, "quote": "IX, 2. Prayer to Kâma (love), personified as a primordial"}]</data></node><node id="av-bloomfield-19-53"><data key="title">AV 19.53 · Prayer to Kâla (time), personified as a primordial (Bloomfield)</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">A selection in the supplied Bloomfield anthology, not a claim of complete book coverage.</data><data key="doc">four-vedas-english-translation</data><data key="page">1442</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "four-vedas-english-translation", "page": 1442, "quote": "XIX, 53. Prayer to Kâla (time), personified as a primordial"}]</data></node><node id="av-bloomfield-19-54"><data key="title">AV 19.54 · Prayer to Kâla (time), personified as a primordial (Bloomfield)</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">A selection in the supplied Bloomfield anthology, not a claim of complete book coverage.</data><data key="doc">four-vedas-english-translation</data><data key="page">1442</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "four-vedas-english-translation", "page": 1442, "quote": "XIX, 54. Prayer to Kâla (time), personified as a primordial"}]</data></node><node id="av-bloomfield-11-7"><data key="title">AV 11.7 · Apotheosis of the ukkhishta, the leavings of the (Bloomfield)</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">A selection in the supplied Bloomfield anthology, not a claim of complete book coverage.</data><data key="doc">four-vedas-english-translation</data><data key="page">1443</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "four-vedas-english-translation", "page": 1443, "quote": "XI, 7. Apotheosis of the ukkhishta, the leavings of the"}]</data></node><node id="av-bloomfield-9-1"><data key="title">AV 9.1 · Hymn to the honey-lash of the Asvins. (Bloomfield)</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">A selection in the supplied Bloomfield anthology, not a claim of complete book coverage.</data><data key="doc">four-vedas-english-translation</data><data key="page">1445</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "four-vedas-english-translation", "page": 1445, "quote": "IX, 1. Hymn to the honey-lash of the Asvins."}]</data></node><node id="u-isavasya-upanishad"><data key="title">Isavasya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Sees the moving world as pervaded by the Lord; places action, renunciation, knowledge and immortality together.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 3, "quote": "Isavasya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-isavasya-upanishad-commentary"><data key="title">Isavasya Upanishad — Nirmalananda Giri commentary</data><data key="type">commentary</data><data key="summary">Modern commentary by Swami Nirmalananda Giri. Interpretations and additional anecdotes are distinct from the preceding Upanishad translation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 4, "quote": "Commentary on the Isavasya Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></node><node id="u-kena-upanishad"><data key="title">Kena Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Asks what directs mind, speech and senses; the Yaksha encounter exposes the gods' dependence on Brahman.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 33, "quote": "Kena Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-kena-upanishad-commentary"><data key="title">Kena Upanishad — Nirmalananda Giri commentary</data><data key="type">commentary</data><data key="summary">Modern commentary by Swami Nirmalananda Giri. Interpretations and additional anecdotes are distinct from the preceding Upanishad translation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">36</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 36, "quote": "Commentary on the Kena Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></node><node id="u-katha-upanishad"><data key="title">Katha Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Nachiketas questions Death about the self, the good and the pleasant, immortality, and disciplined knowledge.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">44</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 44, "quote": "Katha Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-katha-upanishad-commentary"><data key="title">Katha Upanishad — Nirmalananda Giri commentary</data><data key="type">commentary</data><data key="summary">Modern commentary by Swami Nirmalananda Giri. Interpretations and additional anecdotes are distinct from the preceding Upanishad translation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">51</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 51, "quote": "Commentary on the Katha Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></node><node id="u-prasna-upanishad"><data key="title">Prasna Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Six seekers question Pippalada on origins, life-breath, sleep, Om and the sixteen-part Purusha.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">129</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 129, "quote": "Prasna Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-prasna-upanishad-commentary"><data key="title">Prasna Upanishad — Nirmalananda Giri commentary</data><data key="type">commentary</data><data key="summary">Modern commentary by Swami Nirmalananda Giri. Interpretations and additional anecdotes are distinct from the preceding Upanishad translation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">134</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 134, "quote": "Commentary on the Prashna Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></node><node id="u-mundaka-upanishad"><data key="title">Mundaka Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Angiras answers Saunaka's question about knowing all; distinguishes higher and lower knowledge and teaches Brahman.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">153</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 153, "quote": "Mundaka Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-mundaka-upanishad-commentary"><data key="title">Mundaka Upanishad — Nirmalananda Giri commentary</data><data key="type">commentary</data><data key="summary">Modern commentary by Swami Nirmalananda Giri. Interpretations and additional anecdotes are distinct from the preceding Upanishad translation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">158</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 158, "quote": "Commentary on the Mundaka Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></node><node id="u-mandukya-upanishad"><data key="title">Mandukya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Interprets Om through waking, dream, deep sleep and the fourth, nondual self; followed by Gaudapada's Karika.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">181</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 181, "quote": "Mandukya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-mandukya-upanishad-commentary"><data key="title">Mandukya Upanishad — Nirmalananda Giri commentary</data><data key="type">commentary</data><data key="summary">Modern commentary by Swami Nirmalananda Giri. Interpretations and additional anecdotes are distinct from the preceding Upanishad translation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">193</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 193, "quote": "Commentary on the Mandukya Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></node><node id="u-taittiriya-upanishad"><data key="title">Taittiriya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Combines student instruction with layered accounts of self and bliss; Bhrigu investigates Brahman through austerity.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">198</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 198, "quote": "Taittiriya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-taittiriya-upanishad-commentary"><data key="title">Taittiriya Upanishad — Nirmalananda Giri commentary</data><data key="type">commentary</data><data key="summary">Modern commentary by Swami Nirmalananda Giri. Interpretations and additional anecdotes are distinct from the preceding Upanishad translation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">205</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 205, "quote": "Commentary on the Taittiriya Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></node><node id="u-aitareya-upanishad"><data key="title">Aitareya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Narrates the Self's creation and entry into embodied life, then identifies consciousness with Brahman.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">227</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 227, "quote": "Aitareya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-aitareya-upanishad-commentary"><data key="title">Aitareya Upanishad — Nirmalananda Giri commentary</data><data key="type">commentary</data><data key="summary">Modern commentary by Swami Nirmalananda Giri. Interpretations and additional anecdotes are distinct from the preceding Upanishad translation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">230</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 230, "quote": "Commentary on the Aitereya Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></node><node id="u-chandogya-upanishad"><data key="title">Chandogya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Links chant, Om, breath and cosmic correspondences to dialogues on Brahman, the self, and the infinite.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">234</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 234, "quote": "Chandogya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-chandogya-upanishad-commentary"><data key="title">Chandogya Upanishad — Nirmalananda Giri commentary</data><data key="type">commentary</data><data key="summary">Modern commentary by Swami Nirmalananda Giri. Interpretations and additional anecdotes are distinct from the preceding Upanishad translation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">275</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 275, "quote": "Commentary on the Chandogya Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></node><node id="u-brihadaranyaka-upanishad"><data key="title">Brihadaranyaka Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Uses ritual symbolism and dialogues involving Yajnavalkya, Maitreyi, Gargi and Janaka to investigate the self and Brahman.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">312</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 312, "quote": "Brihadaranyaka Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-brihadaranyaka-upanishad-commentary"><data key="title">Brihadaranyaka Upanishad — Nirmalananda Giri commentary</data><data key="type">commentary</data><data key="summary">Modern commentary by Swami Nirmalananda Giri. Interpretations and additional anecdotes are distinct from the preceding Upanishad translation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">352</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 352, "quote": "Commentary on the Brihadaranyaka Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></node><node id="u-brahma-upanishad"><data key="title">Brahma Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Pippalada teaches Saunaka about the inner Brahman, life-breath and the sacred thread understood as knowledge.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">385</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 385, "quote": "Brahma Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-kaivalya-upanishad"><data key="title">Kaivalya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Brahma teaches Asvalayana that faith, devotion, meditation and renunciation lead to the highest knowledge.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">388</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 388, "quote": "Kaivalya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-jabala-upanishad"><data key="title">Jabala Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Yajnavalkya explains Avimukta and renunciation; treats the conditions and conduct of the wandering ascetic.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">391</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 391, "quote": "Jabala Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-svetasvatara-upanishad"><data key="title">Svetasvatara Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Investigates the cause of existence, the Lord, the self, nature and the discipline through which reality is known.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">394</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 394, "quote": "Svetasvatara Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-hamsa-upanishad"><data key="title">Hamsa Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Sanatkumara teaches Gautama the Hamsa discipline, breath-linked contemplation and inner sound.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">402</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 402, "quote": "Hamsa Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-aruni-upanishad"><data key="title">Aruni Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Brahma instructs Aruni on abandoning ritual possessions and worldly ties for the knowledge of Brahman.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">404</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 404, "quote": "Aruni Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-garbha-upanishad"><data key="title">Garbha Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Presents a traditional account of embryonic development, bodily elements and the body interpreted as sacrifice.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">406</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 406, "quote": "Garbha Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-narayana-upanishad"><data key="title">Narayana Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Praises Narayana as the source and identity of all; expounds the eight-syllable Om Namo Narayanaya mantra.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">408</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 408, "quote": "Narayana Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-paramahamsa-upanishad"><data key="title">Paramahamsa Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Brahma answers Narada about the inward freedom, duties and outward simplicity of the Paramahamsa ascetic.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">411</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 411, "quote": "Paramahamsa Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-amrita-bindu-upanishad"><data key="title">Amrita Bindu Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Explains how desire binds the mind and how the mind freed from sense objects becomes a means of liberation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">413</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 413, "quote": "Amrita Bindu Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-amrita-nada-upanishad"><data key="title">Amrita-Nada Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Uses the chariot of Om as an image for contemplation and sets out disciplines of yoga and breath.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">415</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 415, "quote": "Amrita-Nada Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-atharvasiras-upanishad"><data key="title">Atharvasiras Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Identifies Rudra with the gods, cosmic regions and Om; explains the one principle through many divine names.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">418</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 418, "quote": "Atharvasiras Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-atharvasikha-upanishad"><data key="title">Atharvasikha Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Atharva answers sages about meditation on Om and its four aspects, deities and Vedic correspondences.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">422</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 422, "quote": "Atharvasikha Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-maitrayani-upanishad"><data key="title">Maitrayani Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">King Brihadratha renounces his throne and asks the sage Sakayana for self-knowledge beyond decaying embodiment.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">425</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 425, "quote": "Maitrayani Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-kaushitaki-brahmana-upanishad"><data key="title">Kaushitaki Brahmana Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Contains dialogues about rebirth, life-breath and consciousness, including Indra-Pratardana and Balaki-Ajatasatru.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">429</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 429, "quote": "Kaushitaki Brahmana Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-brihad-jabala-upanishad"><data key="title">Brihad Jabala Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Busunda questions Kalagni Rudra about sacred ash, Rudraksha and Shaiva observance as a path to liberation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">441</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 441, "quote": "Brihad Jabala Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-nrisimhatapini-upanishad"><data key="title">Nrisimhatapini Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Presents Poorva and Uttara Narasimha Tapaniya translations on Narasimha, mantra, Om and the supreme self.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">446</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 446, "quote": "Nrisimha Poorva Tapaniya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-kalagni-rudra-upanishad"><data key="title">Kalagni Rudra Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Kalagnirudra explains the three ash marks of Tripundra and their ritual and contemplative correspondences.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">457</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 457, "quote": "Kalagni Rudra Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-maitreya-upanishad"><data key="title">Maitreya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Retells Brihadratha's disenchantment with embodied life and sets out self-knowledge and the renunciant's discipline.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">459</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 459, "quote": "Maitreya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-subala-upanishad"><data key="title">Subala Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Describes cosmic emergence and dissolution, the inner self and Narayana as the basis of beings.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">464</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 464, "quote": "Subala Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-kshurika-upanishad"><data key="title">Kshurika Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Uses a sharp knife of wisdom and inward yoga as imagery for cutting attachment; this version marks verses 2–9 unavailable.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">470</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 470, "quote": "Kshurika Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-mantrika-upanishad"><data key="title">Mantrika Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Presents the subtle, imperishable reality through swan imagery and the relation of knowledge, nature and the absolute.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">472</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 472, "quote": "Mantrika Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-sarva-sara-upanishad"><data key="title">Sarva Sara Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Defines bondage, liberation, ignorance, knowledge, consciousness states, sheaths, individual self and the absolute.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">474</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 474, "quote": "Sarva Sara Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-niralamba-upanishad"><data key="title">Niralamba Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Uses a sequence of questions and answers to define Brahman, God, the individual, nature, knowledge and liberation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">477</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 477, "quote": "Niralamba Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-suka-rahasya-upanishad"><data key="title">Suka Rahasya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Shiva teaches Suka the inner meaning of great Upanishadic sayings, including That Thou Art.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">481</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 481, "quote": "Suka Rahasya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-vajrasuchika-upanishad"><data key="title">Vajrasuchika Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Examines what makes a Brahmana and rejects body, birth and mere social designation as sufficient criteria.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">484</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 484, "quote": "Vajrasuchika Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-tejo-bindu-upanishad"><data key="title">Tejo-Bindu Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Teaches meditation on the seed of spiritual light and develops knowledge of the nondual self.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">486</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 486, "quote": "Tejo-Bindu Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-nada-bindu-upanishad"><data key="title">Nada Bindu Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Interprets Om as a bird and develops contemplation on inner sound as a means of freeing the mind.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">502</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 502, "quote": "Nada Bindu Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-dhyana-bindu-upanishad"><data key="title">Dhyana-Bindu Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Connects seed-syllable, spiritual sound, contemplation and yogic processes with the supreme state beyond sound.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">506</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 506, "quote": "Dhyana-Bindu Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-brahma-vidya-upanishad"><data key="title">Brahma Vidya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Explains Om through its syllables, their Vedic and divine correspondences, inner location and dissolution.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">512</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 512, "quote": "Brahma Vidya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-yoga-tattva-upanishad"><data key="title">Yoga Tattva Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Vishnu teaches Brahma the relation of yoga and knowledge and describes yogic disciplines leading to liberation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">514</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 514, "quote": "Yoga Tattva Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-atma-bodha-upanishad"><data key="title">Atma-Bodha Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Contemplates Narayana and Om as inner Brahman and develops assertions of the self's freedom and consciousness.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">520</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 520, "quote": "Atma-Bodha Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-narada-parivrajaka-upanishad"><data key="title">Narada Parivrajaka Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Narada asks Brahma about renunciation; treats eligibility, conduct, classifications and inner knowledge of ascetics.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">522</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 522, "quote": "Narada Parivrajaka Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-trisikhi-brahmana-upanishad"><data key="title">Trisikhi Brahmana Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">A Brahmana questions the Sun about body, self and cause; the reply links cosmology, inner faculties and yoga.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">549</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 549, "quote": "Trisikhi Brahmana Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-sita-upanishad"><data key="title">Sita Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Prajapati explains Sita as primordial nature and divine power, relating her to sound, knowledge and manifestation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">553</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 553, "quote": "Sita Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-yoga-chudamani-upanishad"><data key="title">Yoga Chudamani Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Teaches breath, Hamsa and yogic ascent; this supplied translation explicitly omits verses 2–29.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">557</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 557, "quote": "Yoga Chudamani Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-nirvana-upanishad"><data key="title">Nirvana Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Describes the Paramahamsa through inward renunciation and symbolic identifications of the ascetic life.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">562</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 562, "quote": "Nirvana Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-mandala-brahmana-upanishad"><data key="title">Mandala Brahmana Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">The solar Narayana teaches Yajnavalkya eightfold yoga, knowledge and inward contemplation of light.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">565</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 565, "quote": "Mandala Brahmana Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-dakshinamurti-upanishad"><data key="title">Dakshinamurti Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Sages ask Markandeya about his enduring life and joy; he teaches the secret knowledge and mantra of Dakshinamurti.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">571</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 571, "quote": "Dakshinamurti Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-sarabha-upanishad"><data key="title">Sarabha Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Praises Shiva as supreme and narrates Sarabha's subduing of Narasimha within this text's Shaiva presentation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">573</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 573, "quote": "Sarabha Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-skanda-upanishad"><data key="title">Skanda Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Presents the identity of Shiva and Vishnu and teaches the nonseparation of the individual self and the divine.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">575</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 575, "quote": "Skanda Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-tripadvibhuti-mahanarayana-upanishad"><data key="title">Tripadvibhuti Mahanarayana Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Presents Narayana as the source of all beings and cosmic forces; expounds Om Namo Narayanaya through eight-syllable mantra and inward realization.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">577</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 577, "quote": "Tripadvibhuti Mahanarayana Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-advaya-taraka-upanishad"><data key="title">Advaya Taraka Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Teaches nondual reality through neti-neti, inner light, Taraka yoga and guidance by a qualified teacher.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">579</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 579, "quote": "Advaya Taraka Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-rama-rahasya-upanishad"><data key="title">Rama Rahasya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Hanuman explains Rama's sacred formulae, their ritual arrangements and their liberating significance.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">581</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 581, "quote": "Rama Rahasya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-rama-tapaniya-upanishad"><data key="title">Rama Tapaniya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Contains Poorva and Uttara parts on Rama, Sita, devotional visualization and the saving Rama mantra.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">596</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 596, "quote": "Rama Tapaniya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-vasudeva-upanishad"><data key="title">Vasudeva Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Vasudeva teaches Narada the rules and significance of the upward Vaishnava forehead mark.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">602</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 602, "quote": "Vasudeva Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-mudgala-upanishad"><data key="title">Mudgala Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Interprets the Purusha Sukta in terms of Vishnu's pervasion, the fourfold Purusha and liberation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">604</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 604, "quote": "Mudgala Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-sandilya-upanishad"><data key="title">Sandilya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Atharvan answers Sandilya on eightfold yoga, including ethical disciplines, postures, breath and meditation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">607</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 607, "quote": "Sandilya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-paingala-upanishad"><data key="title">Paingala Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Yajnavalkya teaches Paingala about secondless Brahman, creation, inner faculties and liberating knowledge.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">618</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 618, "quote": "Paingala Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-bhikshuka-upanishad"><data key="title">Bhikshuka Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Classifies four forms of mendicant ascetic and describes their differing modes of life.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">624</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 624, "quote": "Bhikshuka Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-maha-upanishad"><data key="title">Maha Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Begins with Narayana and creation, then develops teachings about mind, detachment and liberation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">626</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 626, "quote": "Maha Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-sariraka-upanishad"><data key="title">Sariraka Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Describes bodily elements, sense and action organs, inner faculties, qualities and related correspondences.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">647</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 647, "quote": "Sariraka Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-yoga-sikha-upanishad"><data key="title">Yoga Sikha Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Sets out yoga centered on Om, concentration and the relation of yogic realization to knowledge.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">649</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 649, "quote": "Yoga Sikha Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-turiyatita-avadhuta-upanishad"><data key="title">Turiyatita Avadhuta Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Narayana explains the rare Avadhuta who has passed beyond ordinary distinctions and lives in the supreme self.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">653</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 653, "quote": "Turiyatita Avadhuta Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-sannyasa-upanishad"><data key="title">Sannyasa Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Describes the transition to renunciation, including rites, qualifications and conduct of the ascetic.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">655</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 655, "quote": "Sannyasa Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-paramahamsa-parivrajaka-upanishad"><data key="title">Paramahamsa-Parivrajaka Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Narayana answers Brahma about qualifications and conduct of the Paramahamsa wandering monk.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">663</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 663, "quote": "Paramahamsa-Parivrajaka Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-aksha-malika-upanishad"><data key="title">Aksha Malika Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Guha answers Prajapati about the materials, construction, consecration and use of a rosary.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">667</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 667, "quote": "Aksha Malika Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-avyakta-upanishad"><data key="title">Avyakta Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">This supplied item presents transliterated Nasadiya, Hiranyagarbha and Purusha Suktas under stages of cosmic emergence.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">671</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 671, "quote": "Avyakta Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-ekakshara-upanishad"><data key="title">Ekakshara Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Praises the one imperishable reality as the source, indweller and sustainer of the world.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">674</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 674, "quote": "Ekakshara Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-annapurna-upanishad"><data key="title">Annapurna Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Ribhu instructs Nidagha on self-knowledge, mental freedom and liberation, in a teaching connected with Annapurna.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">676</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 676, "quote": "Annapurna Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-surya-upanishad"><data key="title">Surya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Identifies the Sun with the world-self, Brahman and the sources of gods, elements, Vedas and life.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">691</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 691, "quote": "Surya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-akshi-upanishad"><data key="title">Akshi Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Samkriti praises the Sun and receives teachings on knowledge and yoga beyond ordinary embodiment.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">693</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 693, "quote": "Akshi Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-adhyatma-upanishad"><data key="title">Adhyatma Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Explains superimposition of body and possessions on the self and teaches discrimination and realization of Brahman.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">697</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 697, "quote": "Adhyatma Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-kundika-upanishad"><data key="title">Kundika Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Describes the stages and inward requirements of renunciation after studentship and household life.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">703</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 703, "quote": "Kundika Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-savitri-upanishad"><data key="title">Savitri Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Pairs Savitar and Savitri across cosmic and human correspondences and expounds the Savitri formula.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">706</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 706, "quote": "Savitri Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-atma-upanishad"><data key="title">Atma Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Distinguishes the bodily self, inner self and supreme self in an account attributed to Angiras.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">708</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 708, "quote": "Atma Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-pasupata-brahmana-upanishad"><data key="title">Pasupata Brahmana Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Describes Pasupati as the power behind mind and senses and as the self-shining witness of beings.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">711</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 711, "quote": "Pasupata Brahmana Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-para-brahma-upanishad"><data key="title">Para-Brahma Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Pippalada teaches Saunaka about Brahman, creation and the inward significance of the sacred thread.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">713</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 713, "quote": "Para-Brahma Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-avadhuta-upanishad"><data key="title">Avadhuta Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Dattatreya explains to Samkriti the Avadhuta's freedom from worldly bonds and conventions.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">717</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 717, "quote": "Avadhuta Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-tripura-tapini-upanishad"><data key="title">Tripura Tapini Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Presents Tripura as divine power and supreme reality and expounds her mantra through Vedic correspondences.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">720</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 720, "quote": "Tripura Tapini Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-devi-upanishad"><data key="title">Devi Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">The Goddess answers the gods by declaring herself Brahman, nature, consciousness and the forms of the universe.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">732</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 732, "quote": "Devi Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-tripura-upanishad"><data key="title">Tripura Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Uses symbolic verse to describe the Goddess Tripura, her abodes, powers and mantra.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">736</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 736, "quote": "Tripura Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-katharudra-upanishad"><data key="title">Katharudra Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Brahma answers the gods with instructions on renunciation and knowledge of the supreme self.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">739</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 739, "quote": "Katharudra Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-bhavana-upanishad"><data key="title">Bhavana Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Maps the body and its constituents onto the Sri Chakra and the inward worship of divine power.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">743</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 743, "quote": "Bhavana Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-rudra-hridaya-upanishad"><data key="title">Rudra Hridaya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Vyasa answers Suka by teaching the unity of Rudra, Vishnu, Uma and the other divine forms.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">745</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 745, "quote": "Rudra Hridaya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-yoga-kundalini-upanishad"><data key="title">Yoga-Kundalini Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Relates control of mental impressions and life-breath to diet, posture and Kundalini-oriented yoga.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">748</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 748, "quote": "Yoga-Kundalini Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-bhasma-jabala-upanishad"><data key="title">Bhasma Jabala Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Shiva teaches Bhusunda the preparation, application and sacred significance of ash.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">756</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 756, "quote": "Bhasma Jabala Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-rudraksha-jabala-upanishad"><data key="title">Rudraksha Jabala Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Kalagnirudra tells Bhusunda the origin of Rudraksha beads from his tears and their traditional uses.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">759</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 759, "quote": "Rudraksha Jabala Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-ganapati-upanishad"><data key="title">Ganapati Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Addresses Ganapati as the manifest principle and Brahman, with praise, mantra and a meditation form.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">762</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 762, "quote": "Ganapati Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-jabala-darsana-upanishad"><data key="title">Jabala Darsana Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Dattatreya teaches Samkriti the eight branches of yoga and their ethical and bodily disciplines.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">764</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 764, "quote": "Jabala Darsana Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-tara-sara-upanishad"><data key="title">Tara Sara Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Yajnavalkya answers Brihaspati about Avimukta and the saving significance of sacred formulae.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">767</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 767, "quote": "Tara Sara Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-maha-vakya-upanishad"><data key="title">Maha Vakya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Brahma explains inward realization through Hamsa-Soham, breath control and contemplation of the solar Brahman.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">770</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 770, "quote": "Maha Vakya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-pancha-brahma-upanishad"><data key="title">Pancha Brahma Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Explains five aspects of Shiva through cosmic, ritual, Vedic and bodily correspondences.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">772</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 772, "quote": "Pancha Brahma Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-pranagnihotra-upanishad"><data key="title">Pranagnihotra Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Interprets eating and the life-breaths as an inward sacrifice, with the body as a sacrificial field.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">774</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 774, "quote": "Pranagnihotra Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-gopala-tapaniya-upanishad"><data key="title">Gopala-Tapaniya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Brahma answers sages about Krishna as supreme reality, with the Gopala mantra and devotional contemplation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">777</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 777, "quote": "Gopala-Tapaniya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-krishna-upanishad"><data key="title">Krishna Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Connects Rama's forest sages with Krishna's Gopis and interprets Krishna's companions as personified qualities.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">786</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 786, "quote": "Krishna Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-yajnavalkya-upanishad"><data key="title">Yajnavalkya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Yajnavalkya teaches Janaka about the transition into renunciation and the conduct of the monk.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">788</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 788, "quote": "Yajnavalkya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-varaha-upanishad"><data key="title">Varaha Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Varaha teaches Ribhu about reality, bodily principles, self-knowledge and yogic discipline.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">792</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 792, "quote": "Varaha Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-satyayaniya-upanishad"><data key="title">Satyayaniya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Treats the mind as the cause of bondage or liberation and develops renunciation and devotion toward Brahman.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">805</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 805, "quote": "Satyayaniya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-hayagriva-upanishad"><data key="title">Hayagriva Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Brahma teaches Narada mantras of horse-headed Hayagriva as lord of knowledge and Vedic learning.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">809</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 809, "quote": "Hayagriva Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-dattatreya-upanishad"><data key="title">Dattatreya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Narayana teaches Brahma contemplation and mantra of Dattatreya as the infinite Brahman.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">812</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 812, "quote": "Dattatreya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-garuda-upanishad"><data key="title">Garuda Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Presents a traditional Garuda-associated mantra transmission for protection against poison.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">815</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 815, "quote": "Garuda Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-kali-santarana-upanishad"><data key="title">Kali Santarana Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Brahma gives Narada the sixteen-name Hare Rama and Hare Krishna formula as the means of crossing Kali.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">817</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 817, "quote": "Kali Santarana Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-jabali-upanishad"><data key="title">Jabali Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Jabali answers Pippalada about the being, Pasupati, bondage and liberation through knowledge and sacred ash.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">819</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 819, "quote": "Jabali Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-saubhagya-lakshmi-upanishad"><data key="title">Saubhagya Lakshmi Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Narayana teaches the gods the knowledge and worship of Sri, followed by yogic instruction.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">821</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 821, "quote": "Saubhagya Lakshmi Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-sarasvati-rahasya-upanishad"><data key="title">Sarasvati-Rahasya Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Asvalayana explains praise and contemplation of Sarasvati and develops knowledge of the supreme self.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">826</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 826, "quote": "Sarasvati-Rahasya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-bahvricha-upanishad"><data key="title">Bahvricha Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Declares the Goddess the original reality from whom the gods and the universe arise, and identifies her with consciousness.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">833</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 833, "quote": "Bahvricha Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-muktika-upanishad"><data key="title">Muktika Upanishad</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Rama teaches Hanuman a 108-Upanishad curriculum, Vedic affiliations, liberation and the dissolution of mental impressions.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">835</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 835, "quote": "Muktika Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-gaudapada-s-mandukya-karika"><data key="title">Gaudapada’s Mandukya Karika</data><data key="type">commentary</data><data key="summary">Four chapters of metrical philosophical exposition follow the twelve verses of the Mandukya; the Karika is a separate composition.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">182</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 182, "quote": "MANDUKYA KARIKA OF GAUDAPADA"}]</data></node><node id="u-nrisimha-poorva-tapaniya"><data key="title">Nrisimha Poorva Tapaniya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">First of the two Narasimha Tapaniya translations supplied under catalog item 27.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">446</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 446, "quote": "Nrisimha Poorva Tapaniya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-nrisimha-uttara-tapaniya"><data key="title">Nrisimha Uttara Tapaniya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Second Narasimha Tapaniya translation; includes Om, the four consciousness aspects and the supreme self.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">451</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 451, "quote": "Nrisimha Uttara Tapaniya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-rama-poorva-tapaniya"><data key="title">Rama Poorva Tapaniya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">First part of the Rama Tapaniya item, with mantra and devotional visualization.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">596</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 596, "quote": "I. Rama Poorva Tapaniya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-rama-uttara-tapaniya"><data key="title">Rama Uttara Tapaniya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Second part, including Shiva’s Rama mantra and repeated identification of Rama with supreme reality.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">598</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 598, "quote": "II. Rama Uttara Tapaniya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-nachiketas-and-death"><data key="title">Nachiketas and Death</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Nachiketas refuses transient rewards and asks Death to resolve what remains after a person dies. Death teaches self-knowledge and immortality.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">45</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 45, "quote": "named Nachiketas."}]</data></node><node id="u-story-the-chariot-of-the-self"><data key="title">The chariot of the self</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The self is the rider, body the chariot, intellect the charioteer, mind the reins and senses the horses; disciplined discrimination directs the journey.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">48</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 48, "quote": "1-III-3. Know the Self to be the master of the chariot, and the body to be the chariot. Know the intellect"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-the-good-and-the-pleasant"><data key="title">The good and the pleasant</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Death distinguishes the good from the pleasant and commends Nachiketas for choosing lasting understanding.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 46, "quote": "serving different purposes, blind man. Good accrues to him who, of these two, chooses the preferable."}]</data></node><node id="u-story-the-yaksha-the-gods-and-uma"><data key="title">The Yaksha, the gods and Uma</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Agni and Vayu fail to master a mysterious Yaksha. Uma explains to Indra that their victory depended on Brahman.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 34, "quote": "and appeared before them, but they knew not who this Yaksha (worshipful Being) was."}]</data></node><node id="u-story-six-seekers-question-pippalada"><data key="title">Six seekers question Pippalada</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Six students approach Pippalada, live a year in discipline, then ask about origins, life-breath, sleep, Om and the sixteenfold person.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">129</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 129, "quote": "the venerable Pippalada with the belief, “This one will certainly tell us all about It.”"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-the-faculties-and-the-primacy-of-prana"><data key="title">The faculties and the primacy of Prana</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The faculties dispute who sustains the body; Prana demonstrates its central role when its apparent departure draws the others with it.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">130</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 130, "quote": "I-7: That very one rises up who is Prana and fire, who is identified with all creatures, and who is"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-saunaka-asks-how-to-know-all"><data key="title">Saunaka asks how to know all</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The householder Saunaka asks Angiras what knowledge makes everything known; the reply distinguishes higher knowledge from lower learning.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">153</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 153, "quote": "I-i-3: Saunaka, well known as a great householder, having approached Angiras duly, asked, ‘O adorable"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-two-birds-on-one-tree"><data key="title">Two birds on one tree</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Two companion birds inhabit one tree; one eats its fruit while the other watches, an image through which the text teaches the self and its realization.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">156</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 156, "quote": "III-i-1: Two birds that are ever associated and have similar names, cling to the same tree. Of these, one"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-bhrigu-learns-from-varuna"><data key="title">Bhrigu learns from Varuna</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Bhrigu repeatedly investigates Brahman through concentration, moving through food, life-breath, mind and knowledge to bliss.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">203</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 203, "quote": "Bhrigu Valli"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-janashruti-and-raikva"><data key="title">Janashruti and Raikva</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">King Janashruti seeks instruction from Raikva with the cart after hearing his knowledge praised; Raikva teaches the absorbing principle.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">250</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 250, "quote": "IV-i-3: Bhallaksa replied to him, ‘lo, how could you so describe him as if he were Raikva with the"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-satyakama-jabala-s-truthfulness"><data key="title">Satyakama Jabala’s truthfulness</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Satyakama honestly reports his uncertain parentage, is accepted as a student, and receives teachings through the bull, fire and birds.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">251</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 251, "quote": "IV-iv-1: Once upon a time Satyakama Jabala addressed his mother Jabala, ‘Mother, I desire to live the"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-upakosala-and-the-teaching-fires"><data key="title">Upakosala and the teaching fires</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">While Upakosala tends his teacher’s fires, the fires instruct him; Satyakama later completes the instruction.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">252</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 252, "quote": "IV-x-1: Once upon a time Upakosala Kamalayana lived with Satyakama Jabala the life of a"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-asvapati-and-the-cosmic-self"><data key="title">Asvapati and the cosmic self</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Seekers who contemplate different parts of the cosmic person are taught by King Asvapati to understand the whole Vaisvanara self.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">258</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 258, "quote": "V-xi-4: Uddalaka said to the, ‘Revered sirs, at present, Asvapati, the son of Kekaya, is studying this"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-uddalaka-teaches-svetaketu"><data key="title">Uddalaka teaches Svetaketu</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Uddalaka uses ordinary substances and processes to guide Svetaketu toward the subtle ground of all beings and the repeated teaching That Thou Art.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">260</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 260, "quote": "VI-i-1: Om. Once upon a time there was one Svetaketu, the grandson of Aruna. His father said to him,"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-the-seed-and-the-salt-water"><data key="title">The seed and the salt water</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Uddalaka uses the unseen essence in a small seed and dissolved salt to illustrate a subtle reality present throughout existence.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">264</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 264, "quote": "VI-xiii-1-2: ‘Put this salt into water and then come to me in the morning’. He did so. The father said to"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-narada-and-sanatkumara-beyond-learning"><data key="title">Narada and Sanatkumara: beyond learning</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Narada knows many branches of learning but seeks freedom from sorrow; Sanatkumara leads him from names and lesser supports toward the infinite.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">265</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 265, "quote": "VII-i-1: Om. ‘Revered sir, teach me,’ thus saying Narada approached Sanatkumara. Sanatkumara said"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-indra-and-virochana-seek-the-self"><data key="title">Indra and Virochana seek the self</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Indra and Virochana ask Prajapati about the self; Indra repeatedly returns after finding flaws in identification with reflection, dream and sleep.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">272</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 272, "quote": "and so did Virochana from among the demons. Then without communicating with each other, they both"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-balaki-and-ajatasatru"><data key="title">Balaki and Ajatasatru</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Balaki offers successive cosmic identifications of Brahman; King Ajatasatru leads him to the person associated with consciousness beyond those partial accounts.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">320</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 320, "quote": "Ajatasatru, the king of Benares, ‘I will tell you about Brahman’. Ajatasatru said, ‘For this proposal I"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-yajnavalkya-and-maitreyi"><data key="title">Yajnavalkya and Maitreyi</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Maitreyi asks whether wealth can grant immortality. Yajnavalkya teaches that the self should be heard, reflected on and realized.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">322</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 322, "quote": "II-iv-1: ‘Maitreyi, my dear’, said Yajnavalkya, ‘I am going to renounce this life. Allow me to finish"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-janaka-s-assembly-and-yajnavalkya"><data key="title">Janaka’s assembly and Yajnavalkya</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">At King Janaka’s sacrificial assembly, scholars question Yajnavalkya on ritual, self and ultimate reality.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">326</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 326, "quote": "III-i-1: Om. Janaka, Emperor of Videha, performed a sacrifice in which gifts were freely distributed."}]</data></node><node id="u-story-gargi-questions-yajnavalkya"><data key="title">Gargi questions Yajnavalkya</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Gargi asks what supports the world and what supports its support; the exchange culminates in the imperishable.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">328</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 328, "quote": "iii-vi-1: Then Gargi, the daughter of Vacaknu, asked him, ‘Yajnavalkya’, she said, ‘if all this is"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-uddalaka-asks-about-the-inner-controller"><data key="title">Uddalaka asks about the inner controller</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Yajnavalkya answers Uddalaka’s question by describing the unseen controller within earth, elements, beings and the self.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">329</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 329, "quote": "not know it, sir\". The Gandharva said to him and the students, \"Kapya, do you know that Internal Ruler"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-janaka-asks-about-the-light-of-a-person"><data key="title">Janaka asks about the light of a person</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The discussion moves from external sources of light to the self and examines dream, sleep, death and liberation.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">336</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 336, "quote": "IV-iii-2: ‘Yajnavalkya, what serves as the light for a man ?’ ‘The light of the sun, O Emperor’, said"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-indra-and-pratardana"><data key="title">Indra and Pratardana</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Pratardana reaches Indra, who directs him toward understanding life-breath and consciousness as the central teaching.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">435</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 435, "quote": "III-1. Pratardana, the son of Divodasa, by means of fighting and virility, verily reached the beloved"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-balaki-and-ajatasatru-the-kaushitaki-version"><data key="title">Balaki and Ajatasatru: the Kaushitaki version</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">A second version of Balaki’s instruction by Ajatasatru turns from cosmic persons to the self and consciousness.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">437</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 437, "quote": "Having come to Ajatasatru of Kashi, he said, ‘Let me declare Brahman to you’. To him then Ajatasatru"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-brihadratha-seeks-self-knowledge"><data key="title">Brihadratha seeks self-knowledge</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Brihadratha leaves kingship and presses the sage Sakayana to teach the self rather than grant transient pleasures.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">425</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 425, "quote": "The King Brihadratha, setting up his eldest son as king, deeming the body to be impermanent, getting"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-shiva-teaches-suka-the-great-sayings"><data key="title">Shiva teaches Suka the great sayings</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">At Vyasa’s request, Shiva instructs Suka in Brahman and the intended meaning of the Upanishadic great sayings.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">481</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 481, "quote": "Suka Rahasya Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-sarabha-and-narasimha"><data key="title">Sarabha and Narasimha</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">This Shaiva text narrates Mahesvara taking Sarabha form and subduing Narasimha; it should be read as the account of this particular text.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">573</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 573, "quote": "Sarabha Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-rama-s-sages-become-krishna-s-gopis"><data key="title">Rama’s sages become Krishna’s Gopis</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The text links sages who wish to embrace Rama with Gopis in Krishna’s manifestation and interprets companions as personified qualities.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">786</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 786, "quote": "Gopis and then you can embrace me.”[Their wish was fulfilled in Krishnavatara.] 1"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-the-origin-of-rudraksha-beads"><data key="title">The origin of Rudraksha beads</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Kalagnirudra tells Bhusunda that tears shed from his eyes became Rudraksha beads and explains their sacred uses.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">759</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 759, "quote": "From my eyes thus closed, drops of water fell on the earth. These drops of tears turned into"}]</data></node><node id="u-story-rama-and-hanuman-s-curriculum"><data key="title">Rama and Hanuman’s curriculum</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Hanuman asks Rama about liberation; Rama supplies a graded course culminating in the 108 Upanishads and groups them by Veda.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">835</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 835, "quote": "intellect, delighted in contemplating his own form. At the end of this Samadhi, Hanuman asked with"}]</data></node><node id="u-collection-index"><data key="title">108-Upanishad contents and compiler’s note</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The compiler follows the order given in the Muktika and notes commentary on the first ten texts.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 1, "quote": "The order as given in the Muktika Upanishad"}]</data></node><node id="u-sanskrit-glossary"><data key="title">A Brief Sanskrit Glossary</data><data key="type">reference</data><data key="summary">Modern explanatory glossary appended after the Upanishads and commentaries; useful for terminology, distinct from the scripture translations.</data><data key="doc">108upanishads-compiled</data><data key="page">844</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 844, "quote": "A Brief Sanskrit Glossary"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiva"><data key="title">Shiva Mahapurana</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Two supplied files form a split retelling; their boundary pages overlap. Shiva worship, cosmic manifestations, Sati–Parvati, Ganesha, Karttikeya, demons, lingas and yoga.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 1, "quote": "5 Shiva Maha-Purana First of all, Sage Shaunak expressed his desire to Sutji about knowing the means, which could help a man in this era of Kali to attainment lord Shiva, by cleansing all the impurities of his mind and rectifying his inhere"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu"><data key="title">Vishnu Purana</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Two supplied files form a split retelling; their boundary pages overlap. Creation, Dhruva and Prahlada, geography, Vedic branches, dynasties and Krishna.</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 1, "quote": "3 Vishnu Purana This Purana has 6 Chapters (6 Parts) as follows: Part 1: Describes the creation of the universe, birth of the gods and the demons, Pralaya, churning of the sea, tales of the devotee Dhruva; killing of Hiranyakashipu etc. Pa"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas"><data key="title">Agni Purana</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">An encyclopedic supplied retelling: avatars, Rama and Krishna, ritual, sacred geography, kingship, grammar, medicine, yoga and knowledge of Brahman.</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 1, "quote": "10 Agni Purana This Purana is basically a preaching to the sage Vashishth by Agni. In his turn, Vashishth narrated it to Vyasaji, who related it to Sutji. Ultimately, Sutji narrated Agni Purana to an assembly of the sage"}]</data></node><node id="p-bhavisya-puranas"><data key="title">Bhavishya Purana</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">A short supplied devotional selection presenting claims about predictions of religions, kings and teachers. These are claims made by this edition; the map does not establish their date or historical accuracy.</data><data key="doc">bhavisya-puranas</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "bhavisya-puranas", "page": 1, "quote": "11 Bhavishya Purana Eleventh among the Puranas, contains five parts. The first part contains a description of the genesis, greatness of the dates and worship of Lord Vishnu, Shiva and Surya. Second, third and fourth part"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran"><data key="title">Brahma Purana</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">A five-page supplied overview of the iconography and symbolic attributes of Brahma, Sarasvati, Vishnu and Lakshmi; not an exhaustive Brahma Purana edition.</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "1 Brahma Purana This Purana contains 4 Chapters as follows. 1.1 Lord Brahma This chapter contains 9 sections: 1.1.1 A Profile of Brahmaji APPEARANCE OF BRAHMAJI According to the scriptures, Brahmaji was born on the lo"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran"><data key="title">Brahma Vaivarta Purana (supplied title)</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">The introduction advertises the four khands and Radha–Krishna, while the numbered body surveys creation, dynasties and pilgrimage narratives. The supplied title is retained without certifying alignment with a complete canonical edition.</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "9 Brahma Vaivart Purana It’s the twelfth Purana. It contains four parts- Brahma khand, Prakriti khand, Ganesh khand and Shri Krishna Janma khand. In Naimisharanya Tirth, addressing a gathering of great sages like Shauna"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana"><data key="title">Garuda Purana</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">A supplied overview of Vishnu, Garuda, creation, mantras, astrology, ancestral rites, Rama, Krishna, devotion, self-realization and death.</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 1, "quote": "14 Garuda Purana This Purana contains 14 Chapters – each with many sub-sections. 14.1 Introduction This chapter contains xx sections as follows: 14.1.1 Lord Vishnu’s Incarnations Sutji once reached Naimisharanya in cou"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran"><data key="title">Kurma Purana</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Vishnu as Kurma frames teachings on creation, Shiva and Vishnu, Parvati, dynasties, sacred places, ritual and the Ishvara Gita; the supplied retelling explicitly compares narrative variants.</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "12 Kurma Purana Lord Vishnu, in Kurm Avatar (tortoise incarnation), had first preached this Purana to Narad. In his turn, Narad narrated it to Sutji who later narrated this Purana to an assembly of the great sages. Initi"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana"><data key="title">Linga Purana</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">A supplied five-part retelling centered on Shiva, the linga, creation, yoga, devotion, sacred geography and narratives of Dhruva, Andhaka, Jalandhara and Upamanyu.</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 1, "quote": "17 Linga Purana This is the Fifth Purana. Preaching about the greatness of Lord Shiva and propagation of Ling-puja (worship of Shiva-Ling) are the main objectives of this Purana. This Purana has five parts: 1) Descri"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran"><data key="title">Markandeya Purana</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">A supplied retelling framed by Jaimini and the wise birds: Harishchandra, Madalasa and Alarka, Dattatreya, cosmic cycles and the Goddess narratives embedded under Savarni Manu.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "7 Markandeya Purana This Purana consists of 16 chapters: 7.1 Introduction Once Jaimini, a disciple of sage Vedavyasa expressed his curiosity before Markandeya- 'O Lord! In the great epic Mahabharat, which was created by"}]</data></node><node id="p-matsyapurana-english"><data key="title">Matsya Purana</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Supplied English Part I: chapters 1–128 at PDF pages 19–380, plus translated chapters 271–273 in Appendix III at pages 407–428. Editorial appendices and dynastic tables are distinct from the scripture translation. All 488 pages were processed by local OCR; this is a partial edition of the canonical work.</data><data key="doc">matsyapurana-english</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "matsyapurana-english", "page": 3, "quote": "THE MATSYA PURANAM CHAPTERS 1-128", "extraction": "OCR"}, {"doc": "matsyapurana-english", "page": 19, "quote": "CHAPTER I", "extraction": "OCR"}, {"doc": "matsyapurana-english", "page": 407, "quote": "APPENDIX III.", "extraction": "OCR"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran"><data key="title">Narada Purana</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">A supplied Narada retelling with Markandeya, Ganga, Sagara, Saudasa and Bali. Its opening page also contains a carryover from Linga Purana, excluded from Narada narrative classification.</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "Sutji replied--- Anybody who is desirous of Vanquishing his enemy must perform the rituals of Vajreshwari Vidya. The devotee should worship Vajra (a spear) and make offerings in the sacrificial fire holding it (Vajra) in"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran"><data key="title">Padma Purana</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">A supplied five-khanda overview connecting creation, moral tales, sacred places, Rama’s horse sacrifice, Vishnu’s avatars and Krishna.</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "2 Padma Purana"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran"><data key="title">Bhagavata Purana</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">A supplied twelve-chapter retelling centered on devotion: Parikshit and Shukadeva, Kapila, Dhruva, Prahlada, Gajendra and an extensive Krishna narrative.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "16 Shrimad Bhagavata Purana This Purana contains 12 Chapters – each with many sub-sections. Introduction Shrimad Bhagwad is regarded as a delicious fruit of Veda-Kalpa the period of four Yugas during which Vedas are su"}]</data></node><node id="p-the-skanda-purana"><data key="title">Skanda Purana — Part I, Kedara section</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">This supplied PDF is Part I only: Maheshvarakhanda, Kedarakhanda, chapters 1–35, followed by an index. It covers Daksha, ocean churning, Bali, Shiva–Parvati, Karttikeya and Shiva worship.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 1, "quote": "THE - SKANDA-PURANA PART I MOTILAL BANARSIDASS PUBLISHERS PRlVATE LIMITED. DELHI Downloaded from https://www.hol"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran"><data key="title">Vamana Purana</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">A supplied retelling containing Vamana and Bali alongside Shiva–Sati, the Goddess, Nara–Narayana, Kurukshetra pilgrimage, Andhaka and Gajendra.</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "6 Vamana Purana This Purana consists of the following 26 chapters: 6.1 Introduction This chapter contains only 1 section: Narad asks sage Pulastya about Vaaman incarnation: Vaaman Puraan is one of the eighteen Puraans"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana"><data key="title">Varaha Purana</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">A supplied eleven-section retelling framed by Varaha and Prithvi: creation, deities, vows, pilgrimage, ancestors, Nachiketa and the nature of realization.</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 1, "quote": "8 Varaha Purana This Purana consists of the following 11 sections: Narrates the tale of rescuing of the earth from Rasatal by Varaha incarnation of Lord Vishnu. Its contents in brief are as follows:- Mangala charan: Pr"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1"><data key="title">Vishnu Purana — supplied volume 1</data><data key="type">volume</data><data key="summary">A split supplied retelling; follow the page links for the passage in this volume.</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 1, "quote": "3 Vishnu Purana This Purana has 6 Chapters (6 Parts) as follows: Part 1: Describes the creation of the universe, birth of the gods and the demons, Pralaya, churning of the sea, tales of the devotee Dhruva; killing of Hir"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2"><data key="title">Vishnu Purana — supplied volume 2</data><data key="type">volume</data><data key="summary">A split supplied retelling; follow the page links for the passage in this volume.</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 1, "quote": "Kshatriyas got their heads shaven out of fear. Since those Kshatriyas had given up their religion, hence Brahmins boycotted them. As a result they became Malechchha. Thereafter King Sagar returned to his capital and rule"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahmaand-purana"><data key="title">Brahmanda Purana</data><data key="type">text</data><data key="summary">Supplied English Part III, Section III Upodghatapada continued: chapters 44–74 at PDF pages 10–248. Covers Parashurama, Sagara and Ganga, solar and lunar dynasties, music, and praise of Vishnu. All 248 pages were processed by local OCR. This continuation is not the complete Brahmanda Purana.</data><data key="doc">brahmaand-purana</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahmaand-purana", "page": 6, "quote": "THE BRAHMANDA PURANA", "extraction": "OCR; title and Part III designation visually checked"}, {"doc": "brahmaand-purana", "page": 10, "quote": "CHAPTER FORTYFOUR", "extraction": "OCR"}, {"doc": "brahmaand-purana", "page": 220, "quote": "CHAPTER SEVENTYFOUR", "extraction": "OCR"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1"><data key="title">Shiva Mahapurana — supplied volume 1</data><data key="type">volume</data><data key="summary">A split supplied retelling; follow the page links for the passage in this volume.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 1, "quote": "5 Shiva Maha-Purana First of all, Sage Shaunak expressed his desire to Sutji about knowing the means, which could help a man in this era of Kali to attainment lord Shiva, by cleansing all the impurities of his mind and r"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2"><data key="title">Shiva Mahapurana — supplied volume 2</data><data key="type">volume</data><data key="summary">A split supplied retelling; follow the page links for the passage in this volume.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 1, "quote": "Meanwhile Jalandhar went to Parvati in the guise of lord Shiva but was recognized by her. Being enchanted by the beauty of goddess Parvati, he looked at her with his evil intentions, but was immobilized by her wrath. Mo"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-1"><data key="title">The Avatars</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">In the forest that is known as Naimisharanya, Shounaka and the other rishis (sages) were performing a yajna (sacrifice) dedicated to the Lord Vishnu. Suta had also come there, on his way to a pilgrimage. The sages told Suta, “We have welcomed you. Now describe to us that which makes men all-knowing. Describe to us…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 1, "quote": "10.1 The Avatars In the forest that is known as Naimisharanya, Shounaka and the other rishis (sages) were performing a yajna (sacrifice) dedicated to the Lord Vishnu. Suta had also come there, on his way to"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-1-1"><data key="title">Matsya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Vishnu’s fish incarnation guides Manu through the deluge. The story connects preservation across cosmic destruction with the recurring Matsya motif.</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 1, "quote": "10.1.1 Matsya Agni told Vashishtha the story of the fish incarnation."}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-1-2"><data key="title">Kurma</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The tortoise incarnation supports the mountain used in the gods’ and demons’ ocean churning. Follow the other Purana versions for changes in emphasis.</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 2, "quote": "10.1.2 Kurma Many years ago there was a war between the devas (gods) and the daityas (demons) and the gods lost this war. They prayed to Vishnu to rescue them from the oppression of the demons. Vishnu to"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-1-3"><data key="title">Varaha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Vishnu’s next incarnation was in the form of a boar. The sage Kashyapa and his wife Diti had a son named Hiranyaksha. became the king of the asuras. Hiranyaksha’s meditation pleased Brahma and Brahma granted him the boon that he would be invincible in battle. Thus armed. Hiranyaksha went out to fight with the devas.…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 3, "quote": "10.1.3 Varaha Vishnu’s next incarnation was in the form of a boar. The sage Kashyapa and his wife Diti had a son named Hiranyaksha. became the king of the asuras. Hiranyaksha’s meditation pleased Brahma a"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-1-4"><data key="title">Narasimha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Hiranyaksha had a brother named Hiranyakashipu. Hiranyakashipu was furious to learn that his brother had been killed and the resolved to kill Vishnu. But this could not be done unless h e himself became powerful land invincible. Hiranyakashipu, therefore, began to pray to Brahma through difficult meditation. Brahma…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 4, "quote": "10.1.4 Narasimha Hiranyaksha had a brother named Hiranyakashipu. Hiranyakashipu was furious to learn that his brother had been killed and the resolved to kill Vishnu. But this could not be done unless h e hi"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-1-5"><data key="title">Vamana</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Prahlada’s grandson was Vali and Vali became very powerful. When he was the king of the asuras, there was a war between the devas and the asuras. The gods were defeated and were driven off from svarga. As always, the gods fled to Vishnu and began to pray to him to save them. Vishnu assured the gods that he would do…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 4, "quote": "10.1.5 Vamana Prahlada’s grandson was Vali and Vali became very powerful. When he was the king of the asuras, there was a war between the devas and the asuras. The gods were defeated and were driven off f"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-1-6"><data key="title">Parashurama</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The kshatriyas were the second of the four classes. It was their job to wear arms and protect the world. And rule. The brahmanas were the first of the four classes. It was their job to pray, study the sacred texts and perform religious rites. But the kshatriyas became very insolent and began to oppress the world and…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 5, "quote": "10.1.6 Parashurama The kshatriyas were the second of the four classes. It was their job to wear arms and protect the world. And rule. The brahmanas were the first of the four classes. It was their job to pray,"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-1-7"><data key="title">Rama</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Brahma came out of Vishnu’s navel. Brahma’s son was Marichi’s son Kashyapa, Kashyapa’s son Surya, Surya’s son Vaivasvata Manu, Manu’s son Ikshvaku, Ikskhvakku’s son Kakutstha, Kakutstha’s son Raghu, Raghu’s son Aja, Aja’s son Dasharatha, Dasharatha’s sons were Rama, Bharata, Lakshmana and Shatrughna. Since Rama was…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 6, "quote": "10.1.7 Rama Brahma came out of Vishnu’s navel. Brahma’s son was Marichi’s son Kashyapa, Kashyapa’s son Surya, Surya’s son Vaivasvata Manu, Manu’s son Ikshvaku, Ikskhvakku’s son Kakutstha, Kakutstha’s so"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-2"><data key="title">Harivamsha and Mahabharat</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 7, "quote": "10.2 Harivamsha and Mahabharat"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-2-1"><data key="title">The Harivamsha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">As you have already been told, Brahma emerged from Vishnu’s navel. Brahma’s son was Atri, Atri’s son Soma, Soma’s son Pururava, Pururava’s son Ayu, Ayu’s son Nahusha and Nahushja’s son Yayati. Yayati had two wives, Devayani and Sharmishtha. Devayani had two sons, Yadu and Turvusu. And Sharmishtha had three sons,…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 7, "quote": "10.2.1 The Harivamsha As you have already been told, Brahma emerged from Vishnu’s navel. Brahma’s son was Atri, Atri’s son Soma, Soma’s son Pururava, Pururava’s son Ayu, Ayu’s son Nahusha and Nahushja’s son Yayat"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-2-2"><data key="title">The Mahabharat</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The Pandavas were merely a pretext. Krishna used the Pandavas to rid the world of evil men. You have already learnt that one of Yayati’s sons was Puru. In Puru’s line were born Bharata and Kuru. One of Kuru’s descendants was the king Shantanu. Shantanu married Ganga and Bhishma was born from this marriage. But…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 9, "quote": "10.2.2 The Mahabharat The Pandavas were merely a pretext. Krishna used the Pandavas to rid the world of evil men. You have already learnt that one of Yayati’s sons was Puru. In Puru’s line were born Bharata and K"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-3"><data key="title">Buddha/Kalki and Creation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 11, "quote": "10.3 Buddha/Kalki and Creation"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-3-1"><data key="title">Buddha and Kalki</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The ninth avatara of Vishnu was Buddha and the tenth will be Kalki. Many years ago, there was a war between the devas and the asuras in which the demons managed to defeat the gods. The gods went running to Vishnu for protection and Vishnu told them that Mayamoha would be born as Buddha, the son of Shuddhodana. Such…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 11, "quote": "10.3.1 Buddha and Kalki The ninth avatara of Vishnu was Buddha and the tenth will be Kalki. Many years ago, there was a war between the devas and the asuras in which the demons managed to defeat the gods. The gods"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-3-2"><data key="title">Creation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Agni next told Vashishtha the history of creation. Vishnu is the Lord of creation, preservation and destruction. Before creation, it was only the brahman that was everywhere. There was no day, night or sky. First Vishnu created the waters. And in the waters he sowed the seeds of brahmanda. the great egg. From this…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 11, "quote": "10.3.2 Creation Agni next told Vashishtha the history of creation. Vishnu is the Lord of creation, preservation and destruction. Before creation, it was only the brahman that was everywhere. There was no da"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-4"><data key="title">Temples, Holy Places and Astrology</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 12, "quote": "10.4 Temples, Holy Places and Astrology"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-4-1"><data key="title">How to Pray, How to Build Temples and Deities</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The Agni purana next has several chapters on how to pray and on how to build temples and idols. The Techniques of praying to Vishnu , Shiva, Surya and the other gods and goddesses are described, including the special mantras (incantations)that must be used so as to please specific gods and there are prescribed forms…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 12, "quote": "10.4.1 How to Pray, How to Build Temples and Deities The Agni purana next has several chapters on how to pray and on how to build temples and idols. The Techniques of praying to Vishnu , Shiva, Surya and the other gods and goddesses are descri"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-4-2"><data key="title">Places of Pilgrimage</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A visit to a place of pilgrimage (tirtha) brings the same punya that is obtained from performing a yajna. It is because people had not gone on pilgrimages or donated gold and cows in their earlier lives that they were born poor in their next lives. The best place of pilgrimage is Pushkara, Brahma, other gods and sages…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 13, "quote": "10.4.2 Places of Pilgrimage A visit to a place of pilgrimage (tirtha) brings the same punya that is obtained from performing a yajna. It is because people had not gone on pilgrimages or donated gold and cows in their e"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-4-3"><data key="title">Geography</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The world is divided into seven regions (dvipas). Their names are Jambu, Plaksha, Shalmali, Kusha, Krouncha, Shaka, and Pushkara. The seven dvipas are surrounded by seven oceans and the names of these oceans are Lavana, Ikshu, Sura, Sarpih, Dadhi, Dugdha and Jala. Right in the centre of Jambudvipa is Mount Meru.…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 14, "quote": "10.4.3 Geography The world is divided into seven regions (dvipas). Their names are Jambu, Plaksha, Shalmali, Kusha, Krouncha, Shaka, and Pushkara. The seven dvipas are surrounded by seven oceans and the name"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-4-4"><data key="title">Astrology</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The Agni Purana next gives a lot of information on astrology. It states when marriages should take place and when they should not. For example, marriages are never to be held in the months of Chaitra and Pousha or under the signs of Libra or Gemini. If one is going on a trip, then Friday is the best day to start on.…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 15, "quote": "10.4.4 Astrology The Agni Purana next gives a lot of information on astrology. It states when marriages should take place and when they should not. For example, marriages are never to be held in the months o"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-5"><data key="title">Manvataras, Varnashrama and Vratas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 15, "quote": "10.5 Manvataras, Varnashrama and Vratas"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-5-1"><data key="title">Manvataras</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Each manvantara (era) is rule over by a Manu. The first Manu was Svayambhuva. Shatakratu held the title of Indra during this manvantara. The second Manu was Svarochisha. Vipashchita held the title of Indra during this manvantara. Third Manu was Uttama and Sushanti was Indra then. The fourth Manu was tapasa and Shikhi…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 15, "quote": "10.5.1 Manvataras Each manvantara (era) is rule over by a Manu. The first Manu was Svayambhuva. Shatakratu held the title of Indra during this manvantara. The second Manu was Svarochisha. Vipashchita held the"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-5-2"><data key="title">Varnashrama Dharma</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">All the Manus practised the precepts of dharma (righteousness). This meant non-violence, truthfulness, piety, going on pilgrimages donating alms, serving devas and brahmanas, tolerance of all religions and the following of the sacred texts. It also meant the practice of the system of the four classes (varna) and the…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 16, "quote": "10.5.2 Varnashrama Dharma All the Manus practised the precepts of dharma (righteousness). This meant non-violence, truthfulness, piety, going on pilgrimages donating alms, serving devas and brahmanas, tolerance of al"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-5-3"><data key="title">Sins and their Attonement</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">If one commits a sin, one has to atone for it. This is known as prayashchitta. If one does not atone for the sins that one has committed, it is the king’s duty to punish the sinner. If one drinks from a well where the dead body of an animal has been floating, one has to fast for three days. The worst possible sins are…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 17, "quote": "10.5.3 Sins and their Attonement If one commits a sin, one has to atone for it. This is known as prayashchitta. If one does not atone for the sins that one has committed, it is the king’s duty to punish the sinner. If one d"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-5-4"><data key="title">Vratas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Depending on the tithi (lunar day), the day of the week, the nakshatras (stars), the month, the season and the position of the sun, certain specific religious rites and ceremonies have to be performed. These are known as vratas. The first day of the lunar fortnight is known as pratipada. The day of pratipada in the…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 17, "quote": "10.5.4 Vratas Depending on the tithi (lunar day), the day of the week, the nakshatras (stars), the month, the season and the position of the sun, certain specific religious rites and ceremonies have to be"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-6"><data key="title">Hellish Planets, Charity and Gayatri</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 19, "quote": "10.6 Hellish Planets, Charity and Gayatri"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-6-1"><data key="title">Hellish Planets – Narakas (Hells)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">If one worships Vishnu with flowers, one never goes to hell. There are several such hells. Although people do not wish to die, they are bound to die once their predestined time span on earth has been exhausted. One then has to pay for whatever sins one might have committed. The sinners suffer and those who have…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 19, "quote": "10.6.1 Hellish Planets – Narakas (Hells) If one worships Vishnu with flowers, one never goes to hell. There are several such hells. Although people do not wish to die, they are bound to die once their predestined time span on earth"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-6-2"><data key="title">Giving Charity</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Giving alms is extremely important as means for achieving punya. Alms always have to be donated when one goes to visit a temple or a place of pilgrimage. The giver must always face the east and the receiver must always face the north when alms are being given. Such donations have to be made after one has had a bath.…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 19, "quote": "10.6.2 Giving Charity Giving alms is extremely important as means for achieving punya. Alms always have to be donated when one goes to visit a temple or a place of pilgrimage. The giver must always face the east"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-6-3"><data key="title">Gayatri Mantra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Gayatri mantra is a very powerful incantation. The human body has many veins. Out of these, ten veins are important and their names are Ida, Pingala, Sushumna, Gandhari, Hastijihva, Pritha, Yasha, Alambusha, Huha and Shankhini. These veins bear the breath of life. The breath of life is called prana vayu. Apart from…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 20, "quote": "10.6.3 Gayatri Mantra Gayatri mantra is a very powerful incantation. The human body has many veins. Out of these, ten veins are important and their names are Ida, Pingala, Sushumna, Gandhari, Hastijihva, Pritha,"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-6-4"><data key="title">The King</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The king’s duties are many. He has to punish his enemies, ensure the prosperity of his subjects and arrange that his kingdom is ruled well. He has to protect the sages who perform tapasya inside the boundaries of his kingdom. A king should appoint a wise brahmana as his priest. His ministers should also be wise and…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 20, "quote": "10.6.4 The King The king’s duties are many. He has to punish his enemies, ensure the prosperity of his subjects and arrange that his kingdom is ruled well. He has to protect the sages who perform tapasya in"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-7"><data key="title">Dreams, Omens and Sri Rama</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 22, "quote": "10.7 Dreams, Omens and Sri Rama"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-7-1"><data key="title">Dreams</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Some dreams are bad omens. In fact, they are nightmares. Examples are: dreams about grass or trees growing on one’s body, dreams in which the dreamer is shaven-headed or is wearing shabby clothes or dreams in which one is falling form above. It is also bad to dream of marriages, singing, the killing of snakes and the…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 22, "quote": "10.7.1 Dreams Some dreams are bad omens. In fact, they are nightmares. Examples are: dreams about grass or trees growing on one’s body, dreams in which the dreamer is shaven-headed or is wearing shabby cl"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-7-2"><data key="title">Omens and Signs</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">If one is about to go out of the house, one should take care of any bad omens that there might be. Such bad omens are cotton, dried grass, cowdung, coal, molasses, leather, hair, a lunatic, a chandala, a widow, a dead body, ashes, bones and a broken vessel. If one comes across these as one is about to leave, one…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 23, "quote": "10.7.2 Omens and Signs If one is about to go out of the house, one should take care of any bad omens that there might be. Such bad omens are cotton, dried grass, cowdung, coal, molasses, leather, hair, a lunatic,"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-7-3"><data key="title">Battle</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once a king decides to go out to battle, seven days are needed for preliminaries. On the first day, Vishnu, Shiva and Ganesha have to be worshipped. On the second day the dikpalas (guardians of all the directions) are worshipped, the Rudras on the third day, the planets and the stars on the fourth day and the two…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 24, "quote": "10.7.3 Battle Once a king decides to go out to battle, seven days are needed for preliminaries. On the first day, Vishnu, Shiva and Ganesha have to be worshipped. On the second day the dikpalas (guardians"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-7-4"><data key="title">Sri Rama’s Teachings</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Rama had once taught Lakshmana about the duties of a king. The Agni Purana now relates these precepts of Rama’s. The duties of king are fourfold. Firstly, he has to earn wealth. Secondly, he has to increase it. Thirdly, he has to protect it. And fourthly and finally, he has to donate it. The king must also be polite…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 24, "quote": "10.7.4 Sri Rama’s Teachings Rama had once taught Lakshmana about the duties of a king. The Agni Purana now relates these precepts of Rama’s. The duties of king are fourfold. Firstly, he has to earn wealth. Secondly, he"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-8"><data key="title">Dhanurveda, Dynasties and Literature</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 25, "quote": "10.8 Dhanurveda, Dynasties and Literature"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-8-1"><data key="title">Dhanurveda</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The section on Dhanurveda is on arms and weapons. There are five types of weapons that are used in war. The first category is that of yantramukta weapons, released from a machine (yantra). This machine may be a launcher or even a bow. The second category is that of panimukta weapons, weapons that are flung by the hand…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 25, "quote": "10.8.1 Dhanurveda The section on Dhanurveda is on arms and weapons. There are five types of weapons that are used in war. The first category is that of yantramukta weapons, released from a machine (yantra). T"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-8-2"><data key="title">Property</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">What happens to a person’s debts when he dies? If he does not have any sons, the person who inherits the property also inherits the debts and had to pay them off. If there is a son, the son pays the debts off. But a woman is not to be held responsible for debts contracted by her husband or her son. Nor is a man…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 26, "quote": "10.8.2 Property What happens to a person’s debts when he dies? If he does not have any sons, the person who inherits the property also inherits the debts and had to pay them off. If there is a son, the son"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-8-3"><data key="title">Donating the Puranas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The Agni Purana now describes the benefits of giving alms along with the purans. The puranas are to be donated together with cows. And in talking of the mahapuranas, the Agni Purana also mentions most of their length, in terms of the number of shlokas (couplets) that each has. This is worth stating. The Brahma Purana…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 26, "quote": "10.8.3 Donating the Puranas The Agni Purana now describes the benefits of giving alms along with the purans. The puranas are to be donated together with cows. And in talking of the mahapuranas, the Agni Purana also men"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-8-4"><data key="title">Vamsha (Dynasties)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Brahma was born from Vishnu’s navel. Brahma’s son was Marichi, Marichi’s son Kashyapa and Kakshyapa’s son Vivasvana. From this line was descended Pururava and Pururava’s descendants were the kings of the surya (solar) dynasty. Brahma also had a son named Atri and Atri had a son named Soma. Soma performed a rajasuya…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 27, "quote": "10.8.4 Vamsha (Dynasties) Brahma was born from Vishnu’s navel. Brahma’s son was Marichi, Marichi’s son Kashyapa and Kakshyapa’s son Vivasvana. From this line was descended Pururava and Pururava’s descendants were the"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-8-5"><data key="title">Medicine</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Dhanvantari was the physician of the gods and he taught Sushruta the art of ayurveda (medicine). The Agni Purana now describes what the sage Ssushruta had learnt, that is, the treatment for various diseases. This does not simply mean the treatment of human illnesses. There is a section known as vriksha ayurveda, which…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 28, "quote": "10.8.5 Medicine Dhanvantari was the physician of the gods and he taught Sushruta the art of ayurveda (medicine). The Agni Purana now describes what the sage Ssushruta had learnt, that is, the treatment for"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-8-6"><data key="title">Literature and Grammar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Thereafter, the Agni Purana has many chapters on literature and grammar. It describes the different types of chhanda (metres) that are used in poetry. Next it discusses the alphabet. There are sixty-four letters (varna) in the alphabet, of which twenty-one are vowels (svara varna). There are three tones (svara) in…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 28, "quote": "10.8.6 Literature and Grammar Thereafter, the Agni Purana has many chapters on literature and grammar. It describes the different types of chhanda (metres) that are used in poetry. Next it discusses the alphabet. There a"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-9"><data key="title">Destruction, Pralaya and Yama</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 29, "quote": "10.9 Destruction, Pralaya and Yama"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-9-1"><data key="title">Pralaya (Destruction)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Periodically pralayas (destructions) take place. A destruction comes at the end of four thousand yugas on earth. For a hundred years there are no rains and there is widespread drought. Thereafter, Vishnu uses the rays of the sun to drink and dry up all the waters that there are on earth. Seven different suns appear in…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 30, "quote": "10.9.1 Pralaya (Destruction) Periodically pralayas (destructions) take place. A destruction comes at the end of four thousand yugas on earth. For a hundred years there are no rains and there is widespread drought. There"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-9-2"><data key="title">Yama and Hell</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When human beings die, their physical bodies are given up. But they acquire new bodies that are known as ativahika bodies. In these bodies, they are brought to Yama’s abode by Yama’s servants. Living beings other than human are not brought to Yama. Yama then decides whether the dead person should go to heaven or to…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 30, "quote": "10.9.2 Yama and Hell When human beings die, their physical bodies are given up. But they acquire new bodies that are known as ativahika bodies. In these bodies, they are brought to Yama’s abode by Yama’s servant"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-9-3"><data key="title">Yoga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Yoga is the way to circumvent the miseries of life. True knowledge is that which informs one about the true nature of brahman or paramatman. The atman or jivatman is that which characterises an individual. Yoga means union, it is the union of the jivatman with the paramatman. Yoga concentrates one’s mind on the…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 30, "quote": "10.9.3 Yoga Yoga is the way to circumvent the miseries of life. True knowledge is that which informs one about the true nature of brahman or paramatman. The atman or jivatman is that which characterises"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-9-4"><data key="title">The Knowledge of Brahman</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The passage distinguishes atman from body, senses and mind, then identifies the individual self with Brahman. It offers a direct philosophical bridge from a Purana to Upanishadic inquiry.</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 31, "quote": "10.9.4 The Knowledge of Brahman Brahma jnana is the knowledge of brahman. This knowledge, which gives the ultimate bliss, is nothing but the sense that the individual atman is identical with the universal brahman or paramt"}]</data></node><node id="p-agni-puranas-10-9-5"><data key="title">The Gita</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Krishna had taught Arjuna the lessons of the Gita on the plains of Kurukshetra. The Agni Purana now relates the essence of the Gita . If physical body is alive, that is no reason for rejoicing. Just as, if the physical body is dead, that is no reason for mourning. The atman does not die. It does not decay, it cannot…</data><data key="doc">agni-puranas</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 34, "quote": "10.9.5 The Gita Krishna had taught Arjuna the lessons of the Gita on the plains of Kurukshetra. The Agni Purana now relates the essence of the Gita . If physical body is alive, that is no reason for rejoici"}]</data></node><node id="p-bhavisya-puranas-11-1"><data key="title">Biblical and Modern History</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This supplied section asserts: [From the Pratisarga Parva, Chapters Four to Seven.] Suta Goswami said: Once upon a time in Hastinapura, Pradyota the son of Kshemaka was leading an assembly and meanwhile the great sage Narada arrived there. King Pradyota happily honored him. Having him seated on the seat the sage told king Pradyota, "Your father was…</data><data key="doc">bhavisya-puranas</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "bhavisya-puranas", "page": 1, "quote": "11.1 Biblical and Modern History [From the Pratisarga Parva, Chapters Four to Seven.] Suta Goswami said: Once upon a time in Hastinapura, Pradyota the son of Kshemaka was leading an assembly and meanwhile the great sage Nar"}]</data></node><node id="p-bhavisya-puranas-11-2"><data key="title">The Prediction of Islam</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This supplied section asserts: [From the third part of the Pratisarga Parva.] Shri Suta Gosvami said: In the dynasty of king Shalivahana, there were ten kings who went to the heavenly planets after ruling for over 500 years. Then gradually the morality declined on the earth. At that time Bhojaraja was the tenth of the kings on the earth. When he…</data><data key="doc">bhavisya-puranas</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "bhavisya-puranas", "page": 5, "quote": "11.2 The Prediction of Islam [From the third part of the Pratisarga Parva.] Shri Suta Gosvami said: In the dynasty of king Shalivahana, there were ten kings who went to the heavenly planets after ruling for over 500 yea"}]</data></node><node id="p-bhavisya-puranas-11-3"><data key="title">The Prediction of Buddhism</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This supplied section asserts: [From the 29th chapter of the Pratisarga Parva.] Long ago in the country known as Citrartha, the inhabitants of the heavenly planets used to come to play during the time of autumn. One day a heavenly apsara know as Manjughosha came to that place where the sage Shuka was residing. Seeing this beautiful boy, she tried…</data><data key="doc">bhavisya-puranas</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "bhavisya-puranas", "page": 6, "quote": "11.3 The Prediction of Buddhism [From the 29th chapter of the Pratisarga Parva.] Long ago in the country known as Citrartha, the inhabitants of the heavenly planets used to come to play during the time of autumn. One day a"}]</data></node><node id="p-bhavisya-puranas-11-4"><data key="title">The Prediction of India’s Kings</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This supplied section asserts: [From the 31st chapter of the Pratisarga Parva.] Suta Goswami said: The great powerful king of Madrakesa worshipped the mendicants of the heavenly planets (the Asvini kumaras) for five years and after having received a benediction from them he produced ten sons and one beautiful young daughter named Kantinati. King…</data><data key="doc">bhavisya-puranas</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "bhavisya-puranas", "page": 8, "quote": "11.4 The Prediction of India’s Kings [From the 31st chapter of the Pratisarga Parva.] Suta Goswami said: The great powerful king of Madrakesa worshipped the mendicants of the heavenly planets (the Asvini kumaras) for five years"}]</data></node><node id="p-bhavisya-puranas-11-5"><data key="title">The Prediction of Nimbarka Acharya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This supplied section asserts: [From the 7th chapter of the Pratisarga Parva.] The sun god, being pleased by the praise of the demigods, said: By the order of the Lord Krishna, Sudarshana will take birth in the Dvapara-yuga, and will be known as Nimbaditya (Nimbarka). He will be able to remove the declination of religious principles. Suta Goswami…</data><data key="doc">bhavisya-puranas</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "bhavisya-puranas", "page": 9, "quote": "11.5 The Prediction of Nimbarka Acharya [From the 7th chapter of the Pratisarga Parva.] The sun god, being pleased by the praise of the demigods, said: By the order of the Lord Krishna, Sudarshana will take birth in the Dvapara-yu"}]</data></node><node id="p-bhavisya-puranas-11-6"><data key="title">The Prediction of Madhva and Sridhara</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This supplied section asserts: [From the 7th chapter of the Pratisarga Parva.] Brihaspati said: Long ago, during the age of treta, O Indra, there was a brahmana named Shakrasharma, in Ayodhya. He worshipped demigods such as the Asvini Kumaras, Rudras, Vasus, and Surya with the mantras mentioned in the Yajur-veda. After worshipping, he satisfied…</data><data key="doc">bhavisya-puranas</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "bhavisya-puranas", "page": 10, "quote": "11.6 The Prediction of Madhva and Sridhara [From the 7th chapter of the Pratisarga Parva.] Brihaspati said: Long ago, during the age of treta, O Indra, there was a brahmana named Shakrasharma, in Ayodhya. He worshipped demigods such"}]</data></node><node id="p-bhavisya-puranas-11-7"><data key="title">The Prediction of Jayadeva Gosvami</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This supplied section asserts: Brihaspati said: Long ago there was a brahmana named Heli, in the beautiful city Pampapura. He was a worshipper of the sun-god and he knew the 64 arts. He avoided the mood of accepting dakshina (donations) and he became an artist, having established a workshop for clothes, paintings, pictures, metal statues etc. He…</data><data key="doc">bhavisya-puranas</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "bhavisya-puranas", "page": 11, "quote": "11.7 The Prediction of Jayadeva Gosvami Brihaspati said: Long ago there was a brahmana named Heli, in the beautiful city Pampapura. He was a worshipper of the sun-god and he knew the 64 arts. He avoided the mood of accepting daksh"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-1"><data key="title">Lord Brahma</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 9 sections:</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "1.1 Lord Brahma This chapter contains 9 sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-1-1"><data key="title">A Profile of Brahmaji</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "1.1.1 A Profile of Brahmaji"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-upper-1-140"><data key="title">Appearance Of Brahmaji</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">According to the scriptures, Brahmaji was born on the lotus that had arisen from the navel of eternal ommipresent Lord Narayan Vishnu. During the period of deluge (Pralaya) all the creations was destroyed and the creatures that had gone into Maharloka (the fourth of the upper worlds according to the Puranas), take…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "APPEARANCE OF BRAHMAJI According to the scriptures, Brahmaji was born on the lotus that had arisen from the navel of eternal ommipresent Lord Narayan Vishnu. During the period of deluge (Pralaya) all the creations"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-1-2"><data key="title">Scriptural Appearance of Brahmaji</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Scriptures describe the appearance of Brahmaji thus: CHATURMUKHD VEDADHARAH SAKSHASUTRA KAMANDALUH | HANSARUDHO RAKTAVASA BRAHMALOKA PITAMAH || Meaning: He has four heads, holds Vedas, Rosary, Yagyopavita (Sacred thread) and Kamandalu (bowl); wears red cothes and rides a swan. He is none other than Brahmaji,…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "1.1.2 Scriptural Appearance of Brahmaji Scriptures describe the appearance of Brahmaji thus: CHATURMUKHD VEDADHARAH SAKSHASUTRA KAMANDALUH | HANSARUDHO RAKTAVASA BRAHMALOKA PITAMAH || Meaning: He has four heads, holds Vedas, Rosar"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-1-3"><data key="title">Four Heads of Brahmaji</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Brahmaji is shown with four heads, Besides being the creator of the universe, Brahmaji is a deponent of the deeds of all the creatures as well, alloting the creatures various incarnations as per their deed is duty of Brahmaji. Hence the full knowledge of a creatures deed is an indispensable precondition before…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "1.1.3 Four Heads of Brahmaji Brahmaji is shown with four heads, Besides being the creator of the universe, Brahmaji is a deponent of the deeds of all the creatures as well, alloting the creatures various incarnations as"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-1-4"><data key="title">White Beard of Brahmaji</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Brahmaji is the great grandfather of all of us. Hence his appearance must match his status. Since antiquity, in a single day of Brahmaji no one knows how many members of creatures take birth and die. The period since antiquity can be calculated according to astrology. Since Brahmaji has such an eternal existance, his…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "1.1.4 White Beard of Brahmaji Brahmaji is the great grandfather of all of us. Hence his appearance must match his status. Since antiquity, in a single day of Brahmaji no one knows how many members of creatures take birth"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-1-5"><data key="title">Swan as a Vehicle</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Swan is a white bird. God has bestowed it with unique virtues. Virtues that are not to be found in any creature of the eighty-four lakh incarnations, their virtue is NEERA-KSHEERA VIVEKA, that is, if milk is mixed with water, a swan is capable of separating milk from water and taking it rejecting the water. It is an…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "1.1.5 Swan as a Vehicle Swan is a white bird. God has bestowed it with unique virtues. Virtues that are not to be found in any creature of the eighty-four lakh incarnations, their virtue is NEERA-KSHEERA VIVEKA, th"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-1-6"><data key="title">Purpose of Holding Vedas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Brahmaji is also shown as holding Vedas is one of his hands why? It is natural to be curious about it. Vedas are undoubtedly the most ancient scriptures in the word. Even the great deluge (Pralaya) could not destroy Vedas for almighty Brahma himself had taken incarnation to save Vedas during deluge. Hence Vedas have…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "1.1.6 Purpose of Holding Vedas Brahmaji is also shown as holding Vedas is one of his hands why? It is natural to be curious about it. Vedas are undoubtedly the most ancient scriptures in the word. Even the great deluge (P"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-1-7"><data key="title">Rosary in Second Hand</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The great grandfather of all the creatures Lord Brahma teaches by his appearance that the human incarnation is not just for the mundane jobs. Instead among the mundane jobs, human being must keep on remembering the almighty Paramatma. Because without reciting the name of almighty God welfare of any human being is…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "1.1.7 Rosary in Second Hand The great grandfather of all the creatures Lord Brahma teaches by his appearance that the human incarnation is not just for the mundane jobs. Instead among the mundane jobs, human being must"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-1-8"><data key="title">Kamandalu (Coconut Shell Bowl)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">JALAMEV JEEVANAM That is water is life. Water is the basic physical element that caused the whole creation to exist. The coconut shell bowl (Kamandalu) held by Brahmaji; also holds water, thus depicting the hassle free existence and continuence of the whole creation.</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "1.1.8 Kamandalu (Coconut Shell Bowl) JALAMEV JEEVANAM That is water is life. Water is the basic physical element that caused the whole creation to exist. The coconut shell bowl (Kamandalu) held by Brahmaji; also holds water, th"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-1-9"><data key="title">Seat of the Lotus</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lotus is the seat for Lord Brahma Lotus also adorns the hand of Lord Vishnu and is an essential item to worship Lord Shiva. Every one knows that the lotus grows from the muck. Thus, it teaches that inspite of being born of the muck be as pure as possible. Including humans, all creatures are born out of the muck called…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "1.1.9 Seat of the Lotus Lotus is the seat for Lord Brahma Lotus also adorns the hand of Lord Vishnu and is an essential item to worship Lord Shiva. Every one knows that the lotus grows from the muck. Thus, it teach"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-2"><data key="title">Goddess Sarasvati</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 3 sections:</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "1.2 Goddess Sarasvati This chapter contains 3 sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-2-1"><data key="title">Appearance of Sarasvati</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Literally, Saraswati means SARAH - Knowledge, STHANAMYASYAH ASTITI. Thus, Saraswati is the goddess who rules all kinds of knowledge without worshipping her no one can be a learned person. Hence followers of other religious also worship Saraswati, though the name and appearance may differ slightly. Appearance of…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "1.2.1 Appearance of Sarasvati Literally, Saraswati means SARAH - Knowledge, STHANAMYASYAH ASTITI. Thus, Saraswati is the goddess who rules all kinds of knowledge without worshipping her no one can be a learned person. He"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-2-2"><data key="title">Significance of Book and Veena (Lyre)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Noted sage Bhartrihari writes that without literature and music, a man is not more than an animal. Hence the book and veena in the hands of Saraswati teach that if the humans wish to do away with their bruteness, knowledge of literature and music is must for them. Hence, Goddess Saraswati is shown with a book and…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "1.2.2 Significance of Book and Veena (Lyre) Noted sage Bhartrihari writes that without literature and music, a man is not more than an animal. Hence the book and veena in the hands of Saraswati teach that if the humans wish to do away"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-2-3"><data key="title">Beaded Rosary</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The ultimate goal of knowledge is salvation. Salvation is possible only with unification with God. And rosary is a means for unification with God. But why a rosary of white beads only? It is so because white beads depict spotless purity. Thus it teaches us to rise above the blemishes of passion, anger, greed, ego,…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "1.2.3 Beaded Rosary The ultimate goal of knowledge is salvation. Salvation is possible only with unification with God. And rosary is a means for unification with God. But why a rosary of white beads only? It is"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-3"><data key="title">Lord Vishnu</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 11 sections:</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "1.3 Lord Vishnu This chapter contains 11 sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-3-1"><data key="title">Appearance of Lord Vishnu</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Worship of Lord Vishnu is extremely essential for those who wish to receive the supreme salvation. A common rule of worship is that the worshipper must essentially have the virtues of the worshipped. Hence, Lord Vishnu gives his virtues through physical appearance.</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "1.3.1 Appearance of Lord Vishnu Worship of Lord Vishnu is extremely essential for those who wish to receive the supreme salvation. A common rule of worship is that the worshipper must essentially have the virtues of the wo"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-3-2"><data key="title">Reflection of Scriptural Appearance</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">SHANTAKARAM BHUJANGASHAYANAM PADMANABHAM SURESHAM, VISHWADHARAM GAGAN SADRISHAM MEGHAVARNAM SHUBHANGAM| LAKSHMIKANTAN KAMALNAYANAM YOGIMIRDHYANA GAMYAM, VANDE VISHNUM BHAVA BHAYAHARAM SARVA LOKAIKNATHAM || Meaning: I worship that Lord Vishnu who has a peaceful body (postures); who rests on Sheshanaga (the mighty…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "1.3.2 Reflection of Scriptural Appearance SHANTAKARAM BHUJANGASHAYANAM PADMANABHAM SURESHAM, VISHWADHARAM GAGAN SADRISHAM MEGHAVARNAM SHUBHANGAM| LAKSHMIKANTAN KAMALNAYANAM YOGIMIRDHYANA GAMYAM, VANDE VISHNUM BHAVA BHAYAHARAM SARVA"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-3-3"><data key="title">Scientific Depiction of Vishnu’s Appearance</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">It is a common principle the worshipper should be a Deva (God) first and worship the God then, Let us reflect the appearance of Lord Vishnu in light of this principle. The first virtue of Lord Vishnu is his peaceful appearance. Hence his worshipper must also acquire peaceful appearance. This is also the need of the…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "1.3.3 Scientific Depiction of Vishnu’s Appearance It is a common principle the worshipper should be a Deva (God) first and worship the God then, Let us reflect the appearance of Lord Vishnu in light of this principle. The first virtue of Lo"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-3-4"><data key="title">Why does Lord Vishnu rest on Sheshanaga?</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The second virtue (resting on the Sheshanaga) must be considered as the sub-virtue of the peaceful appearance. Scriptures describe Sheshanaga as Kala also. Sheshanaga is supposed to be a highly poisonous snake. But Lord Vishnu is resting on him without fear or worries. It teaches us not to lose patience even while…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "1.3.4 Why does Lord Vishnu rest on Sheshanaga? The second virtue (resting on the Sheshanaga) must be considered as the sub-virtue of the peaceful appearance. Scriptures describe Sheshanaga as Kala also. Sheshanaga is supposed to be a hig"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-3-5"><data key="title">Why is Lotus in the Navel?</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The third virtue of Lord Vishnu is that he bears a lotus on his navel,hence he is also known as Padmanabham. From the lotus rises Lord Brahma. Brahma has only royal virtues (Rajoguna). Thus to remain in peaceful form Lord Vishnu has discarded all the royal virtues through his navel. Similarily he has made the snake,…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "1.3.5 Why is Lotus in the Navel? The third virtue of Lord Vishnu is that he bears a lotus on his navel,hence he is also known as Padmanabham. From the lotus rises Lord Brahma. Brahma has only royal virtues (Rajoguna). Thus"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-3-6"><data key="title">Why does Vishnu stay in the Sea of Milk?</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Before attempting to solve the problems, let us first give a thought to the oceans also. All the oceans, as we know are huge bodies of Saline water. How is then the sea of milk? A couplet of Chanakyaneeti explains it thus : KA CHINTA MAM JIVANE YADI HARIIVISHVAMMBHARO GEEYATE, NO CHEDARBHAKA JIVANAY JANANISTANYAM…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "1.3.6 Why does Vishnu stay in the Sea of Milk? Before attempting to solve the problems, let us first give a thought to the oceans also. All the oceans, as we know are huge bodies of Saline water. How is then the sea of milk? A couplet of"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-3-7"><data key="title">Reason for holding the Wheel</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The famous Sudarshana Chakra (wheel) is whirling on the finger of Lord Vishnu. Through this wheel, God teaches the human kind that the cycle of the universe is whirling at his will. If anybody tries to go against his will (i.e. wheel) or gives up religion and begins to behave irreligiously, I will whirl him like the…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "1.3.7 Reason for holding the Wheel The famous Sudarshana Chakra (wheel) is whirling on the finger of Lord Vishnu. Through this wheel, God teaches the human kind that the cycle of the universe is whirling at his will. If anybo"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-3-8"><data key="title">Reason for holding the Conch</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">White colour and sound are the main virtues of the conch. Through the colour of the conch, God Vishnu teaches us to follow the path of the truth. The sound is like a warning call that is if path of truth is not followed, the violater will be challenged and punished. Thus. Either by fear of by any other thing, ome must…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "1.3.8 Reason for holding the Conch White colour and sound are the main virtues of the conch. Through the colour of the conch, God Vishnu teaches us to follow the path of the truth. The sound is like a warning call that is if"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-3-9"><data key="title">Purpose of Kaustubhamani</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Kaustubha is the best jewel among all the gems. One who bears it, must be gentle, knowledgable, virtuous etc. Thus, bearing Kaustubha Mani, God preaches the common people that if they wish to develop all these virtues in them, they will have to move towards this gem once advanced, they will feel the eternal light in…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "1.3.9 Purpose of Kaustubhamani Kaustubha is the best jewel among all the gems. One who bears it, must be gentle, knowledgable, virtuous etc. Thus, bearing Kaustubha Mani, God preaches the common people that if they wish t"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-3-10"><data key="title">Why does Lord Vishnu hold a Mace?</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Mace is a weapon that shows and dares for the physical strength. It was used by the fighters to demonstrate their strength. Among all the ancient weapons, mace was the main. It application is profusely seen in the epics like Ramayana, Mahabharata by physically powerful and strong characters like Hanumana &amp; Bheema.…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "1.3.10 Why does Lord Vishnu hold a Mace? Mace is a weapon that shows and dares for the physical strength. It was used by the fighters to demonstrate their strength. Among all the ancient weapons, mace was the main. It application i"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-3-11"><data key="title">Why is Garuda (Eagle) the Vehicle of Vishnu?</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Garuda (eagle) is said to be the king of the birds. It is the fearest of bird. Thus God is assuring his devotees to be fearless, because if any calamity befell them, he will reach them in no time.</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "1.3.11 Why is Garuda (Eagle) the Vehicle of Vishnu? Garuda (eagle) is said to be the king of the birds. It is the fearest of bird. Thus God is assuring his devotees to be fearless, because if any calamity befell them, he will reach them in no"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-4"><data key="title">Goddess Laxmi</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 3 sections:</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "1.4 Goddess Laxmi This chapter contains 3 sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-4-1"><data key="title">Why Goddess Laxmi presses the feet of God?</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Goddess Lakshmi presents an example of an ideal spouse before the world.The supreme duty of a woman is described in the service of her husband. Second aspect of the message is for the people who want to get rich, that if they want the wealth, they must develop a devotion for the feet of Narayana, for Lakshmi (Goddess…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "1.4.1 Why Goddess Laxmi presses the feet of God? Goddess Lakshmi presents an example of an ideal spouse before the world.The supreme duty of a woman is described in the service of her husband. Second aspect of the message is for the people"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-4-2"><data key="title">Reason for Liquidity of Laxmi</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The better half of Lord Vishnu can never be freakish, because Satoguna is the main virtue of Lord Vishnu. In the proxmity of God whose main virtue is Satoguna, no one can remain freakish. But the material wealth, that is Gold, and silver, have mobility as their main virtue. Thus the rich people who have these wealths…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "1.4.2 Reason for Liquidity of Laxmi The better half of Lord Vishnu can never be freakish, because Satoguna is the main virtue of Lord Vishnu. In the proxmity of God whose main virtue is Satoguna, no one can remain freakish. Bu"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-puran-1-4-3"><data key="title">Why is the owl the vehicle of Laxmi?</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Owl is the vehicle of goddess Lakshmi. An owl can't see in the day, Hence, Lakshmi visits the house of those devotees who only worship her, on her vehicle owl. And she visits the home of those devotees who worship Lord Vishnu first and then her, with Vishnu riding his vehicle Garuda. It also implies that at places…</data><data key="doc">brahma-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "1.4.3 Why is the owl the vehicle of Laxmi? Owl is the vehicle of goddess Lakshmi. An owl can't see in the day, Hence, Lakshmi visits the house of those devotees who only worship her, on her vehicle owl. And she visits the home of tho"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-1"><data key="title">Preliminaries</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There was a forest known as naimisharanya. The sages (maharshis) arranged for a sacrifice (yajna) in this forest and the ceremony went on for twelve years. Naimisharanya forest was a wonderful place to arrange sacrifices in. The climate was pleasant. There were trees full of climate was pleasant. There were trees full…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "9.1 Preliminaries There was a forest known as naimisharanya. The sages (maharshis) arranged for a sacrifice (yajna) in this forest and the ceremony went on for twelve years. Naimisharanya forest was a wonderf"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-2"><data key="title">More Creation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">To continue with the process of creation, Brahma gave birth to a man and a woman from his own body. The man was named Svayambhuva Manu and the woman was named Shatarupa. Humans are descended from Manu. That is the reason they are known as manava. Manu and Shatarupa had three sons named Vira. Priyavrata and Uttanapada.…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "9.2 More Creation To continue with the process of creation, Brahma gave birth to a man and a woman from his own body. The man was named Svayambhuva Manu and the woman was named Shatarupa. Humans are descended"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-3"><data key="title">Daksha’s Offspring</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Daksha's wife was named Asikli and Asikli gave birth to five thousand sons. They were known as the Haryashvas. The Haryashvas were destined to rule over the world. But the sage Narada went to the Haryashvas and said, "How can you rule over the world if you don't even know what the world looks like? Are you familiar…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "9.3 Daksha’s Offspring Daksha's wife was named Asikli and Asikli gave birth to five thousand sons. They were known as the Haryashvas. The Haryashvas were destined to rule over the world. But the sage Narada went t"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-4"><data key="title">Prithu</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">In Dhruva's line there was a king named Anga, Anga was religious and followed the righteous path. But unfortunately, Anga's son Vena inherited none of the good qualities of his father. Vena's mother was Sunitha and she happened to be the daughter of Mrityu. Mrityu was notorious for his evil ways and deeds. Vena spent…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "9.4 Prithu In Dhruva's line there was a king named Anga, Anga was religious and followed the righteous path. But unfortunately, Anga's son Vena inherited none of the good qualities of his father. Vena'"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-5"><data key="title">Manvataras</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A manvantara is an era. There are four smaller eras (yugas) and their names are satya or krita yuga, treta yuga, dvapara yuga and kali yuga. Each cycle of satya yuga, treat yuga, dvapara yuga and kali yuga is called a mahayuga. A mahayuga comprises of 12,000 years of the gods, or equivalently, 4320,000 years for…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 6, "quote": "9.5 Manvataras A manvantara is an era. There are four smaller eras (yugas) and their names are satya or krita yuga, treta yuga, dvapara yuga and kali yuga. Each cycle of satya yuga, treat yuga, dvapara yug"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-6"><data key="title">The Sun and the Solar Dynasty</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">You have probably forgotten by now that Kashyapa and Aditi had a son named Vivasvana. This was the sun god, also known as Surya or Martanda. Surya was married to Samjna, Vishvakarma's daughter. They had two sons. The fist son was Vaivasvata Manu and the second son was Yama or Shraddhadeva, the god of death. Yama had a…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 6, "quote": "9.6 The Sun and the Solar Dynasty You have probably forgotten by now that Kashyapa and Aditi had a son named Vivasvana. This was the sun god, also known as Surya or Martanda. Surya was married to Samjna, Vishvakarma's daught"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-7"><data key="title">Vaivasvata Manu’s Children</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Vaivasvata Manu has no children and he arranged for a sacrifice so that he might have a son. Nine sons were born as a result of this sacrifice. Their names were Ikshvaku, Nabhaga, Dhrishta, Sharyati, Narishyanta, Pramshu, Rishta, Karusha and Prishadhra. Manu also made an offering to the two gods Mitra and Varuna. As a…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 7, "quote": "9.7 Vaivasvata Manu’s Children Vaivasvata Manu has no children and he arranged for a sacrifice so that he might have a son. Nine sons were born as a result of this sacrifice. Their names were Ikshvaku, Nabhaga, Dhrishta,"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-8"><data key="title">Kubalashva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Kubalashva was one of the kings descended from Kakutstha. Kubalashva's father was named Vrihadashva. After Vrihadashva had ruled for many years, he desired to retire to the forest. He therefore prepared to hand over the kingdom to his son Kubalashva. But learning of King Vrihadashva's resolve, a sage named Utanka came…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 8, "quote": "9.8 Kubalashva Kubalashva was one of the kings descended from Kakutstha. Kubalashva's father was named Vrihadashva. After Vrihadashva had ruled for many years, he desired to retire to the forest. He theref"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-9"><data key="title">Trishanku</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">From Dridashva was descended a king named Trayaruni. Trayaruni was a righteous king and followed all the religious dictates. But Trayarun's son Satyavrata was quite the opposite and refused to follow the righteous path. King Trayaruni's chief priest was the great sage Vashishtha. Vashishtha advised the king that his…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 8, "quote": "9.9 Trishanku From Dridashva was descended a king named Trayaruni. Trayaruni was a righteous king and followed all the religious dictates. But Trayarun's son Satyavrata was quite the opposite and refused"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-10"><data key="title">Sagara</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Trishanku's son was Harishchandra and from Harishchandra was descended a king named Bahu. Bahu devoted too much time to pleasurable pursuits. The upshot of this was that the defence of the kingdom was not properly taken care of. Enemy kings seized this opportunity to attack Bahu's kingdom. They drove Bahu out and Bahu…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 9, "quote": "9.10 Sagara Trishanku's son was Harishchandra and from Harishchandra was descended a king named Bahu. Bahu devoted too much time to pleasurable pursuits. The upshot of this was that the defence of the k"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-11"><data key="title">The Moon and the Lunar Dynasty</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There was a sage named Atri. Atri performed very difficult tapasya. So difficult was the tapasya that Atri's energy was thrown up into the sky. The sky could not bear this energy and hurled it down onto the earth. This energy then gave birth to Soma or Chandra, the moon god. Brahma took Chandra up into his chariot and…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 10, "quote": "9.11 The Moon and the Lunar Dynasty There was a sage named Atri. Atri performed very difficult tapasya. So difficult was the tapasya that Atri's energy was thrown up into the sky. The sky could not bear this energy and hurled"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-12"><data key="title">Yayati</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">In the lunar dynasty, there was born a powerful king named Nahusha. He married Viraja and they had six sons named Yati, Yajati, Samyati, Ayati, Yati, and Suyati. Yati became a hermit. So although Yayati was not the eldest, he was crowned king after Nahusha. Yayati had two wives. The first was Devayani, daughter of…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 11, "quote": "9.12 Yayati In the lunar dynasty, there was born a powerful king named Nahusha. He married Viraja and they had six sons named Yati, Yajati, Samyati, Ayati, Yati, and Suyati. Yati became a hermit. So alt"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-13"><data key="title">Geography</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Having heard accounts of the solar and lunar dynasties, the sages requested Romaharshana. "Tell us a little about the geography of the world. What does the earth look like? What are its limits?" Romaharshana obliged. The earth is divided into seven regions (dvipas). Their names are Jambudvipa, Plakshadvipa,…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 12, "quote": "9.13 Geography Having heard accounts of the solar and lunar dynasties, the sages requested Romaharshana. \"Tell us a little about the geography of the world. What does the earth look like? What are its limi"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-14"><data key="title">Konaraka</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There is an ocean to the south of Bharatavarsha. One the shores of this great ocean there is a land named Ondra or Utkala (present Orissa). Utkala is populated by religious people and the brahmanas who live there are learned in the Vedas. They are very good priests, learned in the Puranas and the shastras and skilled…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 13, "quote": "9.14 Konaraka There is an ocean to the south of Bharatavarsha. One the shores of this great ocean there is a land named Ondra or Utkala (present Orissa). Utkala is populated by religious people and the br"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-15"><data key="title">Indradyumna and Purushottama Kshetra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">In satya yuga there was a king named Indradyumna. He was a very powerful king as Indra himself. He was handsome, honest and truthful, learned in the shastras and the Vedas, and skilled in the use of weapons. His radiance put the sun to shame. Indradyumna was devoted to Vishnu. He once decided that he would worship…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "9.15 Indradyumna and Purushottama Kshetra In satya yuga there was a king named Indradyumna. He was a very powerful king as Indra himself. He was handsome, honest and truthful, learned in the shastras and the Vedas, and skilled in th"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-16"><data key="title">Markandeya</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Many years ago, a great destruction (pralaya) took place. The earth was shrouded in darkness and nothing could be seen. There was neither sun nor moon. Lightning and thunder crushed mountains and trees. There were showers of meteors. Lakes and rivers dried up. The entire earth burnt with fire and the flames of the…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 16, "quote": "9.16 Markandeya Many years ago, a great destruction (pralaya) took place. The earth was shrouded in darkness and nothing could be seen. There was neither sun nor moon. Lightning and thunder crushed mountain"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-17"><data key="title">King Shveta</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">In satya yuga there used to be a king named Shveta. He was such a good king that during his reign people lived for ten thousand years. No one dies as a child. Longevity was high and there was no infant mortality. But there was a sage named Kapalagoutama. Unfortunately, the sage’s son died as an infant. The sage…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 17, "quote": "9.17 King Shveta In satya yuga there used to be a king named Shveta. He was such a good king that during his reign people lived for ten thousand years. No one dies as a child. Longevity was high and there wa"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-18"><data key="title">Vali</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There was a king of the daityas named Vali. He was powerful and invincible. He was also righteous and truthful. The gods cold not bear to see Vali’s prosperity and began to plot how Vali might be foiled. So well did Vali rule that disease, drought and evil disappeared throughout the three worlds. In desperation, the…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 17, "quote": "9.18 Vali There was a king of the daityas named Vali. He was powerful and invincible. He was also righteous and truthful. The gods cold not bear to see Vali’s prosperity and began to plot how Vali mig"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-19"><data key="title">Gautama</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Gautama’s hermitage survives a drought. A plan involving Ganesha leads him to bring a portion of Ganga from Shiva’s hair; the story explains a sacred river and place.</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 18, "quote": "9.19 Gautama Part of the water of the Ganga that got stuck in Shiva’s hair was brought down to earth by Bhagiratha. The remaining part was brought down by the sage Gautama. Parvati was married to Shiva,"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-20"><data key="title">The Doves</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">In a mountain known as Brahmagiri there used to live a hunter who was very cruel. He not only killed birds and animals, but brahmanas and sages as well. The hunter once went on a hunt. He killed many animals and birds and some he put in his cages. He had penetrated so far inside the forest that he was far from home.…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 19, "quote": "9.20 The Doves In a mountain known as Brahmagiri there used to live a hunter who was very cruel. He not only killed birds and animals, but brahmanas and sages as well. The hunter once went on a hunt. He ki"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-21"><data key="title">Garuda and Maninaga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">You have already been told about the great snake (naga) Ananta. Ananta had a son named Maninaga. Garuda was the enemy of the snakes and the snakes were all afraid of Garuda. Maninaga began to pray to Shiva. Having pleased Shiva, he obtained the boon that Garuda would be able to do him no harm. Armed with this boon,…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 20, "quote": "9.21 Garuda and Maninaga You have already been told about the great snake (naga) Ananta. Ananta had a son named Maninaga. Garuda was the enemy of the snakes and the snakes were all afraid of Garuda. Maninaga began t"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-22"><data key="title">Vishvamitra and Indra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Many years ago, there was a terrible drought. There was no food to be had. The sage Vishvamitra had come to the banks of the river Goutami Ganga with his disciples. Vishvamitra’s wife, children and disciples were all hungry. The sage therefore sent his disciples out to look for food. They searched everywhere, but…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "9.22 Vishvamitra and Indra Many years ago, there was a terrible drought. There was no food to be had. The sage Vishvamitra had come to the banks of the river Goutami Ganga with his disciples. Vishvamitra’s wife, child"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-23"><data key="title">Shveta</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There used to be a brahmana named Shveta. He was a friend of the sage Goutama and had a hermitage on the banks of the Goutami Ganga. Shveta was also devoted to Shiva. In due course, Shiveta died and Yama’s messengers arrived to take Shveta to Yama. But they could not even enter Shveta’s house. Finding that Yama’s…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "9.23 Shveta There used to be a brahmana named Shveta. He was a friend of the sage Goutama and had a hermitage on the banks of the Goutami Ganga. Shveta was also devoted to Shiva. In due course, Shiveta"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-24"><data key="title">Kubera</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Kubera was the eldest son of the sage Vishrava. Vishrava had two wives. The fist wife give birth to Kubera. The second wife was a rakshasa (demon) woman and gave birth to Ravana, Kumbhakarna and Vibhishana. Kubera used to rule in Lanka and his relations with his cousins were extremely good. But Ravana’s mother did not…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 22, "quote": "9.24 Kubera Kubera was the eldest son of the sage Vishrava. Vishrava had two wives. The fist wife give birth to Kubera. The second wife was a rakshasa (demon) woman and gave birth to Ravana, Kumbhakarna"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-25"><data key="title">Harishchandra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">In the Ikshvaku dynasty there ruled a king named Harishchandra. Harishchandra had no son. One day two sages named Narada and Parvata came to visit Harishchandra and told him that he would go to hell if he did not have a son. “How can I obtain a son?” asked Harishchandra. “Go to the banks of the Goutami Ganga,” advised…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 22, "quote": "9.25 Harishchandra In the Ikshvaku dynasty there ruled a king named Harishchandra. Harishchandra had no son. One day two sages named Narada and Parvata came to visit Harishchandra and told him that he would go"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-26"><data key="title">Vriddhagoutama</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sage Goutama had a son named Vriddhagoutama. Vriddhagoutama was also a sage. But he was very ugly. Besides, he had no nose; had never had one since he had been born. He was so ashamed of this deformity that he could not bear to join the other children of the sages in studying the Vedas and Shastras under a…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 24, "quote": "9.26 Vriddhagoutama The sage Goutama had a son named Vriddhagoutama. Vriddhagoutama was also a sage. But he was very ugly. Besides, he had no nose; had never had one since he had been born. He was so ashamed of"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-27"><data key="title">Pippalada</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Many years ago, there used to be a sage named Dadhici. His wife was Lopamudra. Dadhichi’s hermitage was right next to the holy river Ganga. Lopamudra’s sister Gabhastini also used to live in the hermitage. Such was the sage Dadhichi’s powers that daityas and danavas dared not set foot inside the hermitage. The gods…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 25, "quote": "9.27 Pippalada Many years ago, there used to be a sage named Dadhici. His wife was Lopamudra. Dadhichi’s hermitage was right next to the holy river Ganga. Lopamudra’s sister Gabhastini also used to live in"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-28"><data key="title">Nageshvara</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There was a city named Pratishthana. A king named Shurasena ruled in that city. Shurasena did not have any sons. After a lot of effort, a son was born to him. But the son happened to be a snake. The king and the queen were mortified at this turn of events. But they brought up their son in great secrecy. Not even the…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 27, "quote": "9.28 Nageshvara There was a city named Pratishthana. A king named Shurasena ruled in that city. Shurasena did not have any sons. After a lot of effort, a son was born to him. But the son happened to be a sn"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-29"><data key="title">Brahma’s Head</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Years ago, there was a war between the gods and the demons and the gods lost. The gods came to Brahma for advice and Brahma asked them to pray to Shiva. When Shiva appeared before them, the gods said, “The demons have defeated us. Please kill them and save us.” Shiva went to fight with the demons. He drove the demons…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 28, "quote": "9.29 Brahma’s Head Years ago, there was a war between the gods and the demons and the gods lost. The gods came to Brahma for advice and Brahma asked them to pray to Shiva. When Shiva appeared before them, the"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-30"><data key="title">The Owl and the Dove</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A dove used to live on the banks of the Goutami Ganga. The dove’s name was Anuhrada and its wife’s name was Heti. Anuhrada was Yama’s grandson. Not very far away, there lived an owl named Uluka. The owl’s wife was called Uluki. The owls were descended from the god Agni. The doves and the owls were enemies. They fought…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 29, "quote": "9.30 The Owl and the Dove A dove used to live on the banks of the Goutami Ganga. The dove’s name was Anuhrada and its wife’s name was Heti. Anuhrada was Yama’s grandson. Not very far away, there lived an owl named Ul"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-31"><data key="title">The Hunter</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">There was a sage named Veda. He used to pray to Shiva every day. The prayers lasted till the afternoon and after the prayers were over, Veda used to go to the nearby villages to beg alms. A hunter named Bhilla used to come to the forest every afternoon to hunt. After the hunt was over, he used to come to Shiva’s linga…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 29, "quote": "9.31 The Hunter There was a sage named Veda. He used to pray to Shiva every day. The prayers lasted till the afternoon and after the prayers were over, Veda used to go to the nearby villages to beg alms. A"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-32"><data key="title">Goutama and Manikundala</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There was a city named Bhouvana. In that city there lived a brahmana named Goutama. The brahmana had a vaishya friend named Manikundala. (A vaishya belongs to the third of the three classes and his primary duties are trade and agriculture). Goutama’s mother gave him a lot of ill advice. As a result of this, Goutama…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 30, "quote": "9.32 Goutama and Manikundala There was a city named Bhouvana. In that city there lived a brahmana named Goutama. The brahmana had a vaishya friend named Manikundala. (A vaishya belongs to the third of the three classes"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-33"><data key="title">Kandu</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There was a sage named Kandu. He had a hermitage on the banks of the Goutami Ganga. The ashrama was a beautiful place and Kandu performed very difficult tapasya there. In the summer Kandu meditated in the hot sun, in the monsoon he meditated on the wet ground, and in the winter he meditated dressed in wet clothing.…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 31, "quote": "9.33 Kandu There was a sage named Kandu. He had a hermitage on the banks of the Goutami Ganga. The ashrama was a beautiful place and Kandu performed very difficult tapasya there. In the summer Kandu me"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-34"><data key="title">Varnashrama</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There are four varnas (classes). Their names are brahmana, kshatriya, vaishya and shudra. The duties of a brahmana are to donate alms, perform tapasya, worship the gods, perform yajnas and study the Vedas. To earn a living, brahmanas are authorized to teach and act as priests at sacrifices. The duties of kshatriya are…</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 32, "quote": "9.34 Varnashrama There are four varnas (classes). Their names are brahmana, kshatriya, vaishya and shudra. The duties of a brahmana are to donate alms, perform tapasya, worship the gods, perform yajnas and s"}]</data></node><node id="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-35"><data key="title">The Chandala and the Brahma-Rakshasa</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A devoted singer promises a brahmarakshasa that he will return after Vishnu worship. The tale connects truthfulness, devotion and the release of the demon.</data><data key="doc">brahma-vaivart-puran</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 33, "quote": "9.35 The Chandala and the Brahma-Rakshasa A chandala is an outcast. On the outskirts of the city named Avanti lived a chandala. There was a temple of Vishnu in Avanti and the chandala was devoted to Vishnu. He was also a good singer"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-1"><data key="title">Introduction</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains xx sections as follows:</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 1, "quote": "14.1 Introduction This chapter contains xx sections as follows:"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-1-1"><data key="title">Lord Vishnu’s Incarnations</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Sutji once reached Naimisharanya in course of his pilgrimage. There he found numerous sages engaged in austerities and penance. All of them were delighted to find Sutji in their midst and considered it as a God sent opportunity to get their doubts related with religious topics cleared. Sage Shaunak was also present…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 1, "quote": "14.1.1 Lord Vishnu’s Incarnations Sutji once reached Naimisharanya in course of his pilgrimage. There he found numerous sages engaged in austerities and penance. All of them were delighted to find Sutji in their midst and co"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-1-2"><data key="title">How Garuda Purana was propagated</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">On being asked by the sages as to how did he come to know about the contents of Garuda Puran, Sutji told them--' Once, I had gone to Badrikashram, where I met sage Vyas. I requested him to enlighten me on various spiritual matters to which he agreed saying that he was going to narrate the tales of Garuda Purana which…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 2, "quote": "14.1.2 How Garuda Purana was propagated On being asked by the sages as to how did he come to know about the contents of Garuda Puran, Sutji told them--' Once, I had gone to Badrikashram, where I met sage Vyas. I requested him to e"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-1-3"><data key="title">Lord Vishnu reveals his omnipotent nature</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Dwelling on length about his power, Lord Vishnu told Shiva-- ' O Rudra! I am the lord of all the deities. I am the one who controls each and every event that occurs in the Universe. I am the one whom mortals worship with the desire of attaining salvation. The universe would have ceased to exist but for me. I am the…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 2, "quote": "14.1.3 Lord Vishnu reveals his omnipotent nature Dwelling on length about his power, Lord Vishnu told Shiva-- ' O Rudra! I am the lord of all the deities. I am the one who controls each and every event that occurs in the Universe. I am the"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-1-4"><data key="title">Lord Vishnu Blesses Garuda</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing how Garuda pleased him with his deep devotion, Lord Vishnu said-- 'During ancient times Garuda once did an austere penance to please me. I appeared before him and expressed my willingness to fulfil anything that he wished for. Garuda wanted to liberate his mother, Vinta from the slavery of Kadru-the mother…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 2, "quote": "14.1.4 Lord Vishnu Blesses Garuda Describing how Garuda pleased him with his deep devotion, Lord Vishnu said-- 'During ancient times Garuda once did an austere penance to please me. I appeared before him and expressed my wil"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-1-5"><data key="title">The Beginning of Creation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva requested Vishnu to shed light on different topics like Sarga (world), Pratisarga, Vansh (dynasties), Manvantar (fourteenth part of Brahma's day) and Vanshanucharit (genealogy). Lord Vishnu replied--' O Rudra! Lord Vasudeva in his incarnation of Nara-Narayan performs his responsibilities of the creator,…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 3, "quote": "14.1.5 The Beginning of Creation Lord Shiva requested Vishnu to shed light on different topics like Sarga (world), Pratisarga, Vansh (dynasties), Manvantar (fourteenth part of Brahma's day) and Vanshanucharit (genealogy). L"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-1-6"><data key="title">The Beginning of Copulative Creation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">'Having created Daksha Prajapati and his wife Prasuti from his right and left thumb respectively, Lord Brahma instructed both of them to make their contribution in increasing the population with the help of copulative creation. In course of time, Daksha Prajapati begot numerous daughters all of whom were given in…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 4, "quote": "14.1.6 The Beginning of Copulative Creation 'Having created Daksha Prajapati and his wife Prasuti from his right and left thumb respectively, Lord Brahma instructed both of them to make their contribution in increasing the population"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-1-7"><data key="title">The Dhruva Dynasty</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lord Vishnu, continuing with the tales of Garuda Puran told Shiva-' Uttanpad had two wives-Suruchi and Suniti. From his former wife he begot a son named Uttam, while Suniti gave birth to Dhruva, who became immortal because of his unflinching devotion in me. Dhruva had a mighty son named 'Shlishta'. Prachinvarhi was…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 4, "quote": "14.1.7 The Dhruva Dynasty Lord Vishnu, continuing with the tales of Garuda Puran told Shiva-' Uttanpad had two wives-Suruchi and Suniti. From his former wife he begot a son named Uttam, while Suniti gave birth to Dhr"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-2"><data key="title">Powerful Mantras</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 4 sections as follows:</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 5, "quote": "14.2 Powerful Mantras This chapter contains 4 sections as follows:"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-2-1"><data key="title">Navgrah Mantra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing the significance of worshipping Navgraha (nine planets), Lord Vishnu told Rudra --' One who worships the Navgraha with appropriate rituals attains all the four 'purusharth'(objectives of man's life)-dharma(religion), artha(money), kaam(sex) and moksha(salvation).' He then went on to give various mantras…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 5, "quote": "14.2.1 Navgrah Mantra Describing the significance of worshipping Navgraha (nine planets), Lord Vishnu told Rudra --' One who worships the Navgraha with appropriate rituals attains all the four 'purusharth'(object"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-2-2"><data key="title">Shiva Mantra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There are various mantras used in the rituals of Shiva worship. A devotee should begin by worshipping the 'Aasan' (the pedestal) on which the deity is seated. There is a specific mantra for this particular ritual- OM HRAM SHIVAY NAMAH. Subsequently, a salutation to lord Shiva is made by chanting - OM SHIVA MURTAYE…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 6, "quote": "14.2.2 Shiva Mantra There are various mantras used in the rituals of Shiva worship. A devotee should begin by worshipping the 'Aasan' (the pedestal) on which the deity is seated. There is a specific mantra for"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-2-3"><data key="title">Sarasvati Mantra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A devotee should begin his worship by making salutations to goddess Sarswati by chanting-- OM HRIM SARSWATYAI NAMAH. He should then perform the rituals of 'Shadangnyas' by chanting the following mantras-- OM HRAM HRIDAYAY NAMAH, OM HRIM SHIRSE SWAHA, OM HOOM SHIKHAYAI VASHAT, OM HRAIM KAVACHAY HOOM, OM HRAUM NETRA…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 7, "quote": "14.2.3 Sarasvati Mantra A devotee should begin his worship by making salutations to goddess Sarswati by chanting-- OM HRIM SARSWATYAI NAMAH. He should then perform the rituals of 'Shadangnyas' by chanting the follo"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-2-4"><data key="title">Laxmi Mantra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A devotee desirous of acquiring wealth and prosperity must worship Goddess Laxmi with appropriate rituals. He should begin by making salutations to the goddess by chanting the following mantra -- OM SRIM HRIM MAHALAKSHMYAI NAMAH. Then follows the rituals of 'Shadangnyas' that is performed by chanting the specific…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 7, "quote": "14.2.4 Laxmi Mantra A devotee desirous of acquiring wealth and prosperity must worship Goddess Laxmi with appropriate rituals. He should begin by making salutations to the goddess by chanting the following mant"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-3"><data key="title">Astrology in Garuda Purana</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 11 sections as follows:</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 7, "quote": "14.3 Astrology in Garuda Purana This chapter contains 11 sections as follows:"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-3-1"><data key="title">The Chariots of Navgrahas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">While describing the chariots of all the nine planets, Lord Vishnu told Rudra--' The chariot of Surya deva(sun) has an unbelievable expanse stretching up to nine thousand yojans. The axle of the chariot is one crore and fifty seven lakh yojans long and wheels are fixed at both its ends. The wheels have six…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 7, "quote": "14.3.1 The Chariots of Navgrahas While describing the chariots of all the nine planets, Lord Vishnu told Rudra--' The chariot of Surya deva(sun) has an unbelievable expanse stretching up to nine thousand yojans. The axle of"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-3-2"><data key="title">Description of Yogas and Muhurtas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Giving elaborate description of inauspicious days on which one should not undertake travels or journeys, Lord Vishnu told Shiva-- 'Various Yoginis (goddesses) dwell in different directions on specific days and one should never set out on journeys on those days. A yogini named Brahmani dwells in the East on…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 7, "quote": "14.3.2 Description of Yogas and Muhurtas Giving elaborate description of inauspicious days on which one should not undertake travels or journeys, Lord Vishnu told Shiva-- 'Various Yoginis (goddesses) dwell in different directions o"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-3-3"><data key="title">Amrit Yoga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">It is the most auspicious yoga for commencing any work. The conjunction of a particular day and specific nakshatra constitute this yoga. Few examples of Amrit yoga are Mool nakshatra falling on Sunday, Shravan nakshatra on Monday, Uttara bhadrapad on Tuesday, Kritika on Wednesday, Punarvasu on Thursday, Purvafalguni…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 8, "quote": "14.3.3 Amrit Yoga It is the most auspicious yoga for commencing any work. The conjunction of a particular day and specific nakshatra constitute this yoga. Few examples of Amrit yoga are Mool nakshatra falling"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-3-4"><data key="title">Visha Yoga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">It is considered to be an inauspicious yoga and unsuitable for commencing any important work. Few examples of this particular yoga are Bharni nakshatra falling on Friday, Chitra nakshatra on Monday, Uttarashadha on Tuesday, Dhanishtha on Wednesday, Shatabhisha on Thursday, Rohini on Friday and Revati nakshatra falling…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 8, "quote": "14.3.4 Visha Yoga It is considered to be an inauspicious yoga and unsuitable for commencing any important work. Few examples of this particular yoga are Bharni nakshatra falling on Friday, Chitra nakshatra on"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-3-5"><data key="title">Graha Dasha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing the period for which a particular planet is supposed to have its influence on the native, Lord Vishnu told Shiva-- ' Surya's dasha continues for six years and brings turbulence, turmoil and agony to the concerned person. Moon's dasha continues for fifteen years and is considered very auspicious as it brings…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 8, "quote": "14.3.5 Graha Dasha Describing the period for which a particular planet is supposed to have its influence on the native, Lord Vishnu told Shiva-- ' Surya's dasha continues for six years and brings turbulence, t"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-3-6"><data key="title">Predictions based on Physical Traits</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Dwelling on length about the importance of physical traits and symptoms apparent in an individual, Lord Vishnu told Shiva-' If one sees the following physical traits in a man, he should immediately understand that he is seeing a prospective King : Hands and feet as soft as a Lotus flower, pink nails and no space left…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 8, "quote": "14.3.6 Predictions based on Physical Traits Dwelling on length about the importance of physical traits and symptoms apparent in an individual, Lord Vishnu told Shiva-' If one sees the following physical traits in a man, he should imme"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-3-7"><data key="title">Predicting Age with the help of Forehead Line</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Vishnu said-' Anybody having three parallel lines on his forehead lives happily for sixty years whereas two parallel lines indicates that he would live till the age of forty years. Only one line on the forehead indicates that he would have a short life but if the line stretches up to both the ears then he lives…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 9, "quote": "14.3.7 Predicting Age with the help of Forehead Line Lord Vishnu said-' Anybody having three parallel lines on his forehead lives happily for sixty years whereas two parallel lines indicates that he would live till the age of forty years. Only"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-3-8"><data key="title">Predicting Age by Palmistry</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Vishnu, continuing with his narration told Shiva that the age of a person could be predicted by the lines found on his palms- ' If the life line reaches the base between index and middle fingers then the concerned person lives for a century. If the life line is long, clear and without intersections from other…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 9, "quote": "14.3.8 Predicting Age by Palmistry Lord Vishnu, continuing with his narration told Shiva that the age of a person could be predicted by the lines found on his palms- ' If the life line reaches the base between index and middl"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-3-9"><data key="title">Prediction based on physical traits of women</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Vishnu said-'A woman having a round face, curly hair and lower portion of her navel slightly slanted towards right, is extremely fortunate not only for herself but also for her whole clan. Similarly, a woman with golden complexion and hands as beautiful and soft as red lotus flower is exceptionally chaste and…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 9, "quote": "14.3.9 Prediction based on physical traits of women Lord Vishnu said-'A woman having a round face, curly hair and lower portion of her navel slightly slanted towards right, is extremely fortunate not only for herself but also for her whole cl"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-3-10"><data key="title">Various Names of ‘Shaligram’</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing how a Shaligram is named on the basis of number of Chakra present on it, Lord Vishnu said-' If there is only one Chakra on the Shaligram idol then it is called 'Sudarshan'. In the same way, Shaligram with two Chakras is known as 'Laxmi Narayan' while that having three Chakras on it is called 'Achyut'.…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 9, "quote": "14.3.10 Various Names of ‘Shaligram’ Describing how a Shaligram is named on the basis of number of Chakra present on it, Lord Vishnu said-' If there is only one Chakra on the Shaligram idol then it is called 'Sudarshan'. In the"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-3-11"><data key="title">How Jewels Originated</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sutji, once narrated the following tale to the assembled sages which sheds light on the origin of jewels and other precious stones - ' During ancient times there lived a mighty demon named Bala. Although he had defeated the deities and driven them out from the heaven, yet he had assured them that he would extend…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 9, "quote": "14.3.11 How Jewels Originated Sutji, once narrated the following tale to the assembled sages which sheds light on the origin of jewels and other precious stones -"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-4"><data key="title">Prominent Places of Pilgrimage</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The holy Ganga, which originates from the Himalayas in the north, ultimately gets merged with the ocean in the east. During her entire course of flow, she passes through numerous holy places, which owe their holiness mainly to her. Although, any place situated on the banks of Ganga is considered holy, yet Prayag,…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 10, "quote": "14.4 Prominent Places of Pilgrimage This chapter contains 5 sections as follows: Sutji, while giving the names of some prominent places of pilgrimage told the assembled sages-'The banks of Ganga are considered to be the holiest among all th"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-4-1"><data key="title">The Significance of Gaya Teertha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, while narrating how Gaya, a prominent place of pilgrimage in eastern part of India got its name, Lord Brahma told sage Vyas-' During ancient times there lived a mighty demon named Gaya. He used to torment the deities, who ultimately went to seek the help of Lord Vishnu. Lord Vishnu successfully killed Gayasur…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 10, "quote": "14.4.1 The Significance of Gaya Teertha Once, while narrating how Gaya, a prominent place of pilgrimage in eastern part of India got its name, Lord Brahma told sage Vyas-' During ancient times there lived a mighty demon named Gaya"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-4-2"><data key="title">The Rituals of Pindadaan</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Before setting out for Gaya to offer Pinda daan, a man should appropriately perform 'Shraddh rituals' in the village or town he lives. Each step taken towards Gaya acts as a stepping stone to the heaven for the ancestors. Getting one's head tonsured or observing fast is prohibited in some places of pilgrimage like…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 11, "quote": "14.4.2 The Rituals of Pindadaan Before setting out for Gaya to offer Pinda daan, a man should appropriately perform 'Shraddh rituals' in the village or town he lives. Each step taken towards Gaya acts as a stepping stone t"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-4-3"><data key="title">Tale of King Vishal</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lord Brahma then narrated a tale glorifying the significance of performing 'Shraddh rituals' at Gaya- ' O Vyas! Once upon a time there lived a trader who was sonless. One day, while he was on his pilgrimage, he met a restless ghost, who requested him to perform the rituals of Pinda daan at Gaya so that not only he…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 11, "quote": "14.4.3 Tale of King Vishal Lord Brahma then narrated a tale glorifying the significance of performing 'Shraddh rituals' at Gaya- ' O Vyas! Once upon a time there lived a trader who was sonless. One day, while he was o"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-4-4"><data key="title">Prajapati Ruchi’s Disenchantment</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, Sutji recounted the following tale to the assembled sages, which had been originally told by sage Markandeya to Kraunchki long ago- 'Once, sage Ruchi became so disenchanted with the world that he decided to remain a bachelor, a decision which made his 'Pitras'(ancestors) extremely worried. They tried to change…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 12, "quote": "14.4.4 Prajapati Ruchi’s Disenchantment Once, Sutji recounted the following tale to the assembled sages, which had been originally told by sage Markandeya to Kraunchki long ago- 'Once, sage Ruchi became so disenchanted with the wo"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-4-5"><data key="title">Prajapati Ruchi marries Manini</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The blessings given by 'Pitras' fructified instantaneously as a beautiful 'Apsara' manifested herself from the river flowing nearby and said-' O great sage! I am Pramlocha. I would consider it as my good fortune if you give your consent to marry my daughter- Manini. Pushkar- son of Varun is her father. In course of…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 12, "quote": "14.4.5 Prajapati Ruchi marries Manini The blessings given by 'Pitras' fructified instantaneously as a beautiful 'Apsara' manifested herself from the river flowing nearby and said-' O great sage! I am Pramlocha. I would consider"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-5"><data key="title">Description of Various Consecrations</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This union along with the whole process of foetus getting transformed into a baby is a lengthy affair during which it imbibes numerous impurities. Consecrations purify a man from all these impurities. Lord Vishnu said -' The mankind has been categorized into four prominent castes- Brahmin, Kshatriya, Vaishya and…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 13, "quote": "14.5 Description of Various Consecrations This chapter contains 6 sections as follows: While describing the significance of various consecrations, Lord Vishnu told Shiva that the union of man's sperm and woman's ovum results into the birth "}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-5-1"><data key="title">Upanayana Sanskara</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Dwelling on length about the appropriate way of performing 'Upanayan sanskar' (Sacred thread ceremony) rituals, Lord Vishnu said-' A Brahmin child should get consecrated with the sacred thread in his eighth year while a Kshatriya child's consecration should be performed when he has attained the age of eleven.…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 13, "quote": "14.5.1 Upanayana Sanskara Dwelling on length about the appropriate way of performing 'Upanayan sanskar' (Sacred thread ceremony) rituals, Lord Vishnu said-' A Brahmin child should get consecrated with the sacred thre"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-5-2"><data key="title">Keshant Sanskara</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After finishing his education the celibate should shave his beards and moustaches for the first time. Strict guidelines have been laid down for each caste with respect to this particular consecration. A Brahmin should get consecrated with this ritual in his sixteenth year while a Kshatriya should get it performed in…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 14, "quote": "14.5.2 Keshant Sanskara After finishing his education the celibate should shave his beards and moustaches for the first time. Strict guidelines have been laid down for each caste with respect to this particular con"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-5-3"><data key="title">Grihasta Ashrama</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">His education being complete now it is the time for him to enter the life of a householder. But, before leaving the hermitage he should pay 'Dakshina' as a mark of gratitude to his Guru for all the knowledge he has imparted to him. With the permission of his Guru, he should then marry a girl who is beautiful, younger…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 14, "quote": "14.5.3 Grihasta Ashrama His education being complete now it is the time for him to enter the life of a householder. But, before leaving the hermitage he should pay 'Dakshina' as a mark of gratitude to his Guru for"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-5-4"><data key="title">Copulation Prohibited on Certain Days</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">An ideal wife is one who obeys the commands of her husband without any inhibition. According to the scriptures the best time for a married couple to copulate is after the woman has had her menstruation, especially till sixteen days after her periods have commenced are considered best for this purpose. But, there are…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 14, "quote": "14.5.4 Copulation Prohibited on Certain Days An ideal wife is one who obeys the commands of her husband without any inhibition. According to the scriptures the best time for a married couple to copulate is after the woman has had her m"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-5-5"><data key="title">Purification of Different Articles</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing the methods of purifying different articles, Lord Vishnu reflected on the instructions which sage Yagyavalkya had once given to other sages in this regard-' Substances like gold, silver, conch, vegetables, rope, leather articles, etc. become pure once they are washed with water. In the same way, utensils…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 14, "quote": "14.5.5 Purification of Different Articles Describing the methods of purifying different articles, Lord Vishnu reflected on the instructions which sage Yagyavalkya had once given to other sages in this regard-' Substances like gold,"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-5-6"><data key="title">Brief Description of Shraadh Rituals</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing the proper way of performing Shraddh rituals, sage Yagyavalkya told the assembled sages-' A performer of Shraddh rituals should invite able Brahmins and offer them seats of Kusha grass. First of all an invocation is made to 'Vishvedeva' by chanting a mantra in his praise, an action which is followed by…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 14, "quote": "14.5.6 Brief Description of Shraadh Rituals Describing the proper way of performing Shraddh rituals, sage Yagyavalkya told the assembled sages-' A performer of Shraddh rituals should invite able Brahmins and offer them seats of Kusha"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-6"><data key="title">Karma and its consequences – Karma Vipak</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">He is also aware that a man reaps the fruits of his evil deeds in the form of 'Adhidaivik'(heavenly wrath), 'Adhyatmik'(obstacles in spiritual development) and 'Adhibhautik' (worldly problems like diseases, poverty, etc.) That is why he endeavours to follow the path of virtuosity so that he can attain salvation- the…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 15, "quote": "14.6 Karma and its consequences – Karma Vipak This chapter contains 1 section as follows: Continuing with his discourses, sage Yagvalkya told the assembled sages that an enlightened soul is aware of the fact that the mortal world being impe"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-6-1"><data key="title">Atoning for Sins</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">While describing various means by which a sinner can atone for his sins, sage Yagyavalkya said-' A man is certain to meet downfall and degradation in the following three conditions- if he goes against the instructions given in the scriptures, indulges in prohibited activities and runs after sensual pleasures. Atoning…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 15, "quote": "14.6.1 Atoning for Sins While describing various means by which a sinner can atone for his sins, sage Yagyavalkya said-' A man is certain to meet downfall and degradation in the following three conditions- if he go"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-7"><data key="title">Different Kind of Austerities</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 15 sections as follows: Describing the various types of austerities, Lord Vishnu named few of them-</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 16, "quote": "14.7 Different Kind of Austerities This chapter contains 15 sections as follows: Describing the various types of austerities, Lord Vishnu named few of them-"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-7-1"><data key="title">Yama (Restraining Passion)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This particular austerity comprises of ten virtues- Celibacy, Forgiveness, Meditation, Truthfulness, Honesty, Non-violence, Not stealing, Gentleness and Self control.</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 16, "quote": "14.7.1 Yama (Restraining Passion) This particular austerity comprises of ten virtues- Celibacy, Forgiveness, Meditation, Truthfulness, Honesty, Non-violence, Not stealing, Gentleness and Self control."}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-7-2"><data key="title">Niyama (Daily Routine)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This austerity stresses the importance of purity: both of the body as well as of the mind. It comprises of the following activities: bathing daily, contemplation, observing fast, performing oblations, self study, abstinence, penance, avoiding anger, being respectful towards teacher and purity.</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 16, "quote": "14.7.2 Niyama (Daily Routine) This austerity stresses the importance of purity: both of the body as well as of the mind. It comprises of the following activities: bathing daily, contemplation, observing fast, performing"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-7-3"><data key="title">Mahasantapana Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This particular austerity comprises of specific rituals related with the usage of 'Panchagavya'(a mixture of cow milk, curd, ghee, cow urine and cow dung). On the first day of the austerity a devotee should have only milk, on the second day curd, on the third day 'ghee', on the fourth day 'gomutra'(cow urine), on the…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 16, "quote": "14.7.3 Mahasantapana Vrata This particular austerity comprises of specific rituals related with the usage of 'Panchagavya'(a mixture of cow milk, curd, ghee, cow urine and cow dung). On the first day of the austerity"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-7-4"><data key="title">Parna Krichchha Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Separate solutions of 'Palash'(Butea frondosa), 'Gular'(Ficus glomerata), 'Kamal'( lotus) and 'Bael' tree (wood apple) are prepared after boiling the leaves of respective vegetation mentioned above. On the first day of the austerity a devotee should have only 'Palash' solution while on the second day he should have…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 16, "quote": "14.7.4 Parna Krichchha Vrata Separate solutions of 'Palash'(Butea frondosa), 'Gular'(Ficus glomerata), 'Kamal'( lotus) and 'Bael' tree (wood apple) are prepared after boiling the leaves of respective vegetation mentione"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-7-5"><data key="title">Tapta Krichchha Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A person observing this austerity should begin by having hot milk on the first day followed by tepid ghee and lukewarm water on the second and third day respectively. He should observe a complete fast on the fourth day.</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 16, "quote": "14.7.5 Tapta Krichchha Vrata A person observing this austerity should begin by having hot milk on the first day followed by tepid ghee and lukewarm water on the second and third day respectively. He should observe a com"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-7-6"><data key="title">Paad Krichchha Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This particular austerity lasts for four days. On the first day a devotee should observe 'Ekabhakta vrata'(having food only once a day in the noon). On the second day he should observe 'Nakta' vrata(having food only once a day in the night). On the third day he should break his fast by having whatever is available…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 16, "quote": "14.7.6 Paad Krichchha Vrata This particular austerity lasts for four days. On the first day a devotee should observe 'Ekabhakta vrata'(having food only once a day in the noon). On the second day he should observe 'Nakt"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-7-7"><data key="title">Ati Krichchha Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Rituals of this austerity is more or less similar to that of Prajapatya krichchhra vrata with a slight difference- here fast is broken with a handful of cooked food. When this particular ritual is observed for four days then it is called Atikrichchha vrata.</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 17, "quote": "14.7.7 Ati Krichchha Vrata Rituals of this austerity is more or less similar to that of Prajapatya krichchhra vrata with a slight difference- here fast is broken with a handful of cooked food. When this particular rit"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-7-8"><data key="title">Krichchha Atikrichha Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Observing the rituals of Atikrichchha vrata for twenty-one days living only on milk and water is called Krichchhakrichha vrata.</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 17, "quote": "14.7.8 Krichchha Atikrichha Vrata Observing the rituals of Atikrichchha vrata for twenty-one days living only on milk and water is called Krichchhakrichha vrata."}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-7-9"><data key="title">Parak Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Observing complete fast for twelve continuous days is called Parak vrata.</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 17, "quote": "14.7.9 Parak Vrata Observing complete fast for twelve continuous days is called Parak vrata."}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-7-10"><data key="title">Chandrayana Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">It's an important austerity lasting for a month which commences on First day of the bright half of a month by observing fast for the whole day and breaking it in the night with one morsel of food. On the next day, i.e. 'dwitiya' one morsel of food is increased which means that instead of one morsel of food now two…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 17, "quote": "14.7.10 Chandrayana Vrata It's an important austerity lasting for a month which commences on First day of the bright half of a month by observing fast for the whole day and breaking it in the night with one morsel of"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-7-11"><data key="title">Anang Trayodashi Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This austerity is related with the worship of Lord Shiva and commences on the thirteenth day of the bright half of the Hindu month Margashirsh and lasts for a year. It concludes exactly after a year when 'Rati' and 'Anang' are worshipped with appropriate rituals. Ten thousand offerings are made in the sacrificial…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 17, "quote": "14.7.11 Anang Trayodashi Vrata This austerity is related with the worship of Lord Shiva and commences on the thirteenth day of the bright half of the Hindu month Margashirsh and lasts for a year. It concludes exactly afte"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-7-12"><data key="title">Akhand Dvadashi Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This austerity is related with the worship of Lord Vishnu. It commences on the twelfth day of the bright half of the Hindu month Margashirsh and lasts for a year consisting of three phases. One important characteristic of this austerity is that after four months, which is the first phase of the austerity, five vessels…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 17, "quote": "14.7.12 Akhand Dvadashi Vrata This austerity is related with the worship of Lord Vishnu. It commences on the twelfth day of the bright half of the Hindu month Margashirsh and lasts for a year consisting of three phases."}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-7-13"><data key="title">Shiva Ratri Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This austerity is related with the worship of Lord Shiva and a fast is observed on the fourteenth day of the dark half of the Hindu month Falgun. A devotee should observe fast in the night and worship Lord Shiva by chanting 'Om Namah Shivay'. He should perform 'havan' with black sesame seeds and worship Lord Shiva at…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 17, "quote": "14.7.13 Shiva Ratri Vrata This austerity is related with the worship of Lord Shiva and a fast is observed on the fourteenth day of the dark half of the Hindu month Falgun. A devotee should observe fast in the night a"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-7-14"><data key="title">Virtues of Observing Fast on Shiva Ratri Night</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">During ancient times there lived a hunter named Sundarsen in the province of Arbuda. One day, he went deep into the forest in search of his prey but unfortunately he found none. He wandered all over the place but in vain. When evening came, he climbed up a wood apple (Bael) tree to protect himself from the wild…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 18, "quote": "14.7.14 Virtues of Observing Fast on Shiva Ratri Night During ancient times there lived a hunter named Sundarsen in the province of Arbuda. One day, he went deep into the forest in search of his prey but unfortunately he found none. He wandered"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-7-15"><data key="title">The Power of Chastity</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, sage Vyas went to Brahmaloka to get his doubts cleared on certain spiritual matters from Lord Brahma. While dwelling on a wide range of religious topics such as importance of austerities and various incarnations Lord Vishnu took to protect righteousness, Lord Brahma narrated a tale of a Brahmin woman who…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 18, "quote": "14.7.15 The Power of Chastity Once, sage Vyas went to Brahmaloka to get his doubts cleared on certain spiritual matters from Lord Brahma. While dwelling on a wide range of religious topics such as importance of austeriti"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-8"><data key="title">The Tale of Ramayana</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lord Brahma then went on to describe the famous tale of Ramayana the mere listening to which absolves a man from all his sins. He began by giving the details of Sri Ram's lineage and said-' I (Lord Brahma) manifested from the Lotus originating from the navel of Lord Vishnu. Sage Marich manifested from me while Kashyap…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 19, "quote": "14.8 The Tale of Ramayana Lord Brahma then went on to describe the famous tale of Ramayana the mere listening to which absolves a man from all his sins. He began by giving the details of Sri Ram's lineage and said-'"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-9"><data key="title">Sri Krshna</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Making a revelation to Vyas that the Almighty God manifests himself whenever the civilization is endangered by the dominance of sinners, Lord Brahma said- 'In Dwapar yuga, Lord Vishnu incarnated as Sri Krishna to protect Dharma from being subjugated by 'Adharma', which had become all pervasive at that time. He was…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 21, "quote": "14.9 Sri Krshna Making a revelation to Vyas that the Almighty God manifests himself whenever the civilization is endangered by the dominance of sinners, Lord Brahma said- 'In Dwapar yuga, Lord Vishnu incarn"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-10"><data key="title">The Tale of Mahabharat</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Giving the reasons that lead to the great battle of Mahabharat, Lord Brahma told sage Vyas that Sri Krishna had planned this great battle and executed it to perfection to relieve the earth from the burden of tyrants. While giving the names of lineal ascendants of Kauravas and Pandavas, he said-' I (Lord Brahma)…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 22, "quote": "14.10The Tale of Mahabharat Giving the reasons that lead to the great battle of Mahabharat, Lord Brahma told sage Vyas that Sri Krishna had planned this great battle and executed it to perfection to relieve the earth f"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-11"><data key="title">The Eternal Truth</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">He also revealed to Vyas that the 'Fire of sorrow' consumed everything that came into its fold and nothing remained untouched by it. A sorrowful man becomes bereft of all his possessions-knowledge, religion, physical strength, patience, happiness and all such qualities. Lord Brahma also told him that virtuous deeds…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 24, "quote": "14.11The Eternal Truth This chapter contains 2 sections as follows: Lord Brahma, while shedding light on various aspects of religion told sage Vyas that a man should look at his sorrows (shoka) with a sense of aloofness and detachment, cons"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-11-1"><data key="title">The Supreme Yoga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, sage Shaunak asked Sutji about the reasons behind man's sorrow. Sutji told him that man's ego and his attachments to this mortal world were the two most important causes for his sorrow and until he gets rid of them he will continue to suffer. Sutji said-' The 'Tree of ignorance' sprouts from the seed of…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 24, "quote": "14.11.1 The Supreme Yoga Once, sage Shaunak asked Sutji about the reasons behind man's sorrow. Sutji told him that man's ego and his attachments to this mortal world were the two most important causes for his sorrow"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-11-2"><data key="title">Devotion – The Path Leading to the Almighty</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Glorifying devotion as the simplest means to reach God, Sutji told Shaunak that there was nothing else that pleased God more than the unflinching devotion of his devotee in him. Sutji said- 'A devotee who chants the holy name of God acquires imperishable virtues and attains salvation even if he were a lowly sinner.…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 24, "quote": "14.11.2 Devotion – The Path Leading to the Almighty Glorifying devotion as the simplest means to reach God, Sutji told Shaunak that there was nothing else that pleased God more than the unflinching devotion of his devotee in him. Sutji said-"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-12"><data key="title">Divine Stotras</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 3 sections as follows:</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 25, "quote": "14.12Divine Stotras This chapter contains 3 sections as follows:"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-12-1"><data key="title">NriSinha Stotra</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Telling about the origin of this 'stotra', Sutji said- 'Lord Shiva had created this 'stotra' praising the glory of Lord Nrisinha and invoking him to protect the world from the wrath of Matrikas. The 'stotra' goes as follows - NAMASTESTU JAGANNATH NARSINHA VAPURDHAR; DAITESHWAR ENDRA SANHARINAKHA BHUKTI VIRAJIT. NAKH…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 25, "quote": "14.12.1 NriSinha Stotra Telling about the origin of this 'stotra', Sutji said- 'Lord Shiva had created this 'stotra' praising the glory of Lord Nrisinha and invoking him to protect the world from the wrath of Matri"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-12-2"><data key="title">Kulamrit Stotra</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Sage Narad once asked Lord Shiva about the means by which a man could become free from the delusions of the world. Lord Shiva revealed to him the contents of 'Kulamrit stotra', eulogizing the majesty of Lord Vishnu. By chanting this 'stotra', Narad successfully attained to that supreme status, which even the most…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 25, "quote": "14.12.2 Kulamrit Stotra Sage Narad once asked Lord Shiva about the means by which a man could become free from the delusions of the world. Lord Shiva revealed to him the contents of 'Kulamrit stotra', eulogizing th"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-12-3"><data key="title">Mrityu Ashtak Stotra</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Describing the origin of this 'stotra', Sutji said- 'Lord Vishnu himself had taught this powerful 'stotra' to sage Markandeya. The daily chanting of this powerful mantra had enabled Markandeya to conquer death. One who daily chants this 'stotra' thrice a day enjoys a long life and never dies a premature death. The…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 26, "quote": "14.12.3 Mrityu Ashtak Stotra Describing the origin of this 'stotra', Sutji said- 'Lord Vishnu himself had taught this powerful 'stotra' to sage Markandeya. The daily chanting of this powerful mantra had enabled Markande"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-13"><data key="title">Salvation Through Yoga</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">A man can not attain salvation until and unless he has seen through the trappings of the worldly illusions. Sutji said-'Practice of Yoga helps a man to live in this world with a sense of detachment and to successfully avoid the allurements of this mortal world. Yoga comprises of six organs:  Pranayam ( Breathing…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 26, "quote": "14.13Salvation Through Yoga This chapter contains 2 sections as follows: Sutji told sage Shaunak that the ultimate aim of a man's life was to attain salvation. A man can not attain salvation until and unless he has seen through the trapping"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-13-1"><data key="title">Self Realization</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, on being asked by sage Narad about the means that helped a man in attaining self realization, Lord Vishnu told him that only those privileged few who have understood the impermanent nature of this world and have successfully controlled their sensual desires can attain self realization. All the mysteries of this…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 27, "quote": "14.13.1 Self Realization Once, on being asked by sage Narad about the means that helped a man in attaining self realization, Lord Vishnu told him that only those privileged few who have understood the impermanent na"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-13-2"><data key="title">Brahm-Gita</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Anybody who studies the sacred texts of 'Brahm-Gita' is certain to attain salvation for it brilliantly propounds the theory of divinity of man by proclaiming that in each individual dwells 'Brahm'-the supreme Almighty. Eulogizing the glory of 'Brahm Gita' Lord Vishnu said-' Brahm-Gita declares that in each individual…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 28, "quote": "14.13.2 Brahm-Gita Anybody who studies the sacred texts of 'Brahm-Gita' is certain to attain salvation for it brilliantly propounds the theory of divinity of man by proclaiming that in each individual dwells '"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-14"><data key="title">Garuda’s Queries</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Naimisharanya was a holy place where sages and hermits went to do penance. After reaching there Sutji met many prominent sages who were pleased at his arrival. They took it as a God sent opportunity to get their doubts cleared on the mystery called death and what exactly happened after a man died. Sage Shaunak, one of…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 28, "quote": "14.14Garuda’s Queries This chapter contains 2 sections as follows: Sutji once reached the forest of 'Naimisharanya' in course of his journey. Naimisharanya was a holy place where sages and hermits went to do penance. After reaching there Su"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-14-1"><data key="title">Salvation through Rituals</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lord Krishna replied--" I am so delighted that you have asked such important questions for the benefit of mankind. I am revealing to you the secrets, which were hitherto unknown even to the deities and yogis. O Garuda! A man should try to beget a son with the help of means as mentioned in the scriptures because there…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 29, "quote": "14.14.1 Salvation through Rituals Lord Krishna replied--\" I am so delighted that you have asked such important questions for the benefit of mankind. I am revealing to you the secrets, which were hitherto unknown even to the"}]</data></node><node id="p-garuda-purana-14-14-2"><data key="title">Death and After</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Continuing with his narration, Lord Krishna said--" O Garuda! Death comes at the predetermined time, which remains fixed and unaltered under all circumstances. A man's sense organs become weak and his body feeble, which gets inflicted with numerous diseases in his old age. At the time of his death, man experiences…</data><data key="doc">garuda-purana</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 30, "quote": "14.14.2 Death and After Continuing with his narration, Lord Krishna said--\" O Garuda! Death comes at the predetermined time, which remains fixed and unaltered under all circumstances. A man's sense organs become we"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-1"><data key="title">Introduction</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">(The samudra manthana story is given in great detail in the Ramayana and the Mahabharata. As for Lakshmi, some of the Puranas state that she was born as the daughter of Khyati and the sage Bhrigu. She was then married to Vishnu. But the demons defeated the gods and Indra. The sage Durvasa therefore cursed Lakshmi that…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "12.1 Introduction (The samudra manthana story is given in great detail in the Ramayana and the Mahabharata. As for Lakshmi, some of the Puranas state that she was born as the daughter of Khyati and the sage B"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-2"><data key="title">Indradyumna</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Many years ago, there was a king named Indradyumna. He ruled the world well and, when he died, was reborn as a brahmana, (The brahmanas constitute the first of the four classes, their primary duties are to study the Vedas and perform sacrifices.) As a brahmana, Indradyumna observed religious rites and meditated. He…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "12.2 Indradyumna Many years ago, there was a king named Indradyumna. He ruled the world well and, when he died, was reborn as a brahmana, (The brahmanas constitute the first of the four classes, their primar"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-3"><data key="title">Varnashrama Dharma</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Vishnu said that before creation began, there was only water in the universe and Vishnu slept on these waters. When it was time for creation to begin, Brahma emerged from Vishnu’s body. And Shiva emerged from Vishnu’s anger. Lakshmi too was created from Vishnu’s body and took her place by Vishnu’s side. Brahma told…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "12.3 Varnashrama Dharma Vishnu said that before creation began, there was only water in the universe and Vishnu slept on these waters. When it was time for creation to begin, Brahma emerged from Vishnu’s body. And"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-4"><data key="title">Creation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Vishnu next narrated to the gods and the sages the history of creation. In the beginning, the brahman was everywhere. The brahman had no form, but nor was it without form. It had no beginning and no end. It had no traits, but nor was it without traits. The brahman is impossible to describe, sense or see. Brahma,…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "12.4 Creation Vishnu next narrated to the gods and the sages the history of creation."}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-5"><data key="title">A Digression on Time</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The smallest unit of time is a nimesha. Fifteen nimeshas make one kashtha, thirty kashthas are one kala and thirty kalas constitute one muhurta. There are thirty muhurtas in a span of day and night (ahoratra). Thirty such ahoratras make up a month. There are two pakshas (fortnight) in every month. Six months…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "12.5 A Digression on Time The smallest unit of time is a nimesha. Fifteen nimeshas make one kashtha, thirty kashthas are one kala and thirty kalas constitute one muhurta. There are thirty muhurtas in a span of day an"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-6"><data key="title">The Boar Incarnation</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">A varaha is a boar and the boar incarnation is usually catalogued as the third of Vishnu’s ten incarnations. When the universe was submerged in water after the destruction that came at the end of padma kalpa, Vishnu slept on the waters. Thus he slept for a thousand mahayugas. Since the word nara means water and ayana…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "12.6 The Boar Incarnation A varaha is a boar and the boar incarnation is usually catalogued as the third of Vishnu’s ten incarnations. When the universe was submerged in water after the destruction that came at the e"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-7"><data key="title">Creation Continued</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Brahma first created five sons through his mental powers. Their names were Sanaka, Sanatana, Sanandana, Kratu and Sanatakumara These five sons became sages and did not have any offspring. Brahma therefore had to create some more beings so that the population of the universe might increase. But prior to that, he…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "12.7 Creation Continued Brahma first created five sons through his mental powers. Their names were Sanaka, Sanatana, Sanandana, Kratu and Sanatakumara These five sons became sages and did not have any offspring. Br"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-8"><data key="title">Brahma, Vishnu and Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The gods and the sages told Vishnu, “We are getting a bit confused. You have told us that Brahma emerged from Vishnu’s body And yet you have also told us how Brahma was born inside a golden egg. Which of these is the correct account? Then again, you have told us that Shiva was born from one of Brahma’s tears. But we…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 6, "quote": "12.8 Brahma, Vishnu and Shiva The gods and the sages told Vishnu, “We are getting a bit confused. You have told us that Brahma emerged from Vishnu’s body And yet you have also told us how Brahma was born inside a golden"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-9"><data key="title">Rudra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Brahma returned to his seat on the lotus flower. Suddenly, two demons named Madhu and Kaitabha appeared, They were exceedingly strong and threatened to destroy everything that Brahma would create. Brahma therefore requested Vishnu to kill these two demons. Vishnu created two beings from his own body and these two…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 8, "quote": "12.9 Rudra Brahma returned to his seat on the lotus flower. Suddenly, two demons named Madhu and Kaitabha appeared, They were exceedingly strong and threatened to destroy everything that Brahma would c"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-10"><data key="title">Parvati’s Thousand Names</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Shiva was married to Daksha’s daughter Sati. Sati died and was reborn as Parvati, the daughter of Himalaya and his wife Mena (alternatively, Menaka). Parvati was married to Shiva. In fact, Himalaya and Mena prayed so that they might have the goddess as their daughter. Pleased with their prayers, the goddess arrived…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 9, "quote": "12.10Parvati’s Thousand Names Shiva was married to Daksha’s daughter Sati. Sati died and was reborn as Parvati, the daughter of Himalaya and his wife Mena (alternatively, Menaka). Parvati was married to Shiva. In fact, H"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-11"><data key="title">The Line of Uttanapada</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">It is hoped that you have not forgotten that Svayambhuva Manu and his wife Shatarupa had a son named Uttanapada. Uttanapada’s brother was Priyavarta. Dhruva was Uttanapada’s son. Dhruva was so devoted to Vishnu that Vishnu earmarked for him a place in the heaven known as Dhruvaloka. (Dhruva became the Pole Star. The…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "12.11The Line of Uttanapada It is hoped that you have not forgotten that Svayambhuva Manu and his wife Shatarupa had a son named Uttanapada. Uttanapada’s brother was Priyavarta. Dhruva was Uttanapada’s son. Dhruva was"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-12"><data key="title">Daksha’s Story</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Daksha’s sacrifice becomes the setting for conflict with Shiva and the destructive intervention of Virabhadra. The supplied retelling explicitly comments on differences between Purana versions.</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 15, "quote": "12.12Daksha’s Story Daksha was Brahma’s son and had a daughter named Sati. Sati was married to Shiva. Daksha was thus Shiva’s father-in-law. Once Daksha came to visit his son-in-law. But although Shiva worshipp"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-13"><data key="title">Hiranyakshipu</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Hiranyakashipu pleased Brahma through his prayers. As a result of the boon that he received from Brahma, he became invisible and started to oppress the world. He drove the gods out of heaven. The gods and the sages went to Brahma to persuade him to do something about Hiranyakashipu. “I cannot really help you,” said…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 18, "quote": "12.13Hiranyakshipu Hiranyakashipu pleased Brahma through his prayers. As a result of the boon that he received from Brahma, he became invisible and started to oppress the world. He drove the gods out of heaven"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-14"><data key="title">Hiranyaksha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After Hiranyakashipu died, Hiranyaksha became the king of the demons. Hiranyaksha promptly began to oppress the world. He defeated the gods and drove them out of heaven. He also took the earth down to the underworld. The gods again went to Brahma in search of a solution and Brahma took them to Vishnu. They prayed to…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 19, "quote": "12.14Hiranyaksha After Hiranyakashipu died, Hiranyaksha became the king of the demons. Hiranyaksha promptly began to oppress the world. He defeated the gods and drove them out of heaven. He also took the ear"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-15"><data key="title">A Digression on Goutama</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Many years ago, there was a terrible drought on earth. There was no food to be had and famine prevailed. There were several sages who lived in the forest, and they too, suffered from a lack of food. Goutama was a very powerful sage and he had a hermitage in the forest. Such were the powers that Goutama had that it…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 20, "quote": "12.15A Digression on Goutama Many years ago, there was a terrible drought on earth. There was no food to be had and famine prevailed. There were several sages who lived in the forest, and they too, suffered from a lack"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-16"><data key="title">Andhaka</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">While Shiva was gone, he left his companion Nandi to look after his household. He also gave Vishnu the overall responsibility of ensuring that all was well with Parvati and the gods and the sages. Realising that Shiva was away, Andhaka thought that this was the opportune moment for abducting Parvati. He found that…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "12.16Andhaka While Shiva was gone, he left his companion Nandi to look after his household. He also gave Vishnu the overall responsibility of ensuring that all was well with Parvati and the gods and the"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-17"><data key="title">Vali</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">With Andhaka thus taken care of, Prahrada’s son Virochana became the king of the demons. He ruled his kingdom well. There was a sage named Sanatakumara who once went to visit Virochana. Virochana was delighted to see the sage and Sanatakumara instructed Virochana on the true nature of the universe. These teachings so…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "12.17Vali With Andhaka thus taken care of, Prahrada’s son Virochana became the king of the demons. He ruled his kingdom well. There was a sage named Sanatakumara who once went to visit Virochana. Viro"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-18"><data key="title">Vana</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Vali had a hundred sons, the eldest among whom was Vana. Vana was devoted to Shiva. He was also extremely powerful. He defeated Indra and conquered the three worlds. Indra and the other gods went to Shiva. “Your devotee, Vana is oppressing us,” they told Shiva. “Please take care of him.” Shiva took up a single arrow…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 22, "quote": "12.18Vana Vali had a hundred sons, the eldest among whom was Vana. Vana was devoted to Shiva. He was also extremely powerful. He defeated Indra and conquered the three worlds. Indra and the other gods"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-19"><data key="title">The Solar Dynasty</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sun-god, Vivasvana, was the son of Kashyapa and Aditi. He had four wives, Samjna, Rajni, Prabha and Chhaya. Samjna’s son was Vaivasvata Manu. Rajni’s children were Yama, yamuna and Revanta. Savarni, Shani, Tapati and Vishti were Chhaya’s children and Prabha’s son was Prabhata. (The names do not tally across the…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 23, "quote": "12.19The Solar Dynasty The sun-god, Vivasvana, was the son of Kashyapa and Aditi. He had four wives, Samjna, Rajni, Prabha and Chhaya. Samjna’s son was Vaivasvata Manu. Rajni’s children were Yama, yamuna and Revan"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-20"><data key="title">The Lunar Dynasty</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Budha was the son of the moon-god Chandra. Budha married Ila and they had a son named Pururava. Pururava married the apsara Urvashi and they had six sons. One of these sons was Ayu and amongst Ayu’s descendants was a king named Yayati. Yayati had two wives. The first was Devayani, daughter of Shukracharya, the…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 23, "quote": "12.20The Lunar Dynasty Budha was the son of the moon-god Chandra. Budha married Ila and they had a son named Pururava. Pururava married the apsara Urvashi and they had six sons. One of these sons was Ayu and among"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-21"><data key="title">Durjaya and Urvashi</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There used to be a king named Durjaya. He was learned in the shastras and a good king. His wife was a beautiful and good woman. One day, King Durjaya went to the banks of the river Kalindi. There he met the apsara Urvashi and fell in love with her. He married Urvashi and lived with her for many years. After several…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 24, "quote": "12.21Durjaya and Urvashi There used to be a king named Durjaya. He was learned in the shastras and a good king. His wife was a beautiful and good woman. One day, King Durjaya went to the banks of the river Kalindi."}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-22"><data key="title">Krshna’s Tapasya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Krishna was the eight incarnation of Vishnu and he was born as the son of Devaki and Vasudeva. Initially, Krishna did not have any sons Desirous of obtaining a son, Krishna went to visit the sage Upamanyu. The sage’s hermitage was beautiful. Wonderous were the trees and flowers and grew there. The constant chanting of…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 25, "quote": "12.22Krshna’s Tapasya Krishna was the eight incarnation of Vishnu and he was born as the son of Devaki and Vasudeva. Initially, Krishna did not have any sons Desirous of obtaining a son, Krishna went to visit the"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-23"><data key="title">The Yugas (Eras)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">You probably remember that there are four yugas or eras - satya yuga or krita yuga, treta yuga, dvapara yuga and kali yuga. Lomaharshana next told the sages about the characteristics of these four eras. Kali yuga is the worst of the four eras. People are sinful and forget the dharma of the four varnas and the four…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 26, "quote": "12.23The Yugas (Eras) You probably remember that there are four yugas or eras - satya yuga or krita yuga, treta yuga, dvapara yuga and kali yuga. Lomaharshana next told the sages about the characteristics of thes"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-24"><data key="title">Lingas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A linga is an image of Shiva. There are several wonderful lingas in the wonderful city of Varanasi. There is a gigantic linga named Omkara. Amongst other famous lingas located in the city are Krittivaseshvara, Madhyadeshvara, Vishveshvara and Kaparddishvara. The Kurma Purana recites the glories of these lingas. It…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 26, "quote": "12.24Lingas A linga is an image of Shiva. There are several wonderful lingas in the wonderful city of Varanasi."}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-25"><data key="title">Geography and Astronomy</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">It is hoped that you have not forgotten that Svayambhuva Manu had a son named Priyavrata. Priyavrata had ten sons. Their names were Agnidhra, Agnivahu, Vapushmana, Dyutimana, Medha, Medhatithi, Bhavya, Savana, Putra and Jyotishmana. Medha, Agnivahu and Putra had no desire to rule. They were not interested in material…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 27, "quote": "12.25Geography and Astronomy It is hoped that you have not forgotten that Svayambhuva Manu had a son named Priyavrata. Priyavrata had ten sons. Their names were Agnidhra, Agnivahu, Vapushmana, Dyutimana, Medha, Medhatit"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-26"><data key="title">Manvataras</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">You already know what a manvantara is. The titles of the seven great sages (saptarshi), the names of the gods and the title of Indra change from the manvantara to another. In the present kalpa (cycle), six manvantaras have passed. The first Manu was Svayambhuva. The second Manu was Svarochisha. The gods then were the…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 28, "quote": "12.26Manvataras You already know what a manvantara is. The titles of the seven great sages (saptarshi), the names of the gods and the title of Indra change from the manvantara to another. In the present kal"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-27"><data key="title">Vedavyasa</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">In every dvapara yuga, a Vedavyasa is born so as to divide the Vedas and disseminate their knowledge. In the present era, there have been twenty-eight dvapara yugas and there have therefore been twenty-eight individuals who have held the title of Vedavyasa. The Kurma Purana gives their names as follows. (1)…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 28, "quote": "12.27Vedavyasa In every dvapara yuga, a Vedavyasa is born so as to divide the Vedas and disseminate their knowledge. In the present era, there have been twenty-eight dvapara yugas and there have therefore"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-28"><data key="title">Shiva’s Incarnations</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Most Puranas only mention Vishnu’s incarnations (avataras). The Kurma Purana is one of the rate ones which mentions Shiva’s incarnations. In each kali yuga, Shiva has had an incarnation. The names of these incarnations are as follows. (1) Shveta. (2) Sutara. (3) Madana. (4) Suhotra. (5) Kankana. (6) Lokakshi. (7)…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 29, "quote": "12.28Shiva’s Incarnations Most Puranas only mention Vishnu’s incarnations (avataras). The Kurma Purana is one of the rate ones which mentions Shiva’s incarnations. In each kali yuga, Shiva has had an incarnation. The"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-29"><data key="title">The Ishvara Gita</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">The Ishvara Gita section turns to Shiva’s instruction on spiritual knowledge. It offers a bridge between devotional narrative and philosophical/yogic teaching.</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 30, "quote": "12.29The Ishvara Gita While Lomaharshana was reciting the Kurma Purana to the assembled sages, Krishna Dvaipayana Vedavyasa arrived on the scene. Lomaharshana and the other sages requested Vedavyasa to instruct t"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-30"><data key="title">Rituals</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sacred thread (upavita) ceremony is very important and must always be performed at eight year of age. Brahma had created the cotton tree so that sacred threads might be made out of cotton. But on occasions it is permissible to make sacred threads out of grass. A guru (teacher) is always to be respected and…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 31, "quote": "12.30Rituals The sacred thread (upavita) ceremony is very important and must always be performed at eight year of age. Brahma had created the cotton tree so that sacred threads might be made out of cotto"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-31"><data key="title">The Sita who was an Illusion</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A substitute or illusory Sita is distinguished from the Sita protected through divine intervention. This is a narrative variant in the supplied retelling, not a claim that every Rama tradition uses it.</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 33, "quote": "12.31The Sita who was an Illusion It is certain that you know the story of the Ramayana and you therefore also known that Ravana, the king of lanka, abducted Sita, Rama’s wife. But you certainly do not know the story of the"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-32"><data key="title">Shiva and Brahma</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Many years ago, Brahma lost his head slightly. He began to imagine that he was superior to Shiva and Vishnu. He told all the sages, “I am the supreme godhead. There is no one else but me.” While Brahma was thus instructing the sages, Vishnu arrived and was enraged at Brahma’s behaviour. “You are indeed ignorant,” he…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 33, "quote": "12.32Shiva and Brahma Many years ago, Brahma lost his head slightly. He began to imagine that he was superior to Shiva and Vishnu. He told all the sages, “I am the supreme godhead. There is no one else but me.” W"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-33"><data key="title">Nandi</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There used to be a sage named Shilada. He was righteous and learned in the shastras. To obtain a son, Shilada began to pray to shiva. The tapasya went on for a thousand years and shiva appeared before Shilada. “I am pleased with your meditation.” said shiva, “what boon do you desire?” “Please grant me the boon that I…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 34, "quote": "12.33Nandi There used to be a sage named Shilada. He was righteous and learned in the shastras. To obtain a son, Shilada began to pray to shiva. The tapasya went on for a thousand years and shiva appea"}]</data></node><node id="p-kurma-puran-12-34"><data key="title">Epilogue</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Vishnu completed his recital of the Kurma Purana and the sages saluted him. They sung his praise. The Kurma Purana is most sacred. a person who reads it attains Brahmaloka. A person who reads only one chapter of the text is forgiven all his sins. Undying punya is attained by an individual who donates this Purana to…</data><data key="doc">kurma-puran</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 35, "quote": "12.34Epilogue Vishnu completed his recital of the Kurma Purana and the sages saluted him. They sung his praise. The Kurma Purana is most sacred. a person who reads it attains Brahmaloka. A person who read"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-1"><data key="title">Linga Purana Part 1</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This section contains 14 sub-sections (to follow):</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 1, "quote": "17.1 Linga Purana Part 1 This section contains 14 sub-sections (to follow):"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-1-1"><data key="title">Sutji Narrates the Divine Tales of Linga Purana</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, sage, Narad arrived at Naimisharanya where he found many sages engaged in austerities. All the sages were delighted to see him. They eulogized him in reverence and offered him seat. Sage Narad narrated some amazing tales describing about the greatness of Linga Puran. This made the sages even more curious about…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 1, "quote": "17.1.1 Sutji Narrates the Divine Tales of Linga Purana Once, sage, Narad arrived at Naimisharanya where he found many sages engaged in austerities. All the sages were delighted to see him. They eulogized him in reverence and offered him seat. Sa"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-1-2"><data key="title">The Beginning of Creation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The whole creation lasts for the total period equivalent to Brahma's day. The almighty God created during daytime and annihilated during night. One day of Lord Brahma is equivalent to one Kalpa of this world. The cycle of four Yugas occur periodically for 1000 times, during which period altogether fourteen Manus take…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 2, "quote": "17.1.2 The Beginning of Creation The whole creation lasts for the total period equivalent to Brahma's day. The almighty God created during daytime and annihilated during night. One day of Lord Brahma is equivalent to one Ka"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-1-3"><data key="title">The Origin of Creation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">In the initial phase of his creation, Lord Brahma created immovable natural things like trees, mountains etc-then birds and animals. In the process of creation, he created various things and human being was his seventh creation. First of all Lord Brahma created his Manasputras-Sanak, Sanandan etc, who had no worldly…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 3, "quote": "17.1.3 The Origin of Creation In the initial phase of his creation, Lord Brahma created immovable natural things like trees, mountains etc-then birds and animals. In the process of creation, he created various things and"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-1-4"><data key="title">Lord Shiva – The Supreme Liberator</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sati- daughter of Daksha had married lord Shiva much against the wish of her father. Once, Daksha organized a grand yagya in which he did not invite his son-in-law. Sati went to attend the yagya ceremony inspite of Shiva's forbiddance. After seeing her, Daksha calumniated Lord Shiva and made fun of him. This…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 3, "quote": "17.1.4 Lord Shiva – The Supreme Liberator Sati- daughter of Daksha had married lord Shiva much against the wish of her father. Once, Daksha organized a grand yagya in which he did not invite his son-in-law. Sati went to attend the y"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-1-5"><data key="title">Incarnations of Manu &amp; Vyas</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">On being asked about the incarnation of Sage Vyas during various eras and Manvantars, Sutji replied-O Brahmin! Now I am going to mention the names of all the incarnations of Vyas who existed during Vaivasvat manvantar of Varah kalp. The names are as follows-Ritu, Satya, Bhargava, Angira, Savita, Mrityu, Shatakratu,…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 4, "quote": "17.1.5 Incarnations of Manu &amp; Vyas On being asked about the incarnation of Sage Vyas during various eras and Manvantars, Sutji replied-O Brahmin! Now I am going to mention the names of all the incarnations of Vyas who existed"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-1-6"><data key="title">The Essence of Yoga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Yoga means union with God. The state of yoga is impossible to attain without the blessings of Lord Shiva. It needs a concentrated and focussed mind. There are some specific spots in the human body concentrating upon which, enables a man to attain the state of yoga-spot between the eyebrows, lower part of the throat,…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 4, "quote": "17.1.6 The Essence of Yoga Yoga means union with God. The state of yoga is impossible to attain without the blessings of Lord Shiva. It needs a concentrated and focussed mind. There are some specific spots in the huma"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-1-7"><data key="title">Obstacles in the Path of Yoga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A man experiences numerous obstacles in the path of Yoga-laziness, restlessness confusion, a diseased body etc. The main reason for being lazy is a bulky physique and one's inability to concentrate his mind. Lack of concentration results in restlessness, which is a major obstacle in the path of yoga. If a person in…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 4, "quote": "17.1.7 Obstacles in the Path of Yoga A man experiences numerous obstacles in the path of Yoga-laziness, restlessness confusion, a diseased body etc. The main reason for being lazy is a bulky physique and one's inability to conc"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-1-8"><data key="title">Bali – The Realization of Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva showers his blessing on one and all without any discrimination, but virtuous people have special privilege in the sense that it is easier for them to realize Lord Shiva.Once, Parvati asked Lord Shiva as to how a devotee could realize him. Lord Shiva recalled an incident when Lord Brahma had asked the same…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 5, "quote": "17.1.8 Bali – The Realization of Shiva Lord Shiva showers his blessing on one and all without any discrimination, but virtuous people have special privilege in the sense that it is easier for them to realize Lord Shiva.Once, Parv"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-1-9"><data key="title">Venerableness of Sadyojaat</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages asked Sutji as to how was lord Brahma able to have a divine glimpse of Sadyojaat. Sutji replied-During the Kalpa named Shwetalohit, once lord Brahma witnessed the manifestation of a divine child who was of mixed (red &amp; white) complexion. The child had a Shikha (topknot) on his head. Considering him to be an…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 5, "quote": "17.1.9 Venerableness of Sadyojaat The sages asked Sutji as to how was lord Brahma able to have a divine glimpse of Sadyojaat. Sutji replied-During the Kalpa named Shwetalohit, once lord Brahma witnessed the manifestation of"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-1-10"><data key="title">Lord Vaamdev</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, during thirtieth kalpa named Rakta, Lord Brahma was meditating on Parameshthi Shiva. Suddenly a divine child appeared before him who was of red complexion and who had put on red apparels. He was none other than Lord Vaamdev. Lord Brahma immediately realized the divinity of that child and was convinced that he…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 5, "quote": "17.1.10 Lord Vaamdev Once, during thirtieth kalpa named Rakta, Lord Brahma was meditating on Parameshthi Shiva. Suddenly a divine child appeared before him who was of red complexion and who had put on red appare"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-1-11"><data key="title">Lord Tatpurush</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The thirty-first kalpa was known as Peet Kalpa. The term 'Peet' means yellow and this Kalpa was named Peet because Lord Brahma had put on yellow apparels during this Kalpa. Once, while he was meditating on the form of Lord Shiva, a divine child manifested before him. The child had a halo of light all around him and…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 5, "quote": "17.1.11 Lord Tatpurush The thirty-first kalpa was known as Peet Kalpa. The term 'Peet' means yellow and this Kalpa was named Peet because Lord Brahma had put on yellow apparels during this Kalpa. Once, while he wa"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-1-12"><data key="title">Lord Aghoresh</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Peet kalpa was followed by Pradhritta kalpa. Black was the predominant colour of this kalpa. Initially, the whole earth was submerged in the water and Lord Brahma desirous of commencing his creations wished for a son. As a result, a divine child manifested whose complexion was dark. Not only that he had put on black…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 6, "quote": "17.1.12 Lord Aghoresh Peet kalpa was followed by Pradhritta kalpa. Black was the predominant colour of this kalpa. Initially, the whole earth was submerged in the water and Lord Brahma desirous of commencing his"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-1-13"><data key="title">Lord Ishaan</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">In the beginning of Vishwaroopa kalpa, lord Brahma, desirous of commencing creation was engrossed in deep meditation. This resulted into the manifestation of a divine cow, which puzzled lord Brahma very much. Actually, this divine cow was none other then Vishwaroopa Saraswati, but lord Brahma failed to recognize her.…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 6, "quote": "17.1.13 Lord Ishaan In the beginning of Vishwaroopa kalpa, lord Brahma, desirous of commencing creation was engrossed in deep meditation. This resulted into the manifestation of a divine cow, which puzzled lord"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-1-14"><data key="title">The Origin of Shiva Linga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Brahma and Vishnu encounter the immeasurable linga. The episode connects their dispute with Shiva’s manifestation as a cosmic sign.</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 6, "quote": "17.1.14 The Origin of Shiva Linga The sages asked Sutji--- Lord Shiva is formless then how come Shiva Linga is worshipped? What is the significance of Shiva Linga? What is the proper method of worshipping a Shiva Linga ? Sut"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-2"><data key="title">Linga Purana Part 2</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This section contains 13 sub-sections (to follow):</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 7, "quote": "17.2 Linga Purana Part 2 This section contains 13 sub-sections (to follow):"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-2-1"><data key="title">Lord Shiva Blesses Brahma and Vishnu</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">At the time, when this incident of mystical Shiva linga had occurred and after which Lord Brahmaand Vishnu had stopped quarelling, Lord Shiva appeared before them and said--- "I am very pleased with both of you, Both of - " O Vishnu! You are the creator, Lord Shiva. This made Lord Shiva extremely pleased and he the…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 7, "quote": "17.2.1 Lord Shiva Blesses Brahma and Vishnu At the time, when this incident of mystical Shiva linga had occurred and after which Lord Brahmaand Vishnu had stopped quarelling, Lord Shiva appeared before them and said--- \"I am very plea"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-2-2"><data key="title">The Manifestation of Lord Brahma</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The Sages requested Sutji to elucidate how Lord Brahma emerged from the navel of Vishnu seated on a lotus flower. Sutji replied--- At the time of deluge Lord Vishnu went in his yoganidra lying on the back of Sheshnag. As the result of his divine play, a huge lotus flower along with a long tubular stalk emerged from…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 7, "quote": "17.2.2 The Manifestation of Lord Brahma The Sages requested Sutji to elucidate how Lord Brahma emerged from the navel of Vishnu seated on a lotus flower. Sutji replied--- At the time of deluge Lord Vishnu went in his yoganidra lyi"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-2-3"><data key="title">Lord Brahma Becomes the Creator</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">At last, Lord Vishnu was successful in convincing Brahma of Shiva's supreme status. Both of them eulogized and sang hymns in the praise of Lord Shiva.Lord Shiva became very pleased by their devotion. He requested Lord Vishnu wanted nothing else but undiluted devotion in him. Lord Shiva agreed and said "So be it". Lord…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 8, "quote": "17.2.3 Lord Brahma Becomes the Creator At last, Lord Vishnu was successful in convincing Brahma of Shiva's supreme status. Both of them eulogized and sang hymns in the praise of Lord Shiva.Lord Shiva became very pleased by their"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-2-4"><data key="title">Description of Different Kalpas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva revealed to Brahma that the Shwet-kalpa derived its name from his complexion. "I took incarnation attired in white apparels. My complexion was fair. You being self-begotten were able to recognize me. Goddess Gayatri had also manifested from my body and later on became famous as 'Shwet-Lohit-Brahm'." "The…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 8, "quote": "17.2.4 Description of Different Kalpas Lord Shiva revealed to Brahma that the Shwet-kalpa derived its name from his complexion. \"I took incarnation attired in white apparels. My complexion was fair. You being self-begotten were a"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-2-5"><data key="title">Incarnations of Shiva During Various Dvapar Yugas</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">On being enquired by Lord Brahma about his various incarnations, Lord Shiva said --- "I shall incarnate as Shwet-Mahamuni during 1st Dwapar yuga of Vaivaswat manvantar. My abode shell is at Chhogal peak of Himalaya mountain. I shall have four disciples who will be proficient in the Vedas." "I will incarnate as Sutar…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 8, "quote": "17.2.5 Incarnations of Shiva During Various Dvapar Yugas On being enquired by Lord Brahma about his various incarnations, Lord Shiva said --- \"I shall incarnate as Shwet-Mahamuni during 1st Dwapar yuga of Vaivaswat manvantar. My abode shell is at"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-2-6"><data key="title">Rituals of Shiva-Linga Worship (Purity)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, Goddess Parvati asked Lord Shiva about the proper methods of worshipping Shiva-linga. While Shiva was elucidating upon the rituals, Nandi who was present there too got enlightened on this sacred topic. In course of time, this knowledge was passed on to Sanat Kumar, who in turn revealed it to Sage Vyas. Sutji…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 10, "quote": "17.2.6 Rituals of Shiva-Linga Worship (Purity) Once, Goddess Parvati asked Lord Shiva about the proper methods of worshipping Shiva-linga. While Shiva was elucidating upon the rituals, Nandi who was present there too got enlightened on t"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-2-7"><data key="title">Lord Shiva Tests the Patience of Sages</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once upon a time, the sages were busy doing their penance to please Lord Mahadev. Lord Shiva decided to test patience and visited daruk forest in naked position. By the virtue of his divine power, he was successful in corrupting the conduct of the sage's wives. Those women became so infatuated by his divine appearance…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 10, "quote": "17.2.7 Lord Shiva Tests the Patience of Sages Once upon a time, the sages were busy doing their penance to please Lord Mahadev. Lord Shiva decided to test patience and visited daruk forest in naked position. By the virtue of his divine"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-2-8"><data key="title">Sage Shweta</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages were very curious to know about sage Shwet. Lord Brahma narrated the following tale --- "Once, there lived a sage named Shwet. He was a great devotee of Lord Shiva. He lived in a cave and engaged himself in severe penance, continuously chanting the holy name of Shiva. When the time of his death approached,…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 11, "quote": "17.2.8 Sage Shweta The sages were very curious to know about sage Shwet. Lord Brahma narrated the following tale --- \"Once, there lived a sage named Shwet. He was a great devotee of Lord Shiva. He lived in a c"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-2-9"><data key="title">Sage Eulogizes Lord Shiva</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Having realized their mistake, the sages returned to Daruk forest and engaged themselves in austere penance to please Shiva. They did a tremendous penance, which lasted for a year. Ultimately their wished were fulfilled as Lord Shiva appeared with ashes smeared all over his body. He had a begging- bowl in his hands…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 11, "quote": "17.2.9 Sage Eulogizes Lord Shiva Having realized their mistake, the sages returned to Daruk forest and engaged themselves in austere penance to please Shiva. They did a tremendous penance, which lasted for a year. Ultimatel"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-2-10"><data key="title">Lord Shiva Preaches the Sages</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva revealed to the sages that a female sex organ was nothing but a symbolic expression of goddess Parvati where as a male sex organ symbolized his own self. He also cautioned them against making fun of hermit's appearance. He said-"These hermits who appear absured by their looks are my great devotees. They…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 11, "quote": "17.2.10 Lord Shiva Preaches the Sages Lord Shiva revealed to the sages that a female sex organ was nothing but a symbolic expression of goddess Parvati where as a male sex organ symbolized his own self. He also cautioned them ag"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-2-11"><data key="title">Dadhichi Subjugates the Sages (Kshoopa)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There ruled a king named Kshoopa whose friend was Sage Dadhichi. Once they got into argument over the superiority of their respective castes. King Kshoopa was of the opinion that a Kshetriya was superior to Brahmin. Very soon, the dispute took an ugly turn and a duel broke out between them. Dadhich punched Kshoopa on…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 12, "quote": "17.2.11 Dadhichi Subjugates the Sages (Kshoopa) There ruled a king named Kshoopa whose friend was Sage Dadhichi. Once they got into argument over the superiority of their respective castes. King Kshoopa was of the opinion that a Kshetriya"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-2-12"><data key="title">Kshoopa Accepts Defeat</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When Lord Vishnu appeared before Kshoopa, he sought his help in defeating Dadhichi. Lord Vishnu expressed his inability by saying--- I don't punish a Brahmin, especially if he is a devotee of Rudra. Dadhichi is both, so forget about defeating him. Anyhow, I will try me best to make Dadhichi devoid of his power. Lord…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 12, "quote": "17.2.12 Kshoopa Accepts Defeat When Lord Vishnu appeared before Kshoopa, he sought his help in defeating Dadhichi. Lord Vishnu expressed his inability by saying--- I don't punish a Brahmin, especially if he is a devotee o"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-2-13"><data key="title">Shilad’s Impossible Demand</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, Shilad did an austere penance to please Indra. He was desirous of begetting a son. When Indra appeared he requested him to bless him with a son who was self-born as well as deathless. Indra expressed his helplessness in fulfilling impossible demand. Even Lord Brahma meets his end after the passage of crores of…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 13, "quote": "17.2.13 Shilad’s Impossible Demand Once, Shilad did an austere penance to please Indra. He was desirous of begetting a son. When Indra appeared he requested him to bless him with a son who was self-born as well as deathless."}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-3"><data key="title">Linga Purana Part 3</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This section contains 13 sub-sections (to follow):</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 13, "quote": "17.3 Linga Purana Part 3 This section contains 13 sub-sections (to follow):"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-3-1"><data key="title">The Characteristics of the Four Eras</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shilad requested Indra to shed light on the special characteristics of each of the four eras. Indra replied---Lord Brahma created the four eras (yugas)--Satya, Treta, Dwapar and Kali. There eras keep on occuring in a cyclic order where the former is followed by the latter.Saoguna (Pure) prevails during Satyayuga.…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 13, "quote": "17.3.1 The Characteristics of the Four Eras Shilad requested Indra to shed light on the special characteristics of each of the four eras. Indra replied---Lord Brahma created the four eras (yugas)--Satya, Treta, Dwapar and Kali. There"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-3-2"><data key="title">Kali Yuga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing about the decline in moral values during Kaliyuga, Indra told Shilad-- In Kaliyuga, people will be under the total influence of the worldly illusions. Ignorance diseases, fear and hunger will be prevalent everywhere. Famine and drought would occur quite frequently as the fall out of inadequate rain. Sinners…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 13, "quote": "17.3.2 Kali Yuga Describing about the decline in moral values during Kaliyuga, Indra told Shilad-- In Kaliyuga, people will be under the total influence of the worldly illusions. Ignorance diseases, fear and"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-3-3"><data key="title">Manifestation of Lord Brahma</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, Lord Brahma did an austere penance to please Lord Shiva. Shiva manifestated as Ardhnarishwar (Partly male partly female) from his forehead and said--'I am your son'. But, the unberabale heat emanating from the effulgence of Ardhanarishwar burnt the whole world including Lord Brahma. Lord Shiva then separated the…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 14, "quote": "17.3.3 Manifestation of Lord Brahma Once, Lord Brahma did an austere penance to please Lord Shiva. Shiva manifestated as Ardhnarishwar (Partly male partly female) from his forehead and said--'I am your son'. But, the unberabal"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-3-4"><data key="title">Manifestation of Lord Nandishwar</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Shilad did a tremendous penance to please Lord Shiva. His penance continued for thousands of year. At last, Lord Shiva appeared before him and said--"I am extremely pleased by you devotion. I shall bless you with a son who will become famous for his knowledge and learning." But, Shilad was desirous of a self-born and…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 14, "quote": "17.3.4 Manifestation of Lord Nandishwar Shilad did a tremendous penance to please Lord Shiva. His penance continued for thousands of year. At last, Lord Shiva appeared before him and said--\"I am extremely pleased by you devotion."}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-3-5"><data key="title">The Nether World</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva is the source from where all the different worlds originate. People in their ignorance are unware of the fact that the whole universe is nothing but a medium through which Shiva manifests himself.The various worlds like Maha, Jana, Tapa, Satya etc. and all the natural e;ements like earth, Sky ocean etc, owe…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 14, "quote": "17.3.5 The Nether World Lord Shiva is the source from where all the different worlds originate. People in their ignorance are unware of the fact that the whole universe is nothing but a medium through which Shiva m"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-3-6"><data key="title">The Seven Islands</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The earth consists of seven islands, many rivers, mountains and seven oceans. The Plaksha, Shaalmali, Kush, Kraunch, Shaak and Pushkar. Lord Shiva alongwith goddess Ambika and his ganas dwell in all these seven islands in one form or the other. The name of the seven oceans are Ksharod, Rasod, Surod, Ghatodakshi,…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 14, "quote": "17.3.6 The Seven Islands The earth consists of seven islands, many rivers, mountains and seven oceans. The Plaksha, Shaalmali, Kush, Kraunch, Shaak and Pushkar. Lord Shiva alongwith goddess Ambika and his ganas dwel"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-3-7"><data key="title">Bharat Varsh</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Agnighra--the ruler of Jambudweepa was a great devotee of Lord Shiva. He had nine sons--Nabhi, Kimpurush, Harivarsha, Ilavrata, Ramya, Hiranyamaan, Kuru, Bhadrashva and Ketumaal. Agnighra appointed Nabhi the king of Hemakhya. Similarly, Kimpurush, Harivarsha, Ilavrata, Ramya, Hiranyaman, Kuru, Bhadrashva and Ketumaal…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 15, "quote": "17.3.7 Bharat Varsh Agnighra--the ruler of Jambudweepa was a great devotee of Lord Shiva. He had nine sons--Nabhi, Kimpurush, Harivarsha, Ilavrata, Ramya, Hiranyamaan, Kuru, Bhadrashva and Ketumaal. Agnighra ap"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-3-8"><data key="title">The Meru Mountain</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Meru mountain is situated in the heart of Jambudweepa. The Meru range is spread in the area of 16 thousand yojan. It's towering peak is 84 thousand in height from the ground level and had even penetrated the depth of earth to the measurement of 16 thousand yojan. Meru mountain is considered to be very sacred and is…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 15, "quote": "17.3.8 The Meru Mountain Meru mountain is situated in the heart of Jambudweepa. The Meru range is spread in the area of 16 thousand yojan. It's towering peak is 84 thousand in height from the ground level and had ev"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-3-9"><data key="title">The Prominent Mountains</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing about the physical characteristics os all tje seven islands, Sutji says --- "Seven mountains are situated in Plaksha dweepa-Gobhedak, Chaandra, Narad, Dundubhi, Soyak, Sumana and Vaibhraj. Similarly, the prominent mountain ranges situated in Shaalmali dweepa are Kumud, Uttam, Balahak, Drona, Karkamahish…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 15, "quote": "17.3.9 The Prominent Mountains Describing about the physical characteristics os all tje seven islands, Sutji says --- \"Seven mountains are situated in Plaksha dweepa-Gobhedak, Chaandra, Narad, Dundubhi, Soyak, Sumana and"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-3-10"><data key="title">The Sun</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The movement of Sun is rapid when it is positioned south of equator (Dakshinayan), on the other hand its movement is slow when positioned north os equator (Uttarayan).The time is indicated by the various positions of Sun while it is on its course of movement in the horizon. While the sun is situated in the South-East…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 16, "quote": "17.3.10 The Sun The movement of Sun is rapid when it is positioned south of equator (Dakshinayan), on the other hand its movement is slow when positioned north os equator (Uttarayan).The time is indicated b"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-3-11"><data key="title">Lord Brahma assigns lordships to the Deities</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The Sages requested Sutji to describe how Lord Brahma assigned Lordships to all the deities and the demons. Sutji replied--- The Sun was made the lord of all the planets. Soma was made the lord of all the constellations and medicinal herbs. Similarly, Vaun was made the Lord of water, while Kuber was assigned the…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 16, "quote": "17.3.11 Lord Brahma assigns lordships to the Deities The Sages requested Sutji to describe how Lord Brahma assigned Lordships to all the deities and the demons. Sutji replied--- The Sun was made the lord of all the planets. Soma was made the l"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-3-12"><data key="title">The Chariots of the Sun, Moon and Planets</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The chariot of Surya is believed to have been created by Lord Brahma. It is made up of gold and its wheels have five spokeseach. The chariot is very huge in size and its expanse covers the area of nine thousand yojan. It is pulled by seven horses which are of green complexion. The movement of Sun results into the…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 16, "quote": "17.3.12 The Chariots of the Sun, Moon and Planets The chariot of Surya is believed to have been created by Lord Brahma. It is made up of gold and its wheels have five spokeseach. The chariot is very huge in size and its expanse covers the a"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-3-13"><data key="title">The Radiance of Surya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The Surya is the source to which all the living beings owe their existence. All the deities derive their effulgence from Surya, who in turn derives its own radiance from Lord Mahadev.Time is determined with the help of Surya's movement. One can not even imagine the various units of time, like Kshana, muhurta day,…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 17, "quote": "17.3.13 The Radiance of Surya The Surya is the source to which all the living beings owe their existence. All the deities derive their effulgence from Surya, who in turn derives its own radiance from Lord Mahadev.Time is"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-4"><data key="title">Linga Purana Part 4</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This section contains 12 sub-sections (to follow):</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 17, "quote": "17.4 Linga Purana Part 4 This section contains 12 sub-sections (to follow):"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-4-1"><data key="title">Dhruva – The Supreme Devotee</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The Sages asked Sutji as to how Dhruva became successful in attaining immortality and supreme status among all the stars. Sutji narrated the following tale--- There lived a king named Uttanpad, who had two queens--Suniti and Suruchi. The eldest queen Suniti had a son named Dhruva. One day while Dhruva was sitting in…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 17, "quote": "17.4.1 Dhruva – The Supreme Devotee The Sages asked Sutji as to how Dhruva became successful in attaining immortality and supreme status among all the stars. Sutji narrated the following tale--- There lived a king named Uttanp"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-4-2"><data key="title">The Origin of Deities</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">On being enquired by the sages about the origin of deities, demons and other celestial entities, Sutji said-- Daksha being desirous of enhancing the population, created ten-thousand sons merely by his wish and instructed them to contribute in the propagation of population. His objective remained unfulfilled as Narad…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 17, "quote": "17.4.2 The Origin of Deities On being enquired by the sages about the origin of deities, demons and other celestial entities, Sutji said-- Daksha being desirous of enhancing the population, created ten-thousand sons mer"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-4-3"><data key="title">Sage Parashar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The Sages asked Sutji how Shakti had been devoured by a demon named Rudhir. Sutiji replied--- Once, Sage Vashishth was presiding over a grand Yagya being organized by a king named Kalmashpad. Vashishth had a rivalry with Vishwamitra. On seeing Vashishth busy in that Yagya, Vishwamitra instigated Rudhir-- a demon, to…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 18, "quote": "17.4.3 Sage Parashar The Sages asked Sutji how Shakti had been devoured by a demon named Rudhir. Sutiji replied--- Once, Sage Vashishth was presiding over a grand Yagya being organized by a king named Kalmashpad"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-4-4"><data key="title">The Lineage of Aditya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">On being enquired by the Sages about the respective lineage of Aaditya (Sun) and Soma (moon) Sutji replied--- Sage Kashyap had a son--Aaditya, from Aditi. Aadity had four wives--Sangya, Rajyi, Prabha and Chhaya. Sangya gave birth to Manu whereas Ragyi was the mother of Yama, Yamuna and Raivat. Prabha gave birth to…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 18, "quote": "17.4.4 The Lineage of Aditya On being enquired by the Sages about the respective lineage of Aaditya (Sun) and Soma (moon) Sutji replied--- Sage Kashyap had a son--Aaditya, from Aditi. Aadity had four wives--Sangya, Rajy"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-4-5"><data key="title">The Yadu Dynasty</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Yadu had five sons among whom Sahastrajeet was the eldest. Shatarjay was the oson of Sahastrajeet and himself had three sons--Haihya, Haya and Venu. Dharma was Haihya's son. Dharmanetra was the son of Dharma and himself had two sons--Kirti and Sanjay.Sanjay's son was Mahishman and Bhadrashrenya was Mahishman's son.…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 19, "quote": "17.4.5 The Yadu Dynasty Yadu had five sons among whom Sahastrajeet was the eldest. Shatarjay was the oson of Sahastrajeet and himself had three sons--Haihya, Haya and Venu. Dharma was Haihya's son. Dharmanetra was"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-4-6"><data key="title">Sri Krshna</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Aahuka came from the lineage of Yadu. He had two sons-- Devak andUgrasen. The eldest son, Devak had eleven children--four sons and seven daughters. The names of his sons were-- Devavaan, Upadeva, Sudeva and Devarakshit. All his seven daughters, which also included Devaki were married to Vasudeva. Aahuk's youngest son,…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 20, "quote": "17.4.6 Sri Krshna Aahuka came from the lineage of Yadu. He had two sons-- Devak andUgrasen. The eldest son, Devak had eleven children--four sons and seven daughters. The names of his sons were-- Devavaan, Upa"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-4-7"><data key="title">Lord Brahma blesses the Demons</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The Sages asked Sutji as to how did Maya--The demon acquire three well fortified forts from Lord Brahma and how was lord Shiva finally successful in destroying them. Sutji replied-- After Tarakasur's death his three sons, Vidyunmali, Kamlaksha and Tarakaksha did tremendous penance to please Lord Brahma. When Lord…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 20, "quote": "17.4.7 Lord Brahma blesses the Demons The Sages asked Sutji as to how did Maya--The demon acquire three well fortified forts from Lord Brahma and how was lord Shiva finally successful in destroying them. Sutji replied-- After Ta"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-4-8"><data key="title">Appointment of Andhak as Lord of the Ganas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">On being asked by the Sages how Andhak--the demon was appointed as the lord of Ganas by Lord Shiva, Sutji narrated the following tale to them.--The mighty demon, Andhak was Hiranyaksh's son. By the virtue of his austere penance he had received a boon of immortality from Lord Brahma. In course of time he conquered all…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 21, "quote": "17.4.8 Appointment of Andhak as Lord of the Ganas On being asked by the Sages how Andhak--the demon was appointed as the lord of Ganas by Lord Shiva, Sutji narrated the following tale to them.--The mighty demon, Andhak was Hiranyaksh's son."}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-4-9"><data key="title">The Liberation of Earth</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The Sages were curious to know how Lord Vishnu in his incarnation of Varah (Boar), had liberated the earth from the captivity of Hiranyaksha. They also wanted to know why Lord Shiva adorned himself with the horn of that boar. Sutji narrated the following tale--- Hiranyaksha--Andhak's father was a tyrant. After…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 21, "quote": "17.4.9 The Liberation of Earth The Sages were curious to know how Lord Vishnu in his incarnation of Varah (Boar), had liberated the earth from the captivity of Hiranyaksha. They also wanted to know why Lord Shiva adorned"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-4-10"><data key="title">Killing of Jalandhar</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The Sages requested Sutji to reveal how Lord Shiva had killed the notorious demon-- Jalandhar. Sutji narrated the following tale--- There was a demon named Jalandhar. He had manifested from the ocean. He had acquired divine power by the virtue of his austere penance.Jalandhar had defeated the deities and conquered the…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 21, "quote": "17.4.10 Killing of Jalandhar The Sages requested Sutji to reveal how Lord Shiva had killed the notorious demon-- Jalandhar. Sutji narrated the following tale--- There was a demon named Jalandhar. He had manifested from"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-4-11"><data key="title">The Origin of Lord Ganesh</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, the deities requested Lord Shiva to device some means so that they no longer had to live under the threat of the demons.The most merciful Lord Pinaki Shiva then took the incarnation of Ganeshwar. He had a human body but his heard resembled like an elephant. He held a trident in his hand. All the deities…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 22, "quote": "17.4.11 The Origin of Lord Ganesh Once, the deities requested Lord Shiva to device some means so that they no longer had to live under the threat of the demons.The most merciful Lord Pinaki Shiva then took the incarnation of"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-4-12"><data key="title">Shiv Tandav</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The deities were curious to know the story behind 'Tandav'- the famous dance of Lord Shiva. So, they asked Sutji how it started and what made Shiva to perform Tandav. Sutji replied--- There lived a demon named Daruk. He had acquired divine powers by the virtue of his austere penance. He started tormenting te deities,…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 22, "quote": "17.4.12 Shiv Tandav The deities were curious to know the story behind 'Tandav'- the famous dance of Lord Shiva. So, they asked Sutji how it started and what made Shiva to perform Tandav. Sutji replied--- There"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-5"><data key="title">Linga Purana Part 5</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This section contains 13 sub-sections (to follow):</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 22, "quote": "17.5 Linga Purana Part 5 This section contains 13 sub-sections (to follow):"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-5-1"><data key="title">Upamanyu</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The sages were curious to know how Lord Shiva had bestowed the designation of Gaanpatya and an ocean of milk to Upamanyu. They requested Sutji to shed light on the two incidents. Sutji narrated the following tale --- Upamanyu was a brilliant child. There is an interesting story how he became the supreme devotee of…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 22, "quote": "17.5.1 Upamanyu The sages were curious to know how Lord Shiva had bestowed the designation of Gaanpatya and an ocean of milk to Upamanyu. They requested Sutji to shed light on the two incidents. Sutji narra"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-5-2"><data key="title">The Greatness of Dwadashakshar Mantra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">On being asked by the sages about the sacred mantra-- the chanting of which enabled a man to attain salvation Sutji replied --- Lord Vishnu gives salvation to those people who endeavour to seek Brahm-- the Almighty God. A man whose mind is preoccupied with the thought of Lord Vishnu and nothing else is entitled to…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 23, "quote": "17.5.2 The Greatness of Dwadashakshar Mantra On being asked by the sages about the sacred mantra-- the chanting of which enabled a man to attain salvation Sutji replied --- Lord Vishnu gives salvation to those people who endeavour to s"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-5-3"><data key="title">Shadakshar Mantra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing about the greatness of Shadakshar mantra that consists of six letters, Sutji said -- The sacred mantra -- OM NAMAH SHIVAY holds a supreme place among all the mantras. During the Meghavahan kalp of the first Treta yuga, there lived a brahmin named Dhoondhmook. He became a licentious person due to the curse…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 24, "quote": "17.5.3 Shadakshar Mantra Describing about the greatness of Shadakshar mantra that consists of six letters, Sutji said -- The sacred mantra -- OM NAMAH SHIVAY holds a supreme place among all the mantras. During the M"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-5-4"><data key="title">Lord Pashupati</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages were curious to know why Lord Shiva was also known as Pashupati-- The lord of all animals. Sutji retold the same tale which sage Shiladi had once narrated to Sanatkumar --- Once, Sanatkumar went to the hermitage of Sage Shiladi and asked him as to why was Lord Shankar also known as Pashupati. He also…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 24, "quote": "17.5.4 Lord Pashupati The sages were curious to know why Lord Shiva was also known as Pashupati-- The lord of all animals. Sutji retold the same tale which sage Shiladi had once narrated to Sanatkumar --- Once, S"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-5-5"><data key="title">The Majestic Lord Maheshwar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Maheshwar is beyond the reach if any kind of bondage. He is totally liberated entity. It is only by his blessings that the Nature-Prakriti, creates intelligence--buddhi. Similarly, intelligence creates ego- ahamkara and subtle forms of matter--tanmatra creates the greatest of matter by the blessings of Lord…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 24, "quote": "17.5.5 The Majestic Lord Maheshwar Lord Maheshwar is beyond the reach if any kind of bondage. He is totally liberated entity. It is only by his blessings that the Nature-Prakriti, creates intelligence--buddhi. Similarly, inte"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-5-6"><data key="title">The Sun – Manifestation of Shiva’s Power</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva manifests himself in the form of Sun. The Sun radiates twelve different types of rays which have specific impacts and influences on the world. The first ray is called Amrit and the deities derive their power from it. The second ray is called Chandra and it helps in the preservation of medicinal herbs. The…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 25, "quote": "17.5.6 The Sun – Manifestation of Shiva’s Power Lord Shiva manifests himself in the form of Sun. The Sun radiates twelve different types of rays which have specific impacts and influences on the world. The first ray is called Amrit and th"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-5-7"><data key="title">Shiva Tatva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The learned and sages call Shiva by many names --- Kshetragya, Prakriti Vyakta, Kalatma etc. The synonyms of Kshetragya and Prakriti are Purush and Pradhan respectively.The delusionary net of the Prakriti is known as Vyakta (apparent). Kala (time) is the resultant by product of Pradhan and Vyakta. Some sages call…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 25, "quote": "17.5.7 Shiva Tatva The learned and sages call Shiva by many names --- Kshetragya, Prakriti Vyakta, Kalatma etc. The synonyms of Kshetragya and Prakriti are Purush and Pradhan respectively.The delusionary net o"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-5-8"><data key="title">The Greatness of Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The almighty Shiva manifested in the form of Sthanu (Pillar) from his imperceptible form and looked at Lord Brahma. Lord Brahma then commenced his creation and established the norms of Vannashram. He also created 'Soma' which is required for the performance of 'Yagya'. This world, including all the deities are the…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 25, "quote": "17.5.8 The Greatness of Shiva The almighty Shiva manifested in the form of Sthanu (Pillar) from his imperceptible form and looked at Lord Brahma. Lord Brahma then commenced his creation and established the norms of Vanna"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-5-9"><data key="title">The Importance of the Guru</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A devotee should commence the worship of Lord Shiva only after taking permission from his Guru, Only such a person is worthy of being called a Guru who is virtuous and well versed in the scripture. A disciple should unwavering by be in the service of his Guru and should try to please him by his devotion because only…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 26, "quote": "17.5.9 The Importance of the Guru A devotee should commence the worship of Lord Shiva only after taking permission from his Guru, Only such a person is worthy of being called a Guru who is virtuous and well versed in the scr"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-5-10"><data key="title">The Rituals of Initiation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Before commencing the rituals of initiation, the Guru should purify the place where the rites are supposed to be performed. He should make an attar and draw an eight-petalled lotus at its centre. He should then establish the various embodiments of Shiva like Aghor, Pranav, Vamadeva, on each of the eight petals. The…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 26, "quote": "17.5.10 The Rituals of Initiation Before commencing the rituals of initiation, the Guru should purify the place where the rites are supposed to be performed. He should make an attar and draw an eight-petalled lotus at its ce"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-5-11"><data key="title">Installation of Shiva Linga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing about the methods of installing a Shiva Linga Sutji says-- A devotee should install a Shivalinga made of either gold, silver or copper. The shivalinga should be installed along with the altar. The altar of the Shivalinga symbolizes goddess Uma. Lord Brahma is said to manifest at the base of Shivalinga while…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 26, "quote": "17.5.11 Installation of Shiva Linga Describing about the methods of installing a Shiva Linga Sutji says-- A devotee should install a Shivalinga made of either gold, silver or copper. The shivalinga should be installed along wi"}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-5-12"><data key="title">Vrajeshwari Vidya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages requested Sutji to elaborate upon the rituals of Vajravahanika Vidya or Vajreshwari Vidya. Sutji replied--- Anybody who is desirous of Vanquishing his enemy must perform the rituals of Vajreshwari Vidya. The devotee should worship Vajra (a spear) and make offerings in the sacrificial fire holding it (Vajra)…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 26, "quote": "17.5.12 Vrajeshwari Vidya The sages requested Sutji to elaborate upon the rituals of Vajravahanika Vidya or Vajreshwari Vidya."}]</data></node><node id="p-linga-purana-17-5-13"><data key="title">Different Types of Yoga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages asked Sutji as to how should a devotee meditate on Lord Triyambak following the path of Yoga. Sutji then retold the tale which Mandishwar had once narrated to Sanatkumar-- 1. The first type of Yoga is called Mantra Yoga. In this type of yoga a devotee tries to attain deep state of meditation by chanting…</data><data key="doc">linga-purana</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 27, "quote": "17.5.13 Different Types of Yoga The sages asked Sutji as to how should a devotee meditate on Lord Triyambak following the path of Yoga. Sutji then retold the tale which Mandishwar had once narrated to Sanatkumar-- 1. The f"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-1"><data key="title">Introduction</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once Jaimini, a disciple of sage Vedavyasa expressed his curiosity before Markandeya- 'O Lord! In the great epic Mahabharat, which was created by Vedavyasa, description of Dharma, Arth, Kama and Moksha appears to be intertwined at times and at other times, it appears to be separate from one another. Vedavyasa had…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "7.1 Introduction Once Jaimini, a disciple of sage Vedavyasa expressed his curiosity before Markandeya- 'O Lord! In the great epic Mahabharat, which was created by Vedavyasa, description of Dharma, Arth, Kama"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-2"><data key="title">Various Creations and Famous Mythological Characters</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "7.2 Various Creations and Famous Mythological Characters"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-2-1"><data key="title">Birth of Four Birds</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says - 'In the lineage of the avian King Garud, there were two brothers- Kank and Kandhar. One day, Kank visited Kailash Parbat where a demon Vidrayudrup, who was a slave of Kuber, was enjoying privacy with his wife and drinking wine. Seeing Kank, the demon showed his displeasure at his arrival at an…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "7.2.1 Birth of Four Birds Markandeya says - 'In the lineage of the avian King Garud, there were two brothers- Kank and Kandhar. One day, Kank visited Kailash Parbat where a demon Vidrayudrup, who was a slave of Kuber"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-2-2"><data key="title">Birds Migrate to Vindhyachal</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sage Shami thereafter began to foster the chicks in his hermitage. With time, the chicks grew feathers and began to fly here and there. But every time the birds went out, they returned to the hermitage by evening. The birds also gained Vedic knowledge by hearing the discourses, which sage Shami used to give daily to…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "7.2.2 Birds Migrate to Vindhyachal Sage Shami thereafter began to foster the chicks in his hermitage. With time, the chicks grew feathers and began to fly here and there. But every time the birds went out, they returned to th"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-2-3"><data key="title">Divine Plays of God</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sage Jaimini reached Vindhyachal and arrived before the birds as per Markandeya's instructions. He said- 'O birds! I am Jaimini, the disciple of Vedavyasa. I have come here with a desire to have your glimpse.' The birds welcomed him saying- 'It is our great fortune that you have arrived here. Your arrival has made us…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "7.2.3 Divine Plays of God Sage Jaimini reached Vindhyachal and arrived before the birds as per Markandeya's instructions. He said- 'O birds! I am Jaimini, the disciple of Vedavyasa. I have come here with a desire to"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-2-4"><data key="title">Draupadi’s Five Husbands</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The birds say- 'Frightened by the severe penance of Trishira, Indra killed him to protect his authority and power. But the sin of killing a Brahmin took away Indra's radiance. Trishira was the son of Twashta. Twashta became furious at his son's death. He plucked one strand of hair from his hair-lock and offered it in…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "7.2.4 Draupadi’s Five Husbands The birds say- 'Frightened by the severe penance of Trishira, Indra killed him to protect his authority and power. But the sin of killing a Brahmin took away Indra's radiance. Trishira was t"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-2-5"><data key="title">Expiation of Balaram</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shri Krishna's brother Balaram had remained impartial in the battle of Mahabharat. Instead of championing the cause either for the Pandavas or for the Kauravas, he had preferred to go on a pilgrimage. His wife Revati also accompanied him on this pilgrimage. Once while travelling, Balaram drank toddy and entered a…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "7.2.5 Expiation of Balaram Shri Krishna's brother Balaram had remained impartial in the battle of Mahabharat. Instead of championing the cause either for the Pandavas or for the Kauravas, he had preferred to go on a p"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-2-6"><data key="title">Killing of Draupadi’s Sons</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">In Treta Yug, there was a king named Harishchandra. Once he was hunting in the Mahabaahu forest when suddenly, he heard loud cries of a woman- 'Save me! Save me!' It was followed by the cries of many women. Hearing those cries, King Harishchandra shouted loudly- 'Don't be afraid' and dashed in the direction of the…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "7.2.6 Killing of Draupadi’s Sons In Treta Yug, there was a king named Harishchandra. Once he was hunting in the Mahabaahu forest when suddenly, he heard loud cries of a woman- 'Save me! Save me!' It was followed by the crie"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-2-7"><data key="title">King Harishchandra</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After being thrown out from his kingdom by Vishwamitra, Harishchandra reached the holy town of Varanasi, which was an abode of Lord Mahadev. There Harishchandra saw Vishwamitra standing before him. Vishwamitra said- 'A month has been completed now. Now, give me my Dakshina.' Harishchandra said- 'There is still half an…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "7.2.7 King Harishchandra After being thrown out from his kingdom by Vishwamitra, Harishchandra reached the holy town of Varanasi, which was an abode of Lord Mahadev. There Harishchandra saw Vishwamitra standing befo"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-2-8"><data key="title">The Stork and the Partridge</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The birds say- O great sage Jaimini! When, King Harishchandra attained his heavenly abode, their family priest, sage Vashishta re-emerged from water. He came to know about the entire development. He was angry at Vishwamitra's stubbornness. He at once cursed Vishwamitra to become a stork. On the other hand Vishwamitra…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 8, "quote": "7.2.8 The Stork and the Partridge The birds say- O great sage Jaimini! When, King Harishchandra attained his heavenly abode, their family priest, sage Vashishta re-emerged from water. He came to know about the entire develop"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-2-9"><data key="title">Birth of Living Creatures</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Jaimini says- O great birds, how does a living being take birth? How does it grow in the womb bearing all the pain? How does it grow after taking birth? What happens to the soul after death? How does it experience the fruits of his Karmas? The greatest surprise is how does a little foetus survive in the womb. Kindly…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 8, "quote": "7.2.9 Birth of Living Creatures Jaimini says- O great birds, how does a living being take birth? How does it grow in the womb bearing all the pain? How does it grow after taking birth? What happens to the soul after death?"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-3"><data key="title">Description of Different Hells</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 9, "quote": "7.3 Description of Different Hells"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-3-1"><data key="title">Introduction</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The Brahmin says- O son! Describe in detail about hell. Sumati says- O father! Yamadoots carry and lynch those people who eat inedible things, who deceive and dishonour their friends, who indulge in illicit relationships, who desert their wife, and who destroy public properties like garden, water sources etc.…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 9, "quote": "7.3.1 Introduction The Brahmin says- O son! Describe in detail about hell. Sumati says- O father! Yamadoots carry and lynch those people who eat inedible things, who deceive and dishonour their friends, who in"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-3-2"><data key="title">Yamadoot and the King of Videha</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Sumati says- I was born in a Vaishya family, seven births before this present one. In that birth, I once prevented cows from drinking water. As a result of this sin, I was thrown in the hell named Daarun where I spent one hundred years without a drop of water. Suddenly one day, cool pleasant wind began to blow, its…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 9, "quote": "7.3.2 Yamadoot and the King of Videha Sumati says- I was born in a Vaishya family, seven births before this present one. In that birth, I once prevented cows from drinking water. As a result of this sin, I was thrown in the hell"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-3-3"><data key="title">Description of Tortures in Hell</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Thus asked by the gentleman, Yamadoots replied politely- 'O king! You once deliberately prevented your wife Pivari from conceiving because you were more attracted to your second wife- Sushobhana. It is because of that action that you have been brought here to undergo severe torture.' The religious minded king said- '…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 9, "quote": "7.3.3 Description of Tortures in Hell Thus asked by the gentleman, Yamadoots replied politely- 'O king! You once deliberately prevented your wife Pivari from conceiving because you were more attracted to your second wife- Sushob"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-3-4"><data key="title">Attenuation of Sins</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Yamadoot says- As a result of accepting money from a degraded person, a Brahmin takes birth as an ass. A Brahmin who carries out Yagya for a degraded person takes birth as worm after undergoing severe sufferings in different types of hell. A person takes birth as an ass or an inferior bird as a result of abusing his…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 10, "quote": "7.3.4 Attenuation of Sins Yamadoot says- As a result of accepting money from a degraded person, a Brahmin takes birth as an ass. A Brahmin who carries out Yagya for a degraded person takes birth as worm after undergo"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-4"><data key="title">Famous Mythological Characters</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 10, "quote": "7.4 Famous Mythological Characters"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-4-1"><data key="title">Greatness of Being Chaste (and birth of Durvasa, Dattatreya, Chandrama)</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Brahmin says- O son! Tell me, what should I do now? Sumati says- Now, you should become a Vanprasthi and lead a life of a Bhikshu by having control over your senses. You will attain that rare Yoga, which helps a man to unite with the almighty after freeing him from the cycles of birth, death and re-birth. Brahmin…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 11, "quote": "7.4.1 Greatness of Being Chaste (and birth of Durvasa, Dattatreya, Chandrama) Brahmin says- O son! Tell me, what should I do now? Sumati says- Now, you should become a Vanprasthi and lead a life of a Bhikshu by having control over your senses. You will attain that rar"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-4-2"><data key="title">Dattatreya Preaches Kartaveerya Arjuna</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sumati says- O father! After the demise of Kaartaveerya, his ministers and priests decided to crown his son Arjun as their new king. But Arjun refused saying that he would not become the king unless and until he had attained the status of a Yogi. Sage Garg then advised Arjun to worship Dattatreya who had helped the…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 12, "quote": "7.4.2 Dattatreya Preaches Kartaveerya Arjuna Sumati says- O father! After the demise of Kaartaveerya, his ministers and priests decided to crown his son Arjun as their new king. But Arjun refused saying that he would not become the kin"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-4-3"><data key="title">Kuvalayashva and the Horse Kuvalaya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sumati says- O father! Now listen to the tale of a Brahmin named Alark. Long ago, there lived a king named Shatrujit who had a meritorious son, Ritudhwaj. Prince Ritudhwaj had many friends with whom he used to play with. In course of time, two sons of Ashwatar, the king of Nagas became his friends in the guise of…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 12, "quote": "7.4.3 Kuvalayashva and the Horse Kuvalaya Sumati says- O father! Now listen to the tale of a Brahmin named Alark. Long ago, there lived a king named Shatrujit who had a meritorious son, Ritudhwaj. Prince Ritudhwaj had many friends w"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-4-4"><data key="title">Kuvalaya goes to Patal Loka, Killing of Demon Patal Ketu and Marriage with Madalasa</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Prince Ritudhwaj stayed in the hermitage of sage Gaalav and helped him to perform his Yagyas without any problem by clearing all the minor obstacles. The demons were not aware of his arrival in the hermitage. One day, a demon arrived in the hermitage disguised as a wild boar and began to rub his bushy head against the…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 12, "quote": "7.4.4 Kuvalaya goes to Patal Loka, Killing of Demon Patal Ketu and Marriage with Madalasa Prince Ritudhwaj stayed in the hermitage of sage Gaalav and helped him to perform his Yagyas without any problem by clearing all the minor obstacles. The demons were not aware of his arrival"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-4-5"><data key="title">Separation of Madalasa</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The newly-wed couple had hardly spent few months together when King Shatrujit once again instructed Ritudhwaj to travel across the entire earth and see whether the Brahmins were performing their religious duties without any obstacles. Ritudhwaj obeyed the dictates of his father. He used to scale vast distances for the…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 13, "quote": "7.4.5 Separation of Madalasa The newly-wed couple had hardly spent few months together when King Shatrujit once again instructed Ritudhwaj to travel across the entire earth and see whether the Brahmins were performing t"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-4-6"><data key="title">Madalasa is Reborn</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When prince Ritudhwaj reached the capital, everyone expressed his pleasure with surprise. Ritudhwaj met everyone happily and went to the palace of his parents. Though the king and the queen blessed him but sad expression on their face surprised the prince. Upon enquiry, the king related the entire incident and also…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "7.4.6 Madalasa is Reborn When prince Ritudhwaj reached the capital, everyone expressed his pleasure with surprise. Ritudhwaj met everyone happily and went to the palace of his parents. Though the king and the queen"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-4-7"><data key="title">Ritudhwaj Reunites with Madalasa</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Ritudhwaj stayed in the palace of Ashwatar. Naagraj did his best to keep Ritudhwaj happy and forget the sorrow of his wife's separation. During his stay, Ashwatar tried many times to coax Ritudhwaj into revealing his heartiest desire. But every time Ritudhwaj intelligently avoided the topic. Once, King Ashwatar, in…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 15, "quote": "7.4.7 Ritudhwaj Reunites with Madalasa Ritudhwaj stayed in the palace of Ashwatar. Naagraj did his best to keep Ritudhwaj happy and forget the sorrow of his wife's separation. During his stay, Ashwatar tried many times to coax Ri"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-4-8"><data key="title">Birth of Madalasa’s Sons</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After returning to his father's kingdom, Ritudhwaj narrated the whole incident to his parents of how he regained Madalasa. Then they began to live happily. After many years, King Shatrujit crowned Ritudhwaj as the king. After King Ritudhwaj's accession to the throne, Madalasa gave birth to her first son. Ritudhwaj…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 15, "quote": "7.4.8 Birth of Madalasa’s Sons After returning to his father's kingdom, Ritudhwaj narrated the whole incident to his parents of how he regained Madalasa. Then they began to live happily. After many years, King Shatrujit c"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-4-9"><data key="title">Preachings of Madalasa</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Madalasa teaches Alarka how a king should govern: protect subjects, examine advisers, restrain desire and avoid complacency. The surrounding narrative connects family instruction with later yoga teaching.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 16, "quote": "7.4.9 Preachings of Madalasa Madalasa used to preach the little prince while played. When Alark attained boyhood, he asked- 'O mother! What should I do in order to mastery over physical and metaphysical matters? How can"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-5"><data key="title">Duties of People Belonging to Various Ashrams</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 16, "quote": "7.5 Duties of People Belonging to Various Ashrams"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-5-1"><data key="title">Varnashram Dharma</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Alark says- 'Tell me about the duties of the different classes and stages of life.' Madalasa says- 'Donation, study and Yagya, these three are the religion of a Brahmin. Performing Yagya for others, teaching and accepting donation are the three vocations of Brahmin. Donation, study and Yagya are also the religious…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 16, "quote": "7.5.1 Varnashram Dharma Alark says- 'Tell me about the duties of the different classes and stages of life.' Madalasa says- 'Donation, study and Yagya, these three are the religion of a Brahmin. Performing Yagya for"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-5-2"><data key="title">Duties of a Grihastha (Householder)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Alark says- What are the duties of a married man by discharging which he becomes free from all kinds of bondage and attains salvation. Kindly tell me about these things. Madalasa says- O son! Those people who discharge their duties as a householder by fostering all the living beings attain to the heaven automatically.…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 17, "quote": "7.5.2 Duties of a Grihastha (Householder) Alark says- What are the duties of a married man by discharging which he becomes free from all kinds of bondage and attains salvation. Kindly tell me about these things. Madalasa says- O son"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-6"><data key="title">Shradha and its Rituals</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 17, "quote": "7.6 Shradha and its Rituals"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-6-1"><data key="title">Regular and Causal Duties</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Madalasa says- O son! A Grihastha has three kinds of duties- regular, causal and a combination of both. Rituals, which are connected with oblations and carried out daily, are called regular duties. Rituals and consecrations performed at the birth of a child are called causal duties. Yearly performance of Shraadh for…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 17, "quote": "7.6.1 Regular and Causal Duties Madalasa says- O son! A Grihastha has three kinds of duties- regular, causal and a combination of both. Rituals, which are connected with oblations and carried out daily, are called regular"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-6-2"><data key="title">Dos and Don’t During a Shraadha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Madalasa says- O son! I am now narrating about the do's and don'ts to be followed during Shraadh. Many kinds of edible items can be offered in the Shraadh. These include cereals like barley, wheat, rice, millet, corn, etc., fish, flesh of deer, rabbit, bird, wild boar, goat and Neelgai (antelope). All these kinds of…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 18, "quote": "7.6.2 Dos and Don’t During a Shraadha Madalasa says- O son! I am now narrating about the do's and don'ts to be followed during Shraadh. Many kinds of edible items can be offered in the Shraadh. These include cereals like barley,"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-6-3"><data key="title">Auspicious Days and Months</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Madalasa says- Shraadh can be performed on any day during the dark lunar phase in the Hindu month of Ashwin i.e. from the first day to the Amavasya. Performing of Shraadh on any of these days brings the following benefits- Performing Shraadh on the first day or Pratipada brings monetary gain. Shraadh performed on the…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 19, "quote": "7.6.3 Auspicious Days and Months Madalasa says- Shraadh can be performed on any day during the dark lunar phase in the Hindu month of Ashwin i.e. from the first day to the Amavasya. Performing of Shraadh on any of these day"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-6-4"><data key="title">Forbidden and Permitted Items</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Madalasa says- O son! Now, I will tell you the do's and don'ts for healthy living. Stale cereals, oil and fats stored over a prolonged period of time, items prepared from wheat and barley and food items which is not prepared from pure ghee or milk should not be consumed. Flesh of turtle, porcupine etc. is worth eating…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 20, "quote": "7.6.4 Forbidden and Permitted Items Madalasa says- O son! Now, I will tell you the do's and don'ts for healthy living. Stale cereals, oil and fats stored over a prolonged period of time, items prepared from wheat and barley an"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-7"><data key="title">King Alark and his queries about Yoga</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "7.7 King Alark and his queries about Yoga"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-7-1"><data key="title">King Alark Receives a Ring</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After reaching youth-hood, Prince Alark got married. By that time, his father King Ritudhwaj had also grown old. Hence before Grihastha, he crowned his son as the new king. At the time of crowning, queen Madalasa presented him a ring and said - 'O son! This ring contains a note which will teach you on how a king…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "7.7.1 King Alark Receives a Ring After reaching youth-hood, Prince Alark got married. By that time, his father King Ritudhwaj had also grown old. Hence before Grihastha, he crowned his son as the new king. At the time of cr"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-7-2"><data key="title">Wisdom of Alark</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After his accession to the throne, King Alark ruled justly. In due course of time, he got many sons. He followed the dictates of Dharma, Arth and Kama while ruling his kingdom. Inspite of enjoying all the luxuries, Alark still could not experience bliss and felt as if he was missing something in his life. Very soon,…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "7.7.2 Wisdom of Alark After his accession to the throne, King Alark ruled justly. In due course of time, he got many sons. He followed the dictates of Dharma, Arth and Kama while ruling his kingdom. Inspite of en"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-7-3"><data key="title">Dattatreya Preaches Alark</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Dattatreya instructs Alarka in detachment and realization. Follow the adjacent yoga sections for concentration, Om, practice and its obstacles.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "7.7.3 Dattatreya Preaches Alark Alark says- 'O lord! With the arising of disenchantment in my heart, I have no miseries now. Only those people feel drowned in the ocean of miseries who are attached to worldly things. A man"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-7-4"><data key="title">Yogadhyay</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Dattatreya says- O king! With the attainment of knowledge, people come to conjugate with the Supreme Almighty and which results into dispersion of their ignorance. To attain Moksha, it is necessary for a man to shun attachment first of all. Only after that, he will become free from sorrows. When he becomes free from…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 22, "quote": "7.7.4 Yogadhyay Dattatreya says- O king! With the attainment of knowledge, people come to conjugate with the Supreme Almighty and which results into dispersion of their ignorance. To attain Moksha, it is ne"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-7-5"><data key="title">Perfection in Yoga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Dattatreya says-During the process of conquering the soul, different kinds of allurements begins to divert the mind of the Yogis. It is imperative for the Yogi to keep his mind busy by observing fast, worshipping and contemplating in God. It is the duty of the Yogi to always contemplate on God, only then he can seek…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 22, "quote": "7.7.5 Perfection in Yoga Dattatreya says-During the process of conquering the soul, different kinds of allurements begins to divert the mind of the Yogis. It is imperative for the Yogi to keep his mind busy by obser"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-7-6"><data key="title">Daily Routine of a Yogi</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Alark says- O lord, now kindly narrate about the daily routine, a Yogi should abide by in order to preserve his piousness. Dattatreya says- O king! Respect and insult are the two reasons for love and hatred. Yogis attain perfection by understanding insult as respect and vice versa. A Yogi should therefore never attend…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 22, "quote": "7.7.6 Daily Routine of a Yogi Alark says- O lord, now kindly narrate about the daily routine, a Yogi should abide by in order to preserve his piousness. Dattatreya says- O king! Respect and insult are the two reasons for"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-7-7"><data key="title">Description of Omkar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Dattatreya says- Those Yogis who abide by their resolution are never degraded from their supreme position. Such Yogis always recite Om while contemplating on the eternal God. 'A', 'U' and 'M', these three syllables constitute the body OM or AUM. These three syllables represent the virtues (gunas) of Sata, Tama and…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 23, "quote": "7.7.7 Description of Omkar Dattatreya says- Those Yogis who abide by their resolution are never degraded from their supreme position. Such Yogis always recite Om while contemplating on the eternal God. 'A', 'U' and 'M"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-7-8"><data key="title">Description of Disastrous Traps</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Dattatreya says- A person who is unable to see the path of the deities, or the heavenly bodies like- Dhruv, Shukra (Venus), Soma (Moon), or his own shadow or Goddess Arundhati, must understand that his death is near. For those people to whom, the Sun appears without radiance but fire appears as the Sun, die within…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 23, "quote": "7.7.8 Description of Disastrous Traps Dattatreya says- A person who is unable to see the path of the deities, or the heavenly bodies like- Dhruv, Shukra (Venus), Soma (Moon), or his own shadow or Goddess Arundhati, must understa"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-7-9"><data key="title">Alark Masters Yoga and Penance of Jada and his Father</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Words of Alark pleased the king of Kashi. Even, Subahu got up gladly and after embracing Alark said to the king of Kashi- "O king, I had taken your refuge to win the kingdom. Now, I have got it without fighting a war and without a drop of blood being shed. But, I am leaving now, renouncing everything." The king of…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 24, "quote": "7.7.9 Alark Masters Yoga and Penance of Jada and his Father Words of Alark pleased the king of Kashi. Even, Subahu got up gladly and after embracing Alark said to the king of Kashi- \"O king, I had taken your refuge to win the kingdom. Now, I have got"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-8"><data key="title">The Universe and Lord Brahma’s Manifestation</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 24, "quote": "7.8 The Universe and Lord Brahma’s Manifestation"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-8-1"><data key="title">The Origin of the Universe</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Jaimini thanked the birds for enlightening him with self-knowledge. But he was still not satisfied. There were many questions to which he had no answers. He asked the birds- 'How did this universe come into being? What will happen to it during the time of final annihilation? How did the deities, the sages, the…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 24, "quote": "7.8.1 The Origin of the Universe Jaimini thanked the birds for enlightening him with self-knowledge. But he was still not satisfied. There were many questions to which he had no answers. He asked the birds- 'How did this un"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-8-2"><data key="title">Lord Brahma’s Life Span</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Kraustuki said- 'Tell me! O lord! How were the living beings created as the period of final annihilation was approaching its end.' Markandeya replied- 'When the nature establishes herself in the soul of the Purusha (supreme Almighty), all the created matter gets annihilated. Prakriti and Purusha are established in the…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 25, "quote": "7.8.2 Lord Brahma’s Life Span Kraustuki said- 'Tell me! O lord! How were the living beings created as the period of final annihilation was approaching its end.' Markandeya replied- 'When the nature establishes herself in"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-8-3"><data key="title">Natural and Flawed Creations</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Kraustuki asked curiously- 'Tell me how Brahma did his creations?' Markandeya replied- 'When Brahma got up from his sleep after the end of dissolution (Pralay) named Padrama, he found a void all around himself. He remembered Narayana who is also known by the name of Nastanu i.e. one who dwells in water. Narayana was…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 26, "quote": "7.8.3 Natural and Flawed Creations Kraustuki asked curiously- 'Tell me how Brahma did his creations?' Markandeya replied- 'When Brahma got up from his sleep after the end of dissolution (Pralay) named Padrama, he found a void"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-8-4"><data key="title">Creation of the Deities</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Kraustuki then asked Markandeya about the origin of the deities. Markandeya replied- 'With the objective of creating the deities, demons, ancestors and human beings, Brahma abandoned the part of his physical body in the ocean. The demons originated from the thighs of his abandoned body. Lord Brahma blessed the demons…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 27, "quote": "7.8.4 Creation of the Deities Kraustuki then asked Markandeya about the origin of the deities. Markandeya replied- 'With the objective of creating the deities, demons, ancestors and human beings, Brahma abandoned the par"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-8-5"><data key="title">Copulative Creations</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- Brahma created one thousand couples from his mouth in the beginning of creation. These couples were radiant and possessed virtuous qualities (Saatvik). Once again, Brahmaji created one thousand couples from his chest but this time they possessed the quality of Rajas. This phenomenon was repeated for…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 28, "quote": "7.8.5 Copulative Creations Markandeya says- Brahma created one thousand couples from his mouth in the beginning of creation. These couples were radiant and possessed virtuous qualities (Saatvik). Once again, Brahmaji"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-8-6"><data key="title">Daksha’s Lineage</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Brahma on seeing that inspite of all his creations the population was not increasing, he decided to create his Manasputras. These Manasputras were created merely by his wish. Their names were Bhrigu, Pulastya, Pulaha, Kratu, Angira, Marichi, Daksha, Atri and Vashishta. After this, he first created…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 28, "quote": "7.8.6 Daksha’s Lineage Markandeya says- 'Brahma on seeing that inspite of all his creations the population was not increasing, he decided to create his Manasputras. These Manasputras were created merely by his wis"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-8-7"><data key="title">Descendants of Duhsah</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Nirmashti, the wife of Duhsah was the daughter of Yama. Altogether sixteen children were born to Duhsah and Nirmashti. Out of them eight were sons and the remaining eight were daughters. The name of the sons were- Dantakrishti, Tathokti, Parivarta, Angadhruka, Shakuni, Ganda, Pranrati, Garbhaha and…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 29, "quote": "7.8.7 Descendants of Duhsah Markandeya says- 'Nirmashti, the wife of Duhsah was the daughter of Yama. Altogether sixteen children were born to Duhsah and Nirmashti. Out of them eight were sons and the remaining eight w"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-8-8"><data key="title">The Creation of Rudras</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Now I am going to tell you about Rudrasarga. One of the eight sons of Brahma started wailing after being created from his (Brahma) body. Brahma asked him as to why he was crying. The crying child requested Brahma to give him a name. Brahma named him as Rudra since he was wailing at the time of his…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 29, "quote": "7.8.8 The Creation of Rudras Markandeya says- 'Now I am going to tell you about Rudrasarga. One of the eight sons of Brahma started wailing after being created from his (Brahma) body. Brahma asked him as to why he was c"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9"><data key="title">The Various Manavantars</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 30, "quote": "7.9 The Various Manavantars"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-1"><data key="title">Swayambhuva Manavantar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Swayambhuva Manu had ten sons. He had divided the whole earth into seven continents. In the beginning of Treta Yuga, the sons of Priyavrata had done the same. Prajavati- the daughter of Kardam Prajapati had ten sons and two daughters from Priyavrata. Names of these ten sons were- Agnighna,…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 30, "quote": "7.9.1 Swayambhuva Manavantar This Section contains 6 sub-sections. Markandeya says- 'Swayambhuva Manu had ten sons. He had divided the whole earth into seven continents. In the beginning of Treta Yuga, the sons of Priyavrata had done the sa"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-1-1"><data key="title">Jambudveep</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says that the total area of Jambudweep comprises of one lakh Yojan while the area of Plakshdweep is twice the area of Jambudweep i.e. two lakh Yojans. Similarly, Shaalmali is twice the area of Plaksh while Kusha is twice the area of Shaalmali. The area of Kraunchdweep is twice the area of Kushdweep whereas…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 31, "quote": "7.9.1.1 Jambudveep Markandeya says that the total area of Jambudweep comprises of one lakh Yojan while the area of Plakshdweep is twice the area of Jambudweep i.e. two lakh Yojans. Similarly, Shaalmali is twic"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-1-2"><data key="title">The Origin of the Ganga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'River Ganga originates from a place named Dhruvaadhar. This particular place is related with Narayana. River Ganga, then falls on the Sumeru Mountain and gets distributed into four main streams. One of these streams flows towards Chaitrartha forest. The name of this particular stream is Sita. The…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 31, "quote": "7.9.1.2 The Origin of the Ganga Markandeya says- 'River Ganga originates from a place named Dhruvaadhar. This particular place is related with Narayana. River Ganga, then falls on the Sumeru Mountain and gets distributed i"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-1-3"><data key="title">A Description of Bharat-Varsha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Kraustuki asked Markandeya- 'O lord! Please describe about Bharatvarsha.' Markandeya replied- 'Bharatvarsha is surrounded by oceans on all its three sides. People living towards the east of Bharatvarsha are known as Kiraat while people living towards the west are called Yavan. The central part of Bharatvarsha is…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 31, "quote": "7.9.1.3 A Description of Bharat-Varsha Kraustuki asked Markandeya- 'O lord! Please describe about Bharatvarsha.' Markandeya replied- 'Bharatvarsha is surrounded by oceans on all its three sides. People living towards the east of"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-1-4"><data key="title">Tortoise Incarnation</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Kraustuki asked Markandeya- 'O lord! How does Shri Hari live in the form of a tortoise?' Markandeya replied- 'Shri Hari in the form of a tortoise sits facing east. States situated in the central portion of his body are Vedi, Madra, Maandavya, Shaalva, Khaasa, Saraswat, Matsya etc. States which are situated in the…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 32, "quote": "7.9.1.4 Tortoise Incarnation Kraustuki asked Markandeya- 'O lord! How does Shri Hari live in the form of a tortoise?' Markandeya replied- 'Shri Hari in the form of a tortoise sits facing east. States situated in the cen"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-1-5"><data key="title">The Descriptions of Bhadrashva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Bhadraashva Varsh is situated towards the east of the great mountain Devakut. Five more mountains are situated within its area- Kauranj, Shwetaparna, Neela, Shaiwaal and Parnashaalagra. Bhadraashva consists of thousands of Janpadas. Numerous rivers like Sita, Shankhavali, Bhadra and Chakravarta flows…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 32, "quote": "7.9.1.5 The Descriptions of Bhadrashva Markandeya says- 'Bhadraashva Varsh is situated towards the east of the great mountain Devakut. Five more mountains are situated within its area- Kauranj, Shwetaparna, Neela, Shaiwaal and Pa"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-1-6"><data key="title">The Description of Kimpurush-Varsha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'The people residing in Kimpurush Varsh enjoy a long life. They live for 10,000 years. They are never bothered by any kind of disease or sorrow. Just behind Kimpurush Varsh is situated Hari Varsh. The residents of Hari Varsh enjoy an eternally youthful life because of the sugarcane juice they intake.…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 33, "quote": "7.9.1.6 The Description of Kimpurush-Varsha Markandeya says- 'The people residing in Kimpurush Varsh enjoy a long life. They live for 10,000 years. They are never bothered by any kind of disease or sorrow. Just behind Kimpurush Varsh"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-2"><data key="title">The Beginning of Swarochish Manavantar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Conversation between the Brahmin and Varudhini: Kraustuki asked Markandeya- 'O great sage! Now tell me something about Swarochish Manavantar?' Markandeya replied- 'Once upon a time, there lived a Brahmin at the bank of river Varuna. One day, a guest arrived at his place. The Brahmin treated his guest with due respect.…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 33, "quote": "7.9.2 The Beginning of Swarochish Manavantar This Section contains 6 sub-sections. Conversation between the Brahmin and Varudhini: Kraustuki asked Markandeya- 'O great sage! Now tell me something about Swarochish Manavantar?' Markandeya rep"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-2-1"><data key="title">Varudhini’s Modesty Breached</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Agni became pleased by the Brahmin's devotion. Agni entered into his body. The Brahmin's body illuminated due to Agni's radiance. When Varudhini saw this, she was fascinated by the Brahmin's appearance. The Brahmin proceeded towards his home. Feeling helpless, Varudhini cursed her fate.' Varudhini…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 33, "quote": "7.9.2.1 Varudhini’s Modesty Breached Markandeya says- 'Agni became pleased by the Brahmin's devotion. Agni entered into his body. The Brahmin's body illuminated due to Agni's radiance. When Varudhini saw this, she was fascinate"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-2-2"><data key="title">His Marriage with Manorama</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">In due course of time, Varudhini became pregnant. She gave birth to a child who was named Swarochi. The child was extra-ordinary. In a very short time, he became proficient in all the scriptures. One day, young Swarochi saw a terrified girl near the Mandaar Mountain. When the girl saw Swarochi, she pleaded for help.…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 34, "quote": "7.9.2.2 His Marriage with Manorama In due course of time, Varudhini became pregnant. She gave birth to a child who was named Swarochi. The child was extra-ordinary. In a very short time, he became proficient in all the script"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-2-3"><data key="title">Swarochi’s Other Marriages</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'After getting cured from their respective diseases, both the companions of Manorama expressed their gratitude to Swarochi. Swarochi married both Vibhaavari as well as Kalavati. To show her gratitude, Vibhaavari taught Swarochi, a special art that helped him to understand the language of all the…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 34, "quote": "7.9.2.3 Swarochi’s Other Marriages Markandeya says- 'After getting cured from their respective diseases, both the companions of Manorama expressed their gratitude to Swarochi. Swarochi married both Vibhaavari as well as Kalav"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-2-4"><data key="title">Swarochi’s Reproachment</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- After getting married, Swarochi started living happily along with all his three wives at Malaya Mountain. One day, impressed by the mutual love, Swarochi and his wives had for each other, a female ruddy goose told another one- 'Very rarely are found such couples who have love for each other.' But the…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 34, "quote": "7.9.2.4 Swarochi’s Reproachment Markandeya says- After getting married, Swarochi started living happily along with all his three wives at Malaya Mountain. One day, impressed by the mutual love, Swarochi and his wives had f"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-2-5"><data key="title">The Origin of Swarochish Manu</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Swarochi became very sad after hearing the deer's utterances. He realised how lowly and mean he had become. He decided to renounce life after abandoning his wives. But this did not happen because as soon as he met them, he forgot everything about renunciation. He continued to live with his wives. In…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 35, "quote": "7.9.2.5 The Origin of Swarochish Manu Markandeya says- 'Swarochi became very sad after hearing the deer's utterances. He realised how lowly and mean he had become. He decided to renounce life after abandoning his wives. But this"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-2-6"><data key="title">Swarochish Manavantar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'During this Swarochish Manavantar, Indra was famously known as Vipaschit. The other deities were known as Paaraavat and Tushit. The names of the Saptarishis who lived during this Manavantar were Urja, Stamb, Pran, Dutt, Ali, Rishabh, Nischar and Arvaveera. Swarochish Manu had seven sons who were very…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">36</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 36, "quote": "7.9.2.6 Swarochish Manavantar Markandeya says- 'During this Swarochish Manavantar, Indra was famously known as Vipaschit. The other deities were known as Paaraavat and Tushit. The names of the Saptarishis who lived durin"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-3"><data key="title">Auttam Manavantar Begins</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This Section contains 5 sub-sections:</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">36</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 36, "quote": "7.9.3 Auttam Manavantar Begins This Section contains 5 sub-sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-3-1"><data key="title">King Uttam Abandons his Wife</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Kraustuki says- 'O lord! Now tell me about the Auttam Manavantar.' Markandeya replied- 'Uttanpada and Suruchi had a son named Uttam. Uttam was married to Bahula. Bahula did not love Uttam and always showed disrespect towards him. One day, while both of them were sitting in the court, watching the proceedings, Uttam…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">36</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 36, "quote": "7.9.3.1 King Uttam Abandons his Wife Kraustuki says- 'O lord! Now tell me about the Auttam Manavantar.' Markandeya replied- 'Uttanpada and Suruchi had a son named Uttam. Uttam was married to Bahula. Bahula did not love Uttam an"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-3-2"><data key="title">Brahmin’s Wife Returns Home</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'When King Uttam reached Utpalaavat forest, he saw a woman eating a Shriphal fruit. The king asked that woman whether she was the wife of that Brahmin- Susharma. The woman replied in the affirmative and said- 'The demon abducted me while I was sleeping. Since that day, he has kept me here. I am very…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">37</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 37, "quote": "7.9.3.2 Brahmin’s Wife Returns Home Markandeya says- 'When King Uttam reached Utpalaavat forest, he saw a woman eating a Shriphal fruit. The king asked that woman whether she was the wife of that Brahmin- Susharma. The woman r"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-3-3"><data key="title">The Sage and King Uttam</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'King Uttam wondered as to what should he do to get liberated from the sins of abandoning his wife. He requested the sage to help him in this regard. The sage informed the king that his wife was living at Ranatal. The king of the serpents- Kapotak is looking after her. Kapotak has a daughter named…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">37</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 37, "quote": "7.9.3.3 The Sage and King Uttam Markandeya says- 'King Uttam wondered as to what should he do to get liberated from the sins of abandoning his wife. He requested the sage to help him in this regard. The sage informed the k"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-3-4"><data key="title">The Birth of Auttam Manu</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Uttam met the Brahmin and told him that now as he had reunited with his wife, it was now his turn to help him (king) to re-unite with his wife. The Brahmin assured Uttam that he would perform a Yagya named Mitravinda, which would help him to achieve his goal. He requested Uttam to bring his wife so…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">38</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 38, "quote": "7.9.3.4 The Birth of Auttam Manu Markandeya says- 'Uttam met the Brahmin and told him that now as he had reunited with his wife, it was now his turn to help him (king) to re-unite with his wife. The Brahmin assured Uttam th"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-3-5"><data key="title">Auttam Manavantar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'O sage! The following Ganas who reigned during Auttam Manavantar are very famous. They are the first Gana- Swadhama. The second Gana- Satya was related with the deities. The third Gana was Shiva, the fourth was named Pratardan and the fifth Gana was Vashvarti. Each of the Ganas are the master of…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">38</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 38, "quote": "7.9.3.5 Auttam Manavantar Markandeya says- 'O sage! The following Ganas who reigned during Auttam Manavantar are very famous. They are the first Gana- Swadhama. The second Gana- Satya was related with the deities. Th"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-4"><data key="title">Tamas Manavantar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'There used to live a king named Swarashtra. The deity Surya had blessed him with a long life. The king had one hundred wives, but they were not as lucky as he was as far as enjoying a long life was concerned. One by one, all of them died. A king from the neighbouring state named Vimard defeated him…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">38</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 38, "quote": "7.9.4 Tamas Manavantar Markandeya says- 'There used to live a king named Swarashtra. The deity Surya had blessed him with a long life. The king had one hundred wives, but they were not as lucky as he was as far as"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-5"><data key="title">Raivat Manavantar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Now I am going to tell you about Raivat Manu. His father was Ritvaak. Raivat was born in Revati Nakshatra. Later on, he became infamous for his loose character. His father was very worried about him. Ritvaak asked sage Garg as to how such a mean child had been born to him. Sage Garg replied- 'The…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">39</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 39, "quote": "7.9.5 Raivat Manavantar Markandeya says- 'Now I am going to tell you about Raivat Manu. His father was Ritvaak. Raivat was born in Revati Nakshatra. Later on, he became infamous for his loose character. His father"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-6"><data key="title">Chakshush Manavantar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Chaakshush had manifested from the eye (Chakshu) of Lord Brahma in his previous birth. For this reason, he was known as Chaakshush. Chaakshush was born to Anmitra and Giribhadra. After taking birth, he was abducted by Jaathaarini and exchanged with the son of King Vikrant who was sleeping besides his mother. Being…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">39</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 39, "quote": "7.9.6 Chakshush Manavantar Chaakshush had manifested from the eye (Chakshu) of Lord Brahma in his previous birth. For this reason, he was known as Chaakshush. Chaakshush was born to Anmitra and Giribhadra. After takin"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-7"><data key="title">Vaivasvat Manavantar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The Birth of Ashwini Kumars and Revant: Markandeya says- 'The Manu of Vaivasvat Manavantar was famously known as Vaivasvat as he was the son of Vivasvaan (Surya). Surya was married to Sangya- the daughter of Vishwakarma. Although, she had borne two sons (Vaivasvat and Yama) and a daughter (Yami) from Surya, yet the…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">40</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 40, "quote": "7.9.7 Vaivasvat Manavantar The Birth of Ashwini Kumars and Revant: Markandeya says- 'The Manu of Vaivasvat Manavantar was famously known as Vaivasvat as he was the son of Vivasvaan (Surya). Surya was married to Sangya"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-8"><data key="title">The Sages of Saavarnik Manavantar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Kraustuki says- 'O revered sage! You have already enlightened me by revealing the names of seven different Manus of each Manavantar. You have also told me about the deities, the kings and the sages who existed during each Manavantar. Now, I am curious to know about the seven Manus, the deities and the sages of this…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">40</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 40, "quote": "7.9.8 The Sages of Saavarnik Manavantar This Section contains 13 sub-sections. Kraustuki says- 'O revered sage! You have already enlightened me by revealing the names of seven different Manus of each Manavantar. You have also told me about "}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-8-1"><data key="title">Killing of Madhu Kaitabha</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Suratha and the merchant Samadhi ask Medha why attachment persists after betrayal. Medha introduces Mahamaya through the account of Vishnu, Madhu and Kaitabha.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">41</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 41, "quote": "7.9.8.1 Killing of Madhu Kaitabha Markandeya says- 'King Surath was the descendant of Chaitra. He was defeated in the battle against King Kolvidhvansi. All his wealth was deceitfully taken over by his cunning ministers. King"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-8-2"><data key="title">The Destruction of Mahishasura’s Army</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The defeated gods combine their radiance into the Goddess, give her their weapons, and watch her destroy Mahishasura’s forces.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">41</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 41, "quote": "7.9.8.2 The Destruction of Mahishasura’s Army Markandeya says- 'During ancient times, the deities and the demons fought a fierce battle which lasted for a hundred years. The deities were defeated in this battle. Mahishasura drove out th"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-8-3"><data key="title">The Deities Eulogize Goddess Bhagwati</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'After the killing of Mahishasura, all the deities including Indra eulogised Goddess Bhagwati and said- 'We make salutations to her who has manifested herself as a result of the unison of the powers of all the deities, one who is revered by all the deities and the sages. May she bless us. We make…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">42</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 42, "quote": "7.9.8.3 The Deities Eulogize Goddess Bhagwati Markandeya says- 'After the killing of Mahishasura, all the deities including Indra eulogised Goddess Bhagwati and said- 'We make salutations to her who has manifested herself as a result of"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-8-4"><data key="title">Goddess Parvati and Shumbh’s Messenger</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'During ancient times, the demons- Shumbh and Nishumbh had captured all the three worlds after defeating the deities. The deities went to Goddess Parvati and eulogised her by saying- 'O goddess! You had promised us that you would come to our help at the time of our need. The time has arrived.' As the…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">42</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 42, "quote": "7.9.8.4 Goddess Parvati and Shumbh’s Messenger Markandeya says- 'During ancient times, the demons- Shumbh and Nishumbh had captured all the three worlds after defeating the deities. The deities went to Goddess Parvati and eulogised her b"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-8-5"><data key="title">Killing of Dhumralochan</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Sugreev went back to Shumbh and narrated the whole story. Shumbh became very furious and instructed Dhumralochan to bring her by force. Dhumralochan went to Goddess Bhagwati accompanied by 60,000 demons. Initially, he requested her to come along with him. He also threatened to take her forcibly if…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">42</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 42, "quote": "7.9.8.5 Killing of Dhumralochan Markandeya says- 'Sugreev went back to Shumbh and narrated the whole story. Shumbh became very furious and instructed Dhumralochan to bring her by force. Dhumralochan went to Goddess Bhagwat"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-8-6"><data key="title">Killing of Chand and Mund</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chand and Mund proceeded with a huge army. At that time, Goddess Bhagwati had positioned herself at the peak of Himachal Mountain. She was mounted on the back of Simha and was smiling sweetly. Chand and Mund tried to enslave her but suddenly, Goddess Kali appeared from her mouth and ferociously attacked the demons.…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">43</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 43, "quote": "7.9.8.6 Killing of Chand and Mund Chand and Mund proceeded with a huge army. At that time, Goddess Bhagwati had positioned herself at the peak of Himachal Mountain. She was mounted on the back of Simha and was smiling sweetl"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-8-7"><data key="title">Killing of Raktabeej</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Raktabija multiplies from each drop of blood. The Goddess directs Kali to drink the blood, ending the multiplication and defeating him.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">43</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 43, "quote": "7.9.8.7 Killing of Raktabeej Markandeya says- 'When Shumbh got the news of Chand and Mund' s death, he became very furious. He proceeded to fight Goddess Bhagwati accompanied by numerous mighty warriors like Udayudh, Ka"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-8-8"><data key="title">Killing of Nishumbh</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'When Shumbh and Nishumbh heard about Raktabeej's death, they were infuriated. Nishumbh attacked the goddess with a huge army. Shumbh attacked from the other side. In the fierce battle, Goddess Bhagwati broke the sword as well as the shield of Nishumbh. Nishumbh then attacked Goddess Bhagwati with his…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">43</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 43, "quote": "7.9.8.8 Killing of Nishumbh Markandeya says- 'When Shumbh and Nishumbh heard about Raktabeej's death, they were infuriated. Nishumbh attacked the goddess with a huge army. Shumbh attacked from the other side. In the fi"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-8-9"><data key="title">Killing of Shumbh</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When Shumbha says that her strength depends on others, the Goddess absorbs the manifested powers into herself before their final battle.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">44</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 44, "quote": "7.9.8.9 Killing of Shumbh Markandeya says- 'Shumbh became angry at the death of his brother- Nishumbh. He furiously told the goddess- 'O wicked lady! Do not be proud of your strength borrowed from others.' Goddess Bh"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-8-10"><data key="title">Devi Stotra</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- After goddess Bhagwati had killed Shumbh, all the deities under the leadership of Indra and Agni started eulogising her- 'O Goddess! Be pleased up on us! Protect the whole world, as you are the goddess of this whole universe. You appear in the form of this earth- the base on which everything is…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">44</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 44, "quote": "7.9.8.10 Devi Stotra Markandeya says- After goddess Bhagwati had killed Shumbh, all the deities under the leadership of Indra and Agni started eulogising her- 'O Goddess! Be pleased up on us! Protect the whole w"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-8-11"><data key="title">Goddess Bhagwati Blesses the Deities</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Goddess Bhagwati says- I will clear all the obstacles from the path of such a man who worships me. A devotee who listens to the great tales of how I killed Mahishasura, Shumbh and Nishumbh, on the eighth, ninth and fourteenth days of any month, would be liberated from all sins and poverty. He would become free from…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">45</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 45, "quote": "7.9.8.11 Goddess Bhagwati Blesses the Deities Goddess Bhagwati says- I will clear all the obstacles from the path of such a man who worships me. A devotee who listens to the great tales of how I killed Mahishasura, Shumbh and Nishumbh,"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-8-12"><data key="title">Goddess Bhagwati Blesses Surath and the Vaishya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Suratha seeks restoration of kingship; the merchant seeks freedom from attachment. Their different requests bring the Goddess narratives back to the opening question about worldly bonds.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">45</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 45, "quote": "7.9.8.12 Goddess Bhagwati Blesses Surath and the Vaishya The sage says- 'O king! I have described in detail about the greatness of Goddess Bhagwati who is the creator of this universe. She is the one who bestows the knowledge of self to us. She is"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-8-13"><data key="title">Savarna Manavantar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'O great sage! Now I am going to tell you about the Manavantar of Saavarn, which will be the ninth Manavantar. Saavarn was the son of Daksha. During this Manavantar, there will be three prominent groups (Ganas) of deities. Each group would comprise of twelve deities. During this Manavantar, Indra…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">45</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 45, "quote": "7.9.8.13 Savarna Manavantar Markandeya says- 'O great sage! Now I am going to tell you about the Manavantar of Saavarn, which will be the ninth Manavantar. Saavarn was the son of Daksha. During this Manavantar, there w"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-9"><data key="title">Rauchya Manavantar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 46, "quote": "7.9.9 Rauchya Manavantar"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-9-1"><data key="title">Birth of Rauchya Manu</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'As soon as the ancestors departed, a beautiful Apsara came near Ruchi. Her name was Pramlocha. She said to him- 'O great sage! I have a daughter named Maalini. The name of my husband is Pushkar. I want to get her betrothed to you. Please accept her as your wife. In course of time, she would give…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 46, "quote": "7.9.9.1 Birth of Rauchya Manu Markandeya says- 'As soon as the ancestors departed, a beautiful Apsara came near Ruchi. Her name was Pramlocha. She said to him- 'O great sage! I have a daughter named Maalini. The name of"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-9-2"><data key="title">Ruchi Advised to get Married</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'During ancient times, the ancestors of Ruchi were not pleased by his unmarried status. He was a sage of high degree and used to have food once a day. The ancestors asked him- 'O son! Why didn't you get married? Grihasthashram holds a very important place in the life of a man. It is only after paying…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 46, "quote": "7.9.9.2 Ruchi Advised to get Married Markandeya says- 'During ancient times, the ancestors of Ruchi were not pleased by his unmarried status. He was a sage of high degree and used to have food once a day. The ancestors asked hi"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-9-3"><data key="title">Ruchi Eulogizes his Ancestors</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'After the departure of his ancestors, Ruchi started wandering here and there in his restlessness. After sometime, when he regained his composure, he decided to do penance to please Lord Brahma. His penance continued for one hundred years. After the accomplishment of his penance, Lord Brahma appeared…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">47</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 47, "quote": "7.9.9.3 Ruchi Eulogizes his Ancestors Markandeya says- 'After the departure of his ancestors, Ruchi started wandering here and there in his restlessness. After sometime, when he regained his composure, he decided to do penance t"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-9-4"><data key="title">Ancestors Bless Ruchi</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Hardly had Ruchi accomplished his eulogy of the ancestors, a very bright effulgence appeared in front of him, who were in fact his ancestors. They said- 'Demand any boon.' Ruchi replied- 'Lord Brahma has instructed me to enhance the process of creation. So I need a wife for that purpose. The…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">47</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 47, "quote": "7.9.9.4 Ancestors Bless Ruchi Markandeya says- 'Hardly had Ruchi accomplished his eulogy of the ancestors, a very bright effulgence appeared in front of him, who were in fact his ancestors. They said- 'Demand any boon.'"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-10"><data key="title">The Beginning of Bhautya Manavantar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Now I am going to tell you about the origin of Bhautya Manavantar. Sage Angira had a disciple named Bhuti. He was short tempered in nature and used to curse anybody at the earliest opportunity. Everybody including the deities like Indra, Vayu, Surya etc. were scared of him. Nobody went against his…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">47</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 47, "quote": "7.9.10 The Beginning of Bhautya Manavantar This Section contains 1 sub-section. Markandeya says- 'Now I am going to tell you about the origin of Bhautya Manavantar. Sage Angira had a disciple named Bhuti. He was short tempered in nature and"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-10-1"><data key="title">Birth of Martandadev</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lord Brahma had created Daksha from the thumb of his right hand. Daksha's wife was created from the thumb of Brahma's left hand. Daksha had a daughter named Aditi. Maartandadev was the son of Aditi and his father was Kashyap. Kraustuki asked- 'O lord! Tell me the reason why Vivasvaan Surya took birth as the son of…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">48</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 48, "quote": "7.9.10.1 Birth of Martandadev Lord Brahma had created Daksha from the thumb of his right hand. Daksha's wife was created from the thumb of Brahma's left hand. Daksha had a daughter named Aditi. Maartandadev was the son o"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-10"><data key="title">Manifestation of the Sun God (Aditya) and the Vedas</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'After Brahma came out from that egg, Rigveda manifested from his first mouth. From the mouth facing south, manifested the Yajurveda. After that, Samaveda manifested from his mouth facing west. In the end, Atharvaveda came out from the mouth facing north. The Rigveda symbolises the Rajo Guna while the…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">48</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 48, "quote": "7.10 Manifestation of the Sun God (Aditya) and the Vedas This chapter contains 6 sections. Markandeya says- 'After Brahma came out from that egg, Rigveda manifested from his first mouth. From the mouth facing south, manifested the Yajurveda"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-10-1"><data key="title">Lord Brahma Eulogizes Surya</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'In the beginning of the creation, Surya shed unbearable amount of heat. This made the process of creation very difficult. Lord Brahma became worried. He thought- 'Due to the extreme heat of the Sun, all the water on this earth is getting evaporated. If this continues to happen then how would the…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">48</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 48, "quote": "7.10.1 Lord Brahma Eulogizes Surya Markandeya says- 'In the beginning of the creation, Surya shed unbearable amount of heat. This made the process of creation very difficult. Lord Brahma became worried. He thought- 'Due to th"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-10-2"><data key="title">Progeny of Kashyap Prajapati and Aditi’s Eulogy of Divakar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Lord Brahma, after having created the universe divided the earth into various islands. He also created the oceans and the mountains. Marichi, the son of Brahma was the father of Kashyap. Kashyap was married to the ten daughters of Daksha. He had numerous sons from them like the deities, the demons…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">49</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 49, "quote": "7.10.2 Progeny of Kashyap Prajapati and Aditi’s Eulogy of Divakar Markandeya says- 'Lord Brahma, after having created the universe divided the earth into various islands. He also created the oceans and the mountains. Marichi, the son of Brahma was the fath"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-10-3"><data key="title">Aditi gives birth to Aditya</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'After being pleased by the eulogy of Aditi, the Sun shed its radiance and became sombre. Now, Aditi could see him. She said- 'O lord! Be pleased upon me. The Daityas and the Daanavas have captured all the three worlds from my sons. I request you to take birth as my son and defeat the demons.' The Sun…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">49</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 49, "quote": "7.10.3 Aditi gives birth to Aditya Markandeya says- 'After being pleased by the eulogy of Aditi, the Sun shed its radiance and became sombre. Now, Aditi could see him. She said- 'O lord! Be pleased upon me. The Daityas and th"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-10-4"><data key="title">Vishwakarma Eulogizes Aditya</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Vishwakarma had married his daughter Sangya to the Sun-god. But being unable to face the unbearable radiance of the Sun, she went to do penance after instructing her shadow to perform the duties of an ideal wife on her behalf. Ultimately, she returned back to her husband only after Vishwakarma had…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">49</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 49, "quote": "7.10.4 Vishwakarma Eulogizes Aditya Markandeya says- 'Vishwakarma had married his daughter Sangya to the Sun-god. But being unable to face the unbearable radiance of the Sun, she went to do penance after instructing her shadow"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-10-5"><data key="title">Rajyavardan’s Subjects Eulogize Aditya for their King’s Long Life</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Kraustuki says- 'O lord! Enlighten us on the greatness of Bhaskar.' Markandeya replied- 'There was a king named Rajyavardhan. His subjects were very happy and satisfied under his rule. They were free from all the diseases. Rajyavardhan ruled for 7,000 long years. His wife was Maanini. One day, while Maanini was…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">50</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 50, "quote": "7.10.5 Rajyavardan’s Subjects Eulogize Aditya for their King’s Long Life Kraustuki says- 'O lord! Enlighten us on the greatness of Bhaskar.' Markandeya replied- 'There was a king named Rajyavardhan. His subjects were very happy and satisfied under his rule. They"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-10-6"><data key="title">Martandadev and his Descendants</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Maartandadev had a son named Manu. The seventh Manavantar in which we are living is named after him. Manu had many sons among whom Narishyant, Naabhaag, Prishadhna and Drisht were the rulers of different kingdoms. Manu had performed a special Yagya named Mitravarun with the desire of having more…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">50</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 50, "quote": "7.10.6 Martandadev and his Descendants Markandeya says- 'Maartandadev had a son named Manu. The seventh Manavantar in which we are living is named after him. Manu had many sons among whom Narishyant, Naabhaag, Prishadhna and Dris"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-11"><data key="title">Famous Mythological Tales</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">51</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 51, "quote": "7.11 Famous Mythological Tales"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-11-1"><data key="title">King Prishadhna</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">King Prishadhna was the son of Manu. One day, he had gone to the forest on a hunting spree. He killed a cow by mistake. This cow belonged to an Agnihotri Brahmin who was performing a Yagya. The Brahmin became very furious and cursed Prishadhna to become a Shudra. King Prishadhna too became angry and he wanted to…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">51</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 51, "quote": "7.11.1 King Prishadhna King Prishadhna was the son of Manu. One day, he had gone to the forest on a hunting spree. He killed a cow by mistake. This cow belonged to an Agnihotri Brahmin who was performing a Yagya."}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-11-2"><data key="title">Prince Nabhag</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Naabhaag was the son of King Dishta. One day, he saw a beautiful woman who was the daughter of a Vaishya. He was very enchanted by her beauty and wanted to marry her. He went to the Vaishya and expressed his desire. The Vaishya was terrified at the prospect of facing the wrath of the king. He said-…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">51</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 51, "quote": "7.11.2 Prince Nabhag Markandeya says- 'Naabhaag was the son of King Dishta. One day, he saw a beautiful woman who was the daughter of a Vaishya. He was very enchanted by her beauty and wanted to marry her. He we"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-11-3"><data key="title">Curse of Sage Pramati</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'The king stopped fighting at the request of Naarad. Naabhaag married the Vaishya girl. After the marriage, the king ordered Naabhaag to follow a Vaishya's occupation. Naabhaag got engaged in activities like animal husbandry and agriculture. With the passage of time, a son was born and who was named…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">52</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 52, "quote": "7.11.3 Curse of Sage Pramati Markandeya says- 'The king stopped fighting at the request of Naarad. Naabhaag married the Vaishya girl. After the marriage, the king ordered Naabhaag to follow a Vaishya's occupation. Naabh"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-11-4"><data key="title">Agastya’s Brother Curses Kripavati</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'The king stopped fighting at the request of Naarad. Naabhaag married the Vaishya girl. After the marriage, the king ordered Naabhaag to follow a Vaishya's occupation. Naabhaag got engaged in activities like animal husbandry and agriculture. With the passage of time, a son was born and who was named…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">52</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 52, "quote": "7.11.4 Agastya’s Brother Curses Kripavati Markandeya says- 'The king stopped fighting at the request of Naarad. Naabhaag married the Vaishya girl. After the marriage, the king ordered Naabhaag to follow a Vaishya's occupation. Naabh"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-11-5"><data key="title">Bhalandan and Vatsapri</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Naabhaag told his wife- 'I would never accept the kingdom for the second time, which I had relinquished at the instruction of my father. He then instructed his son to rule over the kingdom he had won. Following his father's instructions, Bhalandan started ruling over his kingdom. Later on, he got married and begot a…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">53</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 53, "quote": "7.11.5 Bhalandan and Vatsapri Naabhaag told his wife- 'I would never accept the kingdom for the second time, which I had relinquished at the instruction of my father. He then instructed his son to rule over the kingdom h"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-11-6"><data key="title">The Kingdoms of Pranshu, Prajaati and Khanitra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Sunanda gave birth to 12 sons. Their names were Pranshu, Praveer, Shoor, Suchakra, Vikram, Krama, Bala, Balaak, Chand, Prachand, Suvikram and Swarup. After growing up, Pranshu became the king. Pranshu had a son named Prajaati. Prajaati had five sons among whom Khanitra was one of them. Khanitra…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">53</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 53, "quote": "7.11.6 The Kingdoms of Pranshu, Prajaati and Khanitra Markandeya says- 'Sunanda gave birth to 12 sons. Their names were Pranshu, Praveer, Shoor, Suchakra, Vikram, Krama, Bala, Balaak, Chand, Prachand, Suvikram and Swarup. After growing up, Pran"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-11-7"><data key="title">King Khanitra</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'People present at the oblation site were shocked to see the dead bodies of Vishwavedi and the four priests. When Khanitra got this news, he too was shocked. Khanitra went to Vashishta and asked him the reason about the deaths of his brother's minister and the four priests. Vashishta revealed the…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">54</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 54, "quote": "7.11.7 King Khanitra Markandeya says- 'People present at the oblation site were shocked to see the dead bodies of Vishwavedi and the four priests. When Khanitra got this news, he too was shocked. Khanitra went t"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-11-8"><data key="title">King Vivinsha</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'King Kshup ruled justly in the same way as his father Khanitra did. Kshup's wife was Pramatha. They had a son who was handsome and mighty. He had defeated all the kings and annexed their kingdom. He was married to Nandini, the princess of Vidarbha. Nandini gave birth to a son who was named Vivinsha.…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">54</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 54, "quote": "7.11.8 King Vivinsha Markandeya says- 'King Kshup ruled justly in the same way as his father Khanitra did. Kshup's wife was Pramatha. They had a son who was handsome and mighty. He had defeated all the kings and"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-11-9"><data key="title">King Khaninetra</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Khaninetra was the son of King Vivinsha. He was very brave and valiant. The Gandharvas had proclaimed that no one could match Khaninetra's benevolence, who had donated the whole earth after accomplishing 10,000 Yagyas. Khaninetra had given so much wealth as donations to the Brahmins that there was no…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">54</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 54, "quote": "7.11.9 King Khaninetra Markandeya says- 'Khaninetra was the son of King Vivinsha. He was very brave and valiant. The Gandharvas had proclaimed that no one could match Khaninetra's benevolence, who had donated the"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-11-10"><data key="title">Karandham</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'King Khaninetra went at the bank of the river Gomti and started doing a severe penance to please Indra. After being pleased by his devotion and eulogy, Indra appeared before him. He said to Khaninetra- 'O king! I am very much pleased by your devotion. Demand anything you wish.' The king replied- 'O…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">55</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 55, "quote": "7.11.10 Karandham Markandeya says- 'King Khaninetra went at the bank of the river Gomti and started doing a severe penance to please Indra. After being pleased by his devotion and eulogy, Indra appeared befor"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-12"><data key="title">King Aveekshit</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">55</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 55, "quote": "7.12 King Aveekshit"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-12-1"><data key="title">Birth of Aveekshit and Abduction of Vaishaalini</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Shubhvrata- the daughter of Veeryachandra had decided to marry Karandham. A son was born to them who was named as Aveekshit. The astrologers had predicted a very bright future for him. Aveekshit learnt lessons in handling all kinds of weapons from the son of Kanva. In a very short time, he attained…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">55</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 55, "quote": "7.12.1 Birth of Aveekshit and Abduction of Vaishaalini Markandeya says- 'Shubhvrata- the daughter of Veeryachandra had decided to marry Karandham. A son was born to them who was named as Aveekshit. The astrologers had predicted a very bright fut"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-12-2"><data key="title">Aveekshit gets defeated in Battle</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'A ferocious battle ensued between Aveekshit's army and the other kings. Both sides fought bravely and Aveekshit managed to kill many of the kings but was outnumbered and got severely wounded. Aveekshit was finally made captive. Aveekshit and Sudati Vaishaalini were brought to the king of Vishaal. The…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">56</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 56, "quote": "7.12.2 Aveekshit gets defeated in Battle Markandeya says- 'A ferocious battle ensued between Aveekshit's army and the other kings. Both sides fought bravely and Aveekshit managed to kill many of the kings but was outnumbered and go"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-12-3"><data key="title">Liberation of Aveekshit and his Disenchantment</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When king Karandham came to know about his son's defeat and captivity, he consulted his friends. All of them decided to attack the kingdom of Vishaal and release Aveekshit from captivity. Karandham marched towards the kingdom of Vishaal followed by a huge army. A tremendous battle was fought which continued for three…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">56</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 56, "quote": "7.12.3 Liberation of Aveekshit and his Disenchantment When king Karandham came to know about his son's defeat and captivity, he consulted his friends. All of them decided to attack the kingdom of Vishaal and release Aveekshit from captivity. Ka"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-12-4"><data key="title">Aveekshit Promises his Father to get Married</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Once, Veera- the wife of King Karandham and mother of Aveekshit had taken a vow to accomplish a very arduous penance. But this could only be accomplished with Karandham's and Aveekshit's assistance. Karandham had assured her that he would provide all the wealth required for the accomplishment of that…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">56</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 56, "quote": "7.12.4 Aveekshit Promises his Father to get Married Markandeya says- 'Once, Veera- the wife of King Karandham and mother of Aveekshit had taken a vow to accomplish a very arduous penance. But this could only be accomplished with Karandham's a"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-12-5"><data key="title">Aveekshit Rescues Vaishalini</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Once, Aveekshit had gone for a hunt in the forest when suddenly, he heard a female voice shouting for help. He proceeded in the direction of the voice. After reaching there, he saw Danu's (a demon) son Dhridhakesha, holding a lady by her hair. The woman was crying- 'I am the wife of Aveekshit. This…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">57</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 57, "quote": "7.12.5 Aveekshit Rescues Vaishalini Markandeya says- 'Once, Aveekshit had gone for a hunt in the forest when suddenly, he heard a female voice shouting for help. He proceeded in the direction of the voice. After reaching there"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-12-6"><data key="title">Aveekshit Marries Vaishalini</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'After Vaishaalini had finished her lines, Aveekshit told her that he was forced to abandon her in the past after getting defeated but once again, he had regained her after defeating the demon. Both of them decided to get married in the forest itself.' 'Right then, a Gandharva- Tanaya arrived there…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">58</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 58, "quote": "7.12.6 Aveekshit Marries Vaishalini Markandeya says- 'After Vaishaalini had finished her lines, Aveekshit told her that he was forced to abandon her in the past after getting defeated but once again, he had regained her after"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-13"><data key="title">King Marut</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">58</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 58, "quote": "7.13 King Marut"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-13-1"><data key="title">Birth of Marut</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'After Vaishaalini had finished her lines, Aveekshit told her that he was forced to abandon her in the past after getting defeated but once again, he had regained her after defeating the demon. Both of them decided to get married in the forest itself.' 'Right then, a Gandharva- Tanaya arrived there…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">58</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 58, "quote": "7.13.1 Birth of Marut Markandeya says- 'After Vaishaalini had finished her lines, Aveekshit told her that he was forced to abandon her in the past after getting defeated but once again, he had regained her after"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-13-2"><data key="title">Marut Ascends the Throne</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'After the birth of Marut, Aveekshit accompanied by Vaishaalini returned to his father's palace. After making salutations to his father, he kept the new born baby in his lap and told him that he had kept his word. Karandham became very pleased after seeing his grandson.' 'In course of time, Marut grew…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">58</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 58, "quote": "7.13.2 Marut Ascends the Throne Markandeya says- 'After the birth of Marut, Aveekshit accompanied by Vaishaalini returned to his father's palace. After making salutations to his father, he kept the new born baby in his lap"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-13-3"><data key="title">A Hermit Advises Marut on behalf of Veera</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Kraustuki asks Markandeya- 'O lord! We want to know more about Marut.' Markandeya says- 'After becoming the king, Marut started ruling in a just manner. He performed many Yagyas under the supervision of numerous 'Yagyikas' and esteemed priests. Angira's son-Samvart and Brihaspati's brother were his chief priests under…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">59</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 59, "quote": "7.13.3 A Hermit Advises Marut on behalf of Veera Kraustuki asks Markandeya- 'O lord! We want to know more about Marut.' Markandeya says- 'After becoming the king, Marut started ruling in a just manner. He performed many Yagyas under the su"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-13-4"><data key="title">The Serpents take the Refuge of Bhamini (Vaishalini)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Marut was ashamed after hearing the sage's words. He cursed himself for his negligence. He picked up his bow and arrows in anger and went to the oblation site. When he reached there, he saw the dead bodies of seven hermits lying on the ground. Being enraged, he picked up his most devastating weapon…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">59</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 59, "quote": "7.13.4 The Serpents take the Refuge of Bhamini (Vaishalini) Markandeya says- 'Marut was ashamed after hearing the sage's words. He cursed himself for his negligence. He picked up his bow and arrows in anger and went to the oblation site. When he reac"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-13-5"><data key="title">The Hermit’s Sons Come to Life Again</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'When Aveekshit reached the oblation site, he found that the whole atmosphere was ablaze by the tremendous heat generated by Marut's weapon, Samvartak. He requested Marut to drop his weapon but Marut refused by saying- 'O father! These serpents are the culprits. They have killed the hermit's sons.…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">59</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 59, "quote": "7.13.5 The Hermit’s Sons Come to Life Again Markandeya says- 'When Aveekshit reached the oblation site, he found that the whole atmosphere was ablaze by the tremendous heat generated by Marut's weapon, Samvartak. He requested Marut to"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-14"><data key="title">King Narishyant</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'Among the eighteen sons of Marut, Narishyant was the eldest. Marut ruled over his kingdom for 70,015 years after which Narishyant succeeded him as the king. Marut went to the forest to do penance. After becoming the king, Narishyant decided to perform a grand Yagya, the like of which had never been…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">60</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 60, "quote": "7.14 King Narishyant This chapter contains 2 sections. Markandeya says- 'Among the eighteen sons of Marut, Narishyant was the eldest. Marut ruled over his kingdom for 70,015 years after which Narishyant succeeded him as the king. Marut went"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-14-1"><data key="title">Sumana Chooses Dama as her Husband</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- Dama was the son of Narishyant. His mother was Indrasena- the daughter of Babhru. He became the master in archery under the tutelage of Vrishparva. He also learnt the nuances of handling other kinds of weapons under the guidance of Dundubhi. He learnt the scriptures from sage Shakti and the art of…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">60</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 60, "quote": "7.14.1 Sumana Chooses Dama as her Husband Markandeya says- Dama was the son of Narishyant. His mother was Indrasena- the daughter of Babhru. He became the master in archery under the tutelage of Vrishparva. He also learnt the nuance"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-14-2"><data key="title">Killing of Narishyant</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'When the newly married couple- Dama and Sumana returned to the capital, King Narishyant was extremely pleased. King Narishyant after knowing about the brave deeds of his son was very proud of him. As he had become old, he appointed Dama as his successor and he went in the forest to do penance…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">61</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 61, "quote": "7.14.2 Killing of Narishyant Markandeya says- 'When the newly married couple- Dama and Sumana returned to the capital, King Narishyant was extremely pleased. King Narishyant after knowing about the brave deeds of his so"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-15"><data key="title">King Dama and his Marriage with Sumana</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">61</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 61, "quote": "7.15 King Dama and his Marriage with Sumana"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-15-1"><data key="title">Dama Vows to Kill Vayushmaan</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'When Dama came to know about his father's death, he became extremely furious. Although he was very patient by nature but still the news of his father's death made him restless. He thought- 'Shame on me. Inspite of myself being alive, the wicked Vayushmaan had killed my father. I have failed in my…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">61</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 61, "quote": "7.15.1 Dama Vows to Kill Vayushmaan Markandeya says- 'When Dama came to know about his father's death, he became extremely furious. Although he was very patient by nature but still the news of his father's death made him restl"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-15-2"><data key="title">Killing of Vayushmaan</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Markandeya says- 'When Dama came to know about his father's death, he became extremely furious. Although he was very patient by nature but still the news of his father's death made him restless. He thought- 'Shame on me. Inspite of myself being alive, the wicked Vayushmaan had killed my father. I have failed in my…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">62</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 62, "quote": "7.15.2 Killing of Vayushmaan Markandeya says- 'When Dama came to know about his father's death, he became extremely furious. Although he was very patient by nature but still the news of his father's death made him restl"}]</data></node><node id="p-markandeya-puran-7-16"><data key="title">The Benefits of Listening to the Markandaya Purana</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The birds said- 'O Jaimini! This was the way, sage Markandeya had narrated the divine tales to Kraustuki. A person who either studies this Puraan or listens to it achieves great accomplishment. All his desires are fulfilled and he enjoys a long life. He becomes free from all his sins. Markandeya Puraan is the seventh…</data><data key="doc">markandeya-puran</data><data key="page">62</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 62, "quote": "7.16 The Benefits of Listening to the Markandaya Purana The birds said- 'O Jaimini! This was the way, sage Markandeya had narrated the divine tales to Kraustuki. A person who either studies this Puraan or listens to it achieves great accomplishme"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-1"><data key="title">Penance of the Sages in Naimisharn</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once upon a time, about twenty-six thousand sages assembled in the holy ambience of Naimisharanya. All of them had renounced their desires and homely comforts and were worshipping Lord Vishnu for the benefit of the entire humanity. During their stay in Naimisharanya, those sages called a huge assembly to discuss about…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "18.1 Penance of the Sages in Naimisharn Once upon a time, about twenty-six thousand sages assembled in the holy ambience of Naimisharanya. All of them had renounced their desires and homely comforts and were worshipping Lord Vishn"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-2"><data key="title">Narad Prays to Lord Vishnu</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shaunak says: "Sutaji, how did Sanatkumar unfold the mystic character of the religion to Narad? When and where they had the meeting to hold this pious discussion? Kindly tell us in detail." Sutaji says: "The great sage Sanatkumar, who was free from illusions, was the creation of Brahma’s imagination. He always…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "18.2 Narad Prays to Lord Vishnu Shaunak says: \"Sutaji, how did Sanatkumar unfold the mystic character of the religion to Narad? When and where they had the meeting to hold this pious discussion? Kindly tell us in detail.\""}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-3"><data key="title">Profile of the Sage Markandeya</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Sanatkumar says: O sage! Faith has the greatest significance. Achievement of everything and appeasement of Lord Vishnu depend upon the faith. Nothing is attainable without faith. Such as a human being faces inertia in the absence of life and is rendered motionless. Similarly, faith is like a spiritual light without…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "18.3 Profile of the Sage Markandeya Sanatkumar says: O sage! Faith has the greatest significance. Achievement of everything and appeasement of Lord Vishnu depend upon the faith. Nothing is attainable without faith. Such as a h"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-4"><data key="title">Tale of the Sage Markandeya</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Narad says: "O great sage! Why did Lord Vishnu appear as the son of Mrikandu? What did He do as his son? The Puranas say that during the Pralay, Markandeya had witnessed the direct appearance of Lord Vishnu’s illusion. Kindly narrate these things in detail." Sanatkumar says: "Listen O Devarshi! After observing severe…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "18.4 Tale of the Sage Markandeya Narad says: \"O great sage! Why did Lord Vishnu appear as the son of Mrikandu? What did He do as his son? The Puranas say that during the Pralay, Markandeya had witnessed the direct appearanc"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-5"><data key="title">The Greatness of the Ganges</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sutaji says- "The glorious description of devotion for Lord Vishnu caused extreme joy for Narad." Narad says: "O great sage Sanat! Now kindly tell me about such a place of pilgrimage which is even holier than the holiest places of pilgrimage." Sanatkumar says: O Narad! Confluence of Ganga and Yamuna is the holier than…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "18.5 The Greatness of the Ganges Sutaji says- \"The glorious description of devotion for Lord Vishnu caused extreme joy for Narad.\" Narad says: \"O great sage Sanat! Now kindly tell me about such a place of pilgrimage which i"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-6"><data key="title">Fate of King Baahu</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Narad says: "O great sage who in the dynasty of Sagar received salvation from Preta Bhava. Who was Sagar? Kindly narrate his whole story to me." Sanatkumar says: "Long ago, there occurred a king named Baahu in the dynasty of Surya. He was very religious in nature and looked after his subjects like his own offspring.…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "18.6 Fate of King Baahu Narad says: \"O great sage who in the dynasty of Sagar received salvation from Preta Bhava. Who was Sagar? Kindly narrate his whole story to me.\" Sanatkumar says: \"Long ago, there occurred a"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-7"><data key="title">The Birth of Sagar</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Sanatkumar says :- "O Narad! Both the queens were passing their time in the hermitage of sage Aurv. When half of the rainy season had passed, the elder queen began to cultivate evil feelings for the younger. She, therefore, made deceitfully the younger queen to consume poison. But the poison could not affect the queen…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 6, "quote": "18.7 The Birth of Sagar Sanatkumar says :- \"O Narad! Both the queens were passing their time in the hermitage of sage Aurv. When half of the rainy season had passed, the elder queen began to cultivate evil feelings"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-8"><data key="title">Salvation of Saudas</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Narad says: "O great brother! Why did sage Vashishta curse Saudas and how did he recover from this curse by the virtue of the Ganges waters?" Sanatkumar says: "O Narad! Saudas was a virtuous king. He ruled over his subjects religiously. He was very fond of hunting. So, one day, he went on hunting in the forest. He…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 7, "quote": "18.8 Salvation of Saudas Narad says: \"O great brother! Why did sage Vashishta curse Saudas and how did he recover from this curse by the virtue of the Ganges waters?\" Sanatkumar says: \"O Narad! Saudas was a virtuous"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-9"><data key="title">King Bali defeats the Gods</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Narad says: "O brother! Kindly narrate the tale of the Ganges’ origin from the feet of lord Vishnu." Sanatkumar says: Long ago, in the beginning of creation, one sage Kashyap had two wives Diti and Aditi. They both were the daughters of Daksha. Aditi was the mother of the gods while Diti had given birth to demons.…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 9, "quote": "18.9 King Bali defeats the Gods Narad says: \"O brother! Kindly narrate the tale of the Ganges’ origin from the feet of lord Vishnu.\" Sanatkumar says: Long ago, in the beginning of creation, one sage Kashyap had two wives D"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-10"><data key="title">Origin of the Ganges</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Narad says: "O brother! You narrated a wonderful tale. Now, kindly relate to me, how did the Ganges originate?" Sanatkumar says: O Narad! Pleased with the severe penance of Aditi, Lord Vishnu appeared before her and asked her to seek a boon. Feeling elated with exhilaration, Aditi fell flat at the Lord’s feet. She…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 9, "quote": "18.10Origin of the Ganges Narad says: \"O brother! You narrated a wonderful tale. Now, kindly relate to me, how did the Ganges originate?\" Sanatkumar says: O Narad! Pleased with the severe penance of Aditi, Lord Vishn"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-11"><data key="title">Definition of Dharma</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Narad says:"O great sage! I have just heard the tale of sacred Ganges. Now kindly describe about the people who deserve to take donations." Sanatkumar says: O Narad! Brahmin is considered as the excellent among all the four castes. Hence, to make the donation fruitful, one should donate to the Brahmins only. A Brahmin…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 10, "quote": "18.11Definition of Dharma Narad says:\"O great sage! I have just heard the tale of sacred Ganges. Now kindly describe about the people who deserve to take donations.\" Sanatkumar says: O Narad! Brahmin is considered as"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-12"><data key="title">Description of Religious Action</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Dharmaraaj says: "O king! Those who build temples with earth stay in the abode of Vishnu for three kalpas. Then they attain Brahmaloka. One receives double virtues if he builds a temple with wood. Building a temple with bricks and stones gives four-fold virtue while with the precious stones one gets ten-fold virtues.…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 10, "quote": "18.12Description of Religious Action Dharmaraaj says: \"O king! Those who build temples with earth stay in the abode of Vishnu for three kalpas. Then they attain Brahmaloka. One receives double virtues if he builds a temple with"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-13"><data key="title">Expiation for the Sins</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Dharmaraaj says: "O king! I am now going to narrate about the religion. Listen to it faithfully. If a person touches or gets touched by an impure or irreligious thing, he should observe fast for three days and six nights for expiation. He should take bath thrice a day. If a Brahmin defecates during dining, he must…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 11, "quote": "18.13Expiation for the Sins Dharmaraaj says: \"O king! I am now going to narrate about the religion. Listen to it faithfully. If a person touches or gets touched by an impure or irreligious thing, he should observe fast"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-14"><data key="title">Torture in the Hell</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Dharmaraaj says: "O king! Those who commit severe sin on earth are bound to suffer equally severe torturing in hell. I am now describing about such sins and the tortures vouchsafed for them in the hells. Tapan, Balukaakumbh, Maharaurav, Raurav, Kumbhipaak, Pramardan, Lalabhaksha, Vasaakup and Vaitarni are some of the…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 12, "quote": "18.14Torture in the Hell Dharmaraaj says: \"O king! Those who commit severe sin on earth are bound to suffer equally severe torturing in hell. I am now describing about such sins and the tortures vouchsafed for them"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-15"><data key="title">Descent of Ganges and Salvation of Sagar’s Sons</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Narad says:"O Muni! What did king Bhagirath do on Himalayas? How did he please Ganges to descend on the earth?" Sanatkumar says: O Narad! Renouncing all his royal comforts, Bhagirath went to the snow-capped, lofty peaks of the Himalayas. There he saw dense forest and herds of deer and elephants playing. Even his…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 13, "quote": "18.15Descent of Ganges and Salvation of Sagar’s Sons Narad says:\"O Muni! What did king Bhagirath do on Himalayas? How did he please Ganges to descend on the earth?\" Sanatkumar says: O Narad! Renouncing all his royal comforts, Bhagirath went to"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-16"><data key="title">Description of Dwadashi Fast</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages say: "O fortunate one! You have obliged us narrating the tale of sacred Ganges. Now we desire to hear what Narad asked Sanatkumar." Sutaji says: "O great sages! Now I narrate what Narad asked Sanatkumar, listen to it carefully." Narad says: "I am blessed hearing the pious tale of the Ganges. Now kindly tell…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "18.16Description of Dwadashi Fast The sages say: \"O fortunate one! You have obliged us narrating the tale of sacred Ganges. Now we desire to hear what Narad asked Sanatkumar.\" Sutaji says: \"O great sages! Now I narrate what"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-17"><data key="title">Description of Fast for LakshmiNarayana</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sanatkumar says: " O great among the sages! Now I am narrating about an excellent fast, which destroys all the sins. Observing fast on full Moon day is considered excellent among all the fasts. It leads to fulfillment of all the desires and protects the devotee from influence of harsh planets. Like the fast of…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 16, "quote": "18.17Description of Fast for LakshmiNarayana Sanatkumar says: \" O great among the sages! Now I am narrating about an excellent fast, which destroys all the sins. Observing fast on full Moon day is considered excellent among all the fas"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-18"><data key="title">Flagging in the Temple of Lord Vishnu</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sanatkumar says: "O Narad! Now I am narrating about the flagging fast in order to please Lord Vishnu. He who performs this fast is even worshipped by Brahma and other deities. This fast accords virtues equal to those of donating one thousand carats of gold to a Brahmin or those attained taking bath in the Ganges and…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 17, "quote": "18.18Flagging in the Temple of Lord Vishnu Sanatkumar says: \"O Narad! Now I am narrating about the flagging fast in order to please Lord Vishnu. He who performs this fast is even worshipped by Brahma and other deities. This fast acco"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-19"><data key="title">Tale of King Sumit’s Previous Birth</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Narad says: "O Sanatkumar! A king Sumit had performed this flagging fast for the first time. Kindly narrate to me the tale of his life." Sanatkumar says: During Satayuga, a Somavanshi king Sumit was ruling the entire earth. He was very religious in nature and a great devotee of Lord Vishnu. He served the other…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 18, "quote": "18.19Tale of King Sumit’s Previous Birth Narad says: \"O Sanatkumar! A king Sumit had performed this flagging fast for the first time. Kindly narrate to me the tale of his life.\" Sanatkumar says: During Satayuga, a Somavanshi king S"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-20"><data key="title">Description of HariPanchaRatra Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sanatkumar says: O Narad! I am now narrating about another fast. It is Haripanchak fast and is rare in all the worlds. It destroys all the sorrows of the devotee, fulfills his all desires and helps him attain the four goals of life. It is observed for five days in the month of Maargsheersh starting from the tenth day…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 19, "quote": "18.20Description of HariPanchaRatra Vrata Sanatkumar says: O Narad! I am now narrating about another fast. It is Haripanchak fast and is rare in all the worlds. It destroys all the sorrows of the devotee, fulfills his all desires an"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-21"><data key="title">Description of the Fruits of the Fast</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sanatkumar says:"Now I am narrating about an excellent fast for the benefit of the world. Listen to it carefully O Narad! This fast must be observed in the months of Ashaadh, Shraavan, Bhaadrapad or Ashwin. On any day during the brighter phase of these months, the devotees must drink Panchgavya and pass his time in…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 19, "quote": "18.21Description of the Fruits of the Fast Sanatkumar says:\"Now I am narrating about an excellent fast for the benefit of the world. Listen to it carefully O Narad! This fast must be observed in the months of Ashaadh, Shraavan, Bhaad"}]</data></node><node id="p-narad-puran-18-22"><data key="title">Tale of Bhadrasheel Brahmin</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Sanatkumar says: "O Narad! A fast on Ekadashi (eleventh day) in both the phases of a month pleases Shri Hari and fulfills all the desires. On the eleventh day of each phase, one should not dine. Those who dine on the eleventh day commit severe sins. Salvation from sins like killing a Brahmin is possible but there is…</data><data key="doc">narad-puran</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 20, "quote": "18.22Tale of Bhadrasheel Brahmin Sanatkumar says: \"O Narad! A fast on Ekadashi (eleventh day) in both the phases of a month pleases Shri Hari and fulfills all the desires. On the eleventh day of each phase, one should not d"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-1"><data key="title">Shrishtikhand</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Having revealed the contents of all the Puranas to his son Ugrashrava, Sage Lomaharshan instructed him to go to 'Naimisharanya' and spread it among the sages doing penance over there. There is an interesting tale how this sacrosanct forest of Naimish derived it's name -- Once, the sages sought Lord Vishnu's help in…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "2.1 Shrishtikhand This chapter contains 16 sections. Having revealed the contents of all the Puranas to his son Ugrashrava, Sage Lomaharshan instructed him to go to 'Naimisharanya' and spread it among the sages doing penance over there. The"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-1-1"><data key="title">The Beginning of Creation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The Sages requested Sutji to narrate about the incident when sage Pulastya had met Bhishma. Sutji replied---- Bhishma was doing penance at a place called Gangadwar. Being pleased by his austere penance, Lord Brahma instructed Pulastya to go to Gangadwar and bless Bhishma. After reaching there, Pulastya told Bhishma…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "2.1.1 The Beginning of Creation The Sages requested Sutji to narrate about the incident when sage Pulastya had met Bhishma. Sutji replied---- Bhishma was doing penance at a place called Gangadwar. Being pleased by his aust"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-1-2"><data key="title">Lord Brahma’s Life-Span and the Chronological Order of the ‘Eras’</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Pulastya says---Lord Brahma, the embodiment of Lord Vishnu has a life-span of one hundred years. His life-span is also called 'Par', and half of its period is known as 'Parardha'. One 'Kashtha' consists of 15 Nimesha while a Kala consists of 30 'Kashthas'. A muhurta consists of 30 Kalas. The periods of 30 muhurtas are…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "2.1.2 Lord Brahma’s Life-Span and the Chronological Order of the ‘Eras’ Pulastya says---Lord Brahma, the embodiment of Lord Vishnu has a life-span of one hundred years. His life-span is also called 'Par', and half of its period is known as 'Parardha'. One 'Kasht"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-1-3"><data key="title">The Four Prominent Castes</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">On being asked by Bhishma about the origin of the four prominent castes, Sage Pulastya said--- Lord Brahma created the Brahmins and the Kshatriyas from his mouth and chest respectively. Similarly, Vaishyas and Shudras manifested from Lord Brahma's thigh and feet respectively.Lord Brahma had created his 'manasputras'…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "2.1.3 The Four Prominent Castes On being asked by Bhishma about the origin of the four prominent castes, Sage Pulastya said--- Lord Brahma created the Brahmins and the Kshatriyas from his mouth and chest respectively. Simi"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-1-4"><data key="title">The Manifestations of Goddess Laxmi</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Bheeshma expressed his desire to know about the manifestation of goddess Laxmi. Pulastya replied-- Once, after being defeated by the demons, the deities went to Lord Vishnu and sought his help. Lord Vishnu instructed them to collect all the medicinal herbs, and put them in the Ksheersagar, so that after the churning…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "2.1.4 The Manifestations of Goddess Laxmi Bheeshma expressed his desire to know about the manifestation of goddess Laxmi. Pulastya replied-- Once, after being defeated by the demons, the deities went to Lord Vishnu and sought his he"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-1-5"><data key="title">Sati gives up her Life</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">On being enquired by Bheeshma about the reason why Sati gave up her life, Sage Pulastya narrated the following tale ---- Once, Daksha Prajapati had organised a grand yagya in which he had invited everybody except Lord Shiva. This Yagya was presided over by four prominent sages---Vashishth, Angika, Vrihaspati and…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "2.1.5 Sati gives up her Life On being enquired by Bheeshma about the reason why Sati gave up her life, Sage Pulastya narrated the following tale ---- Once, Daksha Prajapati had organised a grand yagya in which he had in"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-1-6"><data key="title">The Origin of Deities, Demons and Serpents</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Bheeshma requested Sage Pulastya to explain how different entities like the deities, demons and serpents came into being. Pulastya replied---Having failed in his repeated attempts of increasing population by the means of 'sankalpa' Daksha Prajapati was left with no option but to take the help of copulative creation.…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "2.1.6 The Origin of Deities, Demons and Serpents Bheeshma requested Sage Pulastya to explain how different entities like the deities, demons and serpents came into being. Pulastya replied---Having failed in his repeated attempts of increas"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-1-7"><data key="title">The Origin of Marudganas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Bheeshma requested Sage Pulastya to describe how all the Marudganas came into existence. Pulastya replied--- All the sons and grandsons of Diti had perished in the battle fought between the deities and demons. Diti---the mother of daityas (demons) wanted to take revenge, so she started doing a penance at the bank of…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 6, "quote": "2.1.7 The Origin of Marudganas Bheeshma requested Sage Pulastya to describe how all the Marudganas came into existence. Pulastya replied--- All the sons and grandsons of Diti had perished in the battle fought between the"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-1-8"><data key="title">King Prithu and the Surya Dynasty</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Bheeshma enquired Sage Pulastya about the mighty King Prithu upon whom the earth was named as Prithvi. Sage Pulastya replied---Anga-Prajapati was the descendant of Swayambhuva Manu and Sunitha was his wife. In course of time, Sunitha gave birth to a son, who was named Ven. After growing up, Ven turned out to be an…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 8, "quote": "2.1.8 King Prithu and the Surya Dynasty Bheeshma enquired Sage Pulastya about the mighty King Prithu upon whom the earth was named as Prithvi. Sage Pulastya replied---Anga-Prajapati was the descendant of Swayambhuva Manu and Sunit"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-1-9"><data key="title">Bashkali, the Demon king</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Bheeshma requested Sage Pulastya to reveal Why Lord Vishnu had to take incarnation as Vaman. Sage Pulastya replied--- Once, during Satyayuga, the demons had defeated the deities and driven them out from their abode--heaven. The mighty demon, Bashkali was their leader. He had become immortal an account of a boon…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 9, "quote": "2.1.9 Bashkali, the Demon king Bheeshma requested Sage Pulastya to reveal Why Lord Vishnu had to take incarnation as Vaman. Sage Pulastya replied--- Once, during Satyayuga, the demons had defeated the deities and driven t"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-1-10"><data key="title">Sri Ram kills Shambuk</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After killing the demons king Ravan, Sri Ram returned to Ayodhya. Many sages had arrived there to bless him on the occasion of his crowning ceremony. Sage Vashishth was one of them.As Sri Ram was conversing with the sages a brahmin arrived there with the corpse of his son and wailed---"O son! Your death is certainly…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 9, "quote": "2.1.10 Sri Ram kills Shambuk After killing the demons king Ravan, Sri Ram returned to Ayodhya. Many sages had arrived there to bless him on the occasion of his crowning ceremony. Sage Vashishth was one of them.As Sri Ra"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-1-11"><data key="title">Manifestation of Ganesh &amp; Kartikeya</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, before taking her bath, goddess Parvati anointed unguent on her body and while removing it created a human form from the accumulated dross. The head of this human form resembled an elephant. Parvati then playfully immersed the human-form into river Ganga. But, to her sheer amazement the human form became alive…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 10, "quote": "2.1.11 Manifestation of Ganesh &amp; Kartikeya Once, before taking her bath, goddess Parvati anointed unguent on her body and while removing it created a human form from the accumulated dross. The head of this human form resembled an ele"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-1-12"><data key="title">An Ideal Brahmin and the Significance of the Gayatri Mantra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing about the qualities of an ideal brahmin, Sage Pulastya said--- Once, Narad had asked Lord Brahma as to who was worthy of receiving Vishnu's blessings. Lord Brahma had told Narad that Lord Vishnu showered his blessings on those who engaged themselves in the service of brahmins. A brahmin should be virtuous…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 10, "quote": "2.1.12 An Ideal Brahmin and the Significance of the Gayatri Mantra Describing about the qualities of an ideal brahmin, Sage Pulastya said--- Once, Narad had asked Lord Brahma as to who was worthy of receiving Vishnu's blessings. Lord Brahma had told Narad t"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-1-13"><data key="title">A Brahmin’s Livelihood</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing how a brahmin should earn his livelihood, Lord Brahma said to Narad--Alms, which a brahmin gets without making any demand for it, is called Vritta. 'Unchhavritti' is even better than Vritti and it means collecting foodgrain which are scattered in places like fields, granary, market-place etc. A brahmin…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 11, "quote": "2.1.13 A Brahmin’s Livelihood Describing how a brahmin should earn his livelihood, Lord Brahma said to Narad--Alms, which a brahmin gets without making any demand for it, is called Vritta. 'Unchhavritti' is even better t"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-1-14"><data key="title">Narottam – The Brahmin</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing about the importance of five virtuous deeds, Pulastya said to Bheeshma---"The five supreme virtuous deeds are--obedience to parents, faithfulness towards husband, equanimity, not having enmity towards friends and devotion towards Lord Vishnu. By pleasing his father, one pleases all the deities. Mother is…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 12, "quote": "2.1.14 Narottam – The Brahmin Describing about the importance of five virtuous deeds, Pulastya said to Bheeshma---\"The five supreme virtuous deeds are--obedience to parents, faithfulness towards husband, equanimity, not"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-1-15"><data key="title">The Significance of Rudraksha, Amla &amp; Tulsi</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, while describing about the importance of Rudraksha to the sages, Vyas said--- One who wears a Rudraksha rosary is supreme among all human beings. The mere sight of such a holy man absolves people of their sin. A Rudraksha bead bears features of a Linga and yoni on it's surface. One should not wear a rudraksha on…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 13, "quote": "2.1.15 The Significance of Rudraksha, Amla &amp; Tulsi Once, while describing about the importance of Rudraksha to the sages, Vyas said--- One who wears a Rudraksha rosary is supreme among all human beings. The mere sight of such a holy man abso"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-1-16"><data key="title">The Importance of Surya’s Worship</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, while describing about the importance of Surya's worship to Vaishampayan, Sage Vyas narrated the following tale --- There lived a king named Bhadreshwar. He ruled over Madhyadesh. Once his left hand was infected with leprosy. Bhadreshwar, fearing the prospect of dreadful leprosy spreading to his whole body,…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "2.1.16 The Importance of Surya’s Worship Once, while describing about the importance of Surya's worship to Vaishampayan, Sage Vyas narrated the following tale --- There lived a king named Bhadreshwar. He ruled over Madhyadesh. Once"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-2"><data key="title">Bhumikhand</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "2.2 Bhumikhand"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-2-1"><data key="title">Different Types of Sin and Virtue</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, Yayati requested Matali to describe the various sinful and virtuous deeds, which a man normally commits in his life. Matali replied----'One who criticizes the vedas and follows the religion of other's after abandoning his own or torments virtuous people is a grave sinner. Similarly, not respecting one's parents,…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "2.2.1 Different Types of Sin and Virtue Once, Yayati requested Matali to describe the various sinful and virtuous deeds, which a man normally commits in his life. Matali replied----'One who criticizes the vedas and follows the rel"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-2-2"><data key="title">King Yayati propagates Vaishnava-Dharma</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">King Yayati was the son of Nahush and a descendant of Soma--dynasty. He was a great devotee of Lord Vishnu and had contributed a lot in the propagation of Vaishnava--dharma. Yayati had sent many emissaries in all directions to propagate Vaishnava--dharma. During Yayati's reign his subject were prosperous and were free…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "2.2.2 King Yayati propagates Vaishnava-Dharma King Yayati was the son of Nahush and a descendant of Soma--dynasty. He was a great devotee of Lord Vishnu and had contributed a lot in the propagation of Vaishnava--dharma. Yayati had sent"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-2-3"><data key="title">Yayati marries Ashrubindumati</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, king Yayati went into a forest for hunting. He saw a stag, which had four horns and chased it. The deer led him deep into the forest and then disappeared. Yayati was tired and thirsty. He saw a lake and decided to quench his thirst. First of all he took his bath and then drank sweet water from the lake.As Yayati…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 15, "quote": "2.2.3 Yayati marries Ashrubindumati Once, king Yayati went into a forest for hunting. He saw a stag, which had four horns and chased it. The deer led him deep into the forest and then disappeared. Yayati was tired and thirsty."}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-2-4"><data key="title">Kunjal – The Enlightened Parrot</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, while describing about the importance of a teacher, Lord Vishnu had narrated the following tale to king Ven--- Sage Chyavan came from the lineage of Bhargav. Once, he arrived at Omkareshwar with the objective of acquiring knowledge. Since he was tired, he decided to take rest under the shade of a Banyan tree. A…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 15, "quote": "2.2.4 Kunjal – The Enlightened Parrot Once, while describing about the importance of a teacher, Lord Vishnu had narrated the following tale to king Ven--- Sage Chyavan came from the lineage of Bhargav. Once, he arrived at Omkare"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-2-5"><data key="title">Kunjal Preaches Vijjwal</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing the virtue of benevolence to Vijjwal. Kunjal--the learned parrot narrated the following tale --- Once, there lived a king named Subahu who ruled over chola desha. A learned brahmin named Jaimini used to counsel him on religious and spiritual matters.One day, while Jaimini was giving a discourse on the…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 16, "quote": "2.2.5 Kunjal Preaches Vijjwal Describing the virtue of benevolence to Vijjwal. Kunjal--the learned parrot narrated the following tale --- Once, there lived a king named Subahu who ruled over chola desha. A learned brahmi"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-2-6"><data key="title">Kunjal Narrates about his Previous Birth</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Sage Chyavan, who had been listening to the narration of Kunjal, was amazed by his profound knowledge. He asked Kunjal---O great soul! Who are you? From where did you acquire such divine knowledge.' Kunjal, who remembered everything about his previous birth said-- In my previous birth, I was the youngest son of a…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 16, "quote": "2.2.6 Kunjal Narrates about his Previous Birth Sage Chyavan, who had been listening to the narration of Kunjal, was amazed by his profound knowledge. He asked Kunjal---O great soul! Who are you? From where did you acquire such divine kno"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-3"><data key="title">Swargakhand</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 17, "quote": "2.3 Swargakhand"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-3-1"><data key="title">Some Prominent Holy Places in Bharat-Varsha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, sages requested Sutji to describe about the most prominent places of pilgrimage situated in Bharatvarsha. Sutji commenced his narration by describing the origin of creation and then switched over to the geographical characteristics of Bharatvarsha. Sutji said---'There are seven prominent mountain ranges in…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 17, "quote": "2.3.1 Some Prominent Holy Places in Bharat-Varsha Once, sages requested Sutji to describe about the most prominent places of pilgrimage situated in Bharatvarsha. Sutji commenced his narration by describing the origin of creation and then sw"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-3-2"><data key="title">Jambubarg, River Narmada, Amarkantak</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Continuing with his narration, Sage Vashishth said--- A man desirous of going on a pilgrimage should first of all visit Jambumarg, because it is revered even by the deities and the sages. By visiting this holy place a man acquires virtue equivalent to the accomplishment of an Ashwamedha yagya and attains to…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 17, "quote": "2.3.2 Jambubarg, River Narmada, Amarkantak Continuing with his narration, Sage Vashishth said--- A man desirous of going on a pilgrimage should first of all visit Jambumarg, because it is revered even by the deities and the sages. By"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-3-3"><data key="title">‘Dharma-Teertha’ &amp; Yamuna-Snan</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sage Narad continued with the description of various holy places and said--- "A pilgrim must visit Dharma Teerth, which is named after the lord of death--Dharma. Once upon a time Dharmaraj had performed an austere penance at that place this is the reason why it became famous as Dharma Teerth. By visiting Dharma…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 18, "quote": "2.3.3 ‘Dharma-Teertha’ &amp; Yamuna-Snan Sage Narad continued with the description of various holy places and said--- \"A pilgrim must visit Dharma Teerth, which is named after the lord of death--Dharma. Once upon a time Dharmaraj h"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-3-4"><data key="title">Kashipuri, Kapardishwar &amp; Gaya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">On being asked by Yudhishthir about the grandeur of holy places like Kashipuri, Kapardishwar &amp; Gaya. Narad said --- Just as Lord Mahadev is supreme among all the deities, in the same manner Kashipuri holds a significant status among all the places of pilgrimage. Hence an individual should make it a point to visit…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 18, "quote": "2.3.4 Kashipuri, Kapardishwar &amp; Gaya On being asked by Yudhishthir about the grandeur of holy places like Kashipuri, Kapardishwar &amp; Gaya. Narad said --- Just as Lord Mahadev is supreme among all the deities, in the same manner"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-3-5"><data key="title">Puranas – The Embodiment of Sri Hari</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">According to Sutji, all the Puranas are nothing but the mediums through which Sri Hari manifests himself---Brahma Puran is said to be the Forhead of Srihari, Padma Puran is said to be the 'heart' of Srihari, Vishnu Puran is said to be the 'right arm' of Srihari. Shiva Puran is said to be the 'left arm' of Srihari.…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 19, "quote": "2.3.5 Puranas – The Embodiment of Sri Hari According to Sutji, all the Puranas are nothing but the mediums through which Sri Hari manifests himself---Brahma Puran is said to be the Forhead of Srihari, Padma Puran is said to be the 'h"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-4"><data key="title">Patalkhand</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 19, "quote": "2.4 Patalkhand"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-4-1"><data key="title">Sheshnag narrates the Tales of Sri Rama’s Return from Lanka</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, Sages requested Sutji to describe about Sri Ram's return from Lanka. Sutji narrated the same tale, which Sheshnag had once told Vatsyayan.Sheshnag said--After the killing of demon king Ravan, Sri Ram appointed Vibhishan as the king of Lanka. He then decided to return to Ayodhya on Pushpak-Viman, which Vibhishan…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 19, "quote": "2.4.1 Sheshnag narrates the Tales of Sri Rama’s Return from Lanka Once, Sages requested Sutji to describe about Sri Ram's return from Lanka. Sutji narrated the same tale, which Sheshnag had once told Vatsyayan.Sheshnag said--After the killing of demon king"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-4-2"><data key="title">Ravana – The Mighty Demon</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">People of Ayodhya lived peacefully during Sri Ram's reign until one fateful day when they were struck by a bad news--Sri Ram had abandoned Sita after a Washerman leveled an unfair charge against her character. Such was the greatness of Sri Ram that opinion of each and every individual was given due respect…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 20, "quote": "2.4.2 Ravana – The Mighty Demon People of Ayodhya lived peacefully during Sri Ram's reign until one fateful day when they were struck by a bad news--Sri Ram had abandoned Sita after a Washerman leveled an unfair charge aga"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-4-3"><data key="title">Agastya Advises Sri Rama to Perform Ashvamedhya Yagya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sri Ram requested sage Agastya to tell about the means by which he could become liberated from the sins of killing brahmins. Sage Agastya advised him to perform Ashwamedha-yagya. He also described the rituals of performing Ashwamedha Yagya--- "Get a horse of white colour and after worshipping it on Vaishakh Purnima…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 20, "quote": "2.4.3 Agastya Advises Sri Rama to Perform Ashvamedhya Yagya Sri Ram requested sage Agastya to tell about the means by which he could become liberated from the sins of killing brahmins. Sage Agastya advised him to perform Ashwamedha-yagya. He also des"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-4-4"><data key="title">The Horse is Let Loose</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">As per the advice of Sage Vashishth, Sri Ram instructed his soldiers to bring a white-horse. A white parasol was fixed on its back and the horse was decorated with colourful clothes. The horse was then let loose and the faithful soldiers of Sri Ram followed it wherever the horse went.A gold-sheet was hanged in the…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "2.4.4 The Horse is Let Loose As per the advice of Sage Vashishth, Sri Ram instructed his soldiers to bring a white-horse. A white parasol was fixed on its back and the horse was decorated with colourful clothes. The hor"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-4-5"><data key="title">Lav Fastens the Horse</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lava stops the sacrificial horse during Rama’s ashvamedha. The ensuing battle sequence moves toward the recognition of Rama’s family.</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "2.4.5 Lav Fastens the Horse Some of the kings tried to stop the horse, but were defeated by Shatrughan. This way the horse continued to move ahead without any problem till it reached the bank of river Ganga.It was earl"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-4-6"><data key="title">Lav Vanquishes the Whole Army</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shatrughan became extremely furious after seeing the condition of his injured soldiers. He instructed Kalnemi to teach Lav a lesson. Kalnemi confronted Lav with a huge army and a ferocious battle took place. Lav managed to kill Kalnemi, which created havoc in the rival's army. The surviving soldiers tried to hold…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "2.4.6 Lav Vanquishes the Whole Army Shatrughan became extremely furious after seeing the condition of his injured soldiers. He instructed Kalnemi to teach Lav a lesson. Kalnemi confronted Lav with a huge army and a ferocious b"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-4-7"><data key="title">Lav Becomes Unconscious</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When Shatrughan learnt about Hanuman's fate he was infuriated and went to fight Lav. But when he saw a tender looking child in front of him he was surprised. Shatrughan asked -- 'O brave child! Who are you! Who is your father?' But Lav was not interested in his irrelevant queries and challenged him for a duel.…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "2.4.7 Lav Becomes Unconscious When Shatrughan learnt about Hanuman's fate he was infuriated and went to fight Lav. But when he saw a tender looking child in front of him he was surprised. Shatrughan asked -- 'O brave chi"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-4-8"><data key="title">The Army Returns to Ayodhya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shatrughan decided to return to Ayodhya. He instructed Sumati to make all the necessary arrangements for the safe return of the Caravan, which comprised not only of Sri Ram's army but also of all the defeated kings and their vanquished armies. When Sri Ram came to know about their arrival, he sent Laxman to receive…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 22, "quote": "2.4.8 The Army Returns to Ayodhya Shatrughan decided to return to Ayodhya. He instructed Sumati to make all the necessary arrangements for the safe return of the Caravan, which comprised not only of Sri Ram's army but also o"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-4-9"><data key="title">Sri Rama sends Laxman to bring Sita</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sage Valmiki was present in Ayodhya to attend the Ashwamedha yagya. Sri Ram asked him about the identity of the two children--Lav and Kush, Valmiki said --- Sita was pregnant at the time you had abandoned her. I gave her refuge in my hermitage where she gave birth to twins--Lav and Kush. Both the children were brought…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 22, "quote": "2.4.9 Sri Rama sends Laxman to bring Sita Sage Valmiki was present in Ayodhya to attend the Ashwamedha yagya. Sri Ram asked him about the identity of the two children--Lav and Kush, Valmiki said --- Sita was pregnant at the time you"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-4-10"><data key="title">Sita Returns to Ayodhya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sri Ram eulogised all the revered sages who had arrived to attend the Ashwamedha yagya. The holy water of Saryu was brought and sprinkled in the yagya kund where Ashwamedha yagya was being performed, amidst the chanting of vedic mantras.Hardly had Sri Ram touched the horse with his hands then it transformed into a…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 22, "quote": "2.4.10 Sita Returns to Ayodhya Sri Ram eulogised all the revered sages who had arrived to attend the Ashwamedha yagya. The holy water of Saryu was brought and sprinkled in the yagya kund where Ashwamedha yagya was being p"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5"><data key="title">Uttarkhand</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 22, "quote": "2.5 Uttarkhand"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-1"><data key="title">Badrik-Ashram</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, on being asked by the sages about the greatness of Badrikasharam, Sutji narrated the same tale, which Lord Shiva had once told sage Narad --- Lord Mahadeva had told Narad that altogether there were one lakh and twenty-five thousand mountains and Badrikashram was supreme among them. Lord Mahadeva…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 22, "quote": "2.5.1 Badrik-Ashram Once, on being asked by the sages about the greatness of Badrikasharam, Sutji narrated the same tale, which Lord Shiva had once told sage Narad ---"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-2"><data key="title">The Descent of River Ganga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing about the reason why river Ganga was brought down on the earth, Lord Mahadeva told Narad--- King Bhagirath was the architect of Ganga's arrival on the earth. He wanted to liberate his dead ancestorss from the curse of Sage Kapil.King Sagar came from the lineage of the famous king--Harishchandra. Sagar had…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 23, "quote": "2.5.2 The Descent of River Ganga Describing about the reason why river Ganga was brought down on the earth, Lord Mahadeva told Narad--- King Bhagirath was the architect of Ganga's arrival on the earth. He wanted to liberate"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-3"><data key="title">Dasharatha Pacifies Shani</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sage Narad curiously asked Lord Mahadeva as to what made Shani such a volatile planet.Lord Mahadeva replied--- Shani is extremely violent by nature and everybody is scarred of this planet. Once, the royal astrologer of Dasharath was petrified at the prospect of Shani entering the constellation of Rohini and warned him…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 23, "quote": "2.5.3 Dasharatha Pacifies Shani Sage Narad curiously asked Lord Mahadeva as to what made Shani such a volatile planet.Lord Mahadeva replied--- Shani is extremely violent by nature and everybody is scarred of this planet. O"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-4"><data key="title">Qualities of a Vaishnava</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Giving a detailed description of the qualities of a Vaishnava, Lord Mahadeva told Narad --- A person who has total devotion in Lord Vishnu is called a Vaishnava. He is truthful, kind and forgiving by nature. He engages himself in austere penance and refrains from any kind of violence. He wears a Tulsi bead in his neck…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 23, "quote": "2.5.4 Qualities of a Vaishnava Giving a detailed description of the qualities of a Vaishnava, Lord Mahadeva told Narad --- A person who has total devotion in Lord Vishnu is called a Vaishnava. He is truthful, kind and for"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-5"><data key="title">Indraprastha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages enquired from Sutji about the holiest city situated on the banks of river yamuna. Sutji narrated the tale which Sage Saubhari had once told Yudhishthir---Once, Narad and Parvat were travelling through an aerial route, while they were flying over Khandav forest, they were lured by the beautiful sight of river…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 23, "quote": "2.5.5 Indraprastha The sages enquired from Sutji about the holiest city situated on the banks of river yamuna. Sutji narrated the tale which Sage Saubhari had once told Yudhishthir---Once, Narad and Parvat wer"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-6"><data key="title">Vaikunth – The Abode of Lord Vishnu</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing about the grandeur of Vaikunth, Lord Mahadeva told Parvati--A person who has total devotion in Lord Vishnu attains to Vaikunth after his death. Vaikuntha is the abode of Vishnu and constitutes of numerous Janapadas. The magnificence of Vaikunth is beyond description and it houses many grand palaces fully…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 24, "quote": "2.5.6 Vaikunth – The Abode of Lord Vishnu Describing about the grandeur of Vaikunth, Lord Mahadeva told Parvati--A person who has total devotion in Lord Vishnu attains to Vaikunth after his death. Vaikuntha is the abode of Vishnu an"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-7"><data key="title">The Incarnations of Matsya and Kurma</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, goddess Parvati expressed her desire to know about all the incarnations of Lord Vishnu.Lord Mahadeva replied-- Lord Brahma had created numerous Prajapatis like Bhrigu, Marichi, Atri, Daksha, Kardam, Pulastya, Pulaha, Angira and Kratu. Marichi was the father of Kashyap. Sage Kashyap had four wives--Aditi, Diti,…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 24, "quote": "2.5.7 The Incarnations of Matsya and Kurma Once, goddess Parvati expressed her desire to know about all the incarnations of Lord Vishnu.Lord Mahadeva replied-- Lord Brahma had created numerous Prajapatis like Bhrigu, Marichi, Atri, D"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-8"><data key="title">NriSimha Incarnation</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Continuing with the various incarnations of Lord Vishnu, Lord Mahadeva told Parvati --- Sage Kashyap had two mighty sons from Diti--Hiranyakashipu and Hiranyaksha. Hiranyaksha had once carried the earth to Rasatala. The deities requested Lord Vishnu to rescue the earth. Lord Vishnu took the incarnation of a boar and…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 25, "quote": "2.5.8 NriSimha Incarnation Continuing with the various incarnations of Lord Vishnu, Lord Mahadeva told Parvati --- Sage Kashyap had two mighty sons from Diti--Hiranyakashipu and Hiranyaksha. Hiranyaksha had once carri"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-9"><data key="title">Vaman Incarnation</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Continuing with the various incarnations of Lord Vishnu, Lord Mahadeva told Parvati --- Prahlad had a son named Virochan.The most benevolent king--'Mahabahu-Bali' was Virochan's son. Bali had defeated the deities and ruled over all the three worlds. Sage Kashyap commenced the most austere penance-- Payovrata to help…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 25, "quote": "2.5.9 Vaman Incarnation Continuing with the various incarnations of Lord Vishnu, Lord Mahadeva told Parvati --- Prahlad had a son named Virochan.The most benevolent king--'Mahabahu-Bali' was Virochan's son. Bali ha"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-10"><data key="title">Sri Rama’s Birth</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Describing about Sri Ram's incarnation Lord Mahadeva told Parvati--'Once, Swayambhuva Manu had expressed his desire of having Lord Vishnu as his son to which Lord Vishnu had agreed. During tretayuga Manu manifested himself as Dasharath and Vishnu kept his promise by taking birth as his son--Sri Ram. Similarly, during…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 26, "quote": "2.5.10 Sri Rama’s Birth Describing about Sri Ram's incarnation Lord Mahadeva told Parvati--'Once, Swayambhuva Manu had expressed his desire of having Lord Vishnu as his son to which Lord Vishnu had agreed. During t"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-11"><data key="title">Sri Rama Grows Up</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Continuing with the tale of Sri Ram, Lord Mahadeva told Parvati--Dasharath requested Vashishth to perform the Jaatkarm Sanskar of Sri Ram. In course of time Kaikeyi gave birth to Bharat--an incarnation of Panchajanya conch. Similarly, Sumitra gave birth to two sons--Laxman and Shatrughan. Laxman was the incarnation of…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 26, "quote": "2.5.11 Sri Rama Grows Up Continuing with the tale of Sri Ram, Lord Mahadeva told Parvati--Dasharath requested Vashishth to perform the Jaatkarm Sanskar of Sri Ram. In course of time Kaikeyi gave birth to Bharat--an"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-12"><data key="title">Sri Rama’s Exile</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Mahadeva continued with the tale of Sri Ram and told Parvati --Dasharath wanted to appoint Sri Ram as his successor but Kaikeyi did not like this idea. She wanted Bharat to be made the king of Ayodhya and Sri Ram to be sent into exile for 14 years. Once, Kaikeyi had saved Dasharath's life while he was fighting a…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 27, "quote": "2.5.12 Sri Rama’s Exile Lord Mahadeva continued with the tale of Sri Ram and told Parvati --Dasharath wanted to appoint Sri Ram as his successor but Kaikeyi did not like this idea. She wanted Bharat to be made the"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-13"><data key="title">Sri Rama’s Coronation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing the coronation of Sri Ram Lord Mahadeva told Parvati--People of Ayodhya rejoiced at the return of Sri Ram. An auspicious day was chosen for his coronation and Sri Ram became the king of Ayodhya amidst the chantings of Vedic mantras. Sri Ram ruled over Ayodhya for one thousand years without any problem.…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 27, "quote": "2.5.13 Sri Rama’s Coronation Describing the coronation of Sri Ram Lord Mahadeva told Parvati--People of Ayodhya rejoiced at the return of Sri Ram. An auspicious day was chosen for his coronation and Sri Ram became the k"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-14"><data key="title">Sri Krshna</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Parvati requested Lord Mahadeva to narrate the divine tales of Vasudeva Krishna. Lord Mahadeva replied--Vasudev was the descendant of yadu. His father was Devameedha. He was married to Devaki-- the daughter of Ugrasena.Ugrasena also had a mighty son named Kansa. Kansa was extremely affectionate towards his sister…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 28, "quote": "2.5.14 Sri Krshna Parvati requested Lord Mahadeva to narrate the divine tales of Vasudeva Krishna. Lord Mahadeva replied--Vasudev was the descendant of yadu. His father was Devameedha. He was married to Devak"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-15"><data key="title">Killing of Kansa</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Continuing with the divine tales of Sri Krishna, Lord Mahadeva told Parvati--When Kansa failed in his repeated attempts to kill Sri Krishna, he instructed 'Akrura' to invite both Krishna and Balaram to Mathura on the pretext of attending a ceremony called Dhanush-yagya. Kansa had planned to kill Sri Krishna by…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 29, "quote": "2.5.15 Killing of Kansa Continuing with the divine tales of Sri Krishna, Lord Mahadeva told Parvati--When Kansa failed in his repeated attempts to kill Sri Krishna, he instructed 'Akrura' to invite both Krishna and"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-16"><data key="title">Jarasandh</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Jarasandh was the father-in-law of Kansa. When he learnt about Kansa' death he surrounded Mathura with a huge army. Sri Krishna was well aware of Jarasandh's might and bravery. He was also convinced that it would not be easy to defeat Jarasandh. So, he remembered Daruk--his eternal charioteer and sought his help.…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 30, "quote": "2.5.16 Jarasandh Jarasandh was the father-in-law of Kansa. When he learnt about Kansa' death he surrounded Mathura with a huge army. Sri Krishna was well aware of Jarasandh's might and bravery. He was also c"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-17"><data key="title">Abduction of Rukmini</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Mahadeva told Parvati--- When Jarasandh learnt about Kaalyavan's death, he attacked Balaram to avenge his friend's death. But, very soon he realized that it was beyond his capacity to defeat Balaram, so he retreated alongwith his army. Sri Krishna and Balaram returned to Dwarka.In course of time, Balaram married…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 30, "quote": "2.5.17 Abduction of Rukmini Lord Mahadeva told Parvati--- When Jarasandh learnt about Kaalyavan's death, he attacked Balaram to avenge his friend's death. But, very soon he realized that it was beyond his capacity to d"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-18"><data key="title">Sri Krshna’s Queens</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Continuing with the divine tales of Sri Krishna, Lord Mahadeva told Parvati--Sri Krishna had 16 thousand queens among whom Satyabhama, Kalindi, Mitravinda, etc. were prominent.King Sattajit had a priceless diamond named--Syamantak, which he had presented to his younger brother--Prasena. Sri Krishna was fascinated by…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 31, "quote": "2.5.18 Sri Krshna’s Queens Continuing with the divine tales of Sri Krishna, Lord Mahadeva told Parvati--Sri Krishna had 16 thousand queens among whom Satyabhama, Kalindi, Mitravinda, etc. were prominent.King Sattajit"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-19"><data key="title">Anirudha Marries Usha</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lord Mahadeva continued with the divine tales of Sri Krishna--O Parvati! Sri Krishna had a son from Rukmini whose name was 'Aniruddha'. Aniruddha was very brave and had killed a demon named Shambasur. There is an interesting tale how Aniruddha married Usha. Once, Usha--the daughter of Bangur, saw a very handsome young…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 32, "quote": "2.5.19 Anirudha Marries Usha Lord Mahadeva continued with the divine tales of Sri Krishna--O Parvati! Sri Krishna had a son from Rukmini whose name was 'Aniruddha'. Aniruddha was very brave and had killed a demon named"}]</data></node><node id="p-padma-puran-2-5-20"><data key="title">The Decline of the Yadu Dynasty</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Parvati was listening to the divine tales of Sri Krishna with rapt attention. Lord Mahadeva told Parvati-- 'Paundrak was the king of Kashi. He did an austere penance for twelve years to please me. When I appeared before him, he requested me to make his appearance look like Sri Krishna. I blessed him with an appearance…</data><data key="doc">padma-puran</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 32, "quote": "2.5.20 The Decline of the Yadu Dynasty Parvati was listening to the divine tales of Sri Krishna with rapt attention. Lord Mahadeva told Parvati-- 'Paundrak was the king of Kashi. He did an austere penance for twelve years to plea"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-1"><data key="title">Chapter 1</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">VASUDEVSUTAM DEVAM KANS CHANUR MARDANAM | DEVAKI PARMANANDAM KRISHAM VANDE JAGAD GURUM || NARAYANAM NAMASKRITY NARAM CHAIV NAROTTAMAM | DEVI SARASWATIM CHAIV TATO JAY MUNDIRAYET || YAH SWANU BHAVAM-KHILK SHRUTI SARMEKAM DHYATM DIPMATI TITIRSHATAMTAMOADAM | SANSARINAM KARUNAAAH PURAN GUHYAM TAM VYAS SUNUMUPAYAMI GURUM…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "16.1 Chapter 1 This chapter contains 12 sub-sections. VASUDEVSUTAM DEVAM KANS CHANUR MARDANAM | DEVAKI PARMANANDAM KRISHAM VANDE JAGAD GURUM || NARAYANAM NAMASKRITY NARAM CHAIV NAROTTAMAM | DEVI SARASWATIM CHAIV TATO JAY MUNDIRAYET || YAH S"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-1-1"><data key="title">Obeisance to the Supreme Being</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">We meditate to that Paramatma (Supreme Being) who is the eternal truth; and who creates the universe, holds it and causes Pralaya. He is the light independence and from of knowledge in himself. He causes illusions even for the most knowledgeous ones. Yet, He is far beyond all the illusions. In Shrimad Bhagwad, created…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "16.1.1 Obeisance to the Supreme Being We meditate to that Paramatma (Supreme Being) who is the eternal truth; and who creates the universe, holds it and causes Pralaya. He is the light independence and from of knowledge in himse"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-1-2"><data key="title">Performance of Gyan Yagya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">In the beginning of the Kalyug, sages like Shaunak et al organized a vast Gyan Yagga in the holy region of the Naimisharanya. The purpose of the yagya was the benefit of the common people and it was to last for a millennium. To quench the spiritual curiosity of the sages, SUTJI, the orator of Puranas, also arrived…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "16.1.2 Performance of Gyan Yagya In the beginning of the Kalyug, sages like Shaunak et al organized a vast Gyan Yagga in the holy region of the Naimisharanya. The purpose of the yagya was the benefit of the common people an"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-1-3"><data key="title">Sutji narrates Purana</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Pleased by the curiosity of the sages, SUTJI said, " O great sages, it is extremely auspicious to talk about Lord Krishna. Only by discussing about the God, can the mind of the people be freed from illusions. Love then begins to emerge in it for God and it begins to detach itself from physical comfort and experience…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "16.1.3 Sutji narrates Purana Pleased by the curiosity of the sages, SUTJI said, \" O great sages, it is extremely auspicious to talk about Lord Krishna. Only by discussing about the God, can the mind of the people be fre"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-1-4"><data key="title">Lord Vishnu – The Source of All Creation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Driven by the desire, the Adipurush (primitive being) Narayan took the help of his Prakriti (nature's female creational force) and generated himself in Chaturbhuj (four-armed) from. And from His navel appeared Lord Brahma, the creator. That form of God was full of pure truth and from the parts of His body, all the…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "16.1.4 Lord Vishnu – The Source of All Creation Driven by the desire, the Adipurush (primitive being) Narayan took the help of his Prakriti (nature's female creational force) and generated himself in Chaturbhuj (four-armed) from. And from"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-1-5"><data key="title">The Creation and Compilation of the Purana</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shaunkji asked, " When that tale was compiled in scriptural form, that was later on related by Shukdevji. Where was it created? For what purpose and by whose inspiration, that scripture was compiled and how did Shukdevji, who was congenital wandered, came to study this scripture, and how he came to see the king…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "16.1.5 The Creation and Compilation of the Purana Shaunkji asked, \" When that tale was compiled in scriptural form, that was later on related by Shukdevji. Where was it created? For what purpose and by whose inspiration, that scripture was"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-1-6"><data key="title">Ved-Vyas – The Partial Incarnation</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Vedvyasji had appeared in Dwapar Yuga. He was the son of Maharshi Parashar and Satyawati then known as Matsyagandha. Vyasji was a partial incarnation of the God. Vedvyasji effected a division of Vedas, created Puranas conceptualized history (Mahabharata-which was actually jotted down by none other than Lord Ganesha.)…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "16.1.6 Ved-Vyas – The Partial Incarnation Vedvyasji had appeared in Dwapar Yuga. He was the son of Maharshi Parashar and Satyawati then known as Matsyagandha. Vyasji was a partial incarnation of the God. Vedvyasji effected a divisio"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-1-7"><data key="title">Naradji’s Advice to Ved-Vyas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Naradji told him that though he had created great epics, yet all of them lack a devotional description of God. That was why his soul was still writhing. Naradji also suggested him to describe the glory of Hari, or neither his voice, nor his creation would ever get pure. Devarshi Narad then narrated the tale of God and…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "16.1.7 Naradji’s Advice to Ved-Vyas Naradji told him that though he had created great epics, yet all of them lack a devotional description of God. That was why his soul was still writhing. Naradji also suggested him to describ"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-1-8"><data key="title">King Parikshit encounters Kali Yuga</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">During his regime, the king Parikshit got untoward reports that Kaliyug had entered within the limits of his otherwise peaceful kingdom. With a huge army, Parikshit at once setout to the spot where Kaliyug had arrived. On the way he saw the religion and the earth in the guises of a bull and a cow respectively. The cow…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "16.1.8 King Parikshit encounters Kali Yuga During his regime, the king Parikshit got untoward reports that Kaliyug had entered within the limits of his otherwise peaceful kingdom. With a huge army, Parikshit at once setout to the spo"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-1-9"><data key="title">King Parikshit dishonors Sage Shamik</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">One day, king Parikshit went on a hunting excursion. Till noon, he could not find a game. He was feeling restless because of hunger, thirst and fatigue. Searching water and shelter the king reached the hermitage of the sage Shamik. Shamik was in deep meditation so he did not welcome the king. For the first time the…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "16.1.9 King Parikshit dishonors Sage Shamik One day, king Parikshit went on a hunting excursion. Till noon, he could not find a game. He was feeling restless because of hunger, thirst and fatigue. Searching water and shelter the king"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-1-10"><data key="title">Shukhdevji’s Arrival</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">At that moment the ever-wandering, indifferent-natured sage Shukdevji also arrived there. He was self indulgent and free from the bondages of ashrams (phases of life). He was then only sixteen years old but appeared quite radiant and graceful. TATRA BHAVAD BHAGVAN VIAS PUTRO, YADRICHCHHAYA GAMARMANO ANOYESHAH |…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 6, "quote": "16.1.10 Shukhdevji’s Arrival At that moment the ever-wandering, indifferent-natured sage Shukdevji also arrived there. He was self indulgent and free from the bondages of ashrams (phases of life). He was then only sixte"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-2"><data key="title">Chapter 2</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This chapter is very short – containing only 2 sub-sections.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 6, "quote": "16.2 Chapter 2 This chapter is very short – containing only 2 sub-sections."}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-2-1"><data key="title">Contents of this Chapter</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shri Shukdevji says 'O King, ordinary people literally waste their lives in petty matters like useless discussions, worry, sleep and feeding themselves. Those who wish to conquer their fears, O Parikshit, must listen to, recite and remember the tales that depict the plays of Lord Shri Krishna. The human birth can be…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 6, "quote": "16.2.1 Contents of this Chapter Shri Shukdevji says 'O King, ordinary people literally waste their lives in petty matters like useless discussions, worry, sleep and feeding themselves. Those who wish to conquer their fears"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-2-2"><data key="title">Creation of the World</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The king Parikshit felt extreme pleasure when Shukdevji satisfied his queries. He then began to feel submerged in the stream of Bhagwad Katha (tale of the God). His selfish worries regarding his own benefits existed no more. He insisted again: O Brahmin, now kindly explain how does the God create the universe out of…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 7, "quote": "16.2.2 Creation of the World The king Parikshit felt extreme pleasure when Shukdevji satisfied his queries. He then began to feel submerged in the stream of Bhagwad Katha (tale of the God). His selfish worries regarding"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-3"><data key="title">Chapter 3</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 7, "quote": "16.3 Chapter 3"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-3-1"><data key="title">Contents of the third Chapter</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">VIDURODDHAV SANVADAH KSHATRMAITREY YOSTATHA | PURAN SANHITA PRASHNO MAHA PURUSH SANATHITIH || AVATARO BhagwadAH KAPILASY MAHATMANAH | DEVHUTYASHCH SANVADH KAPILEN CHA DHIMATA ||</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 7, "quote": "16.3.1 Contents of the third Chapter VIDURODDHAV SANVADAH KSHATRMAITREY YOSTATHA | PURAN SANHITA PRASHNO MAHA PURUSH SANATHITIH || AVATARO BhagwadAH KAPILASY MAHATMANAH | DEVHUTYASHCH SANVADH KAPILEN CHA DHIMATA ||"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-3-2"><data key="title">Vidur warns Dhritarashtra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shri Shukdevji says, "O Parikshit, Vidurji deserted his homely comforts and luxuries because he had to protest against injustice." When, after losing in the game of dice, the Pandavas successfully passed the period of their exile and stay in guise, and were denied even then of their rightful share in the kingdom,…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 7, "quote": "16.3.2 Vidur warns Dhritarashtra Shri Shukdevji says, \"O Parikshit, Vidurji deserted his homely comforts and luxuries because he had to protest against injustice.\" When, after losing in the game of dice, the Pandavas succes"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-3-3"><data key="title">Vidur comes to know about Kaurava’s Destruction</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Travelling through various pilgrimage places, Vidurji reached Prabhas region where he got the news of total destruction of the Kaurvas. The news deeply moved him. In search of solace, he continued to wander from one place of pilgrimage to another place. During his wandering, when he reached the banks of the river…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 8, "quote": "16.3.3 Vidur comes to know about Kaurava’s Destruction Travelling through various pilgrimage places, Vidurji reached Prabhas region where he got the news of total destruction of the Kaurvas. The news deeply moved him. In search of solace, he con"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-3-4"><data key="title">Vidur meets Maitrey</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The pious Vidurji respectfully greeted the sage Maitreya and said, "O lord, the people in this world perform various actions with a desire to have pleasure. But neither do they get pleasure nor is their sorrow removed. Their sorrow increases instead. What is good then, is this context? SUKMAY KARMANI KAROTI LOKE | NA…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 8, "quote": "16.3.4 Vidur meets Maitrey The pious Vidurji respectfully greeted the sage Maitreya and said, \"O lord, the people in this world perform various actions with a desire to have pleasure. But neither do they get pleasure"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-3-5"><data key="title">Description of 10 Ways of Creation by Maitrey</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">1. By the inspiration of God and because of imbalance in pious virtues, creation of Mahattatva (the soul) was the first (Mahattatva the soul). 2. From Mahattatva (the soul) originates Ahankaar (the ego) which generates five primitive elements (the earth, the fire, the air, the water and the ether), sense organs and…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 9, "quote": "16.3.5 Description of 10 Ways of Creation by Maitrey 1. By the inspiration of God and because of imbalance in pious virtues, creation of Mahattatva (the soul) was the first (Mahattatva the soul). 2. From Mahattatva (the soul) originates Ahanka"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-3-6"><data key="title">Sage Kardam</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Continuing the process of creation, Brahma produced Sage Kardam from his shadow and asked him to observe penance first. Thereafter, Brahma dictated Kardam to acquire physical strength and the strength of his sense and undertake the process of creating his offsprings. As per his father's dictate, Kardam observed severe…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 10, "quote": "16.3.6 Sage Kardam Continuing the process of creation, Brahma produced Sage Kardam from his shadow and asked him to observe penance first. Thereafter, Brahma dictated Kardam to acquire physical strength and th"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-3-7"><data key="title">Lord Kapil</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Married life of Kardam and Devhuti is a lesson for all the civilized couples of the world. Abiding to the limits of human behaviour in married life is a guarantee for the wellbeing of the couple. Without religion and morality, a man is not more than an animal. Kardam and Devhuti followed strict rules to welcome the…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 10, "quote": "16.3.7 Lord Kapil Married life of Kardam and Devhuti is a lesson for all the civilized couples of the world. Abiding to the limits of human behaviour in married life is a guarantee for the wellbeing of the co"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-3-8"><data key="title">Lord Kapil Preaches Metaphysical Knowledge to Devhuti</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Mata Devhuti prayed lord Kapil to preach the way for the benefaction of the soul, which is attached with ephimeral body. Lord said 'O mother, Adhayatma Yoga (spiritual conjugation) is the main way of benefaction for the human beings. With devotion for me, soul becomes self-knowing. Total devotion of the soul in my…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 11, "quote": "16.3.8 Lord Kapil Preaches Metaphysical Knowledge to Devhuti Mata Devhuti prayed lord Kapil to preach the way for the benefaction of the soul, which is attached with ephimeral body. Lord said 'O mother, Adhayatma Yoga (spiritual conjugation) is the ma"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-4"><data key="title">Chapter 4</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 12, "quote": "16.4 Chapter 4"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-4-1"><data key="title">Description of the Clans of Manu’s Daughters</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Maitrey then related the description of the Manu's daughters. Manu and Shatarupa had three daughters- Akuti, Devhuti and Prasuti. Akuti was married to a Prajapati named Ruchi. They begot two children-- a son and a daughter. The son later on came to be known as Yagyavatar of Lord Narayana, while the daughter was named…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 12, "quote": "16.4.1 Description of the Clans of Manu’s Daughters Maitrey then related the description of the Manu's daughters. Manu and Shatarupa had three daughters- Akuti, Devhuti and Prasuti. Akuti was married to a Prajapati named Ruchi. They begot two"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-4-2"><data key="title">Misbehavior of Dakshaprajapati with Lord Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Vidur asked Maitrey: 'Lord Shiva is an idol of peace. He has no enemity with anyone. He is self indulgent Why did then Daksh Prajapati misbehave with Him? Maitrey said, " Once a meeting of Brahmavadis (expounders of vedas) was organized. Alongwith all the gods Lord Shiva and Brahma were also present in the meeting.…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 12, "quote": "16.4.2 Misbehavior of Dakshaprajapati with Lord Shiva Vidur asked Maitrey: 'Lord Shiva is an idol of peace. He has no enemity with anyone. He is self indulgent Why did then Daksh Prajapati misbehave with Him? Maitrey said, \" Once a meeting of B"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-4-3"><data key="title">Tale of Dhruv</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The self-begotten Manu had two sons- Priyvrat and Uttanpad. King Uttanpad had two queens- Suniti and Suruchi. Uttanpad loved Suruchi more than he loved Suniti. So Suniti was forced to live outside the royal palace with her son Dhruv, while Suruchi and her son Uttam enjoyed all the luxuries in the palace. Once the King…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "16.4.3 Tale of Dhruv The self-begotten Manu had two sons- Priyvrat and Uttanpad. King Uttanpad had two queens- Suniti and Suruchi. Uttanpad loved Suruchi more than he loved Suniti. So Suniti was forced to live o"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-4-4"><data key="title">Tale of Prithu</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">In the lineage of the king Dhruv, had occurred a king named Anga. Anga's wife was Sumita, who was the daughter of Mrityadev. They had a son named Ven who was evil in nature. When he grew up and occupied the throne, he became a very cruel ruler. Day by day his atrocites increased. Then he began to openly insult the…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 15, "quote": "16.4.4 Tale of Prithu In the lineage of the king Dhruv, had occurred a king named Anga. Anga's wife was Sumita, who was the daughter of Mrityadev. They had a son named Ven who was evil in nature. When he grew up"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-4-5"><data key="title">Fable of Puranjan</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The fable of Puranjana uses a king’s story to convey teaching about embodied life. Read the allegory alongside the text’s explicit instruction passages.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 15, "quote": "16.4.5 Fable of Puranjan King Prithu's son was Vijitashu. In his lineage later on occurred a king named Prachin Barhi. King Prachin Barhi had many sons. All of them were known as Prachetas, they were all devotees of"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-5"><data key="title">Chapter 5</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 16, "quote": "16.5 Chapter 5"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-5-1"><data key="title">Tale of Priyvrata</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Hearing the tale of Prachetas' devotion and receiving God's grace by them, a curiosity arose in the mind of the king Parikshit about self-knowledge and Grihasth Dharm (duties of a married person). He asked Shukdev, "O Brahmin, Priyvrat was a supreme devotee and self-indulgent. Why did he then live in Grihasthi…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 17, "quote": "16.5.1 Tale of Priyvrata Hearing the tale of Prachetas' devotion and receiving God's grace by them, a curiosity arose in the mind of the king Parikshit about self-knowledge and Grihasth Dharm (duties of a married pe"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-5-2"><data key="title">Tale of Bharat</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Rishabhdev was married to Jayanti, the daughter of Indra. Jayanti gave birth to one hundred sons. Bharat was the eldest among them. The youngest nine sons became Yogeshwars. Eighty-one sons accepted Brahaminism. Remaining nine brothers followed their elder brother Bharat. One day Rishabhdev called a meeting of his…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 17, "quote": "16.5.2 Tale of Bharat Rishabhdev was married to Jayanti, the daughter of Indra. Jayanti gave birth to one hundred sons. Bharat was the eldest among them. The youngest nine sons became Yogeshwars. Eighty-one sons"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-5-3"><data key="title">Description of the Extent of the Landmass</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Hearing the tale of the king Priyvrat's lineage, Parikshit asked Shukdev: "O great sage, what is the expanse of the landmass? I wish to hear a description of it alongwith it's islands, because this world is a tangible idol of God alongwith his virtues. Hence, knowing it intangible, infinitesimal form of God can also…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 19, "quote": "16.5.3 Description of the Extent of the Landmass Hearing the tale of the king Priyvrat's lineage, Parikshit asked Shukdev: \"O great sage, what is the expanse of the landmass? I wish to hear a description of it alongwith it's islands, becau"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-6"><data key="title">Chapter 6</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 20, "quote": "16.6 Chapter 6"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-6-1"><data key="title">Tale of Ajamil</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Ajamila’s deathbed utterance of Narayana opens a discussion of divine messengers, the name of the Lord and the relation between moral action and devotion.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 20, "quote": "16.6.1 Tale of Ajamil The king Parikshit requested Shukdev, \"O preacher, so far you have related about the way how to overcome attachment for wordily things. Inadvertently, the lliving beings commit some kinds of"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-6-2"><data key="title">Dialogue of Yama and Yamaduts</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says, "Parikshit, when the servers of Lord Vishnu failed the attempt of Yamadutas, they returned to Yamaloka and complained Yama about the event." "O Yamadev, we had so far known that you are the sole authority of punishing the people as per their deeds. No body had dared so far to violate your rule. But today…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "16.6.2 Dialogue of Yama and Yamaduts Shukdev says, \"Parikshit, when the servers of Lord Vishnu failed the attempt of Yamadutas, they returned to Yamaloka and complained Yama about the event.\" \"O Yamadev, we had so far known tha"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-6-3"><data key="title">Birth of Daksh from Prachetas</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says, "Ten sons of the King Prachinbarhi were known as Prachetas. They observed severe penance under the sea. When they re-emerged, Chandra (the moon-the ruler of the vegetation) calmed them and presented a beautiful daughter of the elf Prabhlocha, to them as their wife. From her, Prachetas begot prachetas…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "16.6.3 Birth of Daksh from Prachetas Shukdev says, \"Ten sons of the King Prachinbarhi were known as Prachetas. They observed severe penance under the sea. When they re-emerged, Chandra (the moon-the ruler of the vegetation) cal"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-6-4"><data key="title">Insult of Brhaspati by the Gods</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says, "O Parikshit, Indra had become very haughty by the luxuries he received as the king of the gods. One day, Devraj Indra was sitting on his throne alongwith his queen Shachi. His court was full of courtiers. Forty-nine Marudganas, eight Vasus, eleven Rudras, Aditya, Ribhuganas Vishvedev, Shadhyaganas and…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 22, "quote": "16.6.4 Insult of Brhaspati by the Gods Shukdev says, \"O Parikshit, Indra had become very haughty by the luxuries he received as the king of the gods. One day, Devraj Indra was sitting on his throne alongwith his queen Shachi. His"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-6-5"><data key="title">Preaching of Narayana Kavach (shield) to Indra by Vishvarup</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The king Parikshit asked, " O Lord, kindly relate to me the knowledge (Vaishnavi Vidya) by the power of which Devraj Indra easily defeated the demons." Shukdev says, " Parikshit now I am telling you about the knowledge that was once taught to Indra by Vishwrup. So listen to it carefully with concentration. Whenever…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 22, "quote": "16.6.5 Preaching of Narayana Kavach (shield) to Indra by Vishvarup The king Parikshit asked, \" O Lord, kindly relate to me the knowledge (Vaishnavi Vidya) by the power of which Devraj Indra easily defeated the demons.\" Shukdev says, \" Parikshit now I am tel"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-6-6"><data key="title">Killing of Vishvarup, Defeat of the Gods and Construction of Vajra with the bones of</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Dadhichi Vishrupa became the second Acharya (teacher) of the gods. He also saw over indulgence of Indra in luscious objects. Vishwarup's mother belonged to demon clan, so he had some inclination for the demons also. Stealthily, he supplied parts of offerings of the oblations to the demons. Very soon, Indra too came to…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 23, "quote": "16.6.6 Killing of Vishvarup, Defeat of the Gods and Construction of Vajra with the bones of Dadhichi Vishrupa became the second Acharya (teacher) of the gods. He also saw over indulgence of Indra in luscious objects. Vishwarup's mother belonged to demon clan, so he had some inclina"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-6-7"><data key="title">Killings of Vritrasur by Indra</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Shri Shukdev says, "O Parikshit, from the gallantry of the gods the demon army began to fled. Seeing his army running away in panic, Vritrasur got infuriated. Charging ahead he stopped the gods' army from advancing. He roared loudly. Many of the gods fainted from the frightening thunder of the roar. Advancing…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 23, "quote": "16.6.7 Killings of Vritrasur by Indra Shri Shukdev says, \"O Parikshit, from the gallantry of the gods the demon army began to fled. Seeing his army running away in panic, Vritrasur got infuriated. Charging ahead he stopped the g"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-7"><data key="title">Chapter 7</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 24, "quote": "16.7 Chapter 7"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-7-1"><data key="title">Removal of Doubt about God’s Partiality</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The king Parikshit asked, " O fortunate one, God is said to have an equal view for everybody. Why then does He favor the gods alone. Why does He kill the demons only? Thus I have some doubts regarding God's impartiality. Kindly remove them." Shukdev says, " Parikshit, God's sketches are so vivid that even the great…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 24, "quote": "16.7.1 Removal of Doubt about God’s Partiality The king Parikshit asked, \" O fortunate one, God is said to have an equal view for everybody. Why then does He favor the gods alone. Why does He kill the demons only? Thus I have some doubts"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-7-2"><data key="title">Dialogue of Narad – Yudhishthira (Tales of Jay-Vijay)</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">During the Rajsuya Yagya in Yudhishthir's court, Lord Krishna had beheaded Shishupal with His wheel. Everyone, including Yudhishthir saw Shishupal's soul annihilating in Lord Krishna. Surprised Yudhishthir had asked Narad then, " O Devarshi, isn't it surprising that Shishupal who was so jealous of Lord Krishna, got…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 25, "quote": "16.7.2 Dialogue of Narad – Yudhishthira (Tales of Jay-Vijay) During the Rajsuya Yagya in Yudhishthir's court, Lord Krishna had beheaded Shishupal with His wheel. Everyone, including Yudhishthir saw Shishupal's soul annihilating in Lord Krishna. Surpri"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-7-3"><data key="title">Tale of Hiranyaksh and Hiranykashipu</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">By the curse of Brahmins, the gatekeepers of the God, Jay-Vijay took their first birth as the demons- Hiranyakashipu and Hiranyaksh. Diti was their mother. Hiranyaksh had stolen the earth and hidden it in Sutala Loka, causing worries for Brahma. To remove the worries of Brahma, Lord appeared as Varahavatar (the boar…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 25, "quote": "16.7.3 Tale of Hiranyaksh and Hiranykashipu By the curse of Brahmins, the gatekeepers of the God, Jay-Vijay took their first birth as the demons- Hiranyakashipu and Hiranyaksh. Diti was their mother. Hiranyaksh had stolen the earth an"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-7-4"><data key="title">Sketch of Prahlad, The Great Devotee</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Pious virtues of Prahlad: - The youngest son of the demon king Hiranykashipu, Prahlad was very virtuous. He used to serve the sages with love. He treated all the beings equally and did never differentiate them as separate from him. He had a respect for the elders. Despite having knowledge, wealth, beauty, and…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 26, "quote": "16.7.4 Sketch of Prahlad, The Great Devotee Pious virtues of Prahlad: - The youngest son of the demon king Hiranykashipu, Prahlad was very virtuous. He used to serve the sages with love. He treated all the beings equally and did never"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-7-5"><data key="title">Attempts to Kill Prahlad</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Hearing his father scoffing at Guruputras, Prahlad said, "Father it was not taught to me by them, it is my natural inclination." These words further infuriated Hiranykashipu. Blinded by anger he ordered his soldiers to kill Prahlad at once. Formidable demons hit Prahlad with their arms. At that time Prahlad's…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 27, "quote": "16.7.5 Attempts to Kill Prahlad Hearing his father scoffing at Guruputras, Prahlad said, \"Father it was not taught to me by them, it is my natural inclination.\" These words further infuriated Hiranykashipu. Blinded by ange"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-8"><data key="title">Chapter 8</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 28, "quote": "16.8 Chapter 8"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-8-1"><data key="title">Description of the Manvataras</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">(1) Swayambhu Manvantra- King Parikshit said to Shukdev, "Gurudev, I just heard that tale of self- begotten Manu's lineage. Now kindly describe to me about the others Manus. Also describe the plays of God's different incarnations which occurred during different Manvantaras." Shukdev said, "Parikshit, during Tamas…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 28, "quote": "16.8.1 Description of the Manvataras (1) Swayambhu Manvantra- King Parikshit said to Shukdev, \"Gurudev, I just heard that tale of self- begotten Manu's lineage. Now kindly describe to me about the others Manus. Also describe th"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-8-2"><data key="title">Tale of Gajendra, the elephant and Grah, the crocodile</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Gajendra the elephant, caught by a crocodile, turns to the divine for refuge. The retelling makes surrender and deliverance central.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 30, "quote": "16.8.2 Tale of Gajendra, the elephant and Grah, the crocodile King Parikshit asked, \"Munivar, God had salve Gajendra, in His Hari incarnation. I wish to hear this tale.\" Shukdev says, \" O king, in Tamas Manvantara, God had appeared as Hari and saved Ga"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-8-3"><data key="title">Churning of the Sea</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The gods and demons churn the ocean together. Their quest for nectar introduces cooperation, rivalry and Vishnu’s intervention.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 31, "quote": "16.8.3 Churning of the Sea King Parikshit asked, \"O great sage, how did God cause churning of the sea. What was the purpose behind it? Kindly tell me?\" Shukdev says, \"Parikshit, in the sixth Chakshush Manvantara,the d"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-8-4"><data key="title">Conquering of Heaven by King Bali</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says, "Parikshit by the virtues of the ambrosia, the gods had become invincible. They began to take revenge and the demon king Bali met his death in the battle. When Brahma saw the imminent extermination of the demons, he sent Devarshi Narad in the battlefield to make the gods abstain from the war. The…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 32, "quote": "16.8.4 Conquering of Heaven by King Bali Shukdev says, \"Parikshit by the virtues of the ambrosia, the gods had become invincible. They began to take revenge and the demon king Bali met his death in the battle. When Brahma saw the i"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-8-5"><data key="title">Tale of Matysa-Avatar</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">King Parikshit asked: 'O Gurudev, Lord is omnipotent. Why did He then take Matsyavatar like an ordinary being bound by karma? I want to hear the tale of God's Matsyavatar.' Shukdev said: 'O Parikshit, God is one. Still for the preservation of His devotees, He takes various incarnations. He also shows His plays by…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 34, "quote": "16.8.5 Tale of Matysa-Avatar King Parikshit asked: 'O Gurudev, Lord is omnipotent. Why did He then take Matsyavatar like an ordinary being bound by karma? I want to hear the tale of God's Matsyavatar.' Shukdev said: 'O"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-9"><data key="title">Chapter 9</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 35, "quote": "16.9 Chapter 9"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-9-1"><data key="title">Description of the Vaivasvat Manu Dynasty</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">King Parikshit requested Shukdev to describe about the dynasty of his contemporary Manu, Shraddhdev. Shukdev said: 'Parikshit, I describe about the dynasty of Manu in short because if I go in detail, it will not be completed in hundreds of years.' Manu Shraddhdev was the son of Surya and Sandhya. Name of Manu's wife…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 35, "quote": "16.9.1 Description of the Vaivasvat Manu Dynasty King Parikshit requested Shukdev to describe about the dynasty of his contemporary Manu, Shraddhdev. Shukdev said: 'Parikshit, I describe about the dynasty of Manu in short because if I go i"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-9-2"><data key="title">Description of Ikshvaku Dynasty</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says, " Once Shraddhdev Manu sneezed violently. From his nostrils, a son was born. He was named Ikshvaku. Ikshvaku had one hundred sons. Vikukshi, Nimi and Dandak were the eldest three of them. Vikukshi :- Once, Ikshvaku sent his eldest son Vikukshi to collect tubers for the offerings to be made to their…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">37</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 37, "quote": "16.9.2 Description of Ikshvaku Dynasty Shukdev says, \" Once Shraddhdev Manu sneezed violently. From his nostrils, a son was born. He was named Ikshvaku. Ikshvaku had one hundred sons. Vikukshi, Nimi and Dandak were the eldest thr"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-9-3"><data key="title">Chandra Dynasty</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says, "Parikshit, now I narrate about Chandra dynasty. Many great and pious kings like Pururava had occurred in this dynasty. Chandrama was the son of Atri, the son of Brahma. Budh was the son of Chandrama while Pururava was the son of Budh. Ila was the mother of Pururava. He was very virtuous. Attracted by…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">39</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 39, "quote": "16.9.3 Chandra Dynasty Shukdev says, \"Parikshit, now I narrate about Chandra dynasty. Many great and pious kings like Pururava had occurred in this dynasty. Chandrama was the son of Atri, the son of Brahma. Budh w"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10"><data key="title">Chapter 10</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This chapter is very large - containing 85 sub-sections. Hearing the tales of Royal dynasties from Shukdev, king Parikshit requested "Guruvar, you have just narrated the suprising tale of Suryvansh and Chandravansh (dynasties). Now I wish to hear the tales of Lord Krishna's life in detail. It was because of God's…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">40</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 40, "quote": "16.10Chapter 10 This chapter is very large - containing 85 sub-sections. Hearing the tales of Royal dynasties from Shukdev, king Parikshit requested \"Guruvar, you have just narrated the suprising tale of Su"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-1"><data key="title">Marriage of Vasudeva and Devaki</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Shursen's son Vasudev was married to Devaki, the daughter of Devak. After the marriage ceremony, Ugrasen's son and Devki's cousin Kansa was driving the chariot of newly wedded couple. On the way a celestial voice proclaimed " O Fool Kansa, the eighth son of your beloved sister, whose chariot you are driving so…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">40</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 40, "quote": "16.10.1 Marriage of Vasudeva and Devaki Shursen's son Vasudev was married to Devaki, the daughter of Devak. After the marriage ceremony, Ugrasen's son and Devki's cousin Kansa was driving the chariot of newly wedded couple. On the"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-2"><data key="title">Arrival of God in Unborn Baby</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Kans had known that during the battle between the gods and demons he was the demon Kalnemi, and he was killed by Lord Vishnu. So fearing Lord Vishnu rallying with Yaduvanshis, Kansa cultivated high degree of hostility towards them. He captured even his father Ugrasen and put him in the prison. Now the reign of the…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">41</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 41, "quote": "16.10.2 Arrival of God in Unborn Baby Kans had known that during the battle between the gods and demons he was the demon Kalnemi, and he was killed by Lord Vishnu. So fearing Lord Vishnu rallying with Yaduvanshis, Kansa cultivat"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-3"><data key="title">Birth of Lord Krshna</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The long awaited day also arrived at last. It was the eighth day in the waning (darker) phase of the lunar month of Bhadrapada (corresponding to August/September). Rohini, the brightest star was rising while dense clouds had gathered in the sky. Before actual arrival, Lord manifested Himself before Vasudev and Devaki.…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">41</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 41, "quote": "16.10.3 Birth of Lord Krshna The long awaited day also arrived at last. It was the eighth day in the waning (darker) phase of the lunar month of Bhadrapada (corresponding to August/September). Rohini, the brightest star"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-4"><data key="title">Festivities in Gokul for Lord’s Birth</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Devaki's seventh baby was transferred before birth into the womb of Rohini. The baby was born as Baldevji. At his birth, Brahmins blessed Nand. But laughingly Nand said: "O holy Brahmins, this baby is not mine. It is born to my friend's wife." Brahmins said then: "Okay Baba, we will return only when your wife bears a…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">42</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 42, "quote": "16.10.4 Festivities in Gokul for Lord’s Birth Devaki's seventh baby was transferred before birth into the womb of Rohini. The baby was born as Baldevji. At his birth, Brahmins blessed Nand. But laughingly Nand said: \"O holy Brahmins, th"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-5"><data key="title">Salvation of Pootana</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says: 'Parikshit, after many days, entrusting other Gopals with the onus of guarding Gokul, Nand went to Mathura to pay annual taxes in Kansa's court. Vasudev too learned about his arrival and went to see him. Nand felt overjoyed to see Vasudev and embraced him affectionately. Vasudev asked Nand: "Your age is…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">43</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 43, "quote": "16.10.5 Salvation of Pootana Shukdev says: 'Parikshit, after many days, entrusting other Gopals with the onus of guarding Gokul, Nand went to Mathura to pay annual taxes in Kansa's court. Vasudev too learned about his a"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-6"><data key="title">Killing of Shakat</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When lord Krishna was twenty-seven days old, a festival was organised in Gokul. Mata Yashoda bathed the baby while the Brahmins recited hymns. Yashoda saw that baby was feeling sleepy, so putting him in a cradle, she left it under a cart. The cart was loaded with pitchers of milk, curd and butter. After sometime, lord…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">44</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 44, "quote": "16.10.6 Killing of Shakat When lord Krishna was twenty-seven days old, a festival was organised in Gokul. Mata Yashoda bathed the baby while the Brahmins recited hymns. Yashoda saw that baby was feeling sleepy, so pu"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-7"><data key="title">Salvation of Trinavart</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once Mata Yashoda was playing with the baby lord in her home. All of a sudden she felt as if the baby was getting disproportionately heavy. Soon, she felt herself unable to bear the child anymore. So she left the child on the ground and began to pray Purushottama Lord Rama. Soon afterwards she forgot about the event,…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">44</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 44, "quote": "16.10.7 Salvation of Trinavart Once Mata Yashoda was playing with the baby lord in her home. All of a sudden she felt as if the baby was getting disproportionately heavy. Soon, she felt herself unable to bear the child an"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-8"><data key="title">Naming of Lord Krshna</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, the supreme ascetic Gargacharya arrived in Gokul. He was the ancestral teacher of Yaduvanshis. Nand joyfully welcomed and treated the hermit. Then he requested him to carry out the ritual of naming and suggest suitable named for both of his sons. Acharya Garg said, "O Nand, everyone knows that I am the ancestral…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">44</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 44, "quote": "16.10.8 Naming of Lord Krshna Once, the supreme ascetic Gargacharya arrived in Gokul. He was the ancestral teacher of Yaduvanshis. Nand joyfully welcomed and treated the hermit. Then he requested him to carry out the rit"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-9"><data key="title">Childhood of Lord Krshna</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says: "O Parikshit, time passed in Gokul as usual. Balrama and Krishna too grew normally. Soon they began to walk on knees and palms. With their childish plays they both amused men and women folk of Gokul. Everyone took special care of them and looked after them to save them from any possible danger.…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">45</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 45, "quote": "16.10.9 Childhood of Lord Krshna Shukdev says: \"O Parikshit, time passed in Gokul as usual. Balrama and Krishna too grew normally. Soon they began to walk on knees and palms. With their childish plays they both amused men a"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-10"><data key="title">Ukhal Bandan &amp; Salvation of Yamarjun</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, wishing to feed Krishna with butter, Yashoda began to churn milk in the morning . Very soon, Krishna too awakened and not finding the mother on her bed, He set out to search her. Soon He found her in the courtyard where she was churning the milk. He began to insist for breast feeding at once. So Yashoda stopped…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">45</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 45, "quote": "16.10.10 Ukhal Bandan &amp; Salvation of Yamarjun Once, wishing to feed Krishna with butter, Yashoda began to churn milk in the morning . Very soon, Krishna too awakened and not finding the mother on her bed, He set out to search her. Soon"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-11"><data key="title">Migration to Vrindavana</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The uprooted Arjun trees fell with thundering sound. All the people shivered with fear and felt as if lightning had struck somewhere. All the elders including Nand met together and discussed the matter. Unanimously they concluded that, of late disturbance had increased in Gokul and the circumstances were no longer…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 46, "quote": "16.10.11 Migration to Vrindavana The uprooted Arjun trees fell with thundering sound. All the people shivered with fear and felt as if lightning had struck somewhere. All the elders including Nand met together and discussed"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-12"><data key="title">Salvation of Vatsasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">One day, Balarama and Krishna were grazing their cattles on the bank of Yamuna River. A demon, meanwhile, took the guise of a calf and mixed with the herd. Obviously, he had malicious intentions. Lord Krishna had already seen the demon taking calf's guise and mixing up with the herd. He signaled Balarama and they…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 46, "quote": "16.10.12 Salvation of Vatsasur One day, Balarama and Krishna were grazing their cattles on the bank of Yamuna River. A demon, meanwhile, took the guise of a calf and mixed with the herd. Obviously, he had malicious intent"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-13"><data key="title">Salvation of Bakasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">One day, all the cowherds took their cattles to a large pond to let them drink water. There they say a huge creature sitting like a hillock on the bank. They were frightened by its appearance. The creature was in fact a demon named Bakasur who had arrived there in the guise of a storke. He was himself very strong and…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 46, "quote": "16.10.13 Salvation of Bakasur One day, all the cowherds took their cattles to a large pond to let them drink water. There they say a huge creature sitting like a hillock on the bank. They were frightened by its appearanc"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-14"><data key="title">Salvation of Aghasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">One day, Krishna planned a picnic near Nandanvan. So rising early in the morning, He awakened his fellow cowherds sounding a horn. Together, all the cowherds, and their cattles went to the forest. There, on the bank of Yamuna river they began to play joyfully. Nandanvana was in fact an abode of the demon Aghasur. He…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">47</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 47, "quote": "16.10.14 Salvation of Aghasur One day, Krishna planned a picnic near Nandanvan. So rising early in the morning, He awakened his fellow cowherds sounding a horn. Together, all the cowherds, and their cattles went to the f"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-15"><data key="title">Attachment of Brahmaji</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says: Parikshit, the cowherds told the tale of Aghasur's salvation to their parents only after a year of the incidence. During that period of one year, Lord Krishna multiplied himself and took the guise of his fellow cowherd and their stock of cattles to remove the Illusion of Brahma,who was the cause of the…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">47</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 47, "quote": "16.10.15 Attachment of Brahmaji Shukdev says: Parikshit, the cowherds told the tale of Aghasur's salvation to their parents only after a year of the incidence. During that period of one year, Lord Krishna multiplied himsel"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-16"><data key="title">Salvation of Dhenukasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When Krishna and Balarama entered sixth year of their ages, they got the permission to take the cattles out for grazing. Along with their fellow cowherds the two brothers too began to take their cattle to Vrindavana. Thus they sanctified the earth of Vrindavana with their pious feet. Seeing the beauty of Vrindavana,…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">47</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 47, "quote": "16.10.16 Salvation of Dhenukasur When Krishna and Balarama entered sixth year of their ages, they got the permission to take the cattles out for grazing. Along with their fellow cowherds the two brothers too began to take t"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-17"><data key="title">Grace on Kalia, the Naga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">One day, Bala-Krishna arrived on the banks of Yamuna with their friends. Balarama was not with them that day. The water of the river Yamuna was toxicated by the poison of Kaliy- The Naga. It was exceptionally hot that day and all the cowherds and cattles were very thirsty. So without giving any thought, they all drank…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">48</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 48, "quote": "16.10.17 Grace on Kalia, the Naga One day, Bala-Krishna arrived on the banks of Yamuna with their friends. Balarama was not with them that day. The water of the river Yamuna was toxicated by the poison of Kaliy- The Naga. It"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-18"><data key="title">Drinking of Davanal (Forest Fire)</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After defeating Kaliy, Lord Krishna and all other people of Vraj felt extremely tired. They were very hungry and thirsty also. So they did not go back to Vrindavana, but stayed on the bank of Yamuna in the night. Because of intense heat of summer, surrounding forests had been shrivelled. At midnight those shrivelled…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">48</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 48, "quote": "16.10.18 Drinking of Davanal (Forest Fire) After defeating Kaliy, Lord Krishna and all other people of Vraj felt extremely tired. They were very hungry and thirsty also. So they did not go back to Vrindavana, but stayed on the bank o"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-19"><data key="title">Salvation of Pralambasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">One day, Balarama and Krishna were playfully grazing their cattles along with other cowherds in the forests. When a demon Pralamb arrived there with an intention of kidnapping Krishna and Balarama. The demon had come in the guise of a cowherd, but the God easily recognised him. They accepted his proposal of…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">49</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 49, "quote": "16.10.19 Salvation of Pralambasur One day, Balarama and Krishna were playfully grazing their cattles along with other cowherds in the forests. When a demon Pralamb arrived there with an intention of kidnapping Krishna and Ba"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-20"><data key="title">Venugeet</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Venugeet is the song centered on Krishna’s flute. It belongs to the devotional poetry embedded within the Krishna narrative.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">49</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 49, "quote": "16.10.20 Venugeet Shri Shukdevji says: \"O king, most of the time of Lord Krishna's boyhood passed in Vrindavana. There he grazed cattles and played flute. His flute had a divine, enchanting sound.The Gopis (g"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-21"><data key="title">Stealing of Clothes</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The unmarried girls of Vrindavana felt as if their lives were dedicated to Lord Krishna. Each of them wished heartly to have lord Krishna as her husband. So in order to get their desire fulfilled, all of the spinster girls of Vraj began to take bath in Yamuna early in morning of Hemant season and worshipped goddess…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">50</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 50, "quote": "16.10.21 Stealing of Clothes The unmarried girls of Vrindavana felt as if their lives were dedicated to Lord Krishna. Each of them wished heartly to have lord Krishna as her husband. So in order to get their desire fulf"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-22"><data key="title">Lifting of Govardhan Mountain</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukhdevji says: "O Parikshit one day, Balarama and Krishna saw that many delicious kind of sweetmeats were being cooked at home. With curiosity and politeness, they asked Nand and other elders: "Father, which festival are you preparing for? Which god will be worshipped? What purpose will such worship serve? Nand Baba…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">50</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 50, "quote": "16.10.22 Lifting of Govardhan Mountain Shukhdevji says: \"O Parikshit one day, Balarama and Krishna saw that many delicious kind of sweetmeats were being cooked at home. With curiosity and politeness, they asked Nand and other eld"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-23"><data key="title">Indignation of Indra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Thus convinced by Kanhaiya's wise reasoning, all the gopas agreed to him. Nand Baba said, "O Krishna, we are making all these preparation for you only and we will do as you say. We will worship Govardhan. For us Govardhan is also like a god. It gives us grass, water, and fuel etc., which are necessary for our lives.…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">50</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 50, "quote": "16.10.23 Indignation of Indra Thus convinced by Kanhaiya's wise reasoning, all the gopas agreed to him. Nand Baba said, \"O Krishna, we are making all these preparation for you only and we will do as you say. We will wors"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-24"><data key="title">Worship of Goverdhan</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">On the Purnima (full moon) day in the month of Kartik (November) all the Gopas (people of Vraj region) gathered near mount Govardhan. During the night all of them circumambulated the mountain. Lord invoked the Ganges by his wish and bathed Govardhan with her water.Then they put vermilion on it, offered basil-leaves,…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">51</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 51, "quote": "16.10.24 Worship of Goverdhan On the Purnima (full moon) day in the month of Kartik (November) all the Gopas (people of Vraj region) gathered near mount Govardhan. During the night all of them circumambulated the mountai"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-25"><data key="title">Angry Retaliation of Indra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukdevji sayd: Parikshit, when Indra learned that the Gopas had stopped worshipping him, he grew outrageous. In anger, he ordered the clouds to rain torrentially over Vraj and cause heavy flood in Vraj region. In no time dense clouds gathered over Vraj. Soon there was lighting thunder, darkness and storm all around.…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">51</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 51, "quote": "16.10.25 Angry Retaliation of Indra Shukdevji sayd: Parikshit, when Indra learned that the Gopas had stopped worshipping him, he grew outrageous. In anger, he ordered the clouds to rain torrentially over Vraj and cause heavy f"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-26"><data key="title">Ceremonial Bath of Lord</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When Indra learned about the happenings in Vraj, he soon realised his mistake. Indra himself reached Vraj and begged lord for his pardon. Then he gave lord a ceremonial bath with the milk of Surabhi. For protecting the cows,Thus the Lord also got one more name "Govind".</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">51</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 51, "quote": "16.10.26 Ceremonial Bath of Lord When Indra learned about the happenings in Vraj, he soon realised his mistake. Indra himself reached Vraj and begged lord for his pardon. Then he gave lord a ceremonial bath with the milk of"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-27"><data key="title">Raas Leela</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukdevji related to Parikshit the tale of Kamadev's humiliation. Shridhar Swami calls Raasleela as Kama-vijay leela (defeating of Kamadeva- Hindu equivalent of cupid). Jagad guru Ballabhacharyaji decribes it as Lords Divy Vihar (divine enjoyment). The purpose of describing this tale is just that common people should…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">51</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 51, "quote": "16.10.27 Raas Leela Shukdevji related to Parikshit the tale of Kamadev's humiliation. Shridhar Swami calls Raasleela as Kama-vijay leela (defeating of Kamadeva- Hindu equivalent of cupid). Jagad guru Ballabhach"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-28"><data key="title">Pitiable Conditions of Gopis in Lord’s Absence</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After the disappearance of God, Gopis were perplexed. Their hearts burned with desire. They had dedicated their entire selves in the feet of Lord. They were entirely merged in the love of Krishna. Thus driven by passion, Gopis began to search Lord Krishna. They asked trees, creepers, vegetations for the whereabouts of…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">52</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 52, "quote": "16.10.28 Pitiable Conditions of Gopis in Lord’s Absence After the disappearance of God, Gopis were perplexed. Their hearts burned with desire. They had dedicated their entire selves in the feet of Lord. They were entirely merged in the love of Kr"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-29"><data key="title">Gopika Geet</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Gopika Geet voices the gopis’ longing in Krishna’s absence, placing a devotional song inside the sequence of disappearance and reunion.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">52</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 52, "quote": "16.10.29 Gopika Geet O Lord, your incarnation has increased the glory of Vrajbhumi. Even the glory of Vaikunth, goddess Lakshmi herself came to stay in Vraj. Govind we have dedicated our lives at your feet. Now"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-30"><data key="title">Reappearance of the Lord Among the Gopis</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says: Parikshit, when the beloved Gopis burst into tears, Krishna's patience gave away. His heart melted at their condition and He appeared amidst them. A sweet smile played on his beautiful face. He wore a garland of fresh Vaijayanti flowers and yellow clothes. His beauty could have moved even Kamadev. Seeing…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">53</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 53, "quote": "16.10.30 Reappearance of the Lord Among the Gopis Shukdev says: Parikshit, when the beloved Gopis burst into tears, Krishna's patience gave away. His heart melted at their condition and He appeared amidst them. A sweet smile played on his b"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-31"><data key="title">Maha-Raas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">From the words of the Lord, Gopis forgot the pains of separation. From the closeness of their beloved, their lives were successful now. With those Gopis, Lord Krishna started Maharaas on the pious banks of river Yamuna. All the gods gathered in sky to witness that divine festival. Gopis were even more fortunate than…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">53</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 53, "quote": "16.10.31 Maha-Raas From the words of the Lord, Gopis forgot the pains of separation. From the closeness of their beloved, their lives were successful now. With those Gopis, Lord Krishna started Maharaas on the"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-32"><data key="title">Salvation of Sudarshan &amp; Shankhchuda</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says: O Parikshit, once on the occasion of Lord Shivaratri, Nand Baba and all other Gopas drove their carts with families and reached Ambikavana on a pilgrimage tour. There they took bath in the river Saraswati and with devotion worshiped lord Lord Shiva and Parvati. They also observed day long fast and…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">53</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 53, "quote": "16.10.32 Salvation of Sudarshan &amp; Shankhchuda Shukdev says: O Parikshit, once on the occasion of Lord Shivaratri, Nand Baba and all other Gopas drove their carts with families and reached Ambikavana on a pilgrimage tour. There they took"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-33"><data key="title">Salvation of Arishtasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, Kansa sent a demon Arishtasur to Vrindavana. The demon arrived there in a bull's guise. That huge bull came to Vrindavana and began to terrorize the people with his loud sound. Seeing the bull, Balaram said to Krishna: "Kanhaiya, I have never seen such a huge bull before." All the people began to cry for help.…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">54</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 54, "quote": "16.10.33 Salvation of Arishtasur Once, Kansa sent a demon Arishtasur to Vrindavana. The demon arrived there in a bull's guise. That huge bull came to Vrindavana and began to terrorize the people with his loud sound. Seeing"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-34"><data key="title">Kans sends Akrurji</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After the killing of Arishthasur, Devarishi Narad visited Kansa and asked: "O Kansa, the girl who had slipped form your hand was in fact the daughter of Yashoda. Krishna and Balaram, who are staying in Vrindavana, are infact the sons of Devaki and Rohini respectively. Because of your fear, Vasudev has kept them under…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">54</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 54, "quote": "16.10.34 Kans sends Akrurji After the killing of Arishthasur, Devarishi Narad visited Kansa and asked: \"O Kansa, the girl who had slipped form your hand was in fact the daughter of Yashoda. Krishna and Balaram, who are"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-35"><data key="title">Departure of Krshna-Balarama for Mathura</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When the Gopis heard about Krishna leaving Gokul to visit Mathura, they began to wail and cry. They were getting so much restless by the news that, they felt, their lives would end before the sunrise. They started imprecating fate that it had no kindness. First it provided them with a closer contact with their beloved…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">55</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 55, "quote": "16.10.35 Departure of Krshna-Balarama for Mathura When the Gopis heard about Krishna leaving Gokul to visit Mathura, they began to wail and cry. They were getting so much restless by the news that, they felt, their lives would end before th"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-36"><data key="title">Appearance of the Lord before Arjun</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Hearing Akrur's words, Lord understood that when he had arrived in Vraj, he was afflicted with a feeling of majesty. But now, it has been replaced by affection now. He, therefore decided to remove his dilemma. Thus, Lord said to him: "Kaka, Mathura is still some distance away. So you take a bath in Yamuna. We brothers…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">55</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 55, "quote": "16.10.36 Appearance of the Lord before Arjun Hearing Akrur's words, Lord understood that when he had arrived in Vraj, he was afflicted with a feeling of majesty. But now, it has been replaced by affection now. He, therefore decided to"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-37"><data key="title">Arrival in Mathura</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After the departure of Akrur, Nandbaba too set out for Mathura along with the Gopas. On the way itself, they caught up with Krishna and Balaram. All of them then reached Mathura together. There they stayed in a garden. After sometime, with Nand's permission, both the brothers set out to see the city of Mathura.…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">56</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 56, "quote": "16.10.37 Arrival in Mathura After the departure of Akrur, Nandbaba too set out for Mathura along with the Gopas. On the way itself, they caught up with Krishna and Balaram. All of them then reached Mathura together. Th"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-38"><data key="title">Grace on Kubja</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">On the way, they met a pretty but hunched women. She introduced herself as Kubja, the maid of Kansa. Her duty was to smear the members of the royal family with sandal-wood paste. God asked her if she would smear him with sandal paste. Kubja said: "O ManMohan, I see no one more fitting than you for the sandal paste."…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">56</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 56, "quote": "16.10.38 Grace on Kubja On the way, they met a pretty but hunched women. She introduced herself as Kubja, the maid of Kansa. Her duty was to smear the members of the royal family with sandal-wood paste. God asked h"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-39"><data key="title">Breaking of the Bow</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After salving Kubja, Lord Krishna and other Gopas moved ahead. At a place they saw a huge bow kept on a high stage. Many strong men were guarding it. Lord entered the canopy and easily lifted the bow and broke it into pieces. There was a big applause from all around. Dau said: "Krishna, now the crowd will increase…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">56</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 56, "quote": "16.10.39 Breaking of the Bow After salving Kubja, Lord Krishna and other Gopas moved ahead. At a place they saw a huge bow kept on a high stage. Many strong men were guarding it. Lord entered the canopy and easily lifte"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-40"><data key="title">Killing of the Elephant</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Early in the morning, Kansa summoned his minister and ordered him to make Kuvalayapeed, the elephant to stand in the centre of the main gate. He thought that the elephant would kill both the boys if they dared to enter the fort through main gate. Back there in the garden, Krishna and Balarama set out in wrestlers…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">56</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 56, "quote": "16.10.40 Killing of the Elephant Early in the morning, Kansa summoned his minister and ordered him to make Kuvalayapeed, the elephant to stand in the centre of the main gate. He thought that the elephant would kill both the"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-41"><data key="title">Salvation of Wrestlers</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Kansa felt very nervous by the news of elephant's killing. Before he could take stock of the situation, Lord Krishna and Balaram arrived in the amphi- theatre. The spectators present in the amphitheatre saw Lord as per their feelings. The menfolk saw Lord as a Jewel among the men. Womenfolk saw him as an incarnation…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">56</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 56, "quote": "16.10.41 Salvation of Wrestlers Kansa felt very nervous by the news of elephant's killing. Before he could take stock of the situation, Lord Krishna and Balaram arrived in the amphi- theatre. The spectators present in the"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-42"><data key="title">Salvation of Kansa</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Seeing the shameful defeat of his wrestlers, Kansa infuriated with anger and proclaimed: "Tie all the opponents; tie Ugarsen, Devaki, Vasudev, Nand in ropes and bring them before me." Lord Krishna could not tolerate this and in a single jump, He reached on the stage where Kansa was present. Lord Krishna caught him by…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">57</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 57, "quote": "16.10.42 Salvation of Kansa Seeing the shameful defeat of his wrestlers, Kansa infuriated with anger and proclaimed: \"Tie all the opponents; tie Ugarsen, Devaki, Vasudev, Nand in ropes and bring them before me.\" Lord K"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-43"><data key="title">Yagyopavit of Krshna-Balarama</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After the successful completion of all the royal ceremonies, the consecration of Krishna and Balarama was carried out. Thereafter, they came to stay at the hermitage of sage Sandipani for formal education. Extraordinarily brilliant Krishna successfully learned all the knowledge in short period of time. Then, as…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">57</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 57, "quote": "16.10.43 Yagyopavit of Krshna-Balarama After the successful completion of all the royal ceremonies, the consecration of Krishna and Balarama was carried out. Thereafter, they came to stay at the hermitage of sage Sandipani for fo"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-44"><data key="title">Sending Udhav as Messenger</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Savant Uddhav, the son of Yadava's minister Brihaspati was an intimate friend of Lord Krishna. Only he had the permission to enter Lord's sanctum. Seeing his friend Krishna in remorse, Uddhav asked: "Mathuranath, you seem to be upset. What is troubling you?" Lord Krishna replied: "Uddhava, I feel perplexed. I remember…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">57</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 57, "quote": "16.10.44 Sending Udhav as Messenger Savant Uddhav, the son of Yadava's minister Brihaspati was an intimate friend of Lord Krishna. Only he had the permission to enter Lord's sanctum. Seeing his friend Krishna in remorse, Uddha"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-45"><data key="title">Bhramar Geet</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Bhramar Geet presents the gopis’ speech associated with the bee and Uddhava’s visit. The episode links absence, memory and devotion.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">57</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 57, "quote": "16.10.45 Bhramar Geet Next day, when the Gopis got the news of Uddhav's arrival, they thronged in and around Nand's residence. They recognized the chariot parked in front of the gate. It was the same chariot on w"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-46"><data key="title">Battles with Jarasandh: Construction of Dvarka-puri</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After the death of Kansa his widowed queens Asti and Prapti returned to their father Jarasandh's home and informed him that Krishna and Balarama had killed their husband Kansa. Infuriated by the news, Jarasandh at once launched a massive attack on Mathura. The people of Mathura were frightened by the strength of…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">59</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 59, "quote": "16.10.46 Battles with Jarasandh: Construction of Dvarka-puri After the death of Kansa his widowed queens Asti and Prapti returned to their father Jarasandh's home and informed him that Krishna and Balarama had killed their husband Kansa. Infuriated by"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-47"><data key="title">Tale of Muchkund</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Parikshit asked: "Gurudev, who was that sleeping man?" Shukdev says: "O king, that sleeping man was Muchkund, the son of the king Mandhata. The gods had sought his assistance in their war against the demon during the Satyayug. With Muchkund's help,the gods had defeated the demons and thus pleased had asked him to seek…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">59</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 59, "quote": "16.10.47 Tale of Muchkund Parikshit asked: \"Gurudev, who was that sleeping man?\" Shukdev says: \"O king, that sleeping man was Muchkund, the son of the king Mandhata. The gods had sought his assistance in their war ag"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-48"><data key="title">Marriage of Balarama</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The king of Anart, Raiwat got his daughter Rewati married to Balarama with the blessing of Brahma.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">60</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 60, "quote": "16.10.48 Marriage of Balarama The king of Anart, Raiwat got his daughter Rewati married to Balarama with the blessing of Brahma."}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-49"><data key="title">Message of Rukmani for the Lord</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukdev said: "O Parikshit, Bheeshmak was the king of Vidarbh. He had five sons and a daughter Rukmani. Rukmi, the eldest son of Bheeshmak, had fixed his sister's engagement with Shishupal, the prince of Chaidi. Narada did not like this development. He went to Kundanpur, the capital of Vidarbh and said in the court of…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">60</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 60, "quote": "16.10.49 Message of Rukmani for the Lord Shukdev said: \"O Parikshit, Bheeshmak was the king of Vidarbh. He had five sons and a daughter Rukmani. Rukmi, the eldest son of Bheeshmak, had fixed his sister's engagement with Shishupal,"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-50"><data key="title">Defeat of Rukmi &amp; Shishupal</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Hearing the news that Krishna has eloped with Rukmani, Rukmi and all other kings present there boiled with anger. Accompanied by their huge armies, they decided to give them a chase. Thus chased my them, Yadnvanishis stopped and encountered the kings boldly. With a true ambition to win, brave Yadav soldiers defeated…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">61</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 61, "quote": "16.10.50 Defeat of Rukmi &amp; Shishupal Hearing the news that Krishna has eloped with Rukmani, Rukmi and all other kings present there boiled with anger. Accompanied by their huge armies, they decided to give them a chase. Thus ch"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-51"><data key="title">Marriage of Rukmini &amp; Krshna</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Thus defeating all the kings, Lord Krishna brought Rukmani to Dwaraka. There they got married formally. All the people of Dwaraka celebrated festivities for many days. People presented them with lot of precious gifts. All the people were in great joy to see Lakshmi as Rukmani with her husband Lord Krishna.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">61</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 61, "quote": "16.10.51 Marriage of Rukmini &amp; Krshna Thus defeating all the kings, Lord Krishna brought Rukmani to Dwaraka. There they got married formally. All the people of Dwaraka celebrated festivities for many days. People presented them"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-52"><data key="title">Birth of Pradyumna, Killing of Shambarasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says: O Parikshit, Kamadev was a part of lord himself. After getting incinerated by Rudra, Kamadev took refuge in the supreme lord to get an incarnation once more. Thus, Kamadev was born as Rukmani,s first son Pradyumn. But just after his birth, Pradyumn was kidnapped by a demon Shambarasur. The demon dropped…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">61</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 61, "quote": "16.10.52 Birth of Pradyumna, Killing of Shambarasur Shukdev says: O Parikshit, Kamadev was a part of lord himself. After getting incinerated by Rudra, Kamadev took refuge in the supreme lord to get an incarnation once more. Thus, Kamadev was"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-53"><data key="title">Tale of Syamantak, the Gem</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">A person named Satrajit was a great devotee of Lord Suryanarayana. Pleased by his devotion, Suryadev presented him a gem called Syamantak. The gem had a radiance equal to the sun. Bearing that gem, Satrajit arrived in Lord's court. By the radiance of his gem, all the people and the courtiers mistook him for Suryadeva…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">61</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 61, "quote": "16.10.53 Tale of Syamantak, the Gem A person named Satrajit was a great devotee of Lord Suryanarayana. Pleased by his devotion, Suryadev presented him a gem called Syamantak. The gem had a radiance equal to the sun. Bearing th"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-54"><data key="title">Marriage of Satyabhama &amp; Krshna</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lord summoned Satrajit to his court and in the presence of the king Ugrasen, related the sequence of incidents that took place in the jungle. Satrajit felt ashamed. With a feeling of repentance he took the gem. He was getting afraid also that he made enemity with lord Krishna without reason. Hence to expiate his…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">62</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 62, "quote": "16.10.54 Marriage of Satyabhama &amp; Krshna Lord summoned Satrajit to his court and in the presence of the king Ugrasen, related the sequence of incidents that took place in the jungle. Satrajit felt ashamed. With a feeling of repenta"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-55"><data key="title">Theft of Syamantak</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Akrur and Kritvarma were not pleased with the marriage of Satyabhama. So they got Satrajit killed by Shatdanva. Shatdanva killed Satrajit in his sleep and absconded with the gem. Lord Krishna was then away in Hastinapur. Satyabhama too reached there and informed Krishna about her father's assassination. With Balarama,…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">62</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 62, "quote": "16.10.55 Theft of Syamantak Akrur and Kritvarma were not pleased with the marriage of Satyabhama. So they got Satrajit killed by Shatdanva. Shatdanva killed Satrajit in his sleep and absconded with the gem. Lord Krishn"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-56"><data key="title">Lord’s Marriage with Kalindi</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once Lord Krishna visited Indraprasth to see Pandavas there. There riding a chariot with Arjuna, He came to the forests. On the bank of river Yamuna a pretty woman was observing penance. By the instinct of God, Arjun drew near her and asked for her identity. She said: "I am Kalindi, the daughter of Suryadev. I am…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">62</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 62, "quote": "16.10.56 Lord’s Marriage with Kalindi Once Lord Krishna visited Indraprasth to see Pandavas there. There riding a chariot with Arjuna, He came to the forests. On the bank of river Yamuna a pretty woman was observing penance. By"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-57"><data key="title">Lord’s Marriage with Mitrvinda</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Mitrvinda was the sister of Vind Anuvind the king of Ujjain. She had a desire to get Lord as her husband. But her brother, Vind Anuvind was a follower of Duryodhan. So he stopped his sister Mitrvinda from getting married to Krishna. But Krishna eloped with Mitrvinda from the his court and got married to her formally…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">62</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 62, "quote": "16.10.57 Lord’s Marriage with Mitrvinda Mitrvinda was the sister of Vind Anuvind the king of Ujjain. She had a desire to get Lord as her husband. But her brother, Vind Anuvind was a follower of Duryodhan. So he stopped his sister"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-58"><data key="title">With Satya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Satya was the daughter of Nagnjit, the king of Kaushal. She was extremely beautiful lady. The king had resolved that he would marry his daughter only to him who would defeat his seven most formidable oxen. Many princes has tried their luck since then but failed. When Lord Krishna heard about that, He reached Kaushal…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">62</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 62, "quote": "16.10.58 With Satya Satya was the daughter of Nagnjit, the king of Kaushal. She was extremely beautiful lady. The king had resolved that he would marry his daughter only to him who would defeat his seven most f"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-59"><data key="title">With Bhadra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukdev said: "Parikshit, Lord's aunt (father's sister) Shrutkirti was married in the kingdom of Kaikauja. Bhadra was the daughter of Shrutkirti. Bhadra's brothers like Santardan etc. themselves had got their sister married to Lord Krishna.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">63</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 63, "quote": "16.10.59 With Bhadra Shukdev said: \"Parikshit, Lord's aunt (father's sister) Shrutkirti was married in the kingdom of Kaikauja. Bhadra was the daughter of Shrutkirti. Bhadra's brothers like Santardan etc. themse"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-60"><data key="title">With Lakshmana</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lakshmana was the daughter of the king of Madra. She was very beautiful and meritorius. Lord abducted her all alone from the Swayamvar organised for her marriage. Later, Lord married to her formally.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">63</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 63, "quote": "16.10.60 With Lakshmana Lakshmana was the daughter of the king of Madra. She was very beautiful and meritorius. Lord abducted her all alone from the Swayamvar organised for her marriage. Later, Lord married to her"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-61"><data key="title">Salvation of Bhaumasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Shukdev said: Parikshit, Prayjyotishpur was the capital of the demon Bhaumasur. He was very strong and powerful. He had snatched the canopy of Varuna, ear-rings of Aditi the mother of the gods, and Maniparvat of the gods on Meru. Apart from these, he had also captured sixteen thousand and one hundred princesses as…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">63</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 63, "quote": "16.10.61 Salvation of Bhaumasur Shukdev said: Parikshit, Prayjyotishpur was the capital of the demon Bhaumasur. He was very strong and powerful. He had snatched the canopy of Varuna, ear-rings of Aditi the mother of the go"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-62"><data key="title">Lord’s Marriage with Captive Princesses</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After slaying Bhaumasur, Lord entered his palace. There he released the sixteen-thousand one- hundred captive princesses. The princesses were very much impressed by the Lord. They had all accepted in their mind, Lord Krishna as their husband. Lord too bowed before their love and accepted them as his wives and arranged…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">63</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 63, "quote": "16.10.62 Lord’s Marriage with Captive Princesses After slaying Bhaumasur, Lord entered his palace. There he released the sixteen-thousand one- hundred captive princesses. The princesses were very much impressed by the Lord. They had all ac"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-63"><data key="title">Pradyumna’s Marriage with Rukmvati</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says: Parikshit, Rukmvati was the daughter of Rukmi, the brother of Rukmani. When a Swayamvar was organised for her, she saw Pradyumn. She was so impressed by him that she chose him as her husband. But it was not acceptable to other princes. They tried to stop their marriage. But defeating them all, Pradyumn…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">63</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 63, "quote": "16.10.63 Pradyumna’s Marriage with Rukmvati Shukdev says: Parikshit, Rukmvati was the daughter of Rukmi, the brother of Rukmani. When a Swayamvar was organised for her, she saw Pradyumn. She was so impressed by him that she chose him"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-64"><data key="title">Marriage of Usha-Anirudha</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The son of the demon king Bali, Banasur was a great devotee of Lord Lord Shiva. Banasur ruled over the kingdom Shonitpur. By the grace of Lord Lord Shiva, he had received thousand arms. Even all the gods including Indra used to serve him. Thus blinded by his physical strength, Banasur sought a boon from Lord to meet a…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">63</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 63, "quote": "16.10.64 Marriage of Usha-Anirudha The son of the demon king Bali, Banasur was a great devotee of Lord Lord Shiva. Banasur ruled over the kingdom Shonitpur. By the grace of Lord Lord Shiva, he had received thousand arms. Even"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-65"><data key="title">Tale of King Nrig</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, Lord Krishna's sons visited the forests. There they saw a huge Chameleon fallen in a large, deep well. They tried to pull it out but in vain. The princes, therefore, returned to the palace and related this strange episode to Lord Krishna. Lord too came to the well and with His left hand, easily pulled the…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">64</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 64, "quote": "16.10.65 Tale of King Nrig Once, Lord Krishna's sons visited the forests. There they saw a huge Chameleon fallen in a large, deep well. They tried to pull it out but in vain. The princes, therefore, returned to the pa"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-66"><data key="title">Salvation of Paundrak &amp; Kashraj</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once Balarama and Krishna had gone to Braj to see Nandbaba there. Meanwhile the king Paundrak of Karush sent an envoy to lord Krishna with a message that said: 'I am Lord Vasudev.' Pandrak's envoy arrived in the court and read out the message: "To bestow my grace on the people, I have taken an incarnation. You have…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">64</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 64, "quote": "16.10.66 Salvation of Paundrak &amp; Kashraj Once Balarama and Krishna had gone to Braj to see Nandbaba there. Meanwhile the king Paundrak of Karush sent an envoy to lord Krishna with a message that said: 'I am Lord Vasudev.' Pandrak's"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-67"><data key="title">Salvation of Dvivid</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The king Parikshit expressed his desire to hear about the life of Balarama. Shukdev said:, "O king, there was once a monkey named Dwivid. He was the friend of Bhaumasur. When Dwivid heard about Bhaumasur's killing by Shri Krishna, he began to cause large scale destruction in the kingdom. His disruptive activities in…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">65</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 65, "quote": "16.10.67 Salvation of Dvivid The king Parikshit expressed his desire to hear about the life of Balarama. Shukdev said:, \"O king, there was once a monkey named Dwivid. He was the friend of Bhaumasur. When Dwivid heard ab"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-68"><data key="title">Marriage of Samb</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Samb was the son of lord Krishna and born to Jambvati. He had Kidnapped Lakshmana, the daughter of Duryodhana from her Swayamvar. Infuriated Kauravas chased them and, with difficulty, they caught Samb and tied him. When the Yaduvanshis got the news, they began preparations to launch an attack on Kauravas. Balarama…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">65</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 65, "quote": "16.10.68 Marriage of Samb Samb was the son of lord Krishna and born to Jambvati. He had Kidnapped Lakshmana, the daughter of Duryodhana from her Swayamvar. Infuriated Kauravas chased them and, with difficulty, they c"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-69"><data key="title">Sending of Emissary (Kings in captivity seek the Lord’s help)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says: "Parikshit, once Lord Krishna was holding His court when an emissary arrived in the court. The kings who were held captive forcibly by Jarasandh had sent him. The emissary related the miseries of those kings to Lord Krishna. Through the emissary, the kings had requested: "O Lord of the world, Kindly get…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">65</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 65, "quote": "16.10.69 Sending of Emissary (Kings in captivity seek the Lord’s help) Shukdev says: \"Parikshit, once Lord Krishna was holding His court when an emissary arrived in the court. The kings who were held captive forcibly by Jarasandh had sent him. The emissary rela"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-70"><data key="title">Lord’s Departure for Indraprastha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Uddhav's advise was in the interest of all. Everyone supported it. Taking permission from His priest and teachers, Lord set out on a chariot with the whole family to reach Indraprastha. In Indraprastha, Pandavas accorded the Lord with warm-hearted felicitations. By the dictate of Lord, Mayasur built a divine looking…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">65</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 65, "quote": "16.10.70 Lord’s Departure for Indraprastha Uddhav's advise was in the interest of all. Everyone supported it. Taking permission from His priest and teachers, Lord set out on a chariot with the whole family to reach Indraprastha. In I"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-71"><data key="title">Killing of Jarasandh</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">During Yudhishthir's Rajsuy Yagya, all the Pandavas set out in all the directions to conquer the kings and expand the boundaries of their kingdom. Warriors like Bheem, Arjun defeated great kings all around and extended the boundaries of Yudhisthir's empire. But to defeat Jarasandh, - Bheema, Arjuna and Lord Krishna…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">65</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 65, "quote": "16.10.71 Killing of Jarasandh During Yudhishthir's Rajsuy Yagya, all the Pandavas set out in all the directions to conquer the kings and expand the boundaries of their kingdom. Warriors like Bheem, Arjun defeated great k"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-72"><data key="title">Salvation of Shishupal</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">King Yudhishthir had invited great vedic Brahmins and Acharayas on the occasion. Those great Sages included Ved Vyas, Bhardwaj, Sumantu, Gautam, Asit, Vashishth, Chyvan, Kanv, Maitrey, Kavash, Chit, Vishvamitra, Vamdev, Sumati, Jemini, Kratu, Pail, Parashurama, Shukracharya, Asuri, Vitihotra, Madhuchchanda, Veersen…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">66</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 66, "quote": "16.10.72 Salvation of Shishupal King Yudhishthir had invited great vedic Brahmins and Acharayas on the occasion. Those great Sages included Ved Vyas, Bhardwaj, Sumantu, Gautam, Asit, Vashishth, Chyvan, Kanv, Maitrey, Kavas"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-73"><data key="title">Salvation of Shalva</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says: "Parikshit, now listen to the tale of how Lord caused salvation for Shalv. Shalv was the childhood friend of Shishupal and had attended the marriage of Rukmani as a member of Shishupal's wedding party. At that time, Yaduvanshis had defeated them all including Jarasandh and Shalv. Right at that moment,…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">66</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 66, "quote": "16.10.73 Salvation of Shalva Shukdev says: \"Parikshit, now listen to the tale of how Lord caused salvation for Shalv. Shalv was the childhood friend of Shishupal and had attended the marriage of Rukmani as a member of S"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-74"><data key="title">Salvation of Dantvaktra &amp; Vidurath</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After the killing of Shishupal, Shalv etc. Dantvaktra arrived in the battlefield carrying a mace. When Lord saw him coming, He too jumped down from the chariot and baulked his movement with a mace. Dantvaktra tried to humiliate God with his abuses and hit him on head with his mace. Lord easily bore the blow of mace…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">67</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 67, "quote": "16.10.74 Salvation of Dantvaktra &amp; Vidurath After the killing of Shishupal, Shalv etc. Dantvaktra arrived in the battlefield carrying a mace. When Lord saw him coming, He too jumped down from the chariot and baulked his movement with"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-75"><data key="title">Sudama – The Lord’s Friend</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">During his stay as a disciple at the hermitage of sage Sandipani, Lord Krishna had a Brahmin friend named Sudama. He was very indifferent in nature with no desires for the material things. After their education, Lord Krishna came to Dwaraka while Sudama, who had no any inclination for accumulating material wealth, got…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">67</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 67, "quote": "16.10.75 Sudama – The Lord’s Friend During his stay as a disciple at the hermitage of sage Sandipani, Lord Krishna had a Brahmin friend named Sudama. He was very indifferent in nature with no desires for the material things. A"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-76"><data key="title">Meeting of Lord with Gopas-Gopis</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says: "O Parikshit, Lord Krishna was passing his time with pleasure in Dwaraka. A total solar eclipse happened to fall during that period. People from all over India thronged in Kurukshetra to take a dip in sacred Ganges on that great occasion. All the Yaduvanshis too arrived there. When Vrajvasis (Inhabitants…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">68</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 68, "quote": "16.10.76 Meeting of Lord with Gopas-Gopis Shukdev says: \"O Parikshit, Lord Krishna was passing his time with pleasure in Dwaraka. A total solar eclipse happened to fall during that period. People from all over India thronged in Kuru"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-77"><data key="title">Organization of Yagya by Vasudeva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Devarishi Narad, Vyasa and many other great sages arrived in Kurukshetra to have a sight of Lord. Lord welcomed them all. Then Vasudev greeted them and enquired about way for his self up liftment. Laughingly, Narad said: "Vasudev, a person, already living at the bank of the Ganges, discards her holy water and goes to…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">68</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 68, "quote": "16.10.77 Organization of Yagya by Vasudeva Devarishi Narad, Vyasa and many other great sages arrived in Kurukshetra to have a sight of Lord. Lord welcomed them all. Then Vasudev greeted them and enquired about way for his self up lif"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-78"><data key="title">Revisiting of Devaki’s Six Sons</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">In Dwaraka, Krishna and Balarama used to greet their parent's first daily in the morning. Now Vasudev had recognised His identity. So after their return from Kurukshetra, when Krishna-Balarama went as usual to greet their parents, Vasudev greeted them first. Lord Krishna then preached his father about the metaphysical…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">69</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 69, "quote": "16.10.78 Revisiting of Devaki’s Six Sons In Dwaraka, Krishna and Balarama used to greet their parent's first daily in the morning. Now Vasudev had recognised His identity. So after their return from Kurukshetra, when Krishna-Balara"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-79"><data key="title">Love &amp; Marriage of Subhadra-Arjun</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">King Parikshit asked: "Gurudev, how did my grandfather Arjun and grandmother Subhadra got married? I want to hear this tale. Kindly narrate it to me." Shukdev said: Parikshit once travelling on a pilgrimage tour, Arjun reached in Prabhas region. There he learned that Balarama was desirous of marrying his sister…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">69</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 69, "quote": "16.10.79 Love &amp; Marriage of Subhadra-Arjun King Parikshit asked: \"Gurudev, how did my grandfather Arjun and grandmother Subhadra got married? I want to hear this tale. Kindly narrate it to me.\" Shukdev said: Parikshit once travelling"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-80"><data key="title">Prayers of Vedas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">King Parikshit asked: "Brahmin, Brahma is beyond the limit of do's and why's. He is free from the virtues like Sat, Raja and Tama. He cannot be seen by mind. On the other hand Guna (Virtues) is the subject of all the Shrutis (Vedas). How do Vedas then depict Brahma?" Shukdev says: "Parikshit, Lord is omnipotent and a…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">70</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 70, "quote": "16.10.80 Prayers of Vedas King Parikshit asked: \"Brahmin, Brahma is beyond the limit of do's and why's. He is free from the virtues like Sat, Raja and Tama. He cannot be seen by mind. On the other hand Guna (Virtues)"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-81"><data key="title">Release of Lord Shiva from Crisis</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Parikshit asked: "Gurudev, those among the gods, human beings and the demons, who worship Lord Lord Shiva, get wealth very soon. But those, who worship Lord Narayana, remain afflicated with scarcity. What is the reason for that?" Shukdev said : "Parikshit, your grandfather Yudhishthir had put the same question before…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">71</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 71, "quote": "16.10.81 Release of Lord Shiva from Crisis Parikshit asked: \"Gurudev, those among the gods, human beings and the demons, who worship Lord Lord Shiva, get wealth very soon. But those, who worship Lord Narayana, remain afflicated with"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-82"><data key="title">Testing of Trinity</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once upon a time, all the sages assembled on the bank of Saraswati river. A dispute errupted among them as to who among the trinity was the greatest. By consensus they appointed sage Bhrigu to test the trinity of Brahma, Vishnu and Mahesh. Bhrigu reached to Brahma, but did not greet him. Brahma got infuriated but…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">71</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 71, "quote": "16.10.82 Testing of Trinity Once upon a time, all the sages assembled on the bank of Saraswati river. A dispute errupted among them as to who among the trinity was the greatest. By consensus they appointed sage Bhrigu"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-10-83"><data key="title">Revival of Brahmin’s dead children</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There in Dwaraka lived a Brahmin. When the first son was born to his wife, it died immediately after birth. The Brahmin took his dead son to the royal court and complained: "My Son has died because of anti-Brahmin and licentious actions of the king." One by one, thus, eight sons were born to the Brahmin couple; but…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">71</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 71, "quote": "16.10.83 Revival of Brahmin’s dead children There in Dwaraka lived a Brahmin. When the first son was born to his wife, it died immediately after birth. The Brahmin took his dead son to the royal court and complained: \"My Son has died"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-11"><data key="title">Chapter 11</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">72</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 72, "quote": "16.11Chapter 11"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-11-1"><data key="title">Curse of Sages to Yaduvanshis</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Shukdev said: "O Parikshit, as the time passed, Yaduvanshis grew stronger and more influencial. Now no longer did they like the welcoming and treating of the great sages like Kanu, Dhrvasa, Maitreya, Dhannmy etc. by the Lord. Lord Krishna knew about their disliking, but secretly He was pleased by this change in…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">72</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 72, "quote": "16.11.1 Curse of Sages to Yaduvanshis Shukdev said: \"O Parikshit, as the time passed, Yaduvanshis grew stronger and more influencial. Now no longer did they like the welcoming and treating of the great sages like Kanu, Dhrvasa,"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-11-2"><data key="title">Preaching of Narad about Bhagvad</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once Narad arrived in Dwaraka. Vasudev prayed him and requested to provide him with pious company of Bhagwad dharma. Narad said: "O king, once upon a time, nine great sages arrived in the court of the king Janak. King Janak had put the same curiosity before them also. I will narrate the conversation that followed…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">73</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 73, "quote": "16.11.2 Preaching of Narad about Bhagvad Once Narad arrived in Dwaraka. Vasudev prayed him and requested to provide him with pious company of Bhagwad dharma. Narad said: \"O king, once upon a time, nine great sages arrived in the co"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-11-3"><data key="title">Lord’s Departure to his Abode</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">To remind the Lord about the moment of His departure, Brahma arrived in Dwaraka accompanied by all the gods. He prayed to Him: "O Lord, you have completed your duty of what we had once requested you. Now, You please return to your abode. Lord said: "On the seventh day from now, Dwaraka shall submerge in the sea and…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">73</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 73, "quote": "16.11.3 Lord’s Departure to his Abode To remind the Lord about the moment of His departure, Brahma arrived in Dwaraka accompanied by all the gods. He prayed to Him: \"O Lord, you have completed your duty of what we had once reque"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-11-4"><data key="title">Bhikshu-Geet</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Bhikshu Geet embeds a renunciant’s song in the later teachings. It connects suffering and mental discipline with the movement toward liberation.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">74</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 74, "quote": "16.11.4 Bhikshu-Geet Lord said: \"Uddhav, in the world, there is indeed a dearth of such tolerant people who can tolerate bitter remarks of evil ones. In this context there is a tale of a greedy Brahmin who lived"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-12"><data key="title">Chapter 12</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">74</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 74, "quote": "16.12Chapter 12"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-12-1"><data key="title">Description of Dynasties in Kaliyuga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukdev describes about the dynasties that would occur in Kaliyug. Ripunjay or Puranjay would be the last king of Brihdrath dynasty. This dynasty would be succeeded by the kings of Nand dynasty. Then a diplomatic Brahmin, named Chanakya, would exterminate this dynasty and enthrone Chandragupt Maurya. In Maurya…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">75</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 75, "quote": "16.12.1 Description of Dynasties in Kaliyuga Shukdev describes about the dynasties that would occur in Kaliyug. Ripunjay or Puranjay would be the last king of Brihdrath dynasty. This dynasty would be succeeded by the kings of Nand dyna"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-12-2"><data key="title">Last Preaching of Shukdevji</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says: O Parikshit, recitation of Shri Hari has occurred in Shrimad Bhagwad Mahapuran. O King, this soul is eternal, so you abandon this animal- like feeling that you would die. You are not the body. You are the eternal form of joy. Imbibe your mind with the reflections of Paramatma (supreme spirit) and have a…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">75</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 75, "quote": "16.12.2 Last Preaching of Shukdevji Shukdev says: O Parikshit, recitation of Shri Hari has occurred in Shrimad Bhagwad Mahapuran. O King, this soul is eternal, so you abandon this animal- like feeling that you would die. You a"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-12-3"><data key="title">Final Beatitude of Parikshit</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Parikshit’s final liberation closes the frame of a king listening to Shukadeva while facing death; it returns the long narrative to the purpose of hearing it.</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">75</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 75, "quote": "16.12.3 Final Beatitude of Parikshit Thus graced by Shukdev, king Parikshit faithfully worshiped Brahmrishi and prayed to him with folded hands: \"Lord, you are an idol of kindness. You have bestowed your graced on me narrating"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-12-4"><data key="title">Nagayagya of Janameejaya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When Janmejay, the son of Parikshit, learned that a snake had stung his father, he organised, Sarpsatra. During the Satra, and invoked by the hymns of Brahmins, snakes drew from far and near and got burnt in the fire. Frightened Takshak approached Indra for refuge. Indra promised to help him. There, on earth, Janmejay…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">76</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 76, "quote": "16.12.4 Nagayagya of Janameejaya When Janmejay, the son of Parikshit, learned that a snake had stung his father, he organised, Sarpsatra. During the Satra, and invoked by the hymns of Brahmins, snakes drew from far and near"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-12-5"><data key="title">Branches of Vedas and Division</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">SUTJI described about the Samhitas and divisions of Vedas to the sages like Shaunak etc. Ved Vyas divided Vedas into four parts and created four Sanhitas viz, Rig, Yajur, Sama and Atharva and taught Rigsanhita to Pail, Nigad sanhita to Vaishampayan, Chhandog Sanhita to Jemini and tought Atharvangiras Sanhita to…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">76</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 76, "quote": "16.12.5 Branches of Vedas and Division SUTJI described about the Samhitas and divisions of Vedas to the sages like Shaunak etc. Ved Vyas divided Vedas into four parts and created four Sanhitas viz, Rig, Yajur, Sama and Atharva an"}]</data></node><node id="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-12-6"><data key="title">Penance of Markandey &amp; Sighting of Maya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">SUTJI narrated the tale of Mrikandu's son Markandey. Taking shelter at Mahakaal (Lord Shiva), Markandey had turned away even Kaal (death). And by penance had a sight of Lord Shri Narayana. He prayed God to show His Maya (illusion). Thus Markandey saw the scene of Pralaya. All the creatures were drowning in the deluge.…</data><data key="doc">shrimad-bhagwat-puran</data><data key="page">76</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 76, "quote": "16.12.6 Penance of Markandey &amp; Sighting of Maya SUTJI narrated the tale of Mrikandu's son Markandey. Taking shelter at Mahakaal (Lord Shiva), Markandey had turned away even Kaal (death). And by penance had a sight of Lord Shri Narayana. H"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-1"><data key="title">Introduction</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">It contains tales pertaining to Lord Vishnu in his Vaaman (dwarf) incarnation. Sage Pulastya while giving answers to Narad's enquiry narrates the tale of Vaaman Puraan. All the Puraans give glimpses of Indian civilization and culture and Vaaman Puraan is no exception. It also teaches the virtue of righteousness and…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "6.1 Introduction This chapter contains only 1 section: Narad asks sage Pulastya about Vaaman incarnation: Vaaman Puraan is one of the eighteen Puraans written by sage Vyasa. It contains tales pertaining to Lord Vishnu in his Vaaman (dwarf) "}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-2"><data key="title">Tales of Lord Shiva</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 4 sections:</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "6.2 Tales of Lord Shiva This chapter contains 4 sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-2-1"><data key="title">Shiva Returns to Mandaar Mountain</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">DAKSHA PERFORMS A GRAND YAGYA Pulastya says- "This way, both Shiva and Sati lived above the clouds till the end of rainy season. When autumn season arrived, both decided on returning to Mandaar Mountain. Daksha had organised a grand Yagya in which he had invited all the prominent deities and sages except his own…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 1, "quote": "6.2.1 Shiva Returns to Mandaar Mountain DAKSHA PERFORMS A GRAND YAGYA Pulastya says- \"This way, both Shiva and Sati lived above the clouds till the end of rainy season. When autumn season arrived, both decided on returning to Mand"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-2-2"><data key="title">Atonement of Lord Shankar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva had committed a grave sin of Brahmahatya. All his efforts of separating Brahma's skull from his left palm went in vain. In the meantime, an entity named Brahmahatya appeared before him and entered into his body. Lord Shankar decided to go to Badrikashrama to atone for his sins. After reaching there, he…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "6.2.2 Atonement of Lord Shankar Lord Shiva had committed a grave sin of Brahmahatya. All his efforts of separating Brahma's skull from his left palm went in vain. In the meantime, an entity named Brahmahatya appeared befor"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-2-3"><data key="title">Sati Gives up her Life</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Sage Pulastya says- "O Narad! This way, Lord Shiva became famous as Kapaali. This was the reason why Daksha did not invite him. In the meantime, Jaya, sage Gautam's daughter went to see Sati at Mandaar Mountain. Seeing her alone, Sati asked about her two sisters, Jayanti and Aparajita. Jaya revealed to her that both…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "6.2.3 Sati Gives up her Life Sage Pulastya says- \"O Narad! This way, Lord Shiva became famous as Kapaali. This was the reason why Daksha did not invite him. In the meantime, Jaya, sage Gautam's daughter went to see Sati"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-2-4"><data key="title">The Destruction of Daksha’s Yagya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Fearing Lord Shiva's wrath, Lord Vishnu went to Hrishikesh and remained aloof for quite some time. Shiva's anger generated so much heat that it was enough to melt the eight Vasus. As a result, a river named Sita started flowing from there. After being attacked by Lord Shiva, the deities fled from the scene. Lord Shiva…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "6.2.4 The Destruction of Daksha’s Yagya Fearing Lord Shiva's wrath, Lord Vishnu went to Hrishikesh and remained aloof for quite some time. Shiva's anger generated so much heat that it was enough to melt the eight Vasus. As a resul"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-3"><data key="title">Tales of Demons</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 6 sections:</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "6.3 Tales of Demons This chapter contains 6 sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-3-1"><data key="title">Andhak – The Demon King</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Sage Narad was curious to know how the demon King Andhak behaved with the deities. He asked Pulastya in this regard. Pulastya replied- "After ascending the throne, Andhak did a severe penance to please Lord Shiva and received a boon according to which he could neither be killed by the deities nor by any mortal. He…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "6.3.1 Andhak – The Demon King Sage Narad was curious to know how the demon King Andhak behaved with the deities. He asked Pulastya in this regard. Pulastya replied- \"After ascending the throne, Andhak did a severe penanc"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-3-2"><data key="title">Sukeshi’s Queries</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sukeshi was the son of demon King Vidyutkeshi. He was a great devotee of Lord Shiva. Being pleased by his devotion, Lord Shiva had blessed him with invincibility and gifted him a city, which was situated in the sky. Sukeshi was a virtuous and just king. Once, he had gone into the Magadh forest where he found numerous…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "6.3.2 Sukeshi’s Queries Sukeshi was the son of demon King Vidyutkeshi. He was a great devotee of Lord Shiva. Being pleased by his devotion, Lord Shiva had blessed him with invincibility and gifted him a city, which"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-3-3"><data key="title">Sinful Deeds</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sukeshi asked the sages about the sinful deeds, which leads to hell. The sages replied- "People who condemn Veda, deities, Brahmins etc. go to hell. Those who don't believe in religious texts like Puraan etc. or those who cause obstacles in the accomplishment of Yagya or those who prevent a charitable man from giving…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "6.3.3 Sinful Deeds Sukeshi asked the sages about the sinful deeds, which leads to hell. The sages replied- \"People who condemn Veda, deities, Brahmins etc. go to hell. Those who don't believe in religious text"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-3-4"><data key="title">Jambudweep and Bharatvarsh</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sukeshi expressed his desire to the sages to know about Jambudweep. The sages replied- "Jambudweep is a very large island. It is sub-divided into nine parts. Ilavritvarsh is situated in its central part while Bhadraashvavarsh is situated towards its eastern side. Some prominent places situated around it are-…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "6.3.4 Jambudweep and Bharatvarsh Sukeshi expressed his desire to the sages to know about Jambudweep. The sages replied- \"Jambudweep is a very large island. It is sub-divided into nine parts. Ilavritvarsh is situated in its"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-3-5"><data key="title">Dashaang Dharma</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages say- "Dharma consists of ten properties such as non-violence, truth, charity, not stealing the property of others, forgiveness, self-restraint, cleanliness etc. Hence it is known as Dashaang Dharma. These properties are obligatory for people belonging to all castes and creeds. A Brahmin's life is categorised…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "6.3.5 Dashaang Dharma The sages say- \"Dharma consists of ten properties such as non-violence, truth, charity, not stealing the property of others, forgiveness, self-restraint, cleanliness etc. Hence it is known a"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-3-6"><data key="title">The Rise and Fall of Sukeshi’s Kingdom</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Pulastya says- "After being enlightened by the sages on various subjects, Sukeshi returned to his capital. He taught the demons all that he had learnt. As a result, all the demons became very virtuous. The newly attained virtuosity and religiousness made the demons invincible and powerful. Sukeshi's capital was…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "6.3.6 The Rise and Fall of Sukeshi’s Kingdom Pulastya says- \"After being enlightened by the sages on various subjects, Sukeshi returned to his capital. He taught the demons all that he had learnt. As a result, all the demons became ver"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-4"><data key="title">Tales of Nar-Narayana</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 3 sections:</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 6, "quote": "6.4 Tales of Nar-Narayana This chapter contains 3 sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-4-1"><data key="title">Manifestation of Nar-Narayana</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Sage Pulastya says- "Dharma, the possessor of divine body and who manifested from the heart of Lord Brahma married Murti, the daughter of Daksha. Four sons were born to them- Hari, Krishna, Nara and Narayan. Hari and Krishna engaged themselves in the practice of Yoga while Nar and Narayan went to the Himalaya Mountain…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 6, "quote": "6.4.1 Manifestation of Nar-Narayana Sage Pulastya says- \"Dharma, the possessor of divine body and who manifested from the heart of Lord Brahma married Murti, the daughter of Daksha. Four sons were born to them- Hari, Krishna,"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-4-2"><data key="title">Prahlad meets Nar-Narayana</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">One Narayan saw all three of them coming and he treated all of them with respect. As he was aware of the reasons for their arrival, he decided to give them a surprise. He made the figure of a beautiful woman on his thigh with a flower. The next moment, the figure transformed into a live woman, which surprised all of…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 6, "quote": "6.4.2 Prahlad meets Nar-Narayana One Narayan saw all three of them coming and he treated all of them with respect. As he was aware of the reasons for their arrival, he decided to give them a surprise. He made the figure of"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-4-3"><data key="title">Prahlad blessed by Nar-Narayana</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">A ferocious battle was fought between Prahlad and Nar-Narayan in which all types of lethal weapons were used. The battle continued for 1000 divine years. Ultimately, Prahlad was defeated in this battle. He went to Vaikunth and asked Lord Vishnu as to why Nar-Narayan was invincible. Lord Vishnu revealed to them that…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 6, "quote": "6.4.3 Prahlad blessed by Nar-Narayana A ferocious battle was fought between Prahlad and Nar-Narayan in which all types of lethal weapons were used. The battle continued for 1000 divine years. Ultimately, Prahlad was defeated in"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-5"><data key="title">Manifestation of Vegetation from the Deities</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Pulastya says- "Lotus flower manifested from Vishnu's navel in the month of Ashwin. Similarly, other vegetation also manifested from the different organs of the deities- Kadamb tree from the palm of Kamadev, Dhatura tree from the heart of Lord Shiva, Khair tree from the middle portion of Brahma's body, Kataiya tree…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 7, "quote": "6.5 Manifestation of Vegetation from the Deities Pulastya says- \"Lotus flower manifested from Vishnu's navel in the month of Ashwin. Similarly, other vegetation also manifested from the different organs of the deities- Kadamb tree from the"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-6"><data key="title">Mahishasura</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 4 sections:</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 7, "quote": "6.6 Mahishasura This chapter contains 4 sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-6-1"><data key="title">Mahishasura’s Atrocities</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">During ancient times, there lived two demons- Rambh and Karambh who would torment people. As both were sonless, they decided to do severe penance by keeping their bodies submerged in Panchmad River with a desire of having sons. Indra becoming fearful, took the guise of a crocodile and killed Karambh while he was doing…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 7, "quote": "6.6.1 Mahishasura’s Atrocities During ancient times, there lived two demons- Rambh and Karambh who would torment people. As both were sonless, they decided to do severe penance by keeping their bodies submerged in Panchma"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-6-2"><data key="title">The Demons – Chand and Mund</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, the demon brothers, Chand and Mund were passing near the Vindhya Mountain when they saw Goddess Chandika there. Both these demons were stunned by her divine beauty. They had never seen such a beautiful woman in their life. They went to Mahishasura and told him about her beauty. Mahishasura proceeded with a huge…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 8, "quote": "6.6.2 The Demons – Chand and Mund Once, the demon brothers, Chand and Mund were passing near the Vindhya Mountain when they saw Goddess Chandika there. Both these demons were stunned by her divine beauty. They had never seen"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-6-3"><data key="title">Killing of Mahishasura</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Narad asked sage Pulastya as to how Goddess Durga kill Mahishasura. Sage Pulastya replied- "After the killing of Chikshur, Mahishasura sent Namar to fight Goddess Durga. Namar proceeded towards the Vindhya Mountain accompanied by a huge battalion. After reaching there, he shot a volley of arrows towards the Vindhya…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 9, "quote": "6.6.3 Killing of Mahishasura Narad asked sage Pulastya as to how Goddess Durga kill Mahishasura. Sage Pulastya replied- \"After the killing of Chikshur, Mahishasura sent Namar to fight Goddess Durga. Namar proceeded towa"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-6-4"><data key="title">Reincarnation of Goddess Durga</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Revealing the reasons for Goddess Durga's re-incarnation, sage Pulastya says- "Goddess Durga is also known as Kaushiki as she had manifested from the cell (Koshika) of Uma's (Parvati's) body. After the manifestation, she went to Vindhya Mountain with the objective of killing the demons- Shumbh and Nishumbh. She was…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 10, "quote": "6.6.4 Reincarnation of Goddess Durga Revealing the reasons for Goddess Durga's re-incarnation, sage Pulastya says- \"Goddess Durga is also known as Kaushiki as she had manifested from the cell (Koshika) of Uma's (Parvati's) body"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-7"><data key="title">Tales of King Kuru and Kurukshetra</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">In course of time, Tapti gave birth to a son, who possessed all the signs of royalty. After growing up, he mastered all the four Vedas and other scriptures within a very short period. At the raw age of 24, he became a scholar. When he attained marriageable age, he was married to Saudamini- the daughter of Sudama.…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 10, "quote": "6.7 Tales of King Kuru and Kurukshetra In course of time, Tapti gave birth to a son, who possessed all the signs of royalty. After growing up, he mastered all the four Vedas and other scriptures within a very short period. At the"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-8"><data key="title">Bali – The Demon King</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After his father's death, he defeated the deities and drove them out from their abodes. Very soon, all the three worlds were under his control. He was a virtuous king and so were his subjects. During his reign, every corner in his kingdom would be abuzz with religious activities. As a result, the whole world became…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 11, "quote": "6.8 Bali – The Demon King This chapter contains 7 sections: Lomaharshan told the sages- \"Bali was the descendant of Virochan and the son of Hiranyakashipu. After his father's death, he defeated the deities and drove them out from their abod"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-8-1"><data key="title">Deities go to Brahma Loka</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages asked- "How were the deities able to defeat Bali and why did Lord Vishnu take the incarnation of Vaaman?" Lomaharshan replied- "After being defeated by Bali, Indra went to Meru Mountain where his mother Aditi lived and narrated the woeful tale of the deities' defeat. Aditi took her husband's (Kashyap's)…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 11, "quote": "6.8.1 Deities go to Brahma Loka The sages asked- \"How were the deities able to defeat Bali and why did Lord Vishnu take the incarnation of Vaaman?\" Lomaharshan replied- \"After being defeated by Bali, Indra went to Meru Mou"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-8-2"><data key="title">The Deities do Penance</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Brahma told the deities that he was aware of their plight. He was of the opinion that the mighty Bali could be defeated only by the Almighty himself and nobody else. Lord Brahma said- "You must go to a place called Amrit, which is situated towards the north of Ksheersagar. The Almighty God is engaged in an…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 12, "quote": "6.8.2 The Deities do Penance Lord Brahma told the deities that he was aware of their plight. He was of the opinion that the mighty Bali could be defeated only by the Almighty himself and nobody else. Lord Brahma said- \""}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-8-3"><data key="title">Lord Narayana Blesses Kashyap</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Narayan became pleased by Kashyap's eulogies and austere penance. He appeared before the deities and asked them to demand anything they wished for. Kashyap requested him to give Aditi, the privilege of becoming his mother. Lord Vishnu agreed to take birth as Aditi's son. The deities became pleased and all of them…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 12, "quote": "6.8.3 Lord Narayana Blesses Kashyap Lord Narayan became pleased by Kashyap's eulogies and austere penance. He appeared before the deities and asked them to demand anything they wished for. Kashyap requested him to give Aditi,"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-8-4"><data key="title">Lord Vishnu Appears</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lomaharshan says- Lord Vishnu appeared and told her that he was most willing to fulfil her wish. Aditi requested him to make her son- Indra, the lord of all the three worlds once again. Lord Vishnu assured her that he would take birth as her son and kill all the demons. Aditi had apprehensions as to how she could bear…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 12, "quote": "6.8.4 Lord Vishnu Appears Lomaharshan says- Lord Vishnu appeared and told her that he was most willing to fulfil her wish. Aditi requested him to make her son- Indra, the lord of all the three worlds once again. Lord"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-8-5"><data key="title">Bali’s Queries to Prahlad</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lomaharshan says- "When Bali saw the demons becoming devoid of radiance and power, he enquired about the reasons from his grandfather, Prahlad. He wondered whether it was a bad omen. Prahlad went into a deep trance and saw the whole earth shaking violently. He also saw high tides rising in the ocean. He was convinced…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 12, "quote": "6.8.5 Bali’s Queries to Prahlad Lomaharshan says- \"When Bali saw the demons becoming devoid of radiance and power, he enquired about the reasons from his grandfather, Prahlad. He wondered whether it was a bad omen. Prahlad"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-8-6"><data key="title">Manifestation of Lord Vaaman</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Bali felt sorry for hurting Prahlad's feelings. He said- "Dear grandfather! My intelligence has been overshadowed by my arrogant attitude. You have done the right thing by cursing me. I am not scared of losing all my prosperity and splendour but your anger is unbearable for me." Now even Prahlad realised that he had…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 13, "quote": "6.8.6 Manifestation of Lord Vaaman Bali felt sorry for hurting Prahlad's feelings. He said- \"Dear grandfather! My intelligence has been overshadowed by my arrogant attitude. You have done the right thing by cursing me. I am n"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-8-7"><data key="title">Lord Vaaman’s Demands</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lomaharshan says- "The shaking and trembling of earth surprised Bali and he enquired about this strange happening to Shukracharya. He was also surprised by the fact that Agni was not accepting the offerings. Shukracharya, after contemplating for a while on this matter, came to know about Vaaman's incarnation. He…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 13, "quote": "6.8.7 Lord Vaaman’s Demands Lomaharshan says- \"The shaking and trembling of earth surprised Bali and he enquired about this strange happening to Shukracharya. He was also surprised by the fact that Agni was not accepti"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-9"><data key="title">Sarasvati River</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages requested Lomaharshan to reveal about river Saraswati's origin, which flowed in Kurukshetra. Lomaharshan said- "Saraswati originates from Paakad tree (Indian fig tree). She enters Dwaitvan after passing through numerous mountains. While describing about her majesty, sage Markandeya felt that Saraswati was…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "6.9 Sarasvati River The sages requested Lomaharshan to reveal about river Saraswati's origin, which flowed in Kurukshetra. Lomaharshan said- \"Saraswati originates from Paakad tree (Indian fig tree). She enters"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-10"><data key="title">Kurukshetra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages requested Lomaharshan to describe about the geographical condition of Kurukshetra. Lomaharshan replied- "There are 7 different forest ranges in Kurukshetra. They are- "Kamyakvan, Aditivan, Vyasavan, Phalkivan, Suryavan, Madhuvan and Sheetvan. Nine rivers flow across Kurukshetra- Saraswati, Vaitarni, Aashga,…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "6.10 Kurukshetra The sages requested Lomaharshan to describe about the geographical condition of Kurukshetra. Lomaharshan replied- \"There are 7 different forest ranges in Kurukshetra. They are- \"Kamyakvan, A"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-11"><data key="title">Sage Mankanak Eulogizes Shiva</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Sage Mankanak was the Manasputra of Kashyap. He had seven sons, who were famously known as Marud Ganas. They are the one who hold all the movable and immovable objects of this world. Once, sage Mankanak injured his finger by the sharp edge of Kusha grass while he was trying to pluck them for his religious rites. But…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "6.11 Sage Mankanak Eulogizes Shiva Sage Mankanak was the Manasputra of Kashyap. He had seven sons, who were famously known as Marud Ganas. They are the one who hold all the movable and immovable objects of this world. Once, s"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-12"><data key="title">Some Sacred Places of Pilgrimage</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 4 sections:</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 15, "quote": "6.12 Some Sacred Places of Pilgrimage This chapter contains 4 sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-12-1"><data key="title">Aushanas Tirtha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing about the greatness of Aushanas, the most sacred place of pilgrimage, Lomaharshan narrates the following tale- "During Treta Yug, Lord Rama had killed many demons while he was in exile. Once, while he was living in Dandak forest, he had severed the head of a wicked demon with his Kshura arrow. The severed…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 15, "quote": "6.12.1 Aushanas Tirtha Describing about the greatness of Aushanas, the most sacred place of pilgrimage, Lomaharshan narrates the following tale- \"During Treta Yug, Lord Rama had killed many demons while he was in"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-12-2"><data key="title">Vashishtha-pavah Tirtha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages asked Lomaharshan about the origin of Vashishthapava Tirtha and about the reason why sage Vashishth was carried along by the powerful current of Saraswati river. Lomaharshan said- "There was great rivalry between sage Vashishth and sage Vishwamitra. In course of time, it turned into a bitter enemity.…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 15, "quote": "6.12.2 Vashishtha-pavah Tirtha The sages asked Lomaharshan about the origin of Vashishthapava Tirtha and about the reason why sage Vashishth was carried along by the powerful current of Saraswati river. Lomaharshan said-"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-12-3"><data key="title">Kamyakvan Tirtha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages asked Lomaharshan about the origin of Kamyakvan Tirtha. Lomaharshan replied- "Once upon a time, the sages of Naimisharanya came to Kurukshetra to take bath in river Saraswati. But, since they were large in number, the banks of river Saraswati could not accommodate all of them. In their helplessness, they…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 16, "quote": "6.12.3 Kamyakvan Tirtha The sages asked Lomaharshan about the origin of Kamyakvan Tirtha. Lomaharshan replied- \"Once upon a time, the sages of Naimisharanya came to Kurukshetra to take bath in river Saraswati. But,"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-12-4"><data key="title">Sthanu Tirtha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages then asked Lomaharshan about the origin of Sthanu Tirtha and the importance of Sthanu Vata. They also expressed their desire to know about the reservoir named Saanihitya and how Indra had filled it up with mud. Sage Lomaharshan replied- "Once, Sanat Kumar upon being asked by Baalkhilya and other sages the…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 16, "quote": "6.12.4 Sthanu Tirtha The sages then asked Lomaharshan about the origin of Sthanu Tirtha and the importance of Sthanu Vata. They also expressed their desire to know about the reservoir named Saanihitya and how In"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-13"><data key="title">Sages Take Lord Shiva’s Refuge</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Ashamed, the sages requested Lord Brahma to tell them the way to pacify Lord Shiva. Lord Brahma assured them that they would be forgiven if they took Lord Shiva's refuge. Lord Brahma went to Kailash Parvat accompanied by all the sages. After reaching there, he eulogised Lord Shiva with deep devotion. The sages also…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 17, "quote": "6.13 Sages Take Lord Shiva’s Refuge Ashamed, the sages requested Lord Brahma to tell them the way to pacify Lord Shiva. Lord Brahma assured them that they would be forgiven if they took Lord Shiva's refuge. Lord Brahma went to"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-14"><data key="title">Greatness of Sthanu Tirtha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 4 sections:</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 17, "quote": "6.14 Greatness of Sthanu Tirtha This chapter contains 4 sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-14-1"><data key="title">The Greatness of Saanihitya Sarovar and Sthanu Tirtha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sanat Kumar says- Describing the greatness of Saanihitya Sarovar, Lord Shiva told the sages that a devotee cannot attain salvation unless he pays a visit to this sacred place of pilgrimage. There is no other Tirtha like this one. "I would be easily accessible to a devotee who worships me with total devotion." After…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 17, "quote": "6.14.1 The Greatness of Saanihitya Sarovar and Sthanu Tirtha Sanat Kumar says- Describing the greatness of Saanihitya Sarovar, Lord Shiva told the sages that a devotee cannot attain salvation unless he pays a visit to this sacred place of pilgrimage."}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-14-2"><data key="title">Other Prominent Lingas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sanat Kumar says- "The sacred places of pilgrimage named Shukra Tirtha and Soma Tirtha are situated towards north and south of Sthanu Vata respectively. Daksha Tirtha is situated towards its south and Skand Tirtha towards its west. The sacrosanct Sthanu Tirtha is situated just in between all these four places of…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 17, "quote": "6.14.2 Other Prominent Lingas Sanat Kumar says- \"The sacred places of pilgrimage named Shukra Tirtha and Soma Tirtha are situated towards north and south of Sthanu Vata respectively. Daksha Tirtha is situated towards its"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-14-3"><data key="title">King Ven, Birth of Prithu</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Sage Markandeya then goes on to describe about the splendours of Sthanu Tirtha- Surya was Manu's father. Ven was Manu's grandson. He was wicked and always criticised the Vedas. Being displeased by his irreligious son, Ven's father left for the forest to do penance where he attained salvation. In course of time, Ven's…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 18, "quote": "6.14.3 King Ven, Birth of Prithu Sage Markandeya then goes on to describe about the splendours of Sthanu Tirtha- Surya was Manu's father. Ven was Manu's grandson. He was wicked and always criticised the Vedas. Being displea"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-14-4"><data key="title">Ven Attains Heaven</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sanat Kumar says- "Lord Shiva became pleased by his devotion and blessed Ven that he would manifest from his body as Andhak in his next birth. Due to the sins acquired by criticising the Vedas, he would be born in the family of the notorious demon Hiranyaaksh." Lord Shiva then asked Ven to demand any boon. Ven…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 18, "quote": "6.14.4 Ven Attains Heaven Sanat Kumar says- \"Lord Shiva became pleased by his devotion and blessed Ven that he would manifest from his body as Andhak in his next birth. Due to the sins acquired by criticising the Ved"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-15"><data key="title">The Origin of Brahmeshwar’s Four Heads</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After creating all the movable and immovable living things, Lord Brahma created a beautiful lady. He was so infatuated by her beauty that he expressed his desire to procreate with her help. As a result of this grave sin committed by Lord Brahma, one of his four heads got detached and fell on the ground. He carried his…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 19, "quote": "6.15 The Origin of Brahmeshwar’s Four Heads After creating all the movable and immovable living things, Lord Brahma created a beautiful lady. He was so infatuated by her beauty that he expressed his desire to procreate with her help."}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-16"><data key="title">Prithudak Tirtha</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Describing the majesty of Prithudak Tirtha, Mahadev told the deities- "All of you must pay a visit to this sacrosanct place of pilgrimage situated near Saanihitya reservoir. The day on which Moon, Sun and Jupiter unitedly come under Mrigshira Nakshatra is called Akshay Tritiya. You must go to that sacred place and…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 19, "quote": "6.16 Prithudak Tirtha Describing the majesty of Prithudak Tirtha, Mahadev told the deities- \"All of you must pay a visit to this sacrosanct place of pilgrimage situated near Saanihitya reservoir. The day on which"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-17"><data key="title">Birth of Uma and her Marriage to Shiva</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 10 sections:</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 19, "quote": "6.17 Birth of Uma and her Marriage to Shiva This chapter contains 10 sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-17-1"><data key="title">Mena gives birth to Uma</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Mena gave birth to three daughters and a son. The eldest daughter- Raagini was red complexioned and had red eyes. Kutila was fair complexioned and had beautiful eyes like that of lotus petals. The youngest daughter- Kali was dark complexioned and had beautiful eyes like the petals of blue lotus. Mena also had a son…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 19, "quote": "6.17.1 Mena gives birth to Uma Mena gave birth to three daughters and a son. The eldest daughter- Raagini was red complexioned and had red eyes. Kutila was fair complexioned and had beautiful eyes like that of lotus petal"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-17-2"><data key="title">Lord Shiva sends Marriage Proposal</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After paying a visit to Prithudak Tirtha, Lord Shiva returned to his abode. At that time, he was living at Mandaar Mountain. After Lord Shiva's contemplation, the Saptarishis arrived. After the formal salutations, Lord Shiva told the sages that his wife- Sati has re-incarnated as Uma. "All of you must go to Himalaya…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 20, "quote": "6.17.2 Lord Shiva sends Marriage Proposal After paying a visit to Prithudak Tirtha, Lord Shiva returned to his abode. At that time, he was living at Mandaar Mountain. After Lord Shiva's contemplation, the Saptarishis arrived. After"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-17-3"><data key="title">Uma Marries Lord Shiva</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lord Vishnu, Brahma, the deities and the Ganas were pleased to hear about Shiva's marriage. Lord Shiva went to his abode- Kailash Mountain to make preparations for the marriage ceremony. Lord Shiva's marriage procession proceeded towards Himalaya Mountain. His appearance was quite terrifying with a tiger skin around…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "6.17.3 Uma Marries Lord Shiva Lord Vishnu, Brahma, the deities and the Ganas were pleased to hear about Shiva's marriage. Lord Shiva went to his abode- Kailash Mountain to make preparations for the marriage ceremony. Lor"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-17-4"><data key="title">Vishwakarma Constructs a Palace</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After reaching Mandaar Mountain, Lord Shiva instructed Vishwakarma to construct a home. Vishwakarma constructed a spacious home for Shiva, which was spread in the area of 64 Yojans. The home was decorated with Swastika symbols and its walls were made of gold. One day, Lord Shiva jokingly referred to Uma as Kali…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "6.17.4 Vishwakarma Constructs a Palace After reaching Mandaar Mountain, Lord Shiva instructed Vishwakarma to construct a home. Vishwakarma constructed a spacious home for Shiva, which was spread in the area of 64 Yojans. The home"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-17-5"><data key="title">Killing of Nishumbh and Shumbh</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Shumbh and Nishumbh were sons of Danu. They wanted to avenge Mahishasura's death and sent many demons like to bring Goddess Chandika after enslaving her. First of all, Sugreev was sent followed by Dhumralochan but both of them were killed by the Goddess. When Shumbh and Nishumbh heard of their death, they were…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 22, "quote": "6.17.5 Killing of Nishumbh and Shumbh Shumbh and Nishumbh were sons of Danu. They wanted to avenge Mahishasura's death and sent many demons like to bring Goddess Chandika after enslaving her. First of all, Sugreev was sent follo"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-17-6"><data key="title">Birth of Kaartikeya</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Swallowing of Lord Shiva's sperm had subdued Agni's radiance. The deities advised him to take Lord Brahma's help. While he was on his way to Brahma loka, he came across a river named Kutila and requested her to hold Shiva's sperm. She agreed and requested him to release it in her waters. In course of time, she became…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 24, "quote": "6.17.6 Birth of Kaartikeya Swallowing of Lord Shiva's sperm had subdued Agni's radiance. The deities advised him to take Lord Brahma's help. While he was on his way to Brahma loka, he came across a river named Kutila"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-17-7"><data key="title">Kaartikeya Kills Taraka and Mahishasura</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After being appointed as the chief commander of the deities, Kaartikeya sought his guardians' permission to kill the demons- Taraka and Mahishasura. All the deities including Lord Vishnu blessed Kaartikeya and wished for his victory. Lord Vishnu eulogised Kaartikeya and praised his valiance. Kaartikeya then proceeded…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 24, "quote": "6.17.7 Kaartikeya Kills Taraka and Mahishasura After being appointed as the chief commander of the deities, Kaartikeya sought his guardians' permission to kill the demons- Taraka and Mahishasura. All the deities including Lord Vishnu ble"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-17-8"><data key="title">Ritudhwaj Injures Paatalketu</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">King Ripujeet- a descendant of Raghu lived with his valiant and brave son named Ritudhwaj. Ritudhwaj being a virtuous prince thought it as his duty to protect all the sages and hermits. Under his protection, the sages performed their religious duties without any problem. During Ripujeet's reign, a great sage- Gaalav…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 26, "quote": "6.17.8 Ritudhwaj Injures Paatalketu King Ripujeet- a descendant of Raghu lived with his valiant and brave son named Ritudhwaj. Ritudhwaj being a virtuous prince thought it as his duty to protect all the sages and hermits. Unde"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-17-9"><data key="title">Lord Shiva does Penance</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva was so deeply attached to Uma that for 1000 years, he was possessed by only her thoughts. He forgot about his duties and as a result, there was anarchy all over the world. His obsession with Uma had also decreased his radiance. He decided to do penance with the objective of regaining his radiance. When Uma…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 26, "quote": "6.17.9 Lord Shiva does Penance Lord Shiva was so deeply attached to Uma that for 1000 years, he was possessed by only her thoughts. He forgot about his duties and as a result, there was anarchy all over the world. His obs"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-17-10"><data key="title">Lord Vishnu Kills Mura</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Mura was the Aurasputra of sage Kashyap and Danu. When he saw the demons being vanquished by the deities, he went to do penance with the objective of getting boons from Lord Brahma. When Lord Brahma appeared before him, he said- "O lord! Bless me with a boon, which enables me to kill anybody merely by the touch of my…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 27, "quote": "6.17.10 Lord Vishnu Kills Mura Mura was the Aurasputra of sage Kashyap and Danu. When he saw the demons being vanquished by the deities, he went to do penance with the objective of getting boons from Lord Brahma. When Lor"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-18"><data key="title">Description of Hells</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Brahma, on being enquired by Sanat Kumar on the different hells said- "Those who have illicit relationship or are in the company of sinners go to the first hell. Felling of trees and stealing the fruits of others' tree lead to the second hell. People who kill creatures unworthy of killing and those who quarrel…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 27, "quote": "6.18 Description of Hells Lord Brahma, on being enquired by Sanat Kumar on the different hells said- \"Those who have illicit relationship or are in the company of sinners go to the first hell. Felling of trees and st"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-19"><data key="title">King Danda and Araja</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 4 sections:</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 28, "quote": "6.19 King Danda and Araja This chapter contains 4 sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-19-1"><data key="title">Andhakasura – The Demon</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Narad asks sage Pulastya- "What did the demon Andhak do after entering Paatal loka and how was Lord Shiva's life at Mandaar Mountain?" Pulastya says- "Andhak was infatuated by Girija's divine beauty and his lust only became stronger as the days passed by. Although Prahlad tried very hard to convince him and narrated…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 28, "quote": "6.19.1 Andhakasura – The Demon Narad asks sage Pulastya- \"What did the demon Andhak do after entering Paatal loka and how was Lord Shiva's life at Mandaar Mountain?\" Pulastya says- \"Andhak was infatuated by Girija's divin"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-19-2"><data key="title">Vishvakarma becomes a Monkey</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Continuing with his story, King Danda says- "Chitrangada stayed for a long period at Saptagodaavar. She used to spend time by engaging in the worship of Lord Haatkeshwar waiting for the day she would meet her husband. Vishwakarma had become a monkey due to the curse of that sage and started living at Shaalveya…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 29, "quote": "6.19.2 Vishvakarma becomes a Monkey Continuing with his story, King Danda says- \"Chitrangada stayed for a long period at Saptagodaavar. She used to spend time by engaging in the worship of Lord Haatkeshwar waiting for the day"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-19-3"><data key="title">Search for Chitrangada and Vedavati</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">In the meantime, both daughters of Yaksha and the demon arrived at Shrikanth Mahadev temple and engaged themselves in the worship of lord Mahadev. One day, sage Gaalav arrived at the temple and was astonished to find two young ladies at such a deserted place. While taking bath, he heard the melodious songs of the two…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 30, "quote": "6.19.3 Search for Chitrangada and Vedavati In the meantime, both daughters of Yaksha and the demon arrived at Shrikanth Mahadev temple and engaged themselves in the worship of lord Mahadev. One day, sage Gaalav arrived at the temple"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-19-4"><data key="title">Sage Shukracharya Curses Danda</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Prahlad says- "Blinded by lust, Danda forcibly breached the modesty of unrelenting Araja and returned to his palace. Araja was saddened by the inability to protect her chastity and started wailing. When Shukracharya returned to his hermitage, he found Araja lying down at the entrance. He asked her about what…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 32, "quote": "6.19.4 Sage Shukracharya Curses Danda Prahlad says- \"Blinded by lust, Danda forcibly breached the modesty of unrelenting Araja and returned to his palace. Araja was saddened by the inability to protect her chastity and started w"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-20"><data key="title">Lord Shiva Prepares for the Battle</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 7 sections:</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 32, "quote": "6.20 Lord Shiva Prepares for the Battle This chapter contains 7 sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-20-1"><data key="title">Ganas Throng Mandar Mountain</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Pulastya says- "When Lord Shiva became aware of Andhak's imminent attack, he requested Nandi to summon all the Ganas who were more than 700 crores in number. Some of the prominent Ganas like Pashupat, Kaalmukh, Mahavrati, Digambar, Maini, Mahapashupat and Vrishabhdhwaj arrived at Mandaar Mountain to help Lord Shiva at…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 32, "quote": "6.20.1 Ganas Throng Mandar Mountain Pulastya says- \"When Lord Shiva became aware of Andhak's imminent attack, he requested Nandi to summon all the Ganas who were more than 700 crores in number. Some of the prominent Ganas like"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-20-2"><data key="title">The Battle Commences</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Pulastya says- "When the Ganas saw Andhak's army approaching the Mandaar Mountain, they made a loud roar, which reverberated through the land and sky. Hearing this roar, Lord Ganesha became apprehensive and went to Mandaar Mountain to ask Lord Shiva about the origin of that tremendous roar. Lord Shiva told him- "I am…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 33, "quote": "6.20.2 The Battle Commences Pulastya says- \"When the Ganas saw Andhak's army approaching the Mandaar Mountain, they made a loud roar, which reverberated through the land and sky. Hearing this roar, Lord Ganesha became"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-20-3"><data key="title">Shukracharya Uses Sanjeevani Vidya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Andhak went to Shukracharya and narrated the woeful tale of demons' defeat. He also requested Shukracharya to help the demons win the battle. Shukracharya was dejected but he assured the demons that he would make alive, all the dead demons with the help of Sanjeevani Vidya and really all the mighty demons like Jambh,…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 33, "quote": "6.20.3 Shukracharya Uses Sanjeevani Vidya Andhak went to Shukracharya and narrated the woeful tale of demons' defeat. He also requested Shukracharya to help the demons win the battle. Shukracharya was dejected but he assured the dem"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-20-4"><data key="title">Lord Shiva Kills Andhak</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Inspite of death and destruction caused to his army, Andhak still wanted to continue fighting against Lord Shiva's army. He even dismissed the advises of Shukracharya who wanted a momentary retreat and rebuffed him by saying- "All the deities including Maheshwar and Indra cannot save themselves from my wrath. Very…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 35, "quote": "6.20.4 Lord Shiva Kills Andhak Inspite of death and destruction caused to his army, Andhak still wanted to continue fighting against Lord Shiva's army. He even dismissed the advises of Shukracharya who wanted a momentary"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-20-5"><data key="title">The Origin of Marut Ganas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Narad asked Pulastya about the battle which Indra fought with the demon at Malay Mountain. Pulastya replied- "Andhak's followers- Maya and Taar tried to enter Pataal loka after being defeated by the deities. While they were passing close to the Malay Mountain, they decided to live there being enchanted by its natural…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">36</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 36, "quote": "6.20.5 The Origin of Marut Ganas Narad asked Pulastya about the battle which Indra fought with the demon at Malay Mountain. Pulastya replied- \"Andhak's followers- Maya and Taar tried to enter Pataal loka after being defeate"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-20-6"><data key="title">Marut Ganas of Different Manvataras</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sage Pulastya says- "O Narad! Now, I am going to describe about the origin of Marut Ganas of various Manvantaras. Savan was Swayambhu Manu's son. He had no sons and when he died, the sorrowful queen- Sudeva heard a heavenly voice, which instructed her to give up her life by jumping into the burning pyre." "You would…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">36</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 36, "quote": "6.20.6 Marut Ganas of Different Manvataras Sage Pulastya says- \"O Narad! Now, I am going to describe about the origin of Marut Ganas of various Manvantaras. Savan was Swayambhu Manu's son. He had no sons and when he died, the sorrowf"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-20-7"><data key="title">Lord Vishnu Kills Kaalnemi</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After hearing the news of Bali's coronation, Maya and some other demons went and advised Bali to avenge the killings of his father- Virochan and other relatives by Indra. Bali became furious and marched with a huge army to fight the deities. When Indra heard this news, he started making preparations for the battle and…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">37</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 37, "quote": "6.20.7 Lord Vishnu Kills Kaalnemi After hearing the news of Bali's coronation, Maya and some other demons went and advised Bali to avenge the killings of his father- Virochan and other relatives by Indra. Bali became furious"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-21"><data key="title">King Bali</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 2 sections:</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">38</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 38, "quote": "6.21 King Bali This chapter contains 2 sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-21-1"><data key="title">Bali Rules Heaven</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The mighty Banasura saw the demons running away from the battlefield and came to their rescue. The arrival of Banasura boosted the morale of the defeated demons and they returned to fight the deities. Lord Vishnu advised the deities to fight gallantly and disappeared from the battlefield. Shukracharya assured the…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">38</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 38, "quote": "6.21.1 Bali Rules Heaven The mighty Banasura saw the demons running away from the battlefield and came to their rescue. The arrival of Banasura boosted the morale of the defeated demons and they returned to fight th"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-21-2"><data key="title">The Splendors of Bali</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Pulastya says- "The just rule of Bali had transformed the Kaliyuga into Satayuga. Even his subjects engaged themselves in virtuous activities like penance and Yagyas. They practised non-violence and always spoke the truth. One day, Goddess Trailokya Lakshmi went to Bali and said- "I have come to bless you because you…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">38</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 38, "quote": "6.21.2 The Splendors of Bali Pulastya says- \"The just rule of Bali had transformed the Kaliyuga into Satayuga. Even his subjects engaged themselves in virtuous activities like penance and Yagyas. They practised non-viol"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-22"><data key="title">Mythological Tales</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 7 sections:</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">39</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 39, "quote": "6.22 Mythological Tales This chapter contains 7 sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-22-1"><data key="title">Dhundhu Performs Ashvamedhya Yagya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Pulastya says- "During his pilgrimage, Prahlad visited many sacred places like Manas Tirtha, Kaushiki, Devahnad and Hastinapur etc. At last he reached the bank of river Yamuna where he worshipped Lord Trivikram (Vaaman)." Narad was surprised at the mention of Vaaman's name and asked Pulastya- "Lord Vishnu is still to…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">39</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 39, "quote": "6.22.1 Dhundhu Performs Ashvamedhya Yagya Pulastya says- \"During his pilgrimage, Prahlad visited many sacred places like Manas Tirtha, Kaushiki, Devahnad and Hastinapur etc. At last he reached the bank of river Yamuna where he worsh"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-22-2"><data key="title">Pururava Regains his Good Looks</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Pulastya says- "After worshipping Lord Trivikram, Prahlad went to Lingabhedak Mountain and worshipped Lord Shankar. He also visited many more sacred places like Kedar Tirtha, Badrikashrama, Bhadrakarna and Vipaasha. At last, he reached Irawati and worshipped that Almighty Lord with whose blessings, Pururava was able…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">39</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 39, "quote": "6.22.2 Pururava Regains his Good Looks Pulastya says- \"After worshipping Lord Trivikram, Prahlad went to Lingabhedak Mountain and worshipped Lord Shankar. He also visited many more sacred places like Kedar Tirtha, Badrikashrama,"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-22-3"><data key="title">The Description of Nakshatra Purusha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sage Narad asks Pulastya about the austerity named Nakshatra Purusha which King Pururava had accomplished to please Lord Vishnu. Pulastya says- "All the constellations dwell in different parts of Lord Vishnu's body. The Mula Nakshatra dwells in his feet, Rohini Nakshatra in his thighs, Ashwini in his knees,…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">40</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 40, "quote": "6.22.3 The Description of Nakshatra Purusha Sage Narad asks Pulastya about the austerity named Nakshatra Purusha which King Pururava had accomplished to please Lord Vishnu. Pulastya says- \"All the constellations dwell in different par"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-22-4"><data key="title">Prahlad’s Pilgrimage</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Pulastya says- "Prahlad went to the sacred place called Rishikanya and took a holy dip in the Irawati River. After that, he worshipped Lord Janardan in the temple situated nearby and then proceeded on his journey towards Kurukshetra. After reaching there, he worshipped Lord Kurudhwaj and paid a visit Lord Narsimha…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">40</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 40, "quote": "6.22.4 Prahlad’s Pilgrimage Pulastya says- \"Prahlad went to the sacred place called Rishikanya and took a holy dip in the Irawati River. After that, he worshipped Lord Janardan in the temple situated nearby and then pr"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-22-5"><data key="title">Tales of Upamanyu and Shridama</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Sage Narad was still not satisfied by Pulastya's explanation and asked Pulastya as to what compelled Lord Vishnu to give his chakra to Lord Shiva. Pulastya says- "There used to live a learned Brahmin named Veetmanyu. He was well versed in all the Vedas and other scriptures. His wife's name was Atreyi and Upamanyu was…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">41</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 41, "quote": "6.22.5 Tales of Upamanyu and Shridama Sage Narad was still not satisfied by Pulastya's explanation and asked Pulastya as to what compelled Lord Vishnu to give his chakra to Lord Shiva. Pulastya says- \"There used to live a learne"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-22-6"><data key="title">Gajendra and the Crocodile</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sage Narad requests Pulastya to describe how Gajendra managed to release itself from the jaws of the crocodile. Pulastya says- "There lived a crocodile in a reservoir near Trikut Mountain. One day, a thirsty elephant arrived there to quench its thirst. The wicked crocodile caught hold of the elephant's leg and dragged…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">41</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 41, "quote": "6.22.6 Gajendra and the Crocodile Sage Narad requests Pulastya to describe how Gajendra managed to release itself from the jaws of the crocodile. Pulastya says- \"There lived a crocodile in a reservoir near Trikut Mountain. O"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-22-7"><data key="title">The Greatness of Sarasvat Stotra</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Sage Pulastya says- "There lived a Kshatriya who kept enemity towards Brahmins. After his death, he was reborn as a demon and spent his life indulging in nefarious activities. Once he saw a hermit doing penance. He wanted to devour him but was unsuccessful in his attempt as the hermit had protected himself by chanting…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">42</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 42, "quote": "6.22.7 The Greatness of Sarasvat Stotra Sage Pulastya says- \"There lived a Kshatriya who kept enemity towards Brahmins. After his death, he was reborn as a demon and spent his life indulging in nefarious activities. Once he saw a"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-23"><data key="title">Bali Performs Ashvamedhya Yagya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 6 sections:</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">42</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 42, "quote": "6.23 Bali Performs Ashvamedhya Yagya This chapter contains 6 sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-23-1"><data key="title">Bali’s Arrival at Kurukshetra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Pulastya says- "After the departure of Prahlad on his pilgrimage, Bali desirous of performing a Yagya went to Kurukshetra. Shukracharya had invited the descendants of sage Bhargava to supervise the proceedings of Ashwamedha Yagya but his decision was not approved by the descendants of some other sages like Atri,…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">42</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 42, "quote": "6.23.1 Bali’s Arrival at Kurukshetra Pulastya says- \"After the departure of Prahlad on his pilgrimage, Bali desirous of performing a Yagya went to Kurukshetra. Shukracharya had invited the descendants of sage Bhargava to superv"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-23-2"><data key="title">Arrival of Lord Vaaman at Kurukshetra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Pulastya says- "After satisfying Bharadwaj's curiosity about his different abodes, Lord Vaaman left for Kurukshetra where Bali was busy performing his Yagya. As soon as Lord Vaaman reached Kurukshetra, the whole earth started shaking violently and high tides rose in the ocean. Being frightened of Lord Vishnu, Agni did…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">43</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 43, "quote": "6.23.2 Arrival of Lord Vaaman at Kurukshetra Pulastya says- \"After satisfying Bharadwaj's curiosity about his different abodes, Lord Vaaman left for Kurukshetra where Bali was busy performing his Yagya. As soon as Lord Vaaman reached K"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-23-3"><data key="title">Lord Vaaman’s Demands</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Vamana makes his request at Bali’s sacrifice. The following scenes narrate the cosmic measure, Brahma’s worship and Bali’s descent to Patala.</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">44</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 44, "quote": "6.23.3 Lord Vaaman’s Demands Bali was pleased that he would get an opportunity to see the Almighty God whom the sages contemplate upon. He was proud of his good fortune that the lord was paying him a visit. Shukracharya"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-23-4"><data key="title">Lord Brahma Worships Lord Vaaman</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Narad asked sage Pulastya as to where did Lord Vaaman go after disappearing from the oblation site. Pulastya replied- "Lord Vaaman went to Brahma loka mounted on his Garud. On seeing him, Lord Brahma gave a warm reception and after worshipping him asked about the reason for his sudden arrival. Lord Vaaman told him…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">45</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 45, "quote": "6.23.4 Lord Brahma Worships Lord Vaaman Narad asked sage Pulastya as to where did Lord Vaaman go after disappearing from the oblation site. Pulastya replied- \"Lord Vaaman went to Brahma loka mounted on his Garud. On seeing him, Lo"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-23-5"><data key="title">Bali Lives in Paatal Loka</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Pulastya says- After reaching the nether world, Bali got constructed a beautiful city made of diamonds and other precious stones. Vishwakarma had constructed an enormous sized altar in the central part of the city. The windows of Bali's palace were well decorated with pearls. Bali lived there in luxury and by enjoying…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">45</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 45, "quote": "6.23.5 Bali Lives in Paatal Loka Pulastya says- After reaching the nether world, Bali got constructed a beautiful city made of diamonds and other precious stones. Vishwakarma had constructed an enormous sized altar in the c"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-23-6"><data key="title">Bali’s Queries to Prahlad</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Bali enquired about the virtues which a man attains by the worship of Lord Vishnu. He also asked Prahlad about the worship and its rituals by which Lord Vishnu is pleased. Prahlad says- "Sages believe that the donations which are made with total devotion and in the name of Lord Vishnu exist forever. Lord Vishnu is…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 46, "quote": "6.23.6 Bali’s Queries to Prahlad Bali enquired about the virtues which a man attains by the worship of Lord Vishnu. He also asked Prahlad about the worship and its rituals by which Lord Vishnu is pleased. Prahlad says- \"Sag"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-24"><data key="title">Benefits of Studying Vamana Purana</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Study of Vaaman Puraan helps a man to enhance his knowledge and makes him free from all kinds of worldly attachments. It also increases his love and devotion towards the lotus feet of Lord Vishnu. There are ten characteristics evident in all the Puraans- 1) Sarg. 2) Visarg. 3) Sthaan. 4) Poshan. 5) Uti. 6) Vritti. 7)…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 46, "quote": "6.24 Benefits of Studying Vamana Purana Study of Vaaman Puraan helps a man to enhance his knowledge and makes him free from all kinds of worldly attachments. It also increases his love and devotion towards the lotus feet of Lord V"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-25"><data key="title">Vaaman Incarnation</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">This chapter contains 2 sections:</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 46, "quote": "6.25 Vaaman Incarnation This chapter contains 2 sections:"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-25-1"><data key="title">Indra does Penance to Atone for his Sins</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Pulastya says after being defeated by the demons Indra went to Brahma loka and narrated his woeful tale to him. Sage Kashyap- his father was also present there. Lord Brahma told Indra that he was reaping the fruits of his evil deeds. Sage Kashyap told him that his miseries were due to the sin acquired by the…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 46, "quote": "6.25.1 Indra does Penance to Atone for his Sins Pulastya says after being defeated by the demons Indra went to Brahma loka and narrated his woeful tale to him. Sage Kashyap- his father was also present there. Lord Brahma told Indra that h"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-25-2"><data key="title">Bali Abuses Lord Vishnu</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After Lord Vishnu had established himself inside Aditi's womb, the demons became devoid of their radiance. Bali was surprised by this sudden development and asked Prahlad about it. Prahlad went into deep meditation and saw Lord Vishnu manifesting in Aditi's womb. After coming out from his meditative trance, he told…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">47</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 47, "quote": "6.25.2 Bali Abuses Lord Vishnu After Lord Vishnu had established himself inside Aditi's womb, the demons became devoid of their radiance. Bali was surprised by this sudden development and asked Prahlad about it. Prahlad w"}]</data></node><node id="p-vamana-puran-6-26"><data key="title">The Deities Resting Days</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Vishnu takes rest on Sheshnaag during the whole period of Uttaraayan, which begins just after the last day of the bright lunar phase in the Hindu month of Ashaadh is over. After he goes to sleep, all the deities, Gandharva, Devamaata etc. follow suit. Narad requested Sage Pulastya to shed light on the days when…</data><data key="doc">vamana-puran</data><data key="page">47</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 47, "quote": "6.26 The Deities Resting Days Lord Vishnu takes rest on Sheshnaag during the whole period of Uttaraayan, which begins just after the last day of the bright lunar phase in the Hindu month of Ashaadh is over. After he goes"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-1"><data key="title">Lord Varah Enlightens Prithvi</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This section contains 6 sections as follows:</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 1, "quote": "8.1 Lord Varah Enlightens Prithvi This section contains 6 sections as follows:"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-1-1"><data key="title">Prithvi’s Queries</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Vishnu, in his incarnation of Varah( a boar),had liberated Prithvi(Mother Earth) from the clutches of Hiranyaksha- the mighty demon who had abducted her to rasatal.After being rescued,Prithvi heaved a sigh of relief and looked at her saviour,who was smiling.She curiously asked lord Varah- "How does the process of…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 1, "quote": "8.1.1 Prithvi’s Queries Lord Vishnu, in his incarnation of Varah( a boar),had liberated Prithvi(Mother Earth) from the clutches of Hiranyaksha- the mighty demon who had abducted her to rasatal.After being rescued,P"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-1-2"><data key="title">Priyavrata Attains to Heavenly Abode</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing the incident which enabled King Priyavrata to attain to heavenly abode Lord Varah told Prithvi- "Swayambhuva Manu lived during the first kalpa.He had two sons-Priyavrata and Uttanpad.Priyavrata was extremely virtuous by nature.In course of time,he relinquished his throne and after distributing his kingdom…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 3, "quote": "8.1.2 Priyavrata Attains to Heavenly Abode Describing the incident which enabled King Priyavrata to attain to heavenly abode Lord Varah told Prithvi- \"Swayambhuva Manu lived during the first kalpa.He had two sons-Priyavrata and Uttan"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-1-3"><data key="title">Ashvashira’s Unification with Lord Narayana</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Prithvi asked lord Varah about the famous incident which described Ashvashira's unification with lord Narayan.Lord Varah then narrated the following tale- "During ancient times,there used to live a virtuous king named Ashvashira.He had performed an ashwamedh yagya in which he had donated large amount of…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 4, "quote": "8.1.3 Ashvashira’s Unification with Lord Narayana Prithvi asked lord Varah about the famous incident which described Ashvashira's unification with lord Narayan.Lord Varah then narrated the following tale- \"During ancient times,there used to"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-1-4"><data key="title">King Vasu and Sage Raimya Unite with Sri Hari</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Prithvi was anxious to know about the effect sage Vrihaspati's discources had on King Vasu as well as sage Raimya.She asked lord Varah-"What did both king Vasu and sage Raimya do after listening to the preachings of sage Vrihaspati?" Lord Varah replied-"King Vasu was so impressed by Vrihaspati's discources that he…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 5, "quote": "8.1.4 King Vasu and Sage Raimya Unite with Sri Hari Prithvi was anxious to know about the effect sage Vrihaspati's discources had on King Vasu as well as sage Raimya.She asked lord Varah-\"What did both king Vasu and sage Raimya do after liste"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-1-5"><data key="title">Matsya Avatar</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, when lord Vishnu awakened from his yoga nidra, he found that the Vedas had submerged in the ocean as the result of pralaya (deluge).He incarnated as matsya(fish) to retrieve the Vedas from the ocean-bed.As soon as lord Matsya entered into the ocean,HE attained such an enormous size that the deities became…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 7, "quote": "8.1.5 Matsya Avatar Once, when lord Vishnu awakened from his yoga nidra, he found that the Vedas had submerged in the ocean as the result of pralaya (deluge).He incarnated as matsya(fish) to retrieve the Vedas"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-1-6"><data key="title">King Durjay</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">During satya yuga,there lived a king named Suprateek.Although he had two queens,yet none of them bore any son to him.One day,Suprateek went to meet sage Durvasa,who had his hermitage at the foothills of chitrakut mountain and engaged himself in the sevice of Durvasa.The sage blessed the king and prophesised that he…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 7, "quote": "8.1.6 King Durjay During satya yuga,there lived a king named Suprateek.Although he had two queens,yet none of them bore any son to him.One day,Suprateek went to meet sage Durvasa,who had his hermitage at the"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-2"><data key="title">Shraddh Rituals</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This section contains 9 sections as follows:</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 8, "quote": "8.2 Shraddh Rituals This section contains 9 sections as follows:"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-2-1"><data key="title">Pitras and the Significance of ‘Shraddh</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once,sage Markandeya arrived at Naimisharanya to see sage Gaurmukh.Sage Gaurmukh was delighted to find such an enlightened soul at his hermitage.After the formalities were over, Gaurmukh requested him to shed light on the venerability of ancestors and the significance of shraddh rituals. Sage Markandeya replied-"Lord…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 8, "quote": "8.2.1 Pitras and the Significance of ‘Shraddh Once,sage Markandeya arrived at Naimisharanya to see sage Gaurmukh.Sage Gaurmukh was delighted to find such an enlightened soul at his hermitage.After the formalities were over, Gaurmukh req"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-2-2"><data key="title">The Appropriate Time for Performing Shraddh</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing the auspicious time for performing the rituals of shraddh,Markandeya told Gurumukh-"A person desirous of performing the rituals of 'kamya shraddh' can do so on any of the following days-a) at the commencement of uttarayan or dakshinayan. b)at the time when vyatipat yoga takes place. c) at the time when…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 9, "quote": "8.2.2 The Appropriate Time for Performing Shraddh Describing the auspicious time for performing the rituals of shraddh,Markandeya told Gurumukh-\"A person desirous of performing the rituals of 'kamya shraddh' can do so on any of the followin"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-2-3"><data key="title">Shraddh Kalpa</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing the types of brahmin,considered to be elligible to preside over the rituals of shraddh, Markandeya told sage Gaurmukh-"The following types of brahmin are elligible to perform shraddh-'trinachiket' 'trimadhu' 'trisuparna' and the brahmins who are well versed in all the Vedas.The shraddh rituals can be…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 9, "quote": "8.2.3 Shraddh Kalpa Describing the types of brahmin,considered to be elligible to preside over the rituals of shraddh, Markandeya told sage Gaurmukh-\"The following types of brahmin are elligible to perform shra"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-2-4"><data key="title">How to Invite Brahmins to a Shraddh Ceremony</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Invitation should be given to the all the brahmins one day in advance.If uivited brahmins arrive to attend the shraddh ceremonythey should be treated with due respect.The brahmin who is supposed to perform the rituals should wash the feet of other brahmins to show his respect towards them.He should then help them…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 9, "quote": "8.2.4 How to Invite Brahmins to a Shraddh Ceremony Invitation should be given to the all the brahmins one day in advance.If uivited brahmins arrive to attend the shraddh ceremonythey should be treated with due respect.The brahmin who is supp"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-2-5"><data key="title">Number of Brahmins</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">As far as the number of invited brahmins to the shraddh ceremony of the ancestors is concerned,the scriptures have strictly put it to odd numbers-one,three,five,etc.Similarly,the number of invited brahmins for the shraddh of the deities is concerned the number has been put to even number-two,four,etc. But,if the…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 9, "quote": "8.2.5 Number of Brahmins As far as the number of invited brahmins to the shraddh ceremony of the ancestors is concerned,the scriptures have strictly put it to odd numbers-one,three,five,etc.Similarly,the number of i"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-2-6"><data key="title">Types of Shraddh</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A person performing the shraddh rituals should have his seat made of 'kusha' grass.After taking his seat,he should invoke the deities and make offerings of 'ardhya' to them.While offering 'ardhya' to the deities water and barley are commonly used alongwith other articles like sandalwood,incense,etc.During the entire…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 10, "quote": "8.2.6 Types of Shraddh A person performing the shraddh rituals should have his seat made of 'kusha' grass.After taking his seat,he should invoke the deities and make offerings of 'ardhya' to them.While offering 'a"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-2-7"><data key="title">Shraddh Mantra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">It is customary to scatter sesame seeds near the place where brahmins are having food in the shraddh ceremony.The performer of the shraddh rituals should imagine that the food partaken by them are actually satiating the ancestors.The chanting of 'Rakshodhna mantra' is a must.</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 10, "quote": "8.2.7 Shraddh Mantra It is customary to scatter sesame seeds near the place where brahmins are having food in the shraddh ceremony.The performer of the shraddh rituals should imagine that the food partaken by th"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-2-8"><data key="title">The Rituals of Pinda Daan</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">One important characteristic of the rituals connected with pinda daan is the offerings made to manes in the form of sesame seeds and water.Another important aspect is that 'pinda' is offered on the 'kusha' grass which are kept facing south-first in the name of one's dead father and then in the name of one's…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 10, "quote": "8.2.8 The Rituals of Pinda Daan One important characteristic of the rituals connected with pinda daan is the offerings made to manes in the form of sesame seeds and water.Another important aspect is that 'pinda' is offered"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-2-9"><data key="title">Balivaishva Deva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The performance of Balivaishva deva rituals mark the end of shraddh ceremony after which the person should sit down along with all the invited guests to have his food.Ancestors become satisfied if shraddh is performed in their names and all the desires of such a man are fulfilled.Three things have great importance in…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 10, "quote": "8.2.9 Balivaishva Deva The performance of Balivaishva deva rituals mark the end of shraddh ceremony after which the person should sit down along with all the invited guests to have his food.Ancestors become satisf"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-3"><data key="title">The Manifestation of Various Deities</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once,while he was on his hunting spree,he entered into a dense forest.He was delighted to see a dear who started running for it's life after hearing the noise. Prajapal let his horse loose in the direction of the running dear.Sage Mahatapa had his hermitage in that forest.While chasing that dear,Prajapal arrived at…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 10, "quote": "8.3 The Manifestation of Various Deities This section contains 16 sections as follows: During tretayuga,there lived a king named Prajapal.Once,while he was on his hunting spree,he entered into a dense forest.He was delighted to see a dear w"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-3-1"><data key="title">Agni</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">"When Agni manifested himself from Brahma's anger,he requested him(Brahma) to assign an auspicious day to him for his worship.Lord Brahma said-'Since you have manifested first of all the deities,therefore the first day(pratipada) will be reserved for your worship.One who performs havan on pratipada would be blessed."</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 10, "quote": "8.3.1 Agni \"When Agni manifested himself from Brahma's anger,he requested him(Brahma) to assign an auspicious day to him for his worship.Lord Brahma said-'Since you have manifested first of all the dei"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-3-2"><data key="title">Ashwini Kumars</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">King Prajapal requested Mahatapa to describe how both the Ashwini kumars manifested themselves.Sage Mahatapa narrated the following tale-'Marichi was Lord Brahma's son while Kashyap was Marichi's son.All the twelve Adityas are the sons of sage Kashyap.One of the Adityas-Martand,was marrird to Vishwakarma's daughter…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 11, "quote": "8.3.2 Ashwini Kumars King Prajapal requested Mahatapa to describe how both the Ashwini kumars manifested themselves.Sage Mahatapa narrated the following tale-'Marichi was Lord Brahma's son while Kashyap was Mari"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-3-3"><data key="title">Goddess Parvati</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">King Prajapal asked Mahatapa about the reason why goddess Gauri-the epithet of power,had to take incaranation.Sage Mahatapa replied -"Goddess Gauri manifested from lord Narayan and was offered to Rudra as his consort by lord Brahma.Lord Brahma then instructed Brahma to begin creation but Rudra being free from all…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 11, "quote": "8.3.3 Goddess Parvati King Prajapal asked Mahatapa about the reason why goddess Gauri-the epithet of power,had to take incaranation.Sage Mahatapa replied -\"Goddess Gauri manifested from lord Narayan and was offer"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-3-4"><data key="title">Lord Ganapati</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">King Prajapal then asked sage Mahatapa about the manifestation of lord Ganapati and about the significance of chaturthi in terms of Ganapati's worship.Mahatapa narrated the following tale- "Once,all the deities went to seek Shiva's help in sorting out a peculiar problem they were faced with- while they experienced all…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 12, "quote": "8.3.4 Lord Ganapati King Prajapal then asked sage Mahatapa about the manifestation of lord Ganapati and about the significance of chaturthi in terms of Ganapati's worship.Mahatapa narrated the following tale- \""}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-3-5"><data key="title">Nag Devta</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">King Prajapal then requested Mahatapa to shed light on the origin of the serpents and the significance of 'panchami'.Sage Mahatapa replied-The serpents came from the lineage of Kadru-Kashyap's wife.Sage Kashyap had numerous progeny from Kadru-Vasuki,Anant,Kambal,Karkotak,Padma,Mahapadma,Shankh,Kulik,etc. When all…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 12, "quote": "8.3.5 Nag Devta King Prajapal then requested Mahatapa to shed light on the origin of the serpents and the significance of 'panchami'.Sage Mahatapa replied-The serpents came from the lineage of Kadru-Kashyap"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-3-6"><data key="title">Kartikeya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing Kartikeya's manifestation and how his worship got associated with the auspicious day of shashthi,sage Mahatapa told king Prajapal-Once,a fierce battle was between the deities and demons in which the deities got defeated.The reason behind their defeat was the absence was the absence of an able commander who…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 12, "quote": "8.3.6 Kartikeya Describing Kartikeya's manifestation and how his worship got associated with the auspicious day of shashthi,sage Mahatapa told king Prajapal-Once,a fierce battle was between the deities and"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-3-7"><data key="title">Lord Aditya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">King Prajapal asked Mahatapa-"The thought of divine radiance taking the form of human radiance bewilders me.Please clear my doubts." Sage Mahatapa replied -"When the almighty God willed to manifest himself in other tangible forms,an effulgence emanated from his being resulting into the creation of 'Surya'(The Sun).The…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 13, "quote": "8.3.7 Lord Aditya King Prajapal asked Mahatapa-\"The thought of divine radiance taking the form of human radiance bewilders me.Please clear my doubts.\" Sage Mahatapa replied -\"When the almighty God willed to m"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-3-8"><data key="title">Ashta Matrikas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Continuing with his narration,sage Mahatapa said -"During ancient times,there lived a mighty demon named 'Andhakasur'.Andhakasur had become arrogant because of a boon of immortality received from lord Brahma. Being tormented by Andhakasur,all the deities went to seek lord Brahma's help.Lord Brahma took the deities to…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 13, "quote": "8.3.8 Ashta Matrikas Continuing with his narration,sage Mahatapa said -\"During ancient times,there lived a mighty demon named 'Andhakasur'.Andhakasur had become arrogant because of a boon of immortality received"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-3-9"><data key="title">Goddess Durga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">King Prajapal requested sage Mahatapa to narrate about the manifestation of goddess Durga and the significance of her worship on navami.Sage Mahatapa replied-The mighty demon-Vetrasur had driven out deities from the heaven.All the deities went to lord Brahma and sought his help.As lord Brahma was wondering about his…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 13, "quote": "8.3.9 Goddess Durga King Prajapal requested sage Mahatapa to narrate about the manifestation of goddess Durga and the significance of her worship on navami.Sage Mahatapa replied-The mighty demon-Vetrasur had dr"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-3-10"><data key="title">Dash Dishas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Having finished the tale of goddess Durga,sage Mahatapa revealed how all the six directions came into existence-"Six divine women manifested themselves from both the ears of lord Brahma during the initial phase of creation.The name of these six divine women were…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 13, "quote": "8.3.10 Dash Dishas Having finished the tale of goddess Durga,sage Mahatapa revealed how all the six directions came into existence-\"Six divine women manifested themselves from both the ears of lord Brahma duri"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-3-11"><data key="title">Kuber</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Continuing with the tales of the manifestation of various deities,sage Mahatapa said-"In the process of doing his creation,lord Brahma bellowed as a result of which Kubera manifested himself.Lord Brahma assigned kubera the lordship of all the wealth and prosperity.It was 'ekadashi' when Kubera had manifested…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 14, "quote": "8.3.11 Kuber Continuing with the tales of the manifestation of various deities,sage Mahatapa said-\"In the process of doing his creation,lord Brahma bellowed as a result of which Kubera manifested himself"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-3-12"><data key="title">Lord Vishnu</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing how lord Vishnu manifested,sage Mahatapa told Prajapal -"Narayan -the almighty God incarnated as lord Vishnu to nurture all the living creatures inhabiting the world.Eventually,lord Vishnu entrusted the job of world's welfare to goddess Yoganidra and himself went into his meditative sleep.While he was still…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 14, "quote": "8.3.12 Lord Vishnu Describing how lord Vishnu manifested,sage Mahatapa told Prajapal -\"Narayan -the almighty God incarnated as lord Vishnu to nurture all the living creatures inhabiting the world.Eventually,lo"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-3-13"><data key="title">Dharma</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sage Mahatapa then told king Mahapal about the manifestation of Dharma and the significance of trayodashi-'Dharma manifested himself from the right part of almighty God and appeared like a bullock. The almighty God instructed him to protect the mankind from all evils and sins.All the four feet of Dharma signified four…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 14, "quote": "8.3.13 Dharma Sage Mahatapa then told king Mahapal about the manifestation of Dharma and the significance of trayodashi-'Dharma manifested himself from the right part of almighty God and appeared like a b"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-3-14"><data key="title">Lord Rudra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sage Mahatapa then went on to describe the incident that led to the manifestation of lord Rudra and the significance of chaturdashi-'In the beginning of creation,when lord Brahma failed in his repeated attempts to create,he became frustrated.Frustration turned into fury and from his fury manifested a divine entity who…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 14, "quote": "8.3.14 Lord Rudra Sage Mahatapa then went on to describe the incident that led to the manifestation of lord Rudra and the significance of chaturdashi-'In the beginning of creation,when lord Brahma failed in h"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-3-15"><data key="title">Tanmatras</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing the importance of amavasya,sage Mahatapa told Prajapal-"Once,while lord Brahma was just about to commence the process of creation,suddenly 'tanmatras'(subtle forms of matter) revealed themselves from his mind.All of them being extremely virtuous were eager to go up in the sky to do penance byt lord Brahma…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 15, "quote": "8.3.15 Tanmatras Describing the importance of amavasya,sage Mahatapa told Prajapal-\"Once,while lord Brahma was just about to commence the process of creation,suddenly 'tanmatras'(subtle forms of matter) reve"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-3-16"><data key="title">Chandra-Ma</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing the importance of purnima sage Mahatapa said-"Daksha Prajapati had cursed Chandrama,his son-in-law for maltreating his daughters.As the fallout of that curse Chandrama started waning day by day and eventually became invisible on amavasya.All the deities became worried and went to seek lord Vishnu's…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 15, "quote": "8.3.16 Chandra-Ma Describing the importance of purnima sage Mahatapa said-\"Daksha Prajapati had cursed Chandrama,his son-in-law for maltreating his daughters.As the fallout of that curse Chandrama started wan"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-4"><data key="title">Austerities Related with Lord Vishnu’s Incarnations</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">This section contains 10 sections as follows:</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 15, "quote": "8.4 Austerities Related with Lord Vishnu’s Incarnations This section contains 10 sections as follows:"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-4-1"><data key="title">Matsya-Dvadashi Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Matsya-dwadashi is celebrated on the twelfth day of hindu month-Margashirsh.On this auspicious day a devotee should worship lord Matsya -the incarnation of lord Vishnu with appropriate rituals and give charity to brahmins.Prithvi was the first one to observe this particular austerity while she was being abducted to…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 15, "quote": "8.4.1 Matsya-Dvadashi Vrata Matsya-dwadashi is celebrated on the twelfth day of hindu month-Margashirsh.On this auspicious day a devotee should worship lord Matsya -the incarnation of lord Vishnu with appropriate ritua"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-4-2"><data key="title">Kurma-Dvadashi Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This particular austerity is dedicated to the worship of lord Kurma-the incarnation of lord Vishnu.It is celebrated on the twelfth day of the bright half of the hindu month 'pausha'.The austerity which commences on the tenth day itself when a devotee is supposed to purify himself by taking a holy bath.On the following…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 15, "quote": "8.4.2 Kurma-Dvadashi Vrata This particular austerity is dedicated to the worship of lord Kurma-the incarnation of lord Vishnu.It is celebrated on the twelfth day of the bright half of the hindu month 'pausha'.The aust"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-4-3"><data key="title">Varah-Dvadashi Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Varah dwadashi is celebrated on the twelfth day of the bright half of the hindu month-magh.This was the day on which Prithvi had been rescued by lord Varah-the incarnation of lord Vishnu.An idol of lord Varah is installed on a pot filled with water and worshipped with appropriate rituals amidst the continuous chanting…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 15, "quote": "8.4.3 Varah-Dvadashi Vrata Varah dwadashi is celebrated on the twelfth day of the bright half of the hindu month-magh.This was the day on which Prithvi had been rescued by lord Varah-the incarnation of lord Vishnu.An"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-4-4"><data key="title">NriSimha-Dvadashi Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This austerity is observed on the twelfth day of the bright half of the hindu month-phalgun.A devotee should observe fast on this day and worship lord Nrisimha with appropriate rituals.This particular austerity is capable of absolving a man from all his sins.</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 16, "quote": "8.4.4 NriSimha-Dvadashi Vrata This austerity is observed on the twelfth day of the bright half of the hindu month-phalgun.A devotee should observe fast on this day and worship lord Nrisimha with appropriate rituals.This"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-4-5"><data key="title">Vaman-Dvadashi Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Vaman dwadashi is celebrated on the twelfth day of the bright half of the hindu month-chaitra.A devotee should observe a fast and worship lord Vaman by saying the following prayer-O Lord!Vaman- the dwarfish incarnation of the almighty Vishnu,who took incarnation on the auspicious day of dwadashi in the month of…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 16, "quote": "8.4.5 Vaman-Dvadashi Vrata Vaman dwadashi is celebrated on the twelfth day of the bright half of the hindu month-chaitra.A devotee should observe a fast and worship lord Vaman by saying the following prayer-O Lord!Vam"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-4-6"><data key="title">Jamadagnya-Dvadashi Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This austerity is related with the worship of lord Parshuram who is also famous as Jamdagnya.It is cele- brated on the twelfth day of the bright half of the hindu month-vaishakh.Before commencing the rituals, a devotee should purify himself by taking a bath and then worship an idol of lord Parshuram kept on a pot…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 16, "quote": "8.4.6 Jamadagnya-Dvadashi Vrata This austerity is related with the worship of lord Parshuram who is also famous as Jamdagnya.It is cele- brated on the twelfth day of the bright half of the hindu month-vaishakh.Before comme"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-4-7"><data key="title">Kalki-Dvadashi Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A devotee should commence this austerity by observing a fast on the eleventh day of the bright half of the hindu month-bhadrapad and break it on the following day-dwadashi.He should worship lord Kalki with appropriate rituals.As per the norms of the rituals,the idol of lord Kalki is placed on a pot which is filled…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 16, "quote": "8.4.7 Kalki-Dvadashi Vrata A devotee should commence this austerity by observing a fast on the eleventh day of the bright half of the hindu month-bhadrapad and break it on the following day-dwadashi.He should worship"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-4-8"><data key="title">Padmanabh-Dvadashi Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This austerity is related with the worship of lord Padmanabh-an epithet of lord Vishnu and is observed on the twelfth day of the bright half of the hindu month-ashwin.One important charecteristic of the ritual is that all the organs of lord Padmanabh is worshipped.The idol is placed on a pot filled with water and…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 16, "quote": "8.4.8 Padmanabh-Dvadashi Vrata This austerity is related with the worship of lord Padmanabh-an epithet of lord Vishnu and is observed on the twelfth day of the bright half of the hindu month-ashwin.One important charecter"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-4-9"><data key="title">Dharni-Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This particular austerity is observed in the hindu month of Kartik.The rituals commence by worshipping various organs of lord Narayan amidst the chanting of different mantras which are as follows- om sahastra shirse namah - head om purushay namah - arms om vishwa rupine namah - throat om gyanastray namah - weapons om…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 16, "quote": "8.4.9 Dharni-Vrata This particular austerity is observed in the hindu month of Kartik.The rituals commence by worshipping various organs of lord Narayan amidst the chanting of different mantras which are as fo"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-4-10"><data key="title">Buddha-Dvadashi Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Buddha is believed to be the incarnation of Lord Vishnu.The austerity begins on the eleventh day of the bright half of the hindu month-shravan.On this day lord Vishnu is worshipped with appropriate rituals and by making offerings of various articles like flowers,fruits,incense,etc.The following mantras should be…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 17, "quote": "8.4.10 Buddha-Dvadashi Vrata Lord Buddha is believed to be the incarnation of Lord Vishnu.The austerity begins on the eleventh day of the bright half of the hindu month-shravan.On this day lord Vishnu is worshipped with"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-5"><data key="title">Some Other Austerities</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This section contains 10 sections as follows:</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 17, "quote": "8.5 Some Other Austerities This section contains 10 sections as follows:"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-5-1"><data key="title">Shubh-Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This particular austerity on the first day of the hindu month-margashirsh.It is related with the worship of lord Vishnu and the rituals have some special characteristics-having one meal on the eleventh day of the month(ekadashi),donating barley to brahmins,worshipping the idol of goddess Prithvi on 'ekadashi', etc.The…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 17, "quote": "8.5.1 Shubh-Vrata This particular austerity on the first day of the hindu month-margashirsh.It is related with the worship of lord Vishnu and the rituals have some special characteristics-having one meal on t"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-5-2"><data key="title">Dhanya-Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This particular austerity is a form of 'nakta-vrata',which means observing fast for the whole day and breaking it in the night.The austerity commences on the first day of the bright half of the hindu month- aghan and lasts for a year.Rituals include worshipping Agnideva-an embodiment of lord Vishnu,chanting of certain…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 17, "quote": "8.5.2 Dhanya-Vrata This particular austerity is a form of 'nakta-vrata',which means observing fast for the whole day and breaking it in the night.The austerity commences on the first day of the bright half of"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-5-3"><data key="title">Kanti-Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This austerity is related with the worship of sri Krishna and Balaram.It commences on the second day of the bright half of the hindu month-kartik and lasts for a year whereby a devotee observes fast on the second day(bright half) of every month that follows.While worshipping Balarama,a devotee should chant-'om…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 18, "quote": "8.5.3 Kanti-Vrata This austerity is related with the worship of sri Krishna and Balaram.It commences on the second day of the bright half of the hindu month-kartik and lasts for a year whereby a devotee obser"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-5-4"><data key="title">Saubhagya-Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This austerity commences on the third day of the bright half of the hindu month-falgun and lasts for a year whereby a devotee observes fast on the third day(bright half) of every month that follows.According to the tradition a devotee can either worship 'Shiva-Parvati' or 'Sri Hari-Laxmi on this auspicious day. While…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 18, "quote": "8.5.4 Saubhagya-Vrata This austerity commences on the third day of the bright half of the hindu month-falgun and lasts for a year whereby a devotee observes fast on the third day(bright half) of every month that"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-5-5"><data key="title">Avighna-Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">As the name implies,this austerity clears all the obstacles faced by a man.It commences on the fourth day of the bright half of the hindu month-falgun and lasts for four months whereby a devotee observes fast on the fourth day(bright half) of every month that follows.One important characteristic of this austerity is…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 18, "quote": "8.5.5 Avighna-Vrata As the name implies,this austerity clears all the obstacles faced by a man.It commences on the fourth day of the bright half of the hindu month-falgun and lasts for four months whereby a dev"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-5-6"><data key="title">Shanti-Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This austerity commences on the fifth day of the bright half of the hindu month-kartik and lasts for a year whereby a devotee observes fast on the fifth day(bright half) of the every month that follows.It is related with the worship of lord Vishnu and Sheshnag.A devotee should abstain from hot and spicy food during…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 18, "quote": "8.5.6 Shanti-Vrata This austerity commences on the fifth day of the bright half of the hindu month-kartik and lasts for a year whereby a devotee observes fast on the fifth day(bright half) of the every month t"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-5-7"><data key="title">Kaam-Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This austerity is related with the worship of lord Kartikeya and is observed in the bright half of the hindu month-pausha.On the sixth day of this month,a devotee is expected to live on nothing else but fruits.Lord Kartikeya is also known by several other names- Shadanan,Senani,Kritikasut,Kumar,Skand,etc. The…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 18, "quote": "8.5.7 Kaam-Vrata This austerity is related with the worship of lord Kartikeya and is observed in the bright half of the hindu month-pausha.On the sixth day of this month,a devotee is expected to live on noth"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-5-8"><data key="title">Aarogya-Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This austerity is related with the worship of lord Surya Narayan who is also known by several other names like Aaditya,Bhaskar,Ravi,Bhanu,Diwakar,Prabhakar,etc.A devotee should observe a fast on the seventh day of any month and worship lord Suryanarayan.The fast should be broken on the next day- ashtami.A devotee can…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 19, "quote": "8.5.8 Aarogya-Vrata This austerity is related with the worship of lord Surya Narayan who is also known by several other names like Aaditya,Bhaskar,Ravi,Bhanu,Diwakar,Prabhakar,etc.A devotee should observe a fas"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-5-9"><data key="title">Putra-Prapti Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This austerity commences on the eighth day of the bright half of the hindu month-bhadrapad.The rituals consist of taking a 'sankalp' on the preceding day(saptami)and worshipping lord sri Hari on the next day with appropriate rituals.Offerings are made to the sacrificial fire,which usually consist of articles like…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 19, "quote": "8.5.9 Putra-Prapti Vrata This austerity commences on the eighth day of the bright half of the hindu month-bhadrapad.The rituals consist of taking a 'sankalp' on the preceding day(saptami)and worshipping lord sri Har"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-5-10"><data key="title">Shaurya and Sarva Bhaum Vrata</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shaurya and Sarva Bhaum vrata is related with the worship of goddess Durga and is observed on the nineth day of the bright half of the hindu month-ashvin.The rituals commence by taking a 'sankalp' as well as observing a fast on the preceding day(saptami).The fast is broken on the next day(ashtami) by having…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 19, "quote": "8.5.10 Shaurya and Sarva Bhaum Vrata Shaurya and Sarva Bhaum vrata is related with the worship of goddess Durga and is observed on the nineth day of the bright half of the hindu month-ashvin.The rituals commence by taking a 'sa"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-6"><data key="title">The Significance of Dhenu-Daan</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This section contains 5 sections as follows:</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 19, "quote": "8.6 The Significance of Dhenu-Daan This section contains 5 sections as follows:"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-6-1"><data key="title">Til Dhenu-Daan</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">As the name implies,an idol of a cow made from powdered sesame seeds is donated to a brahmin.The idol should weigh not less than sixteen 'prasthas'(one prastha=four kudav,ancient units of weight measurment) Similarly,a calf's idol made from powdered sesame seeds and weighing four 'prasthas'is donated to a…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 19, "quote": "8.6.1 Til Dhenu-Daan As the name implies,an idol of a cow made from powdered sesame seeds is donated to a brahmin.The idol should weigh not less than sixteen 'prasthas'(one prastha=four kudav,ancient units of we"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-6-2"><data key="title">Jal Dhenu-Daan</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The method of donating 'Jal Dhenu' is as follows-first of all small area of land is purified by coating it with cow dung and place two small pots are placed at the centre.These pots are filled with different things like water,camphor,sandalwood,etc.The devotee should then imagine goddess Jal Dhenu and her calf to be…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 19, "quote": "8.6.2 Jal Dhenu-Daan The method of donating 'Jal Dhenu' is as follows-first of all small area of land is purified by coating it with cow dung and place two small pots are placed at the centre.These pots are fill"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-6-3"><data key="title">Rasa Dhenu Daan</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">First of all,the area is purified by coating it with cow-dung after which a black deer-skin is spread on it alongwith 'kusha grass'.Two pots-one large and the other a small one are placed side by side.These pots are filled with sugarcane juice and symbolize 'Rasa-Dhenu' and the calf.Four sugarcanes symbolizing the…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 20, "quote": "8.6.3 Rasa Dhenu Daan First of all,the area is purified by coating it with cow-dung after which a black deer-skin is spread on it alongwith 'kusha grass'.Two pots-one large and the other a small one are placed si"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-6-4"><data key="title">Gud-Dhenu Daan</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">As it is amply clear by the name,in this ritual some jaggery is taken and given the shape of a cow and a calf.The land is purified by coating it with cow-dung.After that,a black deer-skin is spread on that purified area.Some kusha grass are then spread on the dear-skin and covered with a piece of cloth.Now,both the…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 20, "quote": "8.6.4 Gud-Dhenu Daan As it is amply clear by the name,in this ritual some jaggery is taken and given the shape of a cow and a calf.The land is purified by coating it with cow-dung.After that,a black deer-skin is"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-6-5"><data key="title">Sharkara-Dhenu Daan</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">In this ritual,idols of a cow and a calf are made from sugar.After being worshipped they should be donated to a brahmin.A devotee should live only on sugar for a day.Donating a Sharkara-Dhenu liberates a man from all his sins. Similarly,donating Madhu-Dhenu(made from honey),Kheer- Dhenu(made from rice and…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 20, "quote": "8.6.5 Sharkara-Dhenu Daan In this ritual,idols of a cow and a calf are made from sugar.After being worshipped they should be donated to a brahmin.A devotee should live only on sugar for a day.Donating a Sharkara-Dhen"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7"><data key="title">Sacred Places of Pilgrimage</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This section contains 18 sections as follows:</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 20, "quote": "8.7 Sacred Places of Pilgrimage This section contains 18 sections as follows:"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-1"><data key="title">Kokamukh Teertha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing the holiness of Kokamukh-teerth,lord Varah narrated a tale to Prithvi-"Once,a fisherman was trying to catch a large fish in a lake situated at Kokamukh.Suddenly,a hawk which was flying in the sky, descended down and after picking the fish in it's beak tried to fly away.But,as the fish was a large one, the…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 20, "quote": "8.7.1 Kokamukh Teertha Describing the holiness of Kokamukh-teerth,lord Varah narrated a tale to Prithvi-\"Once,a fisherman was trying to catch a large fish in a lake situated at Kokamukh.Suddenly,a hawk which was f"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-2"><data key="title">Mayapuri (Haridvara) Teertha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Narrating about the sanctity of Mayapuri,lord Varah told Prithvi- "There lived a brahmin named Somsharma at Mayapuri.He was a great devotee of mine(lord sri Hari)and had an impossible desire of witnessing my divine powers by his mortal eyes.I tried to make him understand about the futility of his desire but to no…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 21, "quote": "8.7.2 Mayapuri (Haridvara) Teertha Narrating about the sanctity of Mayapuri,lord Varah told Prithvi- \"There lived a brahmin named Somsharma at Mayapuri.He was a great devotee of mine(lord sri Hari)and had an impossible desire"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-3"><data key="title">Kubjaamrak (Hrishikesh) Teertha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing how Kubjaamrak teerth derived it's name,lord Varah told Prithvi-"In satyayuga,at the time when the earth was still submerged in water,I had killed two mighty demons named Madhu and Kaitabh.After killing both the demons I looked all around and found sage Raimya engrossed in his penance.Sage Raimya was a…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 21, "quote": "8.7.3 Kubjaamrak (Hrishikesh) Teertha Describing how Kubjaamrak teerth derived it's name,lord Varah told Prithvi-\"In satyayuga,at the time when the earth was still submerged in water,I had killed two mighty demons named Madhu an"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-4"><data key="title">Badrikashram</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Continuing with his tale,lord Varah told Prithvi-"There is a sacred place in the Himalayas which is very inaccessible.It is famously known as Badrikashram and holds an important place among all the holy places.One who makes a pilgrimage to that place gets all his wishes fulfilled.There is a reservoir at the mountain…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 22, "quote": "8.7.4 Badrikashram Continuing with his tale,lord Varah told Prithvi-\"There is a sacred place in the Himalayas which is very inaccessible.It is famously known as Badrikashram and holds an important place among"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-5"><data key="title">Mandar Teertha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Mandar teerth is named after a celestial tree called Mandar.This sacred place of pilgrimage is situated at the foothills of vindhya mountain.There is a large mandar tree overthere which has some unique qualities.This tree is visible only on auspicious days of dwadashi and chaturdashi and becomes invisible on the rest…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 22, "quote": "8.7.5 Mandar Teertha Mandar teerth is named after a celestial tree called Mandar.This sacred place of pilgrimage is situated at the foothills of vindhya mountain.There is a large mandar tree overthere which has"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-6"><data key="title">Someshwar Linga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Chandra deva had once performed an austere penance to get absolved from the curse of his Daksha Prajapati his father-in-law.During the course of his penance Chandra deva made a shiva linga and worshipped it.Finally lord Shiva appeared and agreed to dwell in that particular shiva linga as per the request of Chandra…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 22, "quote": "8.7.6 Someshwar Linga Chandra deva had once performed an austere penance to get absolved from the curse of his Daksha Prajapati his father-in-law.During the course of his penance Chandra deva made a shiva linga a"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-7"><data key="title">Shalgram Kshetra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Prithvi requested lord Varah to shed light on the famous penance which sage Shalankayan had performed.Lord Varah narrated the following tale-"Long long ago sage Shalankayan did an austere penance to please me(lord Vishnu).There was a 'sal' tree nearby and the sage was ignorant of the fact that I dwell in that sacred…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 22, "quote": "8.7.7 Shalgram Kshetra Prithvi requested lord Varah to shed light on the famous penance which sage Shalankayan had performed.Lord Varah narrated the following tale-\"Long long ago sage Shalankayan did an austere pe"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-8"><data key="title">Ruru Kshetra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Varah told Prithvi-"This holy place is named after Ruru-the daughter of Devadutt and an apsara named Pramlocha.Devadutt came from the lineage of sage Bhrigu.Once,Devadutt did an austere penance to please lord Shiva.His penance continued for ten thousand years.Indra became worried and sent a beautiful apsara named…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 22, "quote": "8.7.8 Ruru Kshetra Lord Varah told Prithvi-\"This holy place is named after Ruru-the daughter of Devadutt and an apsara named Pramlocha.Devadutt came from the lineage of sage Bhrigu.Once,Devadutt did an austere"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-9"><data key="title">Gonishkraman Teertha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva told Prithvi-"Gonishkraman teerth is situated in the himalayas.Sage Aurva had his hermitage at this sacred place which was incidentally burnt to ashes by lord Shiva.Aurva cursed Shiva as the result of which Shiva started wandering throughout the world in a restless manner." "Once,lord Shiva visited the…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 23, "quote": "8.7.9 Gonishkraman Teertha Lord Shiva told Prithvi-\"Gonishkraman teerth is situated in the himalayas.Sage Aurva had his hermitage at this sacred place which was incidentally burnt to ashes by lord Shiva.Aurva cursed S"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-10"><data key="title">Stootswami Teertha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Varah then went on to describe the greatness of Stootswami teerth and told Prithvi-"I shall incarnate as Krishna at this place in the era of dwapar.I shall take birth in the house of Vasudeva and Devaki.There is a pond situated at about 5 kosas from Stootswami teerth.Anybody who take holy dips in it's holy water…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 23, "quote": "8.7.10 Stootswami Teertha Lord Varah then went on to describe the greatness of Stootswami teerth and told Prithvi-\"I shall incarnate as Krishna at this place in the era of dwapar.I shall take birth in the house of Va"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-11"><data key="title">Dvaraka</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Varah told Prithvi-"There is a holy place called 'Panchsar' in Dwarka.Anybody who stays there for six days and takes holy dips in 'Panchasar' enjoys all the pleasures of life.Dwarka is situated at the sea- shore and the crocodiles living there never trouble people taking their bath.These crocodiles also have one…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 23, "quote": "8.7.11 Dvaraka Lord Varah told Prithvi-\"There is a holy place called 'Panchsar' in Dwarka.Anybody who stays there for six days and takes holy dips in 'Panchasar' enjoys all the pleasures of life.Dwarka is"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-12"><data key="title">Sanandoor Teertha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This holy place is situated between Malay mountain and the ocean. Nearby,there is another holy place called Sangaman where sri Ram while on his way to lanka had confronted the ocean.There are also many other sacred places in the vicinity like Shakrasar, Shurparak,etc.There are hermitages of sri Ram and Parshuram at…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 23, "quote": "8.7.12 Sanandoor Teertha This holy place is situated between Malay mountain and the ocean. Nearby,there is another holy place called Sangaman where sri Ram while on his way to lanka had confronted the ocean.There ar"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-13"><data key="title">Lohargal Teertha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Varaha told Prithvi-"There is a region inhabited by mlecchas,situated at the distance of some 30 yojans.This region is surrounded by Himalaya mountain in it's central as well as southern parts.Lohargal teerth is situated near this region and is well protected by nature which makes it very inaccessible.…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 23, "quote": "8.7.13 Lohargal Teertha Lord Varaha told Prithvi-\"There is a region inhabited by mlecchas,situated at the distance of some 30 yojans.This region is surrounded by Himalaya mountain in it's central as well as souther"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-14"><data key="title">Mathura</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing the majesty of Mathura,lord Varaha told Prithvi- "Mathura is very dear to me because this is the very place where I shall be taking incarnation as Krishna.Mathura is the most sacro-sanct place and no other holy place can be compared to it. There are many other holy places in and around Mathura- Vishranti,…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 24, "quote": "8.7.14 Mathura Describing the majesty of Mathura,lord Varaha told Prithvi- \"Mathura is very dear to me because this is the very place where I shall be taking incarnation as Krishna.Mathura is the most sacr"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-15"><data key="title">Chakra Teertha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">It is situated to the north of Mathura and lord Bhadreshwar has his dwelling place overthere.A person who makes a pilgrimage to Chakra teerth and observes a fast for a day becomes absolved of heinous of sins.</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 24, "quote": "8.7.15 Chakra Teertha It is situated to the north of Mathura and lord Bhadreshwar has his dwelling place overthere.A person who makes a pilgrimage to Chakra teerth and observes a fast for a day becomes absolved o"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-16"><data key="title">Kapil Teertha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing the origin of 'Kapil Varah'(an idol of lord Varah made by sage Kapil)lord Varah told Prithvi-"Sage Kapil was a great devotee of mine.He had made my idol and worshipped it regularly. Later on,Indra acquired it and started worshipping it.The demon king Ravan took it to lanka after defeating Indra in a…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 24, "quote": "8.7.16 Kapil Teertha Describing the origin of 'Kapil Varah'(an idol of lord Varah made by sage Kapil)lord Varah told Prithvi-\"Sage Kapil was a great devotee of mine.He had made my idol and worshipped it regularl"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-17"><data key="title">Govardhan Teertha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Varah,continuing with the description of various holy places told Prithvi about the magnificence of Govardhan parvat- "Govardhan parvat is situated near Mathura.It will have great religious importance because it will witness a great feat of mine in dwapar yuga.I shall lift this mountain to protect people from the…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 24, "quote": "8.7.17 Govardhan Teertha Lord Varah,continuing with the description of various holy places told Prithvi about the magnificence of Govardhan parvat- \"Govardhan parvat is situated near Mathura.It will have great relig"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-7-18"><data key="title">Gokarn Teertha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Varah told Prithvi about the greatness of Gokarna teerth- "Long long ago there lived a vaishya named Vasukarn at Mathura. Sushila,his wife was deeply religious but inspite of that she was issueless.One day,she had gone to take her bath in river Saraswati where she found many women taking their bath.The children…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 24, "quote": "8.7.18 Gokarn Teertha Lord Varah told Prithvi about the greatness of Gokarna teerth- \"Long long ago there lived a vaishya named Vasukarn at Mathura. Sushila,his wife was deeply religious but inspite of that she w"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-8"><data key="title">Installation of Idols</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This section contains 5 sections as follows:</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 26, "quote": "8.8 Installation of Idols This section contains 5 sections as follows:"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-8-1"><data key="title">Idol Made of Wood</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Prithvi requested lord Varah to describe the proper methods of installing various types of idols. Lord Varah replied that a devotee desirous of making a wooden idol must select the wood of 'Bassia latifolia' for his purpose.The wood should be purified and then given shape as per the appearance of the deity.The idol is…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 26, "quote": "8.8.1 Idol Made of Wood Prithvi requested lord Varah to describe the proper methods of installing various types of idols. Lord Varah replied that a devotee desirous of making a wooden idol must select the wood of '"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-8-2"><data key="title">Stone Idol</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">"A proficient sculptor should be chosen to carve out the deity's shape from a pure and spotless stone.First of all the sculptor should clearly draw the outline of deity's appearance on the stone with a marker and then carefully carve it out from the stone.The idol is then purified by sprinkling 'akshat' on the deity's…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 26, "quote": "8.8.2 Stone Idol \"A proficient sculptor should be chosen to carve out the deity's shape from a pure and spotless stone.First of all the sculptor should clearly draw the outline of deity's appearance on the s"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-8-3"><data key="title">Earthen and Copper Idols</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">"A devotee should carefully make a beautiful earthen idol without any sign of crack.The most auspicious time for the installation of an earthen idol is said to be during 'shravan nakshatra'.An invocation is made to the deity to dwell within the idol after which ablution of the idol is performed with the help of…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 26, "quote": "8.8.3 Earthen and Copper Idols \"A devotee should carefully make a beautiful earthen idol without any sign of crack.The most auspicious time for the installation of an earthen idol is said to be during 'shravan nakshatra'."}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-8-4"><data key="title">Bronze Idol</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">"The most auspicious time for the installation of bronze-idol is said to be during jyeshtha nakshatra.The idol should be installed facing north.At the time of installation the devotee must not forget to keep four small pots in front of the deity,each filled with articles like'panchgavya''water,sandalwood and honey.The…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 26, "quote": "8.8.4 Bronze Idol \"The most auspicious time for the installation of bronze-idol is said to be during jyeshtha nakshatra.The idol should be installed facing north.At the time of installation the devotee must n"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-8-5"><data key="title">Silver and Gold Idols</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">"The rituals for the installation of silver or gold idols are similar to that of copper or bronze idol. Prithvi requested lord Varah to reveal the name of deities whom a devotee can worship at home.Lord Vishnu replied-"A devotee can worship any deity at hom e but worship of two shiva lingas is prohibited.Similarly,a…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 27, "quote": "8.8.5 Silver and Gold Idols \"The rituals for the installation of silver or gold idols are similar to that of copper or bronze idol. Prithvi requested lord Varah to reveal the name of deities whom a devotee can worship"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-9"><data key="title">Rituals Pertaining to Last Rites</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This section contains 4 sections as follows:</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 27, "quote": "8.9 Rituals Pertaining to Last Rites This section contains 4 sections as follows:"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-9-1"><data key="title">Last Rites: Pitra Yagya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Prithvi requested lord Varaha to define the meaning of 'Pitra' yagya(rituals pertaining to last rite) and it's significance. Lord Varaha replied-"Sage Nimi was the first person to perform the the rituals of pitra- yagya after the death of his son Aatreya.Sage Aatreya is famous for his penance which he did for ten…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 27, "quote": "8.9.1 Last Rites: Pitra Yagya Prithvi requested lord Varaha to define the meaning of 'Pitra' yagya(rituals pertaining to last rite) and it's significance. Lord Varaha replied-\"Sage Nimi was the first person to perform th"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-9-2"><data key="title">Last Rites: Ashaucha and Pinda Kalp</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">'Ashaucha' means impurity,so it is the period during which the son of the deceased is considered to be impure.The period begins from the day on which the death has occured and continues for the following ten days.'Pinda-daan' means offering certain kind of food to the deceased in the belief that it would satiate his…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 28, "quote": "8.9.2 Last Rites: Ashaucha and Pinda Kalp 'Ashaucha' means impurity,so it is the period during which the son of the deceased is considered to be impure.The period begins from the day on which the death has occured and continues for"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-9-3"><data key="title">Last Rites: The Flawed Shraddh and its Prevention</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Prithvi requested Lord Varaha to describe about the mistakes committed during shraddh rituals and how they could be prevented-- Lord Varah replied---It is a grave sin to partake of a meal meant for the soul of the deceased person. Anybody who has committed this sin should atone for his sin by observing a fast for full…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 28, "quote": "8.9.3 Last Rites: The Flawed Shraddh and its Prevention Prithvi requested Lord Varaha to describe about the mistakes committed during shraddh rituals and how they could be prevented--"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-9-4"><data key="title">Last Rites: Preparation of ‘Madhupark’</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">While describing the method of preparing Madhupark, Lord Varaha told Prithvi--"Madhupark" manifested from the right half of my body at the time when the process of creation began. This is the reason why it is so pure. Anybody who makes offering of Madhupark in the course of my worship,attains to my abode. For the…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 29, "quote": "8.9.4 Last Rites: Preparation of ‘Madhupark’ While describing the method of preparing Madhupark, Lord Varaha told Prithvi--\"Madhupark\" manifested from the right half of my body at the time when the process of creation began. This is th"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-10"><data key="title">Description of Hell</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This section contains 4 sections as follows:</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 29, "quote": "8.10 Description of Hell This section contains 4 sections as follows:"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-10-1"><data key="title">Nachiketa’s Experience</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Nachiketa visits Yama’s realm after repeatedly asking his father to whom he will be given. This retelling concerns the afterlife and conduct; compare it with the Katha Upanishad without assuming the versions are identical.</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 29, "quote": "8.10.1 Nachiketa’s Experience Once, Janmejay asked Sage Vaishampayan about Yamrajpuri and it's location. He also wanted to know as to what should a man do so that he does not have to go there. Sage Vaishampayan narrated"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-10-2"><data key="title">Karma Vipak</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Karma Vipak means the fruits one reaps according to his or her Karma. While continuing with the description of various aspects of hell, Nachiketa told the assembled sages--At yamapuri, Chitragupta goes through the record of all the deeds, committed by a man when he was alive and brings into the notice of Yamaraj.…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 30, "quote": "8.10.2 Karma Vipak Karma Vipak means the fruits one reaps according to his or her Karma. While continuing with the description of various aspects of hell, Nachiketa told the assembled sages--At yamapuri, Chitr"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-10-3"><data key="title">Virtues of Chastity</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">First of all,the area is purified by coating it with cow-dung after which a black deer-skin is spread on it alongwith 'kusha grass'.Two pots-one large and the other a small one are placed side by side.These pots are filled with sugarcane juice and symbolize 'Rasa-Dhenu' and the calf.Four sugarcanes symbolizing the…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 30, "quote": "8.10.3 Virtues of Chastity First of all,the area is purified by coating it with cow-dung after which a black deer-skin is spread on it alongwith 'kusha grass'.Two pots-one large and the other a small one are placed si"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-10-4"><data key="title">Liberation from Sins</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Narad asked Yamaraj about the reason why joy was so elusive to human beings. He also wanted to know about the reasons for man's sorrows. Yamaraj replied---Man's destiny is defined by his own actions. He has to taste the fruits of his actions. He has to taste the fruits of his each and every actions. So when a man…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 30, "quote": "8.10.4 Liberation from Sins Narad asked Yamaraj about the reason why joy was so elusive to human beings. He also wanted to know about the reasons for man's sorrows. Yamaraj replied---Man's destiny is defined by his own"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-11"><data key="title">Miscellaneous</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This section contains 12 sections as follows:</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 31, "quote": "8.11 Miscellaneous This section contains 12 sections as follows:"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-11-1"><data key="title">Gokarneshwar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The famous battle of 'Tarakamaya' had ended. The deities after having defeated the demons had once again regained the control of heaven. Lord Brahma being satisfied with the postive outcome of the war was engrossed in his meditation when Sanat Kumar arrived and asked--"O Lord! I am anxious to know about the origin of…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 31, "quote": "8.11.1 Gokarneshwar The famous battle of 'Tarakamaya' had ended. The deities after having defeated the demons had once again regained the control of heaven. Lord Brahma being satisfied with the postive outcome"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-11-2"><data key="title">The Means of Vishnu Realization</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, Sage Agastya narrated the following tale to King Bhadrashva--- One day, Narad went to Lord Vishnu and asked about the means by which a devotee could realize him. Lord Vishnu replied--Those who worship me by chanting the mantras of either 'Purushsukta' or 'Vaidik Samhita' can certainly realize me. Those who…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 32, "quote": "8.11.2 The Means of Vishnu Realization Once, Sage Agastya narrated the following tale to King Bhadrashva--- One day, Narad went to Lord Vishnu and asked about the means by which a devotee could realize him. Lord Vishnu replied--T"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-11-3"><data key="title">Lord Vishnu – The Almighty God</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, Sage Agastya had organised a grand yagya in which he had invited all the deities and sages. Lord Vishnu, Lord Brahma and Lord Shiva also went there. Agastya was puzzled as to who was the most superior among all three of them. He asked Lord Shiva in this regard. Lord Shiva replied--One who is worshipped by…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 32, "quote": "8.11.3 Lord Vishnu – The Almighty God Once, Sage Agastya had organised a grand yagya in which he had invited all the deities and sages. Lord Vishnu, Lord Brahma and Lord Shiva also went there. Agastya was puzzled as to who was t"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-11-4"><data key="title">Sage Gautam curses the Brahmins</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">There were many sages present at the yagya organised by Agastya. Some of them asked Lord Shiva as to why he had created some scriptures which appeared so confusing to human beings. Lord Shiva revealed to them that he had done so to protect the brahmins from the curse of Sage Gautam. Then he narrated the incident as it…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 32, "quote": "8.11.4 Sage Gautam curses the Brahmins There were many sages present at the yagya organised by Agastya. Some of them asked Lord Shiva as to why he had created some scriptures which appeared so confusing to human beings. Lord Shiv"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-11-5"><data key="title">Prakriti and Purush</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, Lord Shiva while describing about the incident when he had a divine glimpse of Lord Narayan, told Sage Agastya -- 'Purusha' is none other than Lord Narayan himself and Prakriti is the medium through which he manifests his powers. I have been fortunate to have a divine glimpse of this Purush. Lord Brahma after…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 33, "quote": "8.11.5 Prakriti and Purush Once, Lord Shiva while describing about the incident when he had a divine glimpse of Lord Narayan, told Sage Agastya -- 'Purusha' is none other than Lord Narayan himself and Prakriti is the"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-11-6"><data key="title">Bhuvan-Kosh and the Islands</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">All the sages listened with rapt attention to the description Lord Shiva made of the Almighty Narayan. But, they had few more queries in store, so they asked Lord Shiva-- "O Lord of Uma! please tell us about the expanse of the Earth- the situation of mountains and the prominent islands." Lord Shiva replied--"Bhuloka…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 34, "quote": "8.11.6 Bhuvan-Kosh and the Islands All the sages listened with rapt attention to the description Lord Shiva made of the Almighty Narayan. But, they had few more queries in store, so they asked Lord Shiva-- \"O Lord of Uma! ple"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-11-7"><data key="title">Jambudveepa</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Dwelling upon the prominent islands, Lord Shiva told the assembled sages---"Jambudweepa is spread in the area of one lakh yojan and it consists of numerous Janapadads. It is inhabited by 'siddhas' and 'charanas' and is blessed with incomparable natural beauty." 'A total number of nine 'Varshas' lie within the…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 34, "quote": "8.11.7 Jambudveepa Dwelling upon the prominent islands, Lord Shiva told the assembled sages---\"Jambudweepa is spread in the area of one lakh yojan and it consists of numerous Janapadads. It is inhabited by 'si"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-11-8"><data key="title">Shak Dweepa</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">It's area is approximately twice as compared to Jambudweepa. It is inhabited by virtuous people who are prosperous and who enjoy a long life. They are free from the tortures of old age. This island is covered by oceans on its two sides--Salt water ocean on one side and 'Ksheer Sagar' (Ocean of Milk) on the other. The…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 35, "quote": "8.11.8 Shak Dweepa It's area is approximately twice as compared to Jambudweepa. It is inhabited by virtuous people who are prosperous and who enjoy a long life. They are free from the tortures of old age. This"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-11-9"><data key="title">Kusha Dweepa</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">This island is twice the area of Shak dweepa. There are seven prominent mountain ranges in this island which are known as 'Kula Parvatas'--- Kumud, Unnat, Drona, Kank, Isha, Mahish and Mandar. There are seven prominent rivers flowing through the various parts of this island--Pratoya, Shiva, Chitra, Chandra, Vidyullta,…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 35, "quote": "8.11.9 Kusha Dweepa This island is twice the area of Shak dweepa. There are seven prominent mountain ranges in this island which are known as 'Kula Parvatas'--- Kumud, Unnat, Drona, Kank, Isha, Mahish and Manda"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-11-10"><data key="title">Krauncha Dweepa</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">It is twice the area of Kusha dweepa. It has seven prominent mountain ranges--Kraunch, Paavan, Devavrit, Devishtha, Devaanand, Govind and Pundareek. There are also six prominent rivers flowing in this island--Gauri, Kumudwati, Ratri, Manojava, Khyati and Pundarika. Kraunch dweepa is surrounded by an ocean of charified…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 35, "quote": "8.11.10 Krauncha Dweepa It is twice the area of Kusha dweepa. It has seven prominent mountain ranges--Kraunch, Paavan, Devavrit, Devishtha, Devaanand, Govind and Pundareek. There are also six prominent rivers flowi"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-11-11"><data key="title">Shalmali Dweepa</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">"It is double the area of Kraunch dweepa and covers the 'clarified-butter' ocean from all sides. There are seven prominent mountains as well as seven rivers in this island." This way Lord Shiva disappeared after having finished the narration of all the prominent islands.</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 35, "quote": "8.11.11 Shalmali Dweepa \"It is double the area of Kraunch dweepa and covers the 'clarified-butter' ocean from all sides. There are seven prominent mountains as well as seven rivers in this island.\" This way Lord Sh"}]</data></node><node id="p-varaha-purana-8-11-12"><data key="title">Trishakti</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing the reason behind the manifestation of 'TriShakti' (Three goddesses manifesting from the bodies of Brahma Vishnu and Shiva), Lord Varah told Prithvi--- There lived a mighty demon named 'Andhak' who used to torment the deities. After being driven out of the heaven all the deities sought lord Brahma's help.…</data><data key="doc">varaha-purana</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 35, "quote": "8.11.12 Trishakti Describing the reason behind the manifestation of 'TriShakti' (Three goddesses manifesting from the bodies of Brahma Vishnu and Shiva), Lord Varah told Prithvi--- There lived a mighty demon"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1"><data key="title">Chapter 1 (Part 1)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">NAARAAYANAM NAMASKRUTAM NARAM CHAIV NAROTTAMAM | DEVEEM SARASWATEEM VYAASAM TATO JAYMUDEERYET ||</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 2, "quote": "3.1 Chapter 1 (Part 1) This chapter contains 16 sections. NAARAAYANAM NAMASKRUTAM NARAM CHAIV NAROTTAMAM | DEVEEM SARASWATEEM VYAASAM TATO JAYMUDEERYET || 3.1.1 Introduction and Origin of the Universe from Basic Elements Sage Parashar, the "}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1-1"><data key="title">Introduction and Origin of the Universe from Basic Elements</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sage Parashar, the exponent of Vishnu Puraan had narrated this grand treatise to Maitreya. Sage Suta inherited it from Maitreya. The text presented here is a narration by Suta. Suta says- One day, Maitreya greeted sage Parashar and said- "Gurudev, you have studied all the scriptures. I wish to hear the tale of…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 2, "quote": "3.1.1 Introduction and Origin of the Universe from Basic Elements Sage Parashar, the exponent of Vishnu Puraan had narrated this grand treatise to Maitreya. Sage Suta inherited it from Maitreya. The text presented here is a narration by Suta. Suta says- On"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1-2"><data key="title">Birth of Brahma and Salvation of Earth by Varaha</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">At the end of the previous Kalpa, when Brahma awakened from his long slumber, he saw all the worlds devoid of everything. With a desire to salve the earth from the depth of unfathomable inundation, He took the appearance of Varaha. Entering the water, He reached Paataaloka. Seeing Him, the earth prayed Him with…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 3, "quote": "3.1.2 Birth of Brahma and Salvation of Earth by Varaha At the end of the previous Kalpa, when Brahma awakened from his long slumber, he saw all the worlds devoid of everything. With a desire to salve the earth from the depth of unfathomable inun"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1-3"><data key="title">Divisions of Earth and Origin of Cereals</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">With the desire of creating the world, Brahma produced different kinds of human beings from his different organs. Thus, from his mouth, Brahmins appeared. Kshatriyas appeared from his chest, Vaishyas from his thighs and from his feet, Shudras appeared. Thus, physical body is the greatest means for human beings in…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 4, "quote": "3.1.3 Divisions of Earth and Origin of Cereals With the desire of creating the world, Brahma produced different kinds of human beings from his different organs. Thus, from his mouth, Brahmins appeared. Kshatriyas appeared from his chest,"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1-4"><data key="title">Birth of Prajapatis and their Progeny</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">At one point, the process of creation got saturated and no increase took place in the number of living beings. Then, with a desire of continuing the creation, Lord created nine sages from His thought. Then he produced nine daughters and handed them over as the wives to those nine sages. When initially produced sons of…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 4, "quote": "3.1.4 Birth of Prajapatis and their Progeny At one point, the process of creation got saturated and no increase took place in the number of living beings. Then, with a desire of continuing the creation, Lord created nine sages from Hi"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1-5"><data key="title">Creation of Rudra and Omnipresence of Laxmi</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">In the beginning of Kalpa when Brahma was thinking about a son, who would be like him in virtues, a boy with reddish-blue body appeared in his lap. Brahma addressed the boy with names like Rudra, Bhava, Shiv, Ibhaan, Pashupati, Bheema, Ugra and Mahadev. Brahma also fixed Surya, water, earth, air, fire, sky, Dikshit…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 5, "quote": "3.1.5 Creation of Rudra and Omnipresence of Laxmi In the beginning of Kalpa when Brahma was thinking about a son, who would be like him in virtues, a boy with reddish-blue body appeared in his lap. Brahma addressed the boy with names like R"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1-6"><data key="title">Dhruv’s Migration to the Forest</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Swayambhu Manu had two sons- Priyavrata and Utaanpaad. Utaanpaad had two wives- Suruchi and Suniti. Suruchi had a son- Uttam while Suniti had also a son- Dhruv. King Utaanpaad loved Suruchi more than he loved Suniti. One day the king was sitting on the throne. Prince Uttam was playing in his lap. Meanwhile Dhruv also…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 6, "quote": "3.1.6 Dhruv’s Migration to the Forest Swayambhu Manu had two sons- Priyavrata and Utaanpaad. Utaanpaad had two wives- Suruchi and Suniti. Suruchi had a son- Uttam while Suniti had also a son- Dhruv. King Utaanpaad loved Suruchi"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1-7"><data key="title">Dhruv Pleases God with his Penance</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The prince Dhruv greeted the sages and continued on his journey. At last, he reached a beautiful forest Madhuvan on the bank of the river Yamuna. It was the same forest, which was later occupied by a demon Madhu. Shatrughana, the youngest brother of Lord Rama had killed the demon Lavan, the son of Madhu in the same…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 7, "quote": "3.1.7 Dhruv Pleases God with his Penance The prince Dhruv greeted the sages and continued on his journey. At last, he reached a beautiful forest Madhuvan on the bank of the river Yamuna. It was the same forest, which was later occu"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1-8"><data key="title">Tale of King Ven and Prithu</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Parashar says- " With the help of Dhruv, his wife gave birth to two sons- Sishti and Bhavya. Sishti had five sons- Ripu, Ripunjay, Vipra, Vikal and Vrikteja. Ripu had a brilliant son Chaakshush. Chaakshush had a son Manu. Manu had sons- Puru, Kuru, Shatadyumna, Tapasvi, Satyavaan, Suchi, Agnishtome, Atiraatra,…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 8, "quote": "3.1.8 Tale of King Ven and Prithu Parashar says- \" With the help of Dhruv, his wife gave birth to two sons- Sishti and Bhavya. Sishti had five sons- Ripu, Ripunjay, Vipra, Vikal and Vrikteja. Ripu had a brilliant son Chaaksh"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1-9"><data key="title">Birth of Prachin Barhi Worship of God by Prachetas</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">King Prithu had two sons- Antardhaan and Vaadi who were very religious minded themselves. Antardhaan’s wife Shikhandi gave birth to Ivirdhaan. Prachin Barhi was the son of Ivirdhaan and his wife, Ghishna. Shukra, Gaya, Krishna, Vrij and Ajin were their other sons. Prachin Barhi was a great fosterer of his subjects.…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 10, "quote": "3.1.9 Birth of Prachin Barhi Worship of God by Prachetas King Prithu had two sons- Antardhaan and Vaadi who were very religious minded themselves. Antardhaan’s wife Shikhandi gave birth to Ivirdhaan. Prachin Barhi was the son of Ivirdhaan and his"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1-10"><data key="title">Marriage of Prachetas and Birth of Prajapatis</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Parashar says- During the time when Prachetas were engaged in penance, the earth was unprotected. Hence, huge trees came to cover her everywhere on land. Their foliage was so dense they that cut off entire sky and the sun could not reach the surface of the earth. After their emergence from the sea, Prachetas got very…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 10, "quote": "3.1.10 Marriage of Prachetas and Birth of Prajapatis Parashar says- During the time when Prachetas were engaged in penance, the earth was unprotected. Hence, huge trees came to cover her everywhere on land. Their foliage was so dense they that"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1-11"><data key="title">Tale of Prahlad and Narasimha Incarnation</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Parashar says- By the boon of Brahma, Hiranyakashipu had acquired tremendous power and came to control all the three worlds. He had driven the gods out of heaven and used to receive the oblations offered to Surya, Vayu, Agni, Varun, Chandrama, Kuber, Yamaraaj etc. Because of his fear, all those gods roamed on earth.…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 12, "quote": "3.1.11 Tale of Prahlad and Narasimha Incarnation Parashar says- By the boon of Brahma, Hiranyakashipu had acquired tremendous power and came to control all the three worlds. He had driven the gods out of heaven and used to receive the obla"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1-12"><data key="title">Attempts to Kill Prahlad</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When the demons saw that their all efforts failed to faze Prahlad and that he was preaching the demon children they informed Hiranyakashipu about all these happenings. Hiranyakashipu at once called his cook and ordered him to poison Prahlad through food. But the poisonous food also could not hurt Prahlad. In fact, its…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 14, "quote": "3.1.12 Attempts to Kill Prahlad When the demons saw that their all efforts failed to faze Prahlad and that he was preaching the demon children they informed Hiranyakashipu about all these happenings. Hiranyakashipu at once"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1-13"><data key="title">Description of Lord’s Virtues by Prahlad and Protection of Prahlad by Sudarshan</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chakra Hearing the news of Kritya’s failure to kill Prahlad, killing of teachers and their subsequent revival as effected by Prahlad, Hiranyakashipu called his son and asked if all those miracles were natural or he knew some magical tricks. Prahlad said- "O father, neither I know any magic nor is the miracle a part of…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 15, "quote": "3.1.13 Description of Lord’s Virtues by Prahlad and Protection of Prahlad by Sudarshan Chakra Hearing the news of Kritya’s failure to kill Prahlad, killing of teachers and their subsequent revival as effected by Prahlad, Hiranyakashipu called his son and asked if all those mir"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1-14"><data key="title">Appearance of the Lord</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">As soon as Prahlad began to pray the Lord, his Naagpaash broke and the mountains that the demons had thrown into the sea also lightened up causing great relief for Prahlad. Lord Vishnu appeared before him and said- "O Prahlad! I am pleased with your devotion. You may seek any boon of your wish." Prahlad said- "May I…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 15, "quote": "3.1.14 Appearance of the Lord As soon as Prahlad began to pray the Lord, his Naagpaash broke and the mountains that the demons had thrown into the sea also lightened up causing great relief for Prahlad. Lord Vishnu appea"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1-15"><data key="title">Lineage of Kashyap’s Other Wives and Origin of Marudgana</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After the death of Hiranyakashipu, Prahlad continued his lineage. Prahlad had a son, Virochan. Virochan in his turn had Bali as his son. The prowessive Bali was sent to the nether world by Vaaman incarnation of Lord. Bali had one hundred sons among whom Baanasur was the eldest. Kashyap’s second wife Danu had sons like…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 16, "quote": "3.1.15 Lineage of Kashyap’s Other Wives and Origin of Marudgana After the death of Hiranyakashipu, Prahlad continued his lineage. Prahlad had a son, Virochan. Virochan in his turn had Bali as his son. The prowessive Bali was sent to the nether world by V"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1-16"><data key="title">The Description of Universal Order</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When the great sages crowned Prithu as the king of the entire earth, Brahma had also divided the kingdoms. Thus, Brahma appointed Chandrama as the ruler of all the Nakshatras, planets, Brahmins, vegetation, Yagya, penance etc. He also appointed Kuber as the ruler of the kings, Varun as the ruler of the water bodies,…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 16, "quote": "3.1.16 The Description of Universal Order When the great sages crowned Prithu as the king of the entire earth, Brahma had also divided the kingdoms. Thus, Brahma appointed Chandrama as the ruler of all the Nakshatras, planets, Brahm"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-2"><data key="title">Chapter 2 (Part 2)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 17, "quote": "3.2 Chapter 2 (Part 2)"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-2-1"><data key="title">Description of Priyavrat’s Lineage</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Maitreya says- "O sage! Now I wish to hear the description of the lineage of Priyavrata who was the son of Swayambhu Manu." Parashar says- Priyavrata was married to the daughter of sage Kardam. They had two daughters and ten sons. Three of his sons- Medha, Agnibaahu and Putra knew everything about their previous…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 17, "quote": "3.2.1 Description of Priyavrat’s Lineage Maitreya says- \"O sage! Now I wish to hear the description of the lineage of Priyavrata who was the son of Swayambhu Manu.\" Parashar says- Priyavrata was married to the daughter of sage Kard"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-2-2"><data key="title">Geographical Description</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The earth has seven great islands- Jambu, Plaksha, Shaalmali, Kusha, Kronch, Shaakh and Pushkar. These are surrounded by oceans having saline water, sugarcane juice, wine, ghee, curd, milk and sweet water respectively. Jambudweep is situated at the centre. Situated at the centre of Jambudweep is golden Sumeru Mountain…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 17, "quote": "3.2.2 Geographical Description The earth has seven great islands- Jambu, Plaksha, Shaalmali, Kusha, Kronch, Shaakh and Pushkar. These are surrounded by oceans having saline water, sugarcane juice, wine, ghee, curd, milk a"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-2-3"><data key="title">Division of Bharat and Other Landmasses</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The landmass that is present between Himalayas and the ocean is known as Bharat. It has an expansion of 9,000 Yojans. It is populated by the descendants of Bharat. It has seven native mountains- Mahendra, Malay, Sahaya, Shuktimaan, Riksha, Vindhya and Paariyaatra. Bharatvarsha has nine divisions named Indradweep,…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 18, "quote": "3.2.3 Division of Bharat and Other Landmasses The landmass that is present between Himalayas and the ocean is known as Bharat. It has an expansion of 9,000 Yojans. It is populated by the descendants of Bharat. It has seven native mounta"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-2-4"><data key="title">Description of Other Dvipas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Plakshdweep- Plakshdweep has an expansion of 2,00,000 Yojans. Its ruler Meghatithi had seven sons- Shaanthaya, Shishir, Sukhodaya, Anand, Shiva, Kshemak and Dhruv. Those seven princes later on came to rule Plakshdweep. Hence, Plakshdweep had seven divisions named after these seven princes. Seven mountains mark the…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 19, "quote": "3.2.4 Description of Other Dvipas Plakshdweep- Plakshdweep has an expansion of 2,00,000 Yojans. Its ruler Meghatithi had seven sons- Shaanthaya, Shishir, Sukhodaya, Anand, Shiva, Kshemak and Dhruv. Those seven princes later"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-2-5"><data key="title">Description of the Nether Worlds</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Earth’s total height is 70,000 Yojans. In her depth, are situated seven nether worlds with a depth of 10,000 Yojans each respectively. Names of these seven nether worlds are Atal, Vital, Nital, Gamastimaan, Mahaatal, Sutal and Paatal. These nether worlds have beautiful palaces, and have lands which are white, black,…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 20, "quote": "3.2.5 Description of the Nether Worlds Earth’s total height is 70,000 Yojans. In her depth, are situated seven nether worlds with a depth of 10,000 Yojans each respectively. Names of these seven nether worlds are Atal, Vital, Nit"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-2-6"><data key="title">Description of Hells</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Beneath the earth and water are situated the hells into which, the sinners are thrown. Raurav, Sukar, Rodha, Taal, Vishsan, Mahajwaal, Taptakumbh, Lavan, Vilohit, Rudhirambh, Vaitarni, Krimish, Krimibhojan, Asipatravan, Krishna, Lalabhaksha, Daarun, Puyuvah, Paap, Vahnijwaal, Adhahshira, Sandansh, Kaalsutra, Tamas,…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 20, "quote": "3.2.6 Description of Hells Beneath the earth and water are situated the hells into which, the sinners are thrown. Raurav, Sukar, Rodha, Taal, Vishsan, Mahajwaal, Taptakumbh, Lavan, Vilohit, Rudhirambh, Vaitarni, Krimi"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-2-7"><data key="title">Description of Celestial Worlds</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Maitreya says- "O Brahmin! Kindly narrate now about the celestial worlds and the position of stars and planets there." Parashar says- At a height of 1,00,000 Yojans above the earth is the sphere of the Sun. 1,00,000 Yojans above it is the sphere of the Moon and 1,00,000 Yojans above it is the sphere of the stars. At a…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 21, "quote": "3.2.7 Description of Celestial Worlds Maitreya says- \"O Brahmin! Kindly narrate now about the celestial worlds and the position of stars and planets there.\" Parashar says- At a height of 1,00,000 Yojans above the earth is the sp"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-2-8"><data key="title">Order of the Sun, the Stars and other Constellations</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The Sun travels speedily across the sky like an arrow. He determines the night and the day. With the rising and setting of the Sun, one can have knowledge of the directions. Sun lights up all the places in the entire world except Brahmaloka. Sun’s rays that reach Brahmaloka return back rendered ineffective by the…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 21, "quote": "3.2.8 Order of the Sun, the Stars and other Constellations The Sun travels speedily across the sky like an arrow. He determines the night and the day. With the rising and setting of the Sun, one can have knowledge of the directions. Sun lights up al"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-2-9"><data key="title">Twelve Names of the Sun</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">In the month of Chaitra, the Sun rides his chariot as Dhaata. During that period, elf Kratusthala, sage Pulastya, Naag Vaasuki, Yaksha Rathmirith, demon Heti and the Gandharva Tumbaru are also present on the chariot as his assistants. In the month of Vaishaakh, the Sun rides his chariot as Aryamaa with sage Pulah,…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 22, "quote": "3.2.9 Twelve Names of the Sun In the month of Chaitra, the Sun rides his chariot as Dhaata. During that period, elf Kratusthala, sage Pulastya, Naag Vaasuki, Yaksha Rathmirith, demon Heti and the Gandharva Tumbaru are al"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-2-10"><data key="title">Description of Surya Shakti and Vaishnava Shakti</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Maitreya says- "Lord, if only the seven Ganas are entrusted with the job of causing cold and summer, drought and rain, what is the purpose then of the Sun’s existence? " Parashar says- It is true that Surya is one of the seven Ganas. Yet being the chief among them, it has special significance. Omnipotent para-power of…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 23, "quote": "3.2.10 Description of Surya Shakti and Vaishnava Shakti Maitreya says- \"Lord, if only the seven Ganas are entrusted with the job of causing cold and summer, drought and rain, what is the purpose then of the Sun’s existence? \" Parashar says- It is"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-2-11"><data key="title">Description of the Nine Planets</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The Moon rides a chariot with three wheels. This chariot is hauled by ten horses. During its entire journey, the Moon passes through all the twenty-seven Nakshatras. These horses haul the Moon’s chariot for a complete Kalpa. With his rays, the Sun nourishes the Moon, which loses its strength because of drinking of its…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 23, "quote": "3.2.11 Description of the Nine Planets The Moon rides a chariot with three wheels. This chariot is hauled by ten horses. During its entire journey, the Moon passes through all the twenty-seven Nakshatras. These horses haul the Mo"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-2-12"><data key="title">Life Sketch of Bharat</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Bharata’s life links kingship, attachment and rebirth; the following Jadabharata instruction turns this narrative into a teaching on the self.</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 24, "quote": "3.2.12 Life Sketch of Bharat Maitreya says- \"Lord! Now I wish to hear various tales from the life of King Bharat. He was born in a pious land and always contemplated on God. Even then why he failed to attain salvation?"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-2-13"><data key="title">Jadabharat Preaches the King</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The king said-"O Brahmin. I was going to see sage Kapil and have the preaching regarding excellent human virtues. But now, hearing your words, I feel that I need not go any further. You are an ocean of knowledge. Kindly preach me about human virtues." Jadabharat said- "O king! For those who worship the Lord with a…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 26, "quote": "3.2.13 Jadabharat Preaches the King The king said-\"O Brahmin. I was going to see sage Kapil and have the preaching regarding excellent human virtues. But now, hearing your words, I feel that I need not go any further. You are"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-2-14"><data key="title">Tale of Ribhu and Nidaagh</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Hearing Jadabharat’s words, the king fell in deep thoughts. Jadabharat then narrated the tale of Ribhu and Nidaagh. Once upon a time, in the beautiful town of Veernagar at the bank of the river Devika, there lived Nidaagh, the son of Pulastya. He was a disciple of Ribhu, the son of Brahma. Once, after a gap of one…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 26, "quote": "3.2.14 Tale of Ribhu and Nidaagh Hearing Jadabharat’s words, the king fell in deep thoughts. Jadabharat then narrated the tale of Ribhu and Nidaagh. Once upon a time, in the beautiful town of Veernagar at the bank of the ri"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-2-15"><data key="title">Ribhu Preaches Nidaagh</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Ribhu teaches Nidagha through ordinary distinctions and their limits. This offers a dialogue-centered route into the text’s spiritual teaching.</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 26, "quote": "3.2.15 Ribhu Preaches Nidaagh One thousand years after this incident, Ribhu once again decided to see his disciple Nidaagh. So he once again arrived in Veernagar. At that time, king of that town was leading a procession"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3"><data key="title">Chapter 3 (Part 3)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 27, "quote": "3.3 Chapter 3 (Part 3)"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3-1"><data key="title">Description of Manvataras</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Maitreya says- "O Gurudev! Now I wish to hear the description of all the Manvantaras and the Manus who ruled those Manvantaras." Parashar says- "First Manu was self-begotten. After him five more Manus occurred namely Swarochish, Uttam, Taamas, Raivat and Chaakshush. All these Manus had occurred during the previous…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 27, "quote": "3.3.1 Description of Manvataras Maitreya says- \"O Gurudev! Now I wish to hear the description of all the Manvantaras and the Manus who ruled those Manvantaras.\" Parashar says- \"First Manu was self-begotten. After him five"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3-2"><data key="title">Birth of Savarni Manu and Description of the Next Manvataras</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Maitreya says- "O sage, now kindly narrate about the Manvantaras that are to come in future." Parashar says- Vishwakarma’s daughter Sangya was the wife of Surya. They had three children, Manu, Yama and Yamani. Sangya once felt unable to bear the radiance of Surya. So, appointing Chhaya (her shadow) in the service of…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 28, "quote": "3.3.2 Birth of Savarni Manu and Description of the Next Manvataras Maitreya says- \"O sage, now kindly narrate about the Manvantaras that are to come in future.\" Parashar says- Vishwakarma’s daughter Sangya was the wife of Surya. They had three children, Man"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3-3"><data key="title">Description of Veda Vyasa and the Significance of BrahmaGyana</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Maitreya says- "O Lord! How did God divide Vedas in the form of Vedavyasa during different Yugas?" Parashar says- During each Dwaparyug, in every Kalpa, Lord Vishnu takes incarnation as Vedavyasa and effects the division of Vedas for the benefit of human beings. During every Dwaparyug of the present Vaivasvat…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 29, "quote": "3.3.3 Description of Veda Vyasa and the Significance of BrahmaGyana Maitreya says- \"O Lord! How did God divide Vedas in the form of Vedavyasa during different Yugas?\" Parashar says- During each Dwaparyug, in every Kalpa, Lord Vishnu takes incarnation as Veda"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3-4"><data key="title">Expansion of the Branches of the Rg Veda</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The supplied retelling traces branches of the Rig Veda. This is an explicit Purana-to-Veda textual connection rather than a shared-character link.</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 29, "quote": "3.3.4 Expansion of the Branches of the Rg Veda At the beginning of creation, Rigveda contained all the four Vedas with hundred thousand mantras. That Rigveda helped a lot in the propagation of ten kinds of Yagyas. Then during the twenty-"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3-5"><data key="title">Description of the Branches of the Yajur Veda</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A disciple of Vedavyasa named Vaishampayan created twenty-seven branches of Yajurveda and taught them to his disciples. He had a disciple Yagyavalkya who was the son of Brahmarat. At that time, all the sages together constituted a rule that whoever did not join their group within seven days on Mahameru would be held…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 29, "quote": "3.3.5 Description of the Branches of the Yajur Veda A disciple of Vedavyasa named Vaishampayan created twenty-seven branches of Yajurveda and taught them to his disciples. He had a disciple Yagyavalkya who was the son of Brahmarat. At that ti"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3-6"><data key="title">The Branches of the Sama Veda and the 18 Puranas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sage Jaimini, a disciple of Vedavyasa, effected branches of Samaveda. Sage Jaimini had a son, Sumantu. Sumantu in turn had a son, Sukarma. Both of them studied a branch of Samaveda each. Sukarma divided his branch of Samaveda into sub-branches and taught them to his two disciples- Kaushalya Hiranyanaabh and…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 30, "quote": "3.3.6 The Branches of the Sama Veda and the 18 Puranas Sage Jaimini, a disciple of Vedavyasa, effected branches of Samaveda. Sage Jaimini had a son, Sumantu. Sumantu in turn had a son, Sukarma. Both of them studied a branch of Samaveda each. Suk"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3-7"><data key="title">Yama Gita</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Maitreya says- "Lord! At the end of a life span, all the people come to be controlled by Yamaraaj. When they are bound to undergo scores of suffering in different kinds of hells. Kindly narrate about such action by which a human being gets free from the noose of Yamaraaj." Parashar says- Youngest among the Pandavas,…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 30, "quote": "3.3.7 Yama Gita Maitreya says- \"Lord! At the end of a life span, all the people come to be controlled by Yamaraaj. When they are bound to undergo scores of suffering in different kinds of hells. Kindly narr"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3-8"><data key="title">Duties of Four Classes of Society</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Maitreya says - "Lord! How should one worship Lord Vishnu? Kindly describe it to me. Parashar says- King Sagar had asked the same thing from sage Aurv. I am narrating what sage Aurv had preached him. Only those who fulfil their responsibilities and discharge their duties as per their class can worship Lord Vishnu.…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 31, "quote": "3.3.8 Duties of Four Classes of Society Maitreya says - \"Lord! How should one worship Lord Vishnu? Kindly describe it to me. Parashar says- King Sagar had asked the same thing from sage Aurv. I am narrating what sage Aurv had prea"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3-9"><data key="title">Description of Different Ashrams</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Since birth till death, human life passes through different stages. For the purpose of simplicity in following these stages, our sages have made provisions for four stages or Ashrams in life namely Brahmacharya, Grihastha, Vanprastha and Sanyaas. Upanayana Sanskara (thread ceremony) marks the beginning of education…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 31, "quote": "3.3.9 Description of Different Ashrams Since birth till death, human life passes through different stages. For the purpose of simplicity in following these stages, our sages have made provisions for four stages or Ashrams in life"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3-10"><data key="title">Customs and Consecrations</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">At the time of childbirth, it is the father’s responsibility to carry out certain customs and consecrations for the child. First of all, Jatakarma Sanskara is carried out by which the newly born baby is included in the family. During this custom, a Brahmin couple is fed. It is also necessary for the couple that they…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 32, "quote": "3.3.10 Customs and Consecrations At the time of childbirth, it is the father’s responsibility to carry out certain customs and consecrations for the child. First of all, Jatakarma Sanskara is carried out by which the newly"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3-11"><data key="title">Description of Righteousness</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A married man knows the importance of following a righteous discourse. Married life is like a penance in which a couple gives birth and raises next generation. A married man is expected to get up early in the morning and contemplate on religious things. It helps him to follow righteous discourse. He is expected to…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 32, "quote": "3.3.11 Description of Righteousness A married man knows the importance of following a righteous discourse. Married life is like a penance in which a couple gives birth and raises next generation. A married man is expected to g"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3-12"><data key="title">Sanctification after Birth and Death</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Life on earth is like an infinite ocean in which birth appears like a bubble and death marks the bursting of that bubble after which it exists no more. Atmosphere of a household is said to be defiled when a birth or death occurs in it. There are different sanctifying customs to be carried out on both these occasions.…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 33, "quote": "3.3.12 Sanctification after Birth and Death Life on earth is like an infinite ocean in which birth appears like a bubble and death marks the bursting of that bubble after which it exists no more. Atmosphere of a household is said to b"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3-13"><data key="title">Days for Monthly Oblations</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A Shraadh carried out with devotion pleases the entire world including human beings, Brahma, Indra, Rudra, Ashwini Kumars, Surya, Agni, Vasugana, Marudgana, Vishwadeva, Pitragana, birds, animals, reptiles, sages and of course the dead ancestors. Moonless and eighth day in the darker phase of every lunar month are…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 33, "quote": "3.3.13 Days for Monthly Oblations A Shraadh carried out with devotion pleases the entire world including human beings, Brahma, Indra, Rudra, Ashwini Kumars, Surya, Agni, Vasugana, Marudgana, Vishwadeva, Pitragana, birds, ani"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3-14"><data key="title">Method for Oblations</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">While making oblations to the dead ancestors, one should feed his near and dear ones as well as learned Brahmins who respect their parents. Intimacy with women is barred for those who have taken food in an oblation. Any guest who happens to arrive unexpectedly and uninvited should be treated well. Before feeding,…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 33, "quote": "3.3.14 Method for Oblations While making oblations to the dead ancestors, one should feed his near and dear ones as well as learned Brahmins who respect their parents. Intimacy with women is barred for those who have t"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3-15"><data key="title">Do’s and Don’t during Oblation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Oblation with flesh of fish, rabbit, mongoose, hog, deer, ram and cereals cause extreme satisfaction for the ancestors. But in modern Kaliyug, all types of flesh are forbidden in oblation rituals. Only cereals, milk, honey, etc. are allowed. Offering of oblation in Gaya (a place of pilgrimage in Bihar) is said to…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 34, "quote": "3.3.15 Do’s and Don’t during Oblation Oblation with flesh of fish, rabbit, mongoose, hog, deer, ram and cereals cause extreme satisfaction for the ancestors. But in modern Kaliyug, all types of flesh are forbidden in oblation ri"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3-16"><data key="title">The Battle of Gods and Demons</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Rig, Sama and Yajurveda are like the clothes of Hindu society. Those who give them up are in fact naked. This statement is endorsed by the following tale. Once upon a time, a battle occurred between the demons and the gods. It lasted for one hundred years at the end of which period, the demons defeated the gods. The…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 34, "quote": "3.3.16 The Battle of Gods and Demons Rig, Sama and Yajurveda are like the clothes of Hindu society. Those who give them up are in fact naked. This statement is endorsed by the following tale. Once upon a time, a battle occurred"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-3-17"><data key="title">Dialogue of Mayamoha with Demons</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Mayamoha was produced in a naked form. It reached the bank of Narmada and saw the demons observing penance there. The naked Mayamoha then approached them and said in a sweet voice- " O demons! What is the purpose of your penance?" The demons said- "We are observing penance to achieve the metaphysical world." Mayamoha…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 34, "quote": "3.3.17 Dialogue of Mayamoha with Demons Mayamoha was produced in a naked form. It reached the bank of Narmada and saw the demons observing penance there. The naked Mayamoha then approached them and said in a sweet voice- \" O demon"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-4"><data key="title">Chapter 4 (Part 4)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 35, "quote": "3.4 Chapter 4 (Part 4)"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-4-1"><data key="title">Description of Vaivasvat Manu’s Lineage</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lineage of Vaivasvat Manu finds its origin at Brahma. Brahma was the first to appear from Lord Vishnu who is the primitive cause for the whole creation. From the right hand thumb of Brahma, Daksha Prajapati appeared. Daksha produced Aditi and Aditi produced Vivasvaan. Manu was the son of Vivasvaan. Ikshvaaku, Nrig,…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 35, "quote": "3.4.1 Description of Vaivasvat Manu’s Lineage Lineage of Vaivasvat Manu finds its origin at Brahma. Brahma was the first to appear from Lord Vishnu who is the primitive cause for the whole creation. From the right hand thumb of Brahma,"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-4-2"><data key="title">Description of Ikshvaku’s Lineage</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Ikshvaaku was born from the nostril of Manu as a result of sudden sneezing. Ikshvaaku had hundred sons among whom Vikukshi, Nimi and Danda were prominent. Once Ikshvaaku organised a Shraadh. For the purpose of feeding Brahmins, he ordered his son Vikukshi to bring fresh meat of wild animals. Taking his bow and arrows,…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">36</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 36, "quote": "3.4.2 Description of Ikshvaku’s Lineage Ikshvaaku was born from the nostril of Manu as a result of sudden sneezing. Ikshvaaku had hundred sons among whom Vikukshi, Nimi and Danda were prominent. Once Ikshvaaku organised a Shraadh."}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-4-3"><data key="title">Progeny of Mandhatr, Ascend of Trishanku to Heaven and Birth of Sagar</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once upon a time Gandharvas of collective name Mauneya defeated the Nagas and snatched all their wealth and powers. The Nagas prayed Lord Vishnu to rescue them. Lord Vishnu told them that he would appear in Mandhaata’s son Purukutsa and kill the Gandharvas. Afterwards Narmada brought Purukutsa to Rasaatal where Lord…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">38</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 38, "quote": "3.4.3 Progeny of Mandhatr, Ascend of Trishanku to Heaven and Birth of Sagar Once upon a time Gandharvas of collective name Mauneya defeated the Nagas and snatched all their wealth and powers. The Nagas prayed Lord Vishnu to rescue them. Lord Vishnu told them that he"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-4-4"><data key="title">Description of Suryavansh</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">King Sagar had two queens, Sumati, the daughter of Kashyap and Keshini, the daughter of Vidarbha’s king. Keshini had a son Asmanjas whereas Sumati had sixty thousand sons. Anshuman was the son of Asmanjas. Asmanjas was very whimsical since his childhood. Even in his youth, he did not change his behaviour. Hence King…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-1</data><data key="page">39</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 39, "quote": "3.4.4 Description of Suryavansh King Sagar had two queens, Sumati, the daughter of Kashyap and Keshini, the daughter of Vidarbha’s king. Keshini had a son Asmanjas whereas Sumati had sixty thousand sons. Anshuman was the s"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-4-4"><data key="title">Description of Suryavansh</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">King Sagar had two queens, Sumati, the daughter of Kashyap and Keshini, the daughter of Vidarbha’s king. Keshini had a son Asmanjas whereas Sumati had sixty thousand sons. Anshuman was the son of Asmanjas. Asmanjas was very whimsical since his childhood. Even in his youth, he did not change his behaviour. Hence King…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 1, "quote": "3.4.4 Description of Suryavansh King Sagar had two queens, Sumati, the daughter of Kashyap and Keshini, the daughter of Vidarbha’s king. Keshini had a son Asmanjas whereas Sumati had sixty thousand sons. Anshuman was the s"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-4-5"><data key="title">Tale of Nimi and Description of his Lineage</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Ikshvaku’s son Nimi once decided to organise a Yagya that was to last for one thousand years. He also wanted the sage Vashishta to guide the Yagya. But Indra had already booked Vashishta for a period of five hundred years. So sage Vashishta expressed his inability to conduct any other Yagya till the completion of that…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 2, "quote": "3.4.5 Tale of Nimi and Description of his Lineage Ikshvaku’s son Nimi once decided to organise a Yagya that was to last for one thousand years. He also wanted the sage Vashishta to guide the Yagya. But Indra had already booked Vashishta for"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-4-6"><data key="title">Description of Somavansh and the Tale of Pururava</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The creator of the world, Brahma had a son named Atri. Chandrama was the son of Atri. Once Chandrama organised a Rajsuya Yagya and received great powers. Afterwards blinded by his powers, Chandrama kidnapped Tara, the wife of Brihaspati. Even his grandfather Brahma tried to dissuade him from committing such a grave…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 3, "quote": "3.4.6 Description of Somavansh and the Tale of Pururava The creator of the world, Brahma had a son named Atri. Chandrama was the son of Atri. Once Chandrama organised a Rajsuya Yagya and received great powers. Afterwards blinded by his powers, Ch"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-4-7"><data key="title">Birth of Jamadagni and Vishwamitra</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Pururava had six sons- Aayu, Amavasu, Vishwavasu, Shrutaayu, Shataayu and Ayutayu. Amavasu had a son Bheema and Bheema in turn had a son Kanchan. Jahnu was the son of Kanchan’s son, Suhotra. At the time of Ganga’s descend on earth, when Jahnu saw his hermitage inundated, he drank all her water. He released the Ganges…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 4, "quote": "3.4.7 Birth of Jamadagni and Vishwamitra Pururava had six sons- Aayu, Amavasu, Vishwavasu, Shrutaayu, Shataayu and Ayutayu. Amavasu had a son Bheema and Bheema in turn had a son Kanchan. Jahnu was the son of Kanchan’s son, Suhotra."}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-4-8"><data key="title">Description of Kashyavansh</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Pururava’s son Aayu had been married to Rahu’s daughter. They had five sons- Nahush, Kshatravriddh, Rambh, Vraji and Aneyna. Suhotra was the son of Kshatravriddh. He had three sons- Kaashya, Kaash and Gritsamad. Shaunak was the son of Gritsamad whereas Kaasheya was the son of Kaashya. Lineage of Kaasheya grew as-…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 5, "quote": "3.4.8 Description of Kashyavansh Pururava’s son Aayu had been married to Rahu’s daughter. They had five sons- Nahush, Kshatravriddh, Rambh, Vraji and Aneyna. Suhotra was the son of Kshatravriddh. He had three sons- Kaashya,"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-4-9"><data key="title">Description of King Raji and his Sons</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">King Raji had five hundred prowessive sons. Once at the beginning of battle between the demons and the gods, they appeared before Brahma and enquired as to which part would win. Brahma said that only that part would win which would be aided by King Raji. First the demons approached Raji and requested him to fight from…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 5, "quote": "3.4.9 Description of King Raji and his Sons King Raji had five hundred prowessive sons. Once at the beginning of battle between the demons and the gods, they appeared before Brahma and enquired as to which part would win. Brahma said"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-4-10"><data key="title">Tale of Yayati</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Nahush had six sons- Yati, Yayati, Sanyati, Aayati, Viyati and Kriti. Yayati had two wives, Devyani, the daughter of Shukracharya and Sharmishtha, the daughter of Vrishparva. Devyani gave birth to Yadu and Turvasu whereas Sharmishtha gave birth to Druhayu, Anu and Puru. Because of a curse of Shukracharya old age had…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 5, "quote": "3.4.10 Tale of Yayati Nahush had six sons- Yati, Yayati, Sanyati, Aayati, Viyati and Kriti. Yayati had two wives, Devyani, the daughter of Shukracharya and Sharmishtha, the daughter of Vrishparva. Devyani gave bi"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-4-11"><data key="title">Description of Yaduvansh and the Tale of SahastrArjuna</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Yayati’s eldest son Yadu had four sons- Sahastrajit, Kroshtu, Nala and Nahush. Sahastrajit had a son Shatajit who had three sons Haihaya, Hehaya and Venuhaya. Lineage of Haihaya grew as- Dharma- Dharmanetra- Kunti- Sahajit- Mahishmaan- Bhadrashrenya- Durdabh and Dhanak. Dhanak had four sons- Kritveerya, Kritaagni,…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 6, "quote": "3.4.11 Description of Yaduvansh and the Tale of SahastrArjuna Yayati’s eldest son Yadu had four sons- Sahastrajit, Kroshtu, Nala and Nahush. Sahastrajit had a son Shatajit who had three sons Haihaya, Hehaya and Venuhaya. Lineage of Haihaya grew as- Dha"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-4-12"><data key="title">Description of Kroshtuvansh</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lineage of Kroshtu grew as follows- Kroshtu- Dhwajnivan- Swati- Rushanku- Chitrarath- Rashibindu. Rashibindu had one lakh queens and ten lakh sons among whom Prithusharva, Prithukarma, Prithukirti, Prithuyasha, Prithujay and Prithushan were prominent. In their lineage later on occurred a king named Jyamadh. He was so…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 6, "quote": "3.4.12 Description of Kroshtuvansh Lineage of Kroshtu grew as follows- Kroshtu- Dhwajnivan- Swati- Rushanku- Chitrarath- Rashibindu. Rashibindu had one lakh queens and ten lakh sons among whom Prithusharva, Prithukarma, Prith"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-5"><data key="title">Chapter 5 (Part 5)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 7, "quote": "3.5 Chapter 5 (Part 5)"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-5-1"><data key="title">Puruvansh</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The lineage of Puru grew as Puru- Janmejaya- Prachinvaan- Praveer- Manasyu- Abhayad- Sudayu- Bahugat- Sanyati- Ahamyaati- Raudraashva. Raudraashva had ten sons among whom Riteshu was the most prominent. Riteshu had three sons among whom Apratirath was most prominent. Lineage of Apratirath is as follows- Apratirath-…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 7, "quote": "3.5.1 Puruvansh The lineage of Puru grew as Puru- Janmejaya- Prachinvaan- Praveer- Manasyu- Abhayad- Sudayu- Bahugat- Sanyati- Ahamyaati- Raudraashva. Raudraashva had ten sons among whom Riteshu was the mos"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-5-2"><data key="title">Description of Kuruvansh</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">In the lineage of Kuru, there occurred a King Prateep. He had three sons- Devapi, Shantanu and Vaahlik. Devapi had taken an abode in the forest in his childhood. Hence Shantanu came to become a king after their father Prateep. Once in the kingdom of King Shantanu, there was no rainfall for twelve years at length.…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 7, "quote": "3.5.2 Description of Kuruvansh In the lineage of Kuru, there occurred a King Prateep. He had three sons- Devapi, Shantanu and Vaahlik. Devapi had taken an abode in the forest in his childhood. Hence Shantanu came to becom"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-5-3"><data key="title">Incarnation of Lord Krshna</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Vasudev, the son of Shoorsen was married to Devaki, the daughter of Devak. On that occasion of their marriage, Devaki’s cousin Kansa was driving their chariot. At that time, a celestial voice declared that the eighth son of Devaki would kill Kansa. Kansa in the excitement was ready to kill Devaki ten and there but…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 9, "quote": "3.5.3 Incarnation of Lord Krshna Vasudev, the son of Shoorsen was married to Devaki, the daughter of Devak. On that occasion of their marriage, Devaki’s cousin Kansa was driving their chariot. At that time, a celestial voic"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-5-4"><data key="title">Appearance of Lord in Devaki’s Womb</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">As per the dictate of Lord Vishnu, Yogamaaya implanted six foetuses in the womb of Devaki. All the six children were killed by Kansa immediately after their birth. Yogamaaya transplanted the seventh issue of Devaki into the womb of Rohini who was residing at that time in Gokul. Then Lord Himself appeared as the eighth…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 9, "quote": "3.5.4 Appearance of Lord in Devaki’s Womb As per the dictate of Lord Vishnu, Yogamaaya implanted six foetuses in the womb of Devaki. All the six children were killed by Kansa immediately after their birth. Yogamaaya transplanted the"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-5-5"><data key="title">Killing of Putana and Salvation of other Demons</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When Vasudev was released from the prison, Nand visited Mathura on some official trip. He also came to see Vasudev. Vasudev greeted him for the birth of a son and instructed him to return to Gokul as soon as possible. He also requested Nand to look after the son of Rohini as his own son. After meeting Vasudev when…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 10, "quote": "3.5.5 Killing of Putana and Salvation of other Demons When Vasudev was released from the prison, Nand visited Mathura on some official trip. He also came to see Vasudev. Vasudev greeted him for the birth of a son and instructed him to return to"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-10-3262"><data key="title">Killing Of Shaka</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When Lord Krishna was twenty-seven days old, a festival was organised in Gokul. Mata Yashoda bathed the baby while the Brahmins recited hymns. Yashoda saw that baby was feeling sleepy, so putting him in a cradle, she left it under a cart. The cart was loaded with pitchers of milk, curd and butter. After sometime, lord…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 10, "quote": "KILLING OF SHAKA When Lord Krishna was twenty-seven days old, a festival was organised in Gokul. Mata Yashoda bathed the baby while the Brahmins recited hymns. Yashoda saw that baby was feeling sleepy, so pu"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-11-297"><data key="title">Salvation Of Trinavart</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once Mata Yashoda was playing with the baby lord in her home. All of a sudden she felt as if the baby was getting disproportionately heavy. Soon, she felt herself unable to bear the child anymore. So she left the child on the ground and began to pray Purushottam Lord Rama. Soon afterwards she forgot about the event,…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 11, "quote": "SALVATION OF TRINAVART Once Mata Yashoda was playing with the baby lord in her home. All of a sudden she felt as if the baby was getting disproportionately heavy. Soon, she felt herself unable to bear the child an"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-11-1533"><data key="title">Naming Of Lord Krishna</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, the supreme ascetic Gargacharya arrived in Gokul. He was the ancestral teacher of Yaduvanshis. Nand joyfully welcomed and treated the hermit. Then he requested him to carry out the ritual of naming and suggest suitable named for both of his sons. Acharya Garg said, "O Nand, everyone knows that I am the ancestral…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 11, "quote": "NAMING OF LORD KRISHNA Once, the supreme ascetic Gargacharya arrived in Gokul. He was the ancestral teacher of Yaduvanshis. Nand joyfully welcomed and treated the hermit. Then he requested him to carry out the rit"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-11-3116"><data key="title">Childhood Of Lord Krishna</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The time passed in Gokul as usual. Balrama and Krishna too grew normally. Soon they began to walk on knees and palms. With their childish plays they both amused men and women folk of Gokul. Everyone took special care of them and looked after them to save them from any possible danger. Ladiesfolk were especially…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 11, "quote": "CHILDHOOD OF LORD KRISHNA The time passed in Gokul as usual. Balrama and Krishna too grew normally. Soon they began to walk on knees and palms. With their childish plays they both amused men and women folk of Gokul."}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-12-1241"><data key="title">Ukhal Bandhan And Salvation Of Yamalarjun</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, wishing to feed Krishna with butter, Yashoda began to churn milk in the morning. Very soon, Krishna too awakened and not finding the mother on her bed, He set out to search her. Soon He found her in the courtyard where she was churning the milk. He began to insist for breast feeding at once. So Yashoda stopped…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 12, "quote": "UKHAL BANDHAN AND SALVATION OF YAMALARJUN Once, wishing to feed Krishna with butter, Yashoda began to churn milk in the morning. Very soon, Krishna too awakened and not finding the mother on her bed, He set out to search her. Soon H"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-12-3844"><data key="title">Migration To Vrindavana</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The uprooted Arjun trees fell with thundering sound. All the people shivered with fear and felt as if lightning had struck somewhere. All the elders including Nand met together and discussed the matter. Unanimously they concluded that, of late disturbance had increased in Gokul and the circumstances were no longer…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 12, "quote": "MIGRATION TO VRINDAVANA The uprooted Arjun trees fell with thundering sound. All the people shivered with fear and felt as if lightning had struck somewhere. All the elders including Nand met together and discussed"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-13-887"><data key="title">Salvation Of Vatsasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">One day, Balaram and Krishna were grazing their cattle on the bank of Yamuna River. A demon, meanwhile, took the guise of a calf and mixed with the herd. Obviously, he had malicious intentions. Lord Krishna had already seen the demon taking calf's guise and mixing up with the herd. He signalled Balaram and they…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 13, "quote": "SALVATION OF VATSASUR One day, Balaram and Krishna were grazing their cattle on the bank of Yamuna River. A demon, meanwhile, took the guise of a calf and mixed with the herd. Obviously, he had malicious intentio"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-13-1522"><data key="title">Salvation Of Bakasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">One day, all the cowherds took their cattle to a large pond to let them drink water. There they say a huge creature sitting like a hillock on the bank. They were frightened by its appearance. The creature was in fact a demon named Bakasur who had arrived there in the guise of a storke. He was himself very strong and…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 13, "quote": "SALVATION OF BAKASUR One day, all the cowherds took their cattle to a large pond to let them drink water. There they say a huge creature sitting like a hillock on the bank. They were frightened by its appearance"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-13-2423"><data key="title">Salvation Of Aghasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">One day, Krishna planned a picnic near Nandanvan. So rising early in the morning, He awakened his fellow cowherds sounding a horn. Together, all the cowherds, and their cattle went to the forest. There, on the bank of Yamuna River they began to play joyfully. Nandanvan was in fact an abode of the demon Aghasur. Kansa…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 13, "quote": "SALVATION OF AGHASUR One day, Krishna planned a picnic near Nandanvan. So rising early in the morning, He awakened his fellow cowherds sounding a horn. Together, all the cowherds, and their cattle went to the fo"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-14-0"><data key="title">Attachment Of Brahma, Stealing Of Cattle And Cowherds</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Parashar says: The cowherds told the tale of Aghasur's salvation to their parents only after a year of the incidence. During that period of one year, Lord Krishna multiplied himself and took the guise of his fellow cowherd and their stock of cattle to remove the illusion of Brahma, who was the cause of the following…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 14, "quote": "ATTACHMENT OF BRAHMA, STEALING OF CATTLE AND COWHERDS Parashar says: The cowherds told the tale of Aghasur's salvation to their parents only after a year of the incidence. During that period of one year, Lord Krishna multiplied himself and took"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-14-2154"><data key="title">Salvation Of Dhenukasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When Krishna and Balaram entered sixth year of their ages, they got the permission to take the cattle out for grazing. Along with their fellow cowherds the two brothers too began to take their cattle to Vrindavana. Thus they sanctified the earth of Vrindavana with their pious feet. Seeing the beauty of Vrindavana,…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 14, "quote": "SALVATION OF DHENUKASUR When Krishna and Balaram entered sixth year of their ages, they got the permission to take the cattle out for grazing. Along with their fellow cowherds the two brothers too began to take the"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-14-3758"><data key="title">Grace On Kaliy The Naga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">One day, Bala-Krishna arrived on the banks of Yamuna with their friends. Balaram was not with them that day. The water of the river Yamuna was intoxicated by the poison of Kaliy- the Naga. It was exceptionally hot that day and all the cowherds and cattle were very thirsty. So without giving any thought, they all drank…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 14, "quote": "GRACE ON KALIY THE NAGA One day, Bala-Krishna arrived on the banks of Yamuna with their friends. Balaram was not with them that day. The water of the river Yamuna was intoxicated by the poison of Kaliy- the Naga. I"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-15-1820"><data key="title">Drinking Of Davanal (Forest Fire)</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After defeating Kaliy, Lord Krishna and all other people of Vraj felt extremely tired. They were very hungry and thirsty also. So they did not go back to Vrindavana, but stayed on the bank of Yamuna in the night. Because of intense heat of summer, surrounding forests had been shrivelled. At midnight those shrivelled…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 15, "quote": "DRINKING OF DAVANAL (FOREST FIRE) After defeating Kaliy, Lord Krishna and all other people of Vraj felt extremely tired. They were very hungry and thirsty also. So they did not go back to Vrindavana, but stayed on the bank o"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-15-2460"><data key="title">Salvation Of Pralambasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">One day, Balaram and Krishna were playfully grazing their cattle along with other cowherds in the forests. When a demon Pralamb arrived there with an intention of kidnapping Krishna and Balaram. The demon had come in the guise of a cowherd, but the God easily recognised him. They accepted his proposal of friendship,…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 15, "quote": "SALVATION OF PRALAMBASUR One day, Balaram and Krishna were playfully grazing their cattle along with other cowherds in the forests. When a demon Pralamb arrived there with an intention of kidnapping Krishna and Bala"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-16-2782"><data key="title">Stealing Of Clothes</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The unmarried girls of Vrindavana felt as if their lives were dedicated to Lord Krishna. Each of them wished heartily to have lord Krishna as her husband. So in order to get their desire fulfilled, all of the spinster girls of Vraj began to take bath in Yamuna early in morning of Hemant season and worshipped goddess…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 16, "quote": "STEALING OF CLOTHES The unmarried girls of Vrindavana felt as if their lives were dedicated to Lord Krishna. Each of them wished heartily to have lord Krishna as her husband. So in order to get their desire ful"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-17-0"><data key="title">Lifting Of Govardhan</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Parashar says: One day, Balaram and Krishna saw that many delicious kind of sweetmeats were being cooked at home. With curiosity and politeness, they asked Nand and other elders: "Father, which festival are you preparing for? Which god will be worshipped? What purpose will such worship serve? Nand Baba explained:…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 17, "quote": "LIFTING OF GOVARDHAN Parashar says: One day, Balaram and Krishna saw that many delicious kind of sweetmeats were being cooked at home. With curiosity and politeness, they asked Nand and other elders: \"Father, wh"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-17-1529"><data key="title">Worship Of Govardhan And Indignation Of Indra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Thus convinced by Kanhaiya's wise reasoning, all the Gopas agreed to him. Nand Baba said, "O Krishna, we are making all these preparation for you only and we will do as you say. We will worship Govardhan. For us Govardhan is also like a god. It gives us grass, water, and fuel etc., which are necessary for our lives.…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 17, "quote": "WORSHIP OF GOVARDHAN AND INDIGNATION OF INDRA Thus convinced by Kanhaiya's wise reasoning, all the Gopas agreed to him. Nand Baba said, \"O Krishna, we are making all these preparation for you only and we will do as you say. We will wors"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-18-357"><data key="title">Ceremonial Bath Of Shri Krishna</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When Indra learned about the happenings in Vraj, he soon realised his mistake. Indra himself reached Vraj and begged lord for His pardon. Then he gave lord a ceremonial bath with the milk of Surabhi. For protecting the cows, the Lord also got one more name "Govind". RASS LEELA Parashar related to Maitreya the tale of…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 18, "quote": "CEREMONIAL BATH OF SHRI KRISHNA When Indra learned about the happenings in Vraj, he soon realised his mistake. Indra himself reached Vraj and begged lord for His pardon. Then he gave lord a ceremonial bath with the milk of"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-19-121"><data key="title">Pitiable Condition Of Gopis In God'S Absence</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After the disappearance of God, Gopis were perplexed. Their hearts burned with desire. They had dedicated their entire selves in the feet of Lord. They were entirely merged in the love of Krishna. Thus driven by passion, Gopis began to search Lord Krishna. They asked trees, creepers and vegetation for the whereabouts…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 19, "quote": "PITIABLE CONDITION OF GOPIS IN GOD'S ABSENCE After the disappearance of God, Gopis were perplexed. Their hearts burned with desire. They had dedicated their entire selves in the feet of Lord. They were entirely merged in the love of Kr"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-19-1360"><data key="title">Re-Appearance Of Lord Among Gopis</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When the beloved Gopis burst into tears, Krishna's patience gave away. His heart melted at their condition and He appeared amidst them. A sweet smile played on his beautiful face. He wore a garland of fresh Vaijayanti flowers and yellow clothes. His beauty could have moved even Kamadev. Seeing their beloved Kanha once…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 19, "quote": "RE-APPEARANCE OF LORD AMONG GOPIS When the beloved Gopis burst into tears, Krishna's patience gave away. His heart melted at their condition and He appeared amidst them. A sweet smile played on his beautiful face. He wore a"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-19-2797"><data key="title">Salvation Of Sudarshan And Shankhcur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Parashar says: Once on the occasion of Shivaratri, Nand Baba and all other Gopas drove their carts with families and reached Ambikavana on a pilgrimage tour. There they took bath in the river Saraswati and with devotion worshiped lord Shiva and Parvati. They also observed day long fast and decided to pass their night…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 19, "quote": "SALVATION OF SUDARSHAN AND SHANKHCUR Parashar says: Once on the occasion of Shivaratri, Nand Baba and all other Gopas drove their carts with families and reached Ambikavana on a pilgrimage tour. There they took bath in the rive"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-20-743"><data key="title">Salvation Of Arishtasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, Kansa sent a demon Arishtasur to Vrindavana. The demon arrived there in a bull's guise. That huge bull came to Vrindavana and began to terrorise the people with his loud sound. Seeing the bull, Balaram said to Krishna: "Kanhaiya, I have never seen such a huge bull before." All the people began to cry for help.…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 20, "quote": "SALVATION OF ARISHTASUR Once, Kansa sent a demon Arishtasur to Vrindavana. The demon arrived there in a bull's guise. That huge bull came to Vrindavana and began to terrorise the people with his loud sound. Seeing"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-20-3915"><data key="title">Departure Of Krishna-Balaram For Mathura</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When the Gopis heard about Krishna leaving Gokul to visit Mathura, they began to wail and cry. They were getting so much restless by the news that, they felt, their lives would end before the sunrise. They started imprecating fate that it had no kindness. First it provided them with a closer contact with their beloved…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 20, "quote": "pleased by my realisation of mistake, he had told that when God himself would touch me, I would regain my original appearance.\" Thereafter, Sudarshan went round the God, worshiped Him and with his permission, departed to his abode. On anoth"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-21-3331"><data key="title">Akrur Has A Sight Of Lord In Four-Armed Form</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Hearing Akrur's words, Lord understood that when he had arrived in Vraj, he was afflicted with a feeling of majesty. But now, it has been replaced by affection now. He therefore decided to remove his dilemma. Thus, Lord said to him: "Kaka, Mathura is still some distance away. So you take a bath in Yamuna. We brothers…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 21, "quote": "AKRUR HAS A SIGHT OF LORD IN FOUR-ARMED FORM Hearing Akrur's words, Lord understood that when he had arrived in Vraj, he was afflicted with a feeling of majesty. But now, it has been replaced by affection now. He therefore decided to r"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-22-581"><data key="title">Arrival In Mathura</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After the departure of Akrur, Nandbaba too set out for Mathura along with the Gopas. On the way itself, they caught up with Krishna and Balaram. All of them then reached Mathura together. There they stayed in a garden. After sometime, with Nand's permission, both the brothers set out to see the city of Mathura.…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 22, "quote": "ARRIVAL IN MATHURA After the departure of Akrur, Nandbaba too set out for Mathura along with the Gopas. On the way itself, they caught up with Krishna and Balaram. All of them then reached Mathura together. Th"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-22-1820"><data key="title">Breaking Of The Bow</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After salving Kubja, Lord Krishna and other Gopas moved ahead. At a place they saw a huge bow kept on a high stage. Many strong men were guarding it. Lord entered the canopy and easily lifted the bow and broke it into pieces. There was a big applause from all around. Dau said: "Krishna, now the crowd will increase…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 22, "quote": "BREAKING OF THE BOW After salving Kubja, Lord Krishna and other Gopas moved ahead. At a place they saw a huge bow kept on a high stage. Many strong men were guarding it. Lord entered the canopy and easily lifte"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-22-2459"><data key="title">Killing Of Elephant</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Early in the morning, Kansa summoned his minister and ordered him to make Kuvalayapeed, the elephant to stand in the centre of the main gate. He thought that the elephant would kill both the boys if they dared to enter the fort through main gate. Back there in the garden, Krishna and Balarama set out in wrestlers…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 22, "quote": "KILLING OF ELEPHANT Early in the morning, Kansa summoned his minister and ordered him to make Kuvalayapeed, the elephant to stand in the centre of the main gate. He thought that the elephant would kill both the"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-22-3347"><data key="title">Salvation Of Wrestlers</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Kansa felt very nervous by the news of elephant's killing. Before he could take stock of the situation, Lord Krishna and Balaram arrived in the amphi- theatre. The spectators present in the amphitheatre saw Lord as per their feelings. The menfolk saw Lord as a Jewel among the men. Womenfolk saw him as an incarnation…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 22, "quote": "SALVATION OF WRESTLERS Kansa felt very nervous by the news of elephant's killing. Before he could take stock of the situation, Lord Krishna and Balaram arrived in the amphi- theatre. The spectators present in the"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-23-1392"><data key="title">Salvation Of Kansa</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Seeing the shameful defeat of his wrestlers, Kansa infuriated with anger and proclaimed: "Tie all the opponents; tie Ugrasen, Devaki, Vasudev and Nand in ropes and bring them before me." Lord Krishna could not tolerate this and in a single jump, He reached on the stage where Kansa was present. Lord Krishna caught him…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 23, "quote": "SALVATION OF KANSA Seeing the shameful defeat of his wrestlers, Kansa infuriated with anger and proclaimed: \"Tie all the opponents; tie Ugrasen, Devaki, Vasudev and Nand in ropes and bring them before me.\" Lor"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-23-2278"><data key="title">Yagyopavit Of Krishna-Balarama</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After the successful completion of all the royal ceremonies, the consecration of Krishna and Balaram was carried out. Thereafter, they came to stay at the hermitage of sage Sandipani for formal education. Extraordinarily brilliant Krishna successfully learned all the knowledge in short period of time. Then, as…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 23, "quote": "YAGYOPAVIT OF KRISHNA-BALARAMA After the successful completion of all the royal ceremonies, the consecration of Krishna and Balaram was carried out. Thereafter, they came to stay at the hermitage of sage Sandipani for for"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-23-2990"><data key="title">Sending Uddhav As Messanger</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Savant Uddhav, the son of Yadava's minister Brihaspati was an intimate friend of Lord Krishna. Only he had the permission to enter Lord's sanctum. Seeing his friend Krishna in remorse, Uddhav asked: "Mathuranath, you seem to be upset. What is troubling you?" Lord Krishna replied: "Uddhav, I feel perplexed. I remember…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 23, "quote": "SENDING UDDHAV AS MESSANGER Savant Uddhav, the son of Yadava's minister Brihaspati was an intimate friend of Lord Krishna. Only he had the permission to enter Lord's sanctum. Seeing his friend Krishna in remorse, Uddha"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-23-3696"><data key="title">Bhramar Geet</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Next day, when the Gopis got the news of Uddhav's arrival, they thronged in and around Nand's residence. They recognised the chariot parked in front of the gate. It was the same chariot on which Krishna and Balaram had departed for Mathura. First they thought that their beloved Krishna had returned. But someone…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 23, "quote": "as an incarnation of Kamadev. Cowherds saw their natural friend in Lord, while to Kansa He appeared as his death. But to his mother and father, Devaki and Vasudev and to Nand, Krishna and Balarama appeared as small kids. Sages and ascetics "}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-25-3331"><data key="title">Tale Of Muchkund</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Maitreya asked: "Gurudev, who was that sleeping man?" Parashar says: "O king, that sleeping man was Muchkund, the son of the king Mandhaata. The gods had sought his assistance in their war against the demon during the Satayug. With Muchkund's help, the gods had defeated the demons and thus pleased had asked him to…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 25, "quote": "TALE OF MUCHKUND Maitreya asked: \"Gurudev, who was that sleeping man?\" Parashar says: \"O king, that sleeping man was Muchkund, the son of the king Mandhaata. The gods had sought his assistance in their war a"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-26-456"><data key="title">Marriage Of Balarama</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The king of Anart, Raivat got his daughter Revati married to Balaram with the blessing of Brahma.</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 26, "quote": "MARRIAGE OF BALARAMA The king of Anart, Raivat got his daughter Revati married to Balaram with the blessing of Brahma."}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-26-577"><data key="title">Message Of Rukmani For Lord</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Parashar says: "Bheeshmak was the king of Vidarbh. He had five sons and a daughter Rukmani. Rukmi, the eldest son of Bheeshmak, had fixed his sister's engagement with Shishupal, the prince of Chaidi. Narad did not like this development. He went to Kundanpur, the capital of Vidarbh and said in the court of Bheeshmak: O…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 26, "quote": "MESSAGE OF RUKMANI FOR LORD Parashar says: \"Bheeshmak was the king of Vidarbh. He had five sons and a daughter Rukmani. Rukmi, the eldest son of Bheeshmak, had fixed his sister's engagement with Shishupal, the prince o"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-27-680"><data key="title">Defeat Of Rukmi And Shishupal</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Hearing the news that Krishna has eloped with Rukmani, Rukmi and all other kings present there boiled with anger. Accompanied by their huge armies, they decided to give them a chase. Thus chased my them, Yadnvanishis stopped and encountered the kings boldly. With a true ambition to win, brave Yadav soldiers defeated…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 27, "quote": "DEFEAT OF RUKMI AND SHISHUPAL Hearing the news that Krishna has eloped with Rukmani, Rukmi and all other kings present there boiled with anger. Accompanied by their huge armies, they decided to give them a chase. Thus ch"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-27-1305"><data key="title">Marriage Of Rukmani And Krishna</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Thus defeating all the kings, Lord Krishna brought Rukmani to Dwarka. There they got married formally. All the people of Dwarka celebrated festivities for many days. People presented them with lot of precious gifts. All the people were in great joy to see Lakshmi as Rukmani with her husband Lord Krishna.</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 27, "quote": "MARRIAGE OF RUKMANI AND KRISHNA Thus defeating all the kings, Lord Krishna brought Rukmani to Dwarka. There they got married formally. All the people of Dwarka celebrated festivities for many days. People presented them wi"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-27-1645"><data key="title">Birth Of Pradyumn, Killing Of Shabarasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Kamadev was a part of lord himself. After getting incinerated by Rudra, Kamadev took refuge in the supreme lord to get an incarnation once more. Thus, Kamadev was born as Rukmani’s first son Pradyumn. But just after his birth, Pradyumn was kidnapped by a demon Shambarasur. The demon dropped the baby into the sea,…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 27, "quote": "BIRTH OF PRADYUMN, KILLING OF SHABARASUR Kamadev was a part of lord himself. After getting incinerated by Rudra, Kamadev took refuge in the supreme lord to get an incarnation once more. Thus, Kamadev was born as Rukmani’s first son"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-27-2941"><data key="title">Tale Of Syamantak The Gem</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">A person named Satrajit was a great devotee of Lord Suryanarayana. Pleased by his devotion, Suryadev presented him a gem called Syamantak. The gem had radiance equal to the Sun. Bearing that gem, Satrajit arrived in Lord's court. By the radiance of his gem, all the people and the courtiers mistook him for Suryadev and…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 27, "quote": "TALE OF SYAMANTAK THE GEM A person named Satrajit was a great devotee of Lord Suryanarayana. Pleased by his devotion, Suryadev presented him a gem called Syamantak. The gem had radiance equal to the Sun. Bearing that"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-28-1571"><data key="title">Marriage Of Satyabhama And Krishna</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lord summoned Satrajit to His court and in the presence of the king Ugrasen, related the sequence of incidents that took place in the jungle. Satrajit felt ashamed. With a feeling of repentance he took the gem. He was getting afraid also that he made enemity with lord Krishna without reason. Hence to expiate his…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 28, "quote": "MARRIAGE OF SATYABHAMA AND KRISHNA Lord summoned Satrajit to His court and in the presence of the king Ugrasen, related the sequence of incidents that took place in the jungle. Satrajit felt ashamed. With a feeling of repenta"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-28-2242"><data key="title">Theft Of Syamantak</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Akrur and Kritvarma were not pleased with the marriage of Satyabhama. So they got Satrajit killed by Shatadhanva. Shatadhanva killed Satrajit in his sleep and absconded with the gem. Lord Krishna was then away in Hastinapur. Satyabhama too reached there and informed Krishna about her father's assassination. With…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 28, "quote": "THEFT OF SYAMANTAK Akrur and Kritvarma were not pleased with the marriage of Satyabhama. So they got Satrajit killed by Shatadhanva. Shatadhanva killed Satrajit in his sleep and absconded with the gem. Lord Kr"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-28-3065"><data key="title">Marriage Of Lord Krishna With Kalindi</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once Lord Krishna visited Indraprasth to see Pandavas. There riding a chariot with Arjuna, He came to the forests. On the bank of river Yamuna a pretty woman was observing penance. By the instinct of God, Arjun drew near her and asked for her identity. She said: "I am Kalindi, the daughter of Suryadev. I am penancing…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 28, "quote": "MARRIAGE OF LORD KRISHNA WITH KALINDI Once Lord Krishna visited Indraprasth to see Pandavas. There riding a chariot with Arjuna, He came to the forests. On the bank of river Yamuna a pretty woman was observing penance. By the in"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-28-3546"><data key="title">Lord'S Marriage With Mitrvinda</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Mitrvinda was the sister of Vind Anuvind the king of Ujjain. She had a desire to get Lord as her husband. But her brother, Vind Anuvind was a follower of Duryodhan. So he stopped his sister Mitrvinda from getting married to Krishna. But Krishna eloped with Mitrvinda from the court and got married to her formally later…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 28, "quote": "One day, later on, Satrajit's brother Prasenjit went hunting, wearing the gem in his neck. In the forest a lion killed him and snatched the gem. The lion was in turn killed by the ursine king Jambvant. Jambvant took the gem to his cave and "}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-29-325"><data key="title">Lord'S Marriage With Satya</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Satya was the daughter of Nagnjit, the king of Kaushal. She was extremely beautiful lady. The king had resolved that he would marry his daughter only to him who would defeat his seven most formidable oxen. Many princes has tried their luck since then but failed. When Lord Krishna heard about that, He reached Kaushal…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 29, "quote": "LORD'S MARRIAGE WITH SATYA Satya was the daughter of Nagnjit, the king of Kaushal. She was extremely beautiful lady. The king had resolved that he would marry his daughter only to him who would defeat his seven most f"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-29-912"><data key="title">Lord'S Marriage With Bhadra</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Parashar says: "Parikshit, Lord's aunt (father's sister) Shrutkirti was married in the kingdom of Kaikauja. Bhadra was the daughter of Shrutkirti. Bhadra's brothers like Santardan etc. themselves had got their sister married to Lord Krishna.</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 29, "quote": "LORD'S MARRIAGE WITH BHADRA Parashar says: \"Parikshit, Lord's aunt (father's sister) Shrutkirti was married in the kingdom of Kaikauja. Bhadra was the daughter of Shrutkirti. Bhadra's brothers like Santardan etc. thems"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-29-1184"><data key="title">Lord'S Marriage With Lakshmana</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lakshmana was the daughter of the king of Madra. She was very beautiful and meritorious. Lord abducted her all alone from the Swayamvara organised for her marriage. Later, Lord married to her formally. SALVATION OF BHAUMASUR Pragjyotishpur was the capital of the demon Bhaumasur. He was very strong and powerful. He had…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 29, "quote": "LORD'S MARRIAGE WITH LAKSHMANA Lakshmana was the daughter of the king of Madra. She was very beautiful and meritorious. Lord abducted her all alone from the Swayamvara organised for her marriage. Later, Lord married to he"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-29-3043"><data key="title">Lord'S Marriage With Captive Princesses</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After slaying Bhaumasur, Lord entered his palace. There He released the sixteen thousand one hundred captive princesses. The princesses were very much impressed by the Lord. They had all accepted in their mind, Lord Krishna as their husband. Lord too bowed before their love and accepted them as his wives and arranged…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 29, "quote": "LORD'S MARRIAGE WITH CAPTIVE PRINCESSES After slaying Bhaumasur, Lord entered his palace. There He released the sixteen thousand one hundred captive princesses. The princesses were very much impressed by the Lord. They had all acc"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-30-0"><data key="title">Pradyumn'S Marriage With Rukmvati</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Rukmvati was the daughter of Rukmi, the brother of Rukmani. When a Swayamvara was organised for her, she saw Pradyumn. She was so impressed by him that she chose him as her husband. But it was not acceptable to other princes. They tried to stop their marriage. But defeating them all, Pradyumn abducted Rukmvati and…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 30, "quote": "PRADYUMN'S MARRIAGE WITH RUKMVATI Rukmvati was the daughter of Rukmi, the brother of Rukmani. When a Swayamvara was organised for her, she saw Pradyumn. She was so impressed by him that she chose him as her husband. But it w"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-30-485"><data key="title">Marriage Of Usha-Anirudh</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The son of the demon king Bali, Baanasur was a great devotee of Lord Shiva. Baanasur ruled over the kingdom Shonitpur. By the grace of Lord Shiva, he had received thousand arms. Even all the gods including Indra used to serve him. Thus blinded by his physical strength, Baanasur sought a boon from Lord to meet a match…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 30, "quote": "MARRIAGE OF USHA-ANIRUDH The son of the demon king Bali, Baanasur was a great devotee of Lord Shiva. Baanasur ruled over the kingdom Shonitpur. By the grace of Lord Shiva, he had received thousand arms. Even all the"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-30-1991"><data key="title">Tale Of The King Nrig</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, Lord Krishna's sons visited the forests. There they saw a huge Chameleon fallen in a large, deep well. They tried to pull it out but in vain. The princes, therefore, returned to the palace and related this strange episode to Lord Krishna. Lord too came to the well and with His left hand, easily pulled the…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 30, "quote": "TALE OF THE KING NRIG Once, Lord Krishna's sons visited the forests. There they saw a huge Chameleon fallen in a large, deep well. They tried to pull it out but in vain. The princes, therefore, returned to the pa"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-30-3074"><data key="title">Salvation Of Paundrak</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once Balaram and Krishna had gone to Braj to see Nandbaba there. Meanwhile the king Paundrak of Karush sent an envoy to lord Krishna with a message that said: 'I am Lord Vasudev.' Pandrak's envoy arrived in the court and read out the message: "To bestow my grace on the people, I have taken an incarnation. You have…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 30, "quote": "SALVATION OF PAUNDRAK Once Balaram and Krishna had gone to Braj to see Nandbaba there. Meanwhile the king Paundrak of Karush sent an envoy to lord Krishna with a message that said: 'I am Lord Vasudev.' Pandrak's"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-31-1151"><data key="title">Salvation Of Dwivid</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">There was once a monkey named Dwivid. He was the friend of Bhaumasur. When Dwivid heard about Bhaumasur's killing by Shri Krishna, he began to cause large-scale destruction in the kingdom. His disruptive activities in the country began to terrorise the subjects of Lord Krishna. Once hearing sweet music, the monkey was…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 31, "quote": "SALVATION OF DWIVID There was once a monkey named Dwivid. He was the friend of Bhaumasur. When Dwivid heard about Bhaumasur's killing by Shri Krishna, he began to cause large-scale destruction in the kingdom. H"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-31-1715"><data key="title">Marriage Of Samb</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Samb was the son of lord Krishna and born to Jambvati. He had Kidnapped Lakshmana, the daughter of Duryodhan from her Swayamvara. Infuriated Kauravas chased them and, with difficulty, they caught Samb and tied him. When the Yaduvanshis got the news, they began preparations to launch an attack on Kauravas. Balaram…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 31, "quote": "MARRIAGE OF SAMB Samb was the son of lord Krishna and born to Jambvati. He had Kidnapped Lakshmana, the daughter of Duryodhan from her Swayamvara. Infuriated Kauravas chased them and, with difficulty, they c"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-31-2675"><data key="title">The Kings In Captivity Seek Lord'S Help</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once Lord Krishna was holding His court when an emissary arrived in the court. The kings who were held captive forcibly by Jarasandh had sent him. The emissary related the miseries of those kings to Lord Krishna. Through the emissary, the kings had requested: "O Lord of the world, Kindly get us free from our miseries.…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 31, "quote": "THE KINGS IN CAPTIVITY SEEK LORD'S HELP Once Lord Krishna was holding His court when an emissary arrived in the court. The kings who were held captive forcibly by Jarasandh had sent him. The emissary related the miseries of those"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-31-3642"><data key="title">Lord'S Departure For Indraprastha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Uddhav's advice was in the interest of all. Everyone supported it. Taking permission from His priest and teachers, Lord set out on a chariot with the whole family to reach Indraprasth. In Indraprasth, Pandavas accorded the Lord with warm-hearted felicitations. By the dictate of Lord, Mayasur built a divine looking…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 31, "quote": "Hearing the message of Paundrak, Ugrasen and other courtiers began to laugh. Lord asked the envoy to inform Paundrak that He would launch His wheel on him and his army. Receiving the message Paundrak launched an attack on Dwarka with two Ak"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-32-505"><data key="title">Killing Of Jarasandh</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">During Yudhisthir’s Rajsuy Yagya, all the Pandavas set out in all the directions to conquer the kings and expand the boundaries of their kingdom. Warriors like Bheema, Arjun defeated great kings all around and extended the boundaries of Yudhisthir's empire. But to defeat Jarasandh, - Bheema, Arjuna and Lord Krishna…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 32, "quote": "KILLING OF JARASANDH During Yudhisthir’s Rajsuy Yagya, all the Pandavas set out in all the directions to conquer the kings and expand the boundaries of their kingdom. Warriors like Bheema, Arjun defeated great k"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-32-1849"><data key="title">Salvation Of Shishupal</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">King Yudhishthir had invited great Vedic Brahmins and Acharayas on the occasion. Persons from Kauravas side like Drona, Bheeshma, Kripacharya, Dhritarashtra, Vidur and Duryodhan etc. were too invited to witness the celebrations. Even Brahma, Shiv, Indra, Gandharvas, Vidyadhar had too arrived. But before the Yagya…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 32, "quote": "SALVATION OF SHISHUPAL King Yudhishthir had invited great Vedic Brahmins and Acharayas on the occasion. Persons from Kauravas side like Drona, Bheeshma, Kripacharya, Dhritarashtra, Vidur and Duryodhan etc. were to"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-32-3680"><data key="title">Salvation Of Shalv</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says: "Parikshit, now listen to the tale of how Lord caused salvation for Shalv. Shalv was the childhood friend of Shishupal and had attended the marriage of Rukmani as a member of Shishupal's wedding party. At that time, Yaduvanshis had defeated them all including Jarasandh and Shalv. Right at that moment,…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 32, "quote": "SALVATION OF SHALV Shukdev says: \"Parikshit, now listen to the tale of how Lord caused salvation for Shalv. Shalv was the childhood friend of Shishupal and had attended the marriage of Rukmani as a member of S"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-33-1841"><data key="title">Salvation Of Dantvaktra And Vidurath</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After the killing of Shishupal, Shalv etc. Dantvaktra arrived in the battlefield carrying a mace. When Lord saw him coming, He too jumped down from the chariot and balked his movement with a mace. Dantvaktra tried to humiliate God with his abuses and hit him on head with his mace. Lord easily bore the blow of mace and…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 33, "quote": "SALVATION OF DANTVAKTRA AND VIDURATH After the killing of Shishupal, Shalv etc. Dantvaktra arrived in the battlefield carrying a mace. When Lord saw him coming, He too jumped down from the chariot and balked his movement with a"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-33-2663"><data key="title">Sudama: The Lord'S Friend</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">During his stay as a disciple at the hermitage of sage Sandipani, Lord Krishna had a Brahmin friend named Sudama. He was very indifferent in nature with no desires for the material things. After their education, Lord Krishna came to Dwarka while Sudama, who had no any inclination for accumulating material wealth, got…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 33, "quote": "SUDAMA: THE LORD'S FRIEND During his stay as a disciple at the hermitage of sage Sandipani, Lord Krishna had a Brahmin friend named Sudama. He was very indifferent in nature with no desires for the material things. A"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-34-3304"><data key="title">Meeting Of Lord With Gopis</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukdev says: "O Parikshit, Lord Krishna was passing his time with pleasure in Dwarka. A total solar eclipse happened to fall during that period. People from all over India thronged in Kurukshetra to take a dip in sacred Ganges on that great occasion. All the Yaduvanshis too arrived there. When Vrajvasis (Inhabitants…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 34, "quote": "MEETING OF LORD WITH GOPIS Shukdev says: \"O Parikshit, Lord Krishna was passing his time with pleasure in Dwarka. A total solar eclipse happened to fall during that period. People from all over India thronged in Kuruk"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-35-607"><data key="title">Organisation Of Yagya By Vasudev</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Devarshi Narad, Vyasa and many other great sages arrived in Kurukshetra to have a sight of Lord. Lord welcomed them all. Then Vasudev greeted them and enquired about way for his self-upliftment. Laughingly, Narad said: "Vasudev, a person, already living at the bank of the Ganges, discards her holy water and goes to…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 35, "quote": "ORGANISATION OF YAGYA BY VASUDEV Devarshi Narad, Vyasa and many other great sages arrived in Kurukshetra to have a sight of Lord. Lord welcomed them all. Then Vasudev greeted them and enquired about way for his self-upliftm"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-35-1528"><data key="title">Revisting Of Devaki'S Six Sons</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">In Dwarka, Krishna and Balaram used to greet their parent's first daily in the morning. Now Vasudev had recognised His identity. So after their return from Kurukshetra, when Krishna-Balaram went as usual to greet their parents, Vasudev greeted them first. Lord Krishna then preached his father about the metaphysical…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 35, "quote": "REVISTING OF DEVAKI'S SIX SONS In Dwarka, Krishna and Balaram used to greet their parent's first daily in the morning. Now Vasudev had recognised His identity. So after their return from Kurukshetra, when Krishna-Balaram"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-35-3452"><data key="title">Love And Marriage Of Subhadra And Arjun</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">King Parikshit asked: "Gurudev, how did my grandfather Arjun and grandmother Subhadra got married? I want to hear this tale. Kindly narrate it to me." Shukdev said: Parikshit once travelling on a pilgrimage tour, Arjun reached in Prabhas region. There he learned that Balaram was desirous of marrying his sister…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 35, "quote": "Nand. Lord Krishna and Balaram respectfully greeted mother Yashoda and father Nand. They too embraced Krishna-Balaram cordially. Gopis were specially overwhelmed by Lord's sight. They had no other desire but to have a sight of Lord. That da"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-36-1255"><data key="title">Release Of Shiva From Crisis</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Maitreya asked: "Gurudev, those among the gods, human beings and the demons, who worship Lord Shiva, get wealth very soon. But those, who worship Lord Narayana, remain afflicted with scarcity. What is the reason for that?" In reply, Lord Krishna had said: "O Yudhishthir, I take away all the wealth from those whom I…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">36</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 36, "quote": "RELEASE OF SHIVA FROM CRISIS Maitreya asked: \"Gurudev, those among the gods, human beings and the demons, who worship Lord Shiva, get wealth very soon. But those, who worship Lord Narayana, remain afflicted with scarcit"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-36-3142"><data key="title">Testing Of Trinity</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once upon a time, all the sages assembled on the bank of Saraswati River. A dispute erupted among them as to who among the trinity was the greatest. By consensus they appointed sage Bhrigu to test the trinity of Brahma, Vishnu and Mahesh. Bhrigu reached to Brahma, but did not greet him. Brahma got infuriated but since…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">36</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 36, "quote": "TESTING OF TRINITY Once upon a time, all the sages assembled on the bank of Saraswati River. A dispute erupted among them as to who among the trinity was the greatest. By consensus they appointed sage Bhrigu t"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-37-612"><data key="title">Revival Of Brahmn'S Dead Children</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There in Dwarka lived a Brahmin. When the first son was born to his wife, it died immediately after birth. The Brahmin took his dead son to the royal court and complained: "My Son has died because of anti- Brahmin and licentious actions of the king." One by one, thus, eight sons were born to the Brahmin couple; but…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">37</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 37, "quote": "REVIVAL OF BRAHMN'S DEAD CHILDREN There in Dwarka lived a Brahmin. When the first son was born to his wife, it died immediately after birth. The Brahmin took his dead son to the royal court and complained: \"My Son has died b"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-5-6"><data key="title">The Sages Curse Yaduvanshis</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Parashar said: "As the time passed, Yaduvanshis grew stronger and more influential. Now no longer did they like the welcoming and treating of the great sages by the Lord. Lord Krishna knew about their disliking, but secretly He was pleased by this change in Yaduvanshi's behaviour, for He knew that by disregarding the…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">37</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 37, "quote": "3.5.6 The Sages Curse Yaduvanshis Parashar said: \"As the time passed, Yaduvanshis grew stronger and more influential. Now no longer did they like the welcoming and treating of the great sages by the Lord. Lord Krishna knew a"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-38-3150"><data key="title">Lord Departs To His Abode And Destruction Of Yaduvanshis</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">To remind the Lord about the moment of His departure, Brahma arrived in Dwarka accompanied by all the gods. He prayed to Him: "O Lord, you have completed your duty of what we had once requested you. Now, You please return to your abode. Lord said: "On the seventh day from now, Dwarka shall submerge in the sea and…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">38</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 38, "quote": "LORD DEPARTS TO HIS ABODE AND DESTRUCTION OF YADUVANSHIS To remind the Lord about the moment of His departure, Brahma arrived in Dwarka accompanied by all the gods. He prayed to Him: \"O Lord, you have completed your duty of what we had once reques"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-39-2867"><data key="title">Bhikshu Geet</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Lord said: "Uddhav, in the world, there is indeed a dearth of such tolerant people who can tolerate bitter remarks of evil ones. In this context there is a tale of a greedy Brahmin who lived in Ujjain. That Brahmin had been badly tormented by the evil people after he had lost his wealth. But still the Brahmin did not…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">39</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 39, "quote": "BHIKSHU GEET Lord said: \"Uddhav, in the world, there is indeed a dearth of such tolerant people who can tolerate bitter remarks of evil ones. In this context there is a tale of a greedy Brahmin who lived"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-6"><data key="title">Chapter 6 (Part 6)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">40</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 40, "quote": "3.6 Chapter 6 (Part 6)"}]</data></node><node id="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-6-1"><data key="title">Descriptions of Dynasties in Kali-Yuga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Parashar describes about the dynasties that would occur in Kaliyug. Ripunjay or Puranjay would be the last king of Brihdrath dynasty. The kings of Nand dynasty would succeed this dynasty. Then a diplomat Brahmin, named Chanakya, would exterminate this dynasty and enthrone Chandragupta Maurya. In Maurya dynasty, a…</data><data key="doc">vishnu-puran-2</data><data key="page">40</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 40, "quote": "3.6.1 Descriptions of Dynasties in Kali-Yuga Parashar describes about the dynasties that would occur in Kaliyug. Ripunjay or Puranjay would be the last king of Brihdrath dynasty. The kings of Nand dynasty would succeed this dynasty. Th"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-upper-1-990"><data key="title">Proper Method Of Listening To</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">SHIVPURAN Sage Shaunak requested Sutji to tell about the proper method of listening to Shivpuran, so that the man kind gets complete benefit. Sutji replied- "First of all, an auspicious moment should be determined by an Astrologer. After that, friends and relatives should be invited, especially those who have the…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 1, "quote": "PROPER METHOD OF LISTENING TO SHIVPURAN Sage Shaunak requested Sutji to tell about the proper method of listening to Shivpuran, so that the man kind gets complete benefit. Sutji replied- \"First of all, an auspicious mome"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-upper-1-2423"><data key="title">Right Of Listening To Shivmahapuran:-</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">An uninitiated person does not have the right to listen to the Katha of Shivmahapuran. So a man desirous of listening to Shivmahapuran should first get initiated.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 1, "quote": "RIGHT OF LISTENING TO SHIVMAHAPURAN:- An uninitiated person does not have the right to listen to the Katha of Shivmahapuran. So a man desirous of listening to Shivmahapuran should first get initiated."}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-upper-1-2626"><data key="title">Austerities To Be Followed:</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A devotee who has taken the vow of listening to the Katha of Shivmahapuran, should follow a celibate life. He should sleep on the floor and should have only fruits in the diet. He can have his normal dinner only after the completion of the Katha. The diet should be fresh and pure. He should also try to avoid sinful…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 1, "quote": "AUSTERITIES TO BE FOLLOWED: A devotee who has taken the vow of listening to the Katha of Shivmahapuran, should follow a celibate life. He should sleep on the floor and should have only fruits in the diet. He can have h"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-upper-1-3114"><data key="title">Rites Performed On Completion Of Observance:-</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After the methodical completion of the Katha, a devotee should perform UDDYAPAN (Conclusion of the Katha), which is similar to the CHATURDASHI UDDYAPAN. The devotee should also Charitably make donations to all the brahmins who have helped in the performance of Katha. On the next day the 'Path' of SHIV GITA should be…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 1, "quote": "RITES PERFORMED ON COMPLETION OF OBSERVANCE:- After the methodical completion of the Katha, a devotee should perform UDDYAPAN (Conclusion of the Katha), which is similar to the CHATURDASHI UDDYAPAN. The devotee should also Charitably ma"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1"><data key="title">Vidyeshwar Samhita</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 2, "quote": "5.1 Vidyeshwar Samhita"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-1"><data key="title">Sages Enquire</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Viddyeshwar Samhita which is classified into twenty five chapters, has a narration in its first chapter which goes as follows- Once upon a time, the sages were performing a yagya at Prayag kshetra. Sage Sutji came to know about this and arrived there. All the sages were very pleased to see him. They requested:- "Lord!…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 2, "quote": "5.1.1 Sages Enquire Viddyeshwar Samhita which is classified into twenty five chapters, has a narration in its first chapter which goes as follows- Once upon a time, the sages were performing a yagya at Prayag k"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-2"><data key="title">Proposition and Means</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sutji continued with his narration:- "During the initial period of 'Svet Varah Kalp'. Six prominent sages collected near Triveni and started debating as to who was the greatest deity, among Brahma Vishnu and Mahesh. Their debate remained inconclusive, so they went to lord Brahma to seek the answer. Lord Brahma told…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 2, "quote": "5.1.2 Proposition and Means Sutji continued with his narration:- \"During the initial period of 'Svet Varah Kalp'. Six prominent sages collected near Triveni and started debating as to who was the greatest deity, among"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-3"><data key="title">Listening, Kirtan, Contemplation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">SHRAVAN KIRTAN MANAN Purification of the mind by the help of doing worship and chanting the name of God is called contemplation. Singing devotional songs in the form of stotra, or hymns of the vedas or even in one's own language is called Kirtan. The above mentioned three activities are the supreme means to attain…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 3, "quote": "5.1.3 Listening, Kirtan, Contemplation SHRAVAN KIRTAN MANAN Purification of the mind by the help of doing worship and chanting the name of God is called contemplation. Singing devotional songs in the form of stotra, or hymns of t"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-4"><data key="title">Greatness of Shiva Linga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">According to Sutji, if a person is incapable of following the above mentioned three activities i.e. Shravan, Kirtan and Manan then he should worship the Shivalinga. Even by doing this he can attain liberation from all the bondages of the world. Describing about the majesty of Shivalinga, Sutji says: "Lord Shiva is the…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 3, "quote": "5.1.4 Greatness of Shiva Linga According to Sutji, if a person is incapable of following the above mentioned three activities i.e. Shravan, Kirtan and Manan then he should worship the Shivalinga. Even by doing this he can"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-5"><data key="title">The Deities go to Kailash Mountain</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Nandikeshwarji narrates the tale of battle between Lord Brahma and Lord Vishnu. "Once while travelling lord Brahma reached the abode of Lord Vishnu. He saw Lord Vishnu. He saw lord Vishnu resting on Shesh-Nag and being attended by Garuda and other attendants. When Brahmaji saw that Vishnu did not get up to receive…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 3, "quote": "5.1.5 The Deities go to Kailash Mountain Nandikeshwarji narrates the tale of battle between Lord Brahma and Lord Vishnu. \"Once while travelling lord Brahma reached the abode of Lord Vishnu. He saw Lord Vishnu. He saw lord Vishnu re"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-6"><data key="title">Anaal-Stambh (The Pillar of Fire)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The pillar of fire interrupts the dispute of Brahma and Vishnu. Their search for its limits becomes a story about Shiva’s manifestation.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 3, "quote": "5.1.6 Anaal-Stambh (The Pillar of Fire) \"Though Lord Shiva knew everything, but still feigning ignorance, he asked about the well beings of the world. The deities told him about the battle, fought between Brahmaji and Vishnuji.\" \""}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-7"><data key="title">Shiva Obliges Brahma</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Nandikeshwar continuing with the tale said: "After according same status to Vishnuji as that of his own, Lord Shiva opened his third eye from it manifested 'Bhairav'. He ordered Bhairav to kill Brahmaji. Bhairav severed the fifth head of Lord Brahma with his sword. Brahmaji became very terrified. He was trembling in…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 4, "quote": "5.1.7 Shiva Obliges Brahma Nandikeshwar continuing with the tale said: \"After according same status to Vishnuji as that of his own, Lord Shiva opened his third eye from it manifested 'Bhairav'. He ordered Bhairav to k"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-8"><data key="title">Consecration of Maheshwar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Vishnu and Brahma made salutations to lord Shiva and offered him a seat. They then worshipped him. This was the first time, Brahma and Vishnu had worshipped lord Shiva. Shiva was very pleased. Shiva-ratri has been continued to be celebrated since that day. This particular day is considered to be the most…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 4, "quote": "5.1.8 Consecration of Maheshwar Lord Vishnu and Brahma made salutations to lord Shiva and offered him a seat. They then worshipped him. This was the first time, Brahma and Vishnu had worshipped lord Shiva. Shiva was very p"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-9"><data key="title">Omkar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva then preached Brahmaji and Vishnuji on the five duties (Panchakritya) saying that 'Shrishti' (creation), 'Sthithi' (position), Samhar (Annihilation), Tirobhav (Concealment) and 'Anugrah' (obligation or kindness) are the five duties by which this world functions. The source of this world in 'Sarga' or…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 5, "quote": "5.1.9 Omkar Lord Shiva then preached Brahmaji and Vishnuji on the five duties (Panchakritya) saying that 'Shrishti' (creation), 'Sthithi' (position), Samhar (Annihilation), Tirobhav (Concealment) and 'A"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-10"><data key="title">Rituals of Shiva Worship</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">On the request of the sages, Sutji describes about the methods of worshipping Shiva Linga. He says "One should construct a Shivalinga either of mud, rock or metal and establish it in such a place where it can be worshipped daily without any hindrance." "The 'Char' (mobile) Linga should be small in size and the Sthira…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 5, "quote": "5.1.10 Rituals of Shiva Worship On the request of the sages, Sutji describes about the methods of worshipping Shiva Linga. He says \"One should construct a Shivalinga either of mud, rock or metal and establish it in such a"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-11"><data key="title">Sacred Pilgrimages of Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There are numerous places of pilgrimages connected with Shiva at the banks of river Ganges and river Indus. River Saraswati is considered to be a sacrosanct river and having an opportunity of living at its banks, helps in attaining to the abode of Brahma. Similarly, there are famous temples of Lord Shiva at Kashi,…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 5, "quote": "5.1.11 Sacred Pilgrimages of Shiva There are numerous places of pilgrimages connected with Shiva at the banks of river Ganges and river Indus. River Saraswati is considered to be a sacrosanct river and having an opportunity o"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-12"><data key="title">Conduct of the respective Castes</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">On the request of the sages, Sutji described about the virtuous and invirtuous activities of a man according to the respective castes be belong. He said: "A brahmin who performs the rituals, as described in the Vedas, only is entitled to be called a Dwija. A brahmin who is not that proficient in the Vedas is called a…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 6, "quote": "5.1.12 Conduct of the respective Castes On the request of the sages, Sutji described about the virtuous and invirtuous activities of a man according to the respective castes be belong. He said: \"A brahmin who performs the rituals,"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-13"><data key="title">Types of Dharma</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Dharma is considered to be of two types- 1) Dharma performed by matter and Materials. 2) Dharma performed by indulging in physical activities. The performance of Yagya etc comes in the first category. Making pilgrimages of holy places comes in the second category. During the Satya-Yuga, meditation was the way to…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 6, "quote": "5.1.13 Types of Dharma Dharma is considered to be of two types- 1) Dharma performed by matter and Materials. 2) Dharma performed by indulging in physical activities. The performance of Yagya etc comes in the first"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-14"><data key="title">Appropriate Place and Time for Worshipping Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 7, "quote": "5.1.14 Appropriate Place and Time for Worshipping Shiva"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-upper-7-57"><data key="title">Importance Of Place:</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">In the Viddyeshwar Samhita of Shivapuran, describing about the importance of place and time for worship of Shiva says- "Worshipping Shiva at a pure place in a house gives appropriate fruits, while worship done in a cowshed gives virtue, which are ten-times more than the former one. Worshipping Shiva at the banks of a…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 7, "quote": "IMPORTANCE OF PLACE: In the Viddyeshwar Samhita of Shivapuran, describing about the importance of place and time for worship of Shiva says- \"Worshipping Shiva at a pure place in a house gives appropriate fruits,"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-15"><data key="title">Worshipping the Idol of Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">It has been mentioned in the sixteenth chapter of Viddyeshwar Samhita that all the desires of a man are completely fulfilled, if he worship even in earthen idol of Shiva. For making an idol of Shiva. Day should be acquired from the base of river , pond, well or any such other place. In this clay fragrant powder and…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 7, "quote": "5.1.15 Worshipping the Idol of Shiva It has been mentioned in the sixteenth chapter of Viddyeshwar Samhita that all the desires of a man are completely fulfilled, if he worship even in earthen idol of Shiva. For making an idol"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-16"><data key="title">The Majesty of Pranav Panchakshar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The root sounds Akaar, Ukaar, Makaar, Bindu and Naad, which are free from the delusions and which originates from the mother Nature are called Pranav. It is of two types:- a) Gross, b) Subtle. (Pranav Mantra OM AND OM NAMAH SHIVAY! It symbolizess the unified power of Shiva and Shakti and destroyes all the sins of a…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 7, "quote": "5.1.16 The Majesty of Pranav Panchakshar The root sounds Akaar, Ukaar, Makaar, Bindu and Naad, which are free from the delusions and which originates from the mother Nature are called Pranav. It is of two types:-"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-17"><data key="title">Worldly Bondage and Salvation</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">In the eighteen chapter, all the sages requested Sutji to explain the meaning of bondages of life and Salvation Sutji replied- "Because of the eight types of bondages which a man feels on this earth, the soul is also known as "Jeeva". The Jeeva becomes liberated only after becoming free from these eight bondages.…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 8, "quote": "5.1.17 Worldly Bondage and Salvation In the eighteen chapter, all the sages requested Sutji to explain the meaning of bondages of life and Salvation Sutji replied- \"Because of the eight types of bondages which a man feels on th"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-18"><data key="title">The Worship of Parthiva Linga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sutji then explains the greatness of worshipping a Parthiva Linga-: "Parthiva Linga is the most supreme among all the Shiva-Lingas. All the aspirtions of he deities as well as men are fulfilled by the worship of Parthiv linga. During the era of Satya, jewel was considered to be of prime importance, where as during…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 8, "quote": "5.1.18 The Worship of Parthiva Linga Sutji then explains the greatness of worshipping a Parthiva Linga-: \"Parthiva Linga is the most supreme among all the Shiva-Lingas. All the aspirtions of he deities as well as men are fulfil"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-19"><data key="title">Methods of Worshipping Parthiva Linga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Elaborately describes about the methods of doing worship of Parthiva Linga:- "After becoming fresh in the morning, a man should wear a rudraksha garland in his neck and apply bhasma (Ash) on his forehead. He should then worship the Parthiva Linga. He should chant the various names of Shiva, while worshipping the…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 8, "quote": "5.1.19 Methods of Worshipping Parthiva Linga Elaborately describes about the methods of doing worship of Parthiva Linga:-"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-20"><data key="title">Number of Parthiva Shivalingas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The numbers of Parthiva Linga differ according to one's desires. For example a man who is desirous of learning and knowledge must worship one thousand Parthiva Linga. A man who is desirous of wealth must worship. One thousand five hundred Parthiva Lingas. A man who is desirous of attaining salvation must worship one…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 9, "quote": "5.1.20 Number of Parthiva Shivalingas The numbers of Parthiva Linga differ according to one's desires. For example a man who is desirous of learning and knowledge must worship one thousand Parthiva Linga. A man who is desirous o"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-21"><data key="title">Importance of Naivedya and Bilva Leaves</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sutji then described about the important of Naivedya offered to Lord Shiva. "A devotee gets liberated from all of his sins merely at the sight of the Naivedya, which have been offered to lord Shiva. He attains great virtues by having the Prasada." A man must not accept the Prasada if the worship have been done under…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 9, "quote": "5.1.21 Importance of Naivedya and Bilva Leaves Sutji then described about the important of Naivedya offered to Lord Shiva. \"A devotee gets liberated from all of his sins merely at the sight of the Naivedya, which have been offered to lor"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-22"><data key="title">The Majesty of Shiva’s Name</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sutji then goes on he explain the greatness of Shiva's name and the importance of Bhasm (Ash) and rudraksha beads in his worship. The name of Shiva is as sacred as Ganges; Similarly 'Bhasm' and 'Rudraksha' are as holy as river Yamuna and Saraswati respectively. Therefore a devotee who possesses the name of lord Shiva…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 9, "quote": "5.1.22 The Majesty of Shiva’s Name Sutji then goes on he explain the greatness of Shiva's name and the importance of Bhasm (Ash) and rudraksha beads in his worship. The name of Shiva is as sacred as Ganges; Similarly 'Bhasm'"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-23"><data key="title">The Importance of Bhasma in Shiva’s Worship</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Bhasma are of two types:- 1) Mahabhasma and 2) Swalpabhasma. 'Shrota' (listener), 'Smart; (rites according to the smritis) and Laukik (worldly) are considered to be the Mahabhasma. There are many types of Swalpabhasma 'Shrota' and 'Smart' are meant only for the brahmins. For the rest of the castes, 'Laukik bhasma' is…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 10, "quote": "5.1.23 The Importance of Bhasma in Shiva’s Worship Bhasma are of two types:- 1) Mahabhasma and 2) Swalpabhasma. 'Shrota' (listener), 'Smart; (rites according to the smritis) and Laukik (worldly) are considered to be the Mahabhasma. There are"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-1-24"><data key="title">Importance of Rudraksha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Rudraksha is very dear to lord Shiva and hence all the sins of a man get destroyed if he chants the name of Lord Shiva using a Rudraksha beads. He also attain salvation after his death. It is believed that the origin of Rudraksha is connected with Shiva penance. Once while Sadashiv was performing his penance, his eyes…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 10, "quote": "5.1.24 Importance of Rudraksha Rudraksha is very dear to lord Shiva and hence all the sins of a man get destroyed if he chants the name of Lord Shiva using a Rudraksha beads. He also attain salvation after his death. It i"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2"><data key="title">Rudra Samhita</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 10, "quote": "5.2 Rudra Samhita"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-1"><data key="title">Shrishti-Khand</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages express their desire of knowing about the manifestation of Shiva and Uma, their marriage and their life as a householder and the other aspects of Shiva's divinity Sutji narrated the story of Narad's attachment and lust - how they were ultimately destroyed. He also narrated of Narad's deep desire to know…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 10, "quote": "5.2.1 Shrishti-Khand The sages express their desire of knowing about the manifestation of Shiva and Uma, their marriage and their life as a householder and the other aspects of Shiva's divinity Sutji narrated th"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-2"><data key="title">Victory of Narada over ‘Kama’</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once upon a time a Narad was performing a penance in the cave of Himalaya mountain. Indra becoming fearful sent Kamadev to obstruct his penance. But Kamadeva was unsuccessful in his attempt as the place where Narada was doing penance was the same place where Lord Shiva did penance. After the completion of his penance,…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 10, "quote": "5.2.2 Victory of Narada over ‘Kama’ Once upon a time a Narad was performing a penance in the cave of Himalaya mountain. Indra becoming fearful sent Kamadev to obstruct his penance. But Kamadeva was unsuccessful in his attempt"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-3"><data key="title">Narada’s Ego Subdued:</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">While Narada was returning from Vaikuntha Loka, he saw a beautiful city. This city was ruled by a king named SheelNidhi. The king had a daughter and her Swayamvar was being organised. Numerous kings had arrived to take part in that Swayamvar. Narada, curiously entered the place were Swayamvar was being held. The king…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 11, "quote": "5.2.3 Narada’s Ego Subdued: While Narada was returning from Vaikuntha Loka, he saw a beautiful city. This city was ruled by a king named SheelNidhi. The king had a daughter and her Swayamvar was being organised. Numero"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-4"><data key="title">Vishnu Preaches Narad:</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Vishnu accepted Narada's curse without any hesitation. He then removed the illusionary powers by which Naradas' mind was influenced Now, Narada regretted his actions. Lord Vishnu told Narada that everything happened because of Shiva's divine illusions. "You did not pay heed to his advice and hence Shiva by his…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 11, "quote": "5.2.4 Vishnu Preaches Narad: Lord Vishnu accepted Narada's curse without any hesitation. He then removed the illusionary powers by which Naradas' mind was influenced Now, Narada regretted his actions. Lord Vishnu told N"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-5"><data key="title">Brahmaji Preaches Narad:</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">On Narad's request Brahmaji said- "During the period of Mahapralaya (Final annihilation) nothing exist, except Shiva. The power which Shiva manifested from his own self came to be known as Ambika. This Ambika is understood to be the cause of the whole world as well as the Nature. Lord Shiva then created a Shivaloka,…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 11, "quote": "5.2.5 Brahmaji Preaches Narad: On Narad's request Brahmaji said-"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-6"><data key="title">The Origin of Vishnu</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva, felt the need of somebody, who could look after the creation in his absence, as he wanted to retire along with Ambika at Kashi. The unified energy of Shiva and Ambika, resulted into a radiant physical form of a child. The child asked Shiva about his name and purpose of his being. Lord Shiva named the child…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 12, "quote": "5.2.6 The Origin of Vishnu Lord Shiva, felt the need of somebody, who could look after the creation in his absence, as he wanted to retire along with Ambika at Kashi. The unified energy of Shiva and Ambika, resulted i"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-7"><data key="title">Origin of Brahmaji</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Brahmaji told Narad- "While Vishnuji was sleeping in the water, a lotus flower manifested from the navel of Vishnu according to the wish of Shiva. On that lotus was seated with four heads. I did not see anything except that lotus flower. I had a desire to know about my identity. So I entered into the hollow tubular…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 12, "quote": "5.2.7 Origin of Brahmaji Brahmaji told Narad- \"While Vishnuji was sleeping in the water, a lotus flower manifested from the navel of Vishnu according to the wish of Shiva. On that lotus was seated with four heads. I"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-8"><data key="title">Dispute Between Brahmaji &amp; Vishnuji</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">"I asked him as to who he was. Vishnuji replied that he was the fulfiller of all of his desires. But I replied to him that I only was the creator, nurturer and the supreme soul of this world. Vishnuji became angry and said that undoubtedly I (Brahma) was the creator of this world, but he (Vishnu) was the one, who had…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 12, "quote": "5.2.8 Dispute Between Brahmaji &amp; Vishnuji \"I asked him as to who he was. Vishnuji replied that he was the fulfiller of all of his desires. But I replied to him that I only was the creator, nurturer and the supreme soul of this world"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-9"><data key="title">Shabda-Brahma</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">We heard a sound OM. We became curious to know the origin of that sound. Vishnuji saw a letter 'A' towards the south of that Shivalinga. He also saw the letters 'U' and 'M' centre towards the north of the Shivalinga and in its centre respectively. He also saw the mantra 'OM', which was dazzling like a Sun. There was…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 13, "quote": "5.2.9 Shabda-Brahma We heard a sound OM. We became curious to know the origin of that sound. Vishnuji saw a letter 'A' towards the south of that Shivalinga. He also saw the letters 'U' and 'M' centre towards th"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-10"><data key="title">Shabda-Brahma Tanu</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Then we saw all the vowels and consonants emanating from the physique of Mahadeva. Vishnuji saw the forty eight letters within OMKAR, which in fact were the two following mantras- "TATPURUSHAY VIDDYAMAHE MAHADEVAY DHIMAHI, TANNO RUDRAH PRACHODAYAT." And "TATSAVITURVARENYAM BHARGO DEVASYA DHIMAHI DHIYO YONAH…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 13, "quote": "5.2.10 Shabda-Brahma Tanu Then we saw all the vowels and consonants emanating from the physique of Mahadeva. Vishnuji saw the forty eight letters within OMKAR, which in fact were the two following mantras- \"TATPURUSH"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-11"><data key="title">Shiva Enlightens Brahma &amp; Vishnu</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">"Shiva became very pleased with both of us. He preached us the contents of Veda. Shiva told Vishnuji about the methods by which his (Shiva's) worship could be done. He revealed to us that Vishnuji actually had manifested from the left portion of his Shiva's body and myself from the right portion of his body. He also…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 13, "quote": "5.2.11 Shiva Enlightens Brahma &amp; Vishnu \"Shiva became very pleased with both of us. He preached us the contents of Veda. Shiva told Vishnuji about the methods by which his (Shiva's) worship could be done. He revealed to us that Vi"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-12"><data key="title">Age of Brahma, Vishnu &amp; Rudra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Bramhaji told Narada: "Lord Shiva informed us that my day consists of four thousand eras and similarly my night too consists of four thousand eras. Since a month consists of thirty days and a year consists of twelve months. In this way my age was fixed to be of one hundred years. One day of Vishnu is equivalent to one…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 13, "quote": "5.2.12 Age of Brahma, Vishnu &amp; Rudra Bramhaji told Narada: \"Lord Shiva informed us that my day consists of four thousand eras and similarly my night too consists of four thousand eras. Since a month consists of thirty days and"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-13"><data key="title">The Methods of Worshipping Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">On the request of the sages, Sutji retold the preaching which had been narrated by Brahmaji to Narad. Describing the method of Shiva worship he says- "A devotee should get up early in the morning and contemplate on Shiva, who gives benediction. After that, he should finish his daily routing work and perform rituals…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 13, "quote": "5.2.13 The Methods of Worshipping Shiva On the request of the sages, Sutji retold the preaching which had been narrated by Brahmaji to Narad. Describing the method of Shiva worship he says-"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-14"><data key="title">Acquiring of Shiva Lingas by the Deity</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, Brahmaji went to 'Kshirsagar' (the abode of Vishnu) accompanied by the deities and asked lord Vishnu how a man could be liberated from his sorrows. Lord Vishnu told them that this objective could be met b worshipping Shiva-Linga. All the deities, then prayed to lord Shiva, who after becoming pleased instructed…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 14, "quote": "5.2.14 Acquiring of Shiva Lingas by the Deity Once, Brahmaji went to 'Kshirsagar' (the abode of Vishnu) accompanied by the deities and asked lord Vishnu how a man could be liberated from his sorrows. Lord Vishnu told them that this obje"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-15"><data key="title">Methods of Worshipping Shiva Linga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Brahmaji has described the following methods for the worship of Shivalinga- "After performing the rituals like 'Aachaman' and Pranayam a devotee should apply a tripunda on his forehead and wear a rudraksha on his body. After the study of Shanti-path and the performance of Devata-Namaskar, he should make a resolution…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 14, "quote": "5.2.15 Methods of Worshipping Shiva Linga Brahmaji has described the following methods for the worship of Shivalinga- \"After performing the rituals like 'Aachaman' and Pranayam a devotee should apply a tripunda on his forehead and w"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-16"><data key="title">The Virtues of Worshipping Shiva Methodically</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">It has been mentioned in the Shivapuran that making offerings in the form of lotus, Shatapatra, , Shankhapushpi, the leaves of wood apple tree, helps a man in attaining wealth and prosperity. For becoming free from disease, fifty lotus flowers should be offered to the Shivalinga. Mrityunjay-Jap should be chanted for…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 14, "quote": "5.2.16 The Virtues of Worshipping Shiva Methodically It has been mentioned in the Shivapuran that making offerings in the form of lotus, Shatapatra, , Shankhapushpi, the leaves of wood apple tree, helps a man in attaining wealth and prosperity"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-17"><data key="title">The Origin of Kailash &amp; Vaikunth</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Brahmaji revealed to Narad how the process of creation commenced- "After Lord Shiva vanished from our sight, I transformed myself into the appearance of a swan and Vishnuji transformed his appearance into that of a boar with the purpose of commencing the process of creation. First of all, I created the water. I poured…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 14, "quote": "5.2.17 The Origin of Kailash &amp; Vaikunth Brahmaji revealed to Narad how the process of creation commenced- \"After Lord Shiva vanished from our sight, I transformed myself into the appearance of a swan and Vishnuji transformed his a"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-18"><data key="title">The Emergence of Rudra-Avatar</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Brahmaji said to Narad: "When I accomplished my penance, Lord Shiva manifested in his incarnation of Rudra from in between the eyebrows. Half of his body resembled like that of a woman (Ardhanarishwar). I requested him help me in my creational activities. Rudra created his hosts (Rudragana) who resembled like him. I…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 15, "quote": "5.2.18 The Emergence of Rudra-Avatar Brahmaji said to Narad: \"When I accomplished my penance, Lord Shiva manifested in his incarnation of Rudra from in between the eyebrows. Half of his body resembled like that of a woman (Ardh"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-19"><data key="title">Creation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Preaching Narad on the essence of Shivatattva, Brahmaji said- "With the permission of Shiva, I created the five basic elements from which the matter is made and also all types of arts. I also created the time. Despite all these creations, I was not satisfied. I created sage Marichi from my eyes, Sage Bhrigu from my…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 15, "quote": "5.2.19 Creation Preaching Narad on the essence of Shivatattva, Brahmaji said- \"With the permission of Shiva, I created the five basic elements from which the matter is made and also all types of arts. I als"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-20"><data key="title">Gunanidhi, The Brahmin’s Son</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Naradji asked lord Brahma, when did lord Shiva make Kailash as his abode and what was the reason of friendship between Shivaji and Kubera. Brahmaji narrated the following story- v There used to live a brahmin by the name Yagyadutt in Kampilyanagar, who was proficient in the performance of Somyagya. He had a son named…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 15, "quote": "5.2.20 Gunanidhi, The Brahmin’s Son Naradji asked lord Brahma, when did lord Shiva make Kailash as his abode and what was the reason of friendship between Shivaji and Kubera. Brahmaji narrated the following story- v There used"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-21"><data key="title">Gunanidhi’s Salvation</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When Gunadhi came to know about his father's second marriage, he moved to another place, cursing his fate. He kept on walking till he became tired. He sat under a tree and started thinking about his further course of action. He heart was full of remorse and he repented for his past actions. As his mind was engrossed…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 16, "quote": "5.2.21 Gunanidhi’s Salvation When Gunadhi came to know about his father's second marriage, he moved to another place, cursing his fate. He kept on walking till he became tired. He sat under a tree and started thinking a"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-22"><data key="title">Shiva Blesses Gunanidhi</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">"During the Padma kalpa, Sage Vishrawa was born to sage Pulastya- the manasputra of lord Brahma. Vishrawa's son - Vishrawan ruled over Alkapuri for a long time. This city was constructed by the deity Vishwakarma. Vishrawan was a great devotee of Lord Shiva." "During the kalpa named Meghawahan, Gunanidhi did a…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 16, "quote": "5.2.22 Shiva Blesses Gunanidhi \"During the Padma kalpa, Sage Vishrawa was born to sage Pulastya- the manasputra of lord Brahma. Vishrawa's son - Vishrawan ruled over Alkapuri for a long time. This city was constructed by"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-23"><data key="title">Shiva goes to Kailash</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When Rudra - the Ansha of the Almighty brahma, heard about the tremendous penance, Kubera was doing he started to play his 'damaru' (drum), the sound of which reached all the three worlds. Rudra then reached the place where Kubera was doing penance." "Hearing the sound of his damaru, all the deities including Brahma,…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 17, "quote": "5.2.23 Shiva goes to Kailash When Rudra - the Ansha of the Almighty brahma, heard about the tremendous penance, Kubera was doing he started to play his 'damaru' (drum), the sound of which reached all the three worlds. R"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-24"><data key="title">The Manifestation of ‘Kama’</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">"When I became infatuated by the divine beauty of Sandhya, my infatuation resulted into the manifestation of my another Manas-putra named Kamadeva. His beauty and qualities were incomparable. His manifestation not only inflicted me with lust, but also my other Manasputras became filled up with lust. Kamadeva made…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 18, "quote": "5.2.24 The Manifestation of ‘Kama’ \"When I became infatuated by the divine beauty of Sandhya, my infatuation resulted into the manifestation of my another Manas-putra named Kamadeva. His beauty and qualities were incomparable"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-25"><data key="title">Brahma Curses Kamadeva</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After being named by lord Brahma as 'Pushpavan', Kamadeva was also given various names as Manmath, Kama, Madan etc by sages like Mareech, etc. They also said to him that he would be married to 'Rati' the daughter of Daksha. Kamadeva himself was infatuated with the beauty of Sandhya. To test the authenticity of…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 18, "quote": "5.2.25 Brahma Curses Kamadeva After being named by lord Brahma as 'Pushpavan', Kamadeva was also given various names as Manmath, Kama, Madan etc by sages like Mareech, etc. They also said to him that he would be married"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-26"><data key="title">Kama Married Rati</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Daksha requested Kamadeva to get married with his daughter - Rati. Kamadeva was very pleased at this proposal. Both Kamadeva and Rati got married.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 19, "quote": "5.2.26 Kama Married Rati Daksha requested Kamadeva to get married with his daughter - Rati. Kamadeva was very pleased at this proposal. Both Kamadeva and Rati got married."}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-27"><data key="title">Sandhya does Penance</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sandhya was very ashamed of herself. For the atonement of her sin she decided to do penance. She went to Chandrabhaga mountain and commenced her tremendous penance. Lord Brahma then instructed Vashishtha to go to her in disguise and help in getting her initiated. He went to Chandrabhaga mountain in the guise of a…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 19, "quote": "5.2.27 Sandhya does Penance Sandhya was very ashamed of herself. For the atonement of her sin she decided to do penance. She went to Chandrabhaga mountain and commenced her tremendous penance. Lord Brahma then instruct"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-28"><data key="title">Sandhya Receives a Boon from Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After sage Vashishtha who was disguised as brahmin went away. Sandhayd did penance as per his instructions. After the passing of one Chaturyuga, she had a darshan of lord Shiva. Shiva being pleased by her devotion asked her to demand anything. Sandhya said- "Nobody should have lust towards the member of his own clan.…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 19, "quote": "5.2.28 Sandhya Receives a Boon from Shiva After sage Vashishtha who was disguised as brahmin went away. Sandhayd did penance as per his instructions. After the passing of one Chaturyuga, she had a darshan of lord Shiva. Shiva being"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-29"><data key="title">Sandhya’s Birth &amp; her Marriage with Sage Vashistha</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When lord Shiva went away. Sandhya got up and went to the place where Medhatithi was performing his yagya. She made a mental resolution to have that brahmin as her husband, who in reality was vashishtha and then entered into the sacrificial fire. The fire of the yagya burnt her body and carried it ti the solar-system,…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 20, "quote": "5.2.29 Sandhya’s Birth &amp; her Marriage with Sage Vashistha When lord Shiva went away. Sandhya got up and went to the place where Medhatithi was performing his yagya. She made a mental resolution to have that brahmin as her husband, who in reality wa"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-30"><data key="title">Appearance of Spring Season (Vasant Ritu)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Brahmaji told Narada that since the day he was hermiliated by Shiva, he had grudges against him and wanted to take revenge. "I wanted to subdue the arrogance of Shiva by proving it to him that even he could be binded by attachment. I asked Daksha and sages like Mareech as to how this feat could be achieved. As a…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 20, "quote": "5.2.30 Appearance of Spring Season (Vasant Ritu) Brahmaji told Narada that since the day he was hermiliated by Shiva, he had grudges against him and wanted to take revenge. \"I wanted to subdue the arrogance of Shiva by proving it to him th"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-31"><data key="title">The Creation of Marganas and Kama’s Futile Attempts</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Kama tried his best to bring lord Shiva under his influence. All the living creatures were spell bound by his powers except lord Shiva and Ganesha. Kama returned back to Brahmaji ad told him about his unsuccessful attempts. Brahmaji sighed heavily. From his sighes were created the ferocious ganas. These ganas were…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 20, "quote": "5.2.31 The Creation of Marganas and Kama’s Futile Attempts Kama tried his best to bring lord Shiva under his influence. All the living creatures were spell bound by his powers except lord Shiva and Ganesha. Kama returned back to Brahmaji ad told him"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-32"><data key="title">Dailogue Between Brahma &amp; Vishnu</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Brahma then remembered Vishnuji, who appeared instantaneously. Brahmaji revealed about his intentions to him. LordVishnu told him that it was foolishness on his part to have enemity towards Shiva. But when lord Brahma kept on insisting, he revealed to him that this could be only achieved by the blessings of…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 20, "quote": "5.2.32 Dailogue Between Brahma &amp; Vishnu Lord Brahma then remembered Vishnuji, who appeared instantaneously. Brahmaji revealed about his intentions to him. LordVishnu told him that it was foolishness on his part to have enemity tow"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-33"><data key="title">Goddess Durga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After lord Vishnu went away, Lord Brahma started meditating on the form of goddess Durga. She appeared before him. Lord Brahma said- "I need your help in binding Shiva with your maya." Goddess Durga told Brahmaji that Lord Shiva in his incarnation of Rudra was beyond the reached of any kind of Maya. But when Brahmaji…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 20, "quote": "5.2.33 Goddess Durga After lord Vishnu went away, Lord Brahma started meditating on the form of goddess Durga. She appeared before him. Lord Brahma said-"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-34"><data key="title">Daksha Worships Devi</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">With the permission of lord Brahma, Daksha did penance for three thousand years. As a result goddess Jagdamba appeared before him. She blessed him by saying that she would take birth as his daughter and by her tremendous penance would attain Rudra as her husband. But she warned Daksha that if he showed any kind of…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 21, "quote": "5.2.34 Daksha Worships Devi With the permission of lord Brahma, Daksha did penance for three thousand years. As a result goddess Jagdamba appeared before him. She blessed him by saying that she would take birth as his"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-35"><data key="title">Daksha Curses Narad</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">With the permission of lord Brahma, Daksha Prajapati created many thing just by his mental resolution. But finding the absence of any kind of evolution and development in them, he went to Brahmaji to take his advice. Brahmaji instructed him to create by the help of copulation. Daksha Prajapati then married Asikti -…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 21, "quote": "5.2.35 Daksha Curses Narad With the permission of lord Brahma, Daksha Prajapati created many thing just by his mental resolution. But finding the absence of any kind of evolution and development in them, he went to Br"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-36"><data key="title">Birth of Sati</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When lord Brahma came to know about Daksha Prajapati's anger towards Narad he went to him and cooled him down. Subsequently sixty daughters were born to Daksha. He married his ten daughters with Dharma, thirteen daughters with Kasyap. Twentyseven daughters with Moon, two daughters with Bhutangiras, two daughters with…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 21, "quote": "5.2.36 Birth of Sati When lord Brahma came to know about Daksha Prajapati's anger towards Narad he went to him and cooled him down. Subsequently sixty daughters were born to Daksha. He married his ten daughters"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-37"><data key="title">Sati does Penance</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When Sati attained marriageable age, Daksha started worrying. Sati understood the reason behind her father's worried. She went to her Mother Virani and expressed her desire of marrying lord Shiva. Her mother made all the arrangements so that Sati could worship Shiva without any problem. Sati commenced her austerity…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 21, "quote": "5.2.37 Sati does Penance When Sati attained marriageable age, Daksha started worrying. Sati understood the reason behind her father's worried. She went to her Mother Virani and expressed her desire of marrying lord"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-38"><data key="title">Brahmaji makes a request to Lord Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">All the deities and the sages requested lord Shiva to get married, but Shiva did not want to curtail his freedom by getting married. When the deities insisted he told them to find a suitable match for him. Brahma and Vishnu told Shiva about the tremendous penance Uma was doing to him (Shiva) as her husband. They also…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 22, "quote": "5.2.38 Brahmaji makes a request to Lord Shiva All the deities and the sages requested lord Shiva to get married, but Shiva did not want to curtail his freedom by getting married. When the deities insisted he told them to find a suitable"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-39"><data key="title">Shiva Blesses Uma with a Boon</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After Uma had accomplished her penance named Nandavrat, lord Shiva appeared before her and asked her to demand anything. Uma because of her shyness could not say anything. Lord Shiva knew about her desire so he said- "You will have me as your husband." Uma's face beamed with joy, but she shyly requested lord Shiva to…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 22, "quote": "5.2.39 Shiva Blesses Uma with a Boon After Uma had accomplished her penance named Nandavrat, lord Shiva appeared before her and asked her to demand anything. Uma because of her shyness could not say anything. Lord Shiva knew ab"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-40"><data key="title">Shiva Married Sati</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva was anxiously waiting for his arrival. When Lord Brahma arrived he curiously asked about Daksha's response. When lord Brahma told him about Daksha's approval of the marriage. Shiva was very pleased at this. Lord Shiva proceeded towards Daksha's abode on the auspicious moment of Phalgun, Krishnapaksha and on…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 22, "quote": "5.2.40 Shiva Married Sati Lord Shiva was anxiously waiting for his arrival. When Lord Brahma arrived he curiously asked about Daksha's response. When lord Brahma told him about Daksha's approval of the marriage. Shiv"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-41"><data key="title">Lord Brahma Faces the Wrath of Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Daksha presented invaluable things to his daughter- Sati as dowry. Similarly the brahmins were presented with large quantities of wealth. Lord Brahma was enchanted by the divine beauty of Sati. With a desire to see her face, he put logs of wood which were wet, into the yagni kunda and poured ghee on them. As a result…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 22, "quote": "5.2.41 Lord Brahma Faces the Wrath of Shiva Daksha presented invaluable things to his daughter- Sati as dowry. Similarly the brahmins were presented with large quantities of wealth. Lord Brahma was enchanted by the divine beauty of Sa"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-42"><data key="title">Deformed Face of Brahma</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Though lord Shiva had forgiven Brahmaji, yet he instructed him to apologize by bowing down his head. Brahma did as he was instructed Lord Shiva then established on his head. Brahmaji became ashamed and asked as to how could he atone for his sins. Lord Shiva instructed Brahmaji that by worshipping him he could…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 22, "quote": "5.2.42 Deformed Face of Brahma Though lord Shiva had forgiven Brahmaji, yet he instructed him to apologize by bowing down his head. Brahma did as he was instructed Lord Shiva then established on his head. Brahmaji became"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-43"><data key="title">Shiva-Shakti as Husband &amp; Wife</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After reaching Kailash mountain, lord Shiva instructed all his ganas (attendants), not to disturb them (Shiva and Shakti). When all the ganas went away Shiva and Shakti enjoyed a blissful union for twenty five Deva-years.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 23, "quote": "5.2.43 Shiva-Shakti as Husband &amp; Wife After reaching Kailash mountain, lord Shiva instructed all his ganas (attendants), not to disturb them (Shiva and Shakti). When all the ganas went away Shiva and Shakti enjoyed a blissful un"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-44"><data key="title">Shiva-Shakti Depart for Himalya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When the rainy season arrived, Shakti requested Shiva to make a residence in such a place to that they could not be disturbed by rain etc. Lord Shiva smiled and said- "Dear Sati! The clouds and rain will not dare to disturb if you are present by my side, no matter whereever we live - even if we live on the peaks of…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 23, "quote": "5.2.44 Shiva-Shakti Depart for Himalya When the rainy season arrived, Shakti requested Shiva to make a residence in such a place to that they could not be disturbed by rain etc. Lord Shiva smiled and said- \"Dear Sati! The clouds"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-45"><data key="title">The Importance of Devotion in Kaliyuga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sati requested lord Shiva to enlighten her mind by giving discourses. Shiva revealed to her the importance of devotion in the Kaliyug. He said that the value of knowledge (gyan) and asceticism (vairagya) would diminish to the extent of extinction in the era of Kali and only devotion would help a man in attaining…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 23, "quote": "5.2.45 The Importance of Devotion in Kaliyuga Sati requested lord Shiva to enlighten her mind by giving discourses. Shiva revealed to her the importance of devotion in the Kaliyug. He said that the value of knowledge (gyan) and ascetici"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-46"><data key="title">Sati’s Doubt</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Devarshi Narad who was listening to the divine tales of Shiva, narrated by lord Brahma with rapt attention, requested him to describe about other characteristics of Shiva. Brahmaji said- "Once, lord Shiva accompanies by Sati, arrived at Dandak aranya, where Sri Ram was wandering in search of Sita, who had been…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 23, "quote": "5.2.46 Sati’s Doubt Devarshi Narad who was listening to the divine tales of Shiva, narrated by lord Brahma with rapt attention, requested him to describe about other characteristics of Shiva. Brahmaji said- \"On"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-47"><data key="title">Shiva does not see Sati as his consort</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, lord Shiva requested Vishwakarma to construct a grand palace and a magnificent throne for him. When everything was ready, Shiva invited all the deities for the crowning ceremony of lord Vishnu. Lord Vishnu was requested to be seated on the throne and was worshipped by all the deities including Shiva himself. He…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 23, "quote": "5.2.47 Shiva does not see Sati as his consort Once, lord Shiva requested Vishwakarma to construct a grand palace and a magnificent throne for him. When everything was ready, Shiva invited all the deities for the crowning ceremony of lor"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-48"><data key="title">Daksha’s Animosity towards Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, the sages organised a yagya at Prayaga, which was attended by all the deities. Lord Shiva too had come, accompanies by Sati. He was eulogized and worshipped by all the deities and the sages. Daksha arrived there and after making salutations to lord Brahma sat down without showing any kind of respect to Shiva.…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 24, "quote": "5.2.48 Daksha’s Animosity towards Shiva Once, the sages organised a yagya at Prayaga, which was attended by all the deities. Lord Shiva too had come, accompanies by Sati. He was eulogized and worshipped by all the deities and the"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-49"><data key="title">Daksha Performs Yagya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Daksha organised a yagya at 'Kanakhal' in which everybody except Shiva and Sati were invited. Sage Dadhichi arrived and not finding the seat for Shiva went back, saying that it was impossible to accomplish a yagya without Shiva. Then Daksha commenced the yagya with the assistance of other sages who were present there.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 24, "quote": "5.2.49 Daksha Performs Yagya Daksha organised a yagya at 'Kanakhal' in which everybody except Shiva and Sati were invited. Sage Dadhichi arrived and not finding the seat for Shiva went back, saying that it was impossibl"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-50"><data key="title">Sati’s Arrival at the Oblation Site</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When Sati saw all the deities going happily, she became curious to know where they were going. She requested her companion to ask the moon as to where he was going in such a cheerful mood. The moon told her companion that they were going to attend the yagya ceremony, being organised by Daksha. Sati was very surprised…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 24, "quote": "5.2.50 Sati’s Arrival at the Oblation Site When Sati saw all the deities going happily, she became curious to know where they were going. She requested her companion to ask the moon as to where he was going in such a cheerful mood. T"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-51"><data key="title">Sati’s Dishonor</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When Sati reached, where Daksha was performing his yagya, she met her mother and sisters. They met her with all the respect and honour. But Daksha did not even caste a glance at her. Imitating him many other people did the same. But Sati not bothering about the disrespect shown to her made salutations to both her…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 25, "quote": "5.2.51 Sati’s Dishonor When Sati reached, where Daksha was performing his yagya, she met her mother and sisters. They met her with all the respect and honour. But Daksha did not even caste a glance at her. Imitati"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-52"><data key="title">Sati Gives Up Her Life</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Sati gives up her life at Daksha’s sacrifice after the insult to Shiva; the following episodes narrate Virabhadra and the destruction of the sacrifice.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 25, "quote": "5.2.52 Sati Gives Up Her Life Sati then sat in padmasan and closed her yes. By her yogic powers she united with Shiva and her lifeless body fell into the yagya-kunda. This incident shocked everybody and the rudraganas fu"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-53"><data key="title">Manifestation of Veerbharda &amp; Mahakali</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When Nandi informed Shiva about Sati's death, he became very furious. He angrily pulled out a locks of hair and dashed it against the mountain, which got divided into two parts. From one part manifested the valiant Veerbharda and from the other part Mahakali. Veerbhadra and Mahakali were instructed by Shiva to destroy…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 25, "quote": "5.2.53 Manifestation of Veerbharda &amp; Mahakali When Nandi informed Shiva about Sati's death, he became very furious. He angrily pulled out a locks of hair and dashed it against the mountain, which got divided into two parts. From one par"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-54"><data key="title">Veerbhadra &amp; Kali Assemble their Army</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After getting the instructions from lord Shiva, Veerbhardra marched with a huge army which included the mighty Shivaganas like Dakini, Bhairav and Kapalish etc. Goddess Kali to joined him with her army which consisted of all her nine incarnations like Katyayani etc. As the army marched on numerous auspicious signs…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 25, "quote": "5.2.54 Veerbhadra &amp; Kali Assemble their Army After getting the instructions from lord Shiva, Veerbhardra marched with a huge army which included the mighty Shivaganas like Dakini, Bhairav and Kapalish etc. Goddess Kali to joined him wi"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-55"><data key="title">Daksha Experiences Inauspicious Signs</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">On the other hand Daksha experienced many inauspicious signs. His left eye, left army and left thigh started throbbing. He saw the vultures flying over his head. He heard the sounds of jackals howling.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 25, "quote": "5.2.55 Daksha Experiences Inauspicious Signs On the other hand Daksha experienced many inauspicious signs. His left eye, left army and left thigh started throbbing. He saw the vultures flying over his head. He heard the sounds of jacka"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-56"><data key="title">Lord Vishnu Preaches Daksha</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Daksha then prayed to lord Vishnu to rescue him from the imminent wrath of lord Shiva. Lord Vishnu preached Daksha and said- "Daksha! Since you have committed the greatest sin by dishonouring Shiva and Sati. Even I can not prevent the calamities which you are certain to face." As lord Vishnu was preaching Daksha…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 25, "quote": "5.2.56 Lord Vishnu Preaches Daksha Daksha then prayed to lord Vishnu to rescue him from the imminent wrath of lord Shiva. Lord Vishnu preached Daksha and said-"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-57"><data key="title">Daksha Gets his Head Severed</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A terrible battle was fought between the armies of Veerbhadra and the deities. The deities got defeated and fled away. They went to lord Vishnu and sought his help. Lord Vishnu decided to fight on the side of the deities. The battle recommenced for the second time. Lord Vishnu having a dual fight with Veerbhadra and…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 26, "quote": "5.2.57 Daksha Gets his Head Severed A terrible battle was fought between the armies of Veerbhadra and the deities. The deities got defeated and fled away. They went to lord Vishnu and sought his help. Lord Vishnu decided to fi"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-58"><data key="title">Contention between Kshuva &amp; Dadhichi</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Narad, who was listening to the divine tales of Shiva with rapt attention, was very curious to know about the reasons why lord Vishnu attended a yagya where Shiva was not invited and why did he fight a battle against veerbhadra despite knowing about his invincibility. Lord Brahma told him that all this happened due to…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 26, "quote": "5.2.58 Contention between Kshuva &amp; Dadhichi Narad, who was listening to the divine tales of Shiva with rapt attention, was very curious to know about the reasons why lord Vishnu attended a yagya where Shiva was not invited and why did"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-59"><data key="title">Battle between Vishnu &amp; Dadhichi</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lord Vishnu then visited the hermitage of Sage Dadhichi, disguised as a brahmin. On being asked by Dadhichi about the purpose of his arrival, he said that he had come with a desire of a boon on his heart. Sage Dadhichi, by his yogic power came to know about the real identity of a 'Brahmin'. He told that he had been…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 27, "quote": "5.2.59 Battle between Vishnu &amp; Dadhichi Lord Vishnu then visited the hermitage of Sage Dadhichi, disguised as a brahmin. On being asked by Dadhichi about the purpose of his arrival, he said that he had come with a desire of a boon"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-60"><data key="title">The Deities go to Lord Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The deities after being defeated by Veerdbharda went to Brahmaloka and narrated everything about the destruction of Daksha's yagya and also how his head was severed by Veerbharda. Brahmaji became very sad. To bring Daksha back to life and to accomplish the still unfinished yagya, he went to lord Vishnu to take his…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 27, "quote": "5.2.60 The Deities go to Lord Shiva The deities after being defeated by Veerdbharda went to Brahmaloka and narrated everything about the destruction of Daksha's yagya and also how his head was severed by Veerbharda. Brahmaji b"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-61"><data key="title">Daksha becomes Alive</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva became very pleased and agreed to make Daksha alive. All of them went to Kanakhal-the place where Daksha had organised the yagya ceremony. Veerbhadra too accompanied them. When they reached the site, Lord Shiva could not hold his laughter after seeing the destruction of the site. Anyway, he joined the head…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 27, "quote": "5.2.61 Daksha becomes Alive Lord Shiva became very pleased and agreed to make Daksha alive. All of them went to Kanakhal-the place where Daksha had organised the yagya ceremony. Veerbhadra too accompanied them. When th"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-62"><data key="title">Daksha Organizes Yagya once again</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Becoming pleased by the invocation and eulogy of the deities, Lord Shiva preached Daksha in the following way- "The person who has the right knowledge (gyani) is the supreme among all human beings. An action which is performed in one's ignorance and jealousy does not liberate a man from his world bondages. O Daksha…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 27, "quote": "5.2.62 Daksha Organizes Yagya once again Becoming pleased by the invocation and eulogy of the deities, Lord Shiva preached Daksha in the following way-"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-63"><data key="title">Parvat Khand: Himalya Marries Maina</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Narada requests lord Brahma to shed light on the birth of Maina and also too she was married with Himalaya. Brahmaji Said- "After relinquishing her body, Sati - the daughter of Daksha attained to the abode of Shiva. In her next birth she was born to Maina - the wife of Himalaya and was known as Parvati. Maina had done…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 28, "quote": "5.2.63 Parvat Khand: Himalya Marries Maina Narada requests lord Brahma to shed light on the birth of Maina and also too she was married with Himalaya. Brahmaji Said- \"After relinquishing her body, Sati - the daughter of Daksha attain"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-64"><data key="title">Maina gets Liberated from the Curse</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Daksha had Sixty daughters, 'Swadha' was one of them and was married to the Pitras. In due course of time three daughters were born to them - Maina, Dhanya and Kalawati. Once all the three of them went to Swetadweepa to have a darshan of lord Vishnu. Right then, sages like Sanak, Sanadan etc arrived there. Everybody…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 28, "quote": "5.2.64 Maina gets Liberated from the Curse Daksha had Sixty daughters, 'Swadha' was one of them and was married to the Pitras. In due course of time three daughters were born to them - Maina, Dhanya and Kalawati. Once all the three o"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-65"><data key="title">Invocation of Ua</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Brahmaji told Narad that after getting married with Maina, Himalaya enjoyed a happy married life for a long time. Once, lord Vishnu paid a visit to his place, accompanied by all the deities. Himalaya was very pleased by his arrival. After making salutations, he asked for the purpose of their visit. The deities…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 28, "quote": "5.2.65 Invocation of Ua Brahmaji told Narad that after getting married with Maina, Himalaya enjoyed a happy married life for a long time. Once, lord Vishnu paid a visit to his place, accompanied by all the deities."}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-66"><data key="title">Goddess Uma Consoles the Deities</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Being pleased by the invocation made by the deities Goddess Uma assured the deities about her arrival in this world. She told that her incarnation would take place in the house of Himalaya and by the virtues of her tremendous penance she would get lord Shiva as her husband. She also told the deities that she was…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 29, "quote": "5.2.66 Goddess Uma Consoles the Deities Being pleased by the invocation made by the deities Goddess Uma assured the deities about her arrival in this world. She told that her incarnation would take place in the house of Himalaya a"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-67"><data key="title">Maina and Himalya Commence Penance</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Himalaya and Maina commenced their penance with the objective of getting Uma as their daughter. Maina did a tremendous penance which lasted for twenty-seven years. Goddess uma became very pleased by her penance. She appeared before her and asked her to demand anything she wished for. Maina expressed her desire of…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 29, "quote": "5.2.67 Maina and Himalya Commence Penance Himalaya and Maina commenced their penance with the objective of getting Uma as their daughter. Maina did a tremendous penance which lasted for twenty-seven years. Goddess uma became very pl"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-68"><data key="title">Birth of Parvati</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Himalaya and Maina engaged themselves in the worship of Shiva and Shakti, day and night. After some days Parvati manifested herself by taking birth. After her birth the whole mountaneous region of Himalaya became illuminated by her radiance.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 29, "quote": "5.2.68 Birth of Parvati Himalaya and Maina engaged themselves in the worship of Shiva and Shakti, day and night. After some days Parvati manifested herself by taking birth. After her birth the whole mountaneous reg"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-69"><data key="title">Divine Childhood Plays of Parvati</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After taking her birth, Parvati gradually started growing up. Varioua names given to her like Girja, Uma, and Jagdamba. Himalaya and Maina felt proud of their fate, which had made them the parents of an incarnation. Seeing the childhood plays of Uma, their hearts knew no bounds. One day sage Narad arrived there…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 29, "quote": "5.2.69 Divine Childhood Plays of Parvati After taking her birth, Parvati gradually started growing up. Varioua names given to her like Girja, Uma, and Jagdamba. Himalaya and Maina felt proud of their fate, which had made them the p"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-70"><data key="title">The Dreams of Parvati and Himalya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When Uma had attained the marriagable age, Maina requested her husband a suitable bridegroom for her. Himalya told her that the words of Narada would never go in vain hence Uma should be asked to do penance, so that she could have Rudra as her husband. But Maina was disinclined in forcing her tender daughter - Parvati…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 29, "quote": "5.2.70 The Dreams of Parvati and Himalya When Uma had attained the marriagable age, Maina requested her husband a suitable bridegroom for her. Himalya told her that the words of Narada would never go in vain hence Uma should be ask"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-71"><data key="title">The Birth of ‘Bhaum’-Mars</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, when the ganas of Shiva praised the glory of mother Sati, lord Shiva became overjoyed just like an ordinary human being. In his joy, he travelled all around the three world without any clothes on his body. He returned back to Kailash and went into meditation. While he was engrossed in his Samadhi, three drops of…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 30, "quote": "5.2.71 The Birth of ‘Bhaum’-Mars Once, when the ganas of Shiva praised the glory of mother Sati, lord Shiva became overjoyed just like an ordinary human being. In his joy, he travelled all around the three world without any"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-72"><data key="title">Shiva Arrives at Himalya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">One day lord Shiva accompanied by his ganas like Bhringi, Nandi, etc, arrived at Himalaya, with the purpose of doing penance. When Parvati father Himalaya came to know about his arrival, he went to receive him. After he had made his salutations to Shiva, he was instructed by Shiva to see that he is not disturbed while…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 30, "quote": "5.2.72 Shiva Arrives at Himalya One day lord Shiva accompanied by his ganas like Bhringi, Nandi, etc, arrived at Himalaya, with the purpose of doing penance. When Parvati father Himalaya came to know about his arrival, he"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-73"><data key="title">Conversation between Parvati &amp; Shiva</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When Parvati saw her father becoming worried by Shiva's response she decided to intervene. She said to lord Shiva- "I am 'Prakriti' (Nature) and you are the 'Purusha' (almighty). You exist in the 'Sagun' form (with form) because of me. In my absence, you will find it impossible even to exist." Lord Shiva was impressed…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 30, "quote": "5.2.73 Conversation between Parvati &amp; Shiva When Parvati saw her father becoming worried by Shiva's response she decided to intervene. She said to lord Shiva- \"I am 'Prakriti' (Nature) and you are the 'Purusha' (almighty). You exist i"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-74"><data key="title">The Birth of Vajrang</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Narada was curious to know about Tarakasur. He asked Brahmaji to narrate his tale. Brahmaji said- "Kashyap-the son of Marichi, had thirteen wives Diti was the eldest among them. She was the mother of Hiranyakashipu and Hiranyaksha. Both her sons were killed by lord Vishnu in his incarnations of Nrisimha and Varaha…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 31, "quote": "5.2.74 The Birth of Vajrang Narada was curious to know about Tarakasur. He asked Brahmaji to narrate his tale. Brahmaji said- \"Kashyap-the son of Marichi, had thirteen wives Diti was the eldest among them. She was the"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-75"><data key="title">Birth of Tarakasur and his Penance</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Varangi gave birth to Tarakasur- the valiant and brave demon. During the time of his birth , the world was affected by inauspicious events like earthquakes, cyclones etc, His name Tarakasur was given by Kashyap. After he grew up, Tarakasur went to Madhuvan to do penance. His tremendous penance scarred the deities.…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 31, "quote": "5.2.75 Birth of Tarakasur and his Penance Varangi gave birth to Tarakasur- the valiant and brave demon. During the time of his birth , the world was affected by inauspicious events like earthquakes, cyclones etc, His name Tarakasur"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-76"><data key="title">Tarakasur Relinquishes Heaven on the Advice of Lord Brahma</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After being driven away from the heaven, the deities went to lord Brahma to seek his help. They asked him as to how they could get rid of this menace called Tarakasur. Brahmaji revealed to the deities that Tarakasur could be killed only by such a person, whose parents are Shiva and Parvati. He also advised them to…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 32, "quote": "5.2.76 Tarakasur Relinquishes Heaven on the Advice of Lord Brahma After being driven away from the heaven, the deities went to lord Brahma to seek his help. They asked him as to how they could get rid of this menace called Tarakasur. Brahmaji revealed to t"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-77"><data key="title">Kamadeva Sent to Lord Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Brahma told Narada that the deities decided to send Kamadeva to influence lord Shiva so that the marriage between him and Parvati is felicitated. Indra called Kamadeva and told him that the demon king Tarakasur could be killed only by such a person who was the son of Shiva and Parvati. Indra instructed Kamadeva…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 32, "quote": "5.2.77 Kamadeva Sent to Lord Shiva Lord Brahma told Narada that the deities decided to send Kamadeva to influence lord Shiva so that the marriage between him and Parvati is felicitated. Indra called Kamadeva and told him that"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-78"><data key="title">Efforts of Kamadeva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After reaching the place where lord Shiva was engrossed in his meditation, Kamadeva made repeated attempts to arouse passion in the heart of lord Shiva, but his actions were no avail. Right then, Kamadeva saw Parvati arriving accompanied by her companions. She was looking divine in her beauty. Just at that moment lord…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 32, "quote": "5.2.78 Efforts of Kamadeva After reaching the place where lord Shiva was engrossed in his meditation, Kamadeva made repeated attempts to arouse passion in the heart of lord Shiva, but his actions were no avail. Right"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-79"><data key="title">Kamadeva Reduces to Ashes</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shiva’s fiery response reduces Kamadeva to ashes during the attempt to awaken his desire. The episode belongs to the larger Parvati marriage sequence.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 32, "quote": "5.2.79 Kamadeva Reduces to Ashes Lord Shiva looked all around him. He saw kamadeva standing towards his left side, with a bow and arrows in his hands. Now he was fully convinceed that it was indeed an act of Kamadeva. Kamad"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-80"><data key="title">The Anger of Shiva Subsides</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva's anger did not subside after the death of Kamadeva and the whole world started to feel the wrath of lord Shiva's fury. All the living creatures became terrified. They went to lord Brahma and prayed to him, to save them from Shiva's wrath. Lord Brahma went to lord Shiva and conveyed their request to him.…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 33, "quote": "5.2.80 The Anger of Shiva Subsides Lord Shiva's anger did not subside after the death of Kamadeva and the whole world started to feel the wrath of lord Shiva's fury. All the living creatures became terrified. They went to lor"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-81"><data key="title">Narada Preaches Parvati</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When Parvati reached her home, she became very sad as she was unable to bear the sorrow of Shiva's separation. Sage Narada arrived there. Her father Himalaya narrated the whole story to him Sage Narada then gave the five lettered mantra - "OM NAMAH SHIVAJ' to her and he also instructed her to do penance. Parvati heart…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 33, "quote": "5.2.81 Narada Preaches Parvati When Parvati reached her home, she became very sad as she was unable to bear the sorrow of Shiva's separation. Sage Narada arrived there. Her father Himalaya narrated the whole story to him"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-82"><data key="title">Parvati does Penance</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After taking the permission of her parents and relinquishing all of her ornaments and royal apparels, Parvati went to the same place where Lord Shiva himself had done penance. This sacred place was situated at the Himalayas, from where the holy Ganges originated. Parvati companions too had accompanied her. Parvati…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 33, "quote": "5.2.82 Parvati does Penance After taking the permission of her parents and relinquishing all of her ornaments and royal apparels, Parvati went to the same place where Lord Shiva himself had done penance. This sacred pl"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-83"><data key="title">The Deities go to Lord Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Parvati's power created such heat in the atmosphere that the whole world started to burn. All the deities &amp; sages went to lord Brahma and told him about the effects, Parvati's penance was having on all the three worlds. Lord Brahma accompanied by all of them went to Vishnuji. They visited the place where Parvati was…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 33, "quote": "5.2.83 The Deities go to Lord Shiva Parvati's power created such heat in the atmosphere that the whole world started to burn. All the deities &amp; sages went to lord Brahma and told him about the effects, Parvati's penance was ha"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-84"><data key="title">Salutations Gives His Approval</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva enquired about the purpose of their arrival. Lord Vishnu then revealed to him that how distressed and tormented were the deities by the activities of the demon - Tarakasur. He also told Shiva that he could be killed by such a person, who is born out of the parentage of Shiva and Parvati. Lord Vishnu then…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 34, "quote": "5.2.84 Salutations Gives His Approval Lord Shiva enquired about the purpose of their arrival. Lord Vishnu then revealed to him that how distressed and tormented were the deities by the activities of the demon - Tarakasur. He als"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-85"><data key="title">Parvati’s Love for Shiva Tested by the Sapta-Rishis</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After the deities went back, lord Shiva summoned the Saptarishis (Vashishth etc) and instructed them to test Parvati's love for him. The Saptarishis went to Parvati and tested her resolve to marry lord Shiva. They tried to deter her by all means, but Parvati was firm in her resolve. They went back to lord Shiva and…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 34, "quote": "5.2.85 Parvati’s Love for Shiva Tested by the Sapta-Rishis After the deities went back, lord Shiva summoned the Saptarishis (Vashishth etc) and instructed them to test Parvati's love for him. The Saptarishis went to Parvati and tested her resolve to"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-86"><data key="title">Lord Shiva Tests Parvati</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva then himself went to Parvati in the guise of a brahmin. Parvati on seeing a brahmin welcomed her with full honour. Shiva asked Parvati as to why was she doing penance. Parvati told him that she wanted to have Shiva as her husband. Lord Shiva, who was in the guise of a brahmin started cursing Shiva to see…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 34, "quote": "5.2.86 Lord Shiva Tests Parvati Lord Shiva then himself went to Parvati in the guise of a brahmin. Parvati on seeing a brahmin welcomed her with full honour. Shiva asked Parvati as to why was she doing penance. Parvati tol"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-87"><data key="title">Parvati’s Reply</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Parvati told the brahmin; who in reality was Shiva himself that, she wanted to have Shiva as her husband at any cost. She said- "You say that lord Shiva does not possess anything - not even wealth. He does not put on clothes on his body. You also say that he is not fit to be a bridegroom of me. But all of your…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 34, "quote": "5.2.87 Parvati’s Reply Parvati told the brahmin; who in reality was Shiva himself that, she wanted to have Shiva as her husband at any cost. She said- \"You say that lord Shiva does not possess anything - not even"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-88"><data key="title">Shiva Reveals his True Identity</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Parvati continuing with her statements said that Shiva was the most capable deity in all the three world. "There is no sin greater than condemning Shiva." Said Parvati. As Shiva, who was in the guise of Brahmin was about to say something Parvati said to one of her companion- "This condemner should be killed, if this…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 34, "quote": "5.2.88 Shiva Reveals his True Identity Parvati continuing with her statements said that Shiva was the most capable deity in all the three world. \"There is no sin greater than condemning Shiva.\" Said Parvati. As Shiva, who was in"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-89"><data key="title">Shiva Demands Parvati as his Consort</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Parvati, after successfully accomplishing her penance came back to her home. Everybody was happy at her arrival. After sometime Himalaya went out to take his bath in the river Ganges. Meanwhile lord Shiva arrived in his appearance of Nataraj and started dancing in front of Parvati's mother - Maina. She was so pleased…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 35, "quote": "5.2.89 Shiva Demands Parvati as his Consort Parvati, after successfully accomplishing her penance came back to her home. Everybody was happy at her arrival. After sometime Himalaya went out to take his bath in the river Ganges. Meanwh"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-90"><data key="title">Shiva’s Delusionary Power</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Indra and the other deities became afraid when they saw both Himalaya and Maina having deep devotion towards lord Shiva. They apprehended that if Himalaya happily agrees to marry Parvati with Shiva, then he would no longer live on the earth- He would attain salvation. His departure would make the earth devoid of…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 35, "quote": "5.2.90 Shiva’s Delusionary Power Indra and the other deities became afraid when they saw both Himalaya and Maina having deep devotion towards lord Shiva. They apprehended that if Himalaya happily agrees to marry Parvati wit"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-91"><data key="title">Shiva Instructs Sapta-Rishis to Convince Maina &amp; Himalya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva summoned the Saptarishis and instructed them to convince Maina and Himalaya to marry Parvati with him, so that Tarakasur could be killed. He feared that his actions would have created many misconceptions in their minds. The Saptarishis went to Himalaya and tried to make him, so that Tarakasur could be…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 35, "quote": "5.2.91 Shiva Instructs Sapta-Rishis to Convince Maina &amp; Himalya Lord Shiva summoned the Saptarishis and instructed them to convince Maina and Himalaya to marry Parvati with him, so that Tarakasur could be killed. He feared that his actions would have cre"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-92"><data key="title">Anaranya, The King</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There was a king named Anaranya, who belonged to the lineage of fourteenth Manu-Indrasavarni. He was a great devotee of lord Shiva. He had five queens from whom one hundred sons and a very beautiful daughter named Padma were born. When Padma grew up, the king started looking for a suitable match. One day while Padma…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">36</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 36, "quote": "5.2.92 Anaranya, The King There was a king named Anaranya, who belonged to the lineage of fourteenth Manu-Indrasavarni. He was a great devotee of lord Shiva. He had five queens from whom one hundred sons and a very b"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-93"><data key="title">Padma and Pippalad</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">On the request of Himalayas, Sage Vashishth who was one of the Saptarishis narrated the story of Padma the princess and sage Pippalad- "At the time of his marriage sage Pippalad was old and weak, but still Padma devotely performed the duties of a faithful wife. To test her faithfulness towards her husband, Dharmaraj…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">36</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 36, "quote": "5.2.93 Padma and Pippalad On the request of Himalayas, Sage Vashishth who was one of the Saptarishis narrated the story of Padma the princess and sage Pippalad- \"At the time of his marriage sage Pippalad was old and"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-94"><data key="title">Himalya Sends Lagna-Patrika</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Himalaya then requested sage Garga to prepare a beautiful Lagna-Patrikas which were then sent to his near and dear ones. He then requested Vishwakarma to construct a beautiful 'mandap' for the marriage, which was constructed by him in a very short time.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">37</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 37, "quote": "5.2.94 Himalya Sends Lagna-Patrika Himalaya then requested sage Garga to prepare a beautiful Lagna-Patrikas which were then sent to his near and dear ones. He then requested Vishwakarma to construct a beautiful 'mandap' for t"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-95"><data key="title">Lord Shiva Invites the Deities</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva invited all of his Ganas to the marriage ceremony. He also instructed sage Narada to intimate all the deities, sages and the celestial entities. After getting the invitation from Shiva everybody started making preparations to be a part of Shiva's marriage-procession. The seven mothers- Brahmi, Maheshwari,…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">37</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 37, "quote": "5.2.95 Lord Shiva Invites the Deities Lord Shiva invited all of his Ganas to the marriage ceremony. He also instructed sage Narada to intimate all the deities, sages and the celestial entities. After getting the invitation from"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-96"><data key="title">Lord Shiva’s Marriage Procession</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Shiva proceeded with his marriage procession which considered of crores of his 'Ganas' and deities like Vishnu who were boarded on their vehicles. Even Narad and Lord Brahma were present in his (Shiva's) marriage procession. First of all, Shiva sent Narada to Himalaya's house to inform him about their (marriage…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">37</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 37, "quote": "5.2.96 Lord Shiva’s Marriage Procession Shiva proceeded with his marriage procession which considered of crores of his 'Ganas' and deities like Vishnu who were boarded on their vehicles. Even Narad and Lord Brahma were present in"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-97"><data key="title">Shiva Divine Plays</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When Maina saw that the marriage procession was coming she curiously told Narad about her desire to see her son in law. Shiva understood the arrongance which her desire contained. He wanted to teach her a lesson. He sent all the deities one by one. Maina mistook each one of them to be Shiva, but was later informed by…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">37</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 37, "quote": "5.2.97 Shiva Divine Plays When Maina saw that the marriage procession was coming she curiously told Narad about her desire to see her son in law. Shiva understood the arrongance which her desire contained. He wanted"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-98"><data key="title">Maina’s Wail</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The maid servants arrived instantaneously and helped Maina to regain her consciousness. She started crying and cursing everybody. She thought was responsible for her daughter's marriage with Shiva. Nobody was spared- Narad, the Saptarishis and even her own sons. She even admonished Parvati by saying. "Did you do…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">37</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 37, "quote": "5.2.98 Maina’s Wail The maid servants arrived instantaneously and helped Maina to regain her consciousness. She started crying and cursing everybody. She thought was responsible for her daughter's marriage with"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-99"><data key="title">Himalya Bestows Parvati to Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After the marriage ceremony was over, Sage Garg helped Himalaya in performing the rite of Kanyadan, amidst the chantings of vedic mantras. The women's were singing auspicious songs. Himalaya presented large quantities of dowry to Shiva. Parvati's companions were engaged with Shiva in jocular and humorous conversation.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">38</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 38, "quote": "5.2.99 Himalya Bestows Parvati to Shiva After the marriage ceremony was over, Sage Garg helped Himalaya in performing the rite of Kanyadan, amidst the chantings of vedic mantras. The women's were singing auspicious songs. Himalaya"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-100"><data key="title">Shiva Brings Back Kamadeva Alive</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Rati- the wife of Kamadeva, seeing the moment opportune, arrived and requested Shiva to bring back her dead husband back to life. Her wailings made the other goddesses very sympathetic towards her. They too requested Shiva to make her dead husband back to life. Feeling pity on her condition. Shiva brought back…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">38</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 38, "quote": "5.2.100 Shiva Brings Back Kamadeva Alive Rati- the wife of Kamadeva, seeing the moment opportune, arrived and requested Shiva to bring back her dead husband back to life. Her wailings made the other goddesses very sympathetic towar"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-101"><data key="title">Shiva’s Departure</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shiva remained at his in-laws house for many days. One day he sought the permission of Himalaya to go back. Everybody became sad at the prospect of Shiva's departure. Especially Maina who was very saddened by this news. At last it was time for Shiva to depart for Kailash, accompanied by Parvati. Lord Shiva and Parvati…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">38</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 38, "quote": "5.2.101 Shiva’s Departure Shiva remained at his in-laws house for many days. One day he sought the permission of Himalaya to go back. Everybody became sad at the prospect of Shiva's departure. Especially Maina who wa"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-102"><data key="title">Kartikeya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sage Vishwamitra arrived at the spot where the child had manifested. On the repeated insistence of the child, Vishwamitra performed his purification rites and named him 'Guha'. The divine child blessed Vishwamitra and bestowed divine knowledge to him. He also blessed him to become famous as 'Brahmarshi'. Agnideva…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">39</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 39, "quote": "5.2.102 Kartikeya Sage Vishwamitra arrived at the spot where the child had manifested. On the repeated insistence of the child, Vishwamitra performed his purification rites and named him 'Guha'. The divine ch"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-103"><data key="title">Search for Kartikeya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">One day, Parvati curiously asked Shiva about his semen which had fallen on the ground. Lord Shiva summoned the deities and asked them about it. The deities narrated the whole story. Both Shiva and Parvati were very pleased to know about Kartikeya. Lord Shiva then ordered his Ganas to bring Kartikeya from the…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">39</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 39, "quote": "5.2.103 Search for Kartikeya One day, Parvati curiously asked Shiva about his semen which had fallen on the ground. Lord Shiva summoned the deities and asked them about it. The deities narrated the whole story. Both Shi"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-104"><data key="title">Kartikeya’s Coronation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">At the time or Kartikeya's departure, the mothers had become very emotional. Kartikeya consoled them. He then boarded the beautiful chariot which had been sent by Parvati and proceeded towards Kailash mountain. All the deities including Shiva were eagerly awaiting his arrival. When Kartikeya reached Kailash mountain,…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">39</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 39, "quote": "5.2.104 Kartikeya’s Coronation At the time or Kartikeya's departure, the mothers had become very emotional. Kartikeya consoled them. He then boarded the beautiful chariot which had been sent by Parvati and proceeded towar"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-105"><data key="title">Kartikeya’s Amazing Character</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Kartikeya narrated a story connected with himself:- "One day a brahmin, whose name was Narad came to me and requested me to help him find his he- goat which had got lost. He told me that the goat was supposed to be offered as the sacrifice. "If the goat was not found, my vow would remain unfulfilled." Said Narad I…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">40</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 40, "quote": "5.2.105 Kartikeya’s Amazing Character Kartikeya narrated a story connected with himself:- \"One day a brahmin, whose name was Narad came to me and requested me to help him find his he- goat which had got lost. He told me that the"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-106"><data key="title">The Battle</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The deities were very encouraged by the presence of Kartikeya amidst themselves. They were beaming with confidence and enthusiasm. The deities assembled at the seashore. Tarakasur arrived their with his huge army. The battle began. Tarakasur appeared to be invincible. His blow was so severe that Indra fell down…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">40</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 40, "quote": "5.2.106 The Battle The deities were very encouraged by the presence of Kartikeya amidst themselves. They were beaming with confidence and enthusiasm. The deities assembled at the seashore. Tarakasur arrived th"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-107"><data key="title">Killing of Tarakasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">On being instructed by lord Brahma Kartikeya came forward to fight with Tarakasur. Tarakasur ridiculed the deities for taking the shield of a small child. "If he gets killed by me, the responsibility should lie on you." Thundered Tarakasur. A fierce battle started between both of them in which both of them got…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">40</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 40, "quote": "5.2.107 Killing of Tarakasur On being instructed by lord Brahma Kartikeya came forward to fight with Tarakasur. Tarakasur ridiculed the deities for taking the shield of a small child. \"If he gets killed by me, the respo"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-108"><data key="title">Killings of Pralamb &amp; Banasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The mountain - Kraunch, who was tormented by the activities of the demon named Banasur, went to Kartikeya and narrated his woeful tales. Kartikeya threw his weapon - Shakti in the direction which Banasur lived. The weapon banged right on target and returned back to him. Bamnasur was burnt to ashes. Kraunch went back…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">40</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 40, "quote": "5.2.108 Killings of Pralamb &amp; Banasur The mountain - Kraunch, who was tormented by the activities of the demon named Banasur, went to Kartikeya and narrated his woeful tales. Kartikeya threw his weapon - Shakti in the direction"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-109"><data key="title">Ganesh</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once Narada had a desire to listen to the tales of Ganesha. Brahmaji replied- "There were various Ganeshas in different Kalpas. During the period of 'Shweta-Kalp', Ganesha was born to Shiva and Parvati, when they went to Kailash mountain shortly after their marriage. "Once while going to take her bath, Parvati…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">41</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 41, "quote": "5.2.109 Ganesh Once Narada had a desire to listen to the tales of Ganesha. Brahmaji replied- \"There were various Ganeshas in different Kalpas. During the period of 'Shweta-Kalp', Ganesha was born to Shiva"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-110"><data key="title">Ganesha Defeats The Shivaganas &amp; The Deities</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The ganas of Shiva attacked Ganesha but all of them were no match for him. After being defeated by Ganesha, the Shivaganas went to Shiva and narrated everything. While the Shivaganas were narrating their stories, Lord Brahma, Lord Vishnu and some other deities arrived there. Lord Brahma then went to Ganesha to…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">41</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 41, "quote": "5.2.110 Ganesha Defeats The Shivaganas &amp; The Deities The ganas of Shiva attacked Ganesha but all of them were no match for him. After being defeated by Ganesha, the Shivaganas went to Shiva and narrated everything. While the Shivaganas were na"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-111"><data key="title">Shiva Severes Ganesh’s Head</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shiva severs Ganesha’s head in the conflict at Parvati’s doorway. The next section tells of Ganesha’s restoration.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">41</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 41, "quote": "5.2.111 Shiva Severes Ganesh’s Head A fierce battle was fought between Shiva and Ganesha. When Lord Shiva realized that Ganesha was dominating the fight, he severed his head with his trishul."}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-112"><data key="title">Ganesh Becomes Alive</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Parvati became extremely furious at the death of Ganesha. Her anger resulted into the manifestation of innumerable goddesses, who started creating havoc on the deities. The deities became frightened and they were forced to take the refuge of Parvati. They eulogized her and requested to be pardoned. Parvati told them…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">41</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 41, "quote": "5.2.112 Ganesh Becomes Alive Parvati became extremely furious at the death of Ganesha. Her anger resulted into the manifestation of innumerable goddesses, who started creating havoc on the deities. The deities became fr"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-113"><data key="title">Ganesha’s Marriage</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Both Kartikeya and Ganesha grew up to become handsome youths in due course of time. Shiva and Parvati started to think about their marriage. When Ganesha and Kartikeya came to know about the plan of their marriage both of them started quarreling among themselves as to who should get married first. Lord Shiva and…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">42</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 42, "quote": "5.2.113 Ganesha’s Marriage Both Kartikeya and Ganesha grew up to become handsome youths in due course of time. Shiva and Parvati started to think about their marriage. When Ganesha and Kartikeya came to know about the"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-114"><data key="title">Yudh-Khand: Tripurasur- The Three Demon</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Narada requested lord Brahma to narrate how lord Shiva annihilated the demons. Lord Brahma narrated the tale of Tripurasur. "After the killing of Tarakasur, three of his sons started doing their penance. The eldest among them was Tarkasha, younger to him was Viddyunmali and Kamalaksha was the youngest. I (Brahma)…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">42</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 42, "quote": "5.2.114 Yudh-Khand: Tripurasur- The Three Demon Narada requested lord Brahma to narrate how lord Shiva annihilated the demons. Lord Brahma narrated the tale of Tripurasur. \"After the killing of Tarakasur, three of his sons started doing t"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-115"><data key="title">The Deities Eulogize Shiva &amp; Vishnu</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">The three demon-brothers started tormenting the deities. The deities went to lord Brahma and narrated about their miseries. Lord Brahma instructed them to seek the help of Shiva. They went to lord Shiva and explained about their woes. They also requested Shiva to eliminate those three demons. Lord Shiva instructed…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">43</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 43, "quote": "5.2.115 The Deities Eulogize Shiva &amp; Vishnu The three demon-brothers started tormenting the deities. The deities went to lord Brahma and narrated about their miseries. Lord Brahma instructed them to seek the help of Shiva. They went t"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-116"><data key="title">Emergence of Atheism</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Vishnu came to the conclusion that the Tripurasurs could not be killed till they remain Virtuous and religious. He then created an illusionary entity from his body who had no hairs on his head. He had worn dirty clothes and a bag was hanging down his shoulder. He also had a broom in his head. The entity asked…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">43</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 43, "quote": "5.2.116 Emergence of Atheism Lord Vishnu came to the conclusion that the Tripurasurs could not be killed till they remain Virtuous and religious. He then created an illusionary entity from his body who had no hairs on h"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-117"><data key="title">The Deities Eulogize Lord Shiva</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">When the deities saw that the Tripurasurs had become irreligious completely, they went to lord Shiva and requested him to kill Tripurasurs. Right then mother Parvati arrived there accompanied by Kartik and Ganesha. She requested Shiva to come along with her into the palace. All the deities followed them and kept on…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">43</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 43, "quote": "5.2.117 The Deities Eulogize Lord Shiva When the deities saw that the Tripurasurs had become irreligious completely, they went to lord Shiva and requested him to kill Tripurasurs. Right then mother Parvati arrived there accompanie"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-118"><data key="title">Killings of Tripurasurs</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When Shiva agreed to kill the demon - brothers Tripurasur the deities became extremely joyous. They gave their respective weapons to Shiva, so that he did not have any difficulty in killing the demons. Vishwakarma gave his beautiful chariot to him. Lord Shiva proceeded towards the forts of Tripurasur followed by a…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">44</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 44, "quote": "5.2.118 Killings of Tripurasurs When Shiva agreed to kill the demon - brothers Tripurasur the deities became extremely joyous. They gave their respective weapons to Shiva, so that he did not have any difficulty in killing"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-119"><data key="title">The Deities Receive a Boon from Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After the killings of three demons- Tripurasur, lord Shiva's anger had still not subdivided. The anger was pacified only after the deities and the sages prayed to him. He blessed the deities by saying that he would always be present on their side to protect them. Maya - Who had survived the Shiva's attack, arrived…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">44</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 44, "quote": "5.2.119 The Deities Receive a Boon from Shiva After the killings of three demons- Tripurasur, lord Shiva's anger had still not subdivided. The anger was pacified only after the deities and the sages prayed to him. He blessed the deities"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-120"><data key="title">Lord Shiva Spares The Life of Indra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sutji narrates the following story to the sages- "Once upon a time, Indra was going towards Kailash mountain to have a 'darshan' of lord Shiva. Sage Vrihaspati was accompanying him. Lord Shiva came to know about his arrival. He wanted to test his devotion towards him. While both Indra and Vrihaspati were still on…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">44</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 44, "quote": "5.2.120 Lord Shiva Spares The Life of Indra Sutji narrates the following story to the sages- \"Once upon a time, Indra was going towards Kailash mountain to have a 'darshan' of lord Shiva. Sage Vrihaspati was accompanying him. Lord Shi"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-121"><data key="title">Manifestation of Jaladhar</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The effulgence, which had been divided by lord Shiva into the Ocean resulted into the manifestation of a small child. This incident happened at the place where river Ganges submerged into the ocean and which is also known as Gangasagar now a days. The child was crying so ferociously that an environment of fear was…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">45</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 45, "quote": "5.2.121 Manifestation of Jaladhar The effulgence, which had been divided by lord Shiva into the Ocean resulted into the manifestation of a small child. This incident happened at the place where river Ganges submerged into th"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-122"><data key="title">Battle Between Jalandhar &amp; The Deities</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">One day Sage Bhrigu came to meet Jalandhar. After receiving him with due respect Jalandhar asked him as to who severed the head of Rahu. Sage Bhrigu then told him about Hiranyakashipu who was the maternal uncle of Rahu. Sage Bhrigu also told him about Virochana - the son of the extremely charitable king Bali. Then…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">45</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 45, "quote": "5.2.122 Battle Between Jalandhar &amp; The Deities One day Sage Bhrigu came to meet Jalandhar. After receiving him with due respect Jalandhar asked him as to who severed the head of Rahu. Sage Bhrigu then told him about Hiranyakashipu who wa"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-123"><data key="title">Battle Between Lord Vishnu &amp; Jalandhar</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The terrified deities took the refuge of lord Vishnu and sought his help. Lord Vishnu gave a patient hearing and agreed to help them, but there was a hitch. His consort goddess Laxmi considered Jalandhar as her brother because both of them originated from the sea. She instructed Lord Vishnu against killing Jalandhar.…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 46, "quote": "5.2.123 Battle Between Lord Vishnu &amp; Jalandhar The terrified deities took the refuge of lord Vishnu and sought his help. Lord Vishnu gave a patient hearing and agreed to help them, but there was a hitch. His consort goddess Laxmi conside"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-124"><data key="title">Narad’s Stratagem</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">According to the wish of lord Shiva, Sage Narad came to meet the deities. The deities narrated their woeful tales to him. Feeling pity on their condition, he went accorded a grand reception by Jalandhar which pleased him very much. Narad praised the splendours and prosperity of Jalandhar but added that it was nothing…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 46, "quote": "5.2.124 Narad’s Stratagem According to the wish of lord Shiva, Sage Narad came to meet the deities. The deities narrated their woeful tales to him. Feeling pity on their condition, he went accorded a grand reception"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-125"><data key="title">Battle Between Lord Shiva &amp; Jalandhar</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Rahu went back to Jalandhar and narrated the whole story to him. Jalandhar then attacked Kailash mountain with his huge army. A fierce battle was fought between the Shiva-ganas and the demons. When Jalandhar realized that lord Shiva had begun to dominate the battle he created beautiful 'Apsaras' and 'Gandharvas' by…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 46, "quote": "5.2.125 Battle Between Lord Shiva &amp; Jalandhar Rahu went back to Jalandhar and narrated the whole story to him. Jalandhar then attacked Kailash mountain with his huge army. A fierce battle was fought between the Shiva-ganas and the demon"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-126"><data key="title">Vrinda Gets Dishonored</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">With the help of his divine powers, Lord Vishnu created many inauspicious dreams while Vrinda was asleep. Vrinda became very restless and when the anxieties become unbearable she proceeded towards the forest. Vrinda saw a hermit in the forest who was preaching his disciples. The hermit was none other than lord Vishnu.…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">47</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 47, "quote": "5.2.126 Vrinda Gets Dishonored With the help of his divine powers, Lord Vishnu created many inauspicious dreams while Vrinda was asleep. Vrinda became very restless and when the anxieties become unbearable she proceeded t"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-127"><data key="title">Killing of Jalandhar</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">On the other side, after the departure of Parvati from Kailash Mountain and after the illusionary powers created by Jalandhar had ended all the apsaras and gandharvas vanished. Shiva realized that whatever he was watching, was nothing more than illusion. Lord Shiva recommenced his battle. The demons-Shumbh and…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">47</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 47, "quote": "5.2.127 Killing of Jalandhar On the other side, after the departure of Parvati from Kailash Mountain and after the illusionary powers created by Jalandhar had ended all the apsaras and gandharvas vanished. Shiva realize"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-128"><data key="title">The Deities Express Gratitude</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When the deities came to know about the killing of Jalandhar, they became overjoyed. They came to Lord Shiva and eulogized him. Thy also hailed his great achievement. After expressing their gratitude they returned to their respective abodes.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">47</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 47, "quote": "5.2.128 The Deities Express Gratitude When the deities came to know about the killing of Jalandhar, they became overjoyed. They came to Lord Shiva and eulogized him. Thy also hailed his great achievement. After expressing their"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-129"><data key="title">Manifestation of Amla (Myrobalan), Tulsi (~Basil) and Malti</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lord Vishnu was very much saddened by the death of Vrinda. He took the ashes from the pyre and after applying them on his body started wondering here and there. The deities became very worried to see the condition of lord Vishnu. They went to lord Shiva and requested him to eliminate the false attachment with which…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-1</data><data key="page">47</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 47, "quote": "5.2.129 Manifestation of Amla (Myrobalan), Tulsi (~Basil) and Malti Lord Vishnu was very much saddened by the death of Vrinda. He took the ashes from the pyre and after applying them on his body started wondering here and there. The deities became very worri"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-126"><data key="title">Vrinda Gets Dishonored</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">With the help of his divine powers, Lord Vishnu created many inauspicious dreams while Vrinda was asleep. Vrinda became very restless and when the anxieties become unbearable she proceeded towards the forest. Vrinda saw a hermit in the forest who was preaching his disciples. The hermit was none other than lord Vishnu.…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 1, "quote": "5.2.126 Vrinda Gets Dishonored With the help of his divine powers, Lord Vishnu created many inauspicious dreams while Vrinda was asleep. Vrinda became very restless and when the anxieties become unbearable she proceeded t"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-127"><data key="title">Killing of Jalandhar</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">On the other side, after the departure of Parvati from Kailash Mountain and after the illusionary powers created by Jalandhar had ended all the apsaras and gandharvas vanished. Shiva realized that whatever he was watching, was nothing more than illusion. Lord Shiva recommenced his battle. The demons-Shumbh and…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 1, "quote": "5.2.127 Killing of Jalandhar On the other side, after the departure of Parvati from Kailash Mountain and after the illusionary powers created by Jalandhar had ended all the apsaras and gandharvas vanished. Shiva realize"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-128"><data key="title">The Deities Express Gratitude</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When the deities came to know about the killing of Jalandhar, they became overjoyed. They came to Lord Shiva and eulogized him. Thy also hailed his great achievement. After expressing their gratitude they returned to their respective abodes.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 1, "quote": "5.2.128 The Deities Express Gratitude When the deities came to know about the killing of Jalandhar, they became overjoyed. They came to Lord Shiva and eulogized him. Thy also hailed his great achievement. After expressing their"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-129"><data key="title">Manifestation of Amla (Myrobalan), Tulsi (~Basil) and Malti</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lord Vishnu was very much saddened by the death of Vrinda. He took the ashes from the pyre and after applying them on his body started wondering here and there. The deities became very worried to see the condition of lord Vishnu. They went to lord Shiva and requested him to eliminate the false attachment with which…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">1</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 1, "quote": "5.2.129 Manifestation of Amla (Myrobalan), Tulsi (~Basil) and Malti Lord Vishnu was very much saddened by the death of Vrinda. He took the ashes from the pyre and after applying them on his body started wondering here and there. The deities became very worri"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-130"><data key="title">Birth of Shankhachuda</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Sutji narrated the tale of the birth of Shankhachuda and how Shiva killed him with his Trishula. He told the sages that Shankhachuda was born to the demon king 'Dambha'. Shankhachuda was in fact, Sudama, in his previous life. He was born in the family of demons due to the curse of Radha.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 2, "quote": "5.2.130 Birth of Shankhachuda Sutji narrated the tale of the birth of Shankhachuda and how Shiva killed him with his Trishula. He told the sages that Shankhachuda was born to the demon king 'Dambha'. Shankhachuda was in"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-131"><data key="title">Shankhachuda’s Marriage</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">When Shankhachuda gre up, he went to Pushkar (Ajmer, Rajasthan) and did a tremendous penance to please lord Brahma. Lord Brahma blessed him and said that he would remain invincible. He also instructed Shankhachuda to go to 'Badrikashram' where he would find his would be wife Tulsi- the daughter of Dharmadhwaja.…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 2, "quote": "5.2.131 Shankhachuda’s Marriage When Shankhachuda gre up, he went to Pushkar (Ajmer, Rajasthan) and did a tremendous penance to please lord Brahma. Lord Brahma blessed him and said that he would remain invincible. He also"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-132"><data key="title">Shankhachuda Becomes the Ruler of All Three Worlds</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When Shankhachuda reached his capital after marrying Tulsi, he was crowned as the king of the demons by Shukracharya. After his coronation, Shankhachuda attacked Indrapuri supported by his huge army and defeated the deities. In a very short time all the three worlds were under his control. After being defeated by…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 2, "quote": "5.2.132 Shankhachuda Becomes the Ruler of All Three Worlds When Shankhachuda reached his capital after marrying Tulsi, he was crowned as the king of the demons by Shukracharya. After his coronation, Shankhachuda attacked Indrapuri supported by his h"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-133"><data key="title">Shiva Assures the Deities</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva assured the deities that he will certainly kill Shankhachuda and hence they should not worry about him. The deities then happily returned to their respective abodes.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 2, "quote": "5.2.133 Shiva Assures the Deities Lord Shiva assured the deities that he will certainly kill Shankhachuda and hence they should not worry about him. The deities then happily returned to their respective abodes."}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-134"><data key="title">Conversation Between Pushpadant &amp; Shankhachuda</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva sent his messenger named Pushpadant, to Shankhachuda to ask him to return back the kingdom of the deities to them. Shankhachuda refused to oblige this demand, on the contrary he expressed his willingness and readiness to fight lord Shiva. Pushpadant returned back and narrated the whole story to lord Shiva.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 2, "quote": "5.2.134 Conversation Between Pushpadant &amp; Shankhachuda Lord Shiva sent his messenger named Pushpadant, to Shankhachuda to ask him to return back the kingdom of the deities to them. Shankhachuda refused to oblige this demand, on the contrary he e"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-135"><data key="title">Shiva Proceeds with his Army</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva was now fully convinced about the inevitability of the battle. He first sent all his ganas under the leadership of Kartekiye and Ganesha. Later on Bhadrakali proceeded towards the battlefield with a huge army as per the own wish of lord Shiva himself. At last lord Shiva proceeded towards the battlefield…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">2</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 2, "quote": "5.2.135 Shiva Proceeds with his Army Lord Shiva was now fully convinced about the inevitability of the battle. He first sent all his ganas under the leadership of Kartekiye and Ganesha. Later on Bhadrakali proceeded towards the"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-136"><data key="title">Shankhachuda Proceeds with his Army</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shankhachuda handed over the kingdom to his son and went to his wife to take her permission before going to the battlefield. His wife was reluctant to allow him to go, but he somehow managed to convince her. He then proceeded towards the banks of Chandrabhaga with a huge army.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 3, "quote": "5.2.136 Shankhachuda Proceeds with his Army Shankhachuda handed over the kingdom to his son and went to his wife to take her permission before going to the battlefield. His wife was reluctant to allow him to go, but he somehow managed"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-137"><data key="title">Battle Between the Deities and the Demons</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">A fierce battle started between the deities and the demons. Both of them attacked each other with the most destructive weapons. But when the army of Chandrachuda began to dominate the fight, the deities fled away and took the refuge of lord Shiva. They told Shiva of the number of deities who had been killed while…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 3, "quote": "5.2.137 Battle Between the Deities and the Demons A fierce battle started between the deities and the demons. Both of them attacked each other with the most destructive weapons. But when the army of Chandrachuda began to dominate the fight,"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-138"><data key="title">The Battle Between Shiva’s Family &amp; Shankhachuda</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After the defeat of the deities and the ganas Kartikeya and Ganesha went to fight Shankhachuda. A spectacular battle was fought between Shankhachuda and both of them. Later on they were joined by Bhadrakali. Bhadrakali would have devoured Shankhachuda without any problem, but she spared his life because of the boon,…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 3, "quote": "5.2.138 The Battle Between Shiva’s Family &amp; Shankhachuda After the defeat of the deities and the ganas Kartikeya and Ganesha went to fight Shankhachuda. A spectacular battle was fought between Shankhachuda and both of them. Later on they were join"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-139"><data key="title">Strategy Employed to Kill Shankhachuda</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">While a tremendous battle was being fought between lord Shiva and Shankhachuda. Lord Vishnu appeared and demanded the armour from Shankhachuda, which he had put on, his body in the guise of a brahmin. Shankhachuda gave his armour to him without any kind of suspicion. Lord Vishnu then went to Shankhachuda's wife in the…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 3, "quote": "5.2.139 Strategy Employed to Kill Shankhachuda While a tremendous battle was being fought between lord Shiva and Shankhachuda. Lord Vishnu appeared and demanded the armour from Shankhachuda, which he had put on, his body in the guise of"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-140"><data key="title">Lord Shiva Kills Shankhachuda</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Bhadrakali was creating havoc in the army of Shankhachuda. Shankhachuda became very furious and attacked Shiva. Shiva repulsed his assault and attacked him with his Trishul. Shankhachuda who had became powerless was killed instantaneously. The deities were very pleased at the death of Shankhachuda. After worshipping…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 3, "quote": "5.2.140 Lord Shiva Kills Shankhachuda Bhadrakali was creating havoc in the army of Shankhachuda. Shankhachuda became very furious and attacked Shiva. Shiva repulsed his assault and attacked him with his Trishul. Shankhachuda who"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-141"><data key="title">Tulsi Curses Lord Vishnu</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Tulsi curses Vishnu after the deception that contributes to Shankhachuda’s death. This supplied retelling should be read in its own narrative context.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">3</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 3, "quote": "5.2.141 Tulsi Curses Lord Vishnu After being instructed by goddess Parvati, Lord Vishnu had gone to Tulsi in the guise of her husband- Shankhachuda, so that the breach of Tulsi chastity could help lord Shiva to kill Shankha"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-142"><data key="title">Killing of Hiranyaksha</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Hiranyakashipu was filled with grief when he heard the news of his brothers' death. To avenge Hiranyaksha's death, he started to torment the deities. Becoming homeless, the deities started wandering here and there. Hiranyakashipu was still unsatisfied. He wanted to acquire more power and authority. He went to the…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 4, "quote": "5.2.142 Killing of Hiranyaksha Hiranyakashipu was filled with grief when he heard the news of his brothers' death. To avenge Hiranyaksha's death, he started to torment the deities. Becoming homeless, the deities started w"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-143"><data key="title">Lord Shiva Teaches The Secrets of Mrita-Sanjivani Vidya to Shukracharya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Shukracharya did a severe penance for five thousand years to please Shiva and acquire the secrets of Mritasanjivani Viddya (bringing back dead person alive) from him. When Lord Shiva did not appear even after such a tremendous penance, then he indulges himself in severest form of penance, by quitting having food and…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 4, "quote": "5.2.143 Lord Shiva Teaches The Secrets of Mrita-Sanjivani Vidya to Shukracharya Shukracharya did a severe penance for five thousand years to please Shiva and acquire the secrets of Mritasanjivani Viddya (bringing back dead person alive) from him. When Lord Shiva did not"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-144"><data key="title">Andhak’s Splendors</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, Andhak brothers jokingly told him that, since his real parents were somebody else (Shiva and Parvati) therefore he was not fit to rule them. Though they had told this in good humour, yet Andhak was very much saddened by their remark. He relinquished his throne and went to a deserted place in the forest and…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">4</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 4, "quote": "5.2.144 Andhak’s Splendors Once, Andhak brothers jokingly told him that, since his real parents were somebody else (Shiva and Parvati) therefore he was not fit to rule them. Though they had told this in good humour, y"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-145"><data key="title">Battle Between Lord Shiva and Andhak</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Andhak had become so arrogant that once, he even dared to dishonour his own mother-Parvati, who at that time was living at the Mandarachal mountain. Shiva became very furious with him. Andhak attacked Shiva with his huge army. Lord Shiva sent his numerous ganas to fight him, but all of them were killed by Andhak. When…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 5, "quote": "5.2.145 Battle Between Lord Shiva and Andhak Andhak had become so arrogant that once, he even dared to dishonour his own mother-Parvati, who at that time was living at the Mandarachal mountain. Shiva became very furious with him. Andha"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-146"><data key="title">Reemergence of Shukracharya</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">When lord Shiva swallowed up Shukracharya, he became very restless and started to find out a way through which he could come out from Shiva's stomach. But all of his efforts went in vain. Finding no other option, he started to chant the name of lord Shiva. His chantings continued for one hundred years. By the…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 5, "quote": "5.2.146 Reemergence of Shukracharya When lord Shiva swallowed up Shukracharya, he became very restless and started to find out a way through which he could come out from Shiva's stomach. But all of his efforts went in vain. Fi"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-147"><data key="title">Killing of Gajasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Goddess Durga had killed the demon - Mahishasur, who used to torment the deities. Gajasur was the son of Mahishasur. To avenge his father's death, Gajasur did a tremendous penance to please lord Brahma. Lord Brahma appeared before him and asked him to demand any boon. Gajasur said- "O Lord! Even a 'Jeetendriya' (one…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 5, "quote": "5.2.147 Killing of Gajasur Goddess Durga had killed the demon - Mahishasur, who used to torment the deities. Gajasur was the son of Mahishasur. To avenge his father's death, Gajasur did a tremendous penance to please"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-148"><data key="title">Killing of Nirhaddaitya</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Diti was filled with grief when she came to known about her son's (Hiranyaksha) death, who was killed by lord Vishnu. Nirhad daitya, who was the maternal uncle of Prahlad consoled her an promised to avenge the death of Hiranyaksha. He thought to destroy the vedic religion. According to him the deities derived their…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 6, "quote": "5.2.148 Killing of Nirhaddaitya Diti was filled with grief when she came to known about her son's (Hiranyaksha) death, who was killed by lord Vishnu. Nirhad daitya, who was the maternal uncle of Prahlad consoled her an pro"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-2-149"><data key="title">Killing of Vidal and Utpal</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Long long ago there used to live two demons by the names of Vidul and Utpal. Both of them did a tremendous penance to please lord Brahma. After being blessed by lord Brahma, they became very arrogant and started tormenting the deities and the brahmins. The deities went to lord Brahma and sought his help. Lord Brahma…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">6</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 6, "quote": "5.2.149 Killing of Vidal and Utpal Long long ago there used to live two demons by the names of Vidul and Utpal. Both of them did a tremendous penance to please lord Brahma. After being blessed by lord Brahma, they became very"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3"><data key="title">Shatrudra Samhita</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages requested Sutji to describe about the different incarnations of Lord Shiva. Sutji told them that, although Lord Shiva took many incarnations but five of his incarnations were very important- Sadhojat Namadeva, Tatpurush, Aghoresh and Ishan. This Chapter contains 32 sections.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 7, "quote": "5.3 Shatrudra Samhita The sages requested Sutji to describe about the different incarnations of Lord Shiva. Sutji told them that, although Lord Shiva took many incarnations but five of his incarnations were very"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-1"><data key="title">Sadhojat (1)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva took his first incarnation from the physique incarnation from the physique of Lord Brahma, who was engrossed in his deep state of meditation, during the nineteenth Kalpa named Shweta Lohit. Lord Brahma gave him the name SADHOJAT and eulogized him. Later on, from the physique of Sadhojat four of his disciple…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 7, "quote": "5.3.1 Sadhojat (1) Lord Shiva took his first incarnation from the physique incarnation from the physique of Lord Brahma, who was engrossed in his deep state of meditation, during the nineteenth Kalpa named Shw"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-2"><data key="title">Naamdeva (2)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">During the twentieth Kalpa named 'Rakta' the complexion of lord Brahma turned red, while he was engrossed in his meditative state. From his body manifested an entity who also was of red complexion. Lord Brahma named him Namadeva considering him to be the incarnation of lord Shiva and eulogized him. Later on four sons…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 7, "quote": "5.3.2 Naamdeva (2) During the twentieth Kalpa named 'Rakta' the complexion of lord Brahma turned red, while he was engrossed in his meditative state. From his body manifested an entity who also was of red comp"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-3"><data key="title">Tatpurush (3)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The twenty-first Kalpa on the earth was known as 'Peetavasa'. It was named so because of the apparel of lord Brahma which were of yellow colours. Lord Brahma's prayer resulted into the manifestation of a effulgent entity. Considering this entity as Lord Shiva, Lord Brahma started chanting the mantras of Shiva Gayatri.…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 7, "quote": "5.3.3 Tatpurush (3) The twenty-first Kalpa on the earth was known as 'Peetavasa'. It was named so because of the apparel of lord Brahma which were of yellow colours. Lord Brahma's prayer resulted into the manif"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-4"><data key="title">Ghoresh (4)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After the Peetavasa Kalp came the Shiva Kalpa. A black complexioned manifested while Lord Brahma was engrossed in his deep meditative state. Lord Brahma considering this entity as Aghor Shiva started eulogizing him. Lord Brahma's eulogization resulted into the manifestation of four more entities who had the same black…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 7, "quote": "5.3.4 Ghoresh (4) After the Peetavasa Kalp came the Shiva Kalpa. A black complexioned manifested while Lord Brahma was engrossed in his deep meditative state. Lord Brahma considering this entity as Aghor Shiv"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-5"><data key="title">Ishan (5)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">During the Kalpa named Vishwaroop, manifestations of Saraswati and Ishan Shiva took place. Lord Brahma eulogized Ishan Shiva after which four divine entities named Jati, Mundi, Shikhandi and Ardhamundi manifested from Ishan Shiva. All of them blessed lord Brahma with the power of creation.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">7</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 7, "quote": "5.3.5 Ishan (5) During the Kalpa named Vishwaroop, manifestations of Saraswati and Ishan Shiva took place. Lord Brahma eulogized Ishan Shiva after which four divine entities named Jati, Mundi, Shikhandi and"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-6"><data key="title">Eight Idols of Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After describing about the five chief incarnations of lord Shiva, Sutji explained about the eight famous idols of lord Shiva- Sharva, Bhava, Rudra, Ugra, Bheema, Pashupati, Ishan and Mahadeva. These eight idols of Shiva symbolizes the eight natural elements which help in the process of creation, nurturement and…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 8, "quote": "5.3.6 Eight Idols of Shiva After describing about the five chief incarnations of lord Shiva, Sutji explained about the eight famous idols of lord Shiva- Sharva, Bhava, Rudra, Ugra, Bheema, Pashupati, Ishan and Mahadev"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-7"><data key="title">Shiva as Ardhanarishwar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once upon a time Lord Brahma, not seeing an expansion in his creation became very worried. A heavenly voice instructed him to commence creation with the help of copulative activities. But since all the incarnation of Shiva had been males till then, therefore lord Brahma was finding it impossible. Lord Brahma…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 8, "quote": "5.3.7 Shiva as Ardhanarishwar Once upon a time Lord Brahma, not seeing an expansion in his creation became very worried. A heavenly voice instructed him to commence creation with the help of copulative activities. But si"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-8"><data key="title">Various Incarnations of Vyasa and Lord Shiva</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">During the 'Varah' Kalpa of the Seventh 'Manvantar' lord Vishnu illuminated all the three world by his divine presence. This seventh Manvantara consisted of four yugas which repeated themselves in a cyclic way for twelve times. The first dwapar of this seventh manvantar saw the manifestation of lord Shiva for the…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">8</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 8, "quote": "5.3.8 Various Incarnations of Vyasa and Lord Shiva During the 'Varah' Kalpa of the Seventh 'Manvantar' lord Vishnu illuminated all the three world by his divine presence. This seventh Manvantara consisted of four yugas which repeated themsel"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-9"><data key="title">Nandikeshwar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">While describing about the incarnation of Nandikeshwar Sutji says- Sage Shilad did a tremendous penance to please lord Shiva with an aspiration to have a son. Lord Shiva appeared before him and asked him to demand any boon he wished. Sage Shilad expressed his desire of having a son who is not born from a physical body…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 9, "quote": "5.3.9 Nandikeshwar While describing about the incarnation of Nandikeshwar Sutji says- Sage Shilad did a tremendous penance to please lord Shiva with an aspiration to have a son. Lord Shiva appeared before him"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-10"><data key="title">Bhairav Gets Liberated from his Sin</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Bhairav who was created by lord Shiva from his third eye, had severed one of the five heads of Lord Brahma on the instruction of lord Shiva. Now Brahmaji was left with only four heads. Carrying the skull of Brahma in his hand, Bhairava started wandering in all the three worlds. To atone his sin of severing the head of…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">9</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 9, "quote": "5.3.10 Bhairav Gets Liberated from his Sin Bhairav who was created by lord Shiva from his third eye, had severed one of the five heads of Lord Brahma on the instruction of lord Shiva. Now Brahmaji was left with only four heads. Carry"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-11"><data key="title">Shiva Takes an Incarnation as Sharabh</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">While describing about the incarnation of Sharabh Sutji told sages- “When Vishnuji took his incarnation of Nrisimha to kill the demon king –Hiranyakashipu, his anger could not be subdued even after the killing of Hiranyakashipu. His anger had frightened all the three worlds. Lord Brahma sent Prahalada to Nrisimha so…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 10, "quote": "5.3.11 Shiva Takes an Incarnation as Sharabh While describing about the incarnation of Sharabh Sutji told sages- “When Vishnuji took his incarnation of Nrisimha to kill the demon king –Hiranyakashipu, his anger could not be subdued eve"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-12"><data key="title">Vishwanar Receives a Boon from Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once upon a time, there used to live a brahmin named Vishwanar who was a great devotee of lord Shiva. Shuchismati was his wife who was a devout wife. Being pleased by her sense of dutifulness, Vishwanar tried to reward his wife. Shuchismati expressed her desire of having a son just like lord Shiva. Vishwanar went to…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 10, "quote": "5.3.12 Vishwanar Receives a Boon from Shiva Once upon a time, there used to live a brahmin named Vishwanar who was a great devotee of lord Shiva. Shuchismati was his wife who was a devout wife. Being pleased by her sense of dutifulnes"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-13"><data key="title">Shiva’s Incarnation as Grihapati</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">In due course of time, Shuchismati became pregnant and gave birth to a beautiful child. The whole family celebrated the birth of the child. The whole family celebrated the birth of the child. The name giving ceremony of that child was attended by all the deities and sages including lord Shiva and Parvati. Lord Brahma…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">10</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 10, "quote": "5.3.13 Shiva’s Incarnation as Grihapati In due course of time, Shuchismati became pregnant and gave birth to a beautiful child. The whole family celebrated the birth of the child. The whole family celebrated the birth of the child"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-14"><data key="title">Grihapati’s Penance</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Grihapati commenced his penance at Kashi. The deity Indra arrived there and requested him to demand anything he wished but Grihapati refused. Indra became furious and tried to attack him with his weapon-Vajra. Grihapati was very terrified. Right then Lord Shiva appeared and Indra had to retreat from the scene. Lord…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 11, "quote": "5.3.14 Grihapati’s Penance Grihapati commenced his penance at Kashi. The deity Indra arrived there and requested him to demand anything he wished but Grihapati refused. Indra became furious and tried to attack him wit"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-15"><data key="title">Lord Shiva’s Incarnation as Yaksheshwar</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">During the time when ocean was being churned first of all poison appeared from it. The deities were very terrified to see the tremendous heat it generated. They went to lord Shiva and requested to protect them from the heat of that poison. Lord Shiva drank all the poison but did not let it pass down his throat. After…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 11, "quote": "5.3.15 Lord Shiva’s Incarnation as Yaksheshwar During the time when ocean was being churned first of all poison appeared from it. The deities were very terrified to see the tremendous heat it generated. They went to lord Shiva and reques"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-16"><data key="title">Dash Avatar (10 Incarnations of Shiva)</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Describing about the ten incarnations and their corresponding power (Shakti), Sutji said- The first incarnation of lord Shiva was as Mahakal and his Shakti was called Mahakali. Lord Shiva took his second incarnation as Tar and his Shakti was called 'Tara'. The third incarnation of Lord Shiva was as Bhuvaneshwar and…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 11, "quote": "5.3.16 Dash Avatar (10 Incarnations of Shiva) Describing about the ten incarnations and their corresponding power (Shakti), Sutji said- The first incarnation of lord Shiva was as Mahakal and his Shakti was called Mahakali. Lord Shiva to"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-17"><data key="title">The Origin of Eleven Rudras</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, the deities went to sage Kashyap after being tormented by the demons. They complained to him about the misdeeds of the demons, who also happened to be their step brothers. Sage Kashyap became extremely furious, when he heard about the misdeeds of his sons – the demons. To protect the deities from his sons, he…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 12, "quote": "5.3.17 The Origin of Eleven Rudras Once, the deities went to sage Kashyap after being tormented by the demons. They complained to him about the misdeeds of the demons, who also happened to be their step brothers. Sage Kashyap"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-18"><data key="title">Shiva’s Incarnation as Durvasa</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Describing about the Lord Shiva's incarnation as Durvasa, Sutji told the deities:- “once Atri – the manasputra of lord Brahma went to do penance at the bank of the river Nivindhya which flew by the foothills of Trayakshakul mountain as per the instructions of Lord Brahma himself. He commenced doing a tremendous…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">12</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 12, "quote": "5.3.18 Shiva’s Incarnation as Durvasa Describing about the Lord Shiva's incarnation as Durvasa, Sutji told the deities:- “once Atri – the manasputra of lord Brahma went to do penance at the bank of the river Nivindhya which flew"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-19"><data key="title">Incarnation of Hanuman</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva was so infatuated by the appearance of lord Vishnu in his form of Mohini roopa that his semen was released on the ground. This semen was established by the Saptarishis in the womb of Anjani, with the permission of lord Shiva himself. In this way was born the mighty Hanuman. Once, during his childhood…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 13, "quote": "5.3.19 Incarnation of Hanuman Lord Shiva was so infatuated by the appearance of lord Vishnu in his form of Mohini roopa that his semen was released on the ground. This semen was established by the Saptarishis in the womb"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-20"><data key="title">Shiva’s Incarnation as Mahesh</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, Bhairav who was entrusted with the job of a doorkeeper became so infatuated with Parvati's beauty that he tried to prevent her from going outside. Parvati became furious and cursed him to be born as a mortal man on the earth. Bhairav became very sad but now the damage had been already done. Bhairav later on…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 13, "quote": "5.3.20 Shiva’s Incarnation as Mahesh Once, Bhairav who was entrusted with the job of a doorkeeper became so infatuated with Parvati's beauty that he tried to prevent her from going outside. Parvati became furious and cursed him"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-21"><data key="title">Lord Vishnu Creates Turbulences</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After the churning of the ocean had been accomplished, numerous things had emerged out from the ocean. Jewels, Moon, Laxmi, Poison, Uchchaishrava horse, Eravat elephant, Vessel containing Nectar were some of the things which emerged out from the ocean after the churning. A tremendous battle was fought between the…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 13, "quote": "5.3.21 Lord Vishnu Creates Turbulences After the churning of the ocean had been accomplished, numerous things had emerged out from the ocean. Jewels, Moon, Laxmi, Poison, Uchchaishrava horse, Eravat elephant, Vessel containing Ne"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-22"><data key="title">Shiva’s Incarnation as Vrishabh</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">During his stay in the Netherland, many sons were born to Lord Vishnu, who were very wicked and cruel. These sons of lord Vishnu started tormenting the inhabitants of all the three world. All the deities and the sages went to lord Shiva to take his help. They requested him to kill the cruel sons of Vishnu and…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 14, "quote": "5.3.22 Shiva’s Incarnation as Vrishabh During his stay in the Netherland, many sons were born to Lord Vishnu, who were very wicked and cruel. These sons of lord Vishnu started tormenting the inhabitants of all the three world. Al"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-23"><data key="title">Killing of Vrittrasur</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, when the deities were defeated by vrittrasur, they went to lord Brahma after hiding their weapons in the hermitage of sage Dadhichi. They requested lord Brahma to reveal about the means, how vrittrasur could be killed. Lord Brahma advised the deities to male a weapon named Vajra from the bones of sages Dadhichi,…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 14, "quote": "5.3.23 Killing of Vrittrasur Once, when the deities were defeated by vrittrasur, they went to lord Brahma after hiding their weapons in the hermitage of sage Dadhichi. They requested lord Brahma to reveal about the mean"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-24"><data key="title">Shiva’s Incarnation as Yatinath</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">There used to live a bheel named Aahuk o the Arbudachal mountain. His wife was Aahuka. Both of them were supreme devotee of lord Shiva. Once, lord Shiva wanting to test their devotion, appeared before them disguised a hermit. Aahuk honoured his guest and treated him very well. Lord Shiva then requested him to give…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 14, "quote": "5.3.24 Shiva’s Incarnation as Yatinath There used to live a bheel named Aahuk o the Arbudachal mountain. His wife was Aahuka. Both of them were supreme devotee of lord Shiva."}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-25"><data key="title">Shiva’s Incarnation as Krshna Darshan</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">King Nabhag was born in the nineth geneology of Shraddhdeva who himself was a descendant of Ikshavaku. Nabhag was the grandfather of Ambareesh. During his childhood Nabhag left his home for 'gurukula to get education. In his absence his brothers got the wealth of the kingdom distributed among themselves. When Nabhag…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 15, "quote": "5.3.25 Shiva’s Incarnation as Krshna Darshan King Nabhag was born in the nineth geneology of Shraddhdeva who himself was a descendant of Ikshavaku. Nabhag was the grandfather of Ambareesh. During his childhood Nabhag left his home for"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-26"><data key="title">Lord Shiva Appears as a Beggar</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">There was a king named Satyarath who ruled over Vidarbha. Once he was attacked by a neighbouring king. Satyarath was killed in that battle. His wife somehow managed to save her life by hiding in a forest. The queen was pregnant at that time. At the shore of a pond she gave birth to a child. She was feeling thirsty, so…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 16, "quote": "5.3.26 Lord Shiva Appears as a Beggar There was a king named Satyarath who ruled over Vidarbha. Once he was attacked by a neighbouring king. Satyarath was killed in that battle. His wife somehow managed to save her life by hidin"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-27"><data key="title">Lord Shiva Appears in the Guise of Indra (Sureshwar)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Upamanyu –the son of sage Vyaghrapaad, was brought up in his maternal uncle's home since his childhood. One day Upanyu was very hungry. His mother gave him milk to drink which was not enough to satisfy his hunger. He demanded more and started crying. His mother mined some wheat flour in the water and gave him to drink…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 16, "quote": "5.3.27 Lord Shiva Appears in the Guise of Indra (Sureshwar) Upamanyu –the son of sage Vyaghrapaad, was brought up in his maternal uncle's home since his childhood. One day Upanyu was very hungry. His mother gave him milk to drink which was not enough"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-28"><data key="title">Sage Vyasa Advises the Pandavas to Worship Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After loosing their whole kingdom to Duryodhan in the gamble, Pandavas went to the dwait forest accompanied by Draupadi. They started living in a place which was donated by Velotra Sun. Duryodhan instigated sage Durvasa to torment the Pandavas. Sage Durvasa went to the place where Pandavas were staying, accompanied by…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 17, "quote": "5.3.28 Sage Vyasa Advises the Pandavas to Worship Shiva After loosing their whole kingdom to Duryodhan in the gamble, Pandavas went to the dwait forest accompanied by Draupadi. They started living in a place which was donated by Velotra Sun. Dury"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-29"><data key="title">Indra Appears before Arjun</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Arjuna's penance generated so much of heat that all the living creature of the three worlds were unable to bear its scorching heat. All the living creatures of the three worlds went to Indra to seek his help. Indra went to Arjuna in the guise of a celibate and asked about the purpose for which he was doing penance.…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 17, "quote": "5.3.29 Indra Appears before Arjun Arjuna's penance generated so much of heat that all the living creature of the three worlds were unable to bear its scorching heat. All the living creatures of the three worlds went to Indra"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-30"><data key="title">Lord Shiva Appears in the Guise of Kirat</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Arjuna did a tremendous penance by standing only on one leg and concentrating his gaze upon the blazing sun,. The deities became very impressed by Arjuna's penance. They went to lord Shiva and requested him to bless Arjuna. Lord Shiva accepted their request. Duryodhan had sent a demon named Mooka to kill Arjuna. Mooka…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 18, "quote": "5.3.30 Lord Shiva Appears in the Guise of Kirat Arjuna did a tremendous penance by standing only on one leg and concentrating his gaze upon the blazing sun,. The deities became very impressed by Arjuna's penance. They went to lord Shiva a"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-31"><data key="title">Kirat Blesses Arjun</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva sent his ganas to bring back the arrow. Similarly Arjuna too went near the dead boar to bring his arrow. Arjuna found his arrow lying on the ground. He picked it up in his hand. Right then the ganas arrived there and asked Arjuna to return that arrow as it belonged to their master (Shiva). But Arjuna…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 18, "quote": "5.3.31 Kirat Blesses Arjun Lord Shiva sent his ganas to bring back the arrow. Similarly Arjuna too went near the dead boar to bring his arrow. Arjuna found his arrow lying on the ground. He picked it up in his hand. R"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-3-32"><data key="title">The Twelve Jyotirlingas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing about the twelve Jyotirlingas, Sutji told the sages:- "There are twelve Jyotirlingas which are as Follows : 1) Somnath in Saurashtra, 2) Mallikarjuna in SriShail, 3) Mahakal in Ujjain, 4) Amareshwar in Omkar, 5) Kedar in the Himalaya, 6) Bheemashankar at the bank of Bheema river. 7) Vishwanath in Varanasi.…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 18, "quote": "5.3.32 The Twelve Jyotirlingas Describing about the twelve Jyotirlingas, Sutji told the sages:- \"There are twelve Jyotirlingas which are as Follows : 1) Somnath in Saurashtra, 2) Mallikarjuna in SriShail, 3) Mahakal in Uj"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4"><data key="title">Koti Rudra Samhita</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 19, "quote": "5.4 Koti Rudra Samhita"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-1"><data key="title">The Greatness of Upalingas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After describing about the twelve Jyotirlinga, Sutji mentioned about the various Upalingas originating from them. The Upalinga named Someshwar is situated at a place where the earth meets the ocean. This Upalinga is also known as 'Antakesh'. The Upalinga originating from Mallikarjuna is famous by the name of…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 19, "quote": "5.4.1 The Greatness of Upalingas After describing about the twelve Jyotirlinga, Sutji mentioned about the various Upalingas originating from them. The Upalinga named Someshwar is situated at a place where the earth meets th"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-2"><data key="title">Atri and Anusuya do Penance</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sage Atri and his wife Anusuya were doing penance in the forest called Kamad, situated near the Chitrakut mountain. Once it did not rain for many days. As a result people living in that area faced a severe drought. Anusuya requested her husband to help out the people from their hardships. Sage Atri sat down to…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 20, "quote": "5.4.2 Atri and Anusuya do Penance Sage Atri and his wife Anusuya were doing penance in the forest called Kamad, situated near the Chitrakut mountain. Once it did not rain for many days. As a result people living in that area"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-3"><data key="title">The Greatness of Atri</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">While doing penance, sage Atri felt thirsty. He requested Anusuya to fetch some water. Anusuya went with a Kamandal, in search of water but did not find it anywhere. Ganga appeared before her and said- "I am very pleased with you. You can demand anything from me you like." Anusuya demanded only a Kamandal full of…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 20, "quote": "5.4.3 The Greatness of Atri While doing penance, sage Atri felt thirsty. He requested Anusuya to fetch some water. Anusuya went with a Kamandal, in search of water but did not find it anywhere. Ganga appeared before he"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-4"><data key="title">The Brahmin Woman Attains to Heaven</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once upon a time, there lived a brahmin at a place called 'Karni' situated at the bank of river 'Reva'. When the brahmin became old, he went to Kashi after leaving behind his wife to live with his sons. After sometime the brahmin died. When the brahmins sons came to know about his death, they went to Kashi and…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 20, "quote": "5.4.4 The Brahmin Woman Attains to Heaven Once upon a time, there lived a brahmin at a place called 'Karni' situated at the bank of river 'Reva'. When the brahmin became old, he went to Kashi after leaving behind his wife to live wi"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-5"><data key="title">Mahabal Shiva Linga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing about Mahabal Shiva Linga, Sutji said to the sages- "Mahabal Shivalinga is situated at Gokarna area. A devotee who has a darshan of Mahabal Shivalinga on the eighth or fourteenth day of 'Ardranakshatra falling on Monday, becomes free from all of his sins and attains to the Shivaloka." Having a darshan of…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 21, "quote": "5.4.5 Mahabal Shiva Linga Describing about Mahabal Shiva Linga, Sutji said to the sages- \"Mahabal Shivalinga is situated at Gokarna area. A devotee who has a darshan of Mahabal Shivalinga on the eighth or fourteenth"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-6"><data key="title">The Reason Behind Phallic Worship</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages curiously asked Sutji about the purpose with which Parvati had decided to appear in the form of a Vagina. Sutji narrated the following tale: "Long long ago, some sages used to do penance in a Shiva temple situated near Daruk forest. One day they went to collect woods needed for the Yagya. Lord Shiva wanted…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 22, "quote": "5.4.6 The Reason Behind Phallic Worship The sages curiously asked Sutji about the purpose with which Parvati had decided to appear in the form of a Vagina. Sutji narrated the following tale: \"Long long ago, some sages used to do p"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-7"><data key="title">The Origin of Batuknath</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Long long ago there lived a brahmin by the name of Dadhichi. His wife to a low caste, though his son - Sudarshan was very learned. The name of his wife was Tukula. She had her husband under total control and influence. Sudarshan had four sons. One day Dadhichi planned to go out due to some work. He entrusted the job…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 22, "quote": "5.4.7 The Origin of Batuknath Long long ago there lived a brahmin by the name of Dadhichi. His wife to a low caste, though his son - Sudarshan was very learned. The name of his wife was Tukula. She had her husband under"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-8"><data key="title">The Origin of Somnath</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The moon had twenty seven wives, one of whom was Rohini. The moon loved Rohini very much, which made the rest of his wives very jealous and angry. They went to their father - Daksha and complained about Moon's behaviour. Daksha went to Moon and advised him to give proper attention to all of his wives. But it did not…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 22, "quote": "5.4.8 The Origin of Somnath The moon had twenty seven wives, one of whom was Rohini. The moon loved Rohini very much, which made the rest of his wives very jealous and angry. They went to their father - Daksha and comp"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-9"><data key="title">The Origin of Mallikarjuna</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, under the influence of Sage Narad, Kartikeya had gone to the Kraunch Mountain to do penance. Shiva and Parvati could not bear the pain of his separation for long. Both of them went to the Kraunch mountain to see him. When Kartikeya saw them coming he moved to some other place. When lord Shiva saw this he…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 23, "quote": "5.4.9 The Origin of Mallikarjuna Once, under the influence of Sage Narad, Kartikeya had gone to the Kraunch Mountain to do penance. Shiva and Parvati could not bear the pain of his separation for long. Both of them went to"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-10"><data key="title">The Origin of Mahakaleshwar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There used to live a brahmin at Avantikapuri. He was a great devotee of lord Shiva and used to worship dialy. This brahmin had four sons whose names were Devapriya, Medhapriya, Sukrit and Dharmabahu. By the blessings of lord Shiva the brahmin enjoyed a very happy and contented life. After his death, the brahmin's son…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 23, "quote": "5.4.10 The Origin of Mahakaleshwar There used to live a brahmin at Avantikapuri. He was a great devotee of lord Shiva and used to worship dialy. This brahmin had four sons whose names were Devapriya, Medhapriya, Sukrit and Dh"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-11"><data key="title">The Origin of Omkareshwar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, Narad had gone to meet Vindhya mountain. Vindhya was very arrogant in his behaviour. Narad told him that Sumeru mountain was even greater than him and so his false pride had no basis. Vindhya became very dejected and disheartened. He went to Amgreshwar and started worshipping Shiva after making a Parthiv linga.…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 23, "quote": "5.4.11 The Origin of Omkareshwar Once, Narad had gone to meet Vindhya mountain. Vindhya was very arrogant in his behaviour. Narad told him that Sumeru mountain was even greater than him and so his false pride had no basis."}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-12"><data key="title">The Origin of Kedareshwar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Two incarnations of Lord Vishnu by the names of Nara and Vishnu did penance at Badrikasharam. Both of them used to worship the idol of Shiva daily. Lord Shiva used to arrive in his subtle form and accept their offerings without being noticed by them. One day Lord Shiva appeared before them. Nara and Narayana eulogized…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 24, "quote": "5.4.12 The Origin of Kedareshwar Two incarnations of Lord Vishnu by the names of Nara and Vishnu did penance at Badrikasharam. Both of them used to worship the idol of Shiva daily. Lord Shiva used to arrive in his subtle fo"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-13"><data key="title">The Origin of Bheema Shankar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Bheema - the demon, was the son of Kumbhakarna and Karkati. After Kumbhakarna was killed by Sri Ram. Karkati and Bheema went to live at Sahya mountain. When Bheema grew up he asked Karkati about his father. Karkati told him that his father had been killed by Ram. Bheema swore to avenge his father's death. He did a…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 24, "quote": "5.4.13 The Origin of Bheema Shankar Bheema - the demon, was the son of Kumbhakarna and Karkati. After Kumbhakarna was killed by Sri Ram. Karkati and Bheema went to live at Sahya mountain. When Bheema grew up he asked Karkati a"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-14"><data key="title">The Origin of Vishveshwar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">With the desire of giving liberation to the living creatures of this world, lord Shiva had kept some portion of land on his Trishul after separating it from the rest of Brahma's creation. The name of this sacred place is Manikarnika. The Shiva linga by the name of Avinukta was established by lord Shiva himself/ Later…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 25, "quote": "5.4.14 The Origin of Vishveshwar With the desire of giving liberation to the living creatures of this world, lord Shiva had kept some portion of land on his Trishul after separating it from the rest of Brahma's creation. Th"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-15"><data key="title">The Origin of Trayambakeshwar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">During the ancient time there lived a famous sage named Gautami. The name of his wife was Ahilya. Once it did not rain for one hundred years as the result of which the whole area was affected by drought. Sage Gautami did a tremendous penance to please Varun appeared before Gautam, he was requested to cause rain. But…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 25, "quote": "5.4.15 The Origin of Trayambakeshwar During the ancient time there lived a famous sage named Gautami. The name of his wife was Ahilya. Once it did not rain for one hundred years as the result of which the whole area was affecte"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-16"><data key="title">The Origin of Baidyanath</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The demon king- Ravana did a tremendous penance to please lord Shiva and to get a boon from him. Even after his severe penance when lord Shiva did still not appear, he started offering his heads one by one to lord Shiva. This way he already severed his nine heads and offered them to Shiva. When he was about to severe…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 26, "quote": "5.4.16 The Origin of Baidyanath The demon king- Ravana did a tremendous penance to please lord Shiva and to get a boon from him. Even after his severe penance when lord Shiva did still not appear, he started offering his h"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-17"><data key="title">The Origin of Nageshwar Jyotirlinga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">During ancient times, there lived a demon named Daruk. The name of his wife was Daruka. They used to torment the people living in that area. The residents of that area went to sage 'Aursh' and narrated about their miseries and requested him to end this menace. 'Aursh' assured them that very soon their hardships would…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 26, "quote": "5.4.17 The Origin of Nageshwar Jyotirlinga During ancient times, there lived a demon named Daruk. The name of his wife was Daruka. They used to torment the people living in that area. The residents of that area went to sage 'Aursh' a"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-18"><data key="title">Establishment of Rameshwar</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After Hanuman brought the new that Sita had been held captive by Ravana at Lanka, Sri Ram proceeded towards Lanka with a huge army. After receiving the seashore he faced the problems of crossing over the sea. Sri Ram made a Parthiva linga of Shiva and worshipped it. Lord Shiva became very please with him and appeared.…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 27, "quote": "5.4.18 Establishment of Rameshwar After Hanuman brought the new that Sita had been held captive by Ravana at Lanka, Sri Ram proceeded towards Lanka with a huge army. After receiving the seashore he faced the problems of cros"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-19"><data key="title">The Origin of Dhushmesh Jyotirlinga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There used to live a brahmin by the name of Sudharma, who belonged to the lineage of sage Bhardwaj. He was a great devotee of lord Shiva. Sudeha was his wife. Sudharma had no son. Sudeha desires for a son. She expressed her desire to Sudharma but he did not listen to her pleas. One day Sudeha had gone for an outing…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 27, "quote": "5.4.19 The Origin of Dhushmesh Jyotirlinga There used to live a brahmin by the name of Sudharma, who belonged to the lineage of sage Bhardwaj. He was a great devotee of lord Shiva. Sudeha was his wife. Sudharma had no son. Sudeha des"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-20"><data key="title">Lord Vishnu Receives Sudarshan Chakra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The deities went to lord Vishnu, after being tormented by the demons. They requested him to annihilate the demons. After assuring them, Lord Vishnu went to Kailash mountain to do his penance. But even his tremendous penance was not enough to please Lord Shiva. Lord Vishnu then worshipped lord Shiva by chanting the…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 28, "quote": "5.4.20 Lord Vishnu Receives Sudarshan Chakra The deities went to lord Vishnu, after being tormented by the demons. They requested him to annihilate the demons. After assuring them, Lord Vishnu went to Kailash mountain to do his penance"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-21"><data key="title">Shiva Sahastranaam</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">Sutji told the sages that lord Vishnu had worshipped lord Shiva with the help of his one thousand names. Some of the chief names of Shiva are Shiva, Har, Mrid, Rudra, Pushkar, Pushpalochan, Arthigamya, Sadachar, Sharv, Shambhu, Maheshwar, etc.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 28, "quote": "5.4.21 Shiva Sahastranaam Sutji told the sages that lord Vishnu had worshipped lord Shiva with the help of his one thousand names. Some of the chief names of Shiva are Shiva, Har, Mrid, Rudra, Pushkar, Pushpalochan,"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-22"><data key="title">The Greatness of Sahastranaam</data><data key="type">hymn</data><data key="summary">A devotee who chants these one thousand names of lord Shiva attains all the accomplishments. Chanting it during distress helps a man to become free from all kings of misfortune. Lord Vishnu could kill the demons only because of the power he derived from the chanting of Sahastranaam.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 28, "quote": "5.4.22 The Greatness of Sahastranaam A devotee who chants these one thousand names of lord Shiva attains all the accomplishments. Chanting it during distress helps a man to become free from all kings of misfortune. Lord Vishnu"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-23"><data key="title">The Devotees of Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing about the numerous devotees of Shiva who were famous for their devotion towards lord Shiva, Sutji named a few of them like Durvasa, Vishwamitra, Dadhichi, Gautam, Kanad, Bhargav, Vrihaspati, Vaishampayam, Parashar, Vyas, Upamanyu, Yagyavalkya, Jaimini and Garg etc. He also narrated a tale connected with…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 29, "quote": "5.4.23 The Devotees of Shiva Describing about the numerous devotees of Shiva who were famous for their devotion towards lord Shiva, Sutji named a few of them like Durvasa, Vishwamitra, Dadhichi, Gautam, Kanad, Bhargav,"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-24"><data key="title">Austerities &amp; Fastings Connected with Shiva’s Worship</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once Parvati asked lord Shiva as to which were the austerities when on being performed by a devotee, helps him to attain both worldly accomplishments as well as liberation. Lord Shiva told her about ten austerities connected with his worship and their methods. He said- "On the eighty day of every month a devotee…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 29, "quote": "5.4.24 Austerities &amp; Fastings Connected with Shiva’s Worship Once Parvati asked lord Shiva as to which were the austerities when on being performed by a devotee, helps him to attain both worldly accomplishments as well as liberation. Lord Shiva told h"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-25"><data key="title">Mahashivaratri</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Lord Shiva continued with his statements- "Among all the austerities and fastings Mahashivaratri holds a supreme place. It falls on the fourteenth day of the dark lunar month of Phalgun. On this day the devotee should take a vow to observe a fast after awakening in the morning and finishing his daily choirs. He must…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 29, "quote": "5.4.25 Mahashivaratri Lord Shiva continued with his statements- \"Among all the austerities and fastings Mahashivaratri holds a supreme place. It falls on the fourteenth day of the dark lunar month of Phalgun. On"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-26"><data key="title">Accomplishment of Shivratri (Uddyapan)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After observing fasts for fourteen consecutive years on each of the Shivaratri days, a devotee should perform 'Uddyapan' (religious rites performed on the accomplishment of an observance). On the day preceding the Uddyapan ceremony, a devotee must have food for one time. The next day a devotee should perform Uddyapan…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 29, "quote": "5.4.26 Accomplishment of Shivratri (Uddyapan) After observing fasts for fourteen consecutive years on each of the Shivaratri days, a devotee should perform 'Uddyapan' (religious rites performed on the accomplishment of an observance). O"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-27"><data key="title">The Greatness of Shivaratri</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There used to live a poor bheel named Gurudruha. He was very poor. One day finding no food available in his house, he went to the forest to hunt an animal. Unfortunately he did not find any animal which he could hunt. Being hungry, he sat down on the bank of a pond and pondered as to what would happen to his family…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 30, "quote": "5.4.27 The Greatness of Shivaratri There used to live a poor bheel named Gurudruha. He was very poor. One day finding no food available in his house, he went to the forest to hunt an animal. Unfortunately he did not find any"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-4-28"><data key="title">Different Stages of Liberation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Regarding the different stages of liberation, Sutji told the sages that there were five stages of liberation capable of freeing a man from the sorrows of this world. 1) Sarupya (Similitude with the Almighty), 2) Salokya, 3) Sannidhya (living in the proximity of the almighty), 4) Sayujya (getting united with the…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 31, "quote": "5.4.28 Different Stages of Liberation Regarding the different stages of liberation, Sutji told the sages that there were five stages of liberation capable of freeing a man from the sorrows of this world. 1) Sarupya (Similitude w"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5"><data key="title">Uma Samhita</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 32, "quote": "5.5 Uma Samhita"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-1"><data key="title">Conversation Between Krshna &amp; Upamanyu</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, Lord Krishna went to Kailash mountain to do penance with the desire of having a son. He found Maharishi Upamanyu engrossed in his meditation. He told Upamanyu about his desire and requested him to describe about the greatness of lord Shiva. Upamanu described about his own experience when he had divine glimpses…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 32, "quote": "5.5.1 Conversation Between Krshna &amp; Upamanyu Once, Lord Krishna went to Kailash mountain to do penance with the desire of having a son. He found Maharishi Upamanyu engrossed in his meditation. He told Upamanyu about his desire and requ"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-2"><data key="title">Sri Krshna Does Penance</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sri Krishna then proceeded to do his penance after getting initiated by Upamanyu with the mantra - Om Namah Shivay. He did a tremendous penance for fifteen months by standing on his toe. In the sixteenth month lord Shiva and Parvati appeared before him after being pleased by his penance. Lord Shiva expressed his…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 32, "quote": "5.5.2 Sri Krshna Does Penance Sri Krishna then proceeded to do his penance after getting initiated by Upamanyu with the mantra - Om Namah Shivay. He did a tremendous penance for fifteen months by standing on his toe. In"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-3"><data key="title">Classification of Grave Sins</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing about the sins, Sutji told the sages that altogether there were twelve types of sins committed by a man by his actions, thought speech. Out of them having a desire for another person's wife, being desirous of another's wealth, having evil designs against other people and drifting towards sinful path are…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 32, "quote": "5.5.3 Classification of Grave Sins Describing about the sins, Sutji told the sages that altogether there were twelve types of sins committed by a man by his actions, thought speech. Out of them having a desire for another per"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-4"><data key="title">The Description of Hell</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Each living being has to taste the fruits of his 'Karmas', when his soul reaches the abode of Yama after his death. The soul of a virtuous man enters the hell from the Northern side, where as that of sinful man enters from the southern side. Yamanagri - The abode of Yama is situated at a distance of eighty six…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 33, "quote": "5.5.4 The Description of Hell Each living being has to taste the fruits of his 'Karmas', when his soul reaches the abode of Yama after his death. The soul of a virtuous man enters the hell from the Northern side, where a"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-5"><data key="title">Charity and its Importance</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sutji while warning the sages told- "People who engages themselves in virtuous deeds through out their lives and who have been kind of others, cross the terrifying path of the hell quite easily. A person, who donates shoes or wooden sandals to brahmins, reaches the hell riding on the horse. Similarly, a person who…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 33, "quote": "5.5.5 Charity and its Importance Sutji while warning the sages told- \"People who engages themselves in virtuous deeds through out their lives and who have been kind of others, cross the terrifying path of the hell quite eas"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-6"><data key="title">The Description of the Netherworld (Patal Loka)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The earth is balanced on the hood of Sheshnag. Lord Vishnu is the nurturer of this world. There are seven more worlds beneath the earth, which are Atala, Vitala, Sutala, Rasatala, Tala, Talatala and Patal. Each of these worlds is ten thousand yojans in length and twenty thousand yojans deep. All these worlds are full…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 33, "quote": "5.5.6 The Description of the Netherworld (Patal Loka) The earth is balanced on the hood of Sheshnag. Lord Vishnu is the nurturer of this world. There are seven more worlds beneath the earth, which are Atala, Vitala, Sutala, Rasatala, Tala, Tala"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-7"><data key="title">Liberation From Hell</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Even farther than these seven worlds is situated the hell, Where the soul of the sinner goes to after the death. Some of the main names of hell are - Raurav, Shukar, Rodh, Taal, Vivasan and Mahajwala etc. The soul of a sinner goes to each of these hells according to the magnitude of his sins. A man should worship lord…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 34, "quote": "5.5.7 Liberation From Hell Even farther than these seven worlds is situated the hell, Where the soul of the sinner goes to after the death. Some of the main names of hell are - Raurav, Shukar, Rodh, Taal, Vivasan and"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-8"><data key="title">The Description of Seven Islands</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The whole earth is divided into seven islands or dweepas. The name of these islands is Jambu, Plaksha, Shalmali, Kraunch, Shaaka and Pushkar. All these islands are surrounded by such oceans on all sides. The content of each of these oceans is salt. Sugarcane juice, ghee, milk, curds and honey respectively. Jambudweepa…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 34, "quote": "5.5.8 The Description of Seven Islands The whole earth is divided into seven islands or dweepas. The name of these islands is Jambu, Plaksha, Shalmali, Kraunch, Shaaka and Pushkar. All these islands are surrounded by such oceans"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-9"><data key="title">The Description of Planets</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">As far as the light of Sun and Moon reaches the earth, it is called 'Bhoo loka'. Surya loka is situated one lakh yojan above the Bhooloka. The Chandra loka is situated one lakh yojan above Surya loka. All the constellations as well as the planets are spread in the area of the ten thousand yojans above the Moon.…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 34, "quote": "5.5.9 The Description of Planets As far as the light of Sun and Moon reaches the earth, it is called 'Bhoo loka'. Surya loka is situated one lakh yojan above the Bhooloka. The Chandra loka is situated one lakh yojan above S"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-10"><data key="title">Categories of Austerity (Tapa)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Truth or Satya is the best among all the types of austerity. A truthful man is the supreme devotee and a great accomplisher. Austerity helps a man to attain all the worldly pleasures of life and ultimately salvation. Austerities have been classified into three types on the basis of three basic qualities or gunas i.e.…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 35, "quote": "5.5.10 Categories of Austerity (Tapa) Truth or Satya is the best among all the types of austerity. A truthful man is the supreme devotee and a great accomplisher. Austerity helps a man to attain all the worldly pleasures of life"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-11"><data key="title">The Origin of Body</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The food intaken by a man gets transformed into 'semen' in the body. The semen is transformed to the woman's womb during the time of intercourse where it gets mixed with the blood. It then develops into an egg. Gradually all the organs start to manifest themselves. The body then attains the vital force and ultimately…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 35, "quote": "5.5.11 The Origin of Body The food intaken by a man gets transformed into 'semen' in the body. The semen is transformed to the woman's womb during the time of intercourse where it gets mixed with the blood. It then d"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-12"><data key="title">Purification of the Body</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Before taking birth, the body lives a very filthy environment in mother's womb. It becomes so dirty that even Panchagavya can not make it pure. Remembrance of Shiva is the only means to make it pure.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 35, "quote": "5.5.12 Purification of the Body Before taking birth, the body lives a very filthy environment in mother's womb. It becomes so dirty that even Panchagavya can not make it pure. Remembrance of Shiva is the only means to make"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-13"><data key="title">Control over Death (Ichchha Mrityu)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sutji told the sages that a person who wants to control death should first be seated on a pure seat. He should then perform Pranayama by holding his breath. While doing this exercise care should be taken that lamp is not burning. Both the ears should be shut by the index fingers for the period of one hour. After doing…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 35, "quote": "5.5.13 Control over Death (Ichchha Mrityu) Sutji told the sages that a person who wants to control death should first be seated on a pure seat. He should then perform Pranayama by holding his breath. While doing this exercise care sh"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-14"><data key="title">The Descent of Ganga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, emperor Sagar performed an Ashwamedha Yagya. Indra abducted the horse used in this Yagya and kept it in the hermitage of Sage Kapil. All the sixty thousand sons of Sagar went to search the horse and found it in Kapil's hermitage. Hearing the commotion and noise, sage Kapil who was engrossed in his meditation…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 35, "quote": "5.5.14 The Descent of Ganga Once, emperor Sagar performed an Ashwamedha Yagya. Indra abducted the horse used in this Yagya and kept it in the hermitage of Sage Kapil. All the sixty thousand sons of Sagar went to search"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-15"><data key="title">The Austerities of Ved Vyasa</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Narrating about the life of Sage Ved Vyas, Sutji said to the sages- "Once while sage Parashar was on his pilgrimage, he arrived at the bank of Yamuna. Satyavati, who emitted the smell of fish, helped him cross the river. Sage Parashar was very pleased with her. He transformed the bad smell emitting from her body into…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">36</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 36, "quote": "5.5.15 The Austerities of Ved Vyasa Narrating about the life of Sage Ved Vyas, Sutji said to the sages- \"Once while sage Parashar was on his pilgrimage, he arrived at the bank of Yamuna. Satyavati, who emitted the smell of fis"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-16"><data key="title">Incarnation of Mahakali</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once upon a time king Surath after loosing his kingdom to his enemy fled to the forest. After wandering for some time he reached the hermitage of sage Medha. He started living there, as he had no place to go. The dejected king used to pass his time wondering about his future. One day, Surath met a man named Samadhi,…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">36</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 36, "quote": "5.5.16 Incarnation of Mahakali Once upon a time king Surath after loosing his kingdom to his enemy fled to the forest. After wandering for some time he reached the hermitage of sage Medha. He started living there, as he h"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-17"><data key="title">Incarnation of Mahalaxmi</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Mahishasur - the son of Rambhasur had captured the heaven after defeating the deities. The sorrowful deities came to seek the help of lord Vishnu and Lord Shiva. Lord Shiva and Lord Vishnu became extremely furious after hearing about the misdeeds of Mahishasur. Radiant lights appeared from the bodies of Vishnu, Shiva…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">37</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 37, "quote": "5.5.17 Incarnation of Mahalaxmi Mahishasur - the son of Rambhasur had captured the heaven after defeating the deities. The sorrowful deities came to seek the help of lord Vishnu and Lord Shiva. Lord Shiva and Lord Vishnu b"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-18"><data key="title">Incarnation of Mahasarasvati</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">After being tormented by the demons Shumbha and Nishumbha, the deities went to goddess Parvati and requested her to protect them. Kaushiki manifested from the body of goddess Parvati. Goddess Kaushiki assured the deities and disappeared. By coincidence the attendants of the demons - Shumbha and Nishumbha, happened to…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">37</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 37, "quote": "5.5.18 Incarnation of Mahasarasvati After being tormented by the demons Shumbha and Nishumbha, the deities went to goddess Parvati and requested her to protect them. Kaushiki manifested from the body of goddess Parvati. Goddes"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-19"><data key="title">Uma Warns the Deities</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Once, after having defeated the demons the deities had become very arrogant and proud. They started indulging in self praise. Suddenly they were started by the emergence of radiant light before them. All the deities became scarred and went to Indra. Indra sent Vayu to test the power of that radiant light. As soon as…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">37</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 37, "quote": "5.5.19 Uma Warns the Deities Once, after having defeated the demons the deities had become very arrogant and proud. They started indulging in self praise. Suddenly they were started by the emergence of radiant light bef"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-20"><data key="title">The Incarnation of Shakambari</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">The most valiant demon Durgam had acquired all the four Vedas from lord Brahma after pleasing him. Lord Brahma had also given a boon of invincibility to him. Durgam became very arrogant and started tormenting the whole world. As the consequence it did not rain for one hundred years and the whole world was hit by…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">38</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 38, "quote": "5.5.20 The Incarnation of Shakambari The most valiant demon Durgam had acquired all the four Vedas from lord Brahma after pleasing him. Lord Brahma had also given a boon of invincibility to him. Durgam became very arrogant and"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-6"><data key="title">Kailash Samhita</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">38</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 38, "quote": "5.6 Kailash Samhita"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-6-1"><data key="title">Classifications of Yoga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing about the various types of Yoga, Sutji told the sages that there were three types of Yoga - Gyan Yoga, Kriya Yoga and Bhakti Yoga. "Each of them is capable of giving salvation to a man. When the mind or intellect unites with the soul it is called Gyan Yoga. When the soul gets attached with external objects,…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">38</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 38, "quote": "5.6.1 Classifications of Yoga Describing about the various types of Yoga, Sutji told the sages that there were three types of Yoga - Gyan Yoga, Kriya Yoga and Bhakti Yoga. \"Each of them is capable of giving salvation to"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-6-2"><data key="title">Conduct of a Sanyasi</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A Sanyasi should wake up early in the morning. After getting up he should remember his preceptor or Guru and then he should pray to express his gratitude towards his Guru. After that he should practice Pranayama and try to concentrate his mind on the six chakras present in his bodies. After the completion of Pranayama…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">38</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 38, "quote": "5.6.2 Conduct of a Sanyasi A Sanyasi should wake up early in the morning. After getting up he should remember his preceptor or Guru and then he should pray to express his gratitude towards his Guru. After that he shou"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-6-3"><data key="title">Methods of Shiva Worship</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A Sanyasi should paste the ground with cow dung and construct a quadrangular 'Mandap'. He should then keep a broad leaf of Palm tree at its centre. He should then fence the area with coloured threads from all the four directions. After that he should draw a eight petalled flower in the centre of the Mandap, upon which…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">39</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 39, "quote": "5.6.3 Methods of Shiva Worship A Sanyasi should paste the ground with cow dung and construct a quadrangular 'Mandap'. He should then keep a broad leaf of Palm tree at its centre. He should then fence the area with coloure"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-6-4"><data key="title">The Greatness of Pranay Mantra</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Sage Vamadev was a great devotee of lord Shiva from his birth. He never used to remain at a place for long. One day he had gone to a mountain peak named Kumar, which was situated towards the south of Sumeru Mountain. He met Kartikeya who revealed to him that Pranam Mantra directly signifies the almighty God. Kartikeya…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">39</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 39, "quote": "5.6.4 The Greatness of Pranay Mantra Sage Vamadev was a great devotee of lord Shiva from his birth. He never used to remain at a place for long. One day he had gone to a mountain peak named Kumar, which was situated towards the"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-6-5"><data key="title">Initiation of a Sanyasi</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Vamadeva requested Kartikeya to enlighten his mind with that knowledge, without which a Sanyasi can never attain liberation. Kartikeya then told him about the methods how a Sanyasi should get initiation from his guru. A disciple should worship his guru in any of the following months - Shravan, Ashwin, Kartik, Agahan…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">39</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 39, "quote": "5.6.5 Initiation of a Sanyasi Vamadeva requested Kartikeya to enlighten his mind with that knowledge, without which a Sanyasi can never attain liberation. Kartikeya then told him about the methods how a Sanyasi should ge"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-6-6"><data key="title">Last Rites of an Ascetic (Sanyasi)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Kartikey told Sage Vamadev that an ascetic does not die but takes a Samadhi, therefore instead of being cremated he is buried. Therefore an ascetic must practice the art of Samadhi to perfection. If he has not yet perfected the art of Samadhi, then he should keep on practicing yoga till he attains mastery over the art…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">39</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 39, "quote": "5.6.6 Last Rites of an Ascetic (Sanyasi) Kartikey told Sage Vamadev that an ascetic does not die but takes a Samadhi, therefore instead of being cremated he is buried. Therefore an ascetic must practice the art of Samadhi to perfec"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7"><data key="title">Vayviya Samhita</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Section heading in the supplied retelling. Open the PDF page for context.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">40</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 40, "quote": "5.7 Vayviya Samhita"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-1"><data key="title">The Origin of Vidya (Knowledge)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There are fourteen types of learning or Vidyas - four Vedas, Six Vedangs, Meemansa, Nyay, Puranas and other religious scriptures. These fourteen learning along with Ayurveda, Dhanurveda, Gandharvaveda and Arthashastra becomes eighteen. All these eighteen learning's originate from lord Shiva. Lord Shiva created lord…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">40</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 40, "quote": "5.7.1 The Origin of Vidya (Knowledge) There are fourteen types of learning or Vidyas - four Vedas, Six Vedangs, Meemansa, Nyay, Puranas and other religious scriptures. These fourteen learning along with Ayurveda, Dhanurveda, Gan"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-2"><data key="title">The Tale of Naimisharanya</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Once, lord Brahma released his Manomaya chakra and instructed the sages to follow it. He also told them that the place at which the Manomaya chakra broke down would be very auspicious for doing penance. The sages followed the Manomaya chakra, which after travelling for a long time fell down on a large segment of land…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">40</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 40, "quote": "5.7.2 The Tale of Naimisharanya Once, lord Brahma released his Manomaya chakra and instructed the sages to follow it. He also told them that the place at which the Manomaya chakra broke down would be very auspicious for do"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-3"><data key="title">Time – The Radiance of Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing about the time (Kala), Vayudeva told the sages that 'Kala' or time is the radiance of lord Shiva. Kala or time is also known as 'Kalatma'. The time flows smoothly without being disturbed. Time is under the control of lord Shiva. Since the time contains the element of Shiva (Shivattatva), hence its momentum…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">41</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 41, "quote": "5.7.3 Time – The Radiance of Shiva Describing about the time (Kala), Vayudeva told the sages that 'Kala' or time is the radiance of lord Shiva. Kala or time is also known as 'Kalatma'. The time flows smoothly without being di"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-4"><data key="title">Time – Calculation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The smallest unit for measuring the time is called 'Nimesh'. The time taken to drop one's eyelid is called one Nimesh. A kala consists of fifteen Nimeshas and thirty Kalas make a 'Muhurta'. A day and a night consist of thirty 'Muhurtas'. A month consists of thirty days, divided into two fortnights. One fortnight is…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">41</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 41, "quote": "5.7.4 Time – Calculation The smallest unit for measuring the time is called 'Nimesh'. The time taken to drop one's eyelid is called one Nimesh. A kala consists of fifteen Nimeshas and thirty Kalas make a 'Muhurta'."}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-5"><data key="title">Meditation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Describing about Meditation Vayudeva told the sages that to concentrate one's restless mind during meditative state, a man needs some kind of form (Swarupa) or appearance. Idol worship is very helpful in this regard. If an idol of the deity is worshipped with full devotion, then a time comes when a man can have its…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">42</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 42, "quote": "5.7.5 Meditation Describing about Meditation Vayudeva told the sages that to concentrate one's restless mind during meditative state, a man needs some kind of form (Swarupa) or appearance. Idol worship is ve"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-6"><data key="title">Rituals Connected with Shiva’s Worship</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">One being asked by the sages about the rituals which gives salvation, Vayudeva told them that by having devotion towards lord Shiva, a man can achieve all the pleasures of the world and even attain salvation. Vayudeva also told them about Pashupat Vrata and the benefits derived from it. The whole Pashupat Vrata is…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">42</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 42, "quote": "5.7.6 Rituals Connected with Shiva’s Worship One being asked by the sages about the rituals which gives salvation, Vayudeva told them that by having devotion towards lord Shiva, a man can achieve all the pleasures of the world and even"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-7"><data key="title">Pashupat Vrata and its Rituals</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Pashupat Vrata has been mentioned even in the Atharvasheersha Upanishad. It begins on the full moon day, in the month of Chaitra. It can be performed anywhere - a Shiva temple, any sacred place of pilgrimage, forest or garden. A devotee should get up early in the morning, on the thirteenth day of bright lunar phase…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">42</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 42, "quote": "5.7.7 Pashupat Vrata and its Rituals Pashupat Vrata has been mentioned even in the Atharvasheersha Upanishad. It begins on the full moon day, in the month of Chaitra. It can be performed anywhere - a Shiva temple, any sacred pl"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-8"><data key="title">Introduction of the Universe</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Vayudeva told the sages who had assembled at Naimisharanya that goddess Gauri was the manifestation of Shiva's power and Shankar the omnipotent almighty. Both Shiva and goddess Shiva contain fathomless splendours of which only a fraction is visible in this world. This world is under the control of Shiva and goddess…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">43</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 43, "quote": "5.7.8 Introduction of the Universe Vayudeva told the sages who had assembled at Naimisharanya that goddess Gauri was the manifestation of Shiva's power and Shankar the omnipotent almighty. Both Shiva and goddess Shiva contain"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-9"><data key="title">Duties of a Brahmin</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">An ideal brahmin is supposed to perform the following duties:- a) Trikal Sandhya (worshipping thrice a day) b) Havan (offerings made to the sacred fire) c) Worship of Shivalinga d) Making donations A brahmin should also have the following qualities:- a) To see god in each soul b) Compassion c) Virtuous conducts d)…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">43</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 43, "quote": "5.7.9 Duties of a Brahmin An ideal brahmin is supposed to perform the following duties:- a) Trikal Sandhya (worshipping thrice a day) b) Havan (offerings made to the sacred fire) c) Worship of Shivalinga d) Making do"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-10"><data key="title">Chanting of Mantras</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">After getting initiated with the Shiva mantra, a devotee should chant it for once crore times or fifty lakh times, or twenty lakh times or ten lakh times, while worshipping Shiva. 'Havana' should be performed for tenth part of the total chanting. 'Tarpan' should be performed for tenth part of the number of Havana.…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">44</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 44, "quote": "5.7.10 Chanting of Mantras After getting initiated with the Shiva mantra, a devotee should chant it for once crore times or fifty lakh times, or twenty lakh times or ten lakh times, while worshipping Shiva. 'Havana' s"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-11"><data key="title">Establishing Deities in the form of Alphabets within the Body</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">(MATRIKA NYAS) Asceticism (Nyas) are of three types - Sthiti (posture) Utpatti (origin) and Laya (fusion). Establishing and chanting the cryptic mantras (alphabets) from the thumb to the little finger is called 'Sthiti nyas'. Establishing and then chanting the cryptic mantras from the right thumb to the left thumb is…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">44</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 44, "quote": "5.7.11 Establishing Deities in the form of Alphabets within the Body (MATRIKA NYAS) Asceticism (Nyas) are of three types - Sthiti (posture) Utpatti (origin) and Laya (fusion). Establishing and chanting the cryptic mantras (alphabets) from the thumb to the lit"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-12"><data key="title">Mental Worship of Shiva</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">While doing a mental worship of Shiva, a devotee should worship lord Ganesha by following the 'Nyas' mode of worship. After that he should worship various deities related with lord Shiva like Nandi etc. Then he should bring the form of lord Shiva into his imagination and worship him mentally by making mental…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">44</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 44, "quote": "5.7.12 Mental Worship of Shiva While doing a mental worship of Shiva, a devotee should worship lord Ganesha by following the 'Nyas' mode of worship. After that he should worship various deities related with lord Shiva lik"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-13"><data key="title">Method of Doing Mental Worship</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The devotee should take his seat only after purifying it. He should take a vow after completing all the necessary rituals like 'Achaman'. Pranayam, tying a knot in his Shikha etc. After doing the 'Deepa Pujan' he should worship various deities like Sun god, Moon god, Varun, Ganesha and Kartikeya etc. He should then…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">44</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 44, "quote": "5.7.13 Method of Doing Mental Worship The devotee should take his seat only after purifying it. He should take a vow after completing all the necessary rituals like 'Achaman'. Pranayam, tying a knot in his Shikha etc. After doin"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-14"><data key="title">Method of Performing Havan</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">While performing a Shiva Yagya a devotee should make offerings to the 'havanakunda' made of iron or mud. Fire should be ignited in the havankunda following the methods as prescribed in the scriptures. He can then commence the actual worship. The devotee should make of offerings of ghee with Sruva (a wooden spoon) and…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">45</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 45, "quote": "5.7.14 Method of Performing Havan While performing a Shiva Yagya a devotee should make offerings to the 'havanakunda' made of iron or mud. Fire should be ignited in the havankunda following the methods as prescribed in the s"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-15"><data key="title">Auspicious Days for Performing Shiva Worship</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The eighth day and the fourteenth day of both the fortnights of each Hindu month are considered to be the most auspicious for the worship of lord Shiva. Similarly the solstice day (Sankranti), when the sun is positioned north of equator and eclipse day are considered to be very auspicious. On these days a special…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">45</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 45, "quote": "5.7.15 Auspicious Days for Performing Shiva Worship The eighth day and the fourteenth day of both the fortnights of each Hindu month are considered to be the most auspicious for the worship of lord Shiva. Similarly the solstice day (Sankranti"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-16"><data key="title">Desirable Rituals (Kamya Karma)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Rituals can be categorized in to two types- 1) Rituals performed with the objective of fulfilling worldly desires. 2) Rituals pertaining to Ultra mundan desires. In general, rituals can be classified into five categories - rituals, penance, chanting of mantras, meditation, and all the four collectively. For…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">45</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 45, "quote": "5.7.16 Desirable Rituals (Kamya Karma) Rituals can be categorized in to two types- 1) Rituals performed with the objective of fulfilling worldly desires. 2) Rituals pertaining to Ultra mundan desires. In general, rituals can be c"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-17"><data key="title">Rituals Pertaining to Shiva Worship (Shaiva Kamya Karma)</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The methods for the worship of five 'awarans' of Shiva have been mentioned in Shivamahastotra. Each of the 'awarans' have separate presiding deities, the worship of which should be done according to the methods as described in the Shivapuran or as per the instruction of Guru.</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 46, "quote": "5.7.17 Rituals Pertaining to Shiva Worship (Shaiva Kamya Karma) The methods for the worship of five 'awarans' of Shiva have been mentioned in Shivamahastotra. Each of the 'awarans' have separate presiding deities, the worship of which should be done acco"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-18"><data key="title">Installation of Shiva Linga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">According to the scriptures, a Shiva linga should be made in an auspicious moment. The land, where the Shivalinga is supposed to be installed should be purified by performing 'Bhoomi-Pujan'. After bhoomi-Pujan, worshipping lord Ganesha should be done. After that, the Shiva linga should be purified by washing it with…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 46, "quote": "5.7.18 Installation of Shiva Linga According to the scriptures, a Shiva linga should be made in an auspicious moment. The land, where the Shivalinga is supposed to be installed should be purified by performing 'Bhoomi-Pujan'."}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-19"><data key="title">Classification of Yoga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Yoga means such actions, which after pacifying all the human tendencies helps a man to unite with Shiva. Following are the five divisions of yoga- 1) Mantra Yoga, 2) Sparsh Yoga (union of touch), 3) Bhava Yoga (union by devotion), 4) Abhava yoga (Union without being emotionally attached), 5) Mahayoga (The great…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 46, "quote": "5.7.19 Classification of Yoga Yoga means such actions, which after pacifying all the human tendencies helps a man to unite with Shiva. Following are the five divisions of yoga- 1) Mantra Yoga, 2) Sparsh Yoga (union of to"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-20"><data key="title">Hurdle in the Path of Yoga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">There are possibilities of numerous hurdles being faced by a man who practices yoga like laziness, disease, carelessness, lack of concentration, confusion and sorrow etc. While practicing yoga, one must try to keep himself free from such demerits. After becoming liberated from these flaws a man can easily attain six…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 46, "quote": "5.7.20 Hurdle in the Path of Yoga There are possibilities of numerous hurdles being faced by a man who practices yoga like laziness, disease, carelessness, lack of concentration, confusion and sorrow etc. While practicing yo"}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-21"><data key="title">Shiva Yoga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">A devotee can attain all types of accomplishment merely by having devotion towards lord Shiva and by meditating on him. In the beginning, a yogi should try to meditate on the form of Shiva (Saguna), but later on the switch over to Shiva's formless (nirguna) quality. Meditating on nirguna form of Shiva is not easy. It…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">47</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 47, "quote": "5.7.21 Shiva Yoga A devotee can attain all types of accomplishment merely by having devotion towards lord Shiva and by meditating on him. In the beginning, a yogi should try to meditate on the form of Shiva ("}]</data></node><node id="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-7-22"><data key="title">SanatKumar Receive Shiva-Gyan from Nandi</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The sages thanked Vayudeva for giving the knowledge of Gyan-Yoga. The next day, they took bath in river Saraswati and performed their worship and rituals. After that they proceeded towards Kashi. At Kashi after taking their bath in river Ganga they had a darshan of lord Vishwanath. They saw very radiant effulgence…</data><data key="doc">shiv-maha-purana-2</data><data key="page">47</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 47, "quote": "5.7.22 SanatKumar Receive Shiva-Gyan from Nandi The sages thanked Vayudeva for giving the knowledge of Gyan-Yoga. The next day, they took bath in river Saraswati and performed their worship and rituals. After that they proceeded towards K"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-1"><data key="title">Daksha’s Insolence</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 1 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Daksha’s Insolence. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">5</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 5, "quote": "CHAPTER ONE Dak$ll' s Insolence Obc1:,,ance to Sri Ga1:i.csa. Om. obei~ance to Lord Vasudcva. Om. Arter bowing down to Naniya1).a 1 as well as to (S,:1ge) Nara.:! the n1ost excellent one among men, and to god&lt;l..!s'i S.1ra- svati (the godde"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-2"><data key="title">Sati’s Arrival at Daksha’s Sacrifice</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 2 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Sati’s Arrival at Daksha’s Sacrifice. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">11</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 11, "quote": "CHAPTER1WO Satt's Arrival at Dak1a's Sacrifice 1 Lomasa said: I. Once, a great sacrifice was commenced by him (Dak$a). AU were invited to be present there by the ascetic (Dak,a) (who was) initiated for the sacrifice. 2-3. A number of sages"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-3"><data key="title">Virabhadra Comes to the Yajna</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 3 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Virabhadra Comes to the Yajna. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 17, "quote": "CHAPTER THREE Vlrablzadra Comes to the Yajiia Lomasa said: l-4a. Dak~aya)J.i went to the place w