ati: Their Indrahood and rail The sages enquired: 1-2. It has been mentioned by you that Devendra regained kingdom without his preceptor. It was on account of disrespect to his preceptor that he had been dethro"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-16"><data key="title">Dadhichi’s Gift of His Body</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 16 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Dadhichi’s Gift of His Body. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">122</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 122, "quote": "CHAPTER SIXTEEN Dadhlci's Gift of His Body Lomasa said: 1-9. Thereupon Saci spoke to them the following words full of virtuous advice : \"0 Devas with Brhaspati as the head, do nf't get despondent. All of you hasten to the place where the ex"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-17"><data key="title">Vritra Killed; Bali Prepares for War</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 17 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Vritra Killed; Bali Prepares for War. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">130</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 130, "quote": "CHAPTER SEVENTEEN Vrtra Killed: Bali Prepares for War Lomasa said: 1-3. Then on seeing hin1 absorbed (in the Supreme Spitit), the groups of Suras began to think: 'How do we make (the wea- pons out of this body)?' Then the consort ofSacicall"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-18"><data key="title">Vamana Arrives at Bali’s Sacrifice</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 18 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Vamana Arrives at Bali’s Sacrifice. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">155</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 155, "quote": "CHAPTER EIGHTEEN Viimana's Arrival at Bali's Sacrifice Lomasa said: I. Overwhelmed by his own Karman, Mahendra spoke to Brha~pati, his preceptor: \"What is that rite whereby we can surmount a distress without any very great effort? May it (p"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-19"><data key="title">Shukra Curses Bali; Vamana Grants a Boon</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 19 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Shukra Curses Bali; Vamana Grants a Boon. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">173</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 173, "quote": "CHAPTER NINETEEN Sukra Curses Bali: Viimana Grants Boon to Bali Lomala said: 1. On being addressed thus by his preceptor Bhargava, tho Daitya laughed and spoke these words in a voice as majestic as the (thundering) sound of the cloud: 2. \"l"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-20"><data key="title">Shiva Linga and Manifestation of Bhavani</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Chapter 20 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Shiva Linga and Manifestation of Bhavani. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">180</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 180, "quote": "CHAPTER TWENTY The Nirgul)Qtva of the Siva Linga: The Manifestation of Bhavanl The sages said: I. Brahma, Vi~l).u and Rudra were mentioned by you as (deities) possessing Gu,:,as. Similarly Isa has Linga for his form. How is he beyond Gu!las"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-21"><data key="title">Parvati’s Penance</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 21 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Parvati’s Penance. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">185</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 185, "quote": "CHAPTERTWENTYONE Piirvati's Penance Lonaasa said: I. The chaste girl grew up day by day and shone very 1nuch. Living in the house of Himilaya, she reached the age of eight years. 2. At that time Mahesa was performing a great penance in a Do"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-22"><data key="title">Shiva Reveals Himself to Parvati</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 22 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Shiva Reveals Himself to Parvati. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">200</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 200, "quote": "CHAPTER TWENTYTWO Sankara's Revelation of Himself to Piirvati: Their Dialogue Sura said: 1. On being told thus by Vi~9u, the Parame~thin, all of them went ahead desirous of seeing the Pinaka-bearing Mahesa. 2. On the other shore of the Ocea"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-23"><data key="title">Shiva’s Marriage</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 23 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Shiva’s Marriage. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">211</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 211, "quote": "CHAPTER TWENTYTHREE Siva's Marriage 1 Loma::fa said: 1. In the meantime, the sages deputed by Mahesa came there to Himalaya suddenly. 2 2. On seeing them, the Mountain Himalaya got up imme- diately. With a delighted 111ind, he worshipped al"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-24"><data key="title">Marriage Ceremony: Accommodating the Devas</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Chapter 24 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Marriage Ceremony: Accommodating the Devas. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">218</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 218, "quote": "CHAPTER 1WENTYFOUR The Marriage Ceremony of Siva: The Arrangement for Accommodating Devas and Othe,-s Lomasa saitl: J. Similarly, the Lord of Mountains with the greatest joy, made all arrangements for the sake of his daughter. (The Moun- ta"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-25"><data key="title">The Marriage Ritual</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Chapter 25 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: The Marriage Ritual. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">224</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 224, "quote": "CHAPTER 1WENTYFIVE The Marriage Rituals Lomasa said: 1. All of them well-received and welcomed by Himadri sat ( stayed) there along with their retinues and vehicles. Those Devas were highly delighted. 2. There itself (a mansion) of great di"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-26"><data key="title">Shiva–Parvati Marriage Festivities</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 26 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Shiva–Parvati Marriage Festivities. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">231</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 231, "quote": "CHAPTER 1WENTYSIX The Marriage Celebration of Siva and Piirvau: Auspicious Festivities Lomasa said: 1. Then those excellent Mountains beginning with Meru became bewildered. They all addressed Himavan, the great Moun- tain, simultaneously. T"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-27"><data key="title">Birth of Kumara Karttikeya</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 27 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Birth of Kumara Karttikeya. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">236</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 236, "quote": "CHAPTER TWENTYSEVEN The Birth of Kumiira Kiirttikeya Lomasa said: 1-2. Similarly all the Mountains were exquisitely worship- ped by Vi~i:iu, viz. the Mountain Sahya, Vindhya, Mainaka, Gandhamadana, Malyavan, Malaya, Mahcndra, Mandara and Me"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-28"><data key="title">Preparations for War</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 28 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Preparations for War. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">246</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 246, "quote": "CHAPTER TWENTYEIGHT Preparations of Devas and Daityasfor War Lomasa said: 1. After placing Kumara in his Jap, Rudra, the valorous Bharga, the Jord of the universe, said to Devas including lndra: 2. \"What is to be done by my son, 0 Devas? Le"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-29"><data key="title">The Battle against Taraka</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 29 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: The Battle against Taraka. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">251</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 251, "quote": "CHAPTER TWENTYNINE The Battle Between Suras and Tiiraka Lomasa said: I. The two armies, that of Suras and that of Daityas, had all the four divisions of an army. They had many wonderful (weapons and equipments). They roared at each other li"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-30"><data key="title">Taraka Is Slain</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 30 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Taraka Is Slain. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">258</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 258, "quote": "CHAPTER THIRTY Tiiraka Is Slain Lomasa said: 1. As the demon Taraka came there leaping and bouncing, boasting and swaggering, Indra. the most excellent among the intelligent ones, hit him with great force with his Vajra (thunder- bolt). 2."}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-31"><data key="title">The Greatness of Shivalinga</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Chapter 31 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: The Greatness of Shivalinga. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">262</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 262, "quote": "CHAPTER THIRTYONE The Greatness of Sivalinga1 Saunaka said: I. After killing Taraka in the battle, 0 13rahma1_1as, what great deed was done by the noble-souled Kumara? It behoves you to describe everything. 2-3. Kumara indeed is another Sar"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-32"><data key="title">The Burning of Kala</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 32 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: The Burning of Kala. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">272</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 272, "quote": "CHAPTER THIRTY1WO The Burning of Kala 1 Lomasa said: 1. Thus all the rites pertaining to the worship of Siva (Siva-dharmiib) were narrated by him, 0 Brahma:oas. It was out of grace that the Pasupata doctrines were explained in details in pa"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-33"><data key="title">The Greatness of Shivaratri</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Chapter 33 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: The Greatness of Shivaratri. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">281</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 281, "quote": "CHAPTER TH1RTYTHREE The Greatness of Sivaratri Vrata1 The sages said: l. What was the name of that Kirita? What ho1y rite was performed by him? 0 great BrahmaJJa, narrate it. Wf! are very eager. 2. We wish to hear everything. Let it be desc"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-34"><data key="title">Shiva Loses to Parvati at Dice</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 34 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Shiva Loses to Parvati at Dice. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">291</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 291, "quote": "CHAPTER THIRTYFOUR Siva Loses to Piirvatl in a Game of Dice\" Lomasa saicl: 1. The lord of the universe, the lord of Devas, ruled the kingdom while dwelling on Kailasa accompanied by many Gai:ias and in the company of the great (deity) Virab"}]</data></node><node id="p-skanda-ch-35"><data key="title">Parvati as Shabari Brings Shiva Back</data><data key="type">story</data><data key="summary">Chapter 35 of the supplied Kedarakhanda: Parvati as Shabari Brings Shiva Back. The contents list supplies the title and printed-page location.</data><data key="doc">the-skanda-purana</data><data key="page">305</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 305, "quote": "CHAPTER THIRTYFIVE1 Piirvati as Sabarl Brings Back Siva: Siva's Coronation Lomas a said: L When Mahadeva went to the forest, Girija became dejec- ted on account of separation. The lady of slender limbs did not get any pleasure in the mansio"}]</data></node><node id="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="title">Daksha’s sacrifice and Sati</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Conflict around Daksha’s sacrifice, Sati and Shiva recurs with different details and theological emphases.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 15, "quote": "12.12Daksha’s Story Daksha was Brahma’s son and had a daughter named Sati. Sati was married to Shiva. Daksha was thus Shiva’s father-in-law. Once Daksha came to visit his son-in-law. But although Shiva worshipp"}, {"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "2.1.5 Sati gives up her Life On being enquired by Bheeshma about the reason why Sati gave up her life, Sage Pulastya narrated the following tale ---- Once, Daksha Prajapati had organised a grand yagya in which he had in"}, {"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "6.2.3 Sati Gives up her Life Sage Pulastya says- \"O Narad! This way, Lord Shiva became famous as Kapaali. This was the reason why Daksha did not invite him. In the meantime, Jaya, sage Gautam's daughter went to see Sati"}]</data></node><node id="motif-cosmic-linga"><data key="title">The cosmic linga / pillar of fire</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Brahma–Vishnu disputes and Shiva’s immeasurable manifestation form a recurring Shaiva narrative.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 6, "quote": "17.1.14 The Origin of Shiva Linga The sages asked Sutji--- Lord Shiva is formless then how come Shiva Linga is worshipped? What is the significance of Shiva Linga? What is the proper method of worshipping a Shiva Linga ? Sut"}, {"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 3, "quote": "5.1.6 Anaal-Stambh (The Pillar of Fire) \"Though Lord Shiva knew everything, but still feigning ignorance, he asked about the well beings of the world. The deities told him about the battle, fought between Brahmaji and Vishnuji.\" \""}, {"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 12, "quote": "5.2.8 Dispute Between Brahmaji &amp; Vishnuji \"I asked him as to who he was. Vishnuji replied that he was the fulfiller of all of his desires. But I replied to him that I only was the creator, nurturer and the supreme soul of this world"}]</data></node><node id="motif-ocean-churning"><data key="title">Churning the ocean</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">The churning cycle links Kurma, poison, nectar and Lakshmi; not every selected passage retells the whole cycle.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 2, "quote": "10.1.2 Kurma Many years ago there was a war between the devas (gods) and the daityas (demons) and the gods lost this war. They prayed to Vishnu to rescue them from the oppression of the demons. Vishnu to"}, {"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 24, "quote": "2.5.7 The Incarnations of Matsya and Kurma Once, goddess Parvati expressed her desire to know about all the incarnations of Lord Vishnu.Lord Mahadeva replied-- Lord Brahma had created numerous Prajapatis like Bhrigu, Marichi, Atri, D"}, {"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 31, "quote": "16.8.3 Churning of the Sea King Parikshit asked, \"O great sage, how did God cause churning of the sea. What was the purpose behind it? Kindly tell me?\" Shukdev says, \"Parikshit, in the sixth Chakshush Manvantara,the d"}]</data></node><node id="motif-matsya-flood"><data key="title">Matsya and the deluge</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Vishnu’s fish incarnation links preservation, Manu and cosmic destruction in the supplied stories.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 1, "quote": "10.1.1 Matsya Agni told Vashishtha the story of the fish incarnation."}, {"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 24, "quote": "2.5.7 The Incarnations of Matsya and Kurma Once, goddess Parvati expressed her desire to know about all the incarnations of Lord Vishnu.Lord Mahadeva replied-- Lord Brahma had created numerous Prajapatis like Bhrigu, Marichi, Atri, D"}, {"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 34, "quote": "16.8.5 Tale of Matysa-Avatar King Parikshit asked: 'O Gurudev, Lord is omnipotent. Why did He then take Matsyavatar like an ordinary being bound by karma? I want to hear the tale of God's Matsyavatar.' Shukdev said: 'O"}]</data></node><node id="motif-narasimha-prahlada"><data key="title">Narasimha and Prahlada</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">The supplied texts vary in their treatment of Prahlada, divine protection and Narasimha.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 4, "quote": "10.1.4 Narasimha Hiranyaksha had a brother named Hiranyakashipu. Hiranyakashipu was furious to learn that his brother had been killed and the resolved to kill Vishnu. But this could not be done unless h e hi"}, {"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 18, "quote": "12.13Hiranyakshipu Hiranyakashipu pleased Brahma through his prayers. As a result of the boon that he received from Brahma, he became invisible and started to oppress the world. He drove the gods out of heaven"}, {"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 25, "quote": "2.5.8 NriSimha Incarnation Continuing with the various incarnations of Lord Vishnu, Lord Mahadeva told Parvati --- Sage Kashyap had two mighty sons from Diti--Hiranyakashipu and Hiranyaksha. Hiranyaksha had once carri"}]</data></node><node id="motif-vamana-bali"><data key="title">Vamana and Bali</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Vamana’s request at Bali’s sacrifice, cosmic expansion and Bali’s fate recur across these texts.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 4, "quote": "10.1.5 Vamana Prahlada’s grandson was Vali and Vali became very powerful. When he was the king of the asuras, there was a war between the devas and the asuras. The gods were defeated and were driven off f"}, {"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 17, "quote": "9.18 Vali There was a king of the daityas named Vali. He was powerful and invincible. He was also righteous and truthful. The gods cold not bear to see Vali’s prosperity and began to plot how Vali mig"}, {"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "12.17Vali With Andhaka thus taken care of, Prahrada’s son Virochana became the king of the demons. He ruled his kingdom well. There was a sage named Sanatakumara who once went to visit Virochana. Viro"}]</data></node><node id="motif-varaha-earth"><data key="title">Varaha and the rescue of Earth</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">The boar incarnation and the preservation of Earth provide a recurring cosmological narrative.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 3, "quote": "10.1.3 Varaha Vishnu’s next incarnation was in the form of a boar. The sage Kashyapa and his wife Diti had a son named Hiranyaksha. became the king of the asuras. Hiranyaksha’s meditation pleased Brahma a"}, {"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 19, "quote": "12.14Hiranyaksha After Hiranyakashipu died, Hiranyaksha became the king of the demons. Hiranyaksha promptly began to oppress the world. He defeated the gods and drove them out of heaven. He also took the ear"}, {"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 21, "quote": "17.4.9 The Liberation of Earth The Sages were curious to know how Lord Vishnu in his incarnation of Varah (Boar), had liberated the earth from the captivity of Hiranyaksha. They also wanted to know why Lord Shiva adorned"}]</data></node><node id="motif-dhruva"><data key="title">Dhruva’s devotion</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Dhruva’s devotion, penance and divine encounter recur across supplied Purana retellings.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 4, "quote": "14.1.7 The Dhruva Dynasty Lord Vishnu, continuing with the tales of Garuda Puran told Shiva-' Uttanpad had two wives-Suruchi and Suniti. From his former wife he begot a son named Uttam, while Suniti gave birth to Dhr"}, {"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 17, "quote": "17.4.1 Dhruva – The Supreme Devotee The Sages asked Sutji as to how Dhruva became successful in attaining immortality and supreme status among all the stars. Sutji narrated the following tale--- There lived a king named Uttanp"}, {"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "16.4.3 Tale of Dhruv The self-begotten Manu had two sons- Priyvrat and Uttanpad. King Uttanpad had two queens- Suniti and Suruchi. Uttanpad loved Suruchi more than he loved Suniti. So Suniti was forced to live o"}]</data></node><node id="motif-prithu"><data key="title">Vena, Prithu and the Earth</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">The contrast between Vena’s kingship and Prithu’s rule links royal conduct, Earth and sustenance.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "9.4 Prithu In Dhruva's line there was a king named Anga, Anga was religious and followed the righteous path. But unfortunately, Anga's son Vena inherited none of the good qualities of his father. Vena'"}, {"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 8, "quote": "2.1.8 King Prithu and the Surya Dynasty Bheeshma enquired Sage Pulastya about the mighty King Prithu upon whom the earth was named as Prithvi. Sage Pulastya replied---Anga-Prajapati was the descendant of Swayambhuva Manu and Sunit"}, {"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 15, "quote": "16.4.4 Tale of Prithu In the lineage of the king Dhruv, had occurred a king named Anga. Anga's wife was Sumita, who was the daughter of Mrityadev. They had a son named Ven who was evil in nature. When he grew up"}]</data></node><node id="motif-ganga"><data key="title">The descent and sacred geography of Ganga</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">These episodes connect the descent of Ganga or Gautama’s river with pilgrimage; the accounts should be compared separately.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 18, "quote": "9.19 Gautama Part of the water of the Ganga that got stuck in Shiva’s hair was brought down to earth by Bhagiratha. The remaining part was brought down by the sage Gautama. Parvati was married to Shiva,"}, {"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 24, "quote": "9.26 Vriddhagoutama The sage Goutama had a son named Vriddhagoutama. Vriddhagoutama was also a sage. But he was very ugly. Besides, he had no nose; had never had one since he had been born. He was so ashamed of"}, {"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 30, "quote": "9.32 Goutama and Manikundala There was a city named Bhouvana. In that city there lived a brahmana named Goutama. The brahmana had a vaishya friend named Manikundala. (A vaishya belongs to the third of the three classes"}]</data></node><node id="motif-rama-cycle"><data key="title">Rama and the Ramayana cycle</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Rama’s life and related episodes occur inside several Purana retellings; they are not separate supplied Ramayana editions.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 5, "quote": "10.1.6 Parashurama The kshatriyas were the second of the four classes. It was their job to wear arms and protect the world. And rule. The brahmanas were the first of the four classes. It was their job to pray,"}, {"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 6, "quote": "10.1.7 Rama Brahma came out of Vishnu’s navel. Brahma’s son was Marichi’s son Kashyapa, Kashyapa’s son Surya, Surya’s son Vaivasvata Manu, Manu’s son Ikshvaku, Ikskhvakku’s son Kakutstha, Kakutstha’s so"}, {"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 15, "quote": "10.5 Manvataras, Varnashrama and Vratas"}]</data></node><node id="motif-krishna-cycle"><data key="title">Krishna and his devotees</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Krishna narrative passages link childhood, Mathura, Dvaraka, family, devotion and the close of the Yadu lineage.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 21, "quote": "14.9 Sri Krshna Making a revelation to Vyas that the Almighty God manifests himself whenever the civilization is endangered by the dominance of sinners, Lord Brahma said- 'In Dwapar yuga, Lord Vishnu incarn"}, {"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 25, "quote": "12.22Krshna’s Tapasya Krishna was the eight incarnation of Vishnu and he was born as the son of Devaki and Vasudeva. Initially, Krishna did not have any sons Desirous of obtaining a son, Krishna went to visit the"}, {"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 19, "quote": "17.4.5 The Yadu Dynasty Yadu had five sons among whom Sahastrajeet was the eldest. Shatarjay was the oson of Sahastrajeet and himself had three sons--Haihya, Haya and Venu. Dharma was Haihya's son. Dharmanetra was"}]</data></node><node id="motif-gajendra"><data key="title">Gajendra and the crocodile</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">The elephant’s appeal for refuge becomes a shared devotional narrative.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 30, "quote": "16.8.2 Tale of Gajendra, the elephant and Grah, the crocodile King Parikshit asked, \"Munivar, God had salve Gajendra, in His Hari incarnation. I wish to hear this tale.\" Shukdev says, \" O king, in Tamas Manvantara, God had appeared as Hari and saved Ga"}, {"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 41, "quote": "6.22.6 Gajendra and the Crocodile Sage Narad requests Pulastya to describe how Gajendra managed to release itself from the jaws of the crocodile. Pulastya says- \"There lived a crocodile in a reservoir near Trikut Mountain. O"}]</data></node><node id="motif-harishchandra"><data key="title">Harishchandra and truthfulness</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">The king’s trials provide a recurring narrative around truth and duty.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 22, "quote": "9.25 Harishchandra In the Ikshvaku dynasty there ruled a king named Harishchandra. Harishchandra had no son. One day two sages named Narada and Parvata came to visit Harishchandra and told him that he would go"}, {"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "7.2.7 King Harishchandra After being thrown out from his kingdom by Vishwamitra, Harishchandra reached the holy town of Varanasi, which was an abode of Lord Mahadev. There Harishchandra saw Vishwamitra standing befo"}]</data></node><node id="motif-madalasa-alarka"><data key="title">Madalasa, Alarka and instruction</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Family narrative leads into kingship, detachment and Dattatreya’s yoga teaching.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 12, "quote": "7.4.4 Kuvalaya goes to Patal Loka, Killing of Demon Patal Ketu and Marriage with Madalasa Prince Ritudhwaj stayed in the hermitage of sage Gaalav and helped him to perform his Yagyas without any problem by clearing all the minor obstacles. The demons were not aware of his arrival"}, {"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 13, "quote": "7.4.5 Separation of Madalasa The newly-wed couple had hardly spent few months together when King Shatrujit once again instructed Ritudhwaj to travel across the entire earth and see whether the Brahmins were performing t"}, {"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "7.4.6 Madalasa is Reborn When prince Ritudhwaj reached the capital, everyone expressed his pleasure with surprise. Ritudhwaj met everyone happily and went to the palace of his parents. Though the king and the queen"}]</data></node><node id="motif-karttikeya-taraka"><data key="title">Karttikeya and Taraka</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">The birth of Shiva and Parvati’s son connects their marriage to the defeat of Taraka.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 10, "quote": "2.1.11 Manifestation of Ganesh &amp; Kartikeya Once, before taking her bath, goddess Parvati anointed unguent on her body and while removing it created a human form from the accumulated dross. The head of this human form resembled an ele"}, {"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 24, "quote": "6.17.6 Birth of Kaartikeya Swallowing of Lord Shiva's sperm had subdued Agni's radiance. The deities advised him to take Lord Brahma's help. While he was on his way to Brahma loka, he came across a river named Kutila"}, {"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 24, "quote": "6.17.7 Kaartikeya Kills Taraka and Mahishasura After being appointed as the chief commander of the deities, Kaartikeya sought his guardians' permission to kill the demons- Taraka and Mahishasura. All the deities including Lord Vishnu ble"}]</data></node><node id="motif-ganesha"><data key="title">Ganesha’s manifestation</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Ganesha’s origins and worship recur, with differing narrative settings.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 22, "quote": "17.4.11 The Origin of Lord Ganesh Once, the deities requested Lord Shiva to device some means so that they no longer had to live under the threat of the demons.The most merciful Lord Pinaki Shiva then took the incarnation of"}, {"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 10, "quote": "2.1.11 Manifestation of Ganesh &amp; Kartikeya Once, before taking her bath, goddess Parvati anointed unguent on her body and while removing it created a human form from the accumulated dross. The head of this human form resembled an ele"}, {"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 12, "quote": "8.3.4 Lord Ganapati King Prajapal then asked sage Mahatapa about the manifestation of lord Ganapati and about the significance of chaturthi in terms of Ganapati's worship.Mahatapa narrated the following tale- \""}]</data></node><node id="motif-andhaka"><data key="title">Andhaka and Shiva</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">The demon Andhaka appears in several Shaiva narrative cycles.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "12.16Andhaka While Shiva was gone, he left his companion Nandi to look after his household. He also gave Vishnu the overall responsibility of ensuring that all was well with Parvati and the gods and the"}, {"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 21, "quote": "17.4.8 Appointment of Andhak as Lord of the Ganas On being asked by the Sages how Andhak--the demon was appointed as the lord of Ganas by Lord Shiva, Sutji narrated the following tale to them.--The mighty demon, Andhak was Hiranyaksh's son."}, {"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "6.3.1 Andhak – The Demon King Sage Narad was curious to know how the demon King Andhak behaved with the deities. He asked Pulastya in this regard. Pulastya replied- \"After ascending the throne, Andhak did a severe penanc"}]</data></node><node id="motif-jalandhara"><data key="title">Jalandhara and Vrinda</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">The Jalandhara cycle links divine battle, deception and Vrinda; distinguish it from the separate Shankhachuda–Tulsi story.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 21, "quote": "17.4.10 Killing of Jalandhar The Sages requested Sutji to reveal how Lord Shiva had killed the notorious demon-- Jalandhar. Sutji narrated the following tale--- There was a demon named Jalandhar. He had manifested from"}, {"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 46, "quote": "16.10.11 Migration to Vrindavana The uprooted Arjun trees fell with thundering sound. All the people shivered with fear and felt as if lightning had struck somewhere. All the elders including Nand met together and discussed"}, {"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 12, "quote": "MIGRATION TO VRINDAVANA The uprooted Arjun trees fell with thundering sound. All the people shivered with fear and felt as if lightning had struck somewhere. All the elders including Nand met together and discussed"}]</data></node><node id="motif-tripura"><data key="title">The destruction of Tripura</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Shiva’s destruction of the three cities appears in the supplied Shiva narrative.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 42, "quote": "5.2.114 Yudh-Khand: Tripurasur- The Three Demon Narada requested lord Brahma to narrate how lord Shiva annihilated the demons. Lord Brahma narrated the tale of Tripurasur. \"After the killing of Tarakasur, three of his sons started doing t"}, {"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 44, "quote": "5.2.118 Killings of Tripurasurs When Shiva agreed to kill the demon - brothers Tripurasur the deities became extremely joyous. They gave their respective weapons to Shiva, so that he did not have any difficulty in killing"}]</data></node><node id="motif-brahma-head"><data key="title">Brahma’s head and Bhairava</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Brahma’s severed head and the consequences of killing connect Shiva/Bhairava with expiation.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 28, "quote": "9.29 Brahma’s Head Years ago, there was a war between the gods and the demons and the gods lost. The gods came to Brahma for advice and Brahma asked them to pray to Shiva. When Shiva appeared before them, the"}, {"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 33, "quote": "12.32Shiva and Brahma Many years ago, Brahma lost his head slightly. He began to imagine that he was superior to Shiva and Vishnu. He told all the sages, “I am the supreme godhead. There is no one else but me.” W"}, {"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 9, "quote": "5.3.10 Bhairav Gets Liberated from his Sin Bhairav who was created by lord Shiva from his third eye, had severed one of the five heads of Lord Brahma on the instruction of lord Shiva. Now Brahmaji was left with only four heads. Carry"}]</data></node><node id="motif-nandi"><data key="title">Nandi’s birth and devotion</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Nandi’s origins and relation to Shiva recur as a devotion narrative.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 34, "quote": "12.33Nandi There used to be a sage named Shilada. He was righteous and learned in the shastras. To obtain a son, Shilada began to pray to shiva. The tapasya went on for a thousand years and shiva appea"}, {"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 13, "quote": "17.2.13 Shilad’s Impossible Demand Once, Shilad did an austere penance to please Indra. He was desirous of begetting a son. When Indra appeared he requested him to bless him with a son who was self-born as well as deathless."}, {"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 14, "quote": "17.3.4 Manifestation of Lord Nandishwar Shilad did a tremendous penance to please Lord Shiva. His penance continued for thousands of year. At last, Lord Shiva appeared before him and said--\"I am extremely pleased by you devotion."}]</data></node><node id="motif-yayati"><data key="title">Yayati and desire</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Yayati’s kingship, desire and family relations recur across the dynasty narratives.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 11, "quote": "9.12 Yayati In the lunar dynasty, there was born a powerful king named Nahusha. He married Viraja and they had six sons named Yati, Yajati, Samyati, Ayati, Yati, and Suyati. Yati became a hermit. So alt"}, {"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "2.2.2 King Yayati propagates Vaishnava-Dharma King Yayati was the son of Nahush and a descendant of Soma--dynasty. He was a great devotee of Lord Vishnu and had contributed a lot in the propagation of Vaishnava--dharma. Yayati had sent"}, {"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 15, "quote": "2.2.3 Yayati marries Ashrubindumati Once, king Yayati went into a forest for hunting. He saw a stag, which had four horns and chased it. The deer led him deep into the forest and then disappeared. Yayati was tired and thirsty."}]</data></node><node id="motif-sagara"><data key="title">Sagara and his descendants</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Sagara’s dynasty and its consequences connect kingship, sacrifice and the Ganga cycle.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 9, "quote": "9.10 Sagara Trishanku's son was Harishchandra and from Harishchandra was descended a king named Bahu. Bahu devoted too much time to pleasurable pursuits. The upshot of this was that the defence of the k"}, {"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "18.6 Fate of King Baahu Narad says: \"O great sage who in the dynasty of Sagar received salvation from Preta Bhava. Who was Sagar? Kindly narrate his whole story to me.\" Sanatkumar says: \"Long ago, there occurred a"}, {"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 6, "quote": "18.7 The Birth of Sagar Sanatkumar says :- \"O Narad! Both the queens were passing their time in the hermitage of sage Aurv. When half of the rainy season had passed, the elder queen began to cultivate evil feelings"}]</data></node><node id="motif-markandeya"><data key="title">Markandeya, death and cosmic vision</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Markandeya appears both as narrator and as a figure confronting death or cosmic dissolution; these roles are distinct.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 16, "quote": "9.16 Markandeya Many years ago, a great destruction (pralaya) took place. The earth was shrouded in darkness and nothing could be seen. There was neither sun nor moon. Lightning and thunder crushed mountain"}, {"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 62, "quote": "7.16 The Benefits of Listening to the Markandaya Purana The birds said- 'O Jaimini! This was the way, sage Markandeya had narrated the divine tales to Kraustuki. A person who either studies this Puraan or listens to it achieves great accomplishme"}, {"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "18.3 Profile of the Sage Markandeya Sanatkumar says: O sage! Faith has the greatest significance. Achievement of everything and appeasement of Lord Vishnu depend upon the faith. Nothing is attainable without faith. Such as a h"}]</data></node><node id="motif-nachiketa"><data key="title">Nachiketa and Yama</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">The Varaha retelling supplies a direct comparison point with the Nachiketa dialogue in the Katha Upanishad.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 29, "quote": "8.10.1 Nachiketa’s Experience Once, Janmejay asked Sage Vaishampayan about Yamrajpuri and it's location. He also wanted to know as to what should a man do so that he does not have to go there. Sage Vaishampayan narrated"}]</data></node><node id="motif-dadhichi-vritra"><data key="title">Dadhichi, Vritra and Indra</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Sacrifice of Dadhichi’s body, Indra’s weapon and Vritra form an interlinked narrative cycle.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 12, "quote": "17.2.11 Dadhichi Subjugates the Sages (Kshoopa) There ruled a king named Kshoopa whose friend was Sage Dadhichi. Once they got into argument over the superiority of their respective castes. King Kshoopa was of the opinion that a Kshetriya"}, {"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 23, "quote": "16.6.7 Killings of Vritrasur by Indra Shri Shukdev says, \"O Parikshit, from the gallantry of the gods the demon army began to fled. Seeing his army running away in panic, Vritrasur got infuriated. Charging ahead he stopped the g"}, {"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 26, "quote": "5.2.58 Contention between Kshuva &amp; Dadhichi Narad, who was listening to the divine tales of Shiva with rapt attention, was very curious to know about the reasons why lord Vishnu attended a yagya where Shiva was not invited and why did"}]</data></node><node id="motif-yoga-instruction"><data key="title">Yoga and spiritual instruction</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">These are thematic links among passages about practice and realization, not a claim that their systems or doctrines are identical.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 30, "quote": "10.9.3 Yoga Yoga is the way to circumvent the miseries of life. True knowledge is that which informs one about the true nature of brahman or paramatman. The atman or jivatman is that which characterises"}, {"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 31, "quote": "10.9.4 The Knowledge of Brahman Brahma jnana is the knowledge of brahman. This knowledge, which gives the ultimate bliss, is nothing but the sense that the individual atman is identical with the universal brahman or paramt"}, {"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 7, "quote": "14.3.2 Description of Yogas and Muhurtas Giving elaborate description of inauspicious days on which one should not undertake travels or journeys, Lord Vishnu told Shiva-- 'Various Yoginis (goddesses) dwell in different directions o"}]</data></node><node id="motif-ancestor-rites"><data key="title">Ancestors, death and karma</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Ritual and cosmological passages connect ancestors, death and the consequences of action within the supplied traditions.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 19, "quote": "10.6 Hellish Planets, Charity and Gayatri"}, {"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 19, "quote": "10.6.1 Hellish Planets – Narakas (Hells) If one worships Vishnu with flowers, one never goes to hell. There are several such hells. Although people do not wish to die, they are bound to die once their predestined time span on earth"}, {"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 30, "quote": "10.9.2 Yama and Hell When human beings die, their physical bodies are given up. But they acquire new bodies that are known as ativahika bodies. In these bodies, they are brought to Yama’s abode by Yama’s servant"}]</data></node><node id="motif-vedas-branches"><data key="title">Vedic transmission and branches</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">These sections explicitly discuss the transmission, arrangement or branches of Vedic knowledge.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 28, "quote": "12.27Vedavyasa In every dvapara yuga, a Vedavyasa is born so as to divide the Vedas and disseminate their knowledge. In the present era, there have been twenty-eight dvapara yugas and there have therefore"}, {"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 76, "quote": "16.12.5 Branches of Vedas and Division SUTJI described about the Samhitas and divisions of Vedas to the sages like Shaunak etc. Ved Vyas divided Vedas into four parts and created four Sanhitas viz, Rig, Yajur, Sama and Atharva an"}, {"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 29, "quote": "3.3.3 Description of Veda Vyasa and the Significance of BrahmaGyana Maitreya says- \"O Lord! How did God divide Vedas in the form of Vedavyasa during different Yugas?\" Parashar says- During each Dwaparyug, in every Kalpa, Lord Vishnu takes incarnation as Veda"}]</data></node><node id="p-matsya-ch-001"><data key="title">Matsya 1: Manu and Vishnu in fish form</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Chapter navigation: Manu and Vishnu in fish form. Title normalized from the supplied chapter body and/or closing colophon; the inclusive range may include the next heading page.</data><data key="doc">matsyapurana-english</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "matsyapurana-english", "page": 19, "quote": "CHAPTER I May the lotus feet of Bhava, who shook the diggajas at the time of His dance, disperse all obstacles.-1.", "extraction": "OCR"}]</data></node><node id="p-matsya-ch-002"><data key="title">Matsya 2: Destruction and renewal of the universe</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Chapter navigation: Destruction and renewal of the universe. Title normalized from the supplied chapter body and/or closing colophon; the inclusive range may include the next heading page.</data><data key="doc">matsyapurana-english</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "matsyapurana-english", "page": 24, "quote": "CHAPTER II. Sata, continuing hie narration, said, that, on hearing such words of the Lord, the king begged Him to reveal to him in how many years the", "extraction": "OCR"}]</data></node><node id="p-matsya-ch-003"><data key="title">Matsya 3: Primary creation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Chapter navigation: Primary creation. Title normalized from the supplied chapter body and/or closing colophon; the inclusive range may include the next heading page.</data><data key="doc">matsyapurana-english</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "matsyapurana-english", "page": 27, "quote": "CHAPTER III. lightning, embryo, 