 India During the Practice • Practice sessions should start with a prayer or an invocation as it creates a conducive environment to relax the mind. • Yogic practices shall be performed sl"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-13"><data key="title">Food for Thought / How Yoga Can Help</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Food for Thought / How Yoga Can Help.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">13</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 13, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India Food for Thought A few dietary guidelines can ensure that the body and mind are flexible and well-prepared for Yoga practice. A vegetarian diet is usually recommended, and for a pe"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-14"><data key="title">Illustration page</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Scan page 14 is included for complete file navigation; OCR provides no substantive text beyond possible running labels.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">14</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 14, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-15"><data key="title">Invocation / Prayer — section opening</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Invocation / Prayer — section opening.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">15</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 15, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India 1 Invocation/Prayer"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-16"><data key="title">Invocation / Prayer — Samgacchadhvam</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Invocation / Prayer — Samgacchadhvam.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">16</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 16, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India Yogic practice shall start with a prayer or prayerful mood to enhance the benefits of practice. 30 Samgacchadhvam samvadadhvam sam vo manămsi janatăm devã bhăgam yatha pürve"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-17"><data key="title">Sadilaja / Calana Kriyas — Loosening Practices</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Sadilaja / Calana Kriyas — Loosening Practices.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">17</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 17, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India 2 SADILAJA/CÂLANA KRIYÃS/ | LOOSENING PRACTICES The Cälana Kriyãs/loosening practices/Yogic Sükşma Vyäyămas help to increase microcirculation. These practices can be done while sta"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-18"><data key="title">Neck Movements</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Neck Movements.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">18</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 18, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India I. NECK MOVEMENTS (Grivä Sakti Vikäsaka) Sthiti: Samasthiti (Alert Posture) TECHNIQUE STAGE-1 STAGE 2"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-19"><data key="title">Shoulder Movements — illustration</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Shoulder Movements — illustration.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">19</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 19, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India -STAGE Shoulder movements —STAGE-2- 15"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-20"><data key="title">Shoulder Movements — technique</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Shoulder Movements — technique.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">20</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 20, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India II. SHOULDER MOVEMENTS | Sthiti: Samasthiti (Alert posture) TECHNIQUE STAGE-1 STAGE-2"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-21"><data key="title">Trunk Movement</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Trunk Movement.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">21</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 21, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India III. TRUNK MOVEMENT (Kațiśakti Vikāsaka) Sthiti: Somasthiti (Alert posture) Technique • Keep the legs about 1 foot apart."}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-22"><data key="title">Knee Movement</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Knee Movement.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">22</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 22, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India IV. Knee movement | Sthiti: Samasthiti (Alert posture) Technique Inhale; lift the arms up to the shoulder level, palms facing downwards."}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-23"><data key="title">Tadasana — Palm-tree Posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Tadasana — Palm-tree Posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">23</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 23, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India A. STANDING POSTURES 3 YOGASANAS TADẶSANA (Palm-tree Posture) Tada means palm tree or mountain."}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-24"><data key="title">Vrksasana — Tree Posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Vrksasana — Tree Posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">24</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 24, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India VRKSĂSANA (The Tree Posture) Vrksa means tree. The final position of this asana resembles the shape of a tree, hence the name."}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-25"><data key="title">Ardhacakrasana — Half-Wheel Posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Ardhacakrasana — Half-Wheel Posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">25</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 25, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India ARDHACAKRĂSANA (The Half-Wheel Posture) Ardha means half. Cakra means wheel. As the body takes the shape of a half wheel in this posture, it is"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-26"><data key="title">Pada-Hastasana — Hands-to-Feet Posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Pada-Hastasana — Hands-to-Feet Posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">26</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 26, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India PADA-HASTÃSANA (The Hands-to-Feet Posture) Pãda means feet, hasta means hands. Therefore, Pada-Hastäsana means keeping the palms down towards the feet."}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-27"><data key="title">Trikonasana — Triangle Posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Trikonasana — Triangle Posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">27</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 27, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India TRIKONÃSANA (The Triangle Posture) Trikon means triangle. Tri means three and kona means angle. As the asana resembles the shape of a triangle made by the trunk, arms, and legs, th"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-28"><data key="title">Bhadrasana — Firm / Auspicious Posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Bhadrasana — Firm / Auspicious Posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">28</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 28, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India B. SITTING POSTURES BHADRASANA (The Firm/Auspicious Posture) Bhadra means firm or auspicious. Sthiti: Viśrämäsana (Long sitting posture)."}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-29"><data key="title">Vajrasana — Thunderbolt Posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Vajrasana — Thunderbolt Posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">29</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 29, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India VAJRÃSANA (Thunderbolt Posture) This is considered as one of the meditative postures. While practising it for meditative purposes, one should close his/her eyes at"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-30"><data key="title">Ardhaustrasana — Half-Camel Posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Ardhaustrasana — Half-Camel Posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">30</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 30, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India ARDHAUSTRASANA (The Half-Camel Posture) Ustr means camel. The final version of this sana will resemble the hump of a camel. In this version, only the first stage"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-31"><data key="title">Ustrasana — Camel Posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Ustrasana — Camel Posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">31</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 31, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India UŞȚRĂSANA (The Camel Posture) Ustra means camel. The body in this pose will resemble the hump of a camel, hence the name. Sthiti: Vajrāsana"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-32"><data key="title">Sasakasana — Hare Posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Sasakasana — Hare Posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">32</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 32, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India Technique ŚAŚAKÃSANA Sit in Vajrasana. (The Hare Posture) • Spread both the knees wide apart, keep the big toes touching."}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-33"><data key="title">Uttanamandukasana — Stretched-up Frog Posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Uttanamandukasana — Stretched-up Frog Posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">33</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 33, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India UTTĂNAMANDÜKÄSANA (Stretched-up Frog Posture) Uttana means upright and Mandüka means frog. The final position of Uttanamandükasana would resemble an upright frog, hence the name."}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-34"><data key="title">Vakrasana / Marichyasana — Spinal Twist</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Vakrasana / Marichyasana — Spinal Twist.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">34</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 34, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India VAKRÄSANA/MARICHYÃSANA (The Spinal Twist Posture) Vakrä means twisted. In this sana, the spine is twisted, which has a rejuvenating effect on its functioning."}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-35"><data key="title">Makarasana — prone posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Makarasana — prone posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">35</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 35, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India Technique C. PRONE POSTURES Lie down on stomach with the feet wide apart, toes pointing outwards. MAKARASANA Bend both the arms and place the right palm on the left palm."}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-36"><data key="title">Bhujangasana — Cobra Posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Bhujangasana — Cobra Posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">36</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 36, "quote": "COM MON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India BHUJANGÃSANA Technique (The Cobra Posture] Lie down on your stomach, rest your head on your hands and"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-37"><data key="title">Salabhasana — Locust Posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Salabhasana — Locust Posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">37</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 37, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India Technique ŚALABHÃSANA (The Locust Posture) Lie down on stomach in Makaräsana. Salabha means a locust."}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-38"><data key="title">Setubandhasana — supine posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Setubandhasana — supine posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">38</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 38, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India Technique D. SUPINE POSTURES Bend both the legs at the knees and bring the heels near the buttocks. SETUBANDHASANA • While holding both the ankles firmly, keep the knees and feet i"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-39"><data key="title">Uttanapadasana — Raised Feet Posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Uttanapadasana — Raised Feet Posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">39</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 39, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India UTTÂNAPÃDÃSANA Technique (Raised feet posture) Lie comfortably on the ground with the legs stretched out. Ottana means raised-upwards and Pada"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-40"><data key="title">Ardhahalasana — Half-Plough Posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Ardhahalasana — Half-Plough Posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">40</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 40, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India ARDHAHALÃSANA (Half-Plough Posture) \"Ardha' means half and 'Hala' means plough. This posture is known as Ardhahalāsana because in its final position, the body would"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-41"><data key="title">Pavanamuktasana — Wind-Releasing Posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Pavanamuktasana — Wind-Releasing Posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">41</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 41, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India PAVANAMUKTÃSANA (Wind-Releasing Posture) Pavana means wind and mukta means to release or to make free. As the name suggests, this sana is useful in removing wind or flatulence from"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-42"><data key="title">Savasana — Corpse / Dead-Body Posture</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Savasana — Corpse / Dead-Body Posture.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">42</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 42, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India Technique SAVĂSANA Lie down on back with arms and legs comfortably apart. (Corpse/ Dead-Body Posture) Palms facing upwards, eyes closed."}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-43"><data key="title">Kapalabhati — section opening</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Kapalabhati — section opening.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">43</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 43, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India 4 KAPÃLABHÄTI Kapal means skull, bhati means to shine or to cleanse. It is a cleansing practice which helps to clean the frontal sinuses and upper respirating tract. Sthiti: Sukhās"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-44"><data key="title">Kapalabhati — source benefits and cautions</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The supplied guide’s kapalabhati — source benefits and cautions. This node identifies the subject and location without endorsing the source’s health assertions.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">44</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 44, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India Benefits This practice purifies the frontal air sinuses; helps to overcome cough disorders. • It is useful in treating cold, rhinitis, sinusitis, asthma, and bronchial infections."}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-45"><data key="title">Nadisodhana / Anuloma Viloma Pranayama</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Nadisodhana / Anuloma Viloma Pranayama.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">45</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 45, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India 5 PRĂNAYAMA NADISODHANA or ANULOMA VILOMA PRANÃYÃMA (Alternate Nostril Breathing) The main characteristic feature of this"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-46"><data key="title">Nadisodhana — continuation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Nadisodhana — continuation.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">46</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 46, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India • Gradually make the ratio 1:2 (inhalation:exhalation, respectively). Breathing Breath should be slow, steady, and controlled. It should not be forced or restricted in anyway."}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-47"><data key="title">Sitali Pranayama</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Sitali Pranayama.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">47</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 47, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India SITALİ PRANAYAMA Sitali means cooling. It also means calm and passionless. As the name indicates, this pranayāma cools the mind - and body. It is specially designed to reduce the b"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-48"><data key="title">Sitali — source cautions</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The supplied guide’s sitali — source cautions. This node identifies the subject and location without endorsing the source’s health assertions.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">48</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 48, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India Caution Those who are suffering from severe cold, cough or tonsillitis should not do this pranayama."}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-49"><data key="title">Bhramari Pranayama</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Bhramari Pranayama.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">49</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 49, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India BHRĂMARI PRĂNÂYÃMA Bhramari is derived from bhramara, which means black bee. During the practice of this pranayama, the sound produced resembles the buzzing of a black bee, hence"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-50"><data key="title">Bhramari — source benefits and cautions</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">The supplied guide’s bhramari — source benefits and cautions. This node identifies the subject and location without endorsing the source’s health assertions.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">50</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 50, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India Benefits It relieves stress and helps in alleviating anxiety, anger, and hyper activity. It creates a soothing effect on the nervous system and mind. It is a great tranquiliser, an"}]</data></node><node id="b-yoga-page-51"><data key="title">Dhyana — meditation</data><data key="type">section</data><data key="summary">Source topic in the illustrated Common Yoga Protocol: Dhyana — meditation.</data><data key="doc">common-yoga-aasana-englishebook</data><data key="page">51</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 51, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of Iridia 6 DHYÃNA Dhyana or meditation is an act of continuous contemplation. Sthiti: Any comfortable posture. Technique"}]</data></node><node id="doc-ayurvedic-home-remediesenglishebook"><data key="title">Ayurvedic-Home-RemediesEnglishEbook · source PDF</data><data key="type">document</data><data key="summary">Supplied source witness: 30 PDF pages. Embedded text extracted with page boundaries retained.</data><data key="doc">ayurvedic-home-remediesenglishebook</data><data key="page">1</data></node><node id="page-ayurvedic-home-remediesenglishebook-1"><data key="title">PDF p. 1 · S.NO.                  Page</data><data key="type">page</data><data key="summary">A source-page navigation record. Read the extracted text or open the original page to verify the passage.</data><data key="doc">ayurvedic-home-remediesenglishebook</data><data key="page">1</data></node><node id="page-ayurvedic-home-remediesenglishebook-2"><data key="title">PDF p. 2 · FOREWORD</data><data key="type">page</data><data key="summary">A source-page navigation record. Read the extracted text or open the original page to verify the passage.</data><data key="doc">ayurvedic-home-remediesenglishebook</data><data key="page">2</data></node><node id="page-ayurvedic-home-remediesenglishebook-3"><data key="title">PDF p. 3 · INSTRUCTIONS</data><data key="type">page</data><data key="summary">A source-page navigation record. Read the extracted text or open the original page to verify the passage.</data><data key="doc">ayurvedic-home-remediesenglishebook</data><data key="page">3</data></node><node id="page-ayurvedic-home-remediesenglishebook-4"><data key="title">PDF p. 4 · In general too spicy, salty, chilly, sour, preserved</data><data key="type">page</data><data key="summary">A source-page navigation record. Read the extracted text or open the original page to verify the passage.</data><data key="doc">ayurvedic-home-remediesenglishebook</data><data key="page">4</data></node><node id="page-ayurvedic-home-remediesenglishebook-5"><data key="title">PDF p. 5 · A drak/ Sonth