e passage.</data><data key="doc">yajurveda</data><data key="page">1096</data></node><node id="concept-agni"><data key="title">Agni</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">Named deity and ritual fire; compare the role in each passage. This hub combines literal keyword matches with explicitly tagged editorial connections.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "practical-vastu-professional-guide-1", "page": 3, "quote": ", Lakshmi tattva, Agni respect and daily order. Core principles Vastu Purusha Mandala, Pancha Mahabhu"}, {"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 1, "quote": "10 Agni Purana This Purana is basically a preaching to the sage Vashishth by Agni. In his turn, Vashishth narrated it"}]</data></node><node id="concept-indra"><data key="title">Indra</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">A recurring named deity; the narrative and theological role varies by text. This hub combines literal keyword matches with explicitly tagged editorial connections.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "practical-vastu-professional-guide-1", "page": 31, "quote": "nces commonly describe favorable examples such as E3 Jayanta, E4 Indra, S3 Vitatha, S4 Grihakshat/Grihrakshita, W3 Sugriva, W4 Pushpdanta, and N3-N5 Mukhya/Bhallat/Soma. Use this a"}, {"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 3, "quote": "tree, a beautiful tree that came to occupy the pride of place in Indra’s garden. A jewel named koustubha emerged and was accepted by Vishnu as his adornment. Three wonderful animal"}]</data></node><node id="concept-soma"><data key="title">Soma</data><data key="type">concept</data><data key="summary">A recurring deity, offering, and sacred substance in the supplied translations. This hub combines literal keyword matches with explicitly tagged editorial connections.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "practical-vastu-professional-guide-1", "page": 31, "quote": "rihrakshita, W3 Sugriva, W4 Pushpdanta, and N3-N5 Mukhya/Bhallat/Soma. Use this as a starting interpretation rather 