944, "quote": "Bristle thou up, O Maghavan, our weapons: excite the spirits, of my"}, {"doc": "rigveda", "page": 705, "quote": "Bristle thou up, O Maghavan, our weapons: excite the spirits of my"}]</data></edge><edge id="e467" source="sv-3-2-9-3-5" target="rv-10-103"><data key="type">shared wording</data><data key="summary">At least 12 consecutive normalized English words match in the supplied translations. This is a reading connection, not a claim of complete hymn identity or historical borrowing.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-4-vedas-in-1", "page": 944, "quote": "Bewildering the senses of our foemen, seize thou their bodies, and depart"}, {"doc": "rigveda", "page": 706, "quote": "Bewildering the senses of our foemen, seize thou their bodies and depart"}]</data></edge><edge id="e468" source="sv-3-2-9-3-5" target="rv-6-75"><data key="type">shared wording</data><data key="summary">At least 12 consecutive normalized English words match in the supplied translations. This is a reading connection, not a claim of complete hymn identity or historical borrowing.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-4-vedas-in-1", "page": 944, "quote": "sharpened by our prayer! Go to the foemen, strike them home, and"}, {"doc": "rigveda", "page": 384, "quote": "sharpened by our prayer. Go to the foemen, strike them home, and"}]</data></edge><edge id="e469" source="sv-3-2-9-3-7" target="rv-10-152"><data key="type">shared wording</data><data key="summary">At least 12 consecutive normalized English words match in the supplied translations. This is a reading connection, not a claim of complete hymn identity or historical borrowing.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-4-vedas-in-1", "page": 944, "quote": "O Indra, beat our foes away, humble the men who challenge us"}, {"doc": "rigveda", "page": 735, "quote": "O Indra, beat our foes away, humble the men who challenge us"}]</data></edge><edge id="e470" source="sv-3-2-9-3-8" target="rv-6-75"><data key="type">shared wording</data><data key="summary">At least 12 consecutive normalized English words match in the supplied translations. This is a reading connection, not a claim of complete hymn identity or historical borrowing.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-4-vedas-in-1", "page": 944, "quote": "Thy vital parts I cover with thine armour: with immortality King Soma"}, {"doc": "rigveda", "page": 384, "quote": "Thy vital parts I cover with thine Armour: with immortality King Soma"}]</data></edge><edge id="e471" source="sv-3-2-9-3-9" target="rv-10-180"><data key="type">shared wording</data><data key="summary">At least 12 consecutive normalized English words match in the supplied translations. This is a reading connection, not a claim of complete hymn identity or historical borrowing.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-4-vedas-in-1", "page": 945, "quote": "Like a dread wild beast roaming on the mountain thou hast approached"}, {"doc": "rigveda", "page": 743, "quote": "Like a dread wild beast roaming on the mountain thou hast approached"}]</data></edge><edge id="e472" source="v-samaveda" target="v-rigveda"><data key="type">source-described reuse</data><data key="summary">Griffith’s supplied preface says the Samaveda collection takes hymns, portions, and detached verses mainly from the Rigveda and rearranges them for ritual.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "four-vedas-english-translation", "page": 1177, "quote": "taken mainly from the Rgveda, transposed and re-arranged"}]</data></edge><edge id="e473" source="rv-1-32" target="rv-10-121"><data key="type">thematic comparison</data><data key="summary">Both are concerned with cosmic ordering, through different poetic accounts.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "rigveda", "page": 20, "quote": "HYMN XXXII. Indra."}, {"doc": "rigveda", "page": 719, "quote": "HYMN CXXI. Ka."}]</data></edge><edge id="e474" source="rv-10-90" target="rv-10-121"><data key="type">thematic comparison</data><data key="summary">Two distinct cosmogonic accounts: cosmic Person/sacrifice and golden germ.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "rigveda", "page": 691, "quote": "HYMN XC. Purusa."}, {"doc": "rigveda", "page": 719, "quote": "HYMN CXXI. Ka."}]</data></edge><edge id="e475" source="rv-10-121" target="rv-10-129"><data key="type">thematic comparison</data><data key="summary">Read two different poetic approaches to the origin of the world: offering and inquiry.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "rigveda", "page": 719, "quote": "HYMN CXXI. Ka."}, {"doc": "rigveda", "page": 724, "quote": "HYMN CXXIX. Creation."}]</data></edge><edge id="e476" source="rv-1-164" target="rv-10-125"><data key="type">thematic comparison</data><data key="summary">Both treat divine speech and the relation of multiplicity to a wider unity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "rigveda", "page": 124, "quote": "HYMN CLXIV. Visvedevas."}, {"doc": "rigveda", "page": 722, "quote": "HYMN CXXV. Vak."}]</data></edge><edge id="e477" source="rv-10-34" target="av-7-109"><data key="type">thematic comparison</data><data key="summary">Contrasting dice themes: the gambler’s lament versus a hymn for success with dice.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "rigveda", "page": 638, "quote": "HYMN XXXIV. Dice, Etc."}, {"doc": "atharvaveda", "page": 634, "quote": "For success with the dice."}]</data></edge><edge id="e478" source="rv-10-191" target="av-3-30"><data key="type">thematic comparison</data><data key="summary">Shared theme of concord and common purpose.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "rigveda", "page": 745, "quote": "HYMN CXCI. Agni."}, {"doc": "atharvaveda", "page": 307, "quote": "For concord."}]</data></edge><edge id="e479" source="rv-10-85" target="rv-1-179"><data key="type">thematic comparison</data><data key="summary">Marriage in a ceremonial hymn and in a dialogue over household and desire.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "rigveda", "page": 682, "quote": "HYMN LXXXV. Surya's Bridal."}, {"doc": "rigveda", "page": 138, "quote": "HYMN CLXXIX. Rati."}]</data></edge><edge id="e480" source="rv-10-95" target="rv-10-10"><data key="type">thematic comparison</data><data key="summary">Two dialogues in which desire encounters limits; their figures and outcomes remain distinct.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "rigveda", "page": 696, "quote": "HYMN XCV. Urvasi. Pururavas."}, {"doc": "rigveda", "page": 618, "quote": "HYMN X.Yama Yami."}]</data></edge><edge id="e481" source="u-isavasya-upanishad-commentary" target="u-isavasya-upanishad"><data key="type">commentary-on</data><data key="summary">Modern interpretive commentary on this translation.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 4, "quote": "Commentary on the Isavasya Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></edge><edge id="e482" source="u-kena-upanishad-commentary" target="u-kena-upanishad"><data key="type">commentary-on</data><data key="summary">Modern interpretive commentary on this translation.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 36, "quote": "Commentary on the Kena Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></edge><edge id="e483" source="u-katha-upanishad-commentary" target="u-katha-upanishad"><data key="type">commentary-on</data><data key="summary">Modern interpretive commentary on this translation.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 51, "quote": "Commentary on the Katha Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></edge><edge id="e484" source="u-prasna-upanishad-commentary" target="u-prasna-upanishad"><data key="type">commentary-on</data><data key="summary">Modern interpretive commentary on this translation.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 134, "quote": "Commentary on the Prashna Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></edge><edge id="e485" source="u-mundaka-upanishad-commentary" target="u-mundaka-upanishad"><data key="type">commentary-on</data><data key="summary">Modern interpretive commentary on this translation.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 158, "quote": "Commentary on the Mundaka Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></edge><edge id="e486" source="u-mandukya-upanishad-commentary" target="u-mandukya-upanishad"><data key="type">commentary-on</data><data key="summary">Modern interpretive commentary on this translation.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 193, "quote": "Commentary on the Mandukya Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></edge><edge id="e487" source="u-taittiriya-upanishad-commentary" target="u-taittiriya-upanishad"><data key="type">commentary-on</data><data key="summary">Modern interpretive commentary on this translation.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 205, "quote": "Commentary on the Taittiriya Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></edge><edge id="e488" source="u-aitareya-upanishad-commentary" target="u-aitareya-upanishad"><data key="type">commentary-on</data><data key="summary">Modern interpretive commentary on this translation.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 230, "quote": "Commentary on the Aitereya Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></edge><edge id="e489" source="u-chandogya-upanishad-commentary" target="u-chandogya-upanishad"><data key="type">commentary-on</data><data key="summary">Modern interpretive commentary on this translation.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 275, "quote": "Commentary on the Chandogya Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></edge><edge id="e490" source="u-brihadaranyaka-upanishad-commentary" target="u-brihadaranyaka-upanishad"><data key="type">commentary-on</data><data key="summary">Modern interpretive commentary on this translation.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 352, "quote": "Commentary on the Brihadaranyaka Upanishad–by Swami Nirmalananda Giri"}]</data></edge><edge id="e491" source="u-story-balaki-and-ajatasatru" target="u-story-balaki-and-ajatasatru-the-kaushitaki-version"><data key="type">parallel-account</data><data key="summary">Two supplied Upanishads contain Balaki–Ajatasatru dialogues; wording and setting should be compared rather than merged.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 320, "quote": "Ajatasatru, the king of Benares, ‘I will tell you about Brahman’. Ajatasatru said, ‘For this proposal I"}, {"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 437, "quote": "Having come to Ajatasatru of Kashi, he said, ‘Let me declare Brahman to you’. To him then Ajatasatru"}]</data></edge><edge id="e492" source="u-maitrayani-upanishad" target="u-maitreya-upanishad"><data key="type">parallel-account</data><data key="summary">Both supplied texts open with King Brihadratha leaving kingship and seeking self-knowledge.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 425, "quote": "The King Brihadratha, setting up his eldest son as king, deeming the body to be impermanent, getting"}, {"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 459, "quote": "I-1. The King, Brihadratha by name, had his eldest son installed on the throne and considering the body"}]</data></edge><edge id="e493" source="u-story-uddalaka-teaches-svetaketu" target="u-suka-rahasya-upanishad"><data key="type">shared-teaching</data><data key="summary">The Chandogya teaching That Thou Art is explicitly interpreted among the great sayings in Suka Rahasya.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 263, "quote": "is the true. That is the Atman. That thou art, O Svetaketu.’ ‘Revered sir, please explain it further to me’."}, {"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 481, "quote": "imparted; the special sense 'That Thou Art' etc., with the six limbs, I desire to hear’."}]</data></edge><edge id="e494" source="u-story-the-faculties-and-the-primacy-of-prana" target="u-kaushitaki-brahmana-upanishad"><data key="type">thematic-comparison</data><data key="summary">Both passages make life-breath central to the discussion of faculties and consciousness.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 130, "quote": "I-7: That very one rises up who is Prana and fire, who is identified with all creatures, and who is"}, {"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 432, "quote": "II-5. Now, next, self-restraint according to Pratardana or the Inner Agnihotra as they call it. Verily as"}]</data></edge><edge id="e495" source="u-mandukya-upanishad" target="u-nrisimha-uttara-tapaniya"><data key="type">thematic-comparison</data><data key="summary">Both supplied translations discuss Om and four aspects of the self including waking, dream, deep sleep and the fourth.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 181, "quote": "7. The Fourth is thought of as that which is not conscious of the internal world, nor conscious of the"}, {"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 452, "quote": "The fourth feet (aspect) of this four fold soul is Thureeya. It is something which make all others act,"}]</data></edge><edge id="e496" source="u-rudra-hridaya-upanishad" target="u-skanda-upanishad"><data key="type">shared-teaching</data><data key="summary">Both supplied translations explicitly teach the unity of Shiva and Vishnu.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 745, "quote": "worship Lord Vishnu, worship Siva Himself. And those who worship Siva, worship Lord Vishnu in"}, {"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 575, "quote": "8-9. (I bow) to Shiva of the form of Vishnu and Vishnu who is Shiva; Vishnu is Shiva’s heart and"}]</data></edge><edge id="e497" source="u-sarabha-upanishad" target="u-nrisimhatapini-upanishad"><data key="type">different-presentations</data><data key="summary">These texts present Narasimha in distinct Shaiva and Narasimha-centered devotional settings; the graph preserves both.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 573, "quote": "Sarabha Upanishad"}, {"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 446, "quote": "Bhagawan Narasimha who is partly human and partly lion shines as Parabrahma with Rutha (discipline"}]</data></edge><edge id="e498" source="u-kali-santarana-upanishad" target="u-muktika-upanishad"><data key="type">thematic-comparison</data><data key="summary">Both place Rama in a teaching about liberation, through the divine name in one and an Upanishadic curriculum in the other.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 817, "quote": "1. Hare Rama Hare Rama Rama Rama Hare Hare"}, {"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 835, "quote": "intellect, delighted in contemplating his own form. At the end of this Samadhi, Hanuman asked with"}]</data></edge><edge id="e499" source="u-satyayaniya-upanishad" target="u-amrita-bindu-upanishad"><data key="type">shared-teaching</data><data key="summary">Both open with the mind as the cause of bondage when attached to objects and liberation when freed from them.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 805, "quote": "1. The mind alone is the cause of bondage and liberation of the people; (the mind) attached to the"}, {"doc": "108upanishads-compiled", "page": 413, "quote": "2. It is indeed the mind that is the cause of men’s bondage and liberation. The mind that is attached to"}]</data></edge><edge id="e500" source="p-kurma-puran-12-12" target="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 15, "quote": "12.12Daksha’s Story Daksha was Brahma’s son and had a daughter named Sati. Sati was married to Shiva. Daksha was thus Shiva’s father-in-law. Once Daksha came to visit his son-in-law. But although Shiva worshipp"}]</data></edge><edge id="e501" source="p-padma-puran-2-1-5" target="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "2.1.5 Sati gives up her Life On being enquired by Bheeshma about the reason why Sati gave up her life, Sage Pulastya narrated the following tale ---- Once, Daksha Prajapati had organised a grand yagya in which he had in"}]</data></edge><edge id="e502" source="p-vamana-puran-6-2-3" target="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "6.2.3 Sati Gives up her Life Sage Pulastya says- \"O Narad! This way, Lord Shiva became famous as Kapaali. This was the reason why Daksha did not invite him. In the meantime, Jaya, sage Gautam's daughter went to see Sati"}]</data></edge><edge id="e503" source="p-vamana-puran-6-2-4" target="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "6.2.4 The Destruction of Daksha’s Yagya Fearing Lord Shiva's wrath, Lord Vishnu went to Hrishikesh and remained aloof for quite some time. Shiva's anger generated so much heat that it was enough to melt the eight Vasus. As a resul"}]</data></edge><edge id="e504" source="p-vishnu-puran-2-upper-35-607" target="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 35, "quote": "ORGANISATION OF YAGYA BY VASUDEV Devarshi Narad, Vyasa and many other great sages arrived in Kurukshetra to have a sight of Lord. Lord welcomed them all. Then Vasudev greeted them and enquired about way for his self-upliftm"}]</data></edge><edge id="e505" source="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-36" target="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 21, "quote": "5.2.36 Birth of Sati When lord Brahma came to know about Daksha Prajapati's anger towards Narad he went to him and cooled him down. Subsequently sixty daughters were born to Daksha. He married his ten daughters"}]</data></edge><edge id="e506" source="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-37" target="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 21, "quote": "5.2.37 Sati does Penance When Sati attained marriageable age, Daksha started worrying. Sati understood the reason behind her father's worried. She went to her Mother Virani and expressed her desire of marrying lord"}]</data></edge><edge id="e507" source="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-40" target="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 22, "quote": "5.2.40 Shiva Married Sati Lord Shiva was anxiously waiting for his arrival. When Lord Brahma arrived he curiously asked about Daksha's response. When lord Brahma told him about Daksha's approval of the marriage. Shiv"}]</data></edge><edge id="e508" source="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-46" target="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 23, "quote": "5.2.46 Sati’s Doubt Devarshi Narad who was listening to the divine tales of Shiva, narrated by lord Brahma with rapt attention, requested him to describe about other characteristics of Shiva. Brahmaji said- \"On"}]</data></edge><edge id="e509" source="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-47" target="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 23, "quote": "5.2.47 Shiva does not see Sati as his consort Once, lord Shiva requested Vishwakarma to construct a grand palace and a magnificent throne for him. When everything was ready, Shiva invited all the deities for the crowning ceremony of lor"}]</data></edge><edge id="e510" source="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-48" target="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 24, "quote": "5.2.48 Daksha’s Animosity towards Shiva Once, the sages organised a yagya at Prayaga, which was attended by all the deities. Lord Shiva too had come, accompanies by Sati. He was eulogized and worshipped by all the deities and the"}]</data></edge><edge id="e511" source="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-49" target="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 24, "quote": "5.2.49 Daksha Performs Yagya Daksha organised a yagya at 'Kanakhal' in which everybody except Shiva and Sati were invited. Sage Dadhichi arrived and not finding the seat for Shiva went back, saying that it was impossibl"}]</data></edge><edge id="e512" source="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-50" target="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 24, "quote": "5.2.50 Sati’s Arrival at the Oblation Site When Sati saw all the deities going happily, she became curious to know where they were going. She requested her companion to ask the moon as to where he was going in such a cheerful mood. T"}]</data></edge><edge id="e513" source="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-51" target="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 25, "quote": "5.2.51 Sati’s Dishonor When Sati reached, where Daksha was performing his yagya, she met her mother and sisters. They met her with all the respect and honour. But Daksha did not even caste a glance at her. Imitati"}]</data></edge><edge id="e514" source="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-52" target="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 25, "quote": "5.2.52 Sati Gives Up Her Life Sati then sat in padmasan and closed her yes. By her yogic powers she united with Shiva and her lifeless body fell into the yagya-kunda. This incident shocked everybody and the rudraganas fu"}]</data></edge><edge id="e515" source="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-54" target="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 25, "quote": "5.2.54 Veerbhadra &amp; Kali Assemble their Army After getting the instructions from lord Shiva, Veerbhardra marched with a huge army which included the mighty Shivaganas like Dakini, Bhairav and Kapalish etc. Goddess Kali to joined him wi"}]</data></edge><edge id="e516" source="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-62" target="motif-daksha-sacrifice"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 27, "quote": "5.2.62 Daksha Organizes Yagya once again Becoming pleased by the invocation and eulogy of the deities, Lord Shiva preached Daksha in the following way-"}]</data></edge><edge id="e517" source="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-73" target="motif-daksha-sacri