ive</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 7, "quote": "3.1.7 Dhruv Pleases God with his Penance The prince Dhruv greeted the sages and continued on his journey. At last, he reached a beautiful forest Madhuvan on the bank of the river Yamuna. It was the same forest, which was later occu"}]</data></edge><edge id="e594" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-4" target="motif-prithu"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "9.4 Prithu In Dhruva's line there was a king named Anga, Anga was religious and followed the righteous path. But unfortunately, Anga's son Vena inherited none of the good qualities of his father. Vena'"}]</data></edge><edge id="e595" source="p-padma-puran-2-1-8" target="motif-prithu"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 8, "quote": "2.1.8 King Prithu and the Surya Dynasty Bheeshma enquired Sage Pulastya about the mighty King Prithu upon whom the earth was named as Prithvi. Sage Pulastya replied---Anga-Prajapati was the descendant of Swayambhuva Manu and Sunit"}]</data></edge><edge id="e596" source="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-4-4" target="motif-prithu"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 15, "quote": "16.4.4 Tale of Prithu In the lineage of the king Dhruv, had occurred a king named Anga. Anga's wife was Sumita, who was the daughter of Mrityadev. They had a son named Ven who was evil in nature. When he grew up"}]</data></edge><edge id="e597" source="p-vamana-puran-6-14-3" target="motif-prithu"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 18, "quote": "6.14.3 King Ven, Birth of Prithu Sage Markandeya then goes on to describe about the splendours of Sthanu Tirtha- Surya was Manu's father. Ven was Manu's grandson. He was wicked and always criticised the Vedas. Being displea"}]</data></edge><edge id="e598" source="p-vamana-puran-6-16" target="motif-prithu"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vamana-puran", "page": 19, "quote": "6.16 Prithudak Tirtha Describing the majesty of Prithudak Tirtha, Mahadev told the deities- \"All of you must pay a visit to this sacrosanct place of pilgrimage situated near Saanihitya reservoir. The day on which"}]</data></edge><edge id="e599" source="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-1-8" target="motif-prithu"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 8, "quote": "3.1.8 Tale of King Ven and Prithu Parashar says- \" With the help of Dhruv, his wife gave birth to two sons- Sishti and Bhavya. Sishti had five sons- Ripu, Ripunjay, Vipra, Vikal and Vrikteja. Ripu had a brilliant son Chaaksh"}]</data></edge><edge id="e600" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-19" target="motif-ganga"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 18, "quote": "9.19 Gautama Part of the water of the Ganga that got stuck in Shiva’s hair was brought down to earth by Bhagiratha. The remaining part was brought down by the sage Gautama. Parvati was married to Shiva,"}]</data></edge><edge id="e601" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-26" target="motif-ganga"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 24, "quote": "9.26 Vriddhagoutama The sage Goutama had a son named Vriddhagoutama. Vriddhagoutama was also a sage. But he was very ugly. Besides, he had no nose; had never had one since he had been born. He was so ashamed of"}]</data></edge><edge id="e602" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-32" target="motif-ganga"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 30, "quote": "9.32 Goutama and Manikundala There was a city named Bhouvana. In that city there lived a brahmana named Goutama. The brahmana had a vaishya friend named Manikundala. (A vaishya belongs to the third of the three classes"}]</data></edge><edge id="e603" source="p-kurma-puran-12-15" target="motif-ganga"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 20, "quote": "12.15A Digression on Goutama Many years ago, there was a terrible drought on earth. There was no food to be had and famine prevailed. There were several sages who lived in the forest, and they too, suffered from a lack"}]</data></edge><edge id="e604" source="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-1-2" target="motif-ganga"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 31, "quote": "7.9.1.2 The Origin of the Ganga Markandeya says- 'River Ganga originates from a place named Dhruvaadhar. This particular place is related with Narayana. River Ganga, then falls on the Sumeru Mountain and gets distributed i"}]</data></edge><edge id="e605" source="p-narad-puran-18-5" target="motif-ganga"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "18.5 The Greatness of the Ganges Sutaji says- \"The glorious description of devotion for Lord Vishnu caused extreme joy for Narad.\" Narad says: \"O great sage Sanat! Now kindly tell me about such a place of pilgrimage which i"}]</data></edge><edge id="e606" source="p-narad-puran-18-10" target="motif-ganga"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 9, "quote": "18.10Origin of the Ganges Narad says: \"O brother! You narrated a wonderful tale. Now, kindly relate to me, how did the Ganges originate?\" Sanatkumar says: O Narad! Pleased with the severe penance of Aditi, Lord Vishn"}]</data></edge><edge id="e607" source="p-narad-puran-18-15" target="motif-ganga"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 13, "quote": "18.15Descent of Ganges and Salvation of Sagar’s Sons Narad says:\"O Muni! What did king Bhagirath do on Himalayas? How did he please Ganges to descend on the earth?\" Sanatkumar says: O Narad! Renouncing all his royal comforts, Bhagirath went to"}]</data></edge><edge id="e608" source="p-padma-puran-2-5-2" target="motif-ganga"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "padma-puran", "page": 23, "quote": "2.5.2 The Descent of River Ganga Describing about the reason why river Ganga was brought down on the earth, Lord Mahadeva told Narad--- King Bhagirath was the architect of Ganga's arrival on the earth. He wanted to liberate"}]</data></edge><edge id="e609" source="p-varaha-purana-8-11-4" target="motif-ganga"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 32, "quote": "8.11.4 Sage Gautam curses the Brahmins There were many sages present at the yagya organised by Agastya. Some of them asked Lord Shiva as to why he had created some scriptures which appeared so confusing to human beings. Lord Shiv"}]</data></edge><edge id="e610" source="p-shiv-maha-purana-2-5-5-14" target="motif-ganga"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-2", "page": 35, "quote": "5.5.14 The Descent of Ganga Once, emperor Sagar performed an Ashwamedha Yagya. Indra abducted the horse used in this Yagya and kept it in the hermitage of Sage Kapil. All the sixty thousand sons of Sagar went to search"}]</data></edge><edge id="e611" source="p-agni-puranas-10-1-6" target="motif-rama-cycle"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 5, "quote": "10.1.6 Parashurama The kshatriyas were the second of the four classes. It was their job to wear arms and protect the world. And rule. The brahmanas were the first of the four classes. It was their job to pray,"}]</data></edge><edge id="e612" source="p-agni-puranas-10-1-7" target="motif-rama-cycle"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 6, "quote": "10.1.7 Rama Brahma came out of Vishnu’s navel. Brahma’s son was Marichi’s son Kashyapa, Kashyapa’s son Surya, Surya’s son Vaivasvata Manu, Manu’s son Ikshvaku, Ikskhvakku’s son Kakutstha, Kakutstha’s so"}]</data></edge><edge id="e613" source="p-agni-puranas-10-5" target="motif-rama-cycle"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 15, "quote": "10.5 Manvataras, Varnashrama and Vratas"}]</data></edge><edge id="e614" source="p-agni-puranas-10-5-2" target="motif-rama-cycle"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 16, "quote": "10.5.2 Varnashrama Dharma All the Manus practised the precepts of dharma (righteousness). This meant non-violence, truthfulness, piety, going on pilgrimages donating alms, serving devas and brahmanas, tolerance of al"}]</data></edge><edge id="e615" source="p-agni-puranas-10-7" target="motif-rama-cycle"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 22, "quote": "10.7 Dreams, Omens and Sri Rama"}]</data></edge><edge id="e616" source="p-agni-puranas-10-7-4" target="motif-rama-cycle"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "agni-puranas", "page": 24, "quote": "10.7.4 Sri Rama’s Teachings Rama had once taught Lakshmana about the duties of a king. The Agni Purana now relates these precepts of Rama’s. The duties of king are fourfold. Firstly, he has to earn wealth. Secondly, he"}]</data></edge><edge id="e617" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-34" target="motif-rama-cycle"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 32, "quote": "9.34 Varnashrama There are four varnas (classes). Their names are brahmana, kshatriya, vaishya and shudra. The duties of a brahmana are to donate alms, perform tapasya, worship the gods, perform yajnas and s"}]</data></edge><edge id="e618" source="p-garuda-purana-14-5-3" target="motif-rama-cycle"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 14, "quote": "14.5.3 Grihasta Ashrama His education being complete now it is the time for him to enter the life of a householder. But, before leaving the hermitage he should pay 'Dakshina' as a mark of gratitude to his Guru for"}]</data></edge><edge id="e619" source="p-garuda-purana-14-8" target="motif-rama-cycle"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "garuda-purana", "page": 19, "quote": "14.8 The Tale of Ramayana Lord Brahma then went on to describe the famous tale of Ramayana the mere listening to which absolves a man from all his sins. He began by giving the details of Sri Ram's lineage and said-'"}]</data></edge><edge id="e620" source="p-kurma-puran-12-3" target="motif-rama-cycle"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 2, "quote": "12.3 Varnashrama Dharma Vishnu said that before creation began, there was only water in the universe and Vishnu slept on these waters. When it was time for creation to begin, Brahma emerged from Vishnu’s body. And"}]</data></edge><edge id="e621" source="p-kurma-puran-12-31" target="motif-rama-cycle"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "kurma-puran", "page": 33, "quote": "12.31The Sita who was an Illusion It is certain that you know the story of the Ramayana and you therefore also known that Ravana, the king of lanka, abducted Sita, Rama’s wife. But you certainly do not know the story of the"}]</data></edge><edge id="e622" source="p-markandeya-puran-7-4-1" target="motif-rama-cycle"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 11, "quote": "7.4.1 Greatness of Being Chaste (and birth of Durvasa, Dattatreya, Chandrama) Brahmin says- O son! Tell me, what should I do now? Sumati says- Now, you should become a Vanprasthi and lead a life of a Bhikshu by having control over your senses. You will attain that rar"}]</data></edge><edge id="e623" source="p-markandeya-puran-7-9-2-2" target="motif-rama-cycle"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc