h borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "the-skanda-purana", "page": 113, "quote": "CHAPTER FIFTEEN Nahu~a and Yayati: Their Indrahood and rail The sages enquired: 1-2. It has been mentioned by you that Devendra regained kingdom without his preceptor. It was on account of disrespect to his preceptor that he had been dethro"}]</data></edge><edge id="e780" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-10" target="motif-sagara"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 9, "quote": "9.10 Sagara Trishanku's son was Harishchandra and from Harishchandra was descended a king named Bahu. Bahu devoted too much time to pleasurable pursuits. The upshot of this was that the defence of the k"}]</data></edge><edge id="e781" source="p-narad-puran-18-6" target="motif-sagara"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "18.6 Fate of King Baahu Narad says: \"O great sage who in the dynasty of Sagar received salvation from Preta Bhava. Who was Sagar? Kindly narrate his whole story to me.\" Sanatkumar says: \"Long ago, there occurred a"}]</data></edge><edge id="e782" source="p-narad-puran-18-7" target="motif-sagara"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 6, "quote": "18.7 The Birth of Sagar Sanatkumar says :- \"O Narad! Both the queens were passing their time in the hermitage of sage Aurv. When half of the rainy season had passed, the elder queen began to cultivate evil feelings"}]</data></edge><edge id="e783" source="p-narad-puran-18-15" target="motif-sagara"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 13, "quote": "18.15Descent of Ganges and Salvation of Sagar’s Sons Narad says:\"O Muni! What did king Bhagirath do on Himalayas? How did he please Ganges to descend on the earth?\" Sanatkumar says: O Narad! Renouncing all his royal comforts, Bhagirath went to"}]</data></edge><edge id="e784" source="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-4-3" target="motif-sagara"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 38, "quote": "3.4.3 Progeny of Mandhatr, Ascend of Trishanku to Heaven and Birth of Sagar Once upon a time Gandharvas of collective name Mauneya defeated the Nagas and snatched all their wealth and powers. The Nagas prayed Lord Vishnu to rescue them. Lord Vishnu told them that he"}]</data></edge><edge id="e785" source="p-vishnu-puran-1-3-4-4" target="motif-sagara"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-1", "page": 39, "quote": "3.4.4 Description of Suryavansh King Sagar had two queens, Sumati, the daughter of Kashyap and Keshini, the daughter of Vidarbha’s king. Keshini had a son Asmanjas whereas Sumati had sixty thousand sons. Anshuman was the s"}]</data></edge><edge id="e786" source="p-vishnu-puran-2-3-4-4" target="motif-sagara"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "vishnu-puran-2", "page": 1, "quote": "3.4.4 Description of Suryavansh King Sagar had two queens, Sumati, the daughter of Kashyap and Keshini, the daughter of Vidarbha’s king. Keshini had a son Asmanjas whereas Sumati had sixty thousand sons. Anshuman was the s"}]</data></edge><edge id="e787" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-16" target="motif-markandeya"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 16, "quote": "9.16 Markandeya Many years ago, a great destruction (pralaya) took place. The earth was shrouded in darkness and nothing could be seen. There was neither sun nor moon. Lightning and thunder crushed mountain"}]</data></edge><edge id="e788" source="p-markandeya-puran-7-16" target="motif-markandeya"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "markandeya-puran", "page": 62, "quote": "7.16 The Benefits of Listening to the Markandaya Purana The birds said- 'O Jaimini! This was the way, sage Markandeya had narrated the divine tales to Kraustuki. A person who either studies this Puraan or listens to it achieves great accomplishme"}]</data></edge><edge id="e789" source="p-narad-puran-18-3" target="motif-markandeya"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 3, "quote": "18.3 Profile of the Sage Markandeya Sanatkumar says: O sage! Faith has the greatest significance. Achievement of everything and appeasement of Lord Vishnu depend upon the faith. Nothing is attainable without faith. Such as a h"}]</data></edge><edge id="e790" source="p-narad-puran-18-4" target="motif-markandeya"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "narad-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "18.4 Tale of the Sage Markandeya Narad says: \"O great sage! Why did Lord Vishnu appear as the son of Mrikandu? What did He do as his son? The Puranas say that during the Pralay, Markandeya had witnessed the direct appearanc"}]</data></edge><edge id="e791" source="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-12-6" target="motif-markandeya"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 76, "quote": "16.12.6 Penance of Markandey &amp; Sighting of Maya SUTJI narrated the tale of Mrikandu's son Markandey. Taking shelter at Mahakaal (Lord Shiva), Markandey had turned away even Kaal (death). And by penance had a sight of Lord Shri Narayana. H"}]</data></edge><edge id="e792" source="p-varaha-purana-8-10-1" target="motif-nachiketa"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "varaha-purana", "page": 29, "quote": "8.10.1 Nachiketa’s Experience Once, Janmejay asked Sage Vaishampayan about Yamrajpuri and it's location. He also wanted to know as to what should a man do so that he does not have to go there. Sage Vaishampayan narrated"}]</data></edge><edge id="e793" source="p-linga-purana-17-2-11" target="motif-dadhichi-vritra"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "linga-purana", "page": 12, "quote": "17.2.11 Dadhichi Subjugates the Sages (Kshoopa) There ruled a king named Kshoopa whose friend was Sage Dadhichi. Once they got into argument over the superiority of their respective castes. King Kshoopa was of the opinion that a Kshetriya"}]</data></edge><edge id="e794" source="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-6-7" target="motif-dadhichi-vritra"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 23, "quote": "16.6.7 Killings of Vritrasur by Indra Shri Shukdev says, \"O Parikshit, from the gallantry of the gods the demon army began to fled. Seeing his army running away in panic, Vritrasur got infuriated. Charging ahead he stopped the g"}]</data></edge><edge id="e795" source="p-shiv-maha-purana-1-5-2-58" target="motif-dadhichi-vritra"><data key="type">shared narrative</data><data key="summary">The supplied section explicitly names this narrative or topic in its heading. Compare the passages; the link does not establish borrowing or doctrinal identity.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shiv-maha-purana-1", "page": 26, "quote": "5.2.58 Cont