upa. Humans are descended"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5432" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-4" target="concept-dharma"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 4, "quote": "9.4 Prithu In Dhruva's line there was a king named Anga, Anga was religious and followed the righteous path. But unfortunately, Anga's son Vena inherited none of the good qualities of his father. Vena'"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5433" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-9" target="concept-dharma"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 8, "quote": "9.9 Trishanku From Dridashva was descended a king named Trayaruni. Trayaruni was a righteous king and followed all the religious dictates. But Trayarun's son Satyavrata was quite the opposite and refused"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5434" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-9" target="concept-sacrifice"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 8, "quote": "9.9 Trishanku From Dridashva was descended a king named Trayaruni. Trayaruni was a righteous king and followed all the religious dictates. But Trayarun's son Satyavrata was quite the opposite and refused"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5435" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-10" target="concept-devotion"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 9, "quote": "9.10 Sagara Trishanku's son was Harishchandra and from Harishchandra was descended a king named Bahu. Bahu devoted too much time to pleasurable pursuits. The upshot of this was that the defence of the k"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5436" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-15" target="concept-vishnu"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "9.15 Indradyumna and Purushottama Kshetra In satya yuga there was a king named Indradyumna. He was a very powerful king as Indra himself. He was handsome, honest and truthful, learned in the shastras and the Vedas, and skilled in th"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5437" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-15" target="concept-devotion"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "9.15 Indradyumna and Purushottama Kshetra In satya yuga there was a king named Indradyumna. He was a very powerful king as Indra himself. He was handsome, honest and truthful, learned in the shastras and the Vedas, and skilled in th"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5438" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-15" target="concept-sacrifice"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 14, "quote": "9.15 Indradyumna and Purushottama Kshetra In satya yuga there was a king named Indradyumna. He was a very powerful king as Indra himself. He was handsome, honest and truthful, learned in the shastras and the Vedas, and skilled in th"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5439" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-18" target="concept-dharma"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 17, "quote": "9.18 Vali There was a king of the daityas named Vali. He was powerful and invincible. He was also righteous and truthful. The gods cold not bear to see Vali’s prosperity and began to plot how Vali mig"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5440" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-19" target="concept-shiva"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 18, "quote": "9.19 Gautama Part of the water of the Ganga that got stuck in Shiva’s hair was brought down to earth by Bhagiratha. The remaining part was brought down by the sage Gautama. Parvati was married to Shiva,"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5441" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-21" target="concept-shiva"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 20, "quote": "9.21 Garuda and Maninaga You have already been told about the great snake (naga) Ananta. Ananta had a son named Maninaga. Garuda was the enemy of the snakes and the snakes were all afraid of Garuda. Maninaga began t"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5442" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-23" target="concept-shiva"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "9.23 Shveta There used to be a brahmana named Shveta. He was a friend of the sage Goutama and had a hermitage on the banks of the Goutami Ganga. Shveta was also devoted to Shiva. In due course, Shiveta"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5443" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-23" target="concept-devotion"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 21, "quote": "9.23 Shveta There used to be a brahmana named Shveta. He was a friend of the sage Goutama and had a hermitage on the banks of the Goutami Ganga. Shveta was also devoted to Shiva. In due course, Shiveta"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5444" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-29" target="concept-shiva"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 28, "quote": "9.29 Brahma’s Head Years ago, there was a war between the gods and the demons and the gods lost. The gods came to Brahma for advice and Brahma asked them to pray to Shiva. When Shiva appeared before them, the"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5445" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-31" target="concept-shiva"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 29, "quote": "9.31 The Hunter There was a sage named Veda. He used to pray to Shiva every day. The prayers lasted till the afternoon and after the prayers were over, Veda used to go to the nearby villages to beg alms. A"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5446" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-32" target="concept-dharma"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 30, "quote": "9.32 Goutama and Manikundala There was a city named Bhouvana. In that city there lived a brahmana named Goutama. The brahmana had a vaishya friend named Manikundala. (A vaishya belongs to the third of the three classes"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5447" source="p-brahma-vaivart-puran-9-33" target="concept-yoga"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "brahma-vaivart-puran", "page": 31, "quote": "9.33 Kandu There was a