o suggested him to describ"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5868" source="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-1-7" target="concept-creation"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "16.1.7 Naradji’s Advice to Ved-Vyas Naradji told him that though he had created great epics, yet all of them lack a devotional description of God. That was why his soul was still writhing. Naradji also suggested him to describ"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5869" source="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-1-9" target="concept-yoga"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 5, "quote": "16.1.9 King Parikshit dishonors Sage Shamik One day, king Parikshit went on a hunting excursion. Till noon, he could not find a game. He was feeling restless because of hunger, thirst and fatigue. Searching water and shelter the king"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5870" source="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-2-2" target="concept-creation"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 7, "quote": "16.2.2 Creation of the World The king Parikshit felt extreme pleasure when Shukdevji satisfied his queries. He then began to feel submerged in the stream of Bhagwad Katha (tale of the God). His selfish worries regarding"}]</data></edge><edge id="e5871" source="p-shrimad-bhagwat-puran-16-3-3" target="concept-tirtha"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "shrimad-bhagwat-puran", "page": 8, "quote": "16.3.3 Vidur comes to know about Kaurava’s Destruction Travelling through various 