ture) Pãda means feet, hasta means hands. Therefore, Pada-Hastäsana means keeping the palms down towards the feet."}]</data></edge><edge id="e7006" source="b-yoga-page-27" target="concept-yoga"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 27, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India TRIKONÃSANA (The Triangle Posture) Trikon means triangle. Tri means three and kona means angle. As the asana resembles the shape of a triangle made by the trunk, arms, and legs, th"}]</data></edge><edge id="e7007" source="b-yoga-page-28" target="concept-yoga"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 28, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India B. SITTING POSTURES BHADRASANA (The Firm/Auspicious Posture) Bhadra means firm or auspicious. Sthiti: Viśrämäsana (Long sitting posture)."}]</data></edge><edge id="e7008" source="b-yoga-page-29" target="concept-yoga"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 29, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India VAJRÃSANA (Thunderbolt Posture) This is considered as one of the meditative postures. While practising it for meditative purposes, one should close his/her eyes at"}]</data></edge><edge id="e7009" source="b-yoga-page-30" target="concept-yoga"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 30, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India ARDHAUSTRASANA (The Half-Camel Posture) Ustr means camel. The final version of this sana will resemble the hump of a camel. In this version, only the first stage"}]</data></edge><edge id="e7010" source="b-yoga-page-31" target="concept-yoga"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 31, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India UŞȚRĂSANA (The Camel Posture) Ustra means camel. The body in this pose will resemble the hump of a camel, hence the name. Sthiti: Vajrāsana"}]</data></edge><edge id="e7011" source="b-yoga-page-32" target="concept-yoga"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 32, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India Technique ŚAŚAKÃSANA Sit in Vajrasana. (The Hare Posture) • Spread both the knees wide apart, keep the big toes touching."}]</data></edge><edge id="e7012" source="b-yoga-page-33" target="concept-yoga"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 33, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCOL Ministry of Ayush, Government of India UTTĂNAMANDÜKÄSANA (Stretched-up Frog Posture) Uttana means upright and Mandüka means frog. The final position of Uttanamandükasana would resemble an upright frog, hence the name."}]</data></edge><edge id="e7013" source="b-yoga-page-34" target="concept-yoga"><data key="type">theme</data><data key="summary">Editorial theme tag on this indexed entry; compare the source in context.</data><data key="evidence">[{"doc": "common-yoga-aasana-englishebook", "page": 34, "quote": "COMMON YOGA PROTOCO